window.BANNANJE_VERSE_MEANINGS_EN = {
  "1.1": "[Bannanje's introduction to Chapter One]\n\nCommentators on the Bhagavad Gita generally give little weight to the first chapter, moving straight to the second with the framing 'Krishna's teaching to a bewildered Arjuna on the battlefield.' But the first chapter itself holds many matters belonging to psychology. In this light, calling this chapter of the Gita 'Arjuna Vishada Yoga' (Arjuna's Sorrow) is not quite apt. This chapter contains a remarkable analysis of the mental states of Arjuna and Duryodhana as they stood on the battlefield in the moment before war began. Krishna gave Arjuna's confused mind a remedy through his teaching. But the wicked Duryodhana died still caught in his own confusion. We should therefore not read the first chapter merely as history, but strive to grasp the psychology within it. The psychology spoken of here does not belong only to those who once stood on that battlefield — it belongs to our everyday life. Only if we watch what unfolds here with this awareness, examining it closely, will this chapter become meaningful to us.\n\nThe Bhagavad Gita opens with a question from Dhṛtarāṣṭra. Dhṛtarāṣṭra means one who holds (dhṛta) the kingdom (rāṣṭra) — that is, the jīva that lies blind and closed-eyed to the truth. Duryodhana represents the very peak of wickedness within us. Bhīṣma, Droṇa, Kṛpa, Śalya, and Karṇa — all of these are like our own senses. Out of the wickedness within our own minds, we turn the precious senses we possess toward wicked ends. In themselves, the senses are not wicked — but joined with wickedness, they do wicked things. In this way, every verse of the Gita, and every character of the Mahābhārata, carries within it a meaning of the deepest value and profundity.\n\ndhṛtarāṣṭraḥ uvāca — Dhṛtarāṣṭra asked:\n\ndharmakṣetre kurukṣetre samavetāḥ yuyutsavaḥ |\n\nmāmakāḥ pāṇḍavāḥ ca eva kim akurvata sañjaya -- In Kurukṣetra, the seat of dharma, what did my people and the Pāṇḍavas, gathered eager for battle, do, O Sañjaya?\n\nAs said before, the blind Dhṛtarāṣṭra's question is also the blind question of the 'jīva'. We are so blind that we do not even know that we know nothing! That is why the Gītā begins with Dhṛtarāṣṭra's question.\n\nThe question that the sightless Dhṛtarāṣṭra put to Sañjaya — who had received the powers of far-sight and far-hearing from Vyāsa — is the first śloka of the Gītā. This battlefield, built by Paraśurāma as Samantapañcaka (five lakes all around) and thus known as a dharmakṣetra, later became Kurukṣetra, a supreme holy land, in the time of the king named 'Kuru'. That is what is addressed here as \"dharmakṣetre kurukṣetre\". Our heart is the dharmakṣetra. The mind (Mind) that decides what is dharma and what is adharma is Kurukṣetra (karmakṣetra). The conflict of the mind itself is the Mahābhārata. Here the blind Dhṛtarāṣṭra asks Sañjaya: 'What did \"my people\" and \"the Pāṇḍavas\", facing each other seeking a fight, do?' By addressing them as 'my people and the Pāṇḍavas', Dhṛtarāṣṭra, the reigning king, is expressing the weakness within himself — this stands out clearly in this śloka.",
  "1.2": "sañjaya uvāca — Sañjaya says:\n\ndṛṣṭvā tu pāṇḍava anīkam vyūḍham duryodhanaḥ tadā |\n\nācāryam upasaṅgamya rājā vacanam abravīt -- Then King Duryodhana, seeing the Pāṇḍava force arrayed in formation, walked up to the Ācārya and spoke.\n\nBefore analysing this śloka, let us first understand a little of the meaning of the word 'anīka' used here. As you know, eighteen akṣauhiṇīs of army took part in the Mahābhārata war. Of them, seven akṣauhiṇīs fought on the side of the Pāṇḍavas while the remaining army was on the Kaurava side. Here akṣauhiṇī is the largest division of an army. In olden days the army was divided into nine divisions. They are:\n\n(1) patti: one elephant; one chariot; three horses and five foot-soldiers — one unit.\n\n(2) senāmukha: three pattis\n\n(3) gulma: three senāmukhas\n\n(4) gaṇa: three gulmas\n\n(5) vāhinī: three gaṇas\n\n(6) pṛtanā: three vāhinīs\n\n(7) camū: three pṛtanās\n\n(8) anīkinī: three camūs\n\n(9) akṣauhiṇī: ten anīkinīs — that is, 21870 elephants, 21870 chariots, 65610 horses, 1,09,350 foot-soldiers.\n\nBy the above reckoning, one anīkinī is one-tenth of an akṣauhiṇī — that is, 2187 elephants, 2187 chariots, 6561 horses, 1,09,35 foot-soldiers. This is one-seventieth of the Pāṇḍava army. When fighting, all eighteen akṣauhiṇīs do not join battle at once. One unit at a time forms a specific vyūha and fights.\n\nSeeing one 'anīka' of the Pāṇḍava army standing ready, disciplined, in a distinctive formation (vyūḍham), Duryodhana's dread grows. This is the first shock happening to him mentally. In this state of mind he goes to the Ācārya. Here Ācārya means the elder among the gurus, Droṇācārya. He goes to him and salutes; then, not behaving like a disciple, he speaks — as though he himself were the king — with arrogance, in a way one ought not to speak.\n\nObserve subtly the matter relating to psychology in this śloka. Duryodhana, who had come to the battlefield declaring \"I will surely win\", loses his mental balance the moment he sees one anīka of the Pāṇḍavas. How agitated he became by it is emphasized in this śloka in 'dṛṣṭvā tu' (especially upon seeing). Though he had eleven akṣauhiṇīs of army with him, Duryodhana, mentally overwhelmed at the sight of one small anīka of the Pāṇḍavas, went to Ācārya Droṇa — how arrogantly he spoke there, we shall see in the next śloka.",
  "1.3": "paśya etām pāṇḍuputrāṇām ācārya mahatīm camūm |\nvyūḍhām drupadaputreṇa tava śiṣyeṇa dhī-matā -- Look, Ācārya, at this great army of the sons of Pāṇḍu, arrayed by your clever disciple, the son of Drupada.\n\nDuryodhana, not going to Bhīṣma the commander-in-chief, goes to Droṇācārya and — so that Bhīṣmācārya may hear — speaks venomous words to Ācārya Droṇa. Here Duryodhana pointedly addresses Droṇa as \"the Ācārya of the Pāṇḍavas\". \"Do not show generosity towards them as your beloved disciples\" — that is the meaning of this remark of his.\n\nThe command of the Pāṇḍava army is held by Dṛṣṭadhyumna, the son of Drupada. Drupada and Droṇa were childhood friends. But after some time Drupada ascended the throne. At that time Droṇa, a poor brāhmaṇa, came to Drupada asking — with the familiarity of childhood — to give him one cow so he could feed his son milk. But Drupada insulted him, saying, \"You do not have the standing to call me a friend. I am a king, but you are a poor brāhmaṇa. What friendship between you and me!\" With this very grudge Droṇācārya joined as guru of the Kauravas and Pāṇḍavas, and after their education was complete, had Drupada captured by the Pāṇḍavas, took away half his kingdom, and said, ''Now I am your equal. At least now accept me as a friend.\" Out of that same vengeance Drupada performed a great yāga and obtained as a boon a son capable of killing Droṇa, had him educated under Droṇācārya himself, and waited to settle his score. He is Dṛṣṭadhyumna — the Pāṇḍava commander-in-chief.\n\nDuryodhana says to Droṇa with a sneer: \"Look at the army (camū) formation your disciple, Drupada's son, has built so close by, with such discipline.\" Observe subtly here the mistakes the inwardly frightened Duryodhana is making moment after moment. The desire that he himself must be king; from that, hatred; from that, envy; from that, loss of mental balance; from that, doing what must not be done. This is a most common thing we see in daily life. These are the various stages by which a human being goes astray.",
  "1.4": "atra śūrāḥ mahā iṣu āsāḥ bhīmārjuna samāḥ yudhi |\nyuyudhānaḥ virāṭaḥ ca drupadaḥ ca mahārathaḥ -- Here all are heroes, great bowmen, equals of Bhīma and Arjuna in battle. Sātyaki, Virāṭa and Drupada too are senior chariot-warriors.\n\nDuryodhana, mentally shaken on seeing the Pāṇḍava army, goes to Droṇācārya and speaks about the Pāṇḍava army and the heroes in it. Ordinarily, whomever we fear, it is they who seem to appear everywhere — as Kṛṣṇa appeared to Kaṁsa. This is psychology. Here Duryodhana has the fear of Bhīma and Arjuna. Out of that very fear he says: \"All the heroes there are equals of Bhīma and Arjuna — great heroes who can fight and win wielding extraordinary bows.\" In reality, the great bow of that age was the Gāṇḍīva, and those who could fight wielding the Gāṇḍīva were Arjuna, Bhīma and Śrīkṛṣṇa. In this situation, to Duryodhana all the great heroes in the Pāṇḍava ranks appear as bowmen equal to Bhīma and Arjuna. This tells of Duryodhana's state of mind.\n\nNext Duryodhana begins naming, one by one, the heroes on the Pāṇḍava side. \"Mahārathas like Sātyaki, Virāṭa and Drupada are in the Pāṇḍava army,\" he says. There is a special reason these three names come up. All three of them ought to have come to the Kaurava side. We have seen earlier how the Pāṇḍavas once captured Drupada — who had been with Jarāsandha and fought against Kṛṣṇa — and took away half his kingdom and gave it to Droṇa. But Drupada, having given Draupadī to the Pāṇḍavas and built kinship, stood on the Pāṇḍavas' side. Virāṭa had earlier been ruling his kingdom in fear of Kīcaka. Because Bhīma killed Kīcaka, Virāṭa joined the Pāṇḍava camp. Then there is Sātyaki, who never crossed Balarāma's word. Since Balarāma had gone away on pilgrimage saying \"I will not involve myself in the war\", Sātyaki too stood on the Pāṇḍavas' side. Thus, as he picks out and utters each of these names, Duryodhana carries the ache within: \"Alas, these mahārathas who should have been on my side are in the Pāṇḍavas' camp.\" This is Duryodhana's lament! Instead of shaping his war strategy, he is wasting time here lamenting that those who should have come to his side have joined the Pāṇḍavas. Duryodhana goes on to list the names of many more heroes.",
  "1.5": "dhṛṣṭaketuḥ cekitānaḥ kāśirājaḥ ca vīryavān |\npurujit kuntibhojaḥ ca śaibyaḥ ca narapuṅgavaḥ -- Dhṛṣṭaketu, Cekitāna, the king of Kāśī too — valiant; Purujit, Kuntibhoja, and Śaibya too — bulls among men.\n\nOn the surface we see the names of six heroes in this śloka. But what we must look at here is not merely these six persons — it is the state of mind of Duryodhana who is picking out and uttering these names, his anguish. Only when we look at the history behind each does this become clear. First, Dhṛṣṭaketu. He is Śiśupāla's son — Kṛṣṇa had killed Śiśupāla at the time of the Rājasūya sacrifice. Now that very enemy's son is on the Pāṇḍavas' side! This is Duryodhana's torment. Cekitāna: he too, like Sātyaki, is a Yādava hero — in Balarāma's absence he had joined the Pāṇḍava camp. Next Duryodhana names the king of Kāśī. Here, when we hear 'king of Kāśī,' two such kings come to mind. The city of Kāśī, formed where two rivers meet, is divided into two parts named 'Varaṇā' and 'Asi' — that is today's Vārāṇasī. The kings of these two parts are both called 'kings of Kāśī.' One gave his daughter in marriage to Duryodhana, and the other gave Kālī in marriage to Bhīmasena. The one meant here is, in fact, Bhīmasena's father-in-law — because Duryodhana's father-in-law had earlier been slain by Kṛṣṇa's discus in the war with Pauṇḍraka. Looking closely, we see the worry gnawing at Duryodhana: 'Alas, even the king of Kāśī is fighting on the Pāṇḍavas' side. My own father-in-law, the king of Kāśī, is not with me today.' Duryodhana calls these three 'vīryavān' — men brimming with immense strength.\n\nDuryodhana next names Purujit, Kuntibhoja, and Śaibya. Purujit and Kuntibhoja are kin of Kuntīdevī. Kuntī was originally of the Yādava lineage, sister of Vasudeva; her original name was 'Pṛthā.' She was adopted by the king of the Kunti country. Because of this adoptive tie, Purujit and Kuntibhoja have taken the Pāṇḍavas' side — this is Duryodhana's lament. Then Śaibya — he is the king of the Śibi country. His son had helped Jayadratha in the attempt to abduct Draupadī in the forest, and was for that slain by Bhīmasena. 'Even so, Śaibya has taken the Pāṇḍavas' side,' Duryodhana laments to Droṇa, pouring out the sorrow deep in his heart! These three he addresses as 'narapuṅgavāḥ' — bulls among men. A narapuṅgava is a man whose very gait carries the bearing of a bull; his walk and his gaze alone can make an enemy's heart tremble.\n\nHere we can clearly see how a person caught in mental turmoil, turning over past events in his mind, loses his self-possession, and, because of that, forgets his true strength and acts accordingly. In such a state no one can find victory.",
  "1.6": "yudhāmanyuḥ ca vikrānta uttamaujāḥ ca vīryavān |\nsaubhadraḥ draupadeyāḥ ca sarve eva mahārathāḥ - Yudhāmanyu is valorous; Uttamauja too is a great warrior. Subhadrā's son and Draupadī's sons — all of them, assuredly, senior chariot-warriors.\n\nDuryodhana, inwardly alarmed at the sight of the Pāṇḍava army, here mentions every individual who came from a single house. Yudhāmanyu and Uttamauja are two more sons of Drupada. Duryodhana addresses them as vikrāntas of heroic bearing. Not only that — he addresses Subhadrā's son Abhimanyu, and the five sons of Draupadī, still not of full age, roughly thirteen to eighteen years old, as mahārathas!\n\nWhat we must clearly recognize here is that once we fall prey to fear, a rope begins to look to us like a snake. In such a situation even the real capability within us goes to waste. Though he had eleven akṣauhiṇīs of army, instead of rousing his own army before the war, Duryodhana is talking about the heroes in the Pāṇḍava army — this clearly indicates his state of mind. We should note that he has named eleven heroes here (Sātyaki, Virāṭa, Drupada, Dhṛṣṭaketu, Cekitāna, Kāśirāja, Purujit, Kuntibhoja, Śaibya, Yudhāmanyu and Uttamauja). Though he had heroes representing eleven akṣauhiṇīs of army with him, to him eleven heroes begin to stand out in the Pāṇḍava army of seven akṣauhiṇīs! Such alone is the plight of everyone who, given to adharma, stands far from Bhagavān. They live forever in inner fear, and to hide that fear they speak all manner of pointless words. If a wicked man is speaking badly, without respect, it means he is deeply frightened within. However much strength such people may have, it can never protect them — because they have no confidence in their own strength. This is the psychology we must recognize here.",
  "1.7": "asmākam tu viśiṣṭāḥ ye tān nibodha dvijottama |\nnāyakāḥ mama sainyasya sañjñā artham tān bravīmi te -- O Dvijottama, who the eminent men of our side are, the leaders of my army — hear about them. For your taking note of them, I am naming them.\n\nA true hero, even when his strength and capability are less, acts with strategy in wartime. A leader rouses his army, his followers. He fills his army with the confidence that they are stronger than the enemy's army. But here Duryodhana, leaving aside consultation with his commander-in-chief Bhīṣma, speaks pointedly to Ācārya Droṇa about the heroes of the enemy's army and says, 'For your taking note, I shall tell you of the leaders of our army'!",
  "1.8": "bhavān bhīṣmaḥ ca karṇaḥ ca kṛpaḥ ca samitiñjayaḥ |\naśvatthāmā vikarṇaḥ ca saumadattiḥ tathā eva ca -- You, the venerable one; Bhīṣma and Karṇa; Kṛpa too — victorious in battle. Aśvatthāmā and Vikarṇa; and Somadatta's son Bhūriśravas as well.\n\nTo Duryodhana — who has described the commander-in-chief, eleven great heroes and the valour of the six sons of the Pāṇḍavas in the Pāṇḍava army — the heroes on his own side are simply not visible! Here he sneeringly names seven heroes of his own side. 'Bhavān' means 'you' (as if to say, the Ācārya of the Pāṇḍavas?); Bhīṣmācārya, who before the war blessed Dharmarāya with victory!; Karṇa, who sat out having sworn, 'So long as Bhīṣma is on the battlefield, I will not fight'; Ācārya Kṛpa, master of the Veda; Aśvatthāmā of the distracted mind; Vikarṇa, who condemned the outrage upon Draupadī and tried to profit from it; Bhūriśravas, who came out of obligation — son of Somadatta, the son of the elder brother of Bhīṣmācārya's father Śantanu! In this way Duryodhana sneeringly names seven heroes of his own side to Droṇācārya, so that Bhīṣmācārya would hear.\n\nIf we look at this in detail, on the Kaurava side there were heroes capable of leading eleven akṣauhiṇīs of army. He had his ninety-nine younger brothers. Heroes among heroes like Śalya and Bhagadatta were on Duryodhana's side. But Duryodhana — frightened at the sight of the Pāṇḍava army and forgetting all of this — speaks slightingly of the great heroes on his own side! Here we see clearly how mentally shaken Duryodhana was. Brooding over the outcome of the war, he had forgotten his very duty!",
  "1.9": "anye ca bahavaḥ śūrāḥ mat arthe tyakta jīvitāḥ |\nnānā śastra praharaṇāḥ sarve yuddha viśāradāḥ -- 'There are many more heroes still. Men come to lay down their lives for me. Able to fight with weapons of every kind. All of them seasoned in battle.\n\nIf we look at this śloka on the surface, though it appears to us that Duryodhana is speaking of the heroes on his own side, what he is saying here is something else altogether. He is saying these words to Droṇa, and he began his speech with \"O Ācārya of the Pāṇḍavas\". Here he says with a sneer — there are many valiant men on our side, 'all of them come to give up their lives'. Saying that they can all wield various weapons, Duryodhana expresses the feeling that he has no one of his own.",
  "1.10": "aparyāptam tat asmākam balam bhīṣma abhirakṣitam |\nparyāptam tu idam eteṣām balam bhīma abhirakṣitam -- The array of that army of ours under Bhīṣmācārya's watch is not enough. But this army of theirs under Bhīma's watch stands fully prepared.\n\nHere lies the culmination(Conclusion) of Duryodhana's speech. He says: \"That army of ours — our army under the leadership of the Pāṇḍava-partisan Bhīṣmācārya — its readiness is not enough before this army under Bhīma's watch.\" If we look closely here — Bhīṣmācārya is the Kaurava commander-in-chief. But Bhīma is not the Pāṇḍava commander-in-chief. Duryodhana has hatred toward Bhīma, and fear. For that reason he refers to the Pāṇḍava army as the army under Bhīma's watch. Looking still more closely — Duryodhana is standing at the forefront of his army on the battlefield. The Pāṇḍava army is far from him. Yet when he speaks he says \"this Pāṇḍava army\" and \"that army of ours\". He is speaking as though the Pāṇḍava army has already come and stands right beside him, and his own army is far, far away from him.",
  "1.11": "ayaneṣu ca sarveṣu yathā bhāgam avasthitāḥ |\nbhīṣmam eva abhirakṣantu bhavantaḥ sarve eva hi -- Distributed as assigned at all the strategic points, standing guard — all of you must keep watch over Bhīṣma alone.\n\n\"Distributed as assigned at all the strategic points, standing guard, all of you must keep protecting Bhīṣma,\" says Duryodhana, addressing Droṇa. His words here are as if to say, \"Do look after our old commander-in-chief Bhīṣmācārya a little.\" He has no faith even in his own commander-in-chief!\n\nHearing these words, how much pain Bhīṣmācārya must have felt. When he stood honestly for battle against the grandchildren he loved — against his own conscience, bound by the royal command of the house into which he was born, against his innermost wish — how much pain the sneering words of Duryodhana, speaking with hope lost, must have caused him. It is hard for us even to imagine this. Let us see in the next śloka what Bhīṣmācārya did on hearing these words.",
  "1.12": "tasya sañjanayan harṣam kuruvṛddhaḥ pitāmahaḥ |\nsiṁhanādam vinadya uccaiḥ śaṅkham dadhmau pratāpavān -- To bring him gladness, the eldest grandsire of the Kurus, that stout-hearted Bhīṣma, let out a loud lion-roar and blew his conch.\n\nTo raise the hope of Duryodhana — who, before the war, was speaking in dejection with hope lost — and to fill him with self-confidence, the most senior, mighty elder of the Kuru line, Bhīṣmācārya, roared like a lion and, as befitted his duty, blew his conch.\n\nThe conch is a very great destroyer of evil forces. In earlier times, morning and evening without fail, the conch would be sounded from every corner of the house so as to be heard in all eight directions. The reason for this is the power in the conch-sound to break the heart of evil forces. As far as the sound of the conch is heard, no evil forces can stray there. So that the influence of āsuric forces should not be present on the battlefield — the tāṇḍava-ground of death — to ready one's own men for battle, and to give the enemy the signal that one is ready, the conch was used in the past. Here Bhīṣmācārya, by his conch-blast, has conveyed the signal of their readiness to both the Kaurava army and the Pāṇḍava army.",
  "1.13": "tataḥ śaṅkhāḥ ca bheryaḥ ca paṇava anaka gomukhāḥ |\nsahasā eva abhyahanyanta saḥ śabdaḥ tumulaḥ abhavat -- Thereafter conches, kettledrums, tabors - drums - gomukhas (horns) were sounded all at once. That noise became an ear-splitting din.\n\nAfter the supreme commander Bhīṣmācārya sounded his conch, from the Kaurava army conches, kettledrums-drums-gomukhas were struck all at once. That noise became an ear-splitting din. Here neither Duryodhana nor his brothers blew the conch. Rather, the conch-blast came from the commander-in-chief. Behind it came the conch-blasts of the army's leaders and of the soldiers. Sounded thus without any ordered manner, it seemed like a mere din. Here it stands out clearly that Duryodhana had not organized the strength he possessed. However much resourceful means(Resource) we may have, so long as we do not know how to use those means in a disciplined way, those means, though present, are wasted. A leader(Leader) who, with the means in hand, loses his state of mind through the desire for power can never see success.",
  "1.14": "tataḥ -- Thereafter,\nśvetaiḥ hayaiḥ yukte -- fitted with white horses,\nmahati syandane sthitau -- seated in the great chariot,\nmādhavaḥ pāṇḍavaḥ ca eva -- Śrī Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna,\ndivyau śaṅkhau pradadhmatuḥ -- blew their divine conches.\n\nAfter the din of conch and kettledrum from the Kaurava army, Śrī Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna — seated in the very biggest of all the chariots there, fitted with four white horses — sound their divine conch-blast.(First Śrī Kṛṣṇa, then Arjuna).\n\nIn this śloka two epithets are used: Mādhava and Pāṇḍava. Here Bhagavān is addressed as Mādhavaḥ. 'Mā' means Mother Lakṣmī. Therefore Mādhava means the Lord of Lakṣmī, Bhagavān — Śrīman-Nārāyaṇa. Again, 'mā' also means knowledge. Bhagavān is the master of knowledge. Therefore He is Mādhava. In the Kṛṣṇa-avatāra, because He was born in the Madhu line, He is called Mādhava. In the Veda the word 'mātṛ' is used in the sense of speech or the corpus of sacred words. The seven great works — Ṛgveda, Yajurveda, Sāmaveda, Atharvaveda, Rāmāyaṇa, Mahābhārata and the Purāṇas — are called mātṛ. Therefore Bhagavān, propounded by the whole of Vedic literature, is Mādhava.\n\nHere Arjuna is addressed as 'Pāṇḍava'. Arjuna is the last of Kuntī's three sons. Generally the love for the last son is always greater. That is why they called him Pārtha, Kaunteya, Pāṇḍava. In Arjuna there was a special presence of Bhagavān. He is also the form of 'Nara', the companion of Nārāyaṇa.\n\nOur body too is a chariot. In such a chariot is the presence of Bhagavān, who drives the jīva. The four horses are the four Vedas. The white colour is the symbol of sattva-guṇa.",
  "1.15": "pāñcajanyam hṛṣīkeśaḥ devadattam dhanañjayaḥ |\npauṇḍram dadhmau mahāśaṅkham bhīmakarmā vṛkodaraḥ - Śrī Kṛṣṇa blew the Pāñcajanya, Arjuna the Devadatta, and Bhīma blew the great conch called Pauṇḍra.\n\nFrom the Pāṇḍava side, the very first to sound his conch is Śrī Kṛṣṇa. The name of Śrī Kṛṣṇa's conch is Pāñcajanya. Behind this conch 'Pāñcajanya' there is a story. Śrī Kṛṣṇa did his studies in the āśrama of the sage Sāndīpani. At the time of returning after completing one's studies, it is customary for disciples to give gurudakṣiṇā. Here the gurudakṣiṇā Kṛṣṇa gave his guru was most extraordinary. An asura named 'Pāñcajana' had killed the son of the sage Sāndīpani. At the time of returning after his studies were complete, Kṛṣṇa gave the guru and his wife, as gurudakṣiṇā, their own son back. The conch in which that rākṣasa named Pāñcajana had dwelt is itself the Pāñcajanya. Pāñcajanya — destroyer of asuras and the symbol of the bliss of knowledge.\n\nRight on the heels of Kṛṣṇa's conch-blast, Arjuna blew his conch named 'Devadatta'. For having fought against the asuras during the vānaprastha period, Indra had crowned Arjuna with a special diadem in the world of the gods and given him this conch as a gift. This conch too is a symbol of the subduing of the wicked.\n\nAfter Arjuna's conch-blast, Bhīma — Vṛkodara — blew the great conch called Pauṇḍra. Here Bhīma is addressed as Vṛkodara. Vṛkodara means one who bears in his belly the fire that can burn the entire universe. How mighty a hero Bhīma is has been expressed here in this way.\n\nIn this śloka Śrī Kṛṣṇa is addressed as Hṛṣīkeśa. Hṛṣīkeśa means the Īśa of the hṛṣīkas. Here hṛṣīka means the senses. He is the master of the senses. He who bestows on us each of the senses and, standing within them, drives them, is Hṛṣīkeśa, the Īśa of the hṛṣīkas. The word keśa also has the meaning 'ray of the sun'. In the sun's ray there are seven colours. Bhagavān, who fills these seven hues and gives joy (harṣa) to life, is Hṛṣīkeśaḥ.",
  "1.16": "anantavijayam rājā kuntīputraḥ yudhiṣṭhiraḥ |\nnakulaḥ sahadevaḥ ca sughoṣa maṇipuṣṭakau -- King Yudhiṣṭhira, son of Kuntī, blew the Anantavijaya; Nakula and Sahadeva blew the conches called Sughoṣa and Maṇipuṣṭa.\n\nAfter Bhīma, Kuntī's eldest son, King Yudhiṣṭhira, blew the conch called 'Anantavijaya' — the symbol of dharma and victory. Nakula and Sahadeva sounded, in due order, the conches called Sughoṣa and Maṇipuṣṭa.\n\nNowhere in the Mahābhārata is there any mention of the name of any other king's conch. The names of the Pāṇḍavas' five conches can be seen only in the Bhagavadgītā. Perhaps these five names have been specially mentioned in the Gītā with the intent that they should remain in our daily remembrance.",
  "1.17": "kāśyaḥ ca parama iṣu āsaḥ śikhaṇḍī ca mahārathaḥ |\ndhṛṣṭadyumnaḥ virāṭaḥ ca sātyakiḥ ca aparājitaḥ -- The senior master-archer, the king of Kāśī; including the senior chariot-warrior Śikhaṇḍī; Dhṛṣṭadyumna and Virāṭa, together with the undefeated Sātyaki, sounded the conch.\n\nThereafter the senior master-archer the king of Kāśī, the senior chariot-warrior Śikhaṇḍī, Dhṛṣṭadyumna and Virāṭa, the undefeated Sātyaki — thus, one by one, they sounded the conch.\n\nWe should note that Śikhaṇḍī is addressed here as a mahāratha. Very many people take 'śikhaṇḍī' to mean a eunuch. But śikhaṇḍī does not mean a eunuch. Śikhaṇḍī fundamentally means one who has a śikhaṇḍa (śikha-aṇḍa). Here śikha means the hair of the head. Those who comb the hair of the head well and tie it behind in the shape of an egg are śikhaṇḍis. This Śikhaṇḍī of the Mahābhārata was female at birth and afterwards became male — a mahāratha. Because he was 'formerly a woman', Bhīṣmācārya would not loose arrows against him.",
  "1.18": "drupadaḥ draupadeyāḥ ca sarvaśaḥ pṛthivīpate |\nsaubhadraḥ ca mahābāhuḥ śaṅkhān dadhmuḥ pṛthak pṛthak -- O lord of the earth; Drupada, together with Draupadī's sons, and the full-armed mighty hero Abhimanyu. Thus all of them, one by one, blew their conches.\n\nLooking closely here, the discipline(Discipline) that was on the Pāṇḍava side stands out. In answer to the din-like noise of conches and kettledrums from the Kaurava camp, the Pāṇḍavas sound their conches in a disciplined, ordered manner. Seen psychologically, we can discover here how a small army possessed of discipline can make the heart of a very large army full of indiscipline tremble. The Pāṇḍava army — ready for the dharma-war in a spirit of total surrender to Bhagavān, without any ahaṅkāra, under Bhagavān's charioteership — was mentally fully prepared. The Kaurava army under the leadership of Duryodhana — who, in the intoxication of ahaṅkāra, through the desire for power, having lost his mental balance, stood against Bhagavān — had already lost its self-confidence. Another special point seen here is this — from the Pāṇḍava side, eighteen persons have sounded the conch in due order. Looking at the science of numbers, in the system of letter-numerals eighteen means Jaya. Ja-ya. In letter-numerals the first letter is the units place, the second the tens place. Therefore ja-ya means not eighty-one — eighteen. With the statement that the 'eighteen conch-blasts', the symbol of jaya, resounded in the Pāṇḍava camp, the eighteenth śloka of the Bhagavadgītā comes to a close!",
  "1.19": "saḥ ghoṣaḥ dhārtarāṣṭrāṇām hṛdayāni vyadārayat |\nnabhaḥ ca pṛthivīm ca eva tumulaḥ vyanunādayan -- That sound embraced earth and sky, echoed and roared, and thundered against the hearts of your sons known by the name Dhārtarāṣṭra.\n\nHere Sañjaya addresses Dhṛtarāṣṭra's sons as 'dhārtarāṣṭrāṇām'. Because this is their surname(Surname). This is Sañjaya's sneering reply to Dhṛtarāṣṭra, who earlier had addressed them as \"my sons and the Pāṇḍavas\".\n\nSañjaya says: \"The conch-blast that resounded in due order from the Pāṇḍava side filled earth and sky, echoed and roared, and split the hearts of your sons known by the name Dhārtarāṣṭra.\" As said before, the disciplined conduct of the Pāṇḍavas had already pierced the Kauravas' hearts. Even before the war began, the Pāṇḍavas had defeated the Kauravas mentally.",
  "1.20": "atha vyavasthitān dṛṣṭvā dhārtarāṣṭrān kapidhvajaḥ |\n\npravṛtte śastrasampāte dhanuḥ udyamya pāṇḍavaḥ ॥\n\nhṛṣīkeśam tadā vākyam idam āha mahīpate -- O king, Arjuna of the Hanuman-banner, seeing the Dhārtarāṣṭras arrayed, readied his bow to enter the fight, and spoke these words to Kṛṣṇa.\n\nWhen the conches have sounded on both sides, the war begins. At this juncture, Arjuna — who has the special presence of Āñjaneya on his chariot — speaks to Śrī Kṛṣṇa(Hṛṣīkeśa), the master of the senses.\n\nHere there was the special presence of Prāṇadeva on Arjuna's chariot. In the chariot of our body too, Prāṇa is ever protecting the jīva 'in the presence of Bhagavān'. Where Prāṇa is, there is Bhagavān's presence. The purāṇic reason for Āñjaneya's presence on Arjuna's chariot is Bhīmasena, another form of Vāyudeva. Hanumanta stopped Bhīmasena, who had set out to fetch the saugandhikā flower, with his tail. In the event that took place on that occasion, Āñjaneya gives his word to Bhīmasena that he will be specially present on Arjuna's chariot. Here Bhīmasena and Āñjaneya are two forms of Prāṇadeva (as with Rāma and Paraśurāma).\n\nThe presence of Āñjaneya on his banner, the Gāṇḍīva in his hand, Kṛṣṇa as the charioteer of his chariot, the mighty hero Bhīmasena at his side. Arjuna, readied for war in this fashion, is beset by ahaṅkāra(Ego). Out of this ahaṅkāra he says to Kṛṣṇa:",
  "1.21": "arjunaḥ uvāca- Arjuna spoke\nsenayoḥ ubhayoḥ madhye ratham sthāpaya me acyuta - 'O imperishable Kṛṣṇa', station my chariot between the two armies.\n\nIn this śloka we must subtly note the tone of Arjuna's ahaṅkāra. 'Me' means 'my'. Even though Śrī Kṛṣṇa himself had taken up the charioteership of Arjuna's chariot, here, in his ahaṅkāra, he speaks to Kṛṣṇa as to an ordinary charioteer and says, in a tone of issuing a command, \"Station my chariot in the middle of both armies.\"\n\nHere Arjuna has addressed Śrī Kṛṣṇa as Acyuta. Acyuta means Bhagavān who himself has no fall, and who removes the fall of his devotees. Though outwardly he showed ahaṅkāra, Arjuna's inner self alone remained awake, and that inner self made his tongue utter this name. Ahaṅkāra has spared not even such great souls. The moment we come to know there is strength in our hands, we forget everything and become slaves of ahaṅkāra. This ahaṅkāra pushes us down into degradation. Arjuna is a great jñāni and a devotee of Śrī Kṛṣṇa. Let us see in the ślokas ahead how Śrī Kṛṣṇa responds when his own devotee falls prey to ahaṅkāra.",
  "1.22": "yāvat etān nirīkṣe aham yoddhukāmān avasthitān |\nkaiḥ mayā saha yoddhavyam asmin raṇa samudyame -- Let me once run my eyes over these gathered here, eager to fight. In this business of battle, who is with me, and with whom must I contend?\n\nThe Mahābhārata war is the first world war recorded in history. Standing at the forefront of such a war, Arjuna is speaking out of ahaṅkāra. He says to Śrī Kṛṣṇa: \"I must once run my eyes over these gathered here. I must see once who has come to make war against me, and who has come from my side to make war along with me.\" In \"I must have a look at those who have come to make war with me\" there is sarcasm laden with ahaṅkāra. This is the speech of conceit — that he is a great hero, and that this army gathered before him is nothing to him.",
  "1.23": "yotsyamānān avekṣe aham ye ete atra samāgatāḥ |\n\ndhārtarāṣṭrasya durbuddhe yuddhe priya cikīrṣavaḥ -- Arjuna says: let me have a look at these gathered here, come out to fight — those who, wishing the welfare of the wit-lost Duryodhana, want to lend him their shoulder in the war.\n\n\"I must once see those who, wishing that the wit-lost Duryodhana should have victory, stand against me to give us trouble,\" says Arjuna to Śrī Kṛṣṇa in a voice of command. Here it is clearly visible to us that Arjuna, saying to Śrī Kṛṣṇa \"I must once see those who stand against me,\" has become a slave of ahaṅkāra. In this ahaṅkāra Arjuna is speaking forgetful of Bhagavān himself.",
  "1.24": "sañjaya uvāca- Sañjaya said:\n\nevam uktaḥ hṛṣīkeśaḥ guḍākeśena bhārata |\n\nsenayoḥ ubhayoḥ madhye sthāpayitvā ratha uttamam -- O king of the Bharata line, Hṛṣīkeśa, having listened to Guḍākeśa's words, stationed the great chariot between the two armies.\n\nŚrī Kṛṣṇa, the lord of the senses, without saying anything, stationed the chariot between the two armies. From here onward we can see, in every single move of Śrī Kṛṣṇa, the method of psychological treatment (Psychotherapy). Ahaṅkāra is a mental state. If we are to come out of such a state, we need mental treatment. That is exactly what Kṛṣṇa has demonstrated here. It would not be wrong if we called such a Śrī Kṛṣṇa the grandsire of psychology(Father of Psychology). Many subjects of psychology lie hidden in the very first chapter of the Gītā.\n\nIn this śloka Kṛṣṇa is addressed as Hṛṣīkeśa, and Arjuna as Guḍākeśa. Guḍākeśa means the hero who has conquered sleep.",
  "1.25": "bhīṣmaḥ droṇa pramukhataḥ sarveṣām ca mahīkṣitām |\n\nuvāca pārtha paśya etān samavetān kurūn iti - Stationing the chariot so that Bhīṣma, Droṇa and the other kings would fall directly before the eyes, Kṛṣṇa says: \"O Pārtha, behold these Kurus gathered here.\"\n\nŚrī Kṛṣṇa, as Arjuna had bidden, brought the chariot to the middle of both armies, stationed it so that Bhīṣma-Droṇa and all the kings were directly visible to the eye, and says, in a voice that stirs up the bonds of kinship: \"O Pārtha, behold your 'Kurus' assembled here.\"\n\nThis is Śrī Kṛṣṇa's wondrous mental treatment (Psycho therapy). Here a test of Arjuna's state of mind is under way. Arjuna is a king. His basic duty is to fight on the side of dharma and to protect the people. While thus performing his duty, whoever may stand against dharma, punishing them is his duty. He is like a judge. Here no discrimination of 'my family, my kinsmen, my friends' is permissible. Let us see in the next śloka how Arjuna, engaged in such a responsible task, laments on seeing his near kinsmen and his gurus.",
  "1.26": "tatra apaśyat sthitān pārthaḥ pitṝn atha pitāmahān |\n\nācāryān mātulān bhrātṝn putrān pautrān sakhīn tathā ॥\n\nśvaśurān suhṛdaḥ ca eva senayoḥ ubhayoḥ api - There, in the armies on both sides, Arjuna saw those gathered. Those who stood as fathers to him, grandsires, gurus, maternal uncles, brothers elder and younger, sons, grandsons, companions, fathers-in-law and friends.\n\nBecause Kṛṣṇa had stationed the chariot facing Arjuna's dearest kinsmen, Arjuna looks at all who were gathered there. To him, who all this while had spoken words of vanity, now appear — standing before him, on both sides — men equal to his father, grandsires, the ācāryas who gave him learning, maternal uncles, brothers elder and younger, sons, grandsons, friends and companions, fathers-in-law, well-wishers — all of them.\n\nIf we observe this śloka with alertness: though there were many eminent men on the battlefield, what appeared to Arjuna were only gurus and elders, kinsmen and dear ones. Here it is clearly seen that the love of kin has awakened in him. For a king engaged in his duty as protector of the people, kin-love is not fitting. For a protector of subjects, protecting the subjects is the basic dharma. He must discharge it without ever letting it lapse. While discharging duty in this way, no discrimination of \"my kinsmen, my family' may be made.",
  "1.27": "tān samīkṣya saḥ kaunteyaḥ sarvān bandhūn avasthitān |\nkṛpayā parayā āviṣṭaḥ viṣīdan idam abravīt -- Seeing all those kinsmen gathered in one place, Arjuna, seized by deep pity, overcome with emotion — spoke thus\n\nSeeing all those kinsmen come together, Arjuna, seized with pity, out of compassion, out of sorrow, pours out his turmoil to Kṛṣṇa.\n\nFrom here onward we see Arjuna pouring out his anguish before Śrī Kṛṣṇa in many ways. Before looking at the coming ślokas, let us make a small analysis here. When reading the Gītā, some people do not understand Śrī Kṛṣṇa's conduct. When Arjuna asks, \"Alas — how shall I fight against my own kinsmen,\" Kṛṣṇa says, \"Fight.\" The question of whether this is just troubles most people. What we must note here is this: had Arjuna spoken these words as an ordinary individual, this is something we would certainly have to accept. But here he is a king who stood on Dharmarāya's side, as protector of Bharatakhaṇḍa, to fight for dharma. Had he said, \"Let the people of Bharatakhaṇḍa not die for our sake,\" it could have been accepted. But he did not say so. What he saw there were only kinsmen. For a king, only he who stands on the side of dharma is a kinsman. Whoever stands against dharma is simply an offender. Punishing them is the king's supreme dharma. Just as a judge standing in the seat of justice cannot pardon his own kinsman who has come having done injustice as 'one of my own', so too what a king must look at here is dharma — not kinship.\n\nIn what follows, Arjuna tries in many ways to convince (Convince) Śrī Kṛṣṇa with his sophistry(Arguments). But Śrī Kṛṣṇa nowhere gives a direct answer to this sophistry. Because had he done so, it would have amounted to giving the seat of honour to an argument that deserved none. We see Kṛṣṇa listening, like a good listener, to every single word spoken by Arjuna in his mental turmoil, and thereafter conducting himself in the manner of one giving treatment to a mental patient.\n\nCome, let us look at the next śloka keeping these points in mind.",
  "1.28": "arjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna said:\n\ndṛṣṭvā imam svajanam kṛṣṇa yuyutsum samupasthitam ॥\n\nsīdanti mama gātrāṇi mukham ca pariśuṣyati |\n\nvepathuḥ ca śarīre me roma harṣaḥ ca jāyate -- Kṛṣṇa, when I look at these our own people gathered here in eagerness for war, my limbs are giving way. My mouth too is drying up. All my body bristles and trembles.\n\nArjuna says: \"When I look at my elder kinsmen gathered here, my limbs shrivel. Seeing that we, who ought to live in mutual friendship, stand today on this battlefield face to face to strike at one another, my face withers with shame. That we, who possess understanding, should have descended to such a loathsome business! We ourselves made this decision, and, bound by it, we ourselves stand on the battlefield. Seeing our own people, my body shrinks. My mouth has gone dry and I cannot speak. To what depths have we sunk! When I think of this, a trembling comes over me.\" Here we see expressed in Arjuna's words first compassion, from it shame, from it fear, and from it bewilderment.",
  "1.29": "[In Bannanje's commentary, ślokas 1.28 and 1.29 are expounded together. The explanation is contained in śloka 1.28.]",
  "1.30": "gāṇḍīvam sraṁsate hastāt tvak ca eva paridahyate |\nna ca śaknomi avasthātum bhramati iva ca me manaḥ -- The Gāṇḍīva is slipping from my hand; my skin is aflame. I cannot stay standing where I stand. My mind is turning to confusion.\n\nHere we can see how, when a person loses his self-confidence, his inner experience surfaces bodily. A person without self-confidence cannot clench his fist tight. Arjuna's condition here has become just that. He says: \"The Gāṇḍīva is slipping from my hand; my whole body is in anguish as if it has caught fire.\" This is the expression of the feeling within, of the turmoil. Once the mind is not steady, the body too cannot be steady. In the same way, if the body is not steady, it is not possible to steady the mind. \"I cannot remain seated in this chariot. My mind has become a tangle of confusion,\" Arjuna laments to Kṛṣṇa.",
  "1.31": "nimittāni ca paśyāmi viparītāni keśava |\nna ca śreyaḥ anupaśyāmi hatvā svajanam āhave -- Keśava, I see only evil omens. What good there is in slaying our own people in battle, I cannot see.\n\n'Nimittas' means omens. Here Arjuna recalls certain evil omens that had occurred earlier. He had noticed these omens even before coming to the battlefield. But they are coming back to his memory only now.\n\nThe ancients gave great importance to omens. In this world everything is knit together, one with another. From a single atom up to the brahmāṇḍa, everything is coherently bound together, everything mathematical. For this reason anything whatsoever can be worked out mathematically. This is what jyotiṣya, omens, the pañcāṅga and so on are. Nothing here is accidental. Only when we do not know is it accidental to us.\n\nLet us look here at some omens seen in the time of the Mahābhārata: elephants and horses were shedding tears before the war. Jackals were howling around the army camps. Animals have a foreboding of bloodshed and of death. They can grasp precisely what is going to happen. This was one omen seen before the Mahābhārata war. Then, in the brahmāṇḍa, two eclipses occurred at an interval of thirteen days during the time of the Mahābhārata war. One eclipse on the fifth day after the war began, and again another eclipse at an interval of thirteen days. This is a terrible omen, a portent of death. Vyāsa had informed Dhṛtarāṣṭra of this beforehand: 'By your son, the menace of the world — the world will be destroyed.'\n\nHaving recalled the omens that had occurred before, Arjuna addresses Kṛṣṇa as 'Keśava'. We have seen him address him as 'Kṛṣṇa' at the beginning. Kṛṣṇa means kṛṣi-ṇaḥ — that is, one who has come to give joy to the earth, to fill it with strength. Keśava means ka-īśa-va. Here 'ka' means the four-faced Brahmā, who is the cause of creation; 'īśa' means Śaṅkara, who is the cause of destruction; Keśava means the supreme power who is the cause of creation and destruction.\n\nThere is a special reason for Arjuna addressing Kṛṣṇa here as 'Keśava'. \"You, the creator, who descended down to give the earth joy and strength — why have you taken your stand as the agent of destruction, as the string-puller of this great war of the yuga's end? What is your purpose?\" — this is the tone that lies behind this epithet. We may recall here that on the eighteenth day of the Mahābhārata war the Dvāparayuga ended and the Kaliyuga began.\n\nProceeding from here, Arjuna begins to give some reasons for why he should not fight. \"Na ca śreyaḥ anupaśyāmi hatvā svajanam āhave\" — 'On no ground does it seem to me that by killing our kinsmen our name will remain praiseworthy in history, in the yugas to come,' says Arjuna.",
  "1.32": "na kāṅkṣe vijayam kṛṣṇa na ca rājyam sukhāni ca |\n\nkim naḥ rājyena govinda kim bhogyaiḥ jīvitena vā -- Kṛṣṇa, I do not desire victory; nor kingship; nor pleasures either. Govinda, what is lordship to us? What are luxuries? What indeed is life itself?\n\nArjuna is one who stood to fight on Dharmarāya's side. Whether to stop the war, or to take a decision — that must finally proceed according to Dharmarāya's judgement. Here Arjuna speaks as though he himself were the decider. \"Who wants this victory, Kṛṣṇa; I do not need victory; I do not desire to win by killing my gurus and elders,\" he says. In this śloka he again addresses him as \"Kṛṣṇa\". Here \"Kṛṣṇaḥ\" means the one who draws. The one who attracts by his beauty and draws upward. Arjuna speaks with the underlying sense: \"You, the attracting power of the world — why did you drag us, this army of eighteen akṣauhiṇīs, to the battlefield?\"\n\nContinuing, Arjuna says: \"What is to become of us by gaining this kingdom? Gaining this kingdom, gaining enjoyment — of what use is it, Govinda?\" Here Arjuna addresses Bhagavān as \"Govindaḥ\". Govinda means the one who is expressed by the Vedas, the one who knows the complete meaning of the Vedas, the one who descended down to the earth, the one who tended the cows, and so on. Kṛṣṇa slew so many wicked rākṣasas and kings. But nowhere did he mount a throne and exercise kingship. He is the one who, joining the cowherds, tended the cows. Here Arjuna is speaking in the sense: \"Like you, I have no desire to mount any throne.\" In the spirit of \"You who descended to the earth — why are you urging me on to war?\" he has addressed Bhagavān as 'Govinda'.",
  "1.33": "yeṣām arthe kāṅkṣitam naḥ rājyam bhogāḥ sukhāni ca |\nte ime avasthitāḥ yuddhe prāṇān tyaktvā dhanāni ca - For whose sake is the kingship? For the sake of pleasures and enjoyments these very men stand on the field of battle, having cast aside all regard for life and for wealth.\n\nIf indeed this royal enjoyment is wanted, for whose sake is it? We ought to be living happily in the midst of all our gurus and elders. What happiness are we to enjoy by killing them all? The very people for whose sake we desire the happiness of this life, and the empire — all of them stand before us for battle, having cast aside all regard for life, ready to lose their everything.",
  "1.34": "ācāryāḥ pitaraḥ putrāḥ tathā eva ca pitāmahāḥ |\nmātulāḥ śvaśurāḥ pautrāḥ syālāḥ sambandhinaḥ tathā -- Gurus, those who stand as father and mother, sons. Likewise grandsires, fathers-in-law, maternal uncles, grandsons, brothers-in-law\n\nand kinsfolk.\n\nArjuna recites before Kṛṣṇa the list of his kinsmen, the members of his family. Gurus, those equal to fathers, sons, grandsires, uncles, the fathers-in-law who gave him their daughters, grandsons, the wife's brothers, and kinsfolk. It becomes clear here that Arjuna, giving a list of none but his own relations, has forgotten his social duty and is speaking personally.",
  "1.35": "etān na hantum icchāmi ghnataḥ api madhusūdana |\napi trailokya rājyasya hetoḥ kim nu mahīkṛte - O Madhusūdana, even if they come to kill me, I would not wish to kill them. Not even for the lordship of the three worlds. What then to speak of the earth?\n\nI have no desire to kill them. Even if they were to come to kill me, I shall not kill them. Even if I were told I would gain the lordship of the entire brahmāṇḍa, I would not fight. That being so, why war for this scrap of earth?\n\nHere Arjuna has addressed Śrī Kṛṣṇa as 'Madhusūdana'. Madhu means bliss. Madhusūdana means... the destroyer of bliss. Bhagavān, whose very form is bliss, who destroys the bliss of those who leave the path of dharma and go on as adharmis in mada (ahaṅkāra), and who uplifts the good — he is Madhusūdana. \"You, who give bliss to the sāttvika and seize the bliss of the tāmasika — what have you come here to do?\" — such is the tone in this address. Here Arjuna expresses his turmoil to Kṛṣṇa: \"The confusion of whether I should fight or not is tormenting me.\"",
  "1.36": "nihatya dhārtarāṣṭrān naḥ kā prītiḥ syāt janārdana |\npāpam eva āśrayet asmān hatvā etān ātatāyinaḥ -- What happiness could there be for us, Janārdana, in slaying the Dhārtarāṣṭras? If we kill these ruiners of the clan, it is sin alone that will cling to us.\n\nIf we were to kill the Dhārtarāṣṭras, what joy is there in it for us? From it, until we die, an unceasing consciousness of sin may haunt us. Having killed them we would have to live with the consciousness of sin — beyond that there is no gain: this is Arjuna's argument.\n\nIn this śloka Arjuna has used the word \"ātatāyi\". An ātatāyi means one who sets fire to another's house; one who mixes poison into another's food and gives it; one who seizes another's property; one who lays hands on the wives of others; and so on. The śāstra says: strike down the 'ātatāyins' wherever you see them. Here Duryodhana had done all of the above evil deeds. He had given Bhīma food laced with poison, had committed the stripping of Draupadī's garment, had set fire to the lac-house in which the Pāṇḍavas stayed. But see to what extent kin-love haunts Arjuna here — he laments to Kṛṣṇa, \"Even though all of them are ātatāyins, are they not the sons of my father's elder brother?\"\n\nIn this śloka Arjuna has addressed Śrī Kṛṣṇa as 'Janārdana'. Janārdana means the destroyer of the wicked, and the one who gives to the good a mokṣa-bestowing death with no further birth to follow. \"You, the giver of mokṣa — why are you engaging us in this war, which brings us back again into this cycle of birth and death? War is never mokṣa-bestowing; it is filled with vengeance and hatred. That being so, why this war?\" — this is the tone that lies behind this address.",
  "1.37": "tasmāt na arhā vayam hantum dhārtarāṣṭrān sva bāndhavān |\nsva janam hi katham hatvā sukhinaḥ syāma mādhavā - Therefore it is not fitting for us to kill our own bonded kin, the Dhārtarāṣṭras. Having killed our own people, how could we be happy, Mādhava?\n\nThe Dhārtarāṣṭras are our brothers. Killing them is not fitting. Having ourselves killed our own people, how could we be happy? This is Arjuna's question. When we observe closely, it is clearly seen here that Arjuna is speaking having completely forgotten that he is the protector of the people, and that protecting dharma is his duty.\n\nHere Arjuna addresses Kṛṣṇa as 'Mādhava'. 'Mā' means Mother Lakṣmī. Therefore Mādhava means Bhagavān, the lord of Lakṣmī. Again, 'mā' also means knowledge. Bhagavān is the master of knowledge; therefore he is Mādhava. Since Śrī Kṛṣṇa was born in the Madhu line, he is also called Mādhava. In the Veda the word mātṛ has been used in the sense of speech, or the sacred word (vāṅmaya). Bhagavān, who is propounded by the whole of Vedic vāṅmaya, is Mādhava. There is a reason for Arjuna addressing Śrī Kṛṣṇa in this way. The awareness was in him that Kṛṣṇa was not merely his maternal uncle's son, but some superhuman power. For that reason, in place after place, in one way or another, he has recalled that superhuman power of Bhagavān. \"I know that you are the primal power of this world. Even so, why do you sit as a mute spectator in this compact?\" — this is the purport of his address.\n\nHow strange this is. Arjuna — a great jñāni who was at Bhagavān's side and knew Kṛṣṇa to be a superhuman power — falls into confusion at every moment. That being so, what of the plight of you and me? This is the strange māyā. Even after seeing the cosmic form of Bhagavān in the mouth of Śrī Kṛṣṇa who had eaten mud, Yaśodā proceeds to punish Kṛṣṇa. She remains in the māyā of the affection of 'my child'. Arjuna's condition here is the same.",
  "1.38": "yadi api ete na paśyanti lobha upahata cetasaḥ |\nkulakṣaya kṛtam doṣam mitradrohe ca pātakam- -The hearts of all these men have fallen into the grip of greed. They do not see the evil wrought by the ruin of the family, and the sin that lies in treachery to friends.\n\nBlinded by the desire for money, by greed, their minds have lost the power to think. From this comes the ruin of the family. The entire Bharata line becomes extinct. We who are kinsmen ought to have lived with one another in the closeness of friends. But we hate one another. This is treachery.\n\nHere the word 'mitra' has been used. Who is a true friend? He who foresees, even before we do, when calamity may come upon us, and, acting in advance, without expecting any recompense whatsoever, gives life for life — he is a 'mitra'.",
  "1.39": "katham na jñeyam asmābhiḥ pāpāt asmāt nivartitum |\nkulakṣaya kṛtam doṣam prapaśyadbhi janārdana- O Janārdana, we who can foresee the evil that comes of the family's ruin — how should we not know that we must keep away from such a wrong?\n\nIf we do not understand that such a great sin must not be committed, what difference at all is there between Duryodhana and us? O Janārdana, you who deliver from the bond of birth and death — the fault of destroying an entire family is clearly visible to my eyes. The picture of post-war Bhārata stands before my eyes. If, even knowing the harm that comes of war, we show the mentality of scraping our feet for a fight, do we not sink to a low level just like Duryodhana? This is Arjuna's question.",
  "1.40": "kulakṣaye praṇaśyanti kula dharmāḥ sanātanāḥ |\ndharme naṣṭe kulam kṛtsnam adharmaḥ abhibhavati uta - When the family crumbles, the undying family-dharmas vanish. When dharma disappears, adharma overruns the entire family.\n\nThe great problem that comes of war is the destruction of \"kuladharma\". In this śloka Arjuna uses several words concerning dharma. If words like jātidharma, kuladharma, sanātanadharma and so on are to be understood, we must first know: what is dharma? How many kinds of it are there?\n\nOrdinarily, when we say jātidharma, what appears to us is the caste division of brāhmaṇa-kṣatriya-vaiśya-śūdra. And when we say kuladharma, the hereditary occupation of a household — carpenter, blacksmith, cobbler and so on. But that is not what is spoken of here. Kuladharma means the dharma of society(Religion of Society); jātidharma means the natural personal dharma of a human being, come from the traits of his birth. Beyond these two dharmas is sanātanadharma. That is the eternal dharma. In all times and in all lands, sanātanadharma does not change. The individual's dharma varies between individuals. The dharma of society changes in keeping with the time, in keeping with the land.\n\nJātidharma means our natural inborn trait (svabhāva). Every jīva has a svabhāva all its own, but detecting it is difficult. Because of the two coverings that lie upon it. One is heredity, and the other is the influence of the environment in which one grew. Man, the child of his environment, lives amid the qualities flowing down to him by heredity and the influence of his surroundings. These cover up the true svabhāva.\n\nThe dharma of society: we are not beings who live alone without the help of others. Therefore the collective has a distinct dharma of its own. One has to modify one's individual dharma (one's personal inclination) for the sake of the dharma of society (for example: speaking loudly is my personal dharma. But maintaining silence in a hospital is the dharma of society). The dharma of society changes as society changes, in keeping with society. For example, the dharma of marriage. At what age one should marry changes according to the times. In the Vedic age the custom of the child marriage of girls was in force. The reason was that the gurukula system existed then, and the boys did their studies in the gurukula from their eighth year up to their twentieth year and then returned home (this is called samāvartana). To a boy who had thus learnt and returned they would give an eight-year-old girl in marriage. She would learn as his pupil and, after coming of age, take up family life. By this she acquired the capacity to know her husband and to fit into the atmosphere of her husband's house. Gradually, when the gurukula system came to an end, this child marriage too came to an end. In the age of the Rāmāyaṇa and the Mahābhārata there was no child marriage. Later, more recently, about a thousand years ago, when the foreign invasions came, people stopped sending girls to school and began marrying them off early. This was a change that came of social pressure. Thus the dharma of society changes in keeping with the time, in keeping with society.\n\nSanātanadharma: this is the dharma that never changes, in any age and in any condition. What the Veda teaches is sanātana dharma. For example: one must live honestly, one must not steal another's property, one must speak the truth, one must walk trusting in God, and so on. This is the eternal dharma. The dharma that changes in no land and no age.\n\nThe dharma of society must be adjusted in accordance with sanātana dharma. Jātidharma must be adjusted in accordance with the dharma of society. The extraordinary property (the defining mark) of a thing is its dharma. For example: seeing is the dharma of the eye, hearing is the dharma of the ear. But the very thing that is dharma can itself become adharma. To look at what ought not to be looked at is adharma! There is a limit to looking. When that is crossed, dharma itself becomes adharma.\n\nHere Arjuna says: \"When the family is destroyed, the judicial order that ought to administer the dharma of society is destroyed, and the dharma of society perishes. The social order collapses and falls. The whole society sinks into adharma. This is the gift we give society by making war.\"",
  "1.41": "adharma abhibhavāt kṛṣṇa praduṣyanti kulastriyaḥ\nstrīṣu duṣṭāsu vārṣṇeya jāyate varṇasaṅkaraḥ -O Vārṣṇeya, by the onslaught of adharma the women in society go astray. From that, society falls into varṇasaṅkara.\n\nIf war comes, all the young men die of it. Adharma overruns society. A society without young men comes into being. Socially, woman is left without security. The consequence of that is the abuse of her innocence. From that, varṇasaṅkara arises in society, says Arjuna.\n\nHere varṇasaṅkara means the ruin of the jīva's colour, that is, its svabhāva. There is a statement in the śāstras that a girl should not be forced into higher education against her mind. The reason is that it has an effect upon her womb. Its consequence falls upon the child that is born. When a woman engages herself without her own wish; through the company of many men — a terrible consequence falls upon the offspring to come. There is the possibility of a deranged progeny being born that listens to no one's word and bends to no one's rule. This is the varṇasaṅkara that Arjuna is speaking of here.\n\nHere Arjuna has addressed Kṛṣṇa as 'Vārṣṇeya'. The senior king of the Yādava house was Vṛṣṇi. Kṛṣṇa, who incarnated in that line, is Vārṣṇeya. There is mention of this name in the Veda. Bhagavān, the master of the gods — the higher powers who grant the wished-for desire — is Vārṣṇeya. \"You who fulfil the wishes of all, who descended down for the establishment of dharma — why are you not stopping this war?\" — this tone is present in this address of Arjuna's.",
  "1.42": "saṅkaraḥ narakāya eva kulaghnānām kulasya ca |\npatanti pitaraḥ hi eṣām lupta piṇḍa udaka kriyāḥ -The ruin of the colour pushes the ruiners of the clan, and the clan itself, into naraka. The forefathers of such men suffer, deprived of the piṇḍa and the libations.\n\nBecause children of deranged mind are born, the whole society has to go to naraka. And we too, who are the cause of it, must go to naraka. When society becomes confounded in this way, when it has lost all faith, the pitṛs fall into naraka without the piṇḍa — this is Arjuna's conception of the horror of the war's aftermath.\nNow, what is piṇḍa? What is its significance? To whom does it go, and how does it reach them? Let us understand this. The gross piṇḍa that we offer does not go directly to the departed jīva. After death, the jīva dwelling in the subtle body has no need of a gross piṇḍa. The jīva goes, in accordance with its karma, to where it must go. We offer the piṇḍa to the pitṛ-devatās. This is a host of deities. In it there are a hundred deities in all. Among them, three principal pitṛs. Just as during our lifetime we worship the deities presiding over the tattvas, so after death, once the jīva has come out of this gross body, the host of deities that protects it is the pitṛ-gaṇa. Those who govern these pitṛs are the Vasus, the Rudras and the Ādityas. It is the tradition to worship these pitṛ-devatās on the annual day of our forebears' passing. The reason for making the piṇḍa offering to the pitṛs here is this: the piṇḍa offering is the prayer, 'If for some particular reason there is an obstruction to our elders on their path, remove it.' It is the worship of the pitṛ-devatās, saying, \"Protect them from the prārabdha-karma.\" As for the crow touching the offering — it is the omen-sign that the pitṛ-devatās have accepted our worship.",
  "1.43": "doṣaiḥ etaiḥ kulaghnānām varṇasaṅkarakārakaiḥ |\nutsādyante jātidharmāḥ kuladharmāḥ ca śāśvatāḥ -By these wrongs of the ruiners of the clan, the causes of the ruin of society's colour, the natural dharmas and the undying family-dharmas are uprooted.\n\nThe direct consequence of war upon society is this — a society without leaders, without young men. The imbalance in the ratio of men and women. How many years would it take for this to come right? If we look at the Mahābhārata war, at least fifty lakh people died there. By such a great war we shall become the banes of the clan who destroyed an entire society — this is Arjuna's anguish. \"Because the order of society is destroyed, svabhāva is destroyed. By this, the unfolding of each jīva's svabhāva does not take place, and the development of personality is destroyed. The dharma of society, of eternal value, is destroyed. The stable dharma that society has accepted from time without beginning is swept away,\" — thus Arjuna sets out his argument before Kṛṣṇa.",
  "1.44": "utsanna kuladharmāṇām manuṣyāṇām janārdana |\nnarake niyatam vāsaḥ bhavati iti anuśuśruma- O Janārdana, we have heard it told that for men fallen from the ground of family-dharma, naraka is the unending abode.\n\nNaraka in this world, naraka in the next — thus the whole of one's existence in naraka. I have heard it from those who know, that the condition of rotting forever in naraka itself will come. And it is we ourselves who become the cause of this world becoming naraka, and the next as well.",
  "1.45": "aho bata mahat pāpam kartum vyavasitāḥ vayam |\nyat rājyasukhalobhena hantum svajanam udyatāḥ -- Alas! What a great wrong we had set about doing! Out of greed for the pleasure of lordship, we have set out to kill our very own people!\n\nHere Arjuna speaks in the voice of one collapsing — \"Alas.. what a great tragedy, what a great deed of sin was about to take place before us? What a calamity was about to come to pass? We were putting our hands to a great wrong. Out of greed for the pleasure of the kingdom, the deed of killing our own people and pushing an entire race into downfall was about to happen at our hands.\"",
  "1.46": "yadi mām apratīkāram aśastram śastrapāṇayaḥ |\ndhārtarāṣṭrāḥ raṇe hanyuḥ tat me kṣema taram bhavet -- If the Dhārtarāṣṭras, weapons in hand, should kill me in battle — me, unresisting, holding no weapon — that would be my good fortune.\n\nI shall make no retaliation at all. If they were to come to strike me, I shall not strike back in return. If, sword in hand, they kill me, I shall die counting it my good fortune. Why should one live to see such a tragedy? Whatever may come, I want no war. — thus Arjuna throws up his hands.\n\nThe Arjuna who at the start of the war had said with hauteur, \"Senayor ubhayor madhye rathaṁ sthāpaya me'cyuta\" — melted away within a mere moment. This was the feeling that had sat hidden in his subconscious without his own knowing. Kṛṣṇa, by his psychological treatment, made it come out completely. This is the first treatment given to one caught in mental conflict. Kṛṣṇa is the first great psychologist who showed the world how the mental illness of man can be cured. Had Kṛṣṇa not stationed the chariot before Arjuna's dear ones, all these thoughts would not have come out of his mind. This mental treatment of Śrī Kṛṣṇa becomes intelligible to us only when we analyse it knowing psychology together with philosophy.",
  "1.47": "sañjaya uvāca -Sañjaya said:\n\nevam uktvā arjunaḥ saṅkhye ratha upasthe upāviśat |\n\nvisṛjya saśaram cāpam śoka saṁvigna mānasaḥ -- On the field of battle Arjuna flung down his bow and arrows and, churning with grief, sank down into the lap of the chariot.\n\nArjuna, who had spoken all this standing in the chariot, collapsed and sat down in the middle of the chariot. He let all his weapons fall, threw up his hands, and sat down.\n\nIn this chapter Arjuna has laid open all his feelings before Kṛṣṇa. Every matter Arjuna spoke of is a matter known to us. But what Kṛṣṇa says from here on is what we do not know. Let us see, in the chapters ahead, the answer of Bhagavān, who carries us from darkness towards light.\n\nBefore going to the next chapter, one small analysis. Most among us have a doubt: how did Śrī Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna carry on this conversation of seven hundred ślokas on the battlefield? Did they have that much time? If this question is to find its answer, we must first know the mode of warfare of the Mahābhārata age. According to the rules of war of that time, if the opponent was not ready, one could not attack him. The war would not begin until a man had taken his stand fully prepared. Then there is the question of how long their conversation went on. Arjuna was one of the great geniuses of that age. The conversation here is taking place between two geniuses. Knowledge of that order is hard for us even to imagine today. Their complete conversation did not take place in the form of today's seven hundred ślokas. Vedavyāsa has simply set their conversation before us in the form of seven hundred ślokas so that we may understand it. Today even these ślokas we do not understand. That is why we find the commentary of the Gītā in thousands of pages. But the great geniuses can say this most concisely, in the least time, and can understand it so. The conversation that took place between Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna was just such a concise conversation, packed with substance.\n\nAnother important matter is the question: why did Kṛṣṇa teach the Gītā to Arjuna alone? Why did he not teach it to his elder brother Bhīma, or to the others? The answer to this question is very easy. Medicine is for the one who has the illness, not for the others. The one who was there in inner mental turmoil was Arjuna alone. That is why Śrī Kṛṣṇa gave the teaching of the Gītā to Arjuna (and through him, to us).",
  "2.1": "[Bannanje's introduction to Chapter Two]\n\nKrishna's teaching to Arjuna — who has thrown down his weapons, declared he will not fight, and sunk down in his chariot — begins with this second chapter. This chapter stands as the compendium (panchanga) of the entire Bhagavad Gita: Vyasa has captured the essence of the whole message here. The remaining sixteen chapters are simply the teaching given here, unfolded at greater length.\n\nsañjaya uvāca — Sañjaya said:\n\ntam tathā kṛpayā āviṣṭam aśru pūrṇa ākula īkṣaṇam |\n\nviṣīdantam idam vākyam uvāca madhusūdanaḥ -- Seeing him thus overcome with compassion, his eyes full of tears, his sight clouded, despondent, Madhusūdana spoke these words to him.",
  "2.2": "bhagavān uvāca — Bhagavān said:\n\nkutaḥ tvā kaśmalam idam viṣame samupasthitam |\n\nanārya juṣṭam asvargyam akīrti karam arjuna -- Arjuna, in such a crisis, from where has this filth come over you — unapproved by the wise, unfit for heaven there, and disgraceful here?\n\n\"Where has this thinking come from? How has this physical filth clung to you? At such a moment of emergency, when you ought to act at once — what is this squalid notion? No ārya ever thinks in this way. Following such a line of thought, there is no heaven hereafter and no glory here. The wise, the learned, the elders never think thus\" — with these blunt words Kṛṣṇa demolishes the whole of Arjuna's argument in a single stroke. The word 'ārya' used here does not denote a race. Ārya means one of understanding — the aged and wise, those worthy of respect. In Kannada the word 'ayyā' carries this very meaning.",
  "2.3": "klaibyam mā sma gamaḥ pārtha na etat tvayi upapadyate |\n\nkṣudram hṛdaya daurbalyam tyaktvā uttiṣṭha parantapa -- Pārtha, do not become a coward — this does not become you. O hero who strikes fear into the hearts of foes, cast off this petty weakness of heart and rise up.\n\n\"Do not speak like an impotent man. I did not expect such cowardice from Arjuna. Cast off this petty mental weakness, and fight like a hero against injustice\" — so saying, Kṛṣṇa administers to Arjuna a jolting Shock Treatment.\n\nWhy did Kṛṣṇa speak this way? Did he desire war and bloodshed? Such questions naturally arise in us. Looked at closely: had the war not happened, ten times the injustice Arjuna feared socially would have followed instead. Duryodhana was the great scourge of his age — the man who, in a full assembly, laid hands to disrobe his own sister-in-law. Think what would have become of society had such a man ascended the throne. Kṛṣṇa's concern here is who should hold this country's leadership going forward — not affection for kin. More important than winning or losing is raising one's voice against injustice. Even dying while fighting injustice is no misfortune — but one must stand firm against it. That is Kṛṣṇa's message.\n\nThrough the Mahābhārata, Śrī Kṛṣṇa gave us a new doctrine of dharma. The notion of dharma before the Mahābhārata was different. Take truth-telling, for instance: truth used to mean stating things exactly as they are. But by Kṛṣṇa's reckoning — whatever falsehood benefits society, that is truth; whatever truth harms society, that is falsehood. Take non-violence likewise: if one act of violence makes possible many acts of non-violence, that itself is non-violence. For example, if a man-eating tiger raids a village every day and devours one person at a time, killing it is not violence against a creature. If one war can establish non-violence for generations to come, there is no fault in waging that war. Whatever protects the good — that is truth. What matters in dharma is intention — what matters is the purpose behind the cause for which we fight.",
  "2.4": "arjunaḥ uvāca — Arjuna said:\n\nkatham bhīṣmam aham saṅkhye droṇam ca madhusūdana |\n\niṣubhiḥ pratiyotsyāmi pūjārhā arisūdana -- O Madhusūdana, slayer of foes, how shall I fight in battle with arrows against Bhīṣma and Droṇa — men worthy of worship, O Arisūdana?\n\nNoticing Kṛṣṇa's sharp reply and his smiling face, the tenor of Arjuna's speech now changes. He drops his emotional outburst and turns, with feeling, toward surrender.\n\nArjuna says: \"How shall I confront Bhīṣmācārya and Droṇācārya standing before my eyes? One is the patriarch-sage, the very root of our lineage; the other is the great sage who taught every kṣatriya. How shall I aim arrows at such venerable, worship-worthy men? O Madhusūdana, who at the dawn of creation destroyed the demons Madhu and Kaiṭabha; O Arisūdana, who has incarnated to destroy the wicked — the slaying of the wise and of teachers cannot be your true purpose\" — with this altered tone Arjuna continues his speech.",
  "2.5": "gurūn ahatvā hi mahānubhāvān śreyaḥ bhoktum bhaikṣyam api iha loke |\n\nhatvā artha kāmān tu gurūn iha eva bhuñjīya bhogān rudhira pradigdhān -- Better, instead of killing these great souls, to live even by begging alms in this very world. For if I kill them, I would only enjoy — here, in this life — pleasures and riches smeared with blood.\n\nBetter to live by begging than to enjoy the pleasures of wealth after killing one's teachers and elders — is that not the higher dignity? What throne is worth having, if to gain it we must kill great souls like Bhīṣma and Droṇa, men who have realized the truth within their own hearts?\n\nIf we kill these men — who stand in Duryodhana's service only as bonded retainers of his court, obliged by debt to the palace — and ascend the throne, it would be no different from feasting on pleasures drenched in our teachers' blood.",
  "2.6": "na ca etat vidmaḥ katarat naḥ garīyaḥ yat vā jayema yadi vā naḥ jayeyuḥ |\n\nyān eva hatvā na jijīviṣāmaḥ te avasthitāḥ pramukhe dhārtarāṣṭrāḥ -- Nor do we know which is better for us — that we should conquer, or that they should conquer us. Those very men, killing whom we would have no wish to live, stand arrayed before us — the sons of Dhṛtarāṣṭra.\n\nHaving argued his own case up to now, Arjuna surrenders to Kṛṣṇa and asks him what is truly good for him. \"I do not know whether our good lies in our winning this war, or in our elders winning it. Even the desire to win this war has left me\" — so Arjuna lays bare the indecision of his mind before Kṛṣṇa. Dhṛtarāṣṭra, who is as a father to us, and his sons stand arrayed against us for battle. Arjuna says he has no wish to fight, for the sake of Duryodhana's ambitions, against our great uncle, for whom we would give up our lives.",
  "2.7": "kārpaṇya doṣa upahata svabhāvaḥ pṛcchāmi tvām dharma sammūḍha cetāḥ |\n\nyat śreyaḥ syāt niścitam brūhi tat me śiṣyaḥ te aham śādhi mām tvām prapannam -- My native courage is clouded by the taint of weakness; my mind bewildered about dharma, I ask you — tell me for certain what is truly good for me. I am your disciple; instruct me, who has come to you for refuge.\n\n\"I have fallen into the fault of weakness. I am in the pitiable state of being unable to decide right from wrong. My power of thought regarding dharma has become a nest of confusion. The doubt over whether to fight or not torments me. I do not know what is good for me. I have become your disciple and taken refuge in you — guide me\" — thus Arjuna beseeches Kṛṣṇa. Here we see clearly that Arjuna, who till now spoke before the Lord like a scholar, is now pleading for surrender before him.",
  "2.8": "na hi prapaśyāmi mama apanudyāt yat śokam ucchoṣaṇam indriyāṇām |\n\navāpya bhūmau asapatnam ṛddham rājyam surāṇām api ca ādhipatyam -- I see no way to drive off this grief that is drying up the very sap of my senses — not even if I were to gain, on this earth, an unrivalled and flourishing kingdom, or even sovereignty over the gods.\n\n\"My senses are withering away. I see no path to drive off my sorrow. Even were I to gain dominion on earth or in heaven, I could not shake off this grief,\" says Arjuna.",
  "2.9": "sañjaya uvāca — Sañjaya said:\n\nevam uktvā hṛṣīkeśam guḍākeśaḥ parantapaḥ |\n\nna yotsye iti govindam uktvā tūṣṇīm babhūva ha -- Having spoken thus to Hṛṣīkeśa, Guḍākeśa — the scorcher of foes — said to Govinda, 'I will not fight,' and fell silent.\n\nSañjaya, describing Arjuna's state on the battlefield to Dhṛtarāṣṭra, speaks thus: \"Arjuna, who has conquered sleep, having said 'I will not fight' to Govinda, the Lord of the senses who pervades the whole universe, fell silent.\"",
  "2.10": "tam uvāca hṛṣīkeśaḥ prahasan iva bhārata |\n\nsenayoḥ ubhayoḥ madhye viṣīdantam idam vacaḥ -- O Bhārata, to him — despondent there between the two armies — Hṛṣīkeśa spoke these words, smiling gently.\n\nWhen Arjuna, having surrendered completely, stands pleading before him, Śrī Kṛṣṇa, smiling softly, begins to speak. Here Arjuna has reached the stage of accepting what Kṛṣṇa says. His emotional turmoil has left him, and surrender has arrived. If the Lord is to guide us, we must give up our ego and surrender completely to him — then guidance will surely come to us. The teaching Kṛṣṇa gives Arjuna here is the teaching he gives to every one of us. Bearing this in mind, let us come and listen to Kṛṣṇa's teaching.",
  "2.11": "bhagavān uvāca -- Bhagavān said:\n\naśocyān anvaśocaḥ tvam prajñāvādān ca bhāṣase |\n\ngata asūn agata asūn ca na anuśocanti paṇḍitāḥ -- You are grieving for those who should not be grieved for, and speaking, as it occurs to you, words the wise would not speak. The wise grieve neither for the dead nor for the living.\n\nThis is the first word of the Lord's teaching, and it stands as the seed-almanac (pañcāṅga) for the whole Gītā. Here Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna: 'You are grieving for those who should not be grieved for, and speaking as though you were a great sage of deep learning.' This is the mirror Kṛṣṇa holds up to Arjuna's weakness. The sense 'you understand nothing at all' is also plain here. What torments Arjuna is the fear that some — his teachers and elders — will die, while others — the wicked — will go on living; and from that fear comes grief. The root of grief is fear; the root of fear is ignorance.\n\nA question may arise here: is it wrong to weep if great souls like Bhīṣma and Droṇa die? To answer this we must know the secret of birth and death. When we understand the other dimension of birth and death, this question finds its answer. The jīva is beginningless and eternal; it never dies. This physical body is like a cage for the jīva. Getting caught in this cage is birth. Going out of the cage is death. Seen from this dimension, putting on a new shirt is birth, discarding the old shirt is death. If we have this knowledge, we have no fear of death.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa says: 'You are not speaking the words the wise speak' — because the wise have thoroughly grasped the secret of birth and death. What you are speaking is not the speech of true understanding. Kṛṣṇa says you are speaking out of the delusion that your own understanding is the truth of the universe.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa sets aside all the worldly questions Arjuna has raised and leads him instead toward the direct realization of truth. What Arjuna has seen is only the worldly, material realm; Kṛṣṇa is introducing him to another face of the truth that lies beyond it.\n\nDeath is not something terrible — for one who is born, death is inevitable. The self is never destroyed; the self is beginningless and eternal. But the destruction of the body is inevitable. Death is the process by which a person journeys from their modified state (vikṛti) back toward nature (prakṛti). Kṛṣṇa is here making Arjuna understand that the wise grieve neither for those who have perished nor for those who have not.",
  "2.12": "na tu eva aham jātu na asam na tvam na ime jana adhipāḥ |\n\nna ca eva na bhaviṣyāmaḥ sarve vayam ataḥ param -- There never was a time when I did not exist, nor you, nor these kings; nor shall there ever come a time hereafter when we all shall cease to exist.\n\nKṛṣṇa here speaks in support of, and to justify, his earlier statement that we need not grieve over death. He says: 'I, you, and all these kings — we existed before, we exist now, and we shall exist hereafter too. When the body perishes, the jīva does not perish. Just as I am beginningless and eternal, so too is every single jīva beginningless and eternal. It is impossible to kill the self.' The expanse of the infinite jīvas continues unbroken through infinite time. The self is forever unaging and undying. Every jīva has its own distinct nature. The jīva is like a seed; Bhagavān is like a gardener. He created this world as a garden, sowed this jīva in that garden, and gave it an existence. Each jīva grows in this world according to its own nature. The nature of one jīva differs from the nature of another. It is for this reason that this world is so full of diversity.",
  "2.13": "dehinaḥ asmin yathā dehe kaumāram yauvanam jarā |\n\ntathā dehāntara prāpti dhīraḥ tatra na muhyati -- For the embodied self, just as childhood, youth, and old age happen within this same body, so too does the change of body. The one who is dhīra is not bewildered by this.\n\nFor the jīva dwelling in the body, just as childhood, youth, and old age happen within this same body, so too does the change of body. In such a circumstance, one who is dhīra — composed, steady — does not lose himself.\n\nThe destruction of the body is not the destruction of one's own true nature. The jīva dwelling in this body undergoes a change of body at every moment. The body of childhood is not present in youth; the body of youth is not present in old age. After death, a new body comes again with rebirth. But we have no awareness at all of where we have come from and where we are going — what we know is only our present existence. The body may perish and a new body may come; but the consciousness that never perishes is this 'jīva.' Kṛṣṇa says that one who is dhīra never grieves over this body which is bound, one day, to perish. Here dhīra means the knower (jñāni) who experiences the bliss of awareness (dhī + raḥ). Where there is knowledge, there is no grief.",
  "2.14": "mātrā sparśāḥ tu kaunteya śīta uṣṇa sukhaduḥkhadāḥ |\n\nāgama apāyinaḥ anityāḥ tān titikṣasva bhārata -- O Kaunteya, the contacts of the senses with their objects give rise to the experience of cold and heat, of pleasure and pain. They come and go; they are not permanent. O Bhārata, endure them.\n\nWe lose the body of childhood and gain the body of youth. We lose the body of youth and gain the body of old age. For this reason no one grieves. But in death, the other body that we 'see' [the body of one we love] ceases to be — and then comes grief. The grief that generally afflicts us is the grief that the bond of affection we have built through some body is now going to be no more.\n\nThe root of the pleasure and pain we experience in life is 'mātrā-sparśa' — sense-contact. This is the root of everything. Whatever we grasp through our senses is a mātrā. Sound, touch, form, taste, and smell — these are the five mātrās that are the cause of our pleasures and pains.\n\nThere is no fixed rule for pleasure and pain. What is pleasurable for one may be painful for another. Pleasure and pain arise from contact with objects — it is like heat and cold. What first gave pleasure may later turn into pain. Pleasure and pain are not the nature (dharma) of the jīva. They are things that come and go. They are the product of our own mental construction. The notion of 'mine' is the root cause of grief. In sleep, we have no notion of 'mine' — therefore there is never grief in sleep. This trouble exists only in waking and in dream. In sleep, everything is untroubled.\n\nWe ourselves construct this pleasure and pain out of our own mental feelings and experiences. We ourselves must learn the way out of this. When we search out the root of grief, grief becomes bearable. We must learn to bear our own feelings. We must learn to accept whatever has come. The thinking 'I must have exactly that,' 'it must happen exactly this way' is the root cause of grief. If we have the inner resolve to face whatever comes as it comes, there is no grief.",
  "2.15": "yam hi na vyathayanti ete puruṣam puruṣa ṛṣabha |\n\nsama duḥkha sukham dhīram saḥ amṛtatvāya kalpate -- O foremost among men, the one whom these [sense-contacts] cannot torment — who sees pleasure and pain as equal — is dhīra. Such a one becomes fit to attain the abode free of death.\n\nKṛṣṇa continues: 'The man whom mātrā-sparśa does not torment is the dhīra who can see pleasure and pain as equal.' The word 'puruṣa' occurs here. We hold the superstitious belief that puruṣa means a male, and that only males are fit for sādhana and mokṣa. But puruṣa means 'the jīva dwelling in the pura.' Pura means the complete body — that is, the human body. So puruṣa means a human being, or a striving jīva (sādhaka jīva). Whoever, even while dwelling in the gross body, does not see the pleasure and pain arising from sense-contact as different from one another is called dhīra — one who sees pleasure and pain as equal.\n\nPuruṣa has another meaning too: 'one who has known God, greater than all else.' When we have the knowledge of why pleasure and pain come to us, we neither swell with joy when pleasure comes nor shrink when pain comes. Every pleasure and pain that comes into our life has a cause. Pleasure and pain are waves that arise in life. To choose 'I want this one, not that one' is not right. We must live saying, 'let whatever has come, be.' Pleasure and pain are not given to us by another out of malice — they are the fruit of our own karma. It is Bhagavān who gives us pleasure and pain. Just as a mother punishes her child for the child's own uplift, so does Bhagavān give us grief for our own uplift. This is not punishment — it is education. The experience a person gains growing through grief can never be gained through pleasure. Grief is life's true experience. Behind the grief he gives us lies Bhagavān's great compassion. Only with this knowledge does the state of mind come to us in which we see pleasure and pain as one and the same. One who has this knowledge becomes dhīra. Courage comes from understanding. Whoever gains, in a single birth, the understanding and courage to see pleasure and pain as equal never again returns to be born within the bonds of this saṃsāra. Through such mental equilibrium, they come to know Bhagavān and attain mokṣa.",
  "2.16": "na asataḥ vidyate (a)bhāvaḥ na abhāvaḥ vidyate sataḥ |\n\nubhayoḥ api dṛṣṭaḥ antaḥ tu anayoḥ tattva darśibhiḥ -- Neither does prakṛti perish, nor does Bhagavān perish. [Doing evil does not produce good; doing good does not produce evil.] Those who have seen the truth know the settled conclusion regarding both these.\n\nIt is possible to stand above pleasure and pain — that is a state of mind. But Arjuna's question is: is it not a sin to kill one's own teachers and elders? The answer to this question is here. 'From sat there is no non-being, in asat there is no being' means: doing a good deed never brings grief, and doing a bad deed never brings joy. Sometimes those who do bad deeds may appear to be enjoying happiness, but that is only temporary. One who treads the path of adharma can never find true success. If we analyze the Mahābhārata war, it is not the Pāṇḍavas who are the cause of the war. The Pāṇḍavas are readying themselves for war out of the responsibility of protecting dharma. Here Kṛṣṇa clearly says: 'When doing a good deed, fear no one, and there is no grief from it.' On the path of protecting dharma, there is no room for thinking of some as 'my own people.' This is the final truth arrived at by the wise who have realized the truth of the world.\n\nIt is said that every śloka of the Mahābhārata carries ten meanings. If we look at the above śloka from another dimension — 'na asataḥ abhāvaḥ sataḥ abhāvaḥ na vidyati' — it means: neither sat nor asat has non-being. Asat here means the subtle root-nature (mūla-prakṛti) that is the cause of the gross world. Prakṛti is beginningless and eternal. Bhagavān (puruṣa), who created this prakṛti, is beginningless and eternal. The jīva, which is subject to prakṛti and puruṣa, is also beginningless and eternal. The jīva existed even before this gross body came to it, and will continue to exist hereafter as well. In this body made of the five elements, the jīva is only a guest. This being so, identifying oneself with the body is improper.",
  "2.17": "avināśi tu tat viddhi yena sarvam idam tatam |\n\nvināśam avyayasya asya na kaścit kartum arhati -- Know that which pervades all this to be imperishable. No one whatsoever has the power to bring about the destruction of that changeless one.\n\nThat which pervades everywhere cannot be destroyed. Just as there is a world of form, there is also a world of names — and that too is eternal. Here 'made of names' means akṣara — the fifty-one letters running from 'a' to 'kṣa.' These fifty-one letters constitute the entire word-world (śabda-prapañca) that underlies everything. This is a principle that cannot be destroyed. Therefore the word [the Veda] that establishes Bhagavān, prakṛti, the jīva, and all these principles is beginningless and eternal. No one can destroy it. Bhagavān, who is the very embodiment of the Veda within this world of names, is imperishable. He is not subject to any kind of destruction — not the destruction of his own nature, not the destruction of a body, not the attainment of grief, not incompleteness. He is the infinite reality pervading in terms of time, place, and quality. He is avyaya — undergoing no modification or change — a beginningless, eternal reality.",
  "2.18": "antavanta ime dehāḥ nityasya uktāḥ śarīriṇaḥ |\n\nanāśinaḥ aprameyasya tasmāt yudhyasva bhārata -- These bodies are said to have an end, belonging to the eternal, imperishable, immeasurable embodied self. Therefore, O Bhārata, fight.\n\nThe physical body is antavanta — subject to destruction. Only the gross body, visible to the eye, is destroyed — not the self. Because the jīva is itself the very imprint of Bhagavān. It is the reflection of the all-pervading Bhagavān. Therefore this jīva, which is the reflection of Bhagavān present everywhere, is eternal. Here Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna: 'Fight not out of the selfish wish to become emperor, nor out of hatred for Duryodhana, but fight as though — the fight against injustice is itself the worship of Bhagavān.'\n\nEvery action we perform in our daily life is like this too. It should take the form of an offering to Bhagavān. It is Bhagavān who has given us this body. Therefore there is no need to grieve when that body departs. When we perform every one of our duties as the worship of Bhagavān, and live without fear of death, this life becomes worthwhile.",
  "2.19": "yaḥ enam vetti hantāram yaḥ ca enam manyate hatam |\n\nubhau tau na vijānītaḥ na ayam hanti na hanyate -- Whoever thinks of this [self] as 'the slayer,' and whoever thinks of this as 'the slain' — both of them do not truly know. This [self] neither slays, nor is it slain.\n\nThose who imagine that they can kill, or are killing, the jīva, and those who say it was destroyed or killed, have no true understanding. Because the jīva neither kills nor is killed. It is simply not possible to kill the ātman. What appears to us as dying, or as having died, is only the physical body. The jīva has no freedom to kill or to die even through the medium of the body. Here, Arjuna's thought that 'the Kauravas will die by my hand' is mere delusion. Nothing at all happens without the will of Bhagavān, the doer of creation and destruction. We cannot do anything against his will.",
  "2.20": "na jāyate mriyate vā kadācit na ayam bhūtvā bhavitā vā na bhūyaḥ |\n\najaḥ nityaḥ śāśvataḥ ayam purāṇaḥ na hanyate hanyamāne śarīre -- This [self] is never born, nor does it ever die; nor, having come into being, does it ever cease to be again. Unborn, eternal, everlasting, and ancient, it is not slain when the body is slain.\n\nThis jīva is never born; nor does it die. It is not something that lies hidden according to Bhagavān's own awareness and then becomes something else. This one is unborn, undying, unchanging — the very imprint of Bhagavān. [The Supreme Puruṣa, too, is never born and never dies. Nor is it right to say that, existing in his own true form, he is born through a body. This jīva too is a principle that is unborn, undying, unchanging.] This jīva, which wanders from body to body, does not itself perish even when the body perishes.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa has explained what the true nature of the jīva (jīva-svarūpa) means. By its very nature, this jīva is never born and is never destroyed. It always remains in one and the same form, because the jīva-svarūpa is the reflection of Bhagavān. Bhagavān is unborn. He is eternal and everlasting. The jīva is always wandering from body to body (pura-aṇa). But as the body changes, the jīva-svarūpa does not change. Just as the physical body grows old, the jīva-svarūpa does not grow old. It always remains in one and the same form. But the beliefs that arise from external influence can change — for example, one who is an atheist in one birth may become a complete theist in another. The jīva, distinguished by its own nature, remains forever unchanging and undestroyed.",
  "2.21": "veda avināśinam nityam yaḥ enam ajam avyayam |\n\nkatham saḥ puruṣaḥ pārtha kam ghātayati hanti kam -- O Pārtha, the one who knows this [self] — unborn, undying, changeless, imperishable by anything — how, and whom, does such a person cause to be killed? Whom does he kill?\n\nOne who knows the true nature of the jīva — faultless, eternal, never-changing — how can such a person kill anyone, or cause anyone to be killed? Once we know that the jīva is, by its own nature, indestructible, and that birth and death in the gross world proceed according to Bhagavān's will, who is there left to kill, or to be killed by whom? We need only recognize the divine power standing behind birth and death.\n\nThe jīva is imperishable; birth and death do not apply to it. If so, how are we to understand the birth and death we witness in the world? Kṛṣṇa answers this question in the next verse.",
  "2.22": "vāsāṃsi jīrṇāni yathā vihāya navāni gṛhṇāti naraḥ aparāṇi |\n\ntathā śarīrāṇi vihāya jīrṇāni anyāni saṃyāti navāni dehī -- Just as a person casts off worn-out garments and takes other, new ones, so too the embodied self casts off worn-out bodies and goes on to other, new ones.\n\nJust as we change our clothes when they become old, so too the jīva leaves one body and takes another. There is one special point here: just as a mother changes her child's clothes according to her own wish, so Bhagavān changes the jīva's body. The jīva itself has no freedom to change its own body. An exception to this are yogis who have realized the self; by Bhagavān's grace, they gain the power to change bodies at will. [Many genuine accounts of this can be found in Swami Rama's book Living with Himalayan Masters.] Apart from such great yogis, no one else has the freedom to change bodies at will. Therefore, physically, birth and death are like taking off worn clothes and putting on new ones. By its own nature the jīva never truly undergoes birth or death, and its fundamental nature does not change with a new body.\n\nIs it possible, then, to destroy the ātman at all? Never, says Kṛṣṇa in the next verse.",
  "2.23": "na enam chindanti śastrāṇi na enam dahati pāvakaḥ |\n\nna ca enam kledayanti apaḥ na śoṣayati mārutaḥ -- Weapons do not cut this [self]; fire does not burn it; water does not wet it; wind does not dry it.\n\nThere are four principal means for destroying any object: earth [as a solid weapon], water, fire, and wind. The jīva is the subtlest of the subtle. As the śāstra-writers say, if a single hair from a horse's tail were divided at its very tip into ten thousand parts, the jīva would be the size of one such part. No weapon can cut such a jīva. Fire cannot burn the jīva. Water cannot make the jīva wet. Wind cannot dry the jīva. No weapon at all can destroy a jīva that is subtler than the subtlest.\n\nHere another question arises for us: is this jīva, which cannot be destroyed today, one that will never be destroyed at all? Yes, says Kṛṣṇa in the next verse.",
  "2.24": "acchedyaḥ ayam adāhyaḥ ayam akledyaḥ aśoṣyaḥ eva ca |\n\nnityaḥ sarvagataḥ sthāṇu acalaḥ ayam sanātanaḥ -- This [self] cannot be cut, cannot be burned, cannot be wetted, and indeed cannot be dried. Eternal, all-pervading, immovable, unshaken, and everlasting is this one.\n\nThis jīva cannot be cut. It cannot be burned. It cannot be wetted; nor can it be dried. This one is the imprint of the ancient Puruṣa — everlastingly pervading everywhere, never changed by anything, unshaken. [The ever-atom-formed one who forever holds the all-pervading Bhagavān within. This one is bound to an unalterable order — even to the injunctions and prohibitions of the Veda.]\n\nThe jīva cannot be destroyed by anyone, now or ever hereafter. Because by its very nature it is the imprint of Bhagavān — unchanging, all-pervading, unshaken. The jīva has the nature of remaining, at all times, in one and the same form, untouched by any weapon, ever fixed, unchanging everywhere. The jīva is sanātana — that is, possessed of nātana. Here 'nātana' means 'nādana,' that is, veda-nāda. Being possessed of veda-nāda, the jīva remains, within the embrace of Bhagavān who is expounded by the entire Veda, bound to the Veda's injunctions and prohibitions. It is the great atom that takes refuge in the all-pervading Bhagavān.",
  "2.25": "avyaktaḥ ayam acintyaḥ ayam avikāryaḥ ayam ucyate\n\ntasmāt evam viditvā enam na anuśocitum arhasi -- Bhagavān is imperceptible to the eye; he is beyond the reach of thought; he is unchanging — so the Veda proclaims. Therefore, having thus known his [and the jīva's] nature, it is not fitting to grieve.\n\nBhagavān is avyakta — imperceptible. The jīva, his reflection, remains avyakta even while dwelling in the physical body. For this reason Bhagavān and the jīva are beyond the reach of our thought. Who am I? Where have I come from? What am I? — we do not even know this. Even though the true nature of Bhagavān and of the jīva never changes, it does not fall within the grasp of our thinking. Once we have understood this hard truth, there is no need to grieve over the thought that the jīva is being destroyed.",
  "2.26": "atha ca enam nityajātam nityam vā manyase mṛtam |\n\ntadā api tvam mahābāho na enam śocitum arhasi -- Or, if you think of this [self] as being perpetually born, or perpetually dying, through the body — even then, O mighty-armed one, it is not fitting for you to grieve over it.\n\nSetting Vedānta aside and considering the matter from the world's ordinary viewpoint: the ātman becomes manifest through the body, and becomes unmanifest through death. It is never possible to stand entirely above birth and death. Therefore there is no need to grieve over death. 'You, so mighty, have fought how many wars before now? Why is there a problem now that was not there then? What you are doing is nothing but your own duty' — this is what Kṛṣṇa explains and tells Arjuna.",
  "2.27": "jātasya hi dhruvaḥ mṛtyuḥ dhruvam janma mṛtasya ca |\n\ntasmāt aparihārye arthe na tvam śocitum arhasi -- For one who is born, death is certain; and for one who has died, rebirth too is certain. This being so, it is not fitting for you to grieve over a matter that cannot be avoided.\n\nBirth and death, pleasure and pain, are like the two faces of a single coin. There is no day without night. Once born, death is a parcel already tied and waiting. We must learn to accommodate what is unavoidable in life. Apart from that, it is not fitting to grieve when hardship comes. In the discharge of an unavoidable duty, we should never feel grief.",
  "2.28": "avyakta ādīni bhūtāni vyakta madhyāni bhārata |\n\navyakta nidhanāni eva tatra kā paridevanā -- O scion of Bharata, the root at the beginning of beings' existence is unseen; only the middle is visible; the end, too, is unseen. This being so, why lament?\n\nUp to this point we have seen Kṛṣṇa's words analyze, in many ways, the jīva, the jīva's existence, and birth and death. But the problem that has fundamentally troubled Arjuna is the pull of relationship — 'my teacher, my grandfather.' His problem is how to fight, with detachment, against such great and wise elders. Here Kṛṣṇa answers his problem.\n\nA person becomes related to us only after their birth. But what was that person before birth? Where do they go after death? Before and after this birth and death, we have no relation whatsoever to that jīva. This relationship is a single point on the continuous line of the jīva's ongoing existence. Only that point is manifest to us. The rest is unmanifest. Therefore it is not right to stand grieving at a single point on that line.\n\nThis does not mean we should not love one another while we are alive. Love while they are present — but when they are gone, train your mind to remain at peace, telling yourself that Bhagavān has called them back. If we do not learn to live this way, life becomes unbearable. Be joyful while they are present; do not grieve when they are gone — this is Kṛṣṇa's message.",
  "2.29": "āścaryavat paśyati kaścit enam āścaryavat vadati tathā eva ca anyaḥ |\n\nāścaryavat ca enam anyaḥ śṛṇoti śrutvā api enam veda na ca eva kaścit -- Some see this [self] as a wonder; another likewise speaks of it as a wonder; yet another hears of it as a wonder; and even having heard of it, no one truly knows it at all.\n\nIf anyone comes to realize this truth of the jīva, or of Bhagavān, it is like a wonder. If another points it out — that too is like a wonder. If yet another hears of it and understands — that too is like a wonder. [Someone looks upon Bhagavān in wonder. Someone speaks of him in wonder. Someone hears of him in wonder.] However much one hears, no one has truly and fully grasped either the jīva or Bhagavān.\n\nThis is the verse about the jīva and Bhagavān. Those who have seen Bhagavān are rare. If, through very great striving, one comes to see him, the exclamation of the one who has seen is nothing but wonder — sheer astonishment. This universe itself is a marvel; if so, how much more marvellous must be the Bhagavān who fashioned this universe? Therefore those who have seen him cannot describe him. And even if they do describe him, it is a wonder to the one who hears it. However much anyone hears, in the end Bhagavān remains simply a wonder — because it is impossible to fully know Bhagavān.\n\nThe jīva is the same way. When self-realization comes to us, what we experience is nothing but astonishment. It is impossible to describe it. The jīva is a mass of light — a great wonder. It is a light more brilliant than the sun, and cooler than the moon. Having heard about the truth of the jīva, it is impossible to arrive at any final, settled conclusion about it.",
  "2.30": "dehī nityam avadhyaḥ ayam dehe sarvasya bhārata |\n\ntasmāt sarvāṇi bhūtāni na tvam śocitum arhasi -- O Bhārata, this embodied self, present in the body of every being, can never be slain. [It is never possible to kill this jīva, because Bhagavān stands guard over the jīva in every single body.] Therefore it is not fitting for you to grieve for any of these beings.\n\nFundamentally, it is not possible to kill the jīva present in anyone's body. It is an ordained rule that the body must die, and there is no need to grieve over that. It is not possible for the jīva to give up the body by its own will, nor is it possible for it not to give it up. 'Ayam dehe sarvasya bhārata' — 'You cannot be held responsible for birth and death while the protecting Bhagavān sits within. Born in the house of Bharata, know this truth,' says Kṛṣṇa. When we come to know this spark of light that is the jīva, when its awareness dawns, the secret of birth and death becomes clear. Then death becomes an experience of bliss.\n\nHaving heard this whole analysis of Kṛṣṇa's, an opinion might arise in us: 'Killing is not wrong, because the jīva does not die. Everything is God's will; anyone may kill anyone at all.' But that is not right. Because what we have seen up to now is only the inward view of existence. We, being social beings, using this inner awareness against society, would be adharma. Everyone must observe the social dharma that accords with the śāstra. In the next verse, Kṛṣṇa makes this point clear to us through Arjuna.",
  "2.31": "svadharmam api ca avekṣya na vikampitum arhasi |\n\ndharmyāt hi yuddhāt śreyaḥ anyat kṣatriyasya na vidyate -- Even considering your own duty, you should not waver. For a kṣatriya, there is no greater good than a righteous war that has come unsought.\n\nWhat is Arjuna's sanātana-dharma here? What is the social dharma (samāja-dharma)? And what is his own duty (sva-dharma)? Sanātana-dharma says: one must fight against injustice. Samāja-dharma says: a kṣatriya must fight against adharma, must protect society, and in this meritorious task must not look upon anyone as 'his own.' Since Arjuna is by birth a kṣatriya, his sva-dharma is 'to fight against injustice.' Here individual dharma, social dharma, and sanātana-dharma are one and the same. Therefore fighting against injustice is a work of great merit. Fighting for the safety of society is the kṣatriya's great austerity. There is no more meritorious task than this.",
  "2.32": "yadṛcchayā ca upapannam svarga dvāram apāvṛtam |\n\nsukhinaḥ kṣatriyāḥ pārtha labhante yuddham īdṛśam -- A door to heaven, opened wide, arriving of its own accord without any effort — fortunate kṣatriyas obtain such a war, O Pārtha.\n\nIt is wrong to seek out a war by picking a quarrel. But here the responsibility for a war that has come collectively is not the Pāṇḍavas'. This war, which has of itself come and fallen upon them, is the will of God. When everything happens according to the Lord's will, whatever comes must be accepted as it is. For one who fights a righteous war against injustice, the door of heaven is always open. For such an opportunity for war to come to a kṣatriya, he must have great merit behind him. At such a time, for a kṣatriya to shut his eyes and sit still is a great sin.\n\nHere it becomes clear to us why Kṛṣṇa is telling Arjuna to fight. Every human being must discharge their duty on the foundation of sanātana-dharma, the samāja-dharma that accords with it, and the sva-dharma that accords with both society and sanātana-dharma. No act performed on this foundation can become a sinful act. Kṛṣṇa gives fuller detail on this in the next verse. What sanātana-dharma, samāja-dharma, and sva-dharma mean has already been analyzed in detail in Chapter One, verse 40.",
  "2.33": "atha cet tvam dharmyam imam saṅgrāmam na kariṣyasi |\n\ntataḥ svadharmam kīrtim ca hitvā pāpam avāpsyasi -- But if you do not undertake this righteous battle that has come to you rightfully, then, having forfeited your own duty and your good name, you will incur sin.\n\nKṛṣṇa continues to tell Arjuna: 'You say you do not want war, out of fear of sin. But dharma survives only if the battle is fought, and the door of heaven opens for you. If not, the door of hell will welcome you.'\n\nWhat is unfolding here is a righteous war. Arjuna is a great warrior who stands on the side of dharma. If he does not fight for dharma in such a righteous war, it is as though he has acted against his own natural dharma and abandoned his social dharma. This becomes a stain and a great sin. Kṛṣṇa is here making clear to Arjuna, who is suffering from this fear of sin, what true sin actually is.\n\nContext determines what a sinful act is. For example, one cannot say 'speaking the truth is always a meritorious act.' Whether it is sin or merit is determined by the context. If a person seeking shelter from robbers is hidden, and when the robbers come and ask, speaking the truth becomes a sinful act; telling a lie to protect the hidden person becomes a meritorious act. In the same way, Kṛṣṇa is making Arjuna understand that, for the protection of dharma, standing and fighting against his teachers and grand-elders is itself the one great meritorious act.",
  "2.34": "akīrtim ca api bhūtāni kathayiṣyanti te avyayām |\n\nsambhāvitasya ca akīrtiḥ maraṇāt atiricyate -- People will forever speak the tale of your undying infamy. For a man of honor, disgrace is worse than death.\n\nDescribing the social consequence, Kṛṣṇa says: if a kṣatriya's reputation is destroyed, he suffers humiliation, and through that he may lose his very existence. If Arjuna, who has attained the world-renowned fame of a great archer, does not take part in this righteous war, people will speak of him as a coward. His cowardice will stand in the pages of history as a bad example for future generations. No one will say, 'he withdrew from the battle out of affection for his kinsmen.' They will crudely abuse him as a coward who fled the battle out of fear. For a man of honor, disgrace is worse than death. A kṣatriya cannot bear this.",
  "2.35": "bhayāt raṇāt uparatam maṃsyante tvām mahārathāḥ |\n\nyeṣām ca tvam bahumataḥ bhūtvā yāsyasi lāghavam -- Those great charioteers, in whose eyes you were once a person of the highest regard, will think you have withdrawn from battle out of fear; before them you will become a lightweight.",
  "2.36": "avācya vādān ca bahūn vadiṣyanti tava ahitāḥ |\n\nnindantaḥ tava sāmarthyam tataḥ duḥkhataram nu kim -- Your enemies will speak many unspeakable words, deriding your prowess. What could be more painful than that?\n\n'Those before whom you once stood as a great hero, hands folded and heads bowed, will now look upon you as lower than grass. You, who are an exalted man in society, will have to live in disgrace. Unspeakable, indecent words will arise about you. Because of this, all your past achievements too will be dismissed and mocked as false.'\n\nIf a person who has earned the name of an excellent judge gives a bad verdict for the sake of a close relative in some case, he loses his entire standing as a person. All his past achievements crumble to dust in a single moment. Those he had once punished as criminals will abuse him without shame. Here Śrī Kṛṣṇa is giving Arjuna this social awareness.",
  "2.37": "hataḥ vā prāpsyasi svargam jitvā vā bhokṣyase mahīm |\n\ntasmāt uttiṣṭha kaunteya yuddhāya kṛta niścayaḥ -- Slain, you will attain heaven; victorious, you will enjoy the earth. Therefore, O son of Kuntī, rise up, resolved to fight.\n\n'We do not know that we will win by fighting. In righteous war, whether one dies or wins, both lead to mokṣa. This is not a war being fought for some selfish end. Rise up and fight with the resolve that you will fight against injustice,' says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nWhat matters in any action we undertake is the awareness of dharma. One who follows the path of dharma without any selfish motive need never fear anything. If you walk the path of dharma, there is no need to fear what will happen to you, because whatever the outcome of walking on the path of dharma, it becomes the means that carries us to mokṣa.",
  "2.38": "sukha duḥkhe same kṛtvā lābha alābhau jaya ajayau |\n\ntataḥ yuddhāya yujyasva na evam pāpam avāpsyasi -- Having made pleasure and pain, gain and loss, victory and defeat all equal, engage yourself then in battle; thus you will not incur sin.\n\nPleasure and pain are unavoidable in life. No sin touches one who sees pleasure and pain, gain and loss, victory and defeat all as the same. To see everything with an equal eye, and to fight purely out of a sense of duty, is the formula for success in life. Fight without the selfish wish 'I must win,' without the hatred that wishes the other side to be crushed into the dust — fight with the attitude that everything is Bhagavān's grace,' says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nIf a person incorporates this teaching of the Lord into their life, they can never become miserable. 'Let whatever comes, come — let there only be Govinda's grace' — for one who advances on the path of dharma with this spirit, the door of heaven is always open. To him grief will not appear as grief, nor defeat as defeat. For him both this world and the next are heaven.\n\nUp to this point Kṛṣṇa has given Arjuna a great deal of explanation about the truth of the self. What is life? What is death? What is the inner secret of the self and the Supreme Self? He has explained how these are, without any change whatsoever, beginningless and eternal, and told him of the need for war. From here on, Kṛṣṇa turns from Sāṅkhya toward the side of karma.\n\nHowever much we cultivate Vedānta in our minds, the Gītā makes it clear that the grief of a person in the moment can be irrepressible. We see in the Mahābhārata that Arjuna, who received the teaching of the Gītā directly from Bhagavān, is again shaken when his son Abhimanyu dies in battle. Attaining full self-realization is not so easy a thing. It is not enough merely to know that the self is unaging and undying — one must know it fully. In such a state, all grief can be forgotten. But how is this truth of the self to be known? What is the means for gaining knowledge of the self? This is not a matter that becomes firmly fixed in the mind the instant it is heard.\n\nSo Kṛṣṇa next explains how this exalted mental state may be reached.",
  "2.39": "eṣā te abhihitā sāṅkhye buddhiḥ yoge tu imām śṛṇu |\n\nbuddhvā yuktaḥ yayā pārtha karma bandham prahāsyasi -- This understanding has been declared to you in terms of Sāṅkhya; now hear this in terms of yoga, understanding which, O Pārtha, joined to it, you shall cast off the bondage of karma.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: 'Up to now I have spoken of Sāṅkhya. Now hear what I say about yoga.' The Sāṅkhya Kṛṣṇa speaks of here is Vaidika Sāṅkhya. It was Kapila Vāsudeva who brought Vaidika Sāṅkhya into circulation. Saṅkhyā means samyak-khyāti, correct awareness. This is the fundamental principle that gives awareness of the self. For this reason the ancients called this knowledge Sāṅkhya. Here Sāṅkhya means the certain, settled knowledge of the self.\n\nThe yoga spoken of here is not Patañjali's yoga, but Vaidika yoga. Ordinary yoga does not require firm faith in the existence of God. By continuous concentration of the mind on some object, many miracles can be performed. But this is not true self-realization. We have all heard the story of one who practiced yoga for ten years and learned to walk on water. Vaidika yoga forbids learning and displaying such miracles through yoga. However much inner power one gains, it must not be put on display. The instant one learns to walk on water, self-realization does not follow. Anyone at all can acquire such conjuring feats. What Kṛṣṇa is speaking of here is what discipline must be undertaken in life in order to attain the truth of the self.\n\n'I have told you of the understanding of Sāṅkhya; now, to progress on the path of knowledge and attain siddhi, hear the means — the yoga — that is needed,' says Kṛṣṇa. He goes on to explain what awareness must be gained in order to cross over the bondage of karma and advance on the path of sādhana. What spiritual sādhana means, what form our spiritual sādhana should take in our lives, and how it is to be accomplished — this is the principal matter we need to understand.\n\nMany people describe, in many ways, how one should be on the outside. But no one describes how one should be on the inside. Everyone speaks of matters such as: chant japa for this long, observe ritual purity in this manner, recite so many mantras, and so on. But most people do not know how the inner self should be. Without inner sādhana, nothing at all can be accomplished merely through outward appearance and japa-tapas. Yoga-sādhana is not about what clothes we wear, or what we apply to our body. It is our inner conduct. It is the state in which the inner and the outer are one and the same. Our conduct, our faith, our integrity — these are our true dharma. Such inner sādhana is of the utmost importance. Kṛṣṇa explains this next.\n\nKnowledge is indeed needed — but what should our practice be to attain self-knowledge, what is the sādhana for breaking free of this bondage of saṃsāra — we will see this explained in full detail in the verses that follow.",
  "2.40": "na iha abhikrama nāśaḥ asti pratyavāyaḥ na vidyate |\n\nsu alpam api asya dharmasya trāyate mahataḥ bhayāt -- Here no effort is ever lost, nor is there any adverse result. Even a very little of this dharma protects one from great fear.\n\nBefore speaking of sādhana, Kṛṣṇa resolves, in this verse, a doubt that might occur to us. A question may trouble everyone: first birth, then study, sādhana, then death; again rebirth, again the same sādhana, again death! What use is all this? If one does not attain the realization of Brahman in a single birth, is all that sādhana in vain? Certainly not, says Kṛṣṇa. It is true that the worldly wealth we gain in our lifetime — money, jewelry, fame, property, and so on — does not come with us after death. But spiritual sādhana is not like that. Spiritual sādhana undertaken in one birth is never destroyed. If we climb one step of the spiritual ladder in this birth, we are already standing on the second step the moment we are born in the next. Our birth takes place in an environment that continues from wherever we had stopped in the previous birth. You will notice, socially, children born with extraordinary talent, achieving great things in a very short time. This is the knowledge they have brought forward from a previous birth. This present birth is simply the continuation of the spiritual sādhana of that previous birth. From wherever we stopped in that birth, the sādhana of this birth begins and continues onward.\n\nIf we have an inner yearning to undertake spiritual sādhana, and are unable to fulfil it in this birth, then in the next birth we are born into the kind of environment needed for sādhana, in the company of the learned. Therefore, in our spiritual reckoning, each of our births is like a single grade in school. To fully understand a single text we may need to undertake sādhana across many births. For this reason spiritual sādhana is never in vain. Whatever stage of your life you are at today, this is the right time for spiritual sādhana. Begin your sādhana today. That it will carry you toward the path of mokṣa is a hard truth. Spirituality is not an object for display; it is inner sādhana. Even if in our birth we undertake only a little of spiritual sādhana, it saves us from fear. Spirituality is the one and only means that carries us from fear to fearlessness.\n\nThere are three kinds of sādhaka: the excellent sādhaka, the middling sādhaka, and the lowest sādhaka. The excellent sādhaka believes God is one alone. Such devotees are called bhāgavatas. He has single-pointed devotion toward Bhagavān. He knows the truth that, by whatever name Bhagavān is called, it refers to that same primal, supreme power. This is the true sādhaka, and this is the true path of sādhana. Next, the middling sādhakas: these worship every deity in various ways and, at the end, offer it all up to Bhagavān saying 'Kṛṣṇārpaṇam astu.' They are called trai-vidhyas. Then the lowest sādhaka: he believes there are hundreds of gods and worships accordingly. He does not have the sense that Bhagavān is the indweller of all. He has no true conception of spirituality. Therefore this does not become true spiritual sādhana.\n\nThe sādhana we perform with single-pointed devotion is never in vain. There is no need for outward show here — what is needed is sincere, honest devotion. Whether you worship Gaṇapati, worship Vāyu, worship Sūrya, or call it Allah, or Yesu [Jesus], or Śiva, or Viṣṇu, or by whatever name you worship — once you know the truth that all these names refer to the same primal, supreme power, and hold to it with single-pointed devotion, with pure and sincere reflection, it becomes true spiritual sādhana, and it comes with us without being wasted. In such sādhana there is never hatred, selfishness, ego, or grief. The feeling that everything is Bhagavān's grace carries us to great heights.",
  "2.41": "vyavasāya ātmikā buddhiḥ ekā iha kurunandana |\n\nbahuśākhāḥ hi anantāḥ ca buddhayaḥ avyavasāyinām -- O son of the Kurus, on this path the resolve matured by reflection is of a single kind. For those without such resolve, their endless idle talk branches out into countless directions.\n\nWe have understood that the sādhana we perform with single-pointed devotion is never in vain — but how are we to know what is right and what is wrong? Kṛṣṇa answers our question in this verse.\n\nAt all times, the word of one who has reached a firm conclusion is uniform. Words spoken without any settled conclusion differ from one another. The words spoken out of ignorance, without real knowledge, branch out endlessly and without foundation. But the decisive word of those who truly know is always one and the same. Therefore, says Kṛṣṇa, walk according to the guidance of those who have realized the truth.",
  "2.42": "yām imām puṣṭitām vācam pravadanti avipaścitaḥ |\n\nvedavādaratāḥ pārtha na anyat asti iti vādinaḥ -- O Pārtha, those of half-baked understanding declare: this flowery speech of the Veda is a vine bursting into bloom, and saṃsāra — bound by birth and karma — is its fruit.",
  "2.43": "kāma ātmānaḥ svarga parāḥ janma karma phala pradām |\n\nkriyā viśeṣa bahulām bhoga aiśvarya gatim prati -- For the sake of a life of luxury and opulence, they engage in manifold varieties of ritual action. They remain fixed at the surface meaning of the Veda [raising disputes about the Veda, or merely reciting it by rote without knowing its meaning], holding that there is nothing beyond it — their minds filled with desire, dreaming only of heaven.",
  "2.44": "prasaktānām tayā apahṛta cetasām |\n\nvyavasāya ātmikā buddhiḥ samādhau na vidhīyate -- For those caught up in enjoyment, whose minds are carried away by such half-understood, ill-informed talk, even mature, well-considered understanding cannot bring them steadiness of mind.\n\nThese three verses above are extremely important. Most people fail to grasp them at all — some have even criticized this verse without understanding it. In the ancient texts, in the Vedas, in the śrutis, it appears as though many worldly matters are packed in alongside the purely spiritual. Kṛṣṇa says that reading the bare texts of the Veda alone cannot bring one to know Him. He says that those attached to the letter of the Veda pluck only the flower of its surface meaning, and are thereby cheated of the fruit of the deep ocean of knowledge. Kṛṣṇa speaks this to those who, without understanding the Veda deeply, remain fixed at its surface meaning and declare 'this is all the Veda is.' Here 'vedavādins' means those who dispute about the Veda without knowing its meaning — those who recite the Veda by rote without understanding it, those who remain fixed at its surface meaning. Such people swim only on the surface; they have no awareness of the pearls and gems lying at the bottom. To fail to truly understand the Veda, to form a mistaken notion about it, and to go on teaching that same mistaken notion to people, is a direct road to hell. The priestly office is the same. To officiate as a priest without true knowledge, without knowing the meaning of the mantras, is every bit as dangerous. Priesthood is a work of great responsibility. For a priest to remove a person's sin and lift him up, the priest himself must stand at a height — he must know the secret of the Vedic mantra. Kṛṣṇa says here: 'If the inner meaning of the Veda is not known, everything becomes a misreading. Those who state the surface meaning of the ancient texts and thereby deceive society are avipaścit.' Vipaścit means those who have seen the self, whose very form is knowledge — knowers of the self. Avipaścit are those who state only the surface meaning of the śāstra — Kṛṣṇa calls them the ill-informed! This same warning occurs in every religious scripture in the world. In the Bible this is called the hidden meaning. The Qur'an too carries a word of caution on this: 'In it are verses that are clear, and others are verses that are ambiguous.' This is one line found in the Qur'an. All ancient mystical literature carries this same warning. The ancient texts are like riddles. If one takes only their surface meaning, it becomes incoherent. In spiritual texts, this riddling, symbolic language is used to carry us toward transcendence. If we do not recognize that the Veda is a symbolic language, spiritual reflection turns to nonsense. Sādhana performed without this understanding is of no use whatsoever.\n\nWe find many stories in the ancient texts. Seen only on the surface they seem like entertainment; but if we grasp their inner kernel, life becomes worthwhile. A fine example of this is the story of Gaṇapati: 'Pārvatī fashioned Gaṇapati out of the clay of her own body; Śiva cut off his head; afterward they fitted onto him the head of an elephant that had its head turned toward the north; Gaṇeśa, having eaten the kaḍabu, wound a snake around his belly.' What does this mean? There is almost no one who has not heard this story in part — but not one in a thousand knows its meaning! Only if we analyze this story minutely do we come to know the significance of the story, the significance of the worship, the significance of the faith behind it. Gaṇapati is the deity of ākāśa (space). Ākāśa is the greatest among the five elements. Gaṇapati's rank is the twenty-first — for this reason twenty-one modakas are offered to him. Five inner faculties, five organs of knowledge, five organs of action, five subtle elements (sound, touch, form, taste, smell) — beyond these lie ākāśa, air, fire, water, and earth. Here ākāśa stands at the twenty-first step; it is the twenty-first principle. There are twenty deities presiding over the tiers before Gaṇapati: Brahmā, Vāyu, Garuḍa, Śeṣa, Rudra, Indra, Kāma, Ahampraṇa, Aniruddha, Svāyambhuva-manu, Bṛhaspati, Dakṣa-prajāpati, Pravaha-vāyu, Candra, Sūrya, Yamadharma, Varuṇa, Vahni, Mitra, and Nirṛti. Gaṇapati is the twenty-first.\n\nThen there are Śiva and Pārvatī — husband and wife. Here Śiva presides over the mind, and Pārvatī is the deity of speech. Speech follows as the mind thinks; for this reason theirs is a bond of perfect harmony. The root power of sound is air and space; without space and air there is no sound. The inner resonance of our speech is Pārvatī herself — that which pours out in the form of sound is speech, the gross form of the original resonance. Speech is a person's greatest hazard. What spiritual sādhana requires, more than speech, is silence. Without awareness of one's own self, when speech grows against the principle of ego, there is no knowledge without the beheading of speech. And once beheaded, that same speech must never again continue as before. For this reason a new form was born, called Gaṇapati, bearing the head of an elephant — symbolizing large ears that hear everything, and a long trunk that governs the life-force. The moment we see Gaṇapati, what should come to mind is: reflect more than you speak; be a good listener.\n\nNow, what does it mean to place the head toward the north? As we generally know, one should not sleep with the head to the north; one should not sit facing south for worship; one should not circumambulate a temple in the reverse direction, and so on. But why all these rules? What is the theory behind them? On this earth, the power of the north pole flows from north to east, and the power of the south pole flows from south to west. Our own movement, too, must accord with this flow of power. If we face south, we expose ourselves to the flow of the south pole's power; if we face north, to the flow of the north pole's power. To sleep with the head to the north means, on rising, to face south. Therefore facing south and having the head to the north are the same thing. There is one major difference between these two flows of power: the flow from the north is upward-moving, while the flow from the south draws one into the whirlpool of saṃsāra. For this reason, sleeping with the head to the north, or sitting facing south for worship, brings about our own beheading — we would have to circle again in this whirlpool of saṃsāra.\n\nThen, the snake wound around his belly. The snake is the symbol of the kuṇḍalinī power. Coiled in a circle, head down, it lies between our organs of excretion. For the kuṇḍalinī to awaken means for this snake to raise its hood and stand upright. When the kuṇḍalinī awakens, the power moves upward through the mūlādhāra, maṇipūra, and svādhiṣṭhāna energy centers. The energy center above our navel is the door of knowledge; Gaṇapati is the power-deity who governs these three energy centers and opens for us the door of knowledge. So much lies packed within this small story. This is the way from flower to fruit. There is no harm in remaining at the surface meaning; the real harm is in believing there is nothing beyond it.\n\nWe must not look for what is already in our own head within the Veda; rather, we must fill our head with what is in the Veda. When we see the Veda only at its surface, we remain caught only in birth-and-karma, birth-and-death, this same whirlpool, and never see the path to immortality. Those who see the Veda only at its surface stand, from morning to evening, immersed in enjoyment, wealth, and ritual activity, forgetting self-realization altogether. This is a great tragedy.\n\nNow a question troubles us: why were the ancient spiritual texts written in this riddling form? Why were they not written stage by stage, like a textbook for each grade, in an easily understandable way? The main thing we must understand here is that fitness (yogyatā) is required to understand these texts. The book is one, but within that single book many stages of instruction lie hidden. These Vedic texts never yield themselves to the unfit. They open themselves only before one who can know himself and put that knowledge to good use. The fit person discovers more and more, the deeper he descends into this text. To fully know a single text may require sādhana across many births.",
  "2.45": "traiguṇya viṣayāḥ vedāḥ nistraiguṇyaḥ bhava arjuna |\n\nnirdvandvaḥ nityasattvasthaḥ niryogakṣemaḥ ātmavān -- On the surface, the Vedas describe matters bound up with the three guṇas. [From within, they remove the poison of that very bondage to the three guṇas.] O Arjuna, free yourself from bondage to the three guṇas... Cast off the strain of the pairs of opposites, and fix your mind always on Bhagavān. Hand over the burden of yoga-kṣema to Him, and walk on believing Him alone to be your Master.\n\nUp to now we have seen what Kṛṣṇa has told Arjuna about how to reflect upon the Veda, and how to descend from its surface meaning to its depth. In this verse Kṛṣṇa explains still more clearly how it appears and how it must be seen. This is, in a way, the central matter of the entire discipline of spiritual reflection. Here Kṛṣṇa explains how we must reflect upon it, and how we must ready our minds.\n\nOn the surface, the Veda appears to us to be a worldly matter bound up with sattva, rajas, and tamas — the three guṇas — and to speak only a worldly meaning, just as the whole world looks red to one wearing red-tinted glasses. For example: increase of progeny, cowsheds full of cattle, hands full of money, homes full of children, the rājasūya, the aśvamedha, the puruṣamedha, and so on. These are what appear on the surface of the Veda. All of these are purely worldly matters. Because of this it seems as though the Veda does not carry us into the depths of the spirit. But to truly understand the Veda we must first stand beyond the influence of worldly things. The worldly influence is like red-tinted glasses; seeing the Veda under its influence, all we see is worldly matter alone. For this reason we must become traiguṇya-varjita — free of the three guṇas. Only when we cross beyond the influence of these three guṇas and recognize our own true nature is pure reflection possible. Without crossing the fence of the three guṇas, truth cannot be known. It is impossible to stand entirely outside the influence of the three guṇas. But if we know this and remain alert to it, sādhana becomes possible for us. However impure we may be, remembering Bhagavān's name with the mouth, and the awareness that Bhagavān dwells within us, purifies us.\n\nWhile meditating we must guard our mind so that it does not stray toward the outer world. Performing fire-rituals without self-knowledge is of no use. We must undertake sādhana selflessly, through awareness of Bhagavān, who is the very refuge (ātma-tattva) sheltering us. We must practice a dharma forever free of deceit and fraud. We must ready our minds for pure, wholesome reflection. Only when we stop looking toward our own selfish ends, and stand beyond the three guṇas, do we come to know the true message of the Veda. Reading the Veda in this state, we understand that it is not merely a worldly matter. Every action we perform, every act of worship, must be done selflessly as the worship of Bhagavān. We do not know what we truly need; Bhagavān will surely give us what we need — with this trust, if we hand over our anxiety for the future to Bhagavān and move forward, knowledge is accomplished.\n\nTo obtain what we do not have is yoga; to preserve what we have obtained is kṣema. Our entire life passes away caught up in worldly yoga and kṣema. Setting this aside, we must learn to be content with whatever Bhagavān has given us. Only in the state of niryoga-kṣema does the realization of truth occur. The responsibility for our yoga-kṣema belongs to that Bhagavān. We must take up the reflection on Bhagavān with the trust that He, dwelling within all as the indweller, will forever protect us. This is the mental preparation needed for spiritual sādhana. In such a state of mind, whatever text we read becomes praise of Bhagavān. Without reaching this state of mind, Vedānta will not be understood by us.\n\nFrom Kṛṣṇa's words clarifying what sādhana a person walking the spiritual path must undertake, and what the inner discipline should be, a question may arise for us. Kṛṣṇa says that between karma and jñāna, one must proceed giving weight to jñāna — that the reflection behind any action of ours must be supportive of jñāna. The question is: why give so much importance to the discipline of knowledge? Can heaven not be gained through karma-yoga? Kṛṣṇa answers this question here. There was formerly a tradition of dividing the Veda into three sections — jñāna-kāṇḍa, karma-kāṇḍa, and upāsanā-kāṇḍa. But Kṛṣṇa says the Veda has only a single face. Behind the karma and the upāsanā spoken of in the Veda, knowledge lies concealed. Everything is for the sake of knowledge. Therefore Kṛṣṇa stresses in the next verse that the entire Veda is itself jñāna-kāṇḍa.",
  "2.46": "yāvān arthaḥ udapāne sarvataḥ saṃplutodake |\n\ntāvān sarveṣu vedeṣu brāhmaṇasya vijānataḥ -- Whatever use there is in a well is entirely contained within a lake, brimming and overflowing on every side. Whatever fruit is in all the Vedas is contained within the fruit of the wise one who knows Brahman. [Just as prakṛti is like water flooding everywhere — even at the dissolution remaining a filled vessel, subject to no other's keeping — whatever is obtainable from Bhagavān, who ordains time and all else, is easily available only to the great knower who knows the whole of the Vedas.]\n\nThis verse is like a riddle. Taken at face value it is a verse whose meaning is not understood. To understand this verse we must dive deep into it — only then do we find the pearls and gems that lie at its depths. Only when we look at it having entirely cast off our ego, in complete self-surrender, does it open itself to us. This verse can be unpacked a little as follows:\n\n'yāvān arthaḥ udapāne, yāvān arthaḥ sarvataḥ saṃplutodake, tāvān sarveṣu vedeṣu, tāvān brāhmaṇasya vijānataḥ' — Whatever benefit can be had from a well dug and drawing water, a brimming, overflowing reservoir of water can give that same benefit and countless further benefits besides. The condition of one who performs the karma-kāṇḍa without knowledge is like laboring hard at home to dig a well and draw water from it. The condition of one who has directly realized Bhagavān is like standing on the bank of a brimming, overflowing reservoir of water. The knower of the unmediated truth sees Bhagavān in everything. The knower of Brahman is like one standing beside a forever clear, overflowing body of water. One who has read the whole Veda and remains fixed only in ritual action is like one sweating to draw water from a well.\n\nThe word 'brāhmaṇa' occurs in this verse. If we take this to mean caste, the true message of this verse is lost. Nowhere in the Vedic texts is caste mentioned. Brāhmaṇa means 'brahma jānāti iti' — one who has understood the principle of Brahman. 'Brahma' means that which is greatest of all. Greater than all else are the Veda, and Bhagavān who is known through the Veda. Therefore one who has understood the Veda, who has directly realized the Bhagavān who is known through the Veda, is a brāhmaṇa.\n\nFor the knower who has known Brahman directly, the fruit is the brimming, overflowing reservoir; for the ritualist, it is the fruit of the well. Only the vijñāni who has gained a distinguished awareness through the śāstra can perceive Bhagavān. Here vijñāni means one who has a root-deep, fully grounded knowledge of any given thing.\n\nThere is another matter concealed in this verse. 'Sarvataḥ samplutodaka' means the state of being filled with water. Here 'water' means the flood-waters of the dissolution (pralaya). Prakṛti, in the subtle form of an ocean of atoms, is time itself at the moment of dissolution. The one who lies within this ocean of dissolution is 'udapāna.' Ut-apa-ana — that is: utkṛṣṭa, ever-vigilant (ut); the one who nourishes all, and who has no other, greater keeper above himself, the highest of all (apa); the all-controller who governs everyone (ana). Whatever benefit can be gained through this principle, the knower of Brahman obtains in every single Veda, in each mantra of the Veda, in each sentence of a mantra, in each word of a sentence, in each syllable of a word — perceiving Bhagavān there.\n\nReading the Veda only on the surface brings no benefit whatsoever. Only when one sees Bhagavān in the entire mantra of the Veda, in each single syllable of the mantra, and becomes filled with Bhagavān, does one become worthy of Bhagavān's entire grace and gain the complete benefit.\n\nKṛṣṇa's statement — 'do not perform ritual action out of desire for its fruit; devote the whole Veda to the reflection on Bhagavān' — may cause us a little confusion, because the Veda mentions fruit at every turn. The question arises in us: why is this so? As said earlier, the textbook of spirituality is one and the same at every stage of sādhana — for the young and for the old alike. If those still at the beginning stage are told to follow the path desiring nothing at all, remaining detached, they may never even develop an interest in Vedānta. For this reason, just as medicine is given to children coated with jaggery, the Vedas speak of fruits. This is useful at the beginning stage of Vedic study.\n\nKṛṣṇa explains in detail, in the next verse, what our state of mind should be while performing karma.",
  "2.47": "karmaṇi eva adhikāraḥ te mā phaleṣu kadācana |\n\nmā karmaphala hetuḥ bhūḥ mā te saṅgaḥ astu akarmaṇi -- You have a right to action alone, never to its fruits. Do not act out of belief in the fruit [do not imagine yourself to be the power that grants the fruit of action]; and let there be, likewise, no attachment in you to inaction.\n\nThis verse is misunderstood by more people than understand it correctly. What does it mean to say one should perform action but not desire its fruit? Many have put forward arguments that this reduces a person to inaction, and so on. But none of them have truly tried to grasp the actual meaning of the verse. Nowhere here does it say do not desire the fruit. Instead, Kṛṣṇa says: do not claim authority over the fruit.\n\nAction is in your hands — but the fruit of action is in the hands of Bhagavān. Do not think that you yourself are the cause of the fruit of action. Kṛṣṇa says: keep the awareness that Bhagavān is the cause of the fruit of action. Here Kṛṣṇa has not denied desire itself — but he says that the view 'it must happen exactly this way, otherwise I will not perform this action' is not right. We have the right to desire, but we must have the awareness that it is Bhagavān who grants the fruit. Very often we desire what is wrong without realizing it. Then Bhagavān cannot grant it — because we ourselves have no awareness of what is right and what is wrong. If we hold this awareness, we do not grieve when the fruit does not come. If we perform our action with sincerity as an offering to Bhagavān, and cultivate the disposition of accepting whatever fruit comes as it is, there will be no grief.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: never sit inactive at any time; remain forever engaged in your duty. Setting aside the thoughts 'it must happen exactly this way,' 'exactly this fruit must come,' when we discharge our duty there is no grief at all. Apart from this, if one claims authority over the fruit, the shock and agitation that comes when that fruit is not obtained is terrible. The state of mind that sees defeat and victory alike carries us to great heights. As said before, we cannot learn as much from victory as we learn from defeat. When Bhagavān gives us the fruit, he gives it with our own good in view — not as we ourselves imagine it should be. Kṛṣṇa's doctrine of karma is: accept whatever comes as it comes, never becoming inactive, never claiming authority over the fruit, and move forward. Here it is not wrong for Arjuna to think that he is fighting to protect dharma, for the victory of dharma. At this stage, without thinking about defeat or victory, offering it entirely to Bhagavān, discharging with sincerity, and to the very limit of his strength, the duty that has fallen to his share — this is his dharma. Doing so, whatever result comes at each stage of the battle cannot unsettle him. Whatever the outcome, he need only accept it as Bhagavān's grace and move forward.",
  "2.48": "yogasthaḥ kuru karmāṇi saṅgam tyaktvā dhanaṃjaya |\n\nsiddhi asiddhyoḥ samaḥ bhūtvā samatvam yogaḥ ucyate -- O Dhanañjaya, perform your actions established in yoga, having abandoned attachment — remaining equal in success and failure alike. This equanimity itself is called 'yoga.'\n\nWhatever action one performs, it must never be done thoughtlessly. There must be a yoga behind it. It is Bhagavān who grants the fruit, and it is He alone who makes me perform the action. What fruit, how much fruit, when the fruit comes — all of this belongs to Him. Whether it is obtained or not, there is joy either way. This is physical equilibrium. In such equilibrium one must perform action purely from the standpoint of duty. When this reflection is present in every action of our lives, it becomes the worship of Bhagavān. This itself is karma-yoga.\n\nTo see success and failure with an equal eye is the yoga of the mind. This is a mental state. If one performs action with this yogic disposition, there is no vanity when the fruit comes, and no grief when it does not. Every duty performed thus, with equanimity toward success and failure, becomes the worship of Bhagavān. Even a great sacrifice performed without this reflection cannot become true worship. Only when the reflection is present that 'Bhagavān made me do this work; let it be offered up in the form of worship to Bhagavān' does it become karma-yoga. For our mind to attain union (saṃyoga) with Bhagavān is itself yoga. Every action that is supportive of, and preceded by, this reflection is karma-yoga.",
  "2.49": "dūreṇa hi avaram karma buddhiyogāt dhanaṃjaya |\n\nbuddhau śaraṇam anviccha kṛpaṇāḥ phalahetavaḥ -- O Dhanañjaya, the path of action is far, far inferior to the path of understanding. Therefore take refuge in understanding. [Even after gaining understanding, surrender to Hari.] Those who stand relying only on the fruit are objects of pity.\n\nIn the world some are karma-yogis, others are jñāna-yogis. Some think karma-yogis lack knowledge, and that once knowledge has come, action is no longer needed. Some say action is superior; others say knowledge is superior. Still others argue that karma and jñāna are equal, 'like the two wheels of a cart.' Many such disputes arise from this. But Kṛṣṇa says here plainly: action falls far short before knowledge. Knowledge is of the utmost importance while performing action. Action exists for the sake of knowledge — it is not equal to knowledge. For this reason one must surrender to knowledge and perform action that supports knowledge. The condition of those who perform action out of desire for fruit, without knowledge, is pitiable. Without knowing the inner secret of action, they struggle on, performing action.\n\nTo rise higher on the path of knowledge, one must indeed perform action. Through this, the realization of Bhagavān comes about. In this state, without pride, one must surrender to Him, knowing 'Bhagavān alone is my protector, and He alone is my refuge.' 'I have offered myself to God; He will surely protect me' — this settled conviction is surrender.\n\nWhen any problem comes upon us, we should pray to Bhagavān and to no one else. If we ask wholeheartedly, 'O Bhagavān, why have you placed me in this trouble? Show me a way out of it, Father' — He will certainly deliver us from distress. But here too, without claiming authority over the fruit, we must learn, trusting Bhagavān alone, to live free of anxiety in the midst of all our troubles.\n\nBefore looking at the next verse, let us understand the meaning of the epithet 'Dhanañjaya' that occurs here. This address has two meanings — one worldly, and one spiritual. Seen worldly, Arjuna fought in many places, extended the boundaries of the kingdom, and increased the wealth of the royal treasury — hence he is Dhanañjaya. Seen spiritually, this name carries a special meaning: worship of Bhagavān and spiritual awareness are the true wealth (dhana). Awareness of Bhagavān, knowledge, is the greatest wealth of all. Arjuna was, in his own time, a knower of the unmediated truth; he was Indra himself; he was the very pinnacle of the wealth of knowledge. It is with the sense 'you, being such a knower, surrender to Bhagavān and act with deliberate understanding' that Kṛṣṇa uses this address.",
  "2.50": "buddhiyuktaḥ jahāti iha ubhe sukṛtaduṣkṛte |\n\ntasmāt yogāya yujyasva yogaḥ karmasu kauśalam -- One who is joined with understanding casts off, here itself, both good deeds and bad. Therefore engage yourself in the 'yoga' of understanding. 'Yoga' is this alone: the skill of acting with true knowledge.\n\nIf one performs action with understanding, one casts off all one's sins as well as the merit one does not need, and attains Bhagavān. Here, unwanted merit means, for example: a great soul attains mokṣa through his knowledge and action. While on this path, he also accumulates other, unrelated merit. If he has, say, gained the merit to shine forth as an emperor, that is merit he does not need — because he has no further need of such merit once mokṣa is before him. He casts off this merit right here. This is what is meant by 'unwanted merit.' Whoever lives established in this awareness of Bhagavān — the action they perform does not become the cause of bondage through sin or merit. Therefore it is wisdom to stand in jñāna-yoga and perform action out of that awareness.",
  "2.51": "karmajam buddhiyuktāḥ hi phalam tyaktvā manīṣiṇaḥ |\n\njanmabandha vinirmuktāḥ padam gacchanti anāmayam -- The wise, joined with understanding, having abandoned the fruit born of action, freed from the bondage of birth, attain the state that is free of affliction.\n\nTrue manīṣīs are those who have mastered their senses (jitendriyas), able to govern their own minds. They are those who have walked the path of knowledge accompanied by self-restraint and have gained that awareness. The wise, possessed of understanding, set aside their attachment to the fruit that would come from action, and offer it up to Bhagavān. Thus, whoever renounces the fruit of action with the intent of offering it to Bhagavān, and performs action without deceit, performs action yet is not bound by the bondage of karma; casting off every bondage, they attain a mokṣa free of all fault. Therefore one should perform one's duty as the worship of Bhagavān and attain mokṣa. Apart from this, the stance 'I am a knower, I need do nothing' is not correct.",
  "2.52": "yadā te moha kalilam buddhiḥ vyatitariṣyati |\n\ntadā gantā asi nirvedam śrotavyasya śrutasya ca -- The day your understanding crosses beyond the mire of delusion, that day you will attain complete indifference toward what is yet to be heard and what has already been heard.\n\nWhen does what we have read and heard become worthwhile? However much we hear, if our understanding remains only in confusion, however much we have heard is of no use. Through reading and hearing, the mire of our mind must be washed away, and the mind must become clear.",
  "2.53": "śruti vipratipannā te yadā sthāsyati niścalā |\n\nsamādhau acalā buddhiḥ tadā yogam avāpsyasi -- When your understanding — first unsettled by the śrutis, [and then, once again, brought into harmony by the śrutis] — stands firm through true awareness, unshaken in samādhi, then you will attain yoga.\n\nHere 'vipratipannā' means 'difference of opinion.' At first, hearing (śravaṇa) from the śrutis feels confusing. Then, when one goes from there to the Veda, the mind thinks in opposition to what is said in the Veda. But once the mind becomes clear, it settles firmly into the principle beyond the three guṇas spoken of in the śruti. What was at first the poison of the three guṇas comes to be relieved of that very poison (yāpayati). Once the mind becomes pure, it comes into tune (śrutigūḍu — tune) with the Veda. Because of this, no difference at all appears between what is said in the Veda and our own thoughts. The mind comes to rest in Vedic scripture. Meditating in this state, the mind always comes to rest in Bhagavān. Going still further, in the unshaken state of samādhi, looking into reflection on one's own true nature — the true form of Bhagavān is realized. In this state the mind does not work — the self is at work. Through the eye of the self, the realization of Bhagavān occurs — this is the complete fruit of sādhana.",
  "2.54": "arjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna said:\n\nsthitaprajñasya kā bhāṣā samādhisthasya keśava |\n\nsthitadhīḥ kim prabhāṣeta kim āsīta vrajeta kim -- O ruler of Brahmā and Rudra, how is one recognized whose understanding has become firm — one who has [corrected the errors of understanding and] is able to mount samādhi? What does such a sthitaprajña say? How does he sit? How does he walk?\n\nAt this point Kṛṣṇa pauses his teaching and listens to Arjuna's question. A person's awareness must become firm; through the realization of truth a person can become sthitaprajña. Arjuna's question is: how are we to recognize such a sthitaprajña? It is impossible to recognize a sthitaprajña by his outer dress. How can we recognize one who, without clinging to the outer world, has reached the state of samādhi within the inner world? How does he deal with the outer world? What are his manner and speech like? — this is Arjuna's question.\n\nHere Arjuna addresses Kṛṣṇa as 'Keśava.' This very address shows that Arjuna himself is a great knower. Keśava means kaha + īśa — kaha means the power of Brahmā, īśa means the power of Śiva. The supreme power within the power of Brahmā and the power of Śiva is Keśava. Bhagavān, who stands within the rays of the sun and gives power to the entire world, is Keśava. This address carries the sense: 'I am eager to hear and learn this matter from you, who give light to the world.'\n\nMan's grief is rooted, at its core, in his petty desires. When his desire is not fulfilled, grief and anger and the like begin. Through this he turns his own life into a hell. In such a state he has no awareness of that extraordinary power within him. One who is sthitaprajña, without getting caught in this turmoil, remains ever content, savoring that great bliss within his own inner being. He does not fall prey to desires. The craving 'I must have it' does not torment him. His mind is always serene. Whatever problem comes in life, he never falls prey to tension or stress. His mind remains firm, and because of this he can find success.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as 'Pārtha.' Pārtha means one who has crossed to the far shore — that is, one who has crossed the ocean of truth, of sādhana. This address carries the sense: 'This should be clear to you, who have crossed the ocean of sādhana.'\n\nIn the three verses that follow, Kṛṣṇa explains what it means, and how, to give up desires.",
  "2.55": "prajahāti yadā kāmān sarvān pārtha manaḥgatān |\n\nātmani eva ātmanā tuṣṭaḥ sthitaprajñaḥ tadā ucyate -- O Pārtha, when one abandons all the desires that have made their home in the mind, and stands settled in the Supreme Self alone, contented and rejoicing in the Supreme Self's own grace, one is then called sthitaprajña.\n\nKṛṣṇa, without directly describing the conduct and speech of the sthitaprajña, first explains here what such a one is like. For the true sthitaprajña, fundamentally, no desire at all torments him (here, desire means the evil cravings that lead a person astray). Having abandoned all such petty cravings, he remains ever content, beholding the Bhagavān of knowledge and bliss within himself.",
  "2.56": "duḥkheṣu anudvignamanāḥ sukheṣu vigataspṛhaḥ |\n\nvīta rāga bhaya krodhaḥ sthitadhī muniḥ ucyate -- When distress comes, there is no agitation of mind within. There is no longing for pleasurable things. There is attachment nowhere, no fear, no anger. Such a reflective one is called sthitaprajña.\n\nIn life, pleasure and pain are like day and night. This is constant, and spares no one. Sometimes pleasure comes to us unsought; likewise, at other times, grief follows after us like a string of beads. Money cannot ward off grief, nor can pleasure be purchased. Ordinarily, when grief comes, we become agitated. This unsettles the mind, and through that the body's health is ruined. There is no use sitting and weeping over what is unavoidable. Sitting and weeping does not remove the grief that has come, nor can it ward off the grief that is to come. Grief is 'the preparation for pleasure'; pleasure is 'the preparation for grief.' These are life's unavoidable pairs of opposites. This does not mean we should not feel joy when pleasure comes — only that we should not lose ourselves in that pleasure. The mind must be trained in even-mindedness. One must live one's life knowing the truth that pleasure and grief are interwoven, warp and weft, in life's battle.\n\nIt is easy to say that one should live with a firm mind when grief comes. But following that in actual life is just as difficult. An example of this is Arjuna shedding tears when Abhimanyu died in the Mahābhārata war. Arjuna, who had received teaching directly from Bhagavān himself and gone into battle under Bhagavān's own charioteership as a sthitaprajña, grieves when his son Abhimanyu dies. We must train ourselves to accept grief. Otherwise life becomes unbearable.\n\nThree things should not be present in the mind regarding any object: rāga (attachment), bhaya (fear), and krodha (anger). The state prior to grief is fear; the state that follows fear is grief! Excessive longing is rāga; when the longed-for object is not obtained — disappointment. If that object goes to someone weaker than us, we feel anger toward them; if it goes to someone stronger than us, we feel fear of them! All of this is the mind's own turmoil. As Vyāsa said: 'In this world, nothing that should not have happened has ever happened, nor will it ever happen hereafter.' What is bound to happen happens regardless; there is no benefit in worrying over it. One who has made such reflective steadiness a part of himself is called sthitaprajña.\n\nThe word 'muni' is used here. Muni means one who has conquered desire and anger. We must try to keep the mind uniformly detached in every circumstance. One who sees pleasure and pain with an equal eye in life, saying 'let whatever comes, come — let there only be Bhagavān's grace,' is a muni. 'You too, become such a muni' is the message here.",
  "2.57": "yaḥ sarvatra anabhisnehaḥ tat tat prāpya śubha aśubham |\n\nna abhinandati na dveṣṭi tasya prajñā pratiṣṭhitā -- Whoever, on encountering each respective good or bad thing, does not cling excessively to it anywhere — has no delight over the good, no rage over the bad — such a one's understanding is firmly established.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa says: 'Do not lose yourself when grief comes; do not swell with pride when pleasure comes; practice detachment. Do not have excessive attachment to anything. Achieve balance of mind.' Grief spares no one. This is clear if we look at the suffering endured by the Pāṇḍavas, who were themselves born of divine portions. Difficulty comes to everyone, whether wise or ignorant. We need only change our state of mind toward viewing difficulty. 'I have risen above wanting and not-wanting; I have no right to choose 'I want this, I do not want that'' — living with this truth understood, life becomes smooth. Such a person rises to great heights.\n\nThe wise have this state of mind. For us ordinary humans, mastery of the senses is very difficult. But it is not impossible. Through constant sādhana this can be achieved. Kṛṣṇa explains in the next verse, with an illustration, how a practitioner should perform this discipline of controlling the senses.",
  "2.58": "yadā saṃharate ca ayam kūrmaḥ aṅgāni iva sarvaśaḥ |\n\nindriyāṇi indriya arthebhyaḥ tasya prajñā pratiṣṭhitā -- Just as a tortoise draws its limbs in completely on every side into its shell, so too does the understanding of one who draws the senses back from their objects become firmly established.\n\nOur own senses lead us astray. They create in us the very desire for what is not needed. Here Kṛṣṇa says: just as a tortoise draws its four legs and its head entirely into its shell and remains safe, so too must we draw our five senses into the fortress of a firm mind and turn them inward. Here, 'turning inward' means changing the way we look at things. It means analyzing everything from an inner viewpoint, recognizing and grasping the goodness within it, and reflecting upon the glory of the Bhagavān who created it.\n\nWe look upon this world that Bhagavān has created. If, while looking, we reflect upon the glory of that universal power lying behind it, that becomes an inward-turned, inner vision. When we have the awareness that Bhagavān is present within everything, and the understanding that in each single manifestation there is one of Bhagavān's own glories (vibhūtis), nothing at all can lead us astray. This is the vision with which the wise see the world. We cannot change the world. Once we understand the truth that we cannot control the world according to our own liking, and cultivate the disposition to live in accordance with the world as it is, there is no problem at all. The understanding of one who has thus turned his senses inward becomes firmly established.",
  "2.59": "viṣayāḥ vinivartante nirāhārasya dehinaḥ |\n\nrasavarjam rasaḥ api asya param dṛṣṭvā nivartate -- For an embodied being who abstains from food, only the strength for sense-enjoyment weakens — apart from the craving of the tongue and the craving for enjoyment. Even that craving vanishes when he beholds the Supreme.\n\nWe have seen that to become sthitaprajña, we must turn the senses inward. But that is not so easy a matter. If a practitioner, still dwelling in the body of sādhana, blocks the senses from contact with outer objects (the practice of solitude), all desires to see and to hear may well leave him. But taste (the tongue's fickleness) and passion (sexual desire) are very difficult to overcome. For this reason, if one wishes to observe celibacy, it is easier to undertake the sādhana after once experiencing the enjoyment of passion, understanding what married life is, and only then setting foot on celibacy. A regulated diet and fasting are supportive of spirituality. Overeating, and tāmasic and rājasic food (for example: toor dal, onion, garlic, drumstick, excessively spiced food, meat, and so on) are entirely opposed to spiritual sādhana. [For this reason, one should never force food upon a guest who has come; eating leftover cooked food only because it would otherwise go to waste ruins both the food and the body!] In sum, solitude and control over food are supportive of sādhana in spiritual practice. Through this, even if the fickleness of our tongue and of enjoyment is not fully subdued, they vanish when they surrender completely to Bhagavān.",
  "2.60": "yatataḥ hi api kaunteya puruṣasya vipaścitaḥ |\n\nindriyāṇi pramāthīni haranti prasabham manaḥ -- However much a sādhaka may understand, however much he may strive, the senses forcibly agitate and carry away the mind.\n\nHowever much we read and hear and come to know, this path of sādhana is not so easy. Without the resolve of effort it is impossible. To walk the spiritual path the mind must be firm. But here there is a difficulty: when the mind becomes firm, the senses grow slack. When one sits down with the firm mental resolve that there must be no attachment or aversion, no falling prey to desire or anger, that desire must be reined in — the senses throw the mind into confusion. Even one who has read the śāstra and seen the truth indirectly is not spared this trouble. The senses forcibly lead the mind astray. Because of this, doubt arises as to whether what one first believed should be held on to or let go. This is something that comes within the experience of every sādhaka. Even though this problem appears serious, Kṛṣṇa points to a simple solution for it in the next verse.",
  "2.61": "tāni sarvāṇi saṃyamya yuktaḥ āsīta mat paraḥ |\n\nvaśe hi yasya indriyāṇi tasya prajñā pratiṣṭhitā -- Having restrained all of these, one should sit joined, believing Me to be the Supreme Reality, with the mind fixed on Me alone. Thus the understanding of one who has conquered the senses becomes firmly established.\n\nFor the mind to be stirred by the senses, or for the senses to be set by the mind upon some unwanted action, is a problem that troubles everyone. To come out of this problem we must tame these horses called the senses, bind them with the rein called the mind, and hold that rein so it does not slacken. To carry out this task successfully we must first surrender to Bhagavān. 'Hand this responsibility over to Me,' says Kṛṣṇa. What assurance is this? We must pray to Bhagavān: 'O Bhagavān, dwell within me and inspire me so that my senses do not go astray; make the power of Your reflection remain constantly in my mind.' To try to do everything by one's own effort alone, without gaining surrender to Bhagavān, is impossible. One must make the effort, and pray to Bhagavān that the effort succeed; when one surrenders to Him, sooner or later the grace of realization comes. Then complete mastery of the senses becomes possible. This sādhana belongs to many births. Rather than practicing for ten days and, disappointed, becoming faithless, if one practices continually, success will be ours. Whoever's senses have come under his own control, his understanding becomes firm. He wins in life, on the path of sādhana.",
  "2.62": "Kṛṣṇa explains, in the next two verses, how our own mind becomes the cause of our downfall. Here is an extraordinary mental picture of how a person's mind works.\n\ndhyāyataḥ viṣayān puṃsaḥ saṅgaḥ teṣu upajāyate |\n\nsaṅgāt saṃjāyate kāmaḥ kāmāt krodhaḥ abhijāyate -- For a person who keeps dwelling on sense-objects, an attachment to them grows. From attachment, desire is born. When desire is obstructed, anger arises.",
  "2.63": "krodhāt bhavati sammohaḥ sammohāt smṛti vibhramaḥ |\n\nsmṛtibhraṃśāt buddhināśaḥ buddhināśāt vinaśyati -- From anger comes delusion — the craving to do what should not be done (a wrong perception). From such cravings comes forgetfulness of what is enjoined and forbidden. From forgetfulness, loss of discernment; from loss of discernment, utter ruin!\n\nThrough continuous contact with an object, an infatuation with it grows within us. First acquaintance, then from acquaintance friendship, and then attachment to that friendship. Then the wish that it should become 'mine.' When what we have desired is not fulfilled, anger. The root of this anger is love itself! The root cause of anger is desire. If we wish to prevent anger from arising, we must first give up desire. As desire lessens, anger lessens. From clinging or attachment, desires and cravings grow. When they are not fulfilled, anger. From anger, delusion — that is, incongruous ideas enter the head and produce the craving to do what should not be done. Through this, the power to weigh right from wrong departs, and we sink to the level of justifying wrong as right. The result: an obstinate insistence on taking revenge. From this comes complete loss of discernment, and finally the downfall of the self — ruin, utter ruin!\n\nWhen discernment is destroyed, the mind is as good as dead. Through this the jīva finds himself walking the road to hell. This is the path of downfall. If we wish to rise to a higher plane, we must recognize the greatness of the Supreme Self and surrender to Him. When the mind reflects along a good path, the self can rise higher. For this, we must first reduce our attachment. This does not mean we should love no one, or form no friendships. Let there be love, let there be friendship — but let there be no infatuation. Let there be a firm resolve: let whatever comes, come. Excessive attachment to the idea that 'it must happen exactly this way' is the cause of grief. To be with everyone, to love everyone — but without clinging attachment — this is the root formula of sādhana and of joy.\n\nThe mind must always remain serene. Without serenity we lose our health, both mentally and physically. To remain at ease without losing our head in any circumstance is serenity. How this is to be gained is the picture given in the next verse.",
  "2.64": "rāga dveṣa vimuktaiḥ tu viṣayān indriyaiḥ caran |\n\nātmavaśyaiḥ vidheya ātmā prasādam adhigacchati -- Free from attachment and aversion, experiencing sense-objects through senses that remain under his own control, one whose self is thus disciplined attains clarity of mind.\n\nRestraint of the senses, or non-attachment, does not mean abandoning all sense-enjoyment, withdrawing from worldly dealings, and sitting with the eyes closed. Grasp the object through the senses — that is continuous. But do not become influenced by it and its slave, says Kṛṣṇa. Experience sense-objects through the senses, but do not attach the habit of attachment and aversion to those objects. Experience whatever comes just as it comes. When one cultivates such a disposition, there is no danger in whatever object the senses grasp. There is no danger at all in hearing or seeing anything with a detached mind. An example of a 'detached mind' here: sometimes we meet a friend along the road. We sit and converse there. But if someone comes afterward and asks what color clothes your friend was wearing, we do not know. We saw, but we did not notice — because there was no need for it. In the same way, keeping the mind detached, there is no danger in whatever object one grasps. Through this the senses remain under control, the mind remains obedient. Cultivating detachment from within, one can achieve solitude while being with everyone. One can mingle with everyone and yet remain apart. This is the state of being apart in the inner world. When one learns to live this way, the mind becomes clear. Joy is always welling up and overflowing. Sādhana becomes smooth.",
  "2.65": "prasāde sarva duḥkhānām hāniḥ asya upajāyate |\n\nprasannacetasaḥ hi āśu buddhiḥ pari avatiṣṭhati -- When the mind becomes clear, all the sādhaka's troubles vanish, because the understanding of one whose mind has become clear soon comes to rest in Bhagavān.\n\nIf the mind is dirty, everything is dirty. If the mind is clear, everything is clear. Every problem exists because the mind becomes dirty. If we pay no attention to the cleanliness of our own mind, every other endeavor is in vain. If the mind is clean, it is pure — that itself is the state of serenity. When the mind becomes clear, all grief vanishes of its own accord. This is the state of bliss beyond grief.\n\nFor the mind to become clear means it helps in knowing Bhagavān. Reflection on Bhagavān is not possible with a dirty mind. Once Bhagavān is known, there is no grief. The understanding becomes firm and comes to rest in the direct knowledge of Bhagavān. In this state, in whatever direction thought travels, it ends up coming to rest in Bhagavān. Whatever one hears, whatever one sees, the reflection on the root power of the world lying behind it comes to mind. Then there remains no question of grieving over some small matter. The clear mind becomes filled with Bhagavān. Once Bhagavān has filled the mind, everything is blissful. After that, there is no such thing as grief. This bliss is boundless.",
  "2.66": "na asti buddhiḥ ayuktasya na ca ayuktasya bhāvanā |\n\nna ca abhāvayataḥ śāntiḥ aśāntasya kutaḥ sukham -- For one who has not disciplined the mind, there is no clarified understanding. For one who has not disciplined the mind, there is no meditation either. For one without meditation, there is no liberation [the mind does not settle in Bhagavān]. For one not liberated [whose mind has not gone to Bhagavān], where is happiness?\n\nIf one wants happiness, one needs peace. If the mind is not serene but scattered, it does not go and reach Bhagavān. Whoever's mind does not attain union with Bhagavān has no direct realization. Such a person's mind does not become clear. Such people cannot reflect with one-pointed concentration. Without one-pointed reflection there is no true knowledge. For such people meditation is impossible. Here meditation means reflecting single-pointedly on one object. For us to gain a definite awareness about any single object, we need concentration. This is what is called 'bhāvanā' here. Only when the mind is single-pointed is deep reflection possible. Without reflecting deeply on anything, awareness of it, and of its full breadth, does not arise. For one who does not thus reflect, having descended into the depths, there is no peace. Here 'peace' has two meanings. On the surface, peace means a state of bliss. According to the śāstrakāras, the state of complete bliss for the jīva is mokṣa. Therefore whoever does not come to find Bhagavān through meditation has no mokṣa. For one who does not walk the path of mokṣa there is no happiness. Further, peace means the very summit or pinnacle of bliss. When the mind comes to rest in Bhagavān, we have peace.\n\nThus, restraining the senses, making the mind single-pointed, reflecting deeply in it, one must come to find the truth. Unless we leave the outer world and plunge deep into the inner world, the realization of truth does not occur. Without the realization of truth there is no happiness.",
  "2.67": "indriyāṇām hi caratām yat manaḥ anuvidhīyate |\n\ntat asya harati prajñām vāyuḥ nāvam iva ambhasi -- The mind, is it not shaped to follow after the senses that run toward their objects? Therefore it carries away the sādhaka's awareness astray — just as a storm carries a ship astray at sea.\n\nEven with all this profound matter before it, why does the mind not descend into the depths of the inner world? Kṛṣṇa says this is natural, because the mind has a certain nature. Whatever we have trained it to do, that alone it comes to favor. It rejects what is new, and likes only what is familiar. To the mind, the inner world is unfamiliar. Therefore it does not flow toward the inner world. If told firmly, the mind does turn inward — but we do not make that effort! Kṛṣṇa explains this danger in this verse.\n\nThe mind goes after whatever we have trained it to, from childhood. For example, one who has been vegetarian from childhood feels disgust on seeing meat. But the very butcher who lives amid meat, cooking and eating it, feels no revulsion at all. All this is training we give the mind. The mind constantly makes a habit of whatever it keeps seeing. In this way the mind has been fashioned by Bhagavān. Bhagavān has given the mind the nature of coming to favor whatever the senses habitually see. Not knowing this nature of the mind is dangerous. It will steal away our very awareness. Kṛṣṇa explains this through a fine illustration:\n\nWe are like a sailor traveling in a sailboat. Our mind is the sail tied to that boat. If we do not know the art of turning this sail and controlling the boat as we wish, we go wherever the wind blows, not to where we need to reach. This illustration is very apt. If we do not learn the art of controlling our own mind, life is shattered to pieces. It becomes like a dry leaf caught in a storm — this is the warning Kṛṣṇa gives here.",
  "2.68": "tasmāt yasya mahābāho nigṛhītāni sarvaśaḥ |\n\nindriyāṇi indriyārthebhyaḥ tasya prajñā pratiṣṭhitā -- Therefore, O mighty-armed one, whoever's senses are held completely in check, kept back in every way from the objects of the senses, his understanding is firmly established.\n\nKṛṣṇa says the mind always follows after whatever the outer senses have practiced. It is difficult to control the mind without controlling the senses. For this reason we must first train the senses. For this, teaching children good discipline from an early age is of the utmost importance. It is difficult to break a habit followed from childhood onward. For this reason, in earlier times, many disciplines were cultivated and instilled as training for children, both at school and at home. But today neither school nor home gives children such guidance, and today's younger generation has become like a boat caught in a storm. If discipline supportive of sense-control comes from childhood, the situation of the senses leading the mind astray does not arise. Through this the mind becomes firm. Single-pointed concentration becomes possible. When concentration comes, awareness becomes firm.\n\nKṛṣṇa has given an extensive psychological analysis of how the mind is to be restrained, how a restrained mind behaves, and how those in such a state of mind can become knowers. Kṛṣṇa answers Arjuna's original question in the next verse. To the question of what a sthitaprajña is like, and what his manner and speech are like, Kṛṣṇa gives here a momentous answer.",
  "2.69": "yā niśā sarvabhūtānāṃ tasyām jāgarti saṃyamī |\n\nyasyām jāgrati bhūtāni sā niśā paśyataḥ muneḥ -- That which is a knot of darkness for all beings — there the one who has conquered the senses remains awake. That in which all beings remain awake is deep night for the seeing sage.\n\nWhat is day for the ordinary person is night for one who has realized the truth. What is night for every living creature in the world is day for the sādhaka. That is, the true sādhaka remains awake in what is our night, and our day — the world of worldly dealings — is night to him. He has no engagement at all in the worldly world. He may appear to us like an innocent, like a child. It would be no surprise if one unaware of his knowledge sees him as a madman! Because, though he is with the worldly world, without mixing into it, he is always savoring the bliss within his own inner being. When a knower has beheld Bhagavān, everything else is darkness to him. Having seen the marvel, he remains with his mind fixed single-pointedly on it alone, in wonder. To behold Bhagavān we must always engage in the hearing of the śāstra. Hearing alone is not enough; after hearing, reflection and profound meditation are of the utmost importance. This is what is meant by 'ātmā vā are draṣṭavyaḥ śrotavyaḥ mantavyaḥ nididhyāsitavyaḥ ca.' One given to reflection is 'muni'; he alone is 'paśya' — that is, one able to behold Bhagavān, or one who has beheld him.\n\nIn today's society it is very difficult to recognize one who has realized the truth. Not all accomplished persons (siddha-puruṣas) are those who have seen the truth! Those who perform miracles cannot be said to be those who have beheld Bhagavān. Because a true sādhaka does not, as a rule, perform miracles. Only when it is truly unavoidable might he perform a miracle for the good of the world. Only by continuously seeking after truth and attaining siddhi can one become sthitaprajña.",
  "2.70": "āpūryamāṇam acalapratiṣṭham samudram apaḥ praviśanti yadvat |\n\ntadvat kāmāḥ yam praviśanti sarve saḥ śāntim āpnoti na kāmakāmī -- Just as all waters flow in and enter the ocean, which is ever being filled yet stands unmoved, without overflowing — so too, the one into whom all desires enter without disturbing him attains peace, not the one who runs after his desires.\n\nOne who has beheld Bhagavān is one who has realized the truth. Kṛṣṇa gives further explanation here of what such a person, walking the right path toward beholding Bhagavān, is like. His mind is always blissful. It remains settled in Bhagavān. He is not like a small stream, but like the ocean. Though all the rivers come and join the sea, it does not swell over. The sea does not overflow when the rains come, nor does it dry up in summer. The sādhaka is the same. Though all the enjoyments of the world enter him, he remains unshaken, standing beyond pleasure and pain. His mind is always serene. He holds fast to Bhagavān and attains mokṣa. But one who runs after his desires, never coming out of them, can never find true happiness.",
  "2.71": "vihāya kāmān yaḥ sarvān pumān carati nispṛhaḥ |\n\nnirmamaḥ nirahaṅkāraḥ saḥ śāntim adhigacchati -- Whoever lives, having given up all desires, without being seduced by any object, without falling prey to evil craving, having cast off the pride of 'I' and 'mine' — he attains peace.\n\nWhoever gives up the desire for enjoyment and joyfully experiences whatever has come is said to be a true sādhaka. Practicing a limited diet, sleep, and sexual restraint, living fearlessly within the complete knowledge of Bhagavān's protective armor, not falling prey to desires and cravings, remaining unshaken by any ups and downs — he is called a true 'human being.' Such a one never has the ego of 'I' and 'mine,' and he attains liberation.",
  "2.72": "eṣā brāhmī sthitiḥ pārtha na enām prāpya vimuhyati |\n\nsthitvā asyām antakāle api brahmanirbāṇam ṛcchati -- O Pārtha, this is the mark of the knower of Brahman. Having attained it, one is never again deluded. One who remains established in this state even at the last hour reaches the Supreme Reality that neither is born through the body nor dies.\n\nThe mark of one who has become established in Bhagavān is the brāhmī-sthiti. What has been said up to now is the mark of a brahma-jñāni — the mark of one whose awareness of Brahman, one established in Brahman, has awakened. Such a knower of Brahman cannot be recognized by his outer actions or his dress. His mental state alone reveals his standing.\n\nIf, even at the last moment of his death, the awareness of Bhagavān remains awake, he attains the direct mokṣa that has no further rebirth.\n\nThus ends the second chapter.\n\nChapter Three\n\nIn the second chapter, Kṛṣṇa has explained the knowledge of Bhagavān and the means for gaining that knowledge. In this teaching, at one point Kṛṣṇa says knowledge is the greatest of all, that action is nothing before knowledge. At another point he tells Arjuna to perform the tāmasic act of war! If knowledge is indeed the highest, why is this tāmasic war, full of attachment and aversion, needed at all? The third chapter begins with this very question of Arjuna's.\n\narjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna said:\n\njyāyasī cet karmaṇaḥ te matā buddhiḥ janārdana |\n\ntat kiṃ karmaṇi ghore mām niyojayasi keśava -- O Janārdana, if it is your view that understanding is greater than action, then why are you engaging me in this dreadful, killing task, O Keśava?\n\nArjuna is thrown into confusion by Kṛṣṇa's explanation of knowledge and action. The question of which path is right for him torments him. Kṛṣṇa at one point says: walk the path of knowledge — it is superior; at another point he says: fight! Arjuna's confusion is why he should not walk the superior path of knowledge, and why he must fight instead. In this confusion Arjuna asks Kṛṣṇa: 'O Janārdana, if it is your view that the path of knowledge is superior to action, why do you keep telling me to act? War is a dreadful, tāmasic act full of attachment and aversion. It is an action entirely opposed to spiritual sādhana. This being so, why are you engaging me in this task, Keśava?'\n\nIn this verse we see Arjuna addressing Śrī Kṛṣṇa by two special names, 'Janārdana' and 'Keśava.' Concealed here is the sentiment of Arjuna as he asks his question. Jana-ardana means 'destroyer of people' — here 'jana' means the wicked. Janārdana means the destroyer of the wicked. 'You, who incarnated to destroy the wicked, are making me perform this task. But not everyone gathered here is wicked. How, then, can the act of killing them be superior? You, who liberate people and grant them mokṣa — why are you making me perform this dreadful act of killing my elders and teachers?' — this is Arjuna's question. At the end of the verse, the epithet 'Keśava' signals Arjuna's surrender. Ka + īśa + va — Keśava; here 'ka' is the four-faced Brahmā, cause of creation; 'īśa' is Śaṅkara, cause of dissolution. Keśava means the supreme power comprising the power of Brahmā and the power of Śiva, the cause of creation and dissolution. 'You, who are the cause of creation, sustenance, dissolution, and liberation — deliver me from this confusion,' Arjuna pleads, surrendering to Śrī Kṛṣṇa.\n\nvyāmiśreṇa iva vākyena buddhim mohayasi iva me |\n\ntat... ekam vada niścitya yena śreyaḥ aham āpnuyām -- With what seems like a mixed, ambiguous statement, you seem to be confusing my power of resolve. Therefore, having decided on one single thing, tell me that, by which I may attain the good.\n\nHere 'vyāmiśreṇa' means 'apparent contradiction.' 'With such a mixed statement, no conclusion is coming to me as to what is right and what is wrong, nor do I have the power to know it. I cannot grasp your intent. By walking which path shall I attain the good? Tell me the definite path by which my life becomes worthwhile' — this is what Arjuna asks of Kṛṣṇa.",
  "3.1": "Chapter Three\n\nIn the second chapter, Kṛṣṇa has explained the knowledge of Bhagavān and the means for gaining that knowledge. In this teaching, at one point Kṛṣṇa says knowledge is the greatest of all, that action is nothing before knowledge. At another point he tells Arjuna to perform the tāmasic act of war! If knowledge is indeed the highest, why is this tāmasic war, full of attachment and aversion, needed at all? The third chapter begins with this very question of Arjuna's.\n\narjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna said:\n\njyāyasī cet karmaṇaḥ te matā buddhiḥ janārdana |\n\ntat kiṃ karmaṇi ghore mām niyojayasi keśava -- O Janārdana, if it is your view that understanding is greater than action, then why are you engaging me in this dreadful, killing task, O Keśava?\n\nArjuna is thrown into confusion by Kṛṣṇa's explanation of knowledge and action. The question of which path is right for him torments him. Kṛṣṇa at one point says: walk the path of knowledge — it is superior; at another point he says: fight! Arjuna's confusion is why he should not walk the superior path of knowledge, and why he must fight instead. In this confusion Arjuna asks Kṛṣṇa: 'O Janārdana, if it is your view that the path of knowledge is superior to action, why do you keep telling me to act? War is a dreadful, tāmasic act full of attachment and aversion. It is an action entirely opposed to spiritual sādhana. This being so, why are you engaging me in this task, Keśava?'\n\nIn this verse we see Arjuna addressing Śrī Kṛṣṇa by two special names, 'Janārdana' and 'Keśava.' Concealed here is the sentiment of Arjuna as he asks his question. Jana-ardana means 'destroyer of people' — here 'jana' means the wicked. Janārdana means the destroyer of the wicked. 'You, who incarnated to destroy the wicked, are making me perform this task. But not everyone gathered here is wicked. How, then, can the act of killing them be superior? You, who liberate people and grant them mokṣa — why are you making me perform this dreadful act of killing my elders and teachers?' — this is Arjuna's question. At the end of the verse, the epithet 'Keśava' signals Arjuna's surrender. Ka + īśa + va — Keśava; here 'ka' is the four-faced Brahmā, cause of creation; 'īśa' is Śaṅkara, cause of dissolution. Keśava means the supreme power comprising the power of Brahmā and the power of Śiva, the cause of creation and dissolution. 'You, who are the cause of creation, sustenance, dissolution, and liberation — deliver me from this confusion,' Arjuna pleads, surrendering to Śrī Kṛṣṇa.",
  "3.2": "vyāmiśreṇa iva vākyena buddhim mohayasi iva me |\n\ntat... ekam vada niścitya yena śreyaḥ aham āpnuyām -- With what seems like a mixed, ambiguous statement, you seem to be confusing my power of resolve. Therefore, having decided on one single thing, tell me that, by which I may attain the good.\n\nHere 'vyāmiśreṇa' means 'apparent contradiction.' 'With such a mixed statement, no conclusion is coming to me as to what is right and what is wrong, nor do I have the power to know it. I cannot grasp your intent. By walking which path shall I attain the good? Tell me the definite path by which my life becomes worthwhile' — this is what Arjuna asks of Kṛṣṇa.",
  "3.3": "bhagavān uvāca -- Bhagavān said:\n\nloke asmin dvi vidhā niṣṭhā purā proktā mayā anagha |\n\njñānayogena sāṅkhyānām karmayogena yoginām -- O anagha (sinless one), I have taught before that in this world there are two kinds of standing (mokṣa): for the followers of the path of knowledge, through knowledge-predominant sādhana; for the followers of the path of action, through action-predominant sādhana.\n\nHaving earlier spoken of the path of knowledge and the path of action, Kṛṣṇa now explains, at what level and in what standing, which one is primary. The special exposition of karma-yoga begins from here.\n\nIn this verse the word 'niṣṭhā' is used. Here 'niṣṭhā' means the course of life, or the final state — that itself is 'mokṣa.' Kṛṣṇa says: \"Among sādhakas there are two kinds — the sāṅkhyas and the yogis.\" Here sāṅkhyas means those who pursue sādhana through the path of knowledge. Yogis means those who pursue sādhana through karma.\n\nOn the surface, this verse appears to say: \"Seekers of knowledge should walk the path of knowledge; those who pursue sādhana through action should walk the path of action.\" It may then seem that since Arjuna is one who must pursue sādhana through action, Kṛṣṇa has told him to fight for that reason. But that is not the true meaning. As you know, Arjuna was one of the great knowers of that era. This being so, we must not interpret this verse at that surface level. Deeper reflection is needed here.\n\nAs the scriptures say, mokṣa can be reached only through knowledge. There is no other path to mokṣa. Given this, knowledge is absolutely necessary for the attainment of mokṣa. Action exists for the sake of knowledge. Action that is not conducive to knowledge is not true action at all. Sitting from morning to evening holding prayer beads and reciting \"pure, pure\" will not carry us to a higher state. Whatever action one performs, it must be performed with understanding. The action one performs must be conducive to knowledge. Therefore there is no such thing as pure jñāna-yoga alone, or pure karma-yoga alone. There is no knowledge without action, and no action without knowledge. As the Chāndogya Upaniṣad says: \"yad eva vidyayā karoti śraddhayā upaniṣadā, tad eva vīryavattaram bhavati, vijñānam yajñam tanute\" — meaning, \"Whatever you do, do it with understanding. Action performed with knowledge as its basis bears fruit; otherwise it is in vain.\" One must know why one is performing an action, what its fruit is, and how it is to be done. Action performed in ignorance turns out to be in vain. What Kṛṣṇa is describing here is one path that is action-predominant (as with Janaka and others) and another that is knowledge-predominant (as with Sanaka and others).",
  "3.4": "na karmaṇām anārambhāt naiṣkarmyam puruṣaḥ aśnute |\n\nna ca sannyasanāt eva siddhim samadhigacchati -- A person does not attain the state of actionlessness by not undertaking actions. Nor does one attain siddhi (accomplishment) merely by renouncing the fruit of action.\n\nAs the scriptures say, 'karma is the path of bondage and jñāna is the path of liberation.' But Kṛṣṇa has said that there are two paths to mokṣa — one knowledge-predominant, the other action-predominant. Here Kṛṣṇa explains this further. If mokṣa is to be attained through actionlessness, and action is bondage, then it should be possible for the jīva in a body to remain without doing any action at all — but this is not possible. Once the body has come into being, the activity of action goes on and on. Mokṣa is not obtained the moment one stops performing action. Fundamentally, action cannot be abandoned at all. Mechanical action that is not conducive to knowledge is what binds. Action that is conducive to knowledge never binds. Nor does any siddhi come about merely because one has ceased to desire the fruit of action. Siddhi can never be attained merely by giving up action.",
  "3.5": "na hi kaścit kṣaṇam api jātu tiṣṭhati akarma kṛt |\n\nkāryate hi avaśaḥ karma sarvaḥ prakṛtijaiḥ guṇaiḥ -- No one can remain even for a moment without doing something. Everyone acts, helplessly, driven by the guṇas born of prakṛti (unknowingly, under Bhagavān's control).\n\nSiddhi is not gained by renouncing action. Not only that — renouncing action is not even possible. Our annamaya and prāṇamaya kośas are continuously at work. It is impossible to remain inactive while alive. At no time, not even for a single moment, is it possible for anyone to be without action. \"kāryate hi avaśaḥ\" — the jīva within the body is made to act through his body, through his mind, through his speech. This is not within the jīva's own control. It is under Bhagavān's control. The entire universe has been created out of prakṛti; its root substances are sattva, rajas, tamas. The jīva has its own inherent nature, and the influence of these three guṇas of prakṛti acts upon us in keeping with that nature. Therefore inactivity is meaningless. We must engage ourselves in life. We must become dutiful. Along with this, our mind must remain awake, mentally, toward the truth beyond the worldly realm. All duties and actions must be performed with this awareness. Inactivity is not Vedānta — abandoning one's duty is not spirituality. With awareness of Bhagavān.",
  "3.6": "karmendriyāṇi saṃyamya yaḥ āste manasā caran |\n\nindriya arthān vimūḍha ātmā mithyā ācāraḥ saḥ ucyate -- One who restrains the organs of action, but sits dwelling on the objects of the senses with the mind, is a deluded soul called a hypocrite (mithyācāra).\n\nIn this verse Kṛṣṇa makes an important point. We commonly see, in today's world, greater importance given to outward appearance. There are people who, keeping every desire in the mind, live outwardly as though full of good conduct, out of some fear. Kṛṣṇa calls this hypocritical religiosity. We must first live without deceiving our own conscience. Purity of inner conduct matters more than outer conduct. Without this, there is never any uplift. One who restrains the organs of action but dreams, in the mind alone, of the objects of the senses is a deluded soul called a hypocrite — this is what Kṛṣṇa has said.",
  "3.7": "yaḥ tu indriyāṇi manasā niyamya ārabhate arjuna |\n\nkarma indriyaiḥ karmayogam asaktaḥ saḥ viśiṣyate -- But, O Arjuna, one who, having conquered the senses through the mind, and being unattached to the fruit, undertakes karma-yoga through the senses of action — he is superior.\n\nWhen the mind is pure and holds a firm rein over the senses, the sādhana performed through the organs of action becomes karma-yoga. This is pure spiritual sādhana. In this state the senses do not go astray. Action performed with purity of mind, in keeping with the true nature of one's own self, is real spiritual sādhana. One who engages in this kind of sādhana is called superior. Such people are not one thing within and another without; having conquered the senses through the strength of the mind, and having given up attachment to the fruit, they rise higher.",
  "3.8": "niyatam kuru karma tvam karma jyāyaḥ hi akarmaṇaḥ |\n\nśarīra yātrā api ca te na prasidhyet akarmaṇaḥ -- You perform the enjoined action that is yours to do; action is indeed superior to inaction. Even the mere maintenance of your body would not be accomplished through inaction.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: 'Perform the enjoined action, in keeping with your own inner nature, that is conducive to knowledge.' Looking at Arjuna, his inherent nature is that of a kṣatriya — one who fights injustice and protects the people of the country. Abandoning that, if he were to practice austerity thinking knowledge alone is supreme while injustice runs rampant in the land, that would not be true yoga at all. Kṛṣṇa says: 'Never remain inactive. Perform your own action, and accept whatever comes of it. But let the action you perform be conducive to knowledge. Let awareness of Bhagavān always remain awake.' When action is performed with the feeling 'Bhagavān is making me do this with my own hands, this is offered to Him,' then no action whatsoever becomes binding for us.\n\nPerform the action that is yours to do, eat what comes to you, let awareness of Hari's feet never fail. Hari alone is the supreme god, Hari alone is the guru and refuge, Hari alone is the mother and father of the world.\n\nFor as long as we remain in a body, action is unavoidable. Even when direct (aparokṣa) knowledge has arisen, action continues without cease. In this way Kṛṣṇa has unfolded before us, through this verse, the doctrine of karma.\n\nThere is one matter we must understand correctly here. According to the scriptures, karma is binding. By performing karma again we become caught in the wheel of karma. But here Kṛṣṇa says that karma is unavoidable. Then what is the path to mokṣa? The next verse gives a clear answer to this question of ours.",
  "3.9": "yajñāt arthāt karmaṇaḥ anyatra lokaḥ ayam karma bandhanaḥ |\n\ntat artham karma kaunteya muktasaṅgaḥ samācara -- Except for action performed in the form of worship of Bhagavān, this world is bound by karma. O Kaunteya, for that purpose, giving up attachment to the fruit, perform action as worship of Bhagavān.\n\nThis world is caught in the bondage of karma. But not all karma is binding. Karma performed with understanding never binds. We must perform whatever karma we do with awareness of Bhagavān. We are like puppets on a string; Bhagavān is the one who holds the strings. When we act with this awareness, every single action of ours becomes a yajña. For example, we breathe: we draw in oxygen from outside and expel carbon dioxide. We are not even aware of the design of that special mechanism within us. This process goes on without our knowledge. Plants take in the air we exhale and give us the oxygen we need. All of this is Bhagavān's arrangement. For this reason, Indians worship the aśvattha (peepal) tree, which gives the most oxygen. In that tree lies a special vibhūti (glory) of Bhagavān. When we recognize Bhagavān's greatness in this way in every one of our actions, the action we perform becomes karma-yoga, and it never becomes binding.\n\nThe word 'yajña' used here has the meaning 'yaja-deva-pūjā,' that is, worship of God. God Himself is also called yajña. Therefore it is not only the worship performed at the mouth of the sacrificial fire that is yajña. When we perform each of our actions with awareness of Bhagavān, that becomes yajña. What we must give up here is not action itself, but rather our attitude toward action needs to change, that is all. Karma means 'kara-ma' — the tax or tribute ('kara') that we, as subjects of Bhagavān's kingdom, render to Him. Bhagavān never accepts a monetary tribute. We must render His due tribute by engaging ourselves in the work of society. When this attitude arises, karma, instead of binding, becomes the path to mokṣa.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as 'Kaunteya.' There is a special reason for this form of address. Though Kuntī was born and raised in so great a family, she never once fell prey to the desire for enjoyment. She spent her youth in the forest, and was the courageous woman who filled her sons with courage when they lost heart. The hardship she endured was no small thing. Yet she said to Kṛṣṇa: 'Give me hardship alone, for then we shall always have the fortune of being with You!' She is the courageous woman who showed the world true devotion to duty. Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna, the son of such a great woman, as 'Kaunteya,' with the implication: 'You already know of karma-yoga.'\n\nHaving shown in this way how knowledge and action are to be harmonized in life, Kṛṣṇa now gives, in the form of a story, an explanation so that this becomes still clearer. Let us see that explanation in the verses that follow.",
  "3.10": "saha yajñāḥ prajāḥ sṛṣṭvā purā uvāca prajāpatiḥ |\n\nanena prasaviṣyadhvam eṣa vaḥ astu iṣṭa kāma dhuk -- Long ago, Prajāpati, having created mankind together with yajña (as worship of Bhagavān), said: \"By this, may you prosper; may this be the kāmadhenu (wish-fulfilling cow) that grants your desires.\"\n\nLong ago, the four-faced one created thoughtful human beings, and along with them created the yajña they were to perform. And he said: 'Obtain what you need through this method of worship. This is the kāmadhenu that gives you the desired object you seek.' As said before, 'yajña' means the method of worshiping God; 'dāna' (charity) and 'saṅgatīkaraṇa' (coming together) are also yajña. Dāna has a special meaning. It is not dāna to give away to another what one does not need while having plenty for oneself. When one has food for two meals and another does not even have food for one meal, giving him a share of one's own food and eating together — that is true dāna. To melt at another's hardship is true dāna. And saṅgatīkaraṇa means gathering together at one place for the acquisition of knowledge. A discourse (pravacana) is one form of saṅgatīkaraṇa.",
  "3.11": "devān bhāvayatā anena te devāḥ bhāvayantu vaḥ |\n\nparasparam bhāvayantaḥ śreyaḥ param avāpsyatha -- By this, nourish the gods; may those gods nourish you. Nourishing one another, may you attain the highest good.\n\nHaving created yajña in this way, the four-faced one is said to have declared: \"Through yajñas, help the gods, and may they fulfill your desires.\" \"Thus, nourishing one another, attain the highest good,\" he is said to have said. It is our delusion that we create for ourselves whatever we need on our own. For us to sow and reap, there must be no disruption in the natural order of the environment. Otherwise we cannot grow anything at all. Behind every action, many divine powers are at work. For every one of our organs, there is a presiding deity. If that power did not function, we could do nothing at all. In this way, Bhagavān's arrangement is such that a whole assembly of deities continuously carries out the work of giving us life, giving us eyes to see, ears to hear, and the intelligence to see and hear what is good. This being so, we must render gratitude to such divine powers. Only when we nourish one another in this way can we find our good.\n\nIt is customary to call the worship performed at the mouth of fire \"yajña.\" Fire is the most important symbol in the worship of God, because fire is utterly pure. Whatever is put into fire becomes purified. Fire is a symbol of Bhagavān. Bhagavān has no form; He is a mass of light and is pure. We can see this same quality in fire. Furthermore, if we are to offer any food to Bhagavān, it can only be done through fire. In yajña, what we worship is not the flame itself but, through the medium of fire, Agni-Nārāyaṇa — that is the yajña. If yajña is performed without this understanding, one might as well die from inhaling smoke! Our worship travels from Agni-Nārāyaṇa to Sūrya-Nārāyaṇa and back reaches Nara-Nārāyaṇa. For this reason, the yajña performed for God always takes place during the daytime. The power of yajña, dissolving through the seven colors of fire into the seven colors of the sun, merges into the atmosphere and brings auspiciousness to the world. This is what is called here the act of giving and receiving.",
  "3.12": "iṣṭān bhogān hi vaḥ devāḥ dāsyante yajña bhāvitāḥ |\n\ntaiḥ dattān apradāya ebhyaḥ yaḥ bhuṅkte stenaḥ eva saḥ -- Strengthened by such yajña, the gods will grant you the enjoyments you desire. One who partakes of what they have given without offering back to them is verily a thief.\n\nThis prakṛti gives us whatever we need. This being so, we, who make use of it, ought to feel gratitude. Instead, if one displays the arrogance of ownership, saying \"this is mine,\" there is no uplift for him. One who takes what prakṛti has given without rendering gratitude, and calls it \"mine,\" is verily nothing but a thief!",
  "3.13": "yajña śiṣṭa aśinaḥ santaḥ mucyante sarva kilbiṣaiḥ |\n\nbhuñjate te tu agham pāpāḥ ye pacanti ātma kāraṇāt -- The good, who eat what remains after offering to the gods, are freed from all sins. But those sinners who cook only for themselves eat nothing but their own sin.\n\nThose who offer what they have received to Bhagavān and accept it as His prasāda are freed from all the filth of life and live a clean life. In whichever house food is cooked only for oneself, what that person eats is not food but sin. It is Bhagavān who has given us this food. We must first render gratitude to Him. Instead, if we nurture the feeling \"this is my own earning, why should I share it with another,\" we only build up our own burden of sin. If we do not act in keeping with the purpose of creation, even what we have earned will not truly be ours to enjoy.",
  "3.14": "annāt bhavanti bhūtāni parjanyāt anna sambhavaḥ |\n\nyajñāt bhavati parjanyaḥ yajñaḥ karma samudbhavaḥ -- Living beings arise from food; the growth of food is from the sun (from the clouds). The increase of solar energy (the birth of clouds) is from yajña; the sustaining of yajña is from karma.",
  "3.15": "karma brahma udbhavam viddhi brahma akṣara samudbhavam |\n\ntasmāt sarva gatam brahma nityam yajñe pratiṣṭhitam -- The origination of karma is from Bhagavān; the manifestation of Bhagavān is through the imperishable syllable (the Veda). Therefore Bhagavān, who is present everywhere, is always established in yajña.",
  "3.16": "evam pravartitam cakram na anuvartayati iha yaḥ |\n\nagha āyuḥ indriya ārāmaḥ mogham pārtha saḥ jīvati -- One who, here, does not keep this wheel of the world turning lives a sinful life, lost in the pleasure of the senses. O Pārtha, such a person's life is in vain.\n\nThe birth of living beings is from food. There is no life without food. This food is obtained from crops. The root of the crop is solar energy and rain. Rain comes from yajña — from the honest life we lead, from the attitude of offering to Bhagavān. The sustaining of yajña is from karma, from our own activity. Karma here means dutiful action, honest activity. Such karma proceeds from Bhagavān. This Bhagavān (Brahma) is established in the Veda (akṣara). The Veda resides in living beings (in man). Therefore this is a cycle: man — food — rain — yajña — Bhagavān — Veda — man. Here \"Veda\" does not mean reciting Vedic mantras by rote; it means the awareness, the awakened consciousness, of Vedic wisdom. Within this cycle, we cannot abandon our own action. Action must always be performed as yajña, as an offering to Bhagavān. When awareness of Bhagavān and the consciousness of Vedic wisdom are both present in equal measure, then there is rain, from rain there is crop, and from crop there is life. Whoever does not keep this cycle turning has broken the movement of the cosmic wheel, has obstructed the natural course of society. Such a person only wastes his life lost in mere sensory pleasure and enjoyment.\n\nIf we keep this awareness of the cosmic wheel spoken of here in our daily activity, our life is worthwhile. If we always follow the course of that cosmic power, our uplift is assured. We need never fear anything, on any matter. We must cultivate the habit of turning our daily life into a yajña and living in awareness of Bhagavān. This is true karma-yoga. This alone is the path to mokṣa. This is the path that leads to Bhagavān.\n\nIn this universe, everything is interconnected. In every small action, the powers of the entire universe indirectly participate. Behind the food we eat there are many debts. If we do not remember this, we become ungrateful to the cosmic power.\n\nThe movement of the universe is the \"wheel of karma\"; within it is our \"duty-action\"; our \"yajñas and sacrifices\" — Kṛṣṇa has explained all this in detail. But here a question may arise for us. It is this: earlier Kṛṣṇa said knowledge is supreme, but also said action should not be given up. Yet the scriptures say that the wise have no need of action. What does this mean? Kṛṣṇa himself takes up this very question and answers it in the verses that follow. Does one who performs yajña inwardly have any action remaining? Is there a fault if he does not perform action? What is the state in which a person is free of the bondage of karma? Before mokṣa, is it possible to be without action while still in a worldly state? Is it possible to be entirely in an inward state without action? Kṛṣṇa gives his explanation to these and other such questions ahead.",
  "3.17": "yaḥ tu ātma ratiḥ eva syāt ātma tṛptaḥ ca mānavaḥ |\n\nātmani eva ca santuṣṭaḥ tasya kāryam na vidyate -- But the person who delights in the Self alone, who is satisfied through the Self, and who is content in the Self alone — for him there is nothing left to do.\n\n\"Ātmarati\" is the bliss one experiences upon seeing Bhagavān within. This state can be reached in samādhi. In true samādhi, our senses and mind do not function. Samādhi-sthiti is the state in which the jīva-svarūpa, which is of the nature of knowledge, directly beholds Bhagavān. In this state, direct contact with Bhagavān is possible. One who is in such a state has no taint of karma whatsoever. Having beheld God, this worldly realm becomes fully satisfying to him and he no longer desires it. He remains immersed solely in the ocean of bliss of the direct realization of Bhagavān. In this state, he has no duty-action left; he is beyond all injunctions and prohibitions.",
  "3.18": "na eva tasya kṛtena arthaḥ na akṛtena iha kaścana |\n\nna ca asya sarvabhūteṣu kaścit artha vyapāśrayaḥ -- For such a person, there is no gain from doing anything, nor any harm from not doing it. He has no dependence whatsoever on the fruit through any being.\n\nIn the state of samādhi, he has no hunger, no thirst. He has no awareness at all of the outer world. While in this state, there is neither any uplift to be had from performing some other sādhana, nor any fault in not performing duty-action. He has no need even of the support of the five elements or the five kośas. In this state there is no attachment of the kind that says \"my people, my family.\" He has no dependence on the outer world. One who beholds God in this way, from the state of samādhi, remains beyond duty-action.",
  "3.19": "tasmāt asaktaḥ satatam kāryam karma samācara |\n\nasaktaḥ hi ācaran karma param āpnoti pūruṣaḥ -- Therefore, giving up attachment to the fruit, continually perform the duty-action that is to be done. For it is by performing action without attachment that a person attains the Supreme.\n\nFor all these reasons, we must perform our duty-action. \"Do everything, but do it without clinging to anything. Do what must be done without thinking about its fruit, without seeking ownership of it. If you perform action in this way, it will carry you toward Bhagavān. It will never again push you into the bondage of karma. It is possible to reach Bhagavān by acting in this way, and not by renouncing action,\" says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nHere the word \"pūruṣaḥ\" is used. One who dwells in the pura (the complete body) is puruṣa. A sādhaka who makes full use of the pura is pūruṣaḥ. The jīva who, while in the body meant for sādhana, is engaged in sādhana, is pūruṣaḥ.\n\nHaving explained the doctrine of karma in this way, Kṛṣṇa gives a supporting illustration in the next verse.",
  "3.20": "karmaṇā eva hi saṃsiddhim asthitāḥ janaka ādayaḥ |\n\nlokasaṅgraham eva api sampaśyan kartum arhasi -- It was through action alone that Janaka and others, established in it, attained perfection. Considering also the welfare of the world, you ought to act.\n\nMany royal sages of the past walked this path and attained mokṣa. None of them renounced everything and went to the forest. The finest example of this is Janaka Mahārāja. He was a person who ruled his kingdom amid all its royal splendor. Yet there was no one in that age who was as great a knower as he. Such a great royal sage was he that Śukācārya himself came to Janaka for a proper understanding of renunciation! This is the life of a true sādhaka. Janaka lived detached even while surrounded by every enjoyment of power. This is why Kṛṣṇa brings up Janaka's name here. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Janaka and other royal sages attained knowledge through karma-yoga alone, and rose to great heights while performing action — not by renouncing action.\" \"You, who have become a guide to society in this way, must fight for the sake of society\" — this is what Kṛṣṇa is telling him.\n\nHaving explained duty-action in detail, Kṛṣṇa now, taking himself as the very example, explains in the verses that follow the effect on society when a leader walks a wrong path.",
  "3.21": "yat yat ācarati śreṣṭhaḥ tat tat eva itaraḥ janaḥ |\n\nsaḥ yat pramāṇam kurute lokaḥ tat anuvartate -- Whatever the best of men does, the rest of the people do that very thing. Whatever standard he sets, the world follows it.\n\nWhoever is regarded as elder in society — superior in age, in conduct, in knowledge, one capable of guiding society, a person of standing — whichever path he walks, the rest also walk that same path. Here Arjuna is a leader of society. Whatever he does, society will follow. This is because ordinary people do not understand the scriptures and such things. They only follow those who are regarded as ideal figures. Whatever the leader takes as his standard and acts upon, that is what ordinary people follow.\n\nWe see the message Kṛṣṇa gives in this verse in our daily life. If the conduct and behavior of the head running an organization is not on the right path, that organization comes to ruin. Evil forces rise up. Because whatever the one regarded as leader follows, the people under him begin, without their own awareness, to follow that as the standard. For this reason, a leader can never be too vigilant. If the head running a country is corrupt, there is a real possibility that the entire country will walk the path of corruption. Generally, human beings are more likely, and quicker, to follow the bad than to follow the good. For example, if an excellent teacher has a habit of smoking, there is a greater likelihood that his students will take his bad habit, rather than his knowledge, as their standard and go down a bad path. This is the harmful effect on society when those regarded as elders take a wrong path. This is very dangerous — we must have this awareness.",
  "3.22": "na me pārtha asti kartavyam triṣu lokeṣu kiñcana |\n\nna anavāptam avāptavyam varte eva ca karmaṇi -- O Pārtha, there is nothing whatsoever that I must do in the three worlds, nor is there anything unattained that I need to attain. Yet I remain engaged in action.",
  "3.23": "yadi hi aham na varteyam jātu karmaṇi atandritaḥ |\n\nmama vartma anuvartante manuṣyāḥ pārtha sarvaśaḥ -- O Pārtha, if I were ever not to engage vigilantly in action, human beings would follow my path in every way.",
  "3.24": "utsīdeyuḥ ime lokāḥ na kuryām karma cet aham |\n\nsaṅkarasya ca kartā syām upahanyām imāḥ prajāḥ -- If I did not perform action, all these people would go astray and be ruined. I would become the cause of the mixing of dharma, and would myself be responsible for destroying all these people.\n\nIn these three verses above, Kṛṣṇa takes himself as an example to explain what he has said earlier. Kṛṣṇa, standing as Arjuna's charioteer, says: \"There is nothing at all that I need to do, or that would cause trouble if left undone, yet I remain established in duty-action.\" Bhagavān, who performs creation, sustenance, and dissolution in the three worlds as his eternal action, does everything for the sake of his beloved devotees and for the sake of all living beings, and not for any gain of his own.\n\nIf Kṛṣṇa had not engaged himself in his eternal action, everyone would have taken him as their standard and followed his path. For this reason, when Bhagavān takes an avatāra, he appears as an ordinary human being and carries out his eternal actions, his duty-actions. Looking at Kṛṣṇa's avatāra, we see that Kṛṣṇa never failed to perform basic acts such as sandhyāvandanā. This was not for his own sake, but to guide the people who follow him.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"If I do not act, the entire world will lose its awareness of karma and go astray; license will set in and the established order will disappear.\" Whatever is the dharma appropriate to a person, that must be done. That is the order of things. Otherwise everyone will begin to act as they please. \"From this, disorder will run rampant, and we will become responsible for it,\" Kṛṣṇa says.",
  "3.25": "saktāḥ karmaṇi avidvāṃsaḥ yathā kurvanti bhārata |\n\nkuryāt vidvān tathā asaktaḥ cikīrṣuḥ loka saṅgraham -- O Bhārata, just as the unwise perform actions attached to them, so too should the wise act — but unattached, and desiring the welfare of the world.\n\nThose without understanding perform action out of desire for its fruit. But the one who understands, the wise one, should keep performing niṣkāma karma without seeking any claim on its fruit. \"I am serving Bhagavān as a guide to the generation growing up before my eyes; whatever Bhagavān gives me in return, I am content\" — action should be performed with this resolve. This is true karma-yoga.",
  "3.26": "na buddhi bhedam janayet ajñānām karma saṅginām |\n\njoṣayet sarva karmāṇi vidvān yuktaḥ samācaran -- One should not unsettle the faith of the ignorant who are attached to action for its fruit. The wise, being disciplined, should engage in all actions and encourage them along rather than disturbing them.\n\nThere are many kinds of people in society. Not everyone has understanding. They act according to their own beliefs. To one who understands, this may look like superstition. But one should never go and mock such people all at once. Someone may take a stone, give it a name, and worship it as God. His immense faith and surrender protect him. To look at this and mock it is foolishness. Such people live with confidence in life on the foundation of such faith. They have a certain eternal awareness. Is it not possible for Bhagavān, who is all-pervading, denoted by every word, and all-powerful, to protect him even through a stone? Therefore one should never laugh at such people. Instead, one should try to gradually bring them within the bounds of knowledge. Everything should be done out of social concern, but one should perform all action with detachment while filling the ignorant with knowledge.",
  "3.27": "prakṛteḥ kriyamāṇāni guṇaiḥ karmāṇi sarvaśaḥ |\n\nahaṅkāravimūḍha ātmā kartā aham iti manyate -- All actions of every kind are being carried out by the guṇas that are the limbs of prakṛti (the qualities inherent to the jīva's nature, which are subject to Bhagavān). One deluded by ego thinks, \"I have done this.\"\n\nThis verse unfolds in many ways. Here Kṛṣṇa mainly explains how wrong, and why, it is to take pride, thinking \"I did this.\" Kṛṣṇa says: \"If anyone thinks he has done something himself, that is his ignorance.\" A curious feature of creation is that no one does anything at all — and yet everything is happening through everyone's hands! This body (piṇḍāṇḍa) is a construction of prakṛti. In prakṛti there are the sense organs that are its supporting, constituent limbs. Prakṛti means this body; its guṇas mean the senses. Sound-touch-taste-form-smell; ear-skin-tongue-eye-nose; and correspondingly, space (sound), air (touch), water (taste), fire (form), and earth (smell) — these are the guṇas of prakṛti. This being so, it is meaningless for us to say we did some particular thing ourselves. For example: \"I saw with my eye\" — who gave us this eye? Can you give an eye to one born blind? That is to say, the eye is a gift of prakṛti. Bhagavān gave us the eye, placed an object before the eye, and prompted us to see — and we saw! If we happen to have a good voice, we take pride in it. But who gave us this voice? We never think about this. For any single act to occur, many components of the entire universe must come together and function. Prakṛti is inert; it has no will. \"We do\" means to act with will. We have will; every jīva has its own inherent nature. Every action we perform is in keeping with our jīva-nature. This is willed activity. We have will, but we do not have freedom of will.\n\nFreedom of will belongs only to Bhagavān. All action is carried out in keeping with that will. Therefore the one who governs our nature is Bhagavān, the root doer of the entire universe. The business of the world proceeds in keeping with his will. This is the order of the universe. We are merely one component in the karma-cycle of the entire universe. Therefore there is no meaning at all in taking pride and thinking \"I did this.\" The true doer is Bhagavān. There is one kind of agency for the jīva and another kind of agency for inert matter. We must know the difference between these. Earth is inert; it has no will. A seed is sentient, and has the nature to become a tree, but it has no freedom. The gardener who sows and grows the seed is that very Bhagavān. In keeping with Bhagavān's will, and in keeping with the inherent nature of the \"jīva,\" action takes place in the world. Behind every single action lies the participation of the entire universe.",
  "3.28": "tattvavit tu mahābāho guṇa karma vibhāgayoḥ |\n\nguṇāḥ guṇeṣu vartante iti matvā na sajjate -- But, O mighty-armed one, one who knows the truth of the distinction between the guṇas (of jīva-nature) and their actions understands that the guṇas move only among the objects of the senses, and so does not become attached to them.\n\nOne who has grasped the tattva, the one who knows reality as it is — the tattvavādin — must know the distinction of guṇa-karma, of the senses and the distinctions of action within them. Fundamentally, action proceeds in the senses through the three guṇas called sāttvika, rājasa, and tāmasa. This may be the influence of what we have clung to in the past. We must observe, with detachment, the action that results from this. This matter belongs to the domain of psychology — analyzing, with detachment, the very action we ourselves perform! When we carry out this self-analysis (Self Analysis) of which guṇa of the three guṇas is influencing us, and come to know the principle of the guṇa-karma distinction, we become aware of the influence of the three guṇas. Then we come to know the truth that we have no ownership over it. The senses — upon them the three guṇas of prakṛti — upon that the jīva's own nature — upon that the knowledge-bliss of Bhagavān — all of this is expressed as action through the senses. In inert matter there is mere action (Action); in the jīva there is will together with action (Intentional Action); and in Bhagavān there is independent, willed action (Independent Intentional Action) — this is the distinction of karma. One who has grasped both the distinction of guṇa and the distinction of karma in this way is \"tattvavit.\" When this truth is understood, the ego of \"I have done this\" never troubles us again.\n\nTo look at the above with an example — consider a speaker who talks in an interesting, substance-rich way. For him to speak like this, it is not enough merely to have a mouth; sound must come from the mouth. Not just any sound — the sound appropriate to what is to be said must come. For that sound to come, speech needs a feeling behind it. That feeling must arise, flash, in the mind. In keeping with the feeling that has arisen, the appropriate sound must come. That sound must emerge clearly through the mouth. For sound to arise from feeling, space and air are needed as the medium. Only if all this activity proceeds smoothly can he speak well. Who is it that causes the feeling and the sound to arise in the mind? What if the sound appropriate to what one wishes to say does not arise? What if some particular switch in our brain does not function at that moment? What role do we have in this? When this is understood, there is no room for ego. With this awareness, one can be with everyone, like everyone, without becoming attached.\n\nIn the senses, the sattva-rajo-tamo guṇas work in keeping with the jīva's own nature. Bhagavān's nature, which is knowledge and bliss, governs the guṇas of the jīva's cognitive senses. When this is understood, the sense of \"I\" and \"mine\" departs. One comes to know the truth that, in this vast ocean called the universe, one is but a single grain of sand. One who has recognized the all-facing existence of the universe never says \"I did this,\" nor does he become attached (Attachment) to anything.\n\nIn this verse Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as \"Mahābāho.\" On the surface, Mahābāhu means a hero, a warrior. The sentiment visible here is: you, of mighty arms, should fight without being a coward. But beyond this there is yet another meaning. \"Mahat vahatīti mahābāhu\" — meaning, one who bears something great. It means one who carries the fundamental truth of the world in his heart. \"Grasp, with the great arm of awareness capable of comprehending the great matter of the world, the truth (Bhagavān) that lies beyond the horizon\" — this is another sentiment behind this form of address.",
  "3.29": "prakṛteḥ guṇa sammūḍhāḥ sajjante guṇakarmasu |\n\ntān akṛtsnavidaḥ mandān kṛtsnavit na vicālayet -- Those deluded by the guṇas of prakṛti become attached to the actions of the guṇas. One who knows the whole truth should not unsettle those simple people who do not know it in full.\n\nThose immersed in sensory pleasure, which is a product of the guṇas of prakṛti; those under the delusion of the three guṇas of root prakṛti, who, not knowing the truth, remain in delusion; those without the awareness that they themselves are subject to Bhagavān's guṇas — these Kṛṣṇa calls \"prakṛter guṇa sammūḍhāḥ.\" Such people live under the delusion that Bhagavān's own guṇa and karma are their own, thinking \"everything is I, everything is by me.\" Such people have no fitness to know the truth or the whole. Those who have \"known the whole\" should not go about unsettling them. If they have the fitness to know the truth within them, they will come out of that state on their own. No one can instill a fitness that is not there; no one can take away a fitness that is there. One may join and keep company with those who have the desire to know the truth, and learn from them or explain it to them.",
  "3.30": "mayi sarvāṇi karmāṇi sannyasya adhyātma cetasā |\n\nnirāśīḥ nirmamaḥ bhūtvā yudhyasva vigata jvaraḥ -- Offering all your actions to me, with your mind fixed on the Self, becoming free of longing and free of the sense of \"mine,\" cast off your fever, and fight.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"Perform all the action you do, offering it to me.\" Here the word \"sannyasya\" is used. Everything I have done is not really done by me. Whatever action came about through me was made to happen by Bhagavān through my own hands. The attitude of surrender that says, \"whatever took place through me is the worship of Bhagavān,\" is sannyāsa. \"Nāhaṃ kartā hari kartā; hari kartā hi kevalam\" — \"Let whatever comes from my mouth be your praise. Let everything that takes place through my mind be your remembrance. Cultivate the thought that everything is your worship — even while fighting a war,\" says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nHere the war has been decided upon collectively. About thirteen years earlier, when Dharmarāya performed the Rājasūya sacrifice, Śiśupāla's head was severed there. At that time Vyāsa told Dharmarāya: \"This is a portent of the great war that will take place about thirteen years from now.\" That is to say, this war is fated. Here Arjuna is merely an instrument (Instrument). Therefore Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna: \"Fix your mind on Bhagavān, give up longing and the sense of possession, and fight free of anxiety.\"\n\nOur very life is a struggle. In this struggle, we must perform the work we do honestly. Whatever is to happen will happen regardless. It is not in our power to prevent it. \"Bhagavān had this task assigned to me carried out through my own hands; let this be offered to him, let it be his worship. Whatever fruit came of it is Bhagavān's prasāda.\" When one carries out one's work with this resolve, Bhagavān bears the responsibility for whatever happens. There is no greater worship than this. This is the message for life that Kṛṣṇa has given us through the Gītā. As if to state the fruit of this teaching, the next two verses follow.",
  "3.31": "ye me matam idam nityam anutiṣṭhanti mānavāḥ |\n\nśraddhāvantaḥ anasūyantaḥ mucyante te api karmabhiḥ -- Those human beings who, without envy, faithfully live by this teaching of mine are freed even from the bonds of karma.",
"3.32": "ye tu etat abhyasūyantaḥ na anutiṣṭhanti me matam |\n\nsarva jñāna vimūḍhān tān viddhi naṣṭān acetasaḥ -- But those who, out of envy, do not put this teaching of mine into practice — know them to be deluded in all knowledge, senseless, and heading toward ruin.\n\n\"Bhagavān is making me do everything through my own hands; whatever I do is his worship; what is good for me in terms of fruit is known better to Bhagavān than to me. Therefore, without seeking any claim over the fruit, to accept whatever comes as Bhagavān's prasāda and live by it\" — this should become the manner of our daily practice in life. Human beings (those who are wise, who possess true humanity) who hold this contemplation in every moment of life, giving up disbelief (rājasic) and envy (tāmasic), performing niṣkāma karma, find their knowledge growing. Thereby they become fit to shed the bondage of karma and proceed to mokṣa. When this contemplation is present in daily practice, our very action becomes the step by which we free ourselves from the bondage of karma, and it carries us across that bondage.\n\nThose who, out of envy, do not put this teaching of mine into practice are deprived of all understanding. Know them to be senseless fools heading toward ruin — those who, without knowing the practice described above, out of envy and impatience, hold the view \"I alone am responsible for my own work, I act for my own self-interest, I shall receive its fruit, my existence is in my own hands, no god is responsible for it,\" and live with such arrogance — for them the door of awareness never opens. Such a person is regarded in the world as vimūḍha (a supreme fool). He does not even know that he knows nothing! Such people have no fitness to reflect on the truth. They are the senseless fools who move toward ruin, going from darkness to darkness, reaching utter degradation and losing their very existence.\n\nWhen we hear this message of Kṛṣṇa's above, a question arises in us: why does not everyone follow this fundamental truth of the world? Kṛṣṇa gives a very fascinating answer to this in the next two verses.",
"3.33": "sadṛśam ceṣṭate svasyāḥ prakṛteḥ jñānavān api |\n\nprakṛtim yānti bhūtāni nigrahaḥ kim kariṣyati -- Even the person of knowledge acts in keeping with his own nature. All beings follow their own nature — what will suppression accomplish?\n\nHowever much anyone may be taught, it is never possible for everyone in the whole world to follow one and the same path. The reason for this is our own conduct, our own saṃskāra, and our own jīva-nature. If we look at our past saṃskāra, many experiences — sāttvika, rājasa, tāmasa — from birth after birth remain in our subtle consciousness. This jīva, having flowed through many births, carries the saṃskāra of the experiences of many lives. Even looking at a single birth, the imprint of the environment in which one grew up, this influence, always remains upon us. Besides this, every individual has his own particular jīva-nature. He always acts in keeping with it. Just as a chili plant cannot bear sweet fruit, it is impossible to change a person's jīva-nature. Life is this mixture of nature and influence.\n\nLooking at the above analysis, a question naturally arises for us: \"The jīva's conduct follows its nature; action follows its nature; everything happens in keeping with nature. Then why should one make any effort at all?\" The answer is: it is through constant effort that one can come out from under the influence of saṃskāra or environment. Until one stands firm in one's true, natural disposition, continuous effort to free oneself from that influence is necessary. Continuous spiritual sādhana helps to express our true, natural disposition.",
      "3.34": "indriyasya indriyasya arthe rāga dveṣau vyavasthita |\n\ntayoḥ na vaśam agacchet tau hi asya paripanthinau -- In the object of each and every sense, fondness and hostility have taken root. One must not fall prey to them; they alone are the seeker's foes.\n\nTwo things work as our enemies in our senses. They are: rāga (liking, attachment (Attachment)) and the desire that says \"I want\" (kāma, desire (Desire)). Liking becomes rāga, and from rāga comes kāma. Not liking becomes dveṣa, and from dveṣa comes anger. We desired an object, we did not get it — then anger. Someone else got it — then hatred. We got it, but it is not as we imagined — then anger. Fundamentally, the great fault that exists is desire. The more we desire something, the more we come to grief over it. It is not possible to rise above this without limiting desire. In each of our senses, rāga-dveṣa and kāma-krodha are present in full. But we must never fall under the sway of these likes and dislikes, these attractions and aversions. Excessive attachment that says \"this is mine\" (possessiveness (Possessiveness)) carries us down to ruin. The feeling that \"I want this, let it not go to anyone else\" fills every sense. This is the ego (Ego) that lies behind our tendencies. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Never become a slave to these likes and dislikes; they are an obstruction on the path of your sādhana. They are your true enemies.\"\n\nWe must not fall prey to these enemies, and must analyze our own senses ourselves. What is my sense desiring, why is it desiring it, what selfishness or hatred lies behind it, is this a desire that will lead me astray or one that will uplift me? Reflecting in this way, we must purify our desires. As the Veda says, \"as flour is separated with a sieve\" — we must purify our desires with the sieve that is our mind. Then there, the bond of friendship remains, and there Lakṣmī always dwells.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna: even while fighting, do not hold rāga-dveṣa in your mind; fight only with the detachment of one engaged in a struggle between right and wrong! That is, do not rejoice when Duryodhana dies; do not grieve when your own son Abhimanyu dies — this is the pre-war counsel here.",
  "3.35": "śreyān sva dharmaḥ viguṇaḥ paradharmāt su anuṣṭhitāt |\n\nsvadharme nidhanam śreyaḥ paradharmaḥ bhaya avahaḥ -- Better is one's own dharma, even imperfectly performed, than another's dharma well performed. Death in one's own dharma is better; another's dharma brings danger.\n\nAs said earlier, every jīva has its own particular nature, and it cannot be changed. In keeping with the jīva's nature is our dharma — that is svadharma. This is natural activity. We must do whatever is our own natural disposition, and nothing else. For this reason, parents should not impose their own preferences on their children, but should recognize the children's true nature and shape their future in keeping with it. Otherwise it amounts to ruining their future. A child's talent lies in his own natural disposition. Each person must carry out work in keeping with his own jīva-nature, and not by following someone else's dharma. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"Even if there is deficiency in the performance of your own dharma, it is superior to performing another's dharma.\" However perfectly one performs another's dharma, no good comes of it; it is repugnant, or dangerous.\n\nHere, fighting is Arjuna's own natural dharma. For him to abandon it and go to the forest to practice austerities is contrary to his own svadharma. Therefore, Kṛṣṇa is telling him: uphold your svadharma, and fight, giving up rāga and dveṣa.",
  "3.36": "arjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna asked:\n\natha kena prayuktaḥ ayam pāpam carati pūruṣaḥ |\n\nanicchan api vārṣṇeya balāt iva niyojitaḥ -- O Vārṣṇeya, a person commits sin even without wishing to, as though impelled by some force. By whose prompting does this happen?\n\nHaving heard Kṛṣṇa's teaching, Arjuna raises a very important question that comes to all of us. \"Even knowing sin from merit, even knowing something to be an act one should not do, a person sometimes still does wrong — why is this? Why do we do wrong even while knowing right from wrong?\" ",
  "3.37": "bhagavān uvāca -- Bhagavān said:\n\nkāmaḥ eṣaḥ krodhaḥ eṣaḥ rajaḥ guṇa samudbhavaḥ |\n\nmahā aśanaḥ mahāpāpmā viddhi enam iha vairiṇam -- It is this very desire (kāma), which becomes wrath (krodha); it is born of the rajo-guṇa. It is a great glutton and a great sinner; know this to be your enemy here.\n\nKāma (desire) is the power that impels sin — an offshoot of Kālanemi, the presiding spirit of desire; its own transformation is anger. Its birth is from rajo-guṇa. It is a glutton who is never satisfied however much it eats. It is the home of great sins. Know it to be an enemy on this path of sādhana.\n\nThe root cause of everything is the desire within us. Some desire keeps tormenting us, and within us there is the prompting that this desire can be fulfilled through wrongdoing. The one who makes us do this is Kālanemi, the evil power that presides over desire; when Kālanemi comes and settles within us, we do wrong without our own awareness. Fundamentally, there are two things behind a person doing wrong: desires, and anger when a desire is not fulfilled. It is hard even to say how much wrong a person does in a fit of anger.\n\nWe do wrong and finally repent. The root of this anger is desire. There is no anger without desire. Behind all anger lies a meaningless desire.\n\nThe rajo-guṇa within a person nurtures rāga-dveṣa. However much food you feed it, its stomach is never full! A fine example of this: a person wanders about looking for work; at that time he holds the view that it would be enough if he got some work for his stomach's sake. Once he gets work, his desire flows toward earning more salary. When he gets more, he wants still more — and so it keeps growing. There is never any satisfaction.\n\nIf we habituate ourselves to doing wrong in this way, it becomes \"mahāśana\" (the great glutton). However much feast you offer it, it says \"more\" again — to the point that it makes us do work we should never do. One who has fallen to the resolve of fulfilling his own desire, says the scripture, does not even shrink from killing a brāhmaṇa! Therefore our first enemy is rāga-dveṣa. We must be vigilant about it. Let it remain within you, but let it not rule you. This is what is said in the Qur'an: \"Drive them out from where they drove you out.\" Rāga and dveṣa plunder our personality and rule over us — drive them out, and live as yourself.",
  "3.38": "dhūmena āvriyate vahniḥ yathā ādarśaḥ malena ca |\n\nyathā ulbena āvṛtaḥ garbhaḥ tathā tena idam āvṛtam -- As fire is covered by smoke, as a mirror is covered by dust, as an embryo is covered by the womb, so is this (knowledge) covered by desire.\n\nAs smoke covers fire, so does kāma cover Bhagavān; as dust ruins a mirror, so it covers the mind; as the womb covers the embryo, so it covers the jīva of the wicked — with all this, kāma covers everything. (Kāma covers the good the way smoke covers fire; it covers the middling the way dust ruins a mirror; it covers the wicked the way the womb covers the embryo.)\n\nKṛṣṇa gives here a beautiful illustration of how rāga-dveṣa blind our eyes. The fire that illumines everything, itself shines and illumines everything else. When smoke fills this fire, it cannot be seen and does not shine. Bhagavān is like the fire. Our rajo-guṇa is like the smoke. Between us and Bhagavān, the veil of rajo-guṇa is like the smoke wrapped around the fire. This is the illustration Kṛṣṇa gives for the sāttvika person. For the rājasic, the illustration Kṛṣṇa gives is a mirror smeared with soot. The mind, which is like a mirror, is smeared with the grime called rajo-guṇa, says Kṛṣṇa. For the tāmasic, Kṛṣṇa's illustration is the embryo within the womb — all is darkness there; even with eyes open, one cannot see, just as the little creature called the jīva is kept as though within an egg called rajo-guṇa.\n\nThus, as smoke covers fire, so this rajo-guṇa is a covering over Bhagavān; as soot smeared on a mirror, so this rajo-guṇa is a covering over the inner faculty (antaḥkaraṇa). When the antaḥkaraṇa does not function, Bhagavān cannot be seen; then, for the jīva, as \"in the embryo,\" everything is darkness. Though everything is present, nothing is of use, and it all goes to waste.",
  "3.39": "āvṛtam jñānam etena jñāninaḥ nityavairiṇā |\n\nkāma rūpeṇa kaunteya duṣpūreṇa analena ca -- O Kaunteya, the knowledge of even the wise is covered by this eternal enemy in the form of desire — insatiable as fire, and never filled.\n\nThis veil of rajo-guṇa stands as an obstruction, preventing the knowledge already gained from being of use to one who has knowledge, and preventing knowledge from arising for one who lacks it. Rāga-dveṣa is our bitter enemy. It is so terrible that it may be called \"the fire of desire.\" However much is fed to it, it never says enough. Kṛṣṇa says: you, the son of a great mother like Kuntī, should know this enemy of knowledge — kāma (desire), O Kaunteya.\n\nHere we have come to know that our true enemy is our kāma, or desire, or rāga-dveṣa. Then where does this enemy reside? Kṛṣṇa explains this in the next verse.",
  "3.40": "indriyāṇi manaḥ buddhiḥ asya adhiṣṭhānam ucyate |\n\netaiḥ vimohayati eṣaḥ jñānam āvṛtya dehinam -- The senses, the mind, and the intellect are said to be its seats. Through these it deludes the embodied one, covering his knowledge.\n\nThe garland of our desires first comes and settles in our senses. To see with the eye, to hear with the ear, to smell with the nose, to touch with the body — thus each desire comes and settles, in its own form, in one or another sense. If we do not restrain it there, it seizes our mind. If we let it loose there too, it goes on to seize our intellect. Thus this enemy, coming from the courtyard (the senses), is called into the porch (the mind), and then seated in our inner chamber (the intellect). Once this enemy has seized our intellect, it is very hard afterward to remove it. In this state, desire settles within us as a doctrine and begins to rule over us. Because of this, kāma becomes our very way of life. For example, some matter falls upon our ear. We do not stop to think what use this is to us, why we should pay attention to it. Instead, we long to see what we have heard. Having seen it, the desire arises to touch it (to eat it, to smell it, and so on)! In this way we encourage this enemy called desire in all our senses. Then it slowly seizes our mind. The mind begins to think continuously about that matter. When the mind is thinking, \"it must become mine, it must become mine\" — that means this enemy has seized the mind. From here it moves further, to the intellect: \"I cannot live without it, whatever happens I must obtain it\" — such thoughts. This is the sign that desire has seized our intellect. This is the herald of complete ruin. Thus, sitting in these three places where knowledge becomes deranged, this enemy of ours stands as a veil over knowledge.\n\nHere the word \"dehinam\" is used. Our body is a means by which we can be carried, through knowledge, to a higher state. But when we keep the company of this enemy called desire, it becomes the means that pushes us down into degradation. This is the sense behind this word.\n\nHaving thus given an explanation of who our enemy is, where he resides, and how he attacks us, Kṛṣṇa next explains the way to conquer this enemy.",
  "3.41": "tasmāt tvam indriyāṇi ādau niyamya bharata ṛṣabha |\n\npāpmānam prajahi hi enam jñāna vijñāna nāśanam -- Therefore, O bull among the Bharatas, first restrain the senses, and slay this sinful thing that destroys knowledge and discernment.\n\nWe must control such a great enemy at the very first stage. This enemy must be controlled at its first gateway of entry — the senses themselves. \"Drive out this great sinner, who can ruin our knowledge (through hearing) and our vijñāna (reflection and contemplation), and destroy our power of thought,\" says Kṛṣṇa. But how? Who will help with this? Listen further!",
  "3.42": "indriyāṇi parāṇi āhuḥ indriyebhyaḥ param manaḥ |\n\nmanasaḥ tu parā buddhiḥ yaḥ buddheḥ parataḥ tu saḥ -- The senses, they say, are higher [than the body] (the presiding deities of the senses, such as Indra, are the higher gods). Higher than the senses is the mind (the mind's presiding deity, Rudra, is higher than the deities presiding over the senses). Higher than the mind is the intellect (Sarasvatī, presiding over the intellect, is higher than the deity presiding over the mind). And higher even than the intellect is that Supreme Reality (Bhagavān, who is beyond the grasp even of the deity presiding over the intellect, is the highest reality).",
  "3.43": "evam buddheḥ param buddhvā saṃstabhya ātmānam ātmanā |\n\njahi śatrum mahābāho kāmarūpam durāsadam -- O mighty-armed one, having thus known that Reality beyond the intellect, and having steadied the self by the self, slay this enemy in the form of desire, so hard for the ordinary person to overcome.\n\nJust as there are evil powers, so too are there divine powers who wish us well and stand for our protection. Every sense has a presiding deity. The presiding deity of our senses is Indra. The presiding deity of the mind is Śiva-Pārvatī; the presiding deity of the intellect is Sarasvatī. Beyond the intellect is the Self — and that itself is the seat of Bhagavān.\n\nWe must surrender to the divine power that governs our senses, mind, and intellect. Then, with the help of those divine powers, we can gain knowledge and behold Bhagavān. Each of our senses has its own presiding deity: the sun for the eye, the moon for the ear, fire for the mouth, Varuṇa for the tongue, the Aśvin gods for the nose, Indra for the hand (he is also the lord of all the senses), Upendra, Indra's son, for the foot, and Yama for the organs of excretion and Dakṣa for the organ of procreation. In this way every deity helps us to be freed from our enemy. Thus, praying to the deities and receiving their help, we should move away from the evil power and reach that Supreme Reality beyond all else.\n\nHaving thus known that Reality beyond the reach of the senses, mind, and intellect, tying the reins of the intellect onto the restless horse of the mind, placing that rein in the hands of Bhagavān, and with the help of all the deities, defeating the demon called Kālanemi step by step, one should reach Bhagavān — this is what Kṛṣṇa says. If we recall the explanation given here whenever an evil desire arises in us, we can prevent the demon called Kālanemi from slipping into us. This is because not all desires are bad. One needs the desire to reach Bhagavān. Therefore, surrendering our intellect into the hands of Bhagavān, fighting against evil desires with the help of the deities, we can attain mokṣa. One caution is needed here: it is impossible to fight this evil power all at once and win. It must be won step by step. At various stages of sādhana, various deities help us.\n\nThus ends the third chapter.",
  "4.1": "bhagavān uvāca -- Bhagavān said:\n\nimam vivasvate yogam proktavān aham avyayam |\n\nvivasvān manave prāha manuḥ ikṣvākave abravīt -- I taught this imperishable path of sādhana to the Sun. The Sun told it to Manu; Manu told it to Ikṣvāku.",
  "4.2": "evam paramparā prāptam imam rāja ṛṣayaḥ viduḥ |\n\nsaḥ kālena iha mahatā yogaḥ naṣṭaḥ parantapa -- Thus handed down in succession, the wise royal sages knew this. O scorcher of foes, after a long time, that path of yoga was lost from this world.",
  "4.3": "saḥ eva ayam mayā te adya yogaḥ proktaḥ purātanaḥ |\n\nbhaktaḥ asi me sakhā ca iti rahasyaṃ hi etat uttamam -- That very same ancient yoga I have taught you today, because you are my devotee and friend. This is a most excellent secret.\n\nIn these three verses above, Kṛṣṇa has given us a picture of how this tradition of knowledge developed from the beginning of creation. In truth, the root of knowledge is the Vedas. A text was composed gathering the essence of the Vedas, compiling the meaning of the entire Vedas — it was called the Pañcarātra. The Pañcarātra is the text that forms the combined embodiment of the jñāna-yoga and karma-yoga of Vedic literature. The Bhagavad Gītā is itself the essential compilation of that Pañcarātra. At the beginning of creation, in the Svāyambhuva manvantara, Bhagavān taught the Pañcarātra to the four-faced one. Thus, at the very beginning of the world's creation, the knowledge of the Pañcarātra came to the four-faced one and to the gods. In the Vaivasvata manvantara, the Sun taught it to Manu, and Manu taught it to Ikṣvāku. This is exactly what Kṛṣṇa is explaining here to Arjuna.\n\nThe gods received this knowledge from Bhagavān first of all. Among these gods is the Sun. The Sun is the deity who connects the earth and the gods. From him this knowledge flowed to Manu, the presiding deity of the Vaivasvata manvantara. Manu told this to his son Ikṣvāku, the great royal sage who lived as a universal emperor on this earth. Thus this knowledge flowed from the gods down to the earth.\n\nKnowledge is beginningless and eternal. It is imperishable learning. At the beginning of every creation, this learning is spoken by Bhagavān. Then it passes to the gods, from the gods to the royal sages, then to the sages — thus the tradition of knowledge grows. Today, this learning that has flowed down in this way is vanishing; people are forgetting it. Even the great knower Arjuna has forgotten this knowledge. As said before, Arjuna was a great knower of that era. Yet it does not come to his memory. Here Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as 'Parantapa.' Parantapa means one who is always able to view Bhagavān through the eye of knowledge. 'You are one of direct (aparokṣa) knowledge; that very tradition of knowledge exists within you; yet doubt has come to you. This beginningless, eternal tradition of knowledge is vanishing even within you — what then of the rest?' This is the sentiment carried in this address, 'Parantapa.'\n\nKnowledge has not been destroyed, but the veil of delusion has obstructed it. Therefore I am now telling you that knowledge again, says Kṛṣṇa. Recognizing the immense respect, devotion, and intimacy Arjuna holds for Kṛṣṇa, Kṛṣṇa reminds Arjuna of this beginningless, eternal, and rare knowledge. Arjuna's devotion is of the sakhya (friendship) kind, one of the nine forms of devotion (śravaṇa, kīrtana, smaraṇa, pādasevana, arcana, vandana, dāsya, sakhya, and ātmanivedana). He worshiped Bhagavān as a friend. For this reason Kṛṣṇa says here: 'You are my devotee and friend. For that reason I am telling you this priceless knowledge.'\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa has said, 'I am telling you a secret knowledge.' There are two reasons for keeping knowledge secret. One is its misuse, and the other is its being put to no use at all. It is useless to give knowledge to those who, having received it, will not pass it on to their generation; this way the tradition of knowledge cannot continue to flow. As for misuse: knowledge exists for the uplift of our own inner self and to show the path of uplift to others, and not for conducting business or trade. Knowledge can be used to deceive society. Those without understanding can be cheated and defrauded by another's cleverness. For all these reasons, knowledge is a matter of secrecy. In the past, knowers were aware of learning that could vanish through the attainment of yogic perfection. The misuse of such priceless learning is very easy. For that reason it was kept secret. We once had the knowledge of understanding the language of animals too. This too was kept secret. Thus, whenever there is a possibility of knowledge being misused, it must be kept secret. Kṛṣṇa says he is telling this secret matter, \"having chosen you specifically.\"",
  "4.4": "arjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna said:\n\naparam bhavataḥ janma param janma vivasvataḥ |\n\nkatham etat vijānīyām tvam ādau proktavān iti -- Your birth is recent; the Sun was born first. How am I to understand this, that you spoke it first?\n\nWhile explaining the tradition of knowledge, Kṛṣṇa said, \"I first taught it to the Sun (to the gods).\" Here Arjuna questions Kṛṣṇa about this. In age, Kṛṣṇa is about six months older than Arjuna. This being so, \"you say you told the Sun crores upon crores of years ago\" — how is Arjuna to understand this? This is Arjuna's question.\n\nHere Arjuna has a desire to receive the answer to this question from Bhagavān himself. That is why he has raised this question. Not only that, Arjuna, the great knower standing as the representative of all mankind, raises this question on behalf of all of us. This was already touched upon at the very beginning. Kṛṣṇa had said: \"We all existed before, we exist now, and we will exist hereafter\" (Ch. 2, verse 12) — its full explanation comes here. Kṛṣṇa answers, in the verses that follow, the question of why Bhagavān, who has no bondage of karma whatsoever and is all-capable, descends to the earth at all.",
  "4.5": "bhagavān uvāca -- Bhagavān said.\n\nbahūni me vyatītāni janmāni tava ca arjuna |\n\ntāni aham veda sarvāṇi na tvam vettha parantapa -- O Arjuna, many births have passed for me, and for you as well. O scorcher of foes, I know them all — but you do not know.\n\nJanma means birth or being born. What does not exist does not take birth; we do not say that inert matter is \"born.\" Birth is the appearance of the subtle body within the gross body. Dying means the subtle body abandoning the gross body. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"Both you and I have had many births. I know them, but you, the scorcher of foes, do not know this matter.\"",
  "4.6": "ajaḥ api san avyaya ātmā bhūtānām īśvaraḥ api san |\n\nprakṛtim svām adhiṣṭhāya sambhavāmi ātmamāyayā -- I have no birth. My body too has no death. I am the lord of the lords of all beings. Yet, entering the body made of my own prakṛti, I come forth by my own knowledge-nature (in keeping with my own inherent nature, by my own will).\n\nBhagavān, the lord of all living beings, descends to earth many times. He has no birth, yet he takes birth. He is avyaya (imperishable); he is not born bearing a perishable body. Therefore Bhagavān has no birth or death through a body. The word \"ātmamāyayā\" used here has many meanings. Ātma-māyā is the veil of delusion that covers the jīva; it is also Bhagavān's own glory; it is also Bhagavān's own will and knowledge. Bhagavān's descent to earth is by the glory of his own knowledge, not because he is subject to some māyā. Bhagavān, in order to fulfill the yearning of his devotees, descends by his own will, using as a medium the very prakṛti he himself has created. This is his glory. To us, living under the veil of delusion, this appears to be an ordinary birth, because we cannot perceive his body, which is of the nature of knowledge and bliss. We do not even have awareness of our own jīva-nature — so how can we know Bhagavān? For this reason, Bhagavān appears to us as though he has a body made of the five elements. As we know, some animals cannot perceive color; their eyes lack that capacity, so everything appears to them in black and white. But in reality color exists in the world — those animals simply lack the power to perceive it. In the same way, to us who live behind the veil of delusion, Bhagavān's body of the nature of knowledge and bliss is not visible; instead, a body made of the five elements appears to be born and destroyed. But fundamentally, Bhagavān has neither birth nor destruction. He comes forth by his own inherent nature, by his own will.\n\nFor what reason does Bhagavān descend to earth? What is the divine resolve behind it? The answer to this question can be found in the next two verses.",
  "4.7": "yadā yadā hi dharmasya glāniḥ bhavati bhārata |\n\nabhyutthānam adharmasya tadā ātmānam sṛjāmi aham -- O Bhārata, whenever dharma declines and adharma raises its head, I create myself.\n\nWhenever, in the whole world, dharma becomes tainted and, defeated before adharma, stands with its head bowed down, then I descend to earth, says Kṛṣṇa. Here the defeat of dharma does not refer to a matter concerning one household or one country. It is when a situation arises in which dharma finds no footing anywhere in the whole world that the avatāra of Bhagavān occurs. When evil powers gain hold over the earth and prevent the quality of sattva from raising its head, Bhagavān creates himself upon the earth. In the phrase \"tadātmānaṃ sṛjāmyaham,\" there are two resonances: \"I send my own dear ones\" and \"I myself incarnate.\" When the signs of dharma's defeat appear regionally, Bhagavān sends those dear to him (the gods) to earth. They come as teacher-figures and correct society. When even such an arrangement collapses and tāmasic powers run rampant, Bhagavān finally takes an avatāra himself. Looking at the state of things at the time of Kṛṣṇa's avatāra makes clear what the condition of dharma was in that era. Jarāsandha held about 22,800 princes from every corner of the world captive in his prison. He had given his daughter in marriage to Kaṃsa, incited him, and through his cunning had succeeded in having Śūrasena thrown into prison. His ally, Narakāsura, held about 16,100 princesses captive. Jarāsandha had made a peace pact with Bhīṣmācārya and had devised a scheme to establish sole sovereignty over the entire earth while plundering the people. The India of that time was not like the India of today; it was an extremely vast landmass. Had Kṛṣṇa's avatāra not occurred, the entire earth would have fallen into the hands of these sinners. It was in such a situation that Kṛṣṇa incarnated. Kṛṣṇa killed Kaṃsa and restored Śūrasena as king; he killed Narakāsura and freed the princesses under him. When these freed princesses, fearing a society that would question their honor, felt afraid, Kṛṣṇa gave them refuge, married them himself, and protected their honor. When Jarāsandha attacked Kṛṣṇa with about 23 akṣauhiṇīs of army, Kṛṣṇa feigned flight, and later met Jarāsandha together with Bhīma and Arjuna; in the wrestling match that took place there, he arranged for Jarāsandha to be slain by Bhīma, and sent all the princes held captive by him back to their respective kingdoms.\n\nThus it is only when the entire earth is on the verge of being enslaved by adharma that Bhagavān's avatāra takes place upon the earth.",
  "4.8": "paritrāṇāya sādhūnām vināśāya ca duṣkṛtām |\n\ndharma saṃsthāpana arthāya sambhavāmi yuge yuge -- To protect the good, to destroy the wicked, and to establish dharma, I come forth age after age.\n\nFor the complete destruction of those who do wrong to society and flourish by profiting from it, for the full protection of the good and of the world, and to sow the seed of dharma, Bhagavān incarnates upon the earth. Here \"yuge yuge\" means \"age after age.\" It does not mean that Bhagavān incarnates in every single age. He incarnates only when the situation described above arises. There may be many avatāras in one age, and there may be no avatāra at all in some other age. But the many great persons in whom Bhagavān's manifestation exists come and go age after age. Through them, Bhagavān's protection of dharma continues without cease, age after age.",
  "4.9": "janma karma ca me divyam evam yaḥ vetti tattvataḥ |\n\ntyaktvā deham punaḥ janma na eti mām eti saḥ arjuna -- One who thus knows, in true reality, the divine nature of my birth and action, does not, on leaving the body, take birth again — Arjuna, he attains me.\n\nThus, if one comes to know, exactly as it is (tattvataḥ), the true nature of Bhagavān's knowledge-blissful, divine, sportive (līlāmaya) avatāra, it can show us the path to mokṣa. This is a precious knowledge needed to attain a mokṣa that has no further rebirth. Kṛṣṇa now explains further about those who have attained siddhi by this foundation.",
  "4.10": "vīta rāga bhaya krodhāḥ mat mayā mām upāśritāḥ |\n\nbahavaḥ jñāna tapasā pūtāḥ mat bhāvam āgatāḥ -- Those who have given up attachment, fear, and anger, who see me alone everywhere (who have recognized my greatness), who have taken refuge in me — many such, purified by the tapas of knowledge, have attained my nature.\n\nAs Kṛṣṇa said at the end of the third chapter (verse 39), rāga-dveṣa and the like are our supreme enemies. We must give up attachment/desire (rāga, Attachment), fear (bhaya), and anger (krodha) — the cause of all the problems of our life — and cultivate the disposition of being content with whatever we have, and of joyfully accepting whatever comes, and become man-maya (filled with Me). \"Manmaya\" means filling every course of our life with Bhagavān and reducing our worldly consciousness. Once we engage in contemplation of Bhagavān, this rāga-dveṣa begins to seem trivial. Knowing the truth that Bhagavān is supreme above all, knowing that he is always present for our protection, casting away false pride, those who have surrendered to that Bhagavān find the path to mokṣa. Cultivating this kind of disposition in every single action of our life is a tapas. Through this, our life becomes sanctified. Thus, those who have become free of the filth of rāga-dveṣa reach me, says Kṛṣṇa. We must seat Bhagavān even within our rāga, dveṣa, and krodha! Our desire should be the desire to see Bhagavān; our fear should be the fear that \"if I do wrong, Bhagavān will not forgive me\"; our anger should be directed at our own unwarranted sensory cravings. When we have turned these three enemies toward Bhagavān in this way, it becomes possible to behold him.",
  "4.11": "ye yathā mām prapadyante tān tathā eva bhajāmi aham |\n\nmama vartma anuvartante manuṣyāḥ pārtha sarvaśaḥ -- In whatever way people take refuge in me, in that same way I favor them. O Pārtha, in whatever path human beings walk, they ultimately come to me.\n\nWhat is the correct path for the worship of God? Different people worship in different ways. This causes confusion and agitation. In whatever manner one worships, Bhagavān favors accordingly. In whatever form we worship, it reaches Bhagavān, who is of universal form (viśvarūpa). This is why we worship him in forms such as tulasī, the aśvattha tree, the cow, and so on, in which his special vibhūti resides. But one caution is needed here: the important thing is not to believe the tulasī plant itself to be Bhagavān and worship it as such, but to worship it knowing that Bhagavān's special vibhūti resides within the tulasī. Thus, in each of our actions, we need awareness and knowledge of Bhagavān. We need the awareness that whatever name we call Bhagavān by, it is a name of that Bhagavān who is denoted by every word. Wherever we sit and remember Bhagavān with a fully surrendered heart, it reaches the universal Bhagavān who pervades and sustains the universe. He always protects us.",
  "4.12": "kāṅkṣantaḥ karmaṇām siddhim yajante iha devatāḥ |\n\nkṣipram hi mānuṣe loke siddhiḥ bhavati karmajā -- Those who desire the fruit of their actions worship the gods here, for in this human world the fruit born of action is swiftly obtained.\n\nSome of us, not knowing the distinction between the gods and Bhagavān, worship the gods themselves as Bhagavān. This is a mistaken notion we hold about the gods. God is not many — he is one; but the gods who are subject to Bhagavān are many. Out of a desire for immediate results, we have developed the custom of forgetting Bhagavān and worshiping the assembly of gods. This may yield results, but they are short-lived. Worshiping various gods for various worldly pleasures, out of a desire for earthly happiness, cannot carry us toward eternal mokṣa. Worship performed for sensory enjoyment cannot be called worship at all. The gods can help us along the path of mokṣa, but awareness of the all-powerful Bhagavān is of great importance.\n\nWhatever form we use to worship Bhagavān, it is a matter of the utmost importance in spiritual sādhana that we worship not for worldly enjoyment, but with the contemplation of reaching that all-pervading Bhagavān. You may call your God Kṛṣṇa, or you may call him Allah — that is fine. But let the truth be known that the power that protects and sustains us all is one and the same. Bhagavān may be worshiped in various forms. But worshiping various gods as God for the sake of various pleasures cannot be spiritual sādhana. It is not wrong to see Bhagavān through the gods or within the gods, because Bhagavān is all-pervading; there is no place where he is not. But if, setting this aside, one worships the gods themselves as Bhagavān, even the gods themselves would not approve of it.\n\nHaving explained the purpose and secret of his own avatāra, Kṛṣṇa gave an example of one who attained siddhi through such knowledge. He dispelled the doctrine of polytheism, made clear that God is one and the gods are many, and also stated clearly how worship ought to be conducted. At this point a question may arise for us. The creation of mankind on this earth was by Bhagavān. But one human is not like another. What is the root of this diversity of nature? Why has Bhagavān created each person in a different way? Kṛṣṇa answers this question in the next verse.",
  "4.13": "cāturvarṇyam mayā sṛṣṭam guṇakarma vibhāgaśaḥ |\n\ntasya kartāram api mām viddhi akartāram avyayam -- The fourfold classification (of varṇa) has been created by me, by the division of guṇas and karma. Though I am its maker, know me — imperishable — to be its non-doer.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"I have created the four varṇas according to the division of guṇa and karma.\" As we know, the four varṇas are brāhmaṇa, kṣatriya, vaiśya, and śūdra. Most people misinterpret this statement of Bhagavān's. There are those who ask, if Bhagavān himself created the four varṇas, why does this varṇa-distinction not exist in countries other than India? Fundamentally, what Kṛṣṇa says here is not about jāti (caste by birth) but about the four varṇas. Varṇa and jāti are often used interchangeably, which causes confusion. In Sanskrit a single word often carries many meanings, and having several dimensions to one word sometimes confuses us. The word \"jāti\" has two meanings. One is the inherent nature one is born with (jīva-svabhāva — brought at birth, related to the root three guṇas); that is varṇa. The other is the household one is born into (jāti in this sense — a boy born in a brāhmaṇa household is a brāhmaṇa boy, one born in a kṣatriya household is a kṣatriya boy). If we do not grasp this subtlety, confusion results. Therefore in this verse, \"varṇa\" means one's fundamental birth-nature; varṇa also means \"color.\" What does a person's \"color\" mean? We commonly say, \"his color came out (was exposed),\" meaning his true character was revealed. There is another reason for the word \"color\" here too. Our nature has a color (invisible to our eyes) — sāttvika is white; rājasa is red; tāmasa is black. Thus color represents nature. Moreover, our nature is a power within us. In keeping with our feelings, our body radiates rays of color (invisible to the ordinary human eye). When great anger arises, a red ray from the body; when the mind is joyful, a green ray; from the body of a knower who has plumbed the depths of knowledge, a blue ray. Thus color represents our inner nature. For this reason, Bhagavān, who is of the nature of knowledge and bliss, is nīlameghaśyāma (dark-hued like a blue cloud).\n\nWhen we understand the above explanation, one matter becomes clear to us. Jāti comes depending on the household one is born into and one's parents. Varṇa is our inner world — our fundamental nature. This varṇa can be found in every corner of the world. In India, the writers of the scriptures identified this and simply gave it the names brāhmaṇa, kṣatriya, vaiśya, and śūdra. Jāti is by birth, but the varṇas are classified by guṇa and karma. A boy born in a brāhmaṇa household is a boy of the brāhmaṇa jāti. But by nature he may be a kṣatriya; in a single household, children of all four varṇas may be born. Vedavyāsa, born to a fisherman's daughter, was a great brāhmaṇa — this came in keeping with varṇa. That very Vedavyāsa's son, Vidura, the greatest of knowers, was born of a maidservant — this came from social circumstance! In keeping with jīva-nature and karma, Bhagavān gives a varṇa to the jīva at the very time of its birth.\n\nSociety cannot run smoothly without varṇa. In the Puruṣa-sūkta, these four varṇas are described, in keeping with the karma of the respective varṇa, as four parts of a body:\n\nbrāhmaṇo'sya mukham āsīt | bāhū rājanyaḥ kṛtaḥ |\nūrū tadasya yad vaiśyaḥ | padbhyām śūdro ajāyata ||\n\nThey compared the feet — which are one of the body's five limbs and hold the place of highest reverence in worship — to the śūdra. This is the foundation of the body. Śūdra means one who has melted in sorrow. He has no kingship in him, but the quality of service to others is of great importance in him. One without this quality is not even human. No one belongs to a single varṇa alone; every nature is present in everyone. We belong to whichever varṇa the nature that predominates in us corresponds to. The nature of the śūdra is so important that, without it, no other nature has any worth. We must always perform the action we do with the disposition of service. For example, one born a brāhmaṇa must give the gift of knowledge with the feeling of service to society. Otherwise it does not deserve to be called a gift of knowledge.\n\nThe vaiśya was compared to the waist portion of the body, because his nature is trade and commerce. Generally, in the way of life of earlier times, vaiśyas traveled from town to town conducting their trade. For this reason they were compared to the waist.\n\nThe kṣatriya's nature is to protect society with the strength of his arms and to conduct governance; for this reason they were compared to the arms.\n\nThen there is the brāhmaṇa nature; their fundamental work is the guidance of knowledge. For this reason they were compared to the head. One who is by nature a true brāhmaṇa always holds these fundamental qualities: contemplation of Bhagavān, control of the senses, never speaking falsehood, a life without pretense, and constant purity within and without.\n\nOnly when these four varṇas come together can a complete society be built. There should be no notion of higher or lower here. All are equally fit for mokṣa. This is a spiritual classification and system. \"I have created this on this earth in keeping with the fundamental, beginningless and eternal nature of the jīva, and in keeping with karma,\" Kṛṣṇa has said.\n\nOne fundamental point we must understand here is that Bhagavān did not create the jīva. The jīva and the jīva's nature are beginningless and eternal. It is Bhagavān who creates the body, the powers, and the world that the jīva's nature requires, gives it existence, and, for the development of the jīva's nature, sows and nurtures the jīva. Such a Bhagavān is the doer of everything. Such a Bhagavān has no other doer above him. He is the cause of everything, but there is no other thing that is the cause of him. \"Know that I am the maker, unchanging, and that no one has made me\" — Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna.",
  "4.14": "na mām karmāṇi limpanti na me karmaphale spṛhā |\n\niti mām yaḥ abhijānāti karmabhiḥ na saḥ badhyate -- Actions do not taint me; I have no craving for the fruit of action. One who thus knows me is not bound by actions.\n\nBhagavān, having built the garden that is this world in keeping with each one's nature and each one's karma (guṇa-karma vibhāgaśaḥ), created upon this earth the fourfold, mokṣa-worthy human beings. \"By this, no action taints me at all,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Action taints only when there is the pride of \"this is mine\" or \"I did this.\" Bhagavān has no attachment or tie to action. He has no pride regarding the fruit of action. He has no laborious, effort-driven agency; he has no craving for fruit. One who knows this truth, and who understands it and incorporates it into his own life, is not caught in the bondage of karma.",
  "4.15": "evam jñātvā kṛtam karma pūrvaiḥ api mumukṣubhiḥ |\n\nkuru karma eva tasmāt tvam pūrvaiḥ pūrva taram kṛtam -- Even the seekers of liberation of former times performed action having \"known\" it thus. Therefore, you too should perform the very action that was performed by your predecessors, long ago.\n\nSome might think that \"inactivity\" is the easy way to free oneself from the bondage of karma. Kṛṣṇa vehemently opposes inactivity. \"Live a hundred years, but live performing your duty-action. Do not live inactively.\" This is Kṛṣṇa's doctrine. Performing karma does not mean doing just anything. Action must proceed in keeping with our own jīva-nature (varṇa). The action performed must be known with understanding and performed without pride, ego, or expectation of fruit. \"This is exactly what your former royal sages and sages and munis did. Without the twin yogas of karma-yoga and jñāna-yoga together, there is no union with Bhagavān,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "4.16": "kim karma kim akarma iti kavayaḥ api atra mohitāḥ |\n\ntat te karma pravakṣyāmi yat jñātvā mokṣyase aśubhāt -- Even the wise become confounded as to what is action and what is inaction. I shall explain to you that action, knowing which you shall be freed from evil.",
  "4.17": "karmaṇaḥ hi api boddhavyam boddhavyam ca vikarmaṇaḥ |\n\nakarmaṇaḥ ca boddhavyam gahanā karmaṇaḥ gatiḥ -- One must understand action; one must understand contrary action as well; one must understand the giving up of action too. The course of action is inscrutable.\n\nEven the wise find it a matter of confusion as to what is action and what is inaction. We must do what we do with understanding. Our action must be preceded by knowledge. Therefore, I shall first tell you what action is. By this you shall be freed from evil, says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nUnderstanding action is not so easy. But it must be understood. The course of action is an extremely secret matter. It is very difficult to know what is action and what is inaction — even great knowers do not know this. If we look at certain matters in history, we find ourselves confused. For example, take Vedavyāsa: he was a maiden's son (kānīna), born of an unmarried fisherman's daughter. Vedavyāsa's own sons, Pāṇḍu and Dhṛtarāṣṭra, were born to widows; and the Pāṇḍavas were born to other men while their mother's husband was alive! What is dharma here? How right is it to defend the Pāṇḍavas? Is it even possible to accept Vedavyāsa as a great brāhmaṇa knower? Looking at this example, we become confused as to what is dharma, what is adharma, what is right, and what is wrong.\n\nWe must have the awareness that behind the action, inaction, and contrary action that takes place among us, an infinite divine power is at work. Without this knowledge it is impossible to grasp the secret of karma. Within a single body, Bhagavān, present in many forms (vikarmaṇaḥ), causes action to be performed. Karma is an exceedingly difficult subject. We must come to know it from Bhagavān himself. Kṛṣṇa explains the secret of karma in the verses that follow.",
  "4.18": "karmaṇi akarma yaḥ paśyet akarmaṇi ca karma yaḥ |\n\nsaḥ buddhimān manuṣyeṣu saḥ yuktaḥ kṛtsnakarma kṛt -- One who sees inaction in action, and action in inaction, is the wise one among men. He is the disciplined one; he is the doer of all action, and its fruit.\n\nKṛṣṇa here explains a most fascinating matter about what action really is. As said before, Sanskrit is a language of hidden meaning. For that reason, merely reading the Bhagavad Gītā, without spiritual experience, reveals nothing. The Gītā opens itself to whatever depth we descend to in the level of our spiritual experience. This verse is itself an example of that. On the surface, this verse looks like a mad, riddle-like, contradictory statement! It seems to say: \"Where there is action, there is no action; where there is no action, there is action. One who knows this is wise\" — or: \"What is action is not action; what is not action is action; one who knows this is wise.\" To understand the hidden meaning here, one needs, along with study, direct spiritual experience.\n\n\"Where action exists, there is no action.\" We take it that action exists in us. Our feeling is, \"I have freedom of action — I am beholden to no one.\" But the action present in us is subject to Bhagavān. In this cycle of creation, activity goes on continuously even when we do nothing at all. If you are doing something, it is not that the activity has occurred because of you. Give up the delusion that there is action in me. Prakṛti continually performs its own duty. The activity of the universe goes on even while we remain inactive. Even if we do everything, nothing actually happens through us. This cosmic wheel is unceasing.\n\nLet us look at a worldly illustration to understand this. An elephant (this cosmic wheel) has a tiny ant (an ordinary human) on its head. The elephant is going from east to west. The ant is continually moving from head to tail, that is, from west to east. But wherever this ant on the elephant moves, it will end up wherever the elephant ends up. Here the ant's movement is in vain — of no use whatsoever. Suppose this ant were to think that because of it, the elephant traveled from there to here — how utterly laughable, is it not? In just the same way, behind our own movement there is a great driving power. If we take the entire cosmos, this earth is a tiny point among other planetary bodies. Within that point, our country is a still tinier point. Within that, the \"I\" is a point impossible even to conceive (a fraction of a point in the universe). Yet we take pride as though this entire cosmos runs by us. We are but one component along with the movement of the entire universe — nothing at all happens by our own doing. Therefore, \"see inaction within your action,\" says Kṛṣṇa. One who knows that Bhagavān exists as the cosmic power controlling his action in this way is the wise one (vivekī).\n\n\"Yuktaḥ\" means one who has turned his mind toward truth and has reflected in order to know the truth. One who has thus known right from wrong obtains the full fruit of all his actions. For such a person, though agency exists, there is no pride in it; there is no ego of \"I did this, this happened by me\"; there is no delusion of \"I am independent.\" Therefore every action he performs is fruitful. His life is shaped by an awareness of the supremacy of Bhagavān. Even performing a small action, such a person obtains great fruit. Shedding the ego is a matter of the utmost importance in spirituality. Kṛṣṇa says this repeatedly in the Gītā. Ego is so dangerous that, after reading the Gītā, the very pride \"I have read the Gītā\" arises! Therefore, alongside the acquisition of knowledge, one must always remain vigilant. One must never, at any time, fall under the sway of ego. Ego is not the path of spirituality.\n\nThough Bhagavān performs action, no taint of action touches him. In Bhagavān, who has no taint of action whatsoever, all action exists. Where there is action, is there no taint of action? Only one who has no taint of action obtains the full fruit. One who has fallen into the taint of action, thinking \"I did this, this happened by me,\" struggles caught in a prison. This is Kṛṣṇa's explanation of karma. In Bhagavān there is no taint of action, but there is universal agency. The more we reduce the taint of action within us, the more fruit of action we obtain. One who knows this is wise. He obtains the full fruit of action through true reflection on it. Kṛṣṇa unpacks this further in the next verse.",
  "4.19": "yasya sarve samārambhāḥ kāma saṅkalpavarjitāḥ |\n\njñāna agni dagdha karmāṇam tam āhuḥ paṇḍitam budhāḥ -- One whose every undertaking is free of desire and of self-willed intent, whose actions are burnt up by the fire of knowledge — such a one the wise call a paṇḍita.\n\nBehind the engagement of our life there is always some desire, some resolve. The desire that \"I must have happiness, I must keep my household happy\" — for this we follow some method or other. This is the problem that torments a person behind every action. We engage ourselves in order to fulfill our own desire. One whose engagement has no touch of desire is a paṇḍita. A paṇḍita's learning must be seen not in his words but in his conduct. Give up the desires and cravings that say \"this must happen in my life, I must do this, I must get exactly this from it,\" and perform your duty with ease. Accept whatever Bhagavān gives as his prasāda. Learn to be content with whatever comes. Do not suffer thinking \"I don't have this.\" This is \"kāma-saṅkalpa-varjya\" (not desiring \"nothing at all,\" but not desiring \"exactly this\" — that is kāma-saṅkalpa-varjya). Let whatever comes, come; you remain one who makes the effort. Live joyfully in whatever comes. Action performed with this kind of awareness remains clean. This is the cleansing of karma's grime by the fire of knowledge. One who holds to such conduct is called a paṇḍita. Knowers recognize such people as \"the wise.\" What matters is not what a person says, but what he cultivates as habit. One who fills his mind with desires and lives chasing one desire after another, putting up a pretense, can never rise to any height.",
  "4.20": "tyaktvā karma phala asaṅgam nitya tṛptaḥ nirāśrayaḥ |\n\nkarmaṇi abhipravṛttaḥ api na eva kiñcit karoti saḥ -- Giving up attachment to the fruit of action, ever content, without dependence, knowing himself the very image of Bhagavān, who desires nothing else and bows to no one, and abiding in Bhagavān's shelter, ever at peace — such a person, though engaged in action, in truth does nothing at all.\n\nSome have no desire that this or that be obtained. But when it comes, they are pleased. Excessive attachment to the fruit of action is not good. Bhagavān prompted, and it came. Think about how to make good use of it; do not become inflated. Do not expect that it must come; when it comes unexpectedly, do not become agitated with joy either. Excessive attachment to the fruit is not good. This alone is the method by which a person can live happily and at peace. Without this, there is despair, and from that, mental turmoil. And when wealth comes at an untimely moment, there is elation over it — and from that, the ruin of life! For this reason, do not lose your head whether something has come, has not come, or must come. Engage in your duty with a clear mind. Let there be self-restraint in every circumstance. Train your mind in this way — this is what Kṛṣṇa has said. This is Kṛṣṇa's psychology.\n\nThe jīva is a reflection of Bhagavān. It has no independence. As the original is made, so is the reflection. Bhagavān is ever content; he has no discontent. In the same way, we too must learn to live without discontent. It is not life to live with a selfish mind, wagging one's tail after this person or that in order to fulfill some desire. You, who are the reflection of Bhagavān who depends on no one's shelter, why should you live a life of discontent? Become without dependence, like Bhagavān. Live knowing the truth that you always have Bhagavān's shelter. A person living in this way, though engaged in action, does nothing at all — because he knows the secret that Bhagavān is above everything. Further explanation of this can be found in the verses that follow.",
  "4.21": "nirāśīḥ yata citta ātmā tyakta sarva parigrahaḥ |\n\nśārīram kevalam karma kurvan na āpnoti kilbiṣam -- When mind and citta are held in check, longing recedes; the sense of \"I\" and \"mine\" fades away. Even performing action only for the body's upkeep, no fault attaches.\n\nThere is a desire to be ever content, but how is this to be cultivated? Kṛṣṇa answers this question here. When there is worldly desire, discontent is unavoidable. Desiring what one lacks never brings happiness. To be content, one must first reduce the list of one's wants and aversions. First \"become one whose citta is controlled (yatacitta),\" then \"become desireless (nirāśī),\" says Kṛṣṇa. First we must keep our citta and mind under control. Here citta means our past memories — the storehouse of memory of desires that have arisen before. The citta is packed with a bundle of desires and disappointments. First, see that past desires do not recur. Do not dig up the past again; let it go completely, and control the mind so that it does not run, in the present, toward wants and aversions. If we cultivate this habit, contentment becomes possible. Having achieved contentment in this way, along with it comes \"ātma tyakta sarva parigrahaḥ.\" Here \"parigraha\" does not mean abandoning one's family and household and going to the forest. It means giving up excessive attachment to everything while remaining with it — giving up pride of ownership (detached attachment). Let everything be there, but do not become fixated and drown in it. Live with it, but do not cling to it — as the lotus leaf remains in water without the water clinging to it. Living this way, though we do everything, we do nothing at all — because action performed with understanding carries no taint of karma. If we learn to live without clinging, karma too does not cling to us. Perform the action needed for maintaining the body without mental attachment, without boastfulness; then the grime of karma will not touch you, says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "4.22": "yadṛcchā lābha santuṣṭaḥ dvandva atītaḥ vimatsaraḥ |\n\nsamaḥ siddhau asiddhau ca kṛtvā api na nibadhyate -- One content with whatever comes of itself, who has risen above the dualities of life, who does not envy the prosperity of others, who regards success and failure alike — such a one, though performing action, is not bound by it.\n\nThis describes the chief mark that appears when our mind comes under our own control. One who has attained contentment is happy in whatever he receives. His mind does not desire that what he has received is not enough, or that something else should come. This is because whatever we receive, we receive by Bhagavān's will. Take employment, for example: not everyone with talent gets a job. Getting employment, and getting a salary from it — all of this is according to Bhagavān's will. Therefore, knowing everything to be Bhagavān's resolve, when one engages in selfless action, the mind becomes pure. In this state, ego never comes near. Neither poverty nor wealth, nor the household we are born into — none of this is in our hands. All of it is that Lord's will. To live knowing this is the mark of the yatacittātman.\n\nNow the second mark: \"life beyond the dualities.\" Our life is duality itself. Day and night, gain and loss, defeat and victory, birth and death — everything is duality. We must live in the very midst of this duality. Just as we accommodate ourselves to day and night, \"learn to live without being struck down by the other dualities,\" says Kṛṣṇa. No sorrow when something is not obtained, no elation when it is. To attain a uniform state of mind in both conditions is life beyond the dualities.\n\nOne problem confronts us when living beyond the dualities. While living with the attitude \"what I have is enough,\" if someone alongside us begins to earn more, seeing this kindles envy in us. If those below our own level, or our next-door neighbor, become wealthy for some reason, we feel distress! Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"Never feel envy on seeing another.\" Whoever is to get however much will get exactly that much. However skilled we may be, we cannot rise if Bhagavān does not favor us. This very awareness is \"yadṛcchā-lābha-santuṣṭaḥ.\"\n\nThen there is success and failure. Take meditation, for example: if concentration cannot be attained and something else appears instead, see Bhagavān in whatever appeared instead. Do not fret over it. If concentration is achieved, do not be carried away by illusion either. Because success and failure are not in our hands. Even failure is a step toward success. Such mental equilibrium is of the utmost importance in life. In this state, action never becomes binding.",
  "4.23": "gata saṅgasya muktasya jñāna avasthita cetasaḥ |\n\nyajñāya ācarataḥ karma samagram pravilīyate -- For one who has given up attachment to the fruit, who has abandoned identification with the body, whose mind is fixed on Bhagavān, and who acts as an act of worship — all of his action dissolves away.\n\nIn this verse, Bhagavān places before us, in the form of a summary, everything that has been said above.\n\nDo not hold excessive desire for the fruit; do not hold excessive expectation; let there be devotion to duty; give up identification with the body and with family, and learn to live detached; establish the mind in Bhagavān, who is of the nature of knowledge (cetas). Living in this way, every action of ours becomes yajña, and reaches Bhagavān, who bears the name Yajña, and the grime of action is burnt away in the fire of knowledge.",
  "4.24": "brahma arpaṇam brahma haviḥ brahma agnau brahmaṇā hutam |\n\nbrahma eva tena gantavyam brahma karma samādhinā -- The offering is Bhagavān (offered into Bhagavān). The oblation offered is also Bhagavān (Bhagavān as the oblation). By Bhagavān himself is the oblation offered into Bhagavān present in the form of fire (into fire, which is subject to Bhagavān). And the very agitation of action, along with its focused concentration, is Bhagavān (subject to Bhagavān).\n\nThe answer to what it is like to perform action with the abandonment of pride, in a state of knowledge, is \"brahmārpaṇaṃ brahma haviḥ.\" Here \"arpaṇa\" (offering) may be understood as follows. We offer an oblation into fire to Bhagavān — this is arpaṇa. We give food to a hungry person — this is arpaṇa. We give a daughter in marriage to a worthy groom — this is arpaṇa. We help someone in financial trouble — this is arpaṇa. Here, the giver is that very Bhagavān, and he who dwells within the receiver and accepts is also he. Everything proceeds according to Bhagavān's will. \"Havis\" means the thing given; \"arpaṇa\" is the act of giving. Any object can become a havis. The substance of charity is a havis; the substance offered through the mouth of fire in a yajña is a havis. Since we brought nothing with us at birth, and have received everything from this prakṛti, the havis we give is also subject to that Bhagavān. Here, to say \"mine\" is laughable.\n\nWe offer a substance through the mouth of fire. Here, fire does not mean mere inert flame. In Sanskrit, \"agni\" means the presiding deity of fire, who is sentient. For this reason fire is used as a witness (for example, marrying with fire as witness). Inert matter can never be used as a witness. Within this fire deity, Bhagavān exists as the liberating (tāraka) power. Here, the giving, that which is to be given, the act of giving, the place where it is given — all of this is subject to Bhagavān. Bhagavān's presence exists in every act. It is because of his presence in the object given that it becomes useful. Bhagavān, of the nature of knowledge, present within the fire, accepts the oblation offered in the form of devotion. If our activity proceeds with this contemplation, then when we worship Bhagavān, \"brahmaiva tena gantavyam\" — what is reached, too, is Bhagavān alone. Because \"brahmakarma-samādhinā\" — everything is subject to the all-capable Bhagavān.\n\nHere, \"brahmārpaṇa\" means that all activity is for Bhagavān. It is that very Bhagavān who created everything that exists in the world. We merely make use of what has been created within prakṛti. Fire performs its function subject to Bhagavān. \"The activity we perform is Bhagavān's own prompting. Every action we perform is brahma-karma; it is action in the form of Bhagavān's worship\" — this is the knowledge we must have about karma. There must be no self-interest in the action we perform; it must be in the form of Bhagavān's worship. Yajña does not mean only the act performed through the mouth of fire. When we make our life full of Bhagavān, every course of our life becomes a yajña. When our life is devoted to the contemplation of Bhagavān, our very life is a great yajña. A fire-yajña performed selfishly, without contemplation of Bhagavān, is mere business. The priesthood too is like this. Only when performed with understanding is it true priesthood; otherwise it is merely a trade done for money!\n\nUp to this point, Kṛṣṇa has spoken of the varieties of karma. He has analyzed how every act performed in the form of worship of God is also yajña. Kṛṣṇa next explains a broader meaning of yajña. As said before, worship performed only at the mouth of fire is not the only yajña. Giving, gathering together for a good cause such as worship (saṅgatīkaraṇa), the gift of knowledge, the gift of food, sharing what we have in excess with those who lack it — all of this is yajña. In the verses that follow, Kṛṣṇa gives us the universe-facing picture of yajña.\n\nGenerally, yajña is of two kinds: one is the inward yajña, the other the outward yajña. The continuous inward worship of Bhagavān (deva-pūjā); the union of citta and mind with Bhagavān (saṅgatīkaraṇa); and offering ourselves to Bhagavān — this is the complete mental (mānasa) yajña. In another sense, yajña is of two kinds: yajña that grants knowledge, and yajña that grants karma. Kṛṣṇa explains all of this in the verses that follow.",
  "4.25": "daivam eva apare yajñam yoginaḥ paryupāsate |\n\nbrahma agnau apare yajñam yajñena eva upajuhvati -- Some sādhakas practice the mental yajña of worship of the Divine itself (worshiping Bhagavān himself as the very form of yajña). Others worship Bhagavān, who bears the name Yajña, through an outward yajña by offering into Bhagavān in the form of fire (they practice the outward yajña through Bhagavān himself, who bears the name Yajña).\n\nSome yogis (sādhakas) who walk the path of worship of Bhagavān worship the Divine itself as yajña. That is, their yajña is the continuous, inward worship of Bhagavān. This requires no outward apparatus. This is a meditative yajña. A yajña performed in this way, without external apparatus, is the most excellent yajña. This is generally a yajña that only the most advanced sādhakas can perform. This is a knowledge-predominant yajña, performed by giving up all outward apparatus, through mental engagement alone.\n\nThe other kind of yajña is the action-predominant yajña performed at the mouth of fire. Ordinary human beings follow this. This is worship performed by offering to Bhagavān at the mouth of fire, where Bhagavān's presence resides. This is the outward yajña. This is a matter of great importance. In the Veda, fire is said to be the most important symbol for the worship of Bhagavān. Fire is never impure. Through contact with fire, even the impure becomes pure. If we have the wish to feed Bhagavān something, this can be done only through fire. Of all the symbols before our eyes, fire is the most exalted — this was the conclusion of the sages of the Vedic age. Within the burning fire is the fire-god; within him is prāṇa; within prāṇa is Bhagavān. In this contemplation, one worships Bhagavān, who bears the name Yajña, through yajña, with a most excellent invocation combined with knowledge, devotion, and detachment.",
  "4.26": "śrotra ādīni indriyāṇi anye saṃyama agniṣu juhvati |\n\nśabda ādīn viṣayān anya indriya agniṣu juhvati -- Some offer the senses, beginning with the ear, into the fire of restraint. Others offer the objects of the senses, beginning with sound, into the fire of the senses.\n\nThe special sādhana that ought to be present in a sādhaka is itself a yajña. Offering the oblation that is the senses into the fire called \"restraint\" is a yajña. With the firm resolve not to hear evil, not to speak evil, not to see evil, offer the fickleness of the senses through sense-control. This is the process of conquering sensory fickleness. Offering the oblation that is sensory fickleness into the fire called restraint, and thereby worshiping Bhagavān — may Bhagavān be pleased and, through this, lead me forward on the path of sādhana — is the yajña performed with this kind of contemplation. There is also offering good things into the senses, which is itself a yajña. Putting the oblation that is the singing of Bhagavān's praises into the fire-pit called the ear; putting the oblation that is a symbol of Bhagavān into the fire-pit called the eye; putting the oblation of tulasī and sandal, offered to Bhagavān, into the fire-pit called the nose. Thus, receiving the objects of the senses in this way also becomes a yajña. Just as restraining the senses is a yajña, so too is the reception of the senses a yajña. Take in what is conducive to the contemplation of Bhagavān, and give up what is not — this is an important step on the path of sādhana.",
  "4.27": "sarvāṇi indriya karmāṇi prāṇakarmāṇi ca apare |\n\nātma saṃyama yoga agnau juhvati jñānadīpite -- Some offer all the activities of the senses, and the activities of the vital breath as well, into the fire — illumined by knowledge — of the sādhana of self-restraint.\n\nIn our body, continuous activity goes on through the organs of knowledge and the organs of action. Besides this, there are many activities that occur through the vital energy (prāṇa-śakti) — for example, breathing, strength, the power of procreation, tears at times of joy or sorrow, and so on. Offering (restraining) the activity of the organs of knowledge, the organs of action, and the vital energy into the fire called self-restraint is a yajña. This is not for the sake of obstinacy, but performed in the form of yajña, as an act of Bhagavān's worship. This is self-restraint illumined by the awareness of Bhagavān. It is self-restraint performed for the sake of the awareness of Bhagavān; it is a restraint preceded by knowledge that carries one toward awareness of Bhagavān, not mere impulse.\n\nContinuing to describe the variety of yajñas, Kṛṣṇa now places before us a list of yajñas:",
  "4.28": "dravya yajñāḥ tapaḥ yajñāḥ yoga yajñāḥ tathā apare |\n\nsvādhyāya jñāna yajñāḥ ca yatayaḥ saṃśita vratāḥ -- Some offer their possessions. Some offer tapas itself into Bhagavān. For some, the practice of karma itself is their yajña. For some sharp-willed, effort-driven sādhakas, the study of the Vedas and the awareness of Bhagavān is itself their yajña.\n\nDravya-yajña: Some worship Bhagavān through material substance. This may be through the mouth of fire, or through gifts such as the gift of wealth, the gift of food, the gift of a daughter in marriage, and so on. Giving what is appropriate, to a worthy person, at the appropriate time, so that the Bhagavān dwelling within them may be pleased, is dravya-yajña.\n\nTapo-yajña: This has two aspects, one outward and one inward. One is continuous meditation, which requires no outward apparatus. The second is the self-restraint needed for the contemplation of Bhagavān — this is called tapas. Examples include the observance of brahmacarya, the undertaking of vows, and so on.\n\nYoga-yajña: This means performing various forms of karma-yoga for the pleasure of Bhagavān. This is the worship of Bhagavān through outward actions. For example: 108 circumambulations of the deity, ritual worship (arcana), and so on. Besides this, the observance of yama and niyama as described in the yoga-śāstras.\n\nSvādhyāya-jñāna-yajña: This is the yajña most dear to Bhagavān. Here, \"svādhyāya\" has many meanings. Reading what is relevant to oneself, what one ought to read. Study of the Veda belonging to one's own branch (śākhā); finding, through study, the quality of the supreme Bhagavān, who is independent everywhere, present in all texts — this is svādhyāya. In this age, it is very rare to find those who know the secret of the scriptures. In such a circumstance, without wasting time, using whatever intelligence Bhagavān has given, and studying as much as one is able on one's own — this too is svādhyāya. All such svādhyāya nourishes the growth of our knowledge. What is most dear to Bhagavān is our coming to know, and sharing what we have come to know with another. The one and only wealth that increases by being shared with another is knowledge. To perform jñāna-yajña, we must become \"yatis.\" Here \"yati\" does not mean a sannyāsī. Yati means one who is continuously striving. Along with sādhana, a sharp vow is also needed — that is, a clean, honest life. Without this, no action performed becomes a yajña. Resolve is needed to advance further on the spiritual path.",
  "4.29": "apāne juhvati prāṇam prāṇe apānam tathā apare |\n\nprāṇa apāna gatī ruddhvā prāṇāyāma parāyaṇāḥ -- Yet others are practitioners of prāṇāyāma. Restraining the movement of prāṇa and apāna, they offer prāṇa into apāna, and apāna into prāṇa.\n\nPrāṇāyāma is a special sādhana within yoga. For some, prāṇāyāma is itself a lifelong sādhana. Offering prāṇa and apāna into Bhagavān in the form of yajña, worshiping Bhagavān through prāṇāyāma — this is offering prāṇa into apāna. This pertains to kumbhaka. Releasing the breath from within the body is \"rechaka\"; taking in pure, oxygen-rich air is \"pūraka.\" Holding the vital energy within the vessel of our heart is \"kumbhaka.\" This is very effective.\n\nThis increases concentration, health, and longevity. Generally, a person's lifespan is determined by his breathing. Natural breathing means one breath (rechaka and pūraka) every four seconds. If a person lives a hundred years, his lifespan is \"77 crore, 76 lakh breaths.\" If, instead of every four seconds, one breathes once every eight seconds, one could live 200 years. This was achieved in the past by some sādhakas through prāṇāyāma. Even today there are healthy sādhakas in the Himalayas who are hundreds of years old — so say Paramahamsa Yogananda and Swami Rama, from their own experience, in their book. Prāṇāyāma increases vital energy. By this, another person's illness too can be healed. This is called touch, or \"healing touch.\" By mere touch of the hand, fear, illness, sorrow, madness, and blindness can all be cured. For this reason, when one blesses another, it is done by placing the hand on the head. By this, vital energy flows from the elder to the younger. Thus many sādhanas are possible through prāṇāyāma. Most importantly, through this, the mind becomes established in God. Kṛṣṇa calls this a yajña.\n\nThe energy that flows from above downward is apāna; the energy that flows upward, from the mūlādhāra toward the sahasrāra, is prāṇa. Holding these two energies still at the heart is kumbhaka. Releasing the air fully from within through the right nostril, taking in pure air through the left nostril to the point that no more can enter, and then holding the energy still at the heart so that prāṇa does not go up and apāna does not go down — this is kumbhaka. This is the yajña performed by those devoted to prāṇāyāma. It is the yajña of breathing performed with the contemplation: \"May Bhagavān be pleased and grant me concentration, and may he help me continue my sādhana.\" [Note: prāṇāyāma should not be attempted without a guru's guidance.]",
  "4.30": "apare niyata āhārāḥ prāṇān prāṇeṣu juhvati |\n\nsarve api ete yajñavidaḥ yajñakṣapita kalmaṣāḥ -- Others, restraining their diet, offer the vital energies into the vital energies. All these are knowers of the way of yajña, and their impurities are cleansed away by yajña.\n\nSome restrain their diet and reduce sensory fickleness through sādhana. Dietary restraint means not eating too much, eating less salt, sour, and spice, and so on. This reduces the pull of the senses, whose effect falls directly upon the mind. Thus sādhana through dietary restraint is a yajña. For this reason, the practice of fasting on festival days and occasions such as Ekādaśī has continued socially among us. The resolve behind this is that, at least on that one day, the mind may remain clean and engaged in the worship of Bhagavān.\n\nThere is another kind of yajña: \"prāṇān prāṇeṣu juhvati,\" meaning offering prāṇa into the prāṇas, or offering the senses into the senses. Here, \"senses\" means the deities presiding over the senses. Each of our senses has a presiding deity. These deities work at different levels. Each deity stands at a different rung and performs its function, and they help us on our way to reaching Bhagavān. Contemplating the hierarchy of these deities at their various levels, and through that contemplating Bhagavān, is a yajña. The supreme Bhagavān is first; everything is subject to him. After him comes mother Lakṣmī, the mother of prakṛti; then Brahma-Vāyu, Sarasvatī-Bhāratī, Garuḍa-Śeṣa-Rudra, Suparṇī-Vāruṇī-Pārvatī, Indra-Kāma, Dakṣa and others, Sūrya-Candra-Yama and others, Varuṇa, Agni, the Aśvin gods, Kubera, Yajña. Thus, each subject to the other, they carry out their functions at various levels. Awareness of the deities who govern this wondrous machine called the body, and the knowledge-sādhana that proceeds through them toward Bhagavān — this is the hierarchical jñāna-yajña.\n\nIn yajña, there is no such thing as one being good and another bad. Whichever yajña suits a person, he pursues his sādhana through it. Not everyone can do everything. One should proceed along whichever path feels beneficial and conducive to one's sādhana. One who proceeds toward Bhagavān, worshiped through yajña, who is himself the very embodiment of yajña, and who makes every course of his life an act of worship of Bhagavān, washes away the impurity of his life and lives a clean life.",
  "4.31": "yajña śiṣṭa amṛta bhujaḥ yānti brahma sanātanam |\n\nna ayam lokaḥ asti ayajñasya kutaḥ anyaḥ kurusattama -- Those who partake of the amṛta remaining after offering in yajña (the amṛta that is the fruit of yajña) attain the eternal Bhagavān. O best of the Kurus, for one without yajña, not even this world exists — what then of the other?\n\nWhat remains after offering to Bhagavān in yajña is called \"amṛta.\" It carries us beyond death. If we live this kind of life in every course of our life, we gradually go to reach that Bhagavān who is unchanging forever, eternal and sanātana.\n\nThere is another resonance in the word \"sanātana\" here. Mokṣa is not obtained merely by fasting or prāṇāyāma. \"Sanātana\" (nātana-nādana) means sound. \"Brahma sanātana\" means the Bhagavān denoted by every sound, knowable through the Veda. Mokṣa is possible through a yajña that comes to know such a Bhagavān through the scriptures. Otherwise it is of no use at all. One who worships having learned the qualities described in the Veda from those who know them, one who has studied the Veda and come to know from it, one who, having known, tells another — all such are called sādhakas. What is chiefly needed in yajña is knowledge, unwavering devotion, and surrender. \"One who has no such contemplation in life, who does not incorporate yajña into his life, lives in vain even in this world — what then of the next?\" says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as \"Kurusattama.\" You, the best of the Kuru lineage, engage in action rather than remaining inactive; you, born in a lineage that engaged itself in yajña, the best among knowers, of sāttvika nature — \"make war itself into a yajña\" is the resonance carried in this address.",
  "4.32": "evam bahuvidhāḥ yajñāḥ vitatāḥ brahmaṇaḥ mukhe |\n\nkarmajān viddhi tān sarvān evam jñātvā vimokṣyase -- Thus many kinds of yajña are spread out at the mouth of Bhagavān. Know that all of them arise from action. Knowing this, you shall be liberated.\n\nYajña has many facets. When any course of life is performed with awareness of Bhagavān, when the movement of life becomes a movement toward Bhagavān, it becomes yajña. However yajña is performed, it comes and reaches at the very mouth of Bhagavān. The regulator of all yajñas is Bhagavān alone; the diversity lies only in our own contemplation and action.\n\nWe must perform yajña through our own action. It is not possible to set out saying \"I will worship Bhagavān while giving up action.\" Seeing Bhagavān in our own respective duty-action is yajña. Whatever action is performed with this contemplation carries us toward the path of liberation.\n\nUp to this point, Kṛṣṇa has explained in detail how action becomes yajña, its varieties, and its lord. We have also seen that yajña is fundamentally of two kinds, outward and inward. Of these two kinds of yajña, which carries greater importance, and why — Kṛṣṇa explains this in the next verse.",
  "4.33": "śreyān dravya mayāt yajñāt jñānayajñaḥ parantapa |\n\nsarvam karma akhilam pārtha jñāne parisamāpyate -- O scorcher of foes, the yajña of knowledge is superior to the outward yajña performed with material substance. O Pārtha, all action in its entirety finds its culmination in knowledge.\n\nIn truth, there is no such thing as a purely outward yajña or a purely inward yajña that is complete in itself. Behind the outward yajña we perform, there is awareness; alongside the inward yajña, there is also practice. Of these two, the inward yajña carries greater importance, because through mental meditation, a special power is built up in the mind without any outward action. But action performed without awareness is in vain! For this reason, the awareness behind action is of great importance. So we must perform action preceded by knowledge. If it is merely material, without knowledge behind it, without being done with understanding, it is meaningless. Any action becomes effective only when performed with understanding. For this reason, the knowledge-yajña is of great importance. In this verse Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as \"Parantapa.\" This form of address has a special meaning here, understandable at two levels. Taken plainly, Parantapa means \"the hero who crushes his enemies.\" Here, the yajña Arjuna is performing is the subjugation of the enemy. Behind that yajña there is the support of knowledge. Therefore it becomes a jñāna-yajña — not a dravya-yajña. This is because Arjuna is Parantapa — \"one who ever contemplates the Supreme Self.\" Here there is no contemplation of \"they are my enemies, I must subdue them and establish my authority.\" This is a yajña in the form of the worship of Bhagavān. Fighting against injustice is Arjuna's duty; victory and defeat are left to Bhagavān. When war is fought with this contemplation, it becomes a jñāna-yajña. This epithet \"Parantapa\" indicates here how action can be transformed into knowledge.\n\nKṛṣṇa explains why this is knowledge-natured, saying: \"sarvam karma akhilam\" can be split into words in two ways. One is \"sarva karma akhilam,\" and the other is \"sarva karma ā-khilam.\" All action that is complete with every apparatus is called \"sarva karma akhilam\" — that is, all action performed completely. \"Perform any single action completely,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Any action becomes complete only when performed preceded by knowledge. Knowledge must accompany action. That is the greatness of knowledge. Perform action with knowledge; perform action and gain knowledge. Then it becomes complete. In the Bhāgavata, a yajña performed without knowledge is fiercely opposed. There it is said that one who performs a homa without knowing why the oblation being offered is given is one infatuated with fire, and one who dies inhaling smoke. Awareness of Bhagavān behind action is of great importance. Without it, it is like a boat with a hole in it. Action performed becomes fruitful only when awareness accompanies it.\n\nIn another sense, mere action devoid of knowledge is \"ā-khilam.\" Here \"khilam\" means a fragment. Even all action without knowledge combined together is but a small fragment before knowledge. All action without knowledge, even taken together, is negligible before action full of knowledge. Our action should be for the sake of knowledge, and action should not be performed for its own sake. But it is unfortunate that, in the present day, the number of those who perform action for the sake of knowledge is diminishing.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as \"Pārtha.\" As said before, Pārtha means one who has seen the far shore, one who has seen the shore beyond the ocean of knowledge; one who has drunk the meaning of the Vedas. The resonance carried by this address is: \"this action that you, a great knower, perform with understanding, is not mere action — it is a knowledge-yajña.\" Here we must again recall that Arjuna is fundamentally a great knower. But even great knowers sometimes become confounded and forget everything. Arjuna is in that state here. This is only a temporary veil, and Kṛṣṇa is removing that veil.\n\nIt has become clear that action without knowledge is of no use. But what is the manner of acquiring this knowledge? Kṛṣṇa answers this question in the next verse.",
  "4.34": "tat viddhi praṇipātena paripraśnena sevayā |\n\nupadekṣyanti te jñānam jñāninaḥ tattvadarśinaḥ -- To acquire knowledge, \"you must pursue the wise; obtain knowledge from the seers of truth who have realized reality directly,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Knowledge does not come merely from reading books. It must become direct realization for us. \"Truth is an intuitional flash, it is not Intellectual.\" Truth must dawn upon us as an inner flash.\n\nGenerally, knowers who have realized truth never teach knowledge to those unfit for it. For this reason Kṛṣṇa says: \"Do not let go once you have found the seers of truth. Pursue them, win them over, prove yourself worthy before them. Make your singular longing understood to them; surrender to them.\"",
  "4.35": "tat viddhi praṇipātena paripraśnena sevayā |\n\nupadekṣyanti te jñānam jñāninaḥ tattvadarśinaḥ ||\n\nyat jñātvā na punaḥ moham evam yāsyasi pāṇḍava |\n\nyena bhūtāni aśeṣeṇa drakṣyasi ātmani atha u mayi -- Pāṇḍava, knowing which you shall not again fall into delusion of this kind; through whose dispelling you shall see all beings, without exception, within me, the indweller (present everywhere) — such knowledge will the seers, who have truly seen, impart to you. Bow at their feet, question them in every way, serve them, and come to know it.\n\nTo acquire knowledge, \"you must pursue the wise; obtain knowledge from the seers of truth who have realized reality,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Knowledge does not come merely from reading books; it must become direct realization for us. \"Truth is an intuitional flash, it is not Intellectual.\" Truth must dawn upon us as an inner flash. All the Vedic mantras dawned within the sages inwardly — just as the idea of the force of gravity flashed upon Newton. For this reason, we must first surrender to truth; we must pursue the wise who have seen truth.\n\nGenerally, knowers who have realized truth never teach knowledge to those unfit for it. For this reason Kṛṣṇa says: \"Do not let go once you have found the seers of truth. Pursue them, win them over, prove yourself worthy before them. Make your singular longing understood to them; surrender to them.\" Here \"praṇipāta\" means prostration with eight limbs — touching the head, chest, hands, and feet all to the ground, and, with a devotional gaze, through hymns of praise, with immense reverence held in the mind — this is \"aṣṭāṅga-namana.\" Having surrendered in this way, \"search and search again, ask in every manner, and come to know\" — this is what Kṛṣṇa says. \"Serve the seers of truth, gain knowledge from them, and act from that knowledge\" is the message Kṛṣṇa has given us through Arjuna.\n\nOnce one has gained knowledge from the seers of truth, there is no further room for delusion. What torments Arjuna here is the veil of delusion covering knowledge. For this reason Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as \"Pāṇḍava.\" Here \"paṇḍā\" means knowledge (for example, one who possesses paṇḍā is a paṇḍita), and Pāṇḍava means one who has attained a mass of knowledge. Furthermore, \"pāṇḍu\" means \"white color,\" a symbol of sattva. This address indicates: \"you are yourself a knower and of sāttvika nature.\"\n\nOnce the awareness of truth comes, the noose of delusion falls away; there is no possibility of its returning. In this state, awareness awakens that the whole of creation is dependent on the Self (on Bhagavān), and that Bhagavān is the regulating power of everything. By this, all confusion vanishes. Selfishness, sorrow, envy — all depart, and steady wisdom (sthitaprajñatā) dawns. Bhagavān, atomic within the atom, fills every being as its indwelling image (bimba), and he is the all-pervading one who is great within the great. One comes to know the truth that everything proceeds according to his will. One who has surrendered himself to Bhagavān never has any fear in life.\n\nWe have understood that one must act with knowledge, that one must gain knowledge from the seers of truth, and that from this mokṣa becomes possible. But what becomes of the sinners who committed a great many sinful deeds before coming to know this matter? There is no need for fear on this account, says Kṛṣṇa. Let us see Kṛṣṇa's assurance on this matter in the next verse.",
  "4.36": "api cet asi pāpebhyaḥ sarvebhyaḥ pāpa kṛt tamaḥ |\n\nsarvam jñāna plavena eva vṛjinam santariṣyasi -- Even if you are the very greatest sinner among all sinners, you can cross over all sin by the boat of knowledge alone.\n\n\"Even if you are the greatest sinner of your age, once awareness of Bhagavān dawns in you, you are as good as victorious,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Once understanding comes, if one proceeds along the path of knowledge, no past sin can stand in the way. Knowledge is like a boat upon the ocean of sin. It can carry us to the shore. Knowledge is the one and only means of crossing the ocean of sin. Such is the great assurance Kṛṣṇa has given. Let us all, then, resolve from this very moment to proceed along the path of knowledge — for tomorrow may never come to us!\n\nKṛṣṇa gives an explanation, in the verse that follows, of what becomes of the sinful action of one who has taken up the path of knowledge.",
  "4.37": "yathā edhāṃsi samiddhaḥ agniḥ bhasma sāt kurute arjuna |\n\njñānāgniḥ sarva karmāṇi bhasma sāt kurute tathā -- Arjuna, just as blazing fire reduces fuel to ashes, so does the fire of knowledge reduce all actions to ashes.\n\nKnowledge is an ever-blazing fire. There is no fire more powerful than it. Just as blazing fire burns up wood, so the fire of knowledge burns up all sinful action. As the Bhāgavata says, spiritually, fire is a symbol of the sattva quality, because it is the power that gives light and is ever upward-facing. In the same way, wood is a symbol of the tamo-guṇa, which is ever downward-pulling. Just as fire burns wood, so the knowledge present in a sādhaka burns away tamas. Here Kṛṣṇa addresses him as \"Arjuna.\" Arjuna means one who has \"acquired\" (arjana), one who possesses the fire called knowledge. With this address, Kṛṣṇa is removing the veil of delusion that has covered Arjuna's knowledge.",
  "4.38": "na hi jñānena sadṛśam pavitram iha vidyate |\n\ntat svayam yoga saṃsiddhaḥ kālena ātmani vindati -- There is no purifier here equal to knowledge. One who has been perfected through sādhana obtains it, in time, within himself.\n\nKnowledge is supremely pure. There is no other thing in this world equal to it, because knowledge is Bhagavān. Therefore, \"take up the path of knowledge; when the time is ripe, you shall surely attain siddhi\" — this is Kṛṣṇa's assurance. Sometimes it may take several births to attain siddhi along the path of sādhana. But one who trusts Bhagavān's assurance and proceeds with self-confidence attains siddhi.",
  "4.39": "śraddhāvān labhate jñānam tat paraḥ saṃyata indriyaḥ |\n\njñānam labdhvā parām śāntim ācireṇa adhigacchati -- One who has restrained the senses, and who, with faith, believes me to be the Supreme Deity, gains knowledge, and, having gained knowledge, swiftly attains the highest peace, the source of joy — that is, mokṣa.\n\nMere study is not enough for awareness to dawn within us. For a matter to become fully convincing to us, we must first have faith. The attitude of surrender is of the utmost importance here. We must first give up the pride that says, \"what I know is great, and there is nothing greater than that.\" We must have the faith that whatever we do not know can also be true. The truth of the world does not stand upon our belief. What we know in this world is but an extremely small portion. Faith is knowing that there is a truth we do not know. Our life's goal must be knowledge and the understanding of truth. Along with the thirst for knowledge, there must be restraint of the fickleness of the senses. When this is so, it leads to mental realization, self-realization, and finally the realization of the Supreme Self. Through this course, direct (aparokṣa) knowledge is accomplished, and by this, it becomes possible to attain Bhagavān (mokṣa), who is ever full of knowledge and bliss.",
  "4.40": "ajñaḥ ca aśraddadhānaḥ ca saṃśaya ātmā vinaśyati |\n\nna ayam lokaḥ asti na paraḥ na sukham saṃśaya ātmanaḥ -- The ignorant and faithless doubter, torn in two, moves toward ruin. For the doubter, there is neither this world nor the next, nor any peace.\n\nThose without knowledge and without faith live continually in doubt. When knowledge and faith are absent, doubt begins to grow there. That doubt seizes the mind and then reaches the citta. There is no remedy for a doubt that has reached the intellect (buddhi). It carries us to ruin. Because of this, for such people there is neither this world nor the next, and never any peace.",
  "4.41": "yoga sannyasta karmāṇam jñāna sañchinna saṃśayam |\n\nātmavantam na karmāṇi nibadhnanti dhanañjaya -- O Dhanañjaya, actions do not bind one who, through sādhana, has given up attachment to the fruit of action, whose doubts have been severed by knowledge, and who has become a devotee of Bhagavān.\n\n\"Perform action in life; while performing action, perform karma-sannyāsa,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Here karma-sannyāsa does not mean abandoning action. Offering action to Bhagavān, with the thought \"this action belongs to Bhagavān\" — this is karma-sannyāsa (further explanation of this can be found in the next chapter). Giving up hopes and cravings regarding the fruit of action, viewing everything with an equal eye, remaining unchanged in nature, and continually performing action — this is karma-sannyāsa. When one learns to offer action to Bhagavān, resolve one's doubts through knowledge, keep the citta awakened as one who has discernment, and live as one devoted to the Self, no action ever becomes binding for us. \"You, who won wealth by conquering your enemies in war — you, Dhanañjaya, who have won the wealth of knowledge — action shall not bind you,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "4.42": "tasmāt ajñāna sambhūtam hṛt stham jñāna asinā ātmanaḥ |\n\nchittvā enam saṃśayam yogam atiṣṭha uttiṣṭha bhārata -- Therefore, O Bhārata, cut away, with the sword of knowledge, this doubt of yours, born of ignorance and lodged in your heart; take up yoga and arise.\n\n\"Therefore arise; you, born in the lineage from which great persons such as Emperor Bharata came, a knower devoted to the devotion of Bhagavān, who is of the nature of knowledge (Bhārata); cut away, with the sword of knowledge that is Bhagavān, the doubt seated in your mind. Take up the resolve that fighting against injustice is the worship of Bhagavān, and engage in the sādhana of merit, so as to live a life of truth and honesty,\" says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nThus ends the fourth chapter.",
  "5.1": "arjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna said:\n\nsannyāsam karmaṇām kṛṣṇa punaḥ yogam ca śaṃsasi |\n\nyat śreyaḥ etayoḥ ekam tat me brūhi suniścitam -- Kṛṣṇa, at one time you praise renunciation of action, at another karma-yoga. Tell me for certain which of the two is superior.\n\nArjuna asks: \"O Kṛṣṇa, at one time you say fight; at another you say practice renunciation of action. If renunciation of action means giving up action, this is a contradiction. Tell me for certain which of these is preferable.\" There is duality present in the very act of fighting a war. How can one fight while renouncing duality? How can renunciation of action and karma-yoga both be possible together? Given that war implies attachment and aversion, Kṛṣṇa's words are not becoming certain to us.",
  "5.2": "bhagavān uvāca -- Bhagavān said\n\nsannyāsaḥ karmayogaḥ ca niḥśreyasa karau ubhau |\n\ntayoḥ tu karma sannyāsāt karmayogaḥ viśiṣyate -- Renunciation of action and karma-yoga are both paths of good. Of the two, karma-yoga is superior to renunciation of action.",
  "5.3": "jñeyaḥ saḥ nityasannyāsī yaḥ na dveṣṭi na kāṅkṣati |\n\nnirdvandvaḥ hi mahābāho sukham bandhāt pramucyate -- One who neither hates nor desires should be known as the true, eternal renunciant. O mighty-armed one, one who has conquered such dualities is easily freed from the bondage of action.\n\nKṛṣṇa's answer to Arjuna's question here is in the form of a riddle. This verse must be carefully unpacked and understood. The renunciation spoken of here is not the renunciate order (sannyāsāśrama). One who has crossed over rāga and dveṣa and stands beyond them is a sannyāsī. Sa-ahaṃ-nyāsa: sannyāsa. Here \"sa\" means Bhagavān, who is complete, appropriate, faultless, and full of every good quality. Depositing (nyāsa) our ego (rāga-dveṣa-ahaṅkāra) in Him is sannyāsa. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Karma-yoga is superior to renunciation of action,\" because Arjuna here has said: \"I have no hatred for the Kauravas, I have no desire that we must win, I have no desire for any fruit whatsoever,\" and thinks he can sit detached and inactive without fighting, and calls that renunciation of action. But merely sitting still like this does not amount to performing duty-action (karma-yoga). By this, adharma prevails. Therefore renunciation of action and karma-yoga must go hand in hand. Renunciation of action without karma-yoga never brings good — this is Kṛṣṇa's answer.\n\nFor renunciation to be meaningful, action must accompany the abandonment of desire and anger. One must fight — but not out of attachment or aversion. Without action, renunciation of attachment and aversion has no meaning. Therefore Kṛṣṇa's message is to act beyond the dualities.\n\nOur very life is a struggle. If we withdraw from our own duty-action, give up rāga-dveṣa and kāma-krodha, remain inactive, and think \"I have achieved renunciation of action,\" our life is in vain. From this there is never any good, and no mokṣa. Struggling through our own duty-action, within this battle called life, while giving up attachment and aversion, is the true religion of life. One who lives such a life beyond the dualities is freed from the prison of karma.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as Mahābāhu. As said before, on the surface Mahābāhu means a great hero. Here this epithet has a special meaning. \"Mahābā\" means one immersed in the flood of attachment, aversion, and desire. One who has renounced such great attachment and aversion is Mahābāhu. \"You have renunciation of action within you; alongside it you are the great hero fit to practice karma-yoga\" — this is the resonance in this address. In the struggle of our life, action must be performed while giving up attachment and aversion. The fundamental message is that renunciation of action and karma-yoga must both exist together. To fit this, the epithet \"Mahābāhu\" is used here.",
  "5.4": "sāṅkhya yogau pṛthak bālāḥ pravadanti na paṇḍitāḥ |\n\nekam api asthitaḥ samyak ubhayoḥ vindate phalam -- The path of knowledge and the path of action are different — [one for renunciates and another for householders] — say the immature; not those who know. One who follows even one path properly attains the fruit of both.\n\nAs he has explained renunciation of action and karma-yoga above, Kṛṣṇa here explains sāṅkhya (knowledge — Spiritual Wisdom) and yoga (practice — Spiritual Practice). In the scriptures, sāṅkhya (samyak khyāti) does not mean mathematics; it is pure knowledge of the truth of the Self — knowledge of Him under whose control this entire world moves. Kṛṣṇa says those who do not know this are immature, and that their words have no real foundation. Having said this, Kṛṣṇa says, \"ekam apy āsthitaḥ samyag ubhayor vindate phalam,\" meaning \"whichever one of these you truly bring into practice, you obtain the fruit of both.\" Here again confusion may arise for us. This does not mean practicing only knowledge, or only action. To perform action well means to perform it with understanding, preceded by knowledge. Likewise, for knowledge to be good means to put into practice what one has understood. Therefore one may be \"knowledge-predominant,\" the other \"action-predominant.\" But one is the complement of the other. Those who know this are paṇḍitas, and they obtain the true fruit.",
  "5.5": "yat sāṅkhyaiḥ prāpyate sthānam tat yogaiḥ api gamyate |\n\nekam sāṅkhyam ca yogam ca yaḥ paśyati saḥ paśyati -- The place that jñāna-yogīs attain, karma-yogīs too walk toward. One who knows that the goals of the paths of knowledge and action are one and the same is the one who truly sees.\n\nKnowers of truth must perform action not only for their own sake but also to guide the world, because society follows them. This is the social responsibility resting upon them. Therefore knowledge and action are complementary to each other. The goal of the path that grants knowledge and the path that grants action is one and the same. The goal reached by sages and gods who perform action with knowledge as primary, and by those who perform action as primary and gain knowledge through it, is the same — that Bhagavān. To say here that renunciation of action is one thing and karma-yoga another is meaningless. The two are complementary to each other. Those who know this truth are the ones who \"know.\"",
  "5.6": "sannyāsaḥ tu mahābāho duḥkham āptum ayogataḥ |\n\nyoga yuktaḥ muniḥ brahma na cireṇa adhigacchati -- O mighty-armed one, mere renunciation [renunciation of duality] without karma-yoga only brings misery (giving up action itself, instead of performing action as an act of worship of Bhagavān, is the path to hell). One who has conquered the dualities while performing action is not slow in attaining Bhagavān.\n\nMere renunciation of duality (sannyāsa) without karma-yoga is in vain. If one reads the scriptures and thinks, \"I have gained knowledge, I am beyond the dualities, I need no action at all,\" and becomes inactive out of this belief, it pushes us into ruin. But if one, having conquered kāma and krodha while being beyond the dualities, performs action preceded by knowledge, mokṣa is certain from it.",
  "5.7": "yoga yuktaḥ viśuddha ātmā vijita ātmā jita indriyaḥ |\n\nsarva bhūta ātmabhūta ātmā kurvan api na lipyate -- One who is engaged in karma-yoga, of purified mind, who has conquered the mind, who has conquered the senses, and who knows that Bhagavān, who sustains all beings, is his own lord (whose mind is fixed on Bhagavān alone) — even though he acts, he is not tainted.\n\nOne engaged in karma-yoga must perform pure action with a pure mind, having achieved control of his own mind. To conquer the mind, the favor of Bhagavān is needed. One who has conquered the mind conquers the senses — from this, purity of self follows. Then awareness dawns that Bhagavān, who is the indwelling Self of all beings, is one's own Self. With the practice of realizing that the all-pervading, all-regulating Bhagavān is present within oneself too, that the infinite power governing the world is present, atomic within the atom, even within one's own jīva-nature, and by fixing the mind on Bhagavān, the indweller of all beings, and performing action, action does not cling to us.\n\nHaving explained that renunciation of action and karma-yoga must be blended together in performing action in life, Kṛṣṇa now, in the following verse, gives an explanation of what such performance of action means in our daily life.",
  "5.8": "[This verse's śloka forms one continuous statement with verse 5.9; the padaccheda, translation, and commentary for both are given together under 5.9, matching the book's structure.]\n\npralapan visṛjan gṛhṇan unmiṣan nimiṣan api |\n\nindriyāṇi indriya artheṣu vartanta iti dhārayan",
  "5.9": "na eva kiñcit karomi iti yuktaḥ manyeta tattvavit |\n\npaśyan śṛṇvan spṛśan jighran aśnan gacchan svapan śvasan |\n\npralapan visṛjan gṛhṇan unmiṣan nimiṣan api |\n\nindriyāṇi indriya artheṣu vartante iti dhārayan -- The sādhaka who has grasped the truth should hold the understanding that he himself does nothing at all — while seeing, hearing, touching, smelling, eating, walking, sleeping, breathing, speaking, releasing, receiving, opening the eyelids, and closing the eyelids too — thinking, \"the senses are engaged in their own work.\"\n\nIn our lives, worship of God is not the only form of spiritual practice. Every single course of our life must become karma-yoga. It is not that worship of God is to be done only at some particular time. Our whole life must become a yajña. Yajña, in Kannada, is called \"bēḷuve\" (a way of tending/living); our very living (bāḷuve) must become a bēḷuve.\n\nOne who performs this discipline of action must be aware of the truth: \"I myself do nothing by myself; my action is willed, but I have no freedom in it here.\" What we intend to do, we are not able to do by ourselves; what we desire, we do not get; we have no freedom regarding the fruit. This is a matter that comes within everyone's experience. But the activity does occur through us nonetheless.\n\nThe knower of truth (tattvajñāni) knows: \"I am not doing this out of the desire that 'I must get exactly this, I must not get that,' nor out of the ego of 'I am doing this'; the Bhagavān within me is making this happen.\" Therefore he has no ego of \"I, mine\" regarding anything. Everything proceeds according to Bhagavān's will; knowing the truth that we are not independent agents here, he performs his own action.\n\nPaśyan — while seeing, śṛṇvan — while hearing, spṛśan — in touch, jighran — while smelling, aśnan — while eating, gacchan — while walking, svapan — while sleeping, śvasan — in breath, pralapan — in speech, visṛjan — while releasing, gṛhṇan — while receiving, unmiṣan — while opening, nimiṣan — while closing — thus, in all his activity, the knower of truth holds the awareness that he himself does nothing. For example: we savor the beauty of a flower with our eyes; we smell and enjoy its fragrance with our nose; we touch the flower, wear it, and feel joy. It is that Bhagavān who created this flower and gave it its beauty. It is He who gave us the eye with which to see that flower (one born blind does not have this yoga). If this contemplation is present, there is no room for the ego of \"mine\" in the act of seeing, touching, or smelling. In the same way, with eating: in this world, so many people live without even one meal, stretching out their hands before others. But when we eat with the contemplation that Bhagavān has arranged for us to have food three times a day, that becomes a yajña. Then there is sleep: in sleep we are close to Bhagavān, but we do not have that awareness. What if sleep did not come at all? What if the whole night passed in dreams alone? This is difficult even for us to imagine. When we were born, breathing began without our being aware of it. Even in sleep, the activity of our breath continues without our awareness. But we do not know when this breath will stop. Thus, the contemplation of that cosmic power in every single activity is true karma-yoga.",
  "5.10": "brahmaṇi ādhāya karmāṇi saṅgam tyaktvā karoti yaḥ |\n\nlipyate na saḥ pāpena padmapatram iva ambhasā -- One who performs actions having entrusted them to Bhagavān, giving up attachment, is not tainted by sin — just as a lotus leaf is not by water.\n\nAt the end of worship we say, \"Śrī Kṛṣṇārpaṇamastu.\" This means \"offered to that Bhagavān.\" To offer means the resolve that \"Bhagavān himself, in the form of his own worship, had this action performed through me, for my own uplift.\" When we perform action in this way, no taint of any action clings to us. The ego of \"I, mine, by me\" departs. This is a blissful wonder. Just as a lotus leaf, though remaining in water, does not let the water cling to it, so too — remaining with action, performing action, yet not letting action cling to us — is possible only through this contemplation.",
  "5.11": "kāyena manasā buddhyā kevalaiḥ indriyaiḥ api |\n\nyoginaḥ karma kurvanti saṅgam tyaktvā ātma śuddhaye -- Yogis perform action with the body, with the mind, with the intellect, and with the senses alone, giving up attachment, for the purification of the self.\n\nAs is customary in the scriptures, a matter is stated, and then, to give us confidence in that matter, mention is made of those who have practiced it accordingly — this is called \"arthavāda.\" It is somewhat difficult to be the first to take up any matter ourselves. But when it is said \"this is something practiced since ancient times,\" we feel greater confidence about that matter. Similarly, Kṛṣṇa here brings up that knowers practice karma-yoga combined with renunciation.\n\nYogis, beyond the dualities, without craving for the fruit, knowing that \"Bhagavān within me is making me perform his own worship through me, for my own uplift,\" perform action with the disposition of duty. They have no ego, no possessiveness; from this the mind becomes pure. A pure mind alone can grasp a pure subject. To grasp a subject purely means to grasp it in exactly the sense in which the writers of the scriptures meant it. To understand it in this way, our mind must be in tune with the mind of the writers of the scriptures, or we must rise to their level and think while standing in their position. Otherwise the true meaning is not truly grasped. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"Yogis too, giving up ego and possessiveness (saṅgaṃ tyaktvā), with the intelligence of offering to Bhagavān, perform the discipline of action with the body, the mind, the intellect, and the senses.\" Kṛṣṇa explains this matter with further emphasis in the next verse.",
  "5.12": "yuktaḥ karmaphalam tyaktvā śāntim āpnoti naiṣṭhikīm |\n\nayuktaḥ kāma kāreṇa phale saktaḥ nibadhyate -- One skilled in yoga, giving up attachment to the fruit of action, attains steady, unshakable peace. One who is not a yogi, driven by desire, keeps his eye on the fruit and, craving it, becomes its prisoner.\n\nOne who has engaged himself in karma-yoga, who performs action in awareness of Bhagavān, attains mokṣa, the pinnacle of knowledge and bliss. It is not that if we perform action out of some desire for its fruit, no fruit at all is obtained. But we have no awareness of how useful that fruit will be to us, nor will it be a complete fruit. If we perform action without craving for the fruit, we obtain everything we need. If we engage ourselves in action without desire for the fruit of action, continuing on in action, gaining knowledge from it, becoming purified in our inner faculty, we can become worthy of Bhagavān's grace and attain mokṣa.\n\nWithout karma-yoga, if one performs action with self-centered feelings such as \"I am laboring for myself, this is my wealth, I will spend what I have earned for my own pleasure, no god helps us,\" and so on, action becomes a bondage for us. Even a dumb animal does not live like this. For example, the crow: if it sees something to eat, it calls its whole flock and shares it before eating. Even cruel beasts such as the tiger and the lion never act out of self-interest once their stomachs are full. But we humans live out of self-interest. However much we get, we want still more! One who lives this way gets caught in the wheel of worldly life and meets ruin.",
  "5.13": "sarva karmāṇi manasā sannyasya āste sukham vaśī |\n\nnava dvāre pure dehī na eva kurvan na kārayan -- One who has mentally renounced all actions, who has conquered the senses, remains at ease within this nine-gated city — the embodied self — neither doing anything nor causing anything to be done.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: mentally renounce all action. This is a very important statement. Here Kṛṣṇa is not saying \"give up action\" — he is saying, \"let go of it only in the mind.\" That is, the attachment to the fruit that exists in our mind regarding actions, and the illusion of mental freedom — let these go. Let go, mentally, of the notion of \"for me,\" \"for my sake.\" A jīva has no freedom, at any time, in any agency. Madhvācārya has proclaimed this again and again in all his works. Incorporating this mentally is true karma-sannyāsa.\n\nBhagavān seats us within this nine-gated city (the human body, which has attained completeness) and makes us perform action through it. \"That Bhagavān, having placed me in this city, standing as my own intimate friend beside me, is making me perform action for my own uplift\" — this awareness is karma-yoga. We have seen the extraordinary jñāna-yoga that Kṛṣṇa has explained about karma-yoga. Performing action in this way becomes worship of Bhagavān and becomes a part of the path of knowledge. When action is performed with knowledge, no task, however small, goes to waste — it becomes yoga.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa has said \"neither doing anything nor causing anything to be done.\" Generally, when we do some work, we go around everywhere saying \"I did this,\" \"that happened because I said so.\" We want four people to say so. But knowers do not speak this way. They speak instead as \"somehow God made it happen through my hands,\" \"that God, dwelling within me, made it happen,\" \"Bhagavān prompted them.\" The reason for this is that knowers never cultivate the awareness \"I am an independent agent.\" This is the difference between the contemplation of action held by knowers and by the ignorant.\n\nHaving understood all this, a question arises for us. If God makes everything happen, let him make only good deeds happen through our hands — why does he make us do bad deeds? If he himself is the one making us do bad deeds, why should we be punished for it? Why should we suffer? When I act desiring and deliberately, why am I not the agent? Why do I not get the fruit of it? If God makes everything happen, how does the fruit come to me? Such questions torment everyone. These are natural questions to be troubled by, but they are not the right questions. These questions arise when the essence of the scriptures is not understood. If we carefully analyze the verses that follow, we shall find the answer to this question of ours.",
  "5.14": "na kartṛtvam na karmāṇi lokasya sṛjati prabhuḥ |\n\nna karmaphala saṃyogam svabhāvaḥ tu pravartate -- The jīva is not like inert matter; it is capable of action. Yet it is not responsible — for any action of the world, for merit and sin, nor for the fruit that arises from them. (Bhagavān did not give the jīva-world ownership of action, of merit and sin, or of receiving the fruit of action; the jīva's own nature carries out its work; Bhagavān is the one performing all of this.)\n\nThe answer to all the questions analyzed above lies hidden, in riddle form, in this verse. On the surface it cannot be understood; only when we reflect, with the experience of the scriptures, on each word one by one, does the answer emerge there.\n\nCompared to inert matter, the jīva is a \"prabhu\" (lord) — there is willed activity in it, but that is not agency (kartṛtva). Comparing the jīva with inert matter: inert matter has no power of thought, but the jīva has the power of thought; inert matter has no intellect, but the jīva has one; yet neither has agency. Bhagavān has not given the jīva-world ownership over action, over merit and sin, or over receiving the fruit of action. The jīva does not even have delegated freedom (for example, a king giving his son the authority to rule a portion of the country independently is delegated freedom). To properly understand agency, we must understand the fascinating scriptural process behind it.\n\nIn the world there are three kinds of agency. Mere action existing is itself one kind of agency; this exists even in inert matter. For example, \"the udder gives milk\" — here there is the act of milk flowing from the udder, but this is a most insignificant kind of agency. The second is deliberate action — willed action that is not independent; this belongs to the jīva, but this is not the truly important agency either. Independent, willed action is true agency. This exists only in Bhagavān. The jīva has no delegated freedom even in action. The jīva is dependent on Bhagavān. Therefore it is not the agent. For example, a baby in a cradle: it has intellect, it has the desire to eat, but it has no freedom. It eats whatever the mother puts in its mouth. This is a willed action, but not an independently willed action. Looking now at the fruit with this same illustration: parents tell the child to bow to God; the child does not do it; in such a situation, the parents correct the child, or, if necessary, discipline it into bowing to God, and then the child does it. Here, though it was the parents who made the child bow, the fruit goes to the child. In the same way, Bhagavān, seated invisibly within the jīva, controls the activity of \"living.\" Though it is Bhagavān who makes us perform action through us, the fruit of action is ours, and not Bhagavān's.\n\nContinuing with this illustration, another question troubles us. \"Why does Bhagavān make us do bad deeds through us? Parents never make their children do bad deeds; in the same way, why should Bhagavān not make us do only good deeds?\" This is an action that appears strange compared to what happens in the world. For example, a teacher teaches his lesson to all children equally, without discrimination. But one child may grasp the lesson very quickly, while another may be agitated, not understanding it; yet another may misunderstand it entirely. The teacher is not the cause of this — this is a matter connected to the student's own nature. Kṛṣṇa says \"svabhāvas tu pravartate.\" This is an extraordinary matter. As said before, the jīva is beginningless and eternal; Bhagavān did not create it. This beginningless, eternal jīva has its own extraordinary, beginningless nature. The jīva acts in keeping with that nature.\n\nThe jīva acting in keeping with its own nature means, for example: a mango seed, when it becomes a tree, gives only a mango fruit and not a jackfruit — this is the nature of that seed. The jīva is the seed of the tree called the body. What is expressed through that body is nothing but the fundamental quality of that seed. The jīva is, first of all, subject to its own nature. We cannot do as we please; our decision is exactly as our nature is. Fundamentally, our own feeling follows our true nature; we do not act contrary to it. For this reason, if one is sāttvika by nature, all his lines of thought remain under the influence of the sāttvika quality. If one is rājasa, his line of thought remains under the influence of the rājasa quality. Even though there is the influence of the society one is born and raised in, alongside one's nature, its expression is momentary. At most it is limited to a single birth, but what remains in the end is only the jīva's own nature.\n\nThe nature attached beginninglessly and eternally to the jīva is a mixture of sattva, rajas, and tamas. Those called sāttvika are those in whom the proportion of the sāttvika quality is greater. Whichever quality predominates in someone, he belongs to that group. These three kinds of created beings act subject to their own nature — they do not have the independent agency to decide for themselves how they should act and shape themselves accordingly.\n\nNature is not subject to us; therefore the expression of that nature is not within our freedom either. Then how does that nature express itself? Under whose control does growth in keeping with nature occur? Where does that power for nature come from? The answer to all these questions lies in this very verse. For this we must look at another meaning hidden in this verse. We have no agency, and we have no freedom regarding what particular fruit should come from action. The jīva has no power to sow itself, grow into a tree by itself, and give fruit by itself. The independent Bhagavān, having built the garden called creation, sows and grows the seed called the jīva, like a gardener. From this our body, the tree, grows, and from it a bitter, sour, or sweet fruit is expressed in keeping with the jīva's nature. Therefore bad action (bitter) or good action (sweet) depends on our own nature, and not on Bhagavān. A gardener never pours spicy water on a chili plant. He nourishes and grows that plant just like every other plant. But it gives a spicy fruit in keeping with its own nature. In just the same way, Bhagavān has caused action to proceed in keeping with the nature of the beginningless, eternal jīva; and action has proceeded accordingly.\n\nSometimes we act contrary to what we have thought, because our mind's and intellect's train of thought is one thing, and what we do is another. For this reason some people say one thing outwardly and do the opposite inwardly. We should note here that even Duryodhana said, \"I know that what I am doing is wrong.\" He knew what dharma was. Yet he acted in keeping with his own nature. Therefore our understanding is one thing, our action another. Our nature sets aside all our study, intellect, and reasoning, and makes us perform action in keeping with our nature. This is exactly what Kṛṣṇa means here by \"svabhāvas tu pravartate.\" Whatever your nature is, that is your disposition. Bhagavān causes you to grow in keeping with it. Whoever has whatever nature must remain accordingly, not like someone else. If a mango turns out sour, it is a bad fruit; but if a tamarind fruit is more sour, it is all the better. Therefore it is the natural dharma of the tāmasic to remain tāmasic. The independent Bhagavān has made the activity of growing each one in keeping with whatever is their own natural disposition. It is he alone who gives the fruit of everything. He is never in bondage.\n\nThus this verse stands as the answer to all our doubts. Our doubt is exceedingly deep-seated. A doubt that arises in the mind can be resolved, but a doubt that has settled into the intellect is difficult to remove. The ancient scriptural writers, according to psychology, called this \"sthira-saṃśaya\" (a fixed doubt). The physical grime within us makes us believe \"I am wiser than everyone else.\" Because of this we do not incline our mind toward truth, and from this we gain no faith. Whatever work we do, we must do it knowing and believing it. For this, scriptural insight at every step is essential — what do I mean, what does nature mean, what is that power that governs my nature, who is it that holds our hand and leads us, and who gives the fruit, and so on — when such matters are understood, there is no room for ego.\n\nHere a question may arise for us: why does Bhagavān give us punishment? God's giving us punishment is not out of hatred; behind it is boundless, infinite compassion — just as behind a mother's punishment of her child there is compassion. The jīva is ripened by the sorrows and hardships that come in life. We must be cooked, and cooked well, by the heat of the afflictions that come to us in life. One who has never seen hardship in life never grows. Such people cannot understand another's joy or sorrow. It is precisely when hardships come that the jīva ripens. This is the compassion behind the punishment Bhagavān gives us. Behind our fall lies a seed of upliftment. Behind sorrow lies a seed of joy. When we look at it this way, in a scriptural light, we come to the awareness that the cosmic power behind the entire world is protecting us, as a great embodiment of compassion. Just as a mother looks after a baby that has no strength to get up on its own, Bhagavān is protecting us at every single moment. This is the inner awareness that must be sustained continually on the spiritual path.",
  "5.15": "na ādatte kasyacit pāpam na ca eva sukṛtam vibhuḥ |\n\najñānena āvṛtam jñānam tena muhyanti jantavaḥ -- The jīva, superior to inert matter, cannot take on anyone's sin, nor anyone's merit either. (Bhagavān has no connection whatsoever with the sin or merit of any jīva.) Knowledge is veiled by ignorance; because of this, creatures fall into delusion.\n\nThis verse can be viewed in two ways, in favor of the \"jīva\" and in favor of \"Bhagavān.\" Viewed in favor of the jīva, the word \"vibhuḥ\" gives the meaning of being distinctive, of variety, of diversity, and so on. The jīva, distinctive compared to inert matter — possessing will, thought, and knowledge — is distinctive. The jīva, which may be born as different beings in different births, can display many varieties even within a single birth. For example: a person may live as a wealthy man amid comforts, or may be in poverty; he may live as a great scholar, or as an utterly ignorant fool. This kind of variety exists in the jīva — hence the jīva is \"vibhuḥ.\" From birth to death within a single lifetime, we may experience many varieties across many stages.\n\nOne who was wealthy may lose everything and fall into poverty. When this happens, we search for all sorts of reasons behind it. Generally the first thing we do is go to some astrologer and ask a question. When asked, they say things like, \"you are suffering loss because someone practiced black magic on you.\" But here Kṛṣṇa says: \"You cannot take on anyone else's sin, nor can we obtain another's merit. Whatever we experience is the fruit of the action we ourselves performed.\" From this it is clear that it is wrong to think we were ruined by someone else. Then, does black magic and the like not work, is it false that it causes trouble? — no; as long as the fruit of our good deeds protects us, no black magic can do anything to us at all. Astrologers describe this by saying, \"your planetary positions were good, so nothing has happened so far, but now they have turned bad.\" Here, the planetary position turning bad means that the fruit of our sin has ripened. Therefore, whoever performs artificial rites against us, for it to cause us harm, we must have performed harmful action ourselves in the past. As a result of that, it comes to our experience here. It is wrong to point to another person for our own faults, our poverty, our ignorance, disease and illness, and say \"this happened because of them.\" If we have the prārabdha-karma to suffer an aggravated disease, no one can help us avoid it. For this reason it is wrong to place the blame owl on another. I do not experience the fruit of sinful action done by someone else. I experience only the fruit of the sinful action I myself have done.\n\nAction performed by a priest on behalf of a devotee accrues to the devotee; action performed by a son on behalf of his father may accrue to the father — this is action performed by one person on behalf of another. Apart from this consideration, what we experience is only the fruit of the action we ourselves performed, and not someone else's.\n\nBecause people lack proper understanding, they believe \"I was troubled because of someone else.\" The root cause of all this is the lack of proper awareness about karma, about the jīva, and about the Supreme Self. Knowledge lies covered under the veil of ignorance, and from this, wrong understanding and the illusion of delusion envelop us.\n\nLooking at another face of this verse: \"vibhuḥ\" means the all-capable, all-pervading Bhagavān. Viewed in this sense: Bhagavān has no taint of any action whatsoever. He recognizes the differences within the nature of the jīva and gives growth accordingly. He is never partial. He has no taint of either sin or merit. But this doctrine of karma of Bhagavān's, his all-capability, and his detachment — this awareness is not known to most people, and so they become confused about Bhagavān.\n\nWe are always confused about the matter: \"we have no independent agency, we are subject to him.\" If Bhagavān does everything, why do sinful deeds come to us? — this question torments most people. However much one studies, this doctrine of karma does not become understood. The reason for this is a lack of awareness. This awareness does not come merely from reading. One must grasp it again and again, experience it, and come to be convinced of it. Kṛṣṇa explains this, leaving no room for any confusion, in the next verse.",
  "5.16": "jñānena tu tat ajñānam yeṣām nāśitam ātmanaḥ |\n\nteṣām āditya vat jñānam prakāśayati tat param -- For those whose ignorance of the Self has been destroyed by knowledge, that knowledge, like the sun, illumines the Supreme Reality.\n\nThere is only one path for us to resolve our confusion: to practice sādhana along the path of knowledge and come to know the truth. Our problem is that knowledge exists within our own jīva-nature — but it is covered by the haze called ignorance. We have securely shut away our own jīva-nature within a box called ignorance. Ignorance is the lid over knowledge, and we are unwilling to remove that lid.\n\nThe first thing we must know is \"what is meant by 'I.'\" When that is known, we come to know what our limitation is, what our power is, what our smallness is. We do not have ownership over our own senses; our mind does not obey what we tell it. Therefore, \"I am along with all of this — someone has placed all of this here; experience comes from the senses, I experience it\" — such awareness arises. When it becomes understood who gave us these senses and who gives us the experience through them, the awareness dawns that one is oneself merely a reflection, and that the original image is Bhagavān.\n\nKṛṣṇa explains here how we may cast off the veil of our ignorance and take up the path of knowledge. \"Knowledge is like fire. Keep kindling the spark of knowledge within through continuous inward reflection. Then it will burn away this veil of ignorance.\" Only continuous inward reflection can kindle our inward knowledge. Outward hearing and study support this. Once the veil of ignorance is burnt away and destroyed, we attain the realization of truth. From this, the ego of \"I did this\" cannot possibly arise. Kṛṣṇa says this fire of knowledge is greater than the light of the sun. Just as the sun illumines the outer world, this knowledge illumines the inner world. This is direct (aparokṣa) knowledge, and this knowledge brings about the realization of Bhagavān.\n\nHearing Kṛṣṇa's explanation, we too feel we should attain direct knowledge. But how? What must we do for direct knowledge to come? Kṛṣṇa explains this in the next verse.",
  "5.17": "tat buddhayaḥ tat ātmānaḥ tat niṣṭhāḥ tat parāyaṇāḥ |\n\ngacchanti apunaḥ āvṛttim jñāna nirdhūta kalmaṣāḥ -- Those whose intellect is fixed on him, who believe him alone to be their lord, who are established in him, who have surrendered wholly to him, who, having beheld him, have shed the grime of life — they go to that place from which there is no return.\n\nKṛṣṇa explains here, beautifully, the path to erasing ignorance. First, fixing the intellect on Bhagavān alone (tad-buddhayaḥ); our mind grasps every subject, and the intellect makes the decision. If we fix our intellect steadily on Bhagavān, He comes and settles in our intellect. For this, we must cultivate our awareness concerning Bhagavān. Through continuous sādhana on the subject of Bhagavān, when we establish our intellect in Bhagavān, Bhagavān becomes established in our intellect.\n\nOffer your very self and your mind to Bhagavān (tad-ātmānaḥ); let him become your lord; pray continually, \"Bhagavān alone is the lord who always guides me; under his control may my life proceed auspiciously.\"\n\nWithout devotion to Bhagavān (tan-niṣṭhāḥ) nothing can be done. Niṣṭhā means devotion. Therefore there must always be devotion to Bhagavān. Meditation or worship performed without devotion is of no use whatsoever. Once we come to have knowledge about Bhagavān, devotion comes of its own accord. Devotion without knowledge is blind devotion. Knowledge without devotion (out of ego) is of no use whatsoever from that knowledge — it becomes mere dry pedantry.\n\nThus, when the mind and intellect are established in Bhagavān and knowledge-preceded devotion is cultivated, the awareness dawns that Bhagavān is the greatest refuge of all (tat-parāyaṇāḥ). From this, the illusion that worldly life is everything departs. As Purandaradāsa said: \"Our home is there, what is here is in vain.\" That is, this worldly life is not eternal, and we do not know when we must depart from here. This being so, why the ego of \"mine\"? This is not escapism; we need not give up anything, but we must give up the illusion that it is everything. Bhagavān has given it; we remain for as long as it is given and kept with us. In the same way, we must never disrespect any of the deities presiding over principles; they are Bhagavān's own family who show us the path to reach Bhagavān, but they are not everything either.\n\nWith the awareness of the Supreme Reality that is, above all, the refuge for everyone, perform the duty that has come to your lot as Bhagavān's prasāda. Do not run away; live in worldly life; accept whatever Bhagavān has given. Offer your entire being to him, \"the supreme protector, Bhagavān.\" Living in this way, the grime attached to our self is washed away, the mind becomes clean. Then knowledge shines forth — realization of Bhagavān occurs. By this the jīva can attain eternal mokṣa, from which there is no return.",
  "5.18": "vidyā vinaya sampanne brāhmaṇe gavi hastini |\n\nśuni ca eva śvapāke ca paṇḍitāḥ samadarśinaḥ -- The true paṇḍita — one endowed with learning and humility — can see the one and same Bhagavān equally present in a brāhmaṇa of learning and humility, in a cow, in an elephant, in a dog, and even in a lowly outcaste who cooks dog's flesh.\n\nThis verse is not understood by many, or, more often, is misunderstood. Kṛṣṇa here explains another facet that ought to be present in a sādhaka's worship. This is a verse that demands considerable insight. Here Kṛṣṇa has described three kinds of human condition and three kinds of animal condition. Among humans: one endowed with learning and humility, the brāhmaṇa, and the śvapāka (outcaste). Among animals: the cow, the elephant, and the dog.\n\nAmong humans, one who has reached the summit of knowledge and is the most exalted is \"vidyā-vinaya-sampanna.\" One who honestly reflects on some portion of the Veda and worships accordingly is a brāhmaṇa (the brāhmaṇa spoken of here is the brāhmaṇa varṇa, not caste; we have specifically analyzed this in the previous chapter). One who has read the entire Veda and incorporated the methods enjoined by the Veda into his life is a \"veda-jña.\" One who has understood the inward meaning of the Veda is a \"veda-artha-jña\" — he alone is \"vidyā-vinaya-sampanna.\"\n\nAmong humans, the lowest is the śvapāka. Śvapāka means one who eats the flesh of a dog. That is, he has no refinement (saṃskāra) whatsoever, and eats whatever he can for his stomach's sake. This speaks of the extreme depth of human degradation. Thus, at the very top is vidyā-vinaya-sampanna, below that the brāhmaṇa varṇa, and at the very bottom the śvapāka.\n\nAmong animals, the cow (gava) is the most exalted animal. After it, in the middle, is the elephant (hastinī); and last, the dog. The ancients determined that whatever purifies the mind is to be called \"pure.\" For example, deer skin and tiger skin help in concentrating the mind; they are used to prevent bad vibrations in the earth from entering our body during meditation. Therefore they are pure and exalted. In the same way, the cow: this animal always carries a sāttvika vibration. Another exalted quality present in the cow is this: in this world, the urine and dung of all animals are to be shunned, but the cow's dung and urine are exalted. Thus, among animals, the cow is the most exalted. This is the very reason the cow is brought into the house first at the time of a house-warming.\n\nThe ancients considered the dog to be of the lowest order. As we know, the dog is a domesticated animal ready to give up even its own life for its master. But this is the animal with the most tāmasic vibration. Therefore there is no nourishment at all for our mental development from the dog. For this reason the ancients placed the dog at the very lowest position. This is why the dog was never allowed inside the house. Thus among animals, the cow is exalted, the elephant is middling, and the dog is the lowest.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa says: \"paṇḍitāḥ samadarśinaḥ,\" meaning paṇḍitas possess an equal vision. They see the special manifestation of Bhagavān within each and every thing — whether it be a cow, a dog, or one endowed with learning and humility, whatever it may be. Because Bhagavān is the indweller of all, present within everyone. Only his manifestation differs.\n\nHe manifests through one unique, extraordinary quality in each and every being. For example, the touch-me-not plant (Touch-me-not), which grows where no one wants it, has an extraordinary medicinal quality: if this plant (without the flower) is crushed and tied in cloth and boiled with gruel, and that gruel is consumed, or if a decoction of this plant is drunk, piles can be cured without any surgery. Similarly the honeybee: it is this tiny insect that has the extraordinary power of drawing nectar from flowers and preparing honey. Our technology cannot artificially manufacture honey.\n\nWhatever is born in this world, direct knowers see, present within it as a distinctive power, the regulating power that is the Lord of birth, Bhagavān. Whether it be a dog, a cow, an outcaste, or one endowed with learning and humility — a paṇḍita sees, in all of them, only the glory of that Bhagavān.\n\nBhagavān, present as the indweller in everything, utterly pure, detached, giving each object its own distinctive power, created this infinite world and placed it before our eyes. We must observe and contemplate the glory of such a Bhagavān in every single thing. He is present everywhere, full of knowledge and bliss, complete in every quality, all-pervading, filling every atom and particle — this contemplation is the equal vision needed for direct knowledge.",
  "5.19": "iha eva taiḥ jitaḥ sargaḥ yeṣām sāmye sthitam manaḥ |\n\nnirdoṣam hi samam brahma tasmāt brahmaṇi te sthitāḥ -- Those whose minds are fixed on the equal manifestation of Bhagavān have, here itself, conquered this creation. Bhagavān, everywhere, is one and the same, free of any taint. Therefore those who know this are established in Bhagavān.\n\nWhen our mind is fixed on the contemplation that Bhagavān, full of every quality, present everywhere, fills every single thing, the door of mokṣa beyond birth and death opens for us. Whatever thing Bhagavān is present within, there is no fault in him; he is faultless everywhere. Whatever the substratum he is present in, he remains detached, filling it. The minds of those who know this remain established in Bhagavān. Therefore they never again fall into bondage, and attain eternal mokṣa.\n\nIn the previous verse Kṛṣṇa explained the spiritual equal vision of Bhagavān's form. In the next verse, complementing this, he explains how we should cultivate equal vision socially in our life.",
  "5.20": "na prahṛṣyet priyam prāpya na udvijet prāpya ca apriyam |\n\nsthira buddhiḥ asammūḍhaḥ brahmavit brahmaṇi sthitaḥ -- One should not become elated on obtaining what is pleasant, nor agitated on obtaining what is unpleasant. Such a person is of steady intellect, undeluded, a knower of Brahman, established in Brahman.\n\nOur life is the cyclical wheel of joy and sorrow. No one has only joy, or only sorrow. However much one is surrounded by wealth, difficult moments will be there in life. Likewise, even one in hardship has moments of joy. Life is a flow of joys and sorrows. In this verse Kṛṣṇa says: \"We must have equal vision toward the duality of joy and sorrow.\" Generally we become elated when something we like comes to fruition. When something unpleasant, some event we did not wish for, occurs, we become agitated, collapse, and become distressed. This is naturally a condition we observe in our life. Kṛṣṇa says: \"The mind must be kept steady, so that neither of these happens.\" Whatever is unavoidable must be accepted naturally. Otherwise, if we sit thinking \"this should not happen, that should not happen,\" the mind becomes confused. This is psychology. This does not mean that when joy comes we should not experience it. Accept whatever comes as it is, but do not become excessively elated. We must cultivate the disposition that everything in life comes and goes, that nothing is permanent. If we become excessively attached to something, and it happens to become distant from us, we cannot bear it. From this we become mentally distressed. From this our mind becomes ready to commit a terrible sin such as suicide over small matters. Therefore the successful formula of life is to experience whatever comes, as it comes, without losing our head over anything. The world is not as we wish — we are as the world is. When we learn to welcome it and live without any agitation, the mind becomes firm.\n\nViewed spiritually, what we call pleasant and unpleasant cannot truly be said to be good or bad. What is liked need not be good. Sometimes what is unpleasant to us gives us benefit. But we come to know this only late. Every event that comes in our life is conducive to our future growth. If a person has prārabdha karma leading to downfall, he may get as much pleasure as he wishes! He may lose himself in that pleasure and do what should not be done, and thereby fall; the jīva may ripen through hardship. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad states this matter ironically thus: \"preyo mando yogakṣemād vṛṇīte\" (1.2.2) — \"when both the beneficial and the pleasant come before him, whoever chooses the pleasant is a fool.\"\n\nWhat is pleasant to us need not be beneficial; it is meaningless to be elated over it. Likewise, when some untoward event occurs in life, we need never be confused over it. It may well turn out to our benefit later. Most people will have experienced this. We think, \"why this, this should not have happened,\" but later, precisely because it happened that way, a path opens for our problem. But we come to know this only late. For all these reasons, there should be no illusion of joy when joy comes, and no distress when sorrow comes. Let there be equal vision toward both. Let there be the contemplation: \"I will honestly experience whatever Bhagavān has given.\" No mental confusion. One who has the awareness that Bhagavān knows what is beneficial and what is not in life does not fix his mind on joy and sorrow. Instead, his mind is fixed on Bhagavān. Bhagavān looks after us as a mother looks after her child. Not everything that comes from a mother is liked by the child. But the mother makes the child do that work for the child's own uplift. There, what is liked is not important; what is beneficial is what matters.",
  "5.21": "bāhya sparśeṣu asakta ātmā vindati ātmani yat sukham |\n\nsaḥ brahmayoga yukta ātmā sukham akṣayam aśnute -- One whose self is unattached to external contacts finds within the Self whatever joy there is. Such a person, whose self is joined in union with Bhagavān, tastes imperishable joy.\n\nThere are five means to pleasure in this world: form, taste, smell, touch, sound. There is nothing wrong in experiencing through these. There is joy in obtaining a desired object, but there is greater joy still in giving it up. Knowing this is knowing one important formula of life. Our way of living is \"to suffer today for tomorrow's joy\" — meaning that tomorrow never comes. Suffering for tomorrow until death should not become our life. Kṛṣṇa explains here the truth that there is great bliss in attaining Bhagavān, and that it keeps us ever joyful. Once we begin to experience the joy of our own inward Self-nature, we come to know that there is nothing at all in outward joy. Bliss is hidden within us — a bliss that can be seen through renouncing outward pleasure.\n\nSometimes the bliss within us does come to our experience. Everyone will have had the experience of feeling, \"I don't know why, but I feel very happy.\" This is our inward bliss welling up. When we recognize this bliss and learn to experience it, outward pleasure appears trivial. Just as in sleep we are in bliss, unaware of it, without any touch of outward pleasure, so too, while awake, we must learn to experience the inward bliss. From this, the awareness of outward pleasure recedes.\n\nAs the Upaniṣad says, \"from renouncing outward pleasure, awareness of the inward bliss dawns, and in this lies immortality.\" The bliss present in \"not wanting\" is not present in \"wanting.\" Wanting is always a fetter for us. Wanting brings a chain of problems and sets them before us. \"The very spring of human bliss lies in losing all outward desires,\" says Kṛṣṇa. As wants diminish, a person lives in bliss.\n\nIt is not merely a matter of giving up outward pleasure; along with it, one must grasp the inward bliss. That is infinite bliss. Kṛṣṇa says: \"brahmayoga-yuktātmā sukham akṣayam aśnute.\" In meditation on Bhagavān, an inward experience of bliss occurs — a sādhaka attains it. Through inward contact with Bhagavān, a sādhaka continually savors infinite joy.\n\nJoy is within us. It is a mental state. Without recognizing the joy within us, we chase after outward joy and suffer. A folk tale tells it this way. An old woman drops some money at night in her house. But she has no oil to light a lamp in her house. Seeing the light of a streetlamp outside, the old woman comes out to the street and searches there for the money she dropped in her house. When asked why, she answers, \"There's no light in the house, but there's light here, so I'm searching here!\" We are all like this. True bliss lies within, but we go searching for happiness in the outer world!",
  "5.22": "ye hi saṃsparśa jāḥ bhogāḥ duḥkha yonayaḥ eva te |\n\nādi anta vantaḥ kaunteya na teṣu ramate budhaḥ -- Pleasures born of contact with sense-objects are, verily, wombs of sorrow. O Kaunteya, they have a beginning and an end; the wise do not delight in them.\n\nHow much pleasure can we actually get from the experience of enjoyment obtained through outward sensory contact (saṃsparśa)? We are ready to suffer a pumpkin's worth of sorrow for a gunja seed's worth of pleasure. This is the cause of all worldly wrongdoing today. We see, in our daily life, people who ruin their entire lives falling for the desire for momentary pleasure. Not knowing the truth that what appears outwardly as pleasure is an egg that hatches sorrow, we chase after it thinking it is pleasure. Behind every outward pleasure, a thick darkness of sorrow lies pooled. Outward pleasure-bliss is but a single flash of lightning. By the time it has come, it has already emptied out. Those with understanding do not live in the desire for this outward pleasure.\n\nSome say of spirituality: \"why do we need it now; all that is for after sixty, once peace has come.\" Kṛṣṇa explains, in the next verse, why this is not right.",
  "5.23": "śaknoti iha eva yaḥ soḍhum prāk śarīra vimokṣaṇāt |\n\nkāma krodha udbhavam vegam saḥ yuktaḥ saḥ sukhī naraḥ -- One who is able, here itself, before the release of the body, to bear the surge born of desire and anger — such a person is the true yogi, the truly happy man.\n\nIt is possible to conquer our attachment to worldly pleasure and sensory contact only while this body exists. Contact with objects belongs to the body, and it is possible to conquer objects only while this body exists. Bhagavān has given us the intellectual power to discern right from wrong. Using this power of discernment, we must practice control of the senses before the body falls away. This is not a matter to be postponed and achieved in a single day. This requires continuous sādhana. This sādhana must be undertaken while this body is still strong.\n\nThe fundamental problem that torments a human being is desire and anger. The force of this desire and anger is powerful enough to push aside all our discernment and stand before us. Before it sweeps away your train of thought like a mad flood of the rainy season, restrain it. \"Whoever can restrain this — sa yuktaḥ sa sukhī naraḥ,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Whoever does not pursue desire and anger, restrains outward contact, and exposes himself to inward experience is the truly happy one.\n\nWhat is our inward experience like? Kṛṣṇa explains, in the next verse, what the experience of bliss is that a sādhaka comes to see within himself.",
  "5.24": "yaḥ antaḥ sukhaḥ antaḥ ārāmaḥ tathā antaḥ jyotiḥ eva yaḥ |\n\nsaḥ yogī brahma nirvāṇam brahmabhūtaḥ adhigacchati -- One whose desire has ended, who has found joy within, who has found delight within, and for whom the very light within is Bhagavān — such a sādhaka, having become one with Bhagavān, attains the extinguishing of worldly bondage in Bhagavān.\n\nA person should not desire outward pleasure, but should desire inward bliss. Two words are used in this verse: \"sukha\" (bliss) and \"ārāma\" (ease/delight). There is a subtle distinction between these two words. What occurs when our desires are fulfilled, or when what we wanted is obtained, is \"ārāma\"; the absence of desires altogether is \"sukha.\" What occurs when some lack, trouble, or hardship is resolved, when one is delivered from some affliction, is \"sukha.\" Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"The sādhaka of adhyātma-yoga, who has performed sādhana and obtained its fruit, remains mentally established in Bhagavān. He is at ease within himself.\" The sādhaka, beholding Bhagavān within, remains at ease; giving up desire and anger, he experiences bliss; recognizing the light within himself, beholding that great light, Bhagavān, within himself, he becomes established in Bhagavān alone.",
  "5.25": "labhante brahmanirbāṇam ṛṣayaḥ kṣīṇa kalmaṣāḥ |\n\nchinna dvaidhāḥ yata ātmānaḥ sarvabhūtahite ratāḥ -- Sages who have shed all grime, who have cut away duality, who have disciplined their selves (tempered their minds), and who delight in the welfare of all beings — such knowers attain that eternal Bhagavān.\n\nThose who stand beyond the doubt and duality that arise within themselves are the true sādhakas. They keep their mind ever established inwardly. The bliss they desire is \"inward bliss.\" Not pursuing outward pleasure, desiring the good of all beings, they remain ever established in that eternal Bhagavān and reach him alone.",
  "5.26": "kāma krodha viyuktānām yatīnām yatacetasām |\n\nabhitaḥ brahmanirbāṇam vartate vidita ātmanām -- For those who are striving, who have discarded desire and anger, whose minds are conquered, who know the Self — Bhagavān, of eternal nature, fills them everywhere.\n\nFor those who have completely given up desire and anger, and have achieved full control of the senses, Bhagavān becomes visible everywhere, in every living creature. They have no pain from any worldly affliction. In their mind, Bhagavān, the indweller of all, of eternal nature, remains established.\n\nHaving given this message of turning from outward pleasure toward the inward, essential, natural bliss, Kṛṣṇa now briefly explains, in the verse that follows, the meditation that supports it.",
  "5.27": "sparśān kṛtvā bahiḥ bāhyān cakṣuḥ ca eva antare bhruvoḥ |\n\nprāṇa apānau samau kṛtvā nāsa abhyantara cāriṇau ||27||\n\nyata indriya manaḥ vṛttiḥ muniḥ mokṣa parāyaṇaḥ |\n\nvigata icchā bhaya krodhaḥ yaḥ sadā muktaḥ eva saḥ ||28||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and explanation of these two verses (5.27 and 5.28) are given together under the next verse, 5.28.]",
  "5.28": "sparśān kṛtvā bahiḥ bāhyān cakṣuḥ ca eva antare bhruvoḥ |\n\nprāṇa apānau samau kṛtvā nāsa abhyantara cāriṇau |\n\nyata indriya manaḥ vṛttiḥ muniḥ mokṣa parāyaṇaḥ |\n\nvigata icchā bhaya krodhaḥ yaḥ sadā muktaḥ eva saḥ -- Shutting out external sensory contacts, fixing the gaze between the eyebrows, equalizing the prāṇa and apāna breaths moving within the nostrils, one who has brought the play of the senses and mind under control, who yearns for liberation, who has shed desire, fear, and anger — such a sādhaka is verily ever free.\n\nTo control our mind, we must first control our senses from roaming in the outer world. \"sparśān kṛtvā bahir bāhyān\" — without controlling the external senses, it is not possible to control the mind. For this, the illusion of pleasure called the outer world's form-taste-touch-sound-smell must be left outside. Along with this, the experience of this world that has, unwantedly, filled within us, which stands as an obstruction to seeing the truth, must be expelled. Generally, when we sit for meditation, all sorts of things begin to appear — beautiful things, frightening things, and so on. There is no need to fear this. All of these are unnecessary thoughts we have filled ourselves with. As they go out, they show themselves to us in this way. When sādhana is continued without giving up, gradually all these thoughts go out and the mind becomes clear. This is not something that happens in a single day; it requires continuous sādhana. Once the inner faculty is clean, anything at all can be reflected in it.\n\nAnother important element needed for meditation is steadying the annamaya-kośa (the body). If the body moves, the mind moves — then concentration is not possible. For this we must steady our body, keeping it erect and straight, in whatever posture is possible for us. Kṛṣṇa says: \"cakṣuś caiva antare bhruvoḥ,\" meaning fixing our gaze between our two eyebrows (the scriptures say that if this is difficult, one may instead choose the tip of the nose). Between our two eyebrows lies the ājñā-cakra; when we fix our gaze there, this energy center is activated. By this the annamaya-kośa becomes steady.\n\nAfter the annamaya-kośa comes the prāṇamaya-kośa. \"prāṇāpānau samau kṛtvā nāsābhyantara-cāriṇau\" — there is a continuous movement of air within us; steadying this movement is called kumbhaka. This is of two kinds: internal kumbhaka and external kumbhaka. In internal kumbhaka, one first expels all the air within completely, then takes in as much pure air as possible, and holds the breath still. [This kind of prāṇāyāma must be learned from a guru and performed accordingly — otherwise there is danger in it.] In external kumbhaka, one expels the air within completely and holds the breath still in that very state. Generally, internal kumbhaka is dangerous for those with heart disease; they may perform external kumbhaka. Thus the prāṇamaya-kośa becomes steady through kumbhaka. The next step is the manomaya and vijñānamaya kośas. Our mind is fickle; it does not stay in one place; it must be steadied. With the firm resolve, \"I shall walk this path, practice it, and attain siddhi without fail,\" the mind must be made ready for it. By this the citta becomes concentrated toward Bhagavān. This state is the greatest state we can reach while in the physical realm. The bliss here is boundless. If one experiences the joy present in this state, the mind will never again run after the pleasure of the outer world thereafter. The mind becomes established in Bhagavān. By this, our desire, fear, and anger all disappear. \"Such a sādhaka is verily ever free,\" says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nHaving explained how, by ripening the fourfold inner faculty — the annamaya, prāṇamaya, manomaya, and vijñānamaya kośas — we can attain the vision of the Bhagavān within us, Kṛṣṇa now explains, in the next verse, in what manner we should behold that Bhagavān whom we have never beheld.",
  "5.29": "bhoktāram yajña tapasām sarvaloka maheśvaram |\n\nsuhṛdam sarva bhūtānām jñātvā mām śāntim ṛcchati -- One who knows me as the enjoyer of all sacrifices and austerities, as the great lord of all worlds, as the true friend of all beings — attains peace.\n\nThat Bhagavān is the enjoyer of all yajñas. (Here yajña does not mean only the yajña performed at the mouth of fire, but every kind of yajña explained earlier.) The oblation we offer in yajña in the name of the gods must also be offered with the feeling that it is offered to Bhagavān, who dwells within that deity as the indweller. The gods never accept any oblation offered without being offered to Bhagavān. There must be contemplation of Bhagavān in every single one of our actions. He is all-capable, the regulator of all activity, and everything belongs to him — this contemplation is of great importance in meditation. There must be an unwavering vow, bodily, mentally, and verbally: the vow of never speaking dishonestly at any time, constant deep reflection on truth in the mind, and constant goodwill toward all — this itself is a tapas. Whomever we worship, forgetting Bhagavān, it is in vain.\n\nTo some, Bhagavān means fear! Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"suhṛdaṃ sarva-bhūtānām.\" There is never any need to fear Bhagavān. There is no more intimate friend than he. Bhagavān is the power that ever loves us, that continually fills us within and without and ever protects us. He is the \"suhṛt\" (true friend) who nurtures each person according to his own worth, who, when we err, gives punishment only to correct us and show us the way, and who ever lifts us up with compassion. The knowledge that there is no one else so compassionate is of great importance in meditation. One who knows and follows this attains mokṣa, the pinnacle of knowledge and bliss, says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nThus ends the fifth chapter.",
  "6.1": "Chapter Six\n\nIn the previous chapter Kṛṣṇa mainly explained the practice of worshipping Bhagavān; now, in the sixth chapter, he explains dhyāna-yoga and dhyāna-samādhi down to their very foundation.\n\nbhagavān uvāca -- Bhagavān said:\n\nanāśritaḥ karmaphalam kāryam karma karoti yaḥ |\nsaḥ sannyāsī ca yogī ca na niḥ agniḥ na ca akriyaḥ ||1||\n\nsaḥ sannyāsī ca yogī ca na niḥ agniḥ na ca akriyaḥ -- One who performs the duty that is to be done without depending on the fruit of action -- that one alone is the true sannyāsī and the true yogī; not one who has merely stopped offering into the fire, nor one who has given up action.\n\nWe usually divide people into two groups -- karma-yogīs on one side, sannyāsīs on the other. Here Kṛṣṇa sets before us an account that differs from this worldly notion of who is a sannyāsī and who is a karma-yogī. Kṛṣṇa says: \"anāśritaḥ karmaphalam.\" We commonly assume that those who have given up action are sannyāsīs, and those who perform action are householders. But Kṛṣṇa says everyone must be a karma-yogī, and everyone must also be a sannyāsī -- one is not had by giving up the other. Sannyāsa does not mean abandoning one's family and going to the forest; karma-yoga must still be performed. \"kāryam karma karoti yaḥ\" -- whatever duty falls to whomever, that one must perform it. For this very reason sannyāsa and karma-yoga always go together. The true sannyāsī is a karma-yogī, and the true karma-yogī is a sannyāsī. Beyond this, as we ordinarily understand it, those who have merely given up the sacrificial fire, or merely given up action, are neither sannyāsīs nor yogīs.\n\nA sannyāsī is never inactive. Everyone has an activity proper to them. A brahmacārī performs study, residing in the guru's household, and service to the guru; a householder performs hospitality to guests and the care of family and home. Thus everyone has some karma.",
  "6.2": "na hi asannyasta saṅkalpaḥ yogī bhavati kaścana -- Know that which they call sannyāsa to be the very same as yoga, O Pāṇḍava. No one who has not renounced desire can become a karma-yogī who has grasped its inner essence.\n\nSannyāsa too is itself a yoga. Renouncing the fruit of action is itself one face of the yoga of the very karma-yogī who performs action. Offering everything to Bhagavān and acting purely with the outlook of worshipping him, without craving any fruit, is the practice of the sāttvika. Sannyāsa means offering desire-and-resolve (kāma-saṅkalpa) to Bhagavān. Kāma is the wish \"I must do this\"; saṅkalpa is the determination \"I will do it.\" Freedom from kāma-saṅkalpa is sannyāsa. What Kṛṣṇa is explaining here is not a matter belonging to the sannyāsa-āśrama; it belongs to sādhanā. Kṛṣṇa's message is that on the path of sādhanā, yogīs must become sannyāsīs, and sannyāsīs must become yogīs.\n\nWhen we practise sādhanā, both action and the offering of its fruit must be present. Given this, has any particular action been marked out as the one everyone must perform? What is a sādhaka's duty toward society? How should a sādhaka respond to society? Where does action come to an end? What is the relation between sādhanā and action, and how does the latter support the former? Kṛṣṇa answers all these questions in the next verse.\n\nārurukṣoḥ muneḥ yogam karma kāraṇam ucyate |\nyoga ārūḍhasya tasya eva śamaḥ kāraṇam ucyate ||3||",
  "6.3": "ārurukṣoḥ muneḥ yogam karma kāraṇam ucyate | yoga ārūḍhasya tasya eva śamaḥ kāraṇam ucyate -- For the sage wishing to climb into yoga, karma-yoga [service to people in its many forms] is said to be the means of reaching the goal. For that very same one who has climbed into yoga, stillness (śama) [rest from service to people, and steadfastness in meditation on Bhagavān] is said to be the means to an increase of joy.\n\nThis verse has two dimensions. One is how far a sādhaka must continue daily duty, and from what point it may be set aside; the other is what a sādhaka's duty toward society is. This verse explains both.\n\nA sādhaka who has not yet attained realization, who is still on the path of sādhanā, must at that stage continue performing every enjoined duty -- nothing may be set aside. Once realized, he can see Bhagavān from within, in the state of samādhi; in that state, the act of seeing Bhagavān within becomes more important to him than the remaining duties. One who has attained aparokṣa-jñāna cannot remain in samādhi forever; he must return to the outer world. Then his chief actions are hearing scripture, chanting mantras, giving discourse, and the like. Hence the binding obligation of enjoined duties holds only up to the attainment of aparokṣa-jñāna. For example: a knower of direct truth is seated in meditation, seeing Bhagavān within and immersed in that bliss. It is the hour of dawn, of sunrise, the time for bathing and sandhyāvandana -- it would be wrong for us to wake him because he happens to be sitting in meditation at that hour. If he does not perform sandhyāvandana, no taint of action touches him, because sandhyāvandana is itself performed for the sake of the vision of Bhagavān -- and he is already receiving that very vision. Thus, for the knower of direct truth, there is no binding obligation of action. Once he comes out of the state of samādhi, his remaining duty is to sing Bhagavān's glories, give discourse, study, and chant.\n\nMadhvācārya has beautifully explained another dimension of this verse in his Gītā-tātparya. This mainly explains what a sādhaka's conduct in society should be. Ācārya gives an extraordinary sense to the word karma. Karma is kara-ma. Kara means a due, a tax [Duty]. What we must render, knowing it as a due, is \"kara-ma.\" We are subjects in Bhagavān's kingdom; the due -- the kara -- we render him for that is karma. Placing an offering in a temple out of some personal longing is not a genuine due, and Bhagavān does not even want it. The karma Bhagavān wants from us is service to those in distress. For a sādhaka, until he becomes one of realization, this is a chief practice. Compassion and a spirit of service toward every living being matter greatly. Yoga-śāstra says the very same thing: a sādhaka should hold four attitudes -- maitrī, karuṇā, muditā, and upekṣā. Maitrī is fellowship with the good -- affection wherever goodness is found. Karuṇā is compassion and help offered on seeing another's suffering. Muditā is rejoicing at another's uplift. Upekṣā is keeping away from the wicked -- not quarrelling with the bad, but staying clear of their company without any hatred. This is a sādhaka's weighty duty toward society. One established in yoga (yogārūḍha) is always inward-turned; this social obligation does not apply to him -- indeed, it is not even possible for him. The cause of the growth of his bliss is śama -- keeping the mind fixed on Bhagavān alone at all times; he has no other action apart from this.\n\nThus Kṛṣṇa clearly states in this verse that service to the world must become an inseparable part of a sādhaka's life. When Kṛṣṇa says that one established in yoga has no social obligation and remains seeing Bhagavān within, a question may arise for us: how are such knowers of direct truth to be recognized? The next verse answers this very question.\n\nyadā hi na indriya artheṣu na karmasu anuṣajjate |\nsarva saṅkalpa sannyāsī yogārūḍhaḥ tadā ucyate ||4||",
  "6.4": "yadā hi na indriya artheṣu na karmasu anuṣajjate | sarva saṅkalpa sannyāsī yogārūḍhaḥ tadā ucyate -- When one, having offered the responsibility for all action to Bhagavān, loses all attachment to sense-objects and to actions, that one is said to have reached the goal of sādhanā.\n\nDescribing the mark of the knower of direct truth, Kṛṣṇa says: \"yadā hi na indriya artheṣu na karmasu anuṣajjate.\" That is, the knower of direct truth is not attached to whatever draws the senses -- sound, form, taste, smell, touch, or any object whatsoever. He acts, but does not attach himself to the action. Where a sādhaka still on the path restrains the senses only with effort, for the knower of direct truth this is a natural quality. As the Ṛgveda says: \"Narasiṃha is the ruler of the world; let his wish alone be my wish. Whatever he desires in his heart, let that alone be my own feeling; let no resolve contrary to his ever come to me.\" This is how the knower of direct truth wishes. He does not desire, nor grieve, to experience anything through the senses; nor, once influenced by whatever experience comes through the senses, does he attach himself to it -- he watches everything with equanimity. If we sit on the seashore watching the ocean, there are waves of many kinds; we watch those waves and take delight in them. The waves that come are not within our control. There is joy in savouring, with equanimity, whatever wave comes as it comes. In the ocean of life, hardship and happiness, sorrow and joy, all come and go like waves; their control is not in our hands. Just as one takes delight watching the ocean's waves, so living naturally, taking delight in watching this wave of life exactly as it comes, is the mark of the knower of direct truth.\n\nIn this chapter Kṛṣṇa goes on to explain the complete process of meditation. Toward that end, the next verse introduces how we must prepare the mind, and what must be done in order to become established in yoga.\n\nuddharet ātmanā ātmānam na ātmānam avasādayet |\nātmā eva hi ātmanaḥ bandhuḥ ātmā eva ripuḥ ātmanaḥ ||5||",
  "6.5": "uddharet ātmanā ātmānam na ātmānam avasādayet | ātmā eva hi ātmanaḥ bandhuḥ ātmā eva ripuḥ ātmanaḥ -- By the strength of the self [by Bhagavān's grace] one must lift the self upward; one must not drag the self down. The self [Bhagavān] alone is the self's friend; the self [Bhagavān] alone is the self's foe.\n\nAs said before, the word ātmā carries many meanings -- the body, the mind, the individual self (jīva), and the Supreme Self (Paramātmā). Looking at this verse with all these meanings joined together, many things become clear. \"uddharet ātmanā ātmānam na ātmānam avasādayet\" means: the mind is the cause of our downfall, and the mind is likewise the cause of our uplift. So Kṛṣṇa says: seek the path of the jīva's uplift by means of the mind. Never send the mind downward. Do not leave the mind free to run wild in a way that leads to the jīva's ruin; carry it upward instead. If the mind goes low, it drags us down into the depths. Send that mind aloft; a mind gone aloft carries you aloft. Uplift your own true nature as jīva by means of the mind. The mind alone is the jīva's friend, and the mind alone is its enemy. So, if you wish to practise sādhanā, turn the mind into a friend. If the mind stands as your enemy, there is no uplift for you within this very life. For that, Kṛṣṇa's message is: first, train the mind by the strength of the intellect, and conquer it. Once the mind is conquered, everything is at peace -- no confusion whatsoever remains. This is the state required as preparation for meditation.\n\nOn hearing this message of Kṛṣṇa's, a question confronts us. As Kṛṣṇa himself said earlier, nothing is in our own hands; without Bhagavān's grace we can never independently control our own mind. So how, then, are we to control our mind? This very verse holds the answer to our question. Looked at in the subtle sense where ātmā means Paramātmā, this verse answers our question. \"uddharet ātmanā ātmānam\" means: \"By the Self [by Bhagavān] alone must the self [the jīva] be uplifted.\" Ordinarily the mind does not listen to us; it says, \"I cannot do this.\" But Bhagavān has given us intellect, and we must use it. Using the intellect, pray unceasingly to that Bhagavān: \"Give me a good mind; lead me on the path of uplift; give me the right understanding needed to practise sādhanā.\" He is our most intimate well-wisher and kinsman. He alone is the eternal, selfless, unfeigned, ever-present kinsman. He will surely heed our prayer and come to settle within our mind.\n\nBhagavān manifests in whatever manner we perceive him. The devas saw him as the all-knowing one, and he appeared to them as such and uplifted them. The demons knew him as a killer and an enemy, and hated him -- he appeared to them as an enemy. Hence Kṛṣṇa says: \"ātmā eva ripuḥ ātmanaḥ.\" That is, when we go astray through ego, he appears to us as our own enemy. The next verse sheds further light on this.\n\nbandhuḥ ātmā ātmanaḥ tasya yena ātmā eva ātmanā jitaḥ |\nanātmanaḥ tu śatrutve varteta ātmā eva śatruvat ||6||",
  "6.6": "bandhuḥ ātmā ātmanaḥ tasya yena ātmā eva ātmanā jitaḥ | anātmanaḥ tu śatrutve varteta ātmā eva śatruvat -- For the jīva who, by discernment, can conquer the self [conquer Bhagavān through devotion] -- for the jīva by whom the self is subdued -- the self [Bhagavān] is a friend. For one who cannot conquer the self [Bhagavān], the self [Bhagavān] alone stands as an adversary, like an enemy.\n\nTo those who have won Bhagavān -- who have become fit for Bhagavān's love -- he is a kinsman. To become fit for his love one needs his grace; and to those who, by his grace, have won him over and become fit for his love, he is a kinsman. Those who turn their back on Bhagavān and go forward are ever moving away from him. To those who turn their back on him and hate him, he appears as an enemy. For this reason, pray to Bhagavān without ceasing: \"Come and dwell within me as a kinsman; lead me on the good path; turn me toward you and carry me onward.\" Such prayer and surrender make our mind settle within him. This is the first step of meditation. Let us see, in the verses that follow, what happens when one makes this very effort to win Bhagavān's grace in order to subdue the mind.",
  "6.7": "jitātmanaḥ praśāntasya paramātmā samāhitaḥ |\n\nśītoṣṇasukhaduḥkheṣu tathā mānāpamānayoḥ ||7||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and explanation of these two verses (6.7 and 6.8) are given together under the next verse, 6.8.]",
  "6.8": "jitātmanaḥ praśāntasya paramātmā samāhitaḥ |\n\nśītoṣṇasukhaduḥkheṣu tathā mānāpamānayoḥ |\n\njñānavijñānatṛptātmā kūṭasthaḥ vijitendriyaḥ |\n\nyuktaḥ iti ucyate yogī samaloṣṭāśmakāñcanaḥ -- In the mind of one who has conquered himself through devotion, who has stopped being drawn toward sense-objects and delights only in Bhagavān, Bhagavān comes and takes his seat. Such a person stands unshaken amid dualities like cold-heat, pleasure-pain, and honor-dishonor; he is filled with the direct realization and knowledge of Bhagavān; he has his senses under control. To him, earth, stone, and gold are all the same. Such a yoga-sādhaka is said to have reached his goal.\n\nWhen, through devotion, one receives Bhagavān's grace, the mind first comes to rest in him. In no circumstance whatsoever does the mind then become agitated. \"Paramātmā samāhitaḥ\" — Bhagavān comes and settles in that person's heart. Whenever we wish, direct inner vision of him becomes possible. Kṛṣṇa explains here, once again, what the effect of this direct (aparokṣa) knowledge is for those who see Bhagavān in this way.\n\nChiefly, direct knowers are never troubled by the fickleness of the senses. Dishonor never breaks him down. Honor and dishonor are never a great matter to him. One who has reached this state is called accomplished (siddha). He is so free of agitation that he begins to see everything as equal. Wealth and gold all seem unnecessary to him, and he stays distant from them. (Reading the autobiography of Ramakrishna Paramahamsa helps us understand the mental state of a direct knower.)\n\nIn the next verse Kṛṣṇa explains a very important matter — what the mental state of a yogi should be as a preliminary to meditation.",
  "6.9": "suhṛt mitra ari udāsīna madhyastha dveṣya bandhuṣu |\n\nsādhuṣu api ca pāpeṣu samabuddhiḥ viśiṣyate -- One who regards with equal understanding well-wishers, friends, enemies, the indifferent, those who stand between two sides, those we cannot bear, relatives, the good, and the wicked — knowing that all of them are, at root, of the nature of knowledge (regarding each according to what they truly are) — is superior.\n\nThe mind cannot meditate unless it is balanced. If all sorts of thoughts about all sorts of people begin to circle in the mind, meditation becomes impossible. There are many kinds of people in the world. For example: suhṛt — those with a good heart, who help everyone without expecting anything in return. After them, mitra — the friend, who always helps his intimate companion and corrects us when we err. Ari — enemies who wish to kill us out of hostility; there is no need to hate them. Udāsīna — those who are neither for good nor for bad. Madhyastha — those who do good when good is done to them and bad when bad is done to them. Dveṣya — one whom we cannot bear to look at (hatred). And finally, bandhu — relatives. Thus the human race is full of both good and bad people. (Among gods and demons there is no such mixed category — gods are only good, asuras are always sinful; but only among humans do we find both these kinds mixed together.)\n\nFor Bhagavān to be revealed to us in meditation, we must first cultivate equal understanding, within our own heart, toward all these kinds of people. We must recognize that a person's behavior may come from the environment he grew up in, or from a lack of understanding, and take care that no ill feeling toward him remains in our mind. Someone may torment us like an enemy — this may be due to his and our own prārabdha karma. When we see him with the feeling that God is having him play the role given to him in this birth, no hatred arises in us. When another abuses us, we do not become agitated. The feeling that should arise in us is: \"how well is he playing the role Bhagavān has given him!\" Whether relative or enemy, whoever it may be — during meditation, a pure, equal disposition must be established in the mind. When we sit for meditation in this state, none of these people appear before us, and the vision of Bhagavān occurs.\n\nOne point of caution here: this inward equal disposition cannot be applied practically in the outer world in the same way. In practice we must cultivate the four dispositions mentioned earlier — friendliness, compassion, joy, and equanimity (maitrī, karuṇā, mudita, upekṣā). Seeing everyone equally means seeing each in his own proper place — treating a father as a father, a son as a son, staying away from the wicked (upekṣā) — this is the equal vision of the practical world. In the inner world, we must see only the Bhagavān indwelling within everyone. This is the preliminary mental state needed for meditation. We must mentally shed every relational attachment before sitting for meditation. In meditation there is no category of father, mother, teacher, disciple, enemy, friend, and so on. All is one — merely the knowledge-natured jīva, and within it, Bhagavān in the form of the reflected image (bimba). This is the mental state needed for meditation.\n\nHaving thus explained the preparation needed for meditation, Kṛṣṇa next explains the method of sitting down directly for meditation.",
  "6.10": "yogī yuñjīta satatam ātmānam rahasi sthitaḥ |\n\nekākī yata citta ātmā nirāśīḥ aparigrahaḥ -- The yoga-sādhaka should sit alone in solitude, tempering body and mind, without craving, without grasping at anything, and should continually make his inner faculty ready for samādhi.\n\nA person practicing the path of meditation must strive to engage the mind continually in dhyāna-yoga and to make it one-pointed. Where there is a crowd of people, the practice of meditation is impossible. For this reason, the ancients set aside the early hours of dawn (araṇodaya) for meditation.\n\nIf one sits for meditation about ninety-six minutes before sunrise, the whole of nature is at its most peaceful at that hour. This time is most conducive to bringing the mind to concentration. This is called \"brahma-muhūrta.\" [It begins ninety-six minutes (four ghaṭikās) before sunrise and lasts forty-eight minutes (two ghaṭikās) — for example, if sunrise is at 5:44, brahma-muhūrta is from 4:08 to 4:56.] The presiding deity of this muhūrta is the four-faced Brahmā, who is also the deity of our citta. For this reason, this time is most excellent for japa, remembrance, and meditation. Meditation is an extremely inward act. Here there should be only Bhagavān and the sādhaka-jīva. If a third companion comes, it causes trouble. The surrounding atmosphere is spoiled by a person of polluted mind. For this reason, the practice of meditation should always be done in solitude. We must keep our citta and mind under control, allow no craving or desire to enter the mind, and prepare for meditation with a contented mind.\n\nIn the next verse Kṛṣṇa explains what the place for sitting in meditation should be like, and how one should sit.",
  "6.11": "śucau deśe pratiṣṭhāpya sthiram āsanam ātmanaḥ |\n\nna atyucchritam na atinīcam celājinakuśottaram ||11||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and explanation of these two verses (6.11 and 6.12) are given together under the next verse, 6.12.]",
  "6.12": "śucau deśe pratiṣṭhāpya sthiram āsanam ātmanaḥ |\n\nna atyucchritam na atinīcam celājinakuśottaram |\n\ntatra ekāgram manaḥ kṛtvā yaśacittendriyakriyaḥ |\n\nupaviśya āsane yuñjyāt yogam ātmaviśuddhaye -- In a clean place, having set up for oneself a steady seat, neither too high nor too low, spread with cloth, deer-skin, and kuśa-grass, one should sit upon it, holding the mind fixed on one goal, stilling the play of the senses and inner faculty, and should accomplish samādhi for the purification of the self.\n\nThe place chosen for meditation should be clean. A spot in the house with sāttvika vibration should be chosen for meditation. In earlier times, people would identify the spot in the house with the most sāttvika vibration and build the prayer room there. Unfortunately, today we do the opposite — we make whatever leftover space remains in the house into the prayer room. We can easily identify a spot with strong sāttvika vibration by noticing where our mind becomes calm and conducive to meditation on God when we sit there. This is what is meant here by \"śuci deśa\" (a clean place).\n\nIt is not enough merely to identify a place with sāttvika vibration. However good the vibration there, if we sit directly on the ground, the bad vibration in the earth may enter our body. For this reason, our seating arrangement must be correct. A steady seat is of the utmost importance — cushioned seats like a sofa should not be used. Kṛṣṇa explains here what kind of seat is suitable. The seat we sit on should not be too low, nor too high. First a mat of kuśa-grass should be spread at the sitting place. Over it should be spread deer-skin (for householders) or tiger-skin (for those who have renounced household life — lifelong celibates). Over that, cloth (a silk cloth or a cotton blanket). This is the most excellent seat for meditation.\n\nKuśa-grass is prescribed here for use. On the surface, kuśa looks like grass that grows uselessly; but it has a remarkable power to check evil forces in the atmosphere. For this reason it is worn as a ring during yajñas, and placed around the sacrificial fire-pit to control evil forces. During meditation, this kuśa mat helps us by keeping evil forces from troubling us. Deer-skin and cloth provide us a great sāttvika vibration and prevent bad vibration from the earth entering our body. Some of us use a wooden plank instead, but when sitting on a plank there is a possibility that some part of our body touches the ground. For this reason the seat Kṛṣṇa describes above is very suitable. Having prepared such a seat, we should sit erect, in whatever posture is possible for us. Sitting forcibly in a posture that is not possible for us is not conducive to meditation. Generally, vajrāsana is well known; in this posture the body remains erect and it is conducive to meditation. Kṛṣṇa explains this in more detail further ahead.\n\nHaving prepared the seat as described above and sat down, meditation does not become possible right away. Sitting on the seat in a suitable posture, the mind must be made one-pointed (ekāgra) toward Bhagavān. The word \"ekāgra\" has two meanings. Eka-agra — a single target, like an arrow. What is that single target to be aimed at? This same word gives the answer: \"eṣa eva karotīti ekāgrahaḥ\" — fixing the mind on him who is the cause of the creation, sustenance, dissolution, regulation, knowledge-ignorance, bondage, and mokṣa of this world is \"ekāgra.\" Setting aside the habit built up since childhood in favor of a new practice takes the mind a good deal of time. So no one should panic if the mind does not become one-pointed the moment one sits for meditation. Concentration becomes possible only when continuous effort is never given up and sādhana is done with resolve.\n\nOnce the mind is one-pointed, \"yaśacittendriyakriyaḥ\" — all outer sensory activity must be brought to a complete stillness. In this state, outer sound is not heard by us, outer smell does not strike the nose. Even if a mosquito bites, we do not become aware of the pain. This is like sleep; in sleep, our inner world is not awake, but in meditation the inner world remains awake. Having thus stilled the outer sensory activity, the citta must then be controlled. So much accumulated thought lies stored in our citta! But leaving aside all previously recorded matters, only the aspect of Bhagavān's quality should be meditated upon.\n\nControlling our mind and citta in this way requires sādhana across many births. Some, after continuous effort in this sādhana, become disheartened when no siddhi results. But as said before, even a single stumbling step we take in spirituality is never in vain. If siddhi is not achieved in this birth, our sādhana will certainly begin in the next birth from exactly the point where it stood in this one (Kṛṣṇa explains this in more detail further ahead). Therefore there should never be despair — only let the effort be continuous.\n\nOnce our mind and citta have come under control in this way, we must hold them in that very state (yuñjyād yogam). In this state, the grime clinging to our knowledge-blissful self is washed away, and self-purification occurs. Only with such a purified self is it possible to behold the pure Bhagavān.\n\nIn the next verse Kṛṣṇa gives a still clearer picture of the meditation posture (the manner of sitting).",
  "6.13": "samam kāyaśirogrīvam dhārayann acalam sthiraḥ |\n\nsamprekṣya nāsikāgram svam diśaḥ ca anavalokayan ||13||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and explanation of these two verses (6.13 and 6.14) are given together under the next verse, 6.14.]",
  "6.14": "samam kāyaśirogrīvam dhārayann acalam sthiraḥ |\n\nsamprekṣya nāsikāgram svam diśaḥ ca anavalokayan |\n\npraśāntātmā vigatabhīḥ brahmacārivrate sthitaḥ |\n\nmanaḥ saṃyamya maccittaḥ yuktaḥ āsīta matparaḥ -- Holding the back, head, and neck erect and unmoving, steady, without looking about, fixing the gaze on the tip of one's own nose, with a serenely calm self, free of fear, established in the vow of celibacy, restraining the mind, with the citta fixed on me, believing me alone to be the supreme deity, remembering me continually, one should accomplish samādhi.\n\nWhen sitting for meditation, our body above the waist should be erect, in a straight line. The back should not bend; the neck and head should not tilt. The body must be made motionless — not shaken even a little. Without sitting in this way, steadiness of mind is impossible. The mind is like the water inside the vessel that is this body. If the vessel shakes, waves arise in the water that is the mind. This disrupts concentration.\n\nAnother important part of the body is the eye. After steadying the body, the eyes must be steadied. Fixing the gaze on the tip of the nose, the gaze should be made steady in one direction, so that no shape in any direction is seen. (One may meditate with the eyes closed, but closing the eyes carries a greater risk of falling asleep.)\n\nWhen this state of meditation is reached, the mind becomes serenely calm. In this state we experience an indescribable, wondrous bliss, free of all confusion. From this, all fear departs. Another element of the utmost importance for meditation is the observance of celibacy (brahmacarya). Here the word brahmacarya carries three meanings: the vow of moving toward the supreme Brahman; continuous study of the Veda; and, for householders, the observance of regulated sexuality [the capacity to easily stay away from sexuality, along with regulated sexuality], and, for renunciates (lifelong celibates), a life entirely free of sexuality. With unwavering devotion, believing Bhagavān alone to be supreme, placing complete trust in Bhagavān, remembering him continually, and never losing heart, one should accomplish samādhi.\n\nWhat is the effect of meditation? Why should we meditate at all? The answer is in the next verse!\n\nBefore analyzing the next verse, let us consider a question that generally troubles everyone here. Kṛṣṇa taught Arjuna the Bhagavad Gītā while Arjuna stood on the battlefield. Given this, why did he bring up meditation, samādhi in meditation, and the manner of sitting for meditation at all? What was the need for it? This is a question that generally troubles everyone. In truth, Arjuna did not have these questions; Arjuna's question was something else. It was while answering that question that the subject of meditation came up in general, and Kṛṣṇa, so that it might help the entire human community, explained the complete process of meditation. We do not know exactly how subtly Kṛṣṇa taught this to Arjuna on the battlefield. But the sage Vyāsa has unfolded it thread by thread and served it to us in the form of a chapter. When Kṛṣṇa taught the Bhagavad Gītā to Arjuna, what stood before Kṛṣṇa was not Arjuna alone, but the entire sāttvika portion of humankind. The Gītā was taught with the purpose of showing the path of truth to all sāttvika people. We may recall here that, with this very purpose, Śrī Kṛṣṇa taught the Gītā once again to Uddhava.",
  "6.15": "yuñjan evam sadā ātmānam yogī niyata mānasaḥ |\n\nśāntim nirvāṇaparamām matsaṃsthām adhigacchati -- Thus, ever meditating on Bhagavān, the sādhaka of controlled mind experiences, after leaving the body, the bliss of abiding in me alone.\n\nUp to this point in this chapter, what Kṛṣṇa has explained is meditation done through the mind — meditation performed by stilling the outer senses and controlling the citta and mind. Beyond this lies the \"turīya state.\" The state in which our mind is made completely still, and the jīva, of the very nature of the Self, directly beholds Bhagavān, is the turīya state. In this verse Kṛṣṇa gives us a hint of the turīya state. This matter lies hidden here in a mysterious way.\n\nIn meditation, what we see is not Bhagavān directly, but a symbol of Bhagavān — because the mind cannot directly grasp Bhagavān, who is of the nature of knowledge and bliss. For his direct vision, one must go beyond meditation to the turīya state. Bhagavān must be seen through the eye of the jīva's own true nature. Fixing the mind continuously on Bhagavān, once his mental form becomes visible in meditation through the mind, the mind must then be stilled completely. Then the turīya state awakens. At this stage, union of the Self with Bhagavān occurs. Then it becomes possible to see him directly. A meditation-yogi who has reached this state is called a true yogi.\n\nThe greatest peak of bliss a jīva can attain on its path of sādhana is the direct realization (sākṣātkāra) of Bhagavān. There is no greater awareness, no greater vision of bliss, than this. One who has thus realized Bhagavān never again falls into the bondage of this worldly cycle. He remains established in Bhagavān alone and attains mokṣa.\n\nKṛṣṇa next explains what the way of life of those who practice samādhi should be. This way of life is not conducive to meditation alone — it is also very important for our study and for a healthy life. The writers of the scriptures designed every one of our observances in keeping with this. But today we lack this awareness. Whether it is the Ekādaśī fast or any other observance, it should be followed in a way conducive to meditation. Any observance followed merely mechanically, without this knowledge, cannot carry us to any height.",
  "6.16": "na atyaśnataḥ tu yogaḥ asti na ca atyantam anaśnataḥ |\n\nna ca atisvapnaśīlasya jāgrataḥ na eva ca arjuna -- O Arjuna, meditation-yoga is not possible for a glutton; nor for one who eats nothing at all; nor for one who sleeps excessively; nor for the sleepless.\n\nMeditation is not possible for one who eats to excess. When too much food goes into the stomach, the mind becomes dull. Therefore one given to reflection should not be a glutton. Excessive food intake is not good. For this reason it is wrong to force-feed a guest at a meal. In the same way, if one never eats properly, the body loses nourishment and becomes weak. The direct realization of Bhagavān happens only through this very body. Therefore we must cultivate the habit of eating regulated food at regulated times.\n\nAlong with food, our sleeping habit must also be regulated. As we train it, so does our mind become. Excessive sleep is never good. Likewise, if one is sleep-deprived, meditation is certainly impossible. Regulated food and sleep are conducive to our health and are essential for meditation.",
  "6.17": "yukta āhāravihārasya yukta ceṣṭasya karmasu |\n\nyukta svapnāvabodhasya yogaḥ bhavati duḥkhahā -- For one who has regulated eating and recreation, regulated activity in action, and regulated sleep and waking, meditation-yoga removes sorrow.\n\nWhen practicing yoga, our way of life must be disciplined. Regulated and suitable food intake is very important. Sāttvika food that readies the mind for meditation is conducive to meditation. Some foods have a bad effect on our mind — for example, onion and garlic. Even though these have medicinal qualities, they are foods that affect our mind. That we get bad dreams on days we consume onion or garlic in excess is proof that this food affects the mind. [But if a physician prescribes it as medicine for health reasons, there is no harm in eating it, because health must come first, even before study.] Those who undertake Vedic study should not consume such mind-disturbing food. Food that nourishes both our body and, with sāttvika nourishment, our mind, is what Kṛṣṇa here calls \"yukta āhāra.\"\n\nIt is very important to give our mind as much rest as it needs. But this rest should not turn into laziness. When giving the mind rest, it should be given complete relaxation, without any thinking at all. Then the mind functions well, conducive to meditation. \"Yukta-ceṣṭasya karmasu\" — whatever work we must do, we should do it ourselves, in an orderly and disciplined manner. There should be no laziness in work that must be done.\n\nScripturally prescribed, clean, orderly food intake; regulated sleep and waking at the proper time; disciplined effort and rest — when these are so, \"yogo bhavati duḥkhahā\" — a sorrowless, blissful life becomes possible.\n\nThrough meditation-yoga the mind comes into a certain tempering. When the mind is tempered, we grow accustomed to viewing, with detachment, the joys and sorrows we experience in the outer world. From this there is no elation when joy comes, nor collapse into despair when sorrow comes. The equal vision that joy-sorrow and defeat-victory together are simply life comes about through meditation-yoga.",
  "6.18": "yadā viniyatam cittam ātmani eva avatiṣṭhate |\n\nniḥspṛhaḥ sarva kāmebhyaḥ yuktaḥ iti ucyate tadā -- When the well-tempered inner faculty comes to rest in Bhagavān alone, and when longing for all desires has perished, one is then called a \"meditation-yogi.\"\n\nThrough the practice of yoga, a yogi's subconscious comes under control. Generally, past bad memories stored in our citta continually trouble us. Some hatred, some love — setting all of this aside, the yogi is able to seat only Bhagavān on the throne of his inner being. When this is done, the vision of Bhagavān occurs continually within. After beholding that Bhagavān, nothing else seems needed, because Bhagavān is the most excellent of all the things we could desire. Once the spring of inward bliss opens, petty outward desires perish, and one is called a meditation-yogi.",
  "6.19": "yathā dīpaḥ nivātasthaḥ na iṅgate sā upamā matā |\n\nyoginaḥ yaśacittasya yuñjataḥ yogam ātmanaḥ -- As a lamp in a windless place does not flicker — this is the illustration given for the meditation-yogi whose inner being is tempered, remembering Bhagavān.\n\nLike the flame of a fire burning in a place where no wind blows, in the mind of a yogi who has tempered his inner being, the \"yoga concerning Bhagavān\" (ātma-yoga) remains motionless. It does not waver.",
  "6.20": "yatra uparamate cittam niruddham yogasevayā |\n\nyatra ca eva ātmanā ātmānam paśyan ātmani tuṣyati ||20||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and explanation of these four verses (6.20, 6.21, 6.22, and 6.23) are given together under the next verse, 6.23.]",
  "6.21": "sukham ātyantikam yat tat buddhigrāhyam atīndriyam |\n\nvetti yatra na ca eva ayam sthitaḥ calati tattvataḥ ||21||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and explanation of this verse (6.21) are given together with verses 6.20, 6.22, and 6.23 under the next verse, 6.23.]",
  "6.22": "yam labdhvā ca aparam lābham manyate na adhikam tataḥ |\n\nyasmin sthitaḥ na duḥkhena guruṇā api vicālyate ||22||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and explanation of this verse (6.22) are given together with verses 6.20, 6.21, and 6.23 under the next verse, 6.23.]",
  "6.23": "yatra uparamate cittam niruddham yogasevayā |\n\nyatra ca eva ātmanā ātmānam paśyan ātmani tuṣyati ||20||\n\nsukham ātyantikam yat tat buddhigrāhyam atīndriyam |\n\nvetti yatra na ca eva ayam sthitaḥ calati tattvataḥ ||21||\n\nyam labdhvā ca aparam lābham manyate na adhikam tataḥ |\n\nyasmin sthitaḥ na duḥkhena guruṇā api vicālyate ||22||\n\ntam vidyāt duḥkhasaṃyogaviyogam yogasañjñitam |\n\nsaḥ niścayena yoktavyaḥ yogaḥ anirviṇṇacetasā ||23|| -- The inner faculty, tempered by the practice of meditation, does not, in this state, run out toward outward objects. Here one delights, seeing Bhagavān within himself alone (by Bhagavān's own prasāda). Here one experiences the extraordinary bliss that unfolds within, unattainable through the senses. One who has come to stand in this state never again moves away from Bhagavān. Having attained this, no other gain seems greater than it. One who stands here is never shaken even by the greatest sorrow. This alone should be known as yoga — the liberation from the bondage of every sorrow. One who has grown weary of sense-objects and turned his back on them must certainly master this yoga; this yoga is to be practiced with a resolute, undespairing mind.\n\nWhat happens when the mind, losing contact with the outer world, comes to rest in Bhagavān? Kṛṣṇa says: \"uparamate cittam\" — the mind comes to a standstill in that very excellent bliss. The feeling arises that one does not wish to be separated from that spring of bliss. Generally the mind does not go to this state at once; a great deal of effort is needed for it. This effort is not of a single birth; without the effort of many births, reaching this state all at once is impossible. Through continuous practice of yoga, the fluctuations of the citta must be restrained — that is, our mind must be held back. It must be obstructed from running anywhere else, and made to come to rest in Bhagavān alone.\n\nWhen Self-realization comes to us, an unprecedented bliss such as never occurred before arises. This is the experience of the most excellent bliss. When the devotee's devotion to Bhagavān and Bhagavān's affection for the devotee meet together, \"fearlessness\" occurs. Beholding, with the inner eye, in the state of meditation, the indwelling Bhagavān, is the experience that comes in meditation. What we see here is that grand form of Bhagavān that our mind has, so to speak, carved. This is merely a symbol of Bhagavān. For us to truly see Bhagavān, he must love us. When Bhagavān's grace occurs, our own true nature beholds Bhagavān. This is a very intense state. In this state the mind becomes utterly still, and directly, the true nature of the Self beholds Bhagavān through its own eye. This state can grant complete contentment. In that stage, the truth becomes known that the bliss obtained through the senses is relative. After experiencing this intense bliss, there is no further wish to rise higher or descend lower. One who has had this complete experience of Bhagavān is never disturbed by any worldly sorrow. This is the highest state attainable in human life. To see true bliss, we must reach this intense state.\n\nHearing what has been said above, some become deluded and rashly attempt to give up everything at once to reach this state. But doing so never brings success. This bliss can be attained only through continuous sādhana. Only after one has experienced the pleasures of life and come to see that worldly pleasure is enough does the effort to descend into the depths of the spiritual matter. Otherwise there is danger.",
  "6.24": "saṅkalpaprabhavān kāmān tyaktvā sarvān aśeṣataḥ |\n\nmanasā eva indriyagrāmam viniyamya samantataḥ ||24||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and explanation of these two verses (6.24 and 6.25) are given together under the next verse, 6.25.]",
  "6.25": "saṅkalpaprabhavān kāmān tyaktvā sarvān aśeṣataḥ |\n\nmanasā eva indriyagrāmam viniyamya samantataḥ |\n\nśanaiḥ śanaiḥ uparamet buddhyā dhṛtigṛhītayā |\n\nātmasaṃstham manaḥ kṛtvā na kiñcit api cintayet -- Having completely given up every desire that arises from resolve; having, by the mind alone, brought the entire assembly of the senses under control from every side; one should, very gradually, through an understanding gripped by firm resolve, withdraw the mind from sense-objects and place it in Bhagavān — and, having placed it there, think of nothing else.\n\nKṛṣṇa again explains here the method for establishing the mind in Bhagavān. Kṛṣṇa says: \"saṅkalpaprabhavān kāmān tyaktvā sarvān aśeṣataḥ.\" We have already seen the background of this. The root of all our problems is our own resolves (saṅkalpa) — the decisions in the mind about what all must be done. Before doing any work, we do it mentally first. \"I must go here today, I must meet such-and-such person, I must obtain such-and-such there\" — such a schedule is planted in our mind. A thousand resolves like this, and a thousand desires to fulfill them. \"This must be done, that must result from it\" — such wishes. Life is the attempt to give outward shape, in the external world, to the pattern of life imagined in our mind. If we keep filling the mind with thousands of such resolves and desires this way, the mind gets no chance to think beyond them at all. The mind is nothing but resolve and counter-resolve — building some dream or other (saṅkalpa), and then thinking whether to do it or not, whether it will succeed or not if done (vikalpa), and getting confused by this. The mind never goes beyond this. Kṛṣṇa says: \"First give up your resolves.\" These resolves are like a beggar's dream. The more resolves that come to our mind, the more despair grows, and from that, agitation. After that, one is unable to think of anything at all!\n\nTo desire is the natural quality of the mind; there is no living without desiring. For this, we must learn to desire only that whatever comes, let it come, and give up our other resolves and counter-resolves. This is the formula for success. Since what is desired is not obtained, and things do not happen as desired, there is no meaning at all in building unnecessary castles of dreams. Our resolve should be only one, and one alone: \"as Bhagavān's resolve is, so let it be.\" As Madhvācārya says in the Dvādaśa Stotra: perform your own enjoined action, and eat what comes; may awareness of Hari's feet never fail; let there be no separate resolve of \"mine.\" As the Veda says: \"let Bhagavān's desire become my desire, let his will become my will, so that my desire is in tune with Bhagavān's desire.\" When this is so, no discordant note arises. When our desire resonates with Bhagavān's will (a state of resonance), the music of life produces sweetness. Kṛṣṇa says \"sarvān aśeṣataḥ,\" meaning give up all your resolves completely. There should not be even one resolve within us that runs contrary to Bhagavān's will. \"Whatever you (Bhagavān) desire, that alone is my resolve\" — this is the one and only resolve a yogi must engage in during meditation.\n\nA resolve must be checked exactly where it is born. Resolve is born from the mind itself! We must continually tell our mind: \"do not make foolish resolves; if you do, you will not get it, and afterward you will fret over not getting it — so give up these foolish resolves.\" Telling the mind this repeatedly in this way, it must be tamed. It is the mind that leads a sādhaka astray, and it is the mind that leads him further. So if we keep telling the mind this continually, it eventually accepts it.\n\nTo give up our foolish resolves, we must first keep our assembly of senses under control. We must cultivate good saṃskāra within ourselves. For this, in the past, children were taught many spiritual saṃskāras from a young age. In childhood the mind is like fresh clay — whatever is told is absorbed exactly as it is. The nature of the mind is such that when we restrain it in one place, it slips away in another. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Do not let the mind slip away; give up all resolve, accept what Bhagavān has provided as his prasāda; enjoy it, experience it with joy; do not grieve, wanting what you do not have.\"\n\nAs said before, the mind cannot be turned all at once. It must be turned very slowly. Little by little, the mind must be turned toward that excellent bliss. For this, Kṛṣṇa says, \"use your analytical intellect.\" This requires resolve, it requires courage. The conviction that \"I will not be shaken until I reach the goal,\" and courage (dhṛti), are very important. In this way, going beyond logic, the mind must be readied toward the Bhagavān who is beyond logic. The mind must first recognize our own true nature and come to rest there, and afterward come to rest in the Bhagavān present within that true nature. Once the mind has come to rest, it must be established there without exposing it to any other worry.",
  "6.26": "yataḥ yataḥ niścarati manaḥ cañcalam asthiram |\n\ntataḥ tataḥ niyamya etat ātmani eva vaśam nayet -- Wherever this untempered, fickle, unsteady mind runs off to, from that very place it should be drawn back and brought under control, so that it comes to rest in Bhagavān alone.\n\nOur mind is very fickle. For this reason it keeps running here and there. Wherever it slips off to, right there we must place Bhagavān as a barrier and make it come to rest in Bhagavān. This requires continuous effort.",
  "6.27": "praśāntamanasam hi enam yoginam sukham uttamam |\n\nupaiti śāntarajasam brahmabhūtam akalmaṣam -- The greatest bliss comes upon such a yogi — one whose mind has ceased running toward sense-objects and become serene, whose heat of rajo-guṇa has been extinguished and become clear, who has come to rest in Bhagavān alone.\n\nOnce our mind has come to rest in Bhagavān, the mind that was a den of confusion becomes serene. A person who has achieved this is never agitated in any situation. In this state one comes to know what the truly excellent bliss is that we ought to experience in life. The disappointment present in the rājasic pleasure the mind ordinarily desires is never present here. In this state we become clean. This is true purity. There is no use at all in achieving outward purity without achieving purity of mind. Nothing is impure to a pure mind! If the mind is pure, there is no possibility at all of impurity befalling that person. This is the supreme, blissful state a human being can attain in his lifetime.",
  "6.28": "yuñjan evam sadā ātmānam yogī vigatakalmaṣaḥ |\n\nsukhena brahmasaṃsparśam atyantam sukham aśnute -- Thus, having shed all grime, the sādhaka who continually meditates upon Bhagavān happily partakes of the supreme bliss of the experience of Bhagavān.\n\nKṛṣṇa here explains, in yet another way, the inward bliss that occurs upon the experience of Bhagavān. Kṛṣṇa says: \"yuñjann evaṃ sadātmānaṃ yogī vigatakalmaṣaḥ.\" This statement of Kṛṣṇa's can be seen in two forms: first, \"vigatakalmaṣaḥ san ātmānaṃ yuñjan,\" and second, \"ātmānaṃ yuñjan san vigatakalmaṣaḥ.\" We must first behold Bhagavān with a mind free of grime. Then, meditating upon Bhagavān, we must become free of grime. It is possible to cleanse the mind for meditation on Bhagavān and behold him once. But the moment we descend from the experience of Bhagavān, a tāmasic impulse may spoil our mind. For this reason one must remain ever established in the experience of Bhagavān. This does not mean remaining in meditation for all twenty-four hours of the day; rather, it means remaining always immersed in the thought of Bhagavān. When this is so, the mind remains continuously clean. When awareness of Bhagavān is thus made firm, the sādhaka effortlessly attains the supremely blissful experience of beholding Bhagavān directly.\n\nHaving explained the process of meditation, Kṛṣṇa explains in the next verse what quality of contemplation we should hold regarding Bhagavān. God does not mean some person seated somewhere in an inner chamber of Vaikuṇṭha, beyond Satyaloka. Here Kṛṣṇa explains what our contemplation of God should be like.",
  "6.29": "sarva bhūta stham ātmānam sarva bhūtāni ca ātmani |\n\nīkṣate yogayukta ātmā sarvatra samadarśanaḥ -- A sādhaka whose inner being is readied for meditation-yoga sees Bhagavān within all beings, and sees all beings within Bhagavān. He sees the Bhagavān present everywhere as one and the same.\n\nWe call Bhagavān \"brahma,\" we call him \"paramātmā.\" What do these mean? Brahma is that vast thing which fills all times and all places. Paramātmā is that which pervades everywhere, full of every quality, and is infinite in time, in place, in quality, and in power. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"sarvabhūtasthamātmānam\" — meaning, within every jīva-being, Bhagavān is present as the indweller, in the form of a reflected image. Not only that — \"sarva bhūtāni ca ātmani,\" meaning all beings are within Bhagavān. The full meaning of this is: Bhagavān fills us both within and without. This is the chief matter we must understand when we perform upāsanā. This is how a sādhaka established in meditation-yoga sees Bhagavān's all-pervasiveness.\n\nThough Bhagavān fills every being, a sādhaka sees him as \"sarvatra samadarśanaḥ\" — that is, in one and the same form. Bhagavān's avatāra, his indwelling presence (āveśa), his nearness, his qualities, his acts, his forms — all of these are undivided. There is no difference among them. He who fills everything, within and without, is one and the same. This contemplation — that Bhagavān is all-indwelling and all-pervading — must be present in our daily worship.",
  "6.30": "yaḥ mām paśyati sarvatra sarvam ca mayi paśyati |\n\ntasya aham na praṇaśyāmi saḥ ca me na praṇaśyati -- One who sees me everywhere, and sees everything within me — to him I am never lost, and he is never lost to me.\n\nContinuing, Kṛṣṇa says: \"One who thus sees me in everyone and everyone in me — to him I never become nonexistent. The responsibility for his welfare (yoga-kṣema) is always mine. In the same way, the awareness that 'I am looking after his welfare' remains ever present in him.\"\n\nThus this mutual relationship with Bhagavān — the awareness that \"God never abandons us\" — remains ever awake within us. As said before, Bhagavān is as we see him to be. For this reason, because of this awareness within us, no problem in life ever remains unresolved for us. Even when it comes through prārabdha-karma, we experience an unseen hand guiding and leading us. The courage arises that \"there is one who protects me, who looks after my welfare — why should I fear?\" Bad thoughts recede. One is never disturbed by social reactions, criticism, slander, or reproach, whatever they may be. The devotee never comes to a state where Bhagavān has abandoned him. Bhagavān is never distant from his own intimate devotee. For this reason Bhagavān is called \"bhakta-parādhīna\" (subject to his devotee). Kṛṣṇa explains this further, in more detail, in the next verse.",
  "6.31": "sarvabhūtasthitam yaḥ mām bhajati ekatvam āsthitaḥ |\n\nsarvathā vartamānaḥ api saḥ yogī mayi vartate -- One who, standing in the awareness of oneness, worships me as present within all beings — that yogi remains in me, in whatever condition he lives.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"The sādhaka who holds the contemplation that the all-pervading Bhagavān is one and the same everywhere, and who has continuous devotion — whatever his condition, it is certain that he reaches me.\" One who has attained direct (aparokṣa) knowledge never moves away from Bhagavān. He attains mokṣa and abides in Bhagavān.",
  "6.32": "ātmaupamyena sarvatra samam paśyati yaḥ arjuna |\n\nsukham vā yadi vā duḥkham saḥ yogī paramaḥ mataḥ -- O Arjuna, one who, comparing others to himself, sees everyone alike everywhere — whether in joy or in sorrow — is judged to be the highest sādhaka.\n\nKṛṣṇa has spoken to us before about equal vision. Here he mainly explains how a sādhaka is to cultivate equal vision with the sāttvika and with the devotees of Bhagavān. The best among yogis knows another to be just like himself. He knows the truth that whatever brings us sorrow brings sorrow to another as well. He conducts himself with the awareness that everyone has both joy and sorrow, and that, just like himself, others too do not wish for sorrow. Whoever feels another's pain as keenly as his own pain, and whoever rejoices at another's happiness and success as his own success — he is a very great yoga-sādhaka.\n\nBhagavān has a special presence within his devotees. Therefore, when we respond to their joys and sorrows, it becomes worship of the Bhagavān dwelling within them.\n\nUp to this point in this chapter, Kṛṣṇa has spoken at length about meditation. In Kṛṣṇa's explanation, the one common difficulty everyone finds is this: \"we, who have a fickle mind, must control it and cultivate equal vision\" — is this really possible? In the next verse Arjuna raises this very question with Kṛṣṇa, on behalf of all of us.",
  "6.33": "arjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna said:\n\nyaḥ ayam yogaḥ tvayā proktaḥ sāmyena madhusūdana |\n\netasya aham na paśyāmi cañcalatvāt sthitim sthirām -- O Madhusūdana, you have taught this meditation-yoga of equal vision. It seems to me that, because the mind is fickle, it is not possible to stand firm in it.\n\nArjuna addresses Śrī Kṛṣṇa as \"O Madhusūdana\" — the one who gives joy to the sāttvika (as the slayer of the demon Madhu) — and says: \"How can we possibly adopt this system of meditation you have summarized? Meditation is an entirely mental process. But our mind is turbulent and fickle. Controlling such a mind and accomplishing this steady meditation-yoga you have taught seems impossible,\" says Arjuna.",
  "6.34": "cañcalam hi manaḥ kṛṣṇa pramādi balavat dṛḍham |\n\ntasya aham nigraham manye vāyoḥ iva su duṣkaram -- Kṛṣṇa, is not the mind fickle? It is so strong and powerful that it can overwhelm us. To restrain it, I feel, is as hard as restraining the wind.\n\nThe mind is very fickle; it keeps leaping from one object to another. My mind is continually overpowering me, but I am not able to overpower it. It is the mind's nature to bend another to its own will. It smashes our resolve regarding Bhagavān to pieces and takes it entirely under its own control. How can an ordinary person restrain such a mind? Just as it is impossible to grasp the blowing wind in one's fist, so, I feel, it is impossible to restrain the mind, says Arjuna.\n\nThis question of Arjuna's will have partly arisen in the mind of every one of our readers while reading this chapter up to this point. \"All of this is not for people like us,\" or \"this is not possible for us\" — this is generally the first thought that comes to our mind. Knowing this, \"Nara,\" the representative of all of us, has questioned Nārāyaṇa in this way here. Now you will all be eager to know how Kṛṣṇa answers Nara's question — because we too have the resolve that our fickle mind must be overpowered, and, having overpowered it, established in Bhagavān. But we also fear that this mind is immensely powerful! Come, let us see in the remainder of this chapter what Madhusūdana answered to our question.",
  "6.35": "bhagavān uvāca -- Bhagavān said:\n\nasaṃśayam mahābāho manaḥ durnigraham calam |\n\nabhyāsena tu kaunteya vairāgyeṇa ca gṛhyate -- O mighty-armed one, without doubt, the mind is fickle and hard to restrain. But, O Kaunteya, it can be grasped through practice and through detachment.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"There is no doubt in what you say. Restraining the mind is not an easy task, but it can be gradually accomplished through practice and detachment.\" We cannot accomplish anything without effort. Effort is needed for everything. We have grown accustomed to dancing as our mind dictates. We have never made the effort to have the mind listen to us instead. Here Kṛṣṇa emphasizes two matters: detachment (vairāgya) and practice (abhyāsa). Vairāgya means keeping at a distance, not feeding the mind, whatever it is toward which the mind ought not to run. God has given us the intellect to know what is good and what is bad. Using that intellect to keep our mind away from what is bad, not introducing the mind to what is bad — whatever obstructs our growth, keeping it away from the mind — this is vairāgya. Along with this, continuous, unfailing practice toward concentration is needed. By this we can certainly control the mind, says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nGenerally, we give our mind unwanted training every single day. For example, we engage our mind completely with whatever daily television serial or film happens to be on, in our precious time. We have no time at all to read spiritual texts! Even while worshiping God, we are anxious about the next episode of the serial. Instead of reading some bad book or watching some bad serial before sleeping at night, we should cultivate the habit of reading a good spiritual text, and cultivate detachment toward what we do not need. This is just one example. If we carefully observe our daily activity, we will find a thousand examples like this.\n\nThe mind does not pay much attention to a matter it does not know. But once it has had a taste of it, it will not leave you. A good example of this is the drinking of alcohol. To one who has never drunk alcohol, it appears repulsive. But once, under some compulsion, we drink unwanted alcohol and introduce it to our mind, the mind then says: \"you've already had a drink once anyway, nothing will happen if you have one more — drink!\" There is no need to explain here what the result of this is.\n\nAnother great fault of ours is poking our head into matters that do not concern us. When some remark like \"so-and-so did such-and-such\" falls on our ear, we poke our head into that matter without pausing to think what use it is to us to know it. Once we poke our head in, the mind will not let it go. Giving up everything else, it chases after that matter. Therefore, before poking our head into some matter, we must make a deliberate, intelligent decision about it, and hold the mind back from running after unnecessary thoughts. A fine illustration of this is a well-known story about the Greek philosopher Socrates and his \"triple filter.\" A certain acquaintance once came to Socrates eager to tell him some gossip he had heard about a friend of his. Before letting him speak, Socrates asked him to test what he was about to say through three filters: was he certain it was true; was it something good; and was it something useful to Socrates? The man had to admit that his news failed all three tests — he had only heard it, it was not good, and it would serve no purpose. Socrates concluded that something neither true, nor good, nor useful was hardly worth telling at all, and turned him away.\n\nFriends, what we must learn here is this: before engaging the mind in some idle matter, learn to filter it three times over. Cultivate detachment toward what is unnecessary, and train the mind in the sādhana of concentration. For those who walk this path, meditation-yoga is never impossible.",
  "6.36": "asaṃyatātmanā yogaḥ duṣprāpaḥ iti me matiḥ |\n\nvaśyātmanā tu satatam śakyaḥ avāptum aśeṣataḥ -- For one whose self is untempered, samādhi is not something to be had — this is my judgment. But one who has always kept the self under control can fully accomplish samādhi.\n\nFor one who, out of laziness or evil worldly craving, does not make this effort, samādhi never comes within reach. One must overpower the mind by the means of detachment and practice, and make it do the work we require of it. When this is done, samādhi can be accomplished — this is the assurance Kṛṣṇa gives here.\n\nIn this answer of Kṛṣṇa's we have found the means needed for meditation. But now another question confronts us all. It is this: if we, who are engaged in worldly life, happen to stop midway in the path of sādhana without accomplishing complete samādhi, or if this birth of ours ends after only partial sādhana, what then becomes of us? Is the sādhana of this birth in vain? Is it impossible for me to reach Bhagavān? Arjuna asks this very question of ours to Kṛṣṇa in the next verse.",
  "6.37": "arjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna said:\n\nayatiḥ śraddhayā upetaḥ yogāt calitamānasaḥ |\n\naprāpya yogasaṃsiddhim kām gatim kṛṣṇa gacchati -- Kṛṣṇa, one who has faith but whose effort has not succeeded, whose mind has slipped from meditation-yoga — what becomes of one who, unable to attain the accomplishment of yoga, has this happen to him?\n\nOne who has practiced sādhana in yoga, who has made the effort to meditate, who has striven with faith toward concentration up to a certain stage, but who, for some reason, has not reached the full goal — what becomes of such a person? Do such people not attain siddhi?",
  "6.38": "kaccit na ubhaya vibhraṣṭaḥ chinna abhram iva naśyati |\n\napratiṣṭhaḥ mahābāho vimūḍhaḥ brahmaṇaḥ pathi -- O mighty-armed one, does he not, having found no footing, having lost his way on the path to Bhagavān, vanish like a torn cloud, belonging neither here nor there?\n\nDoes the life of such a person, belonging neither to this world nor to the next, vanish uselessly, like a single torn scrap of cloud that neither casts a shadow on one side nor showers rain on the other, of no use to anything? You, who are of the nature of knowledge, strength, and will (Mahābāho) — what do you do with such people who have fallen from the path?\n\nThis is my doubt, Kṛṣṇa...",
  "6.39": "etat me saṃśayam kṛṣṇa chettum arhasi aśeṣataḥ |\n\ntvat anyaḥ saṃśayasya asya chettā na hi upapadyate -- Kṛṣṇa, you alone are capable of resolving this doubt of mine completely. Apart from you, there is no one else able to resolve this doubt.\n\nArjuna's humble acknowledgment — that Bhagavān alone is capable of answering. \"Tvad-anyaḥ\" — apart from you. \"Na hy upapadyate\" — it is not possible. At the end of the question comes the devotee's surrender — a fitting conclusion.",
  "6.40": "bhagavān uvāca -- Bhagavān said:\n\npārtha na eva iha na amutra vināśaḥ tasya vidyate |\n\nna hi kalyāṇakṛt kaścit durgatim tāta gacchati -- O Pārtha, his sādhana is not in vain — not here, and not in the next world. My child, one who does good never comes to a bad end.\n\nKṛṣṇa's answer to Arjuna's question is a great assurance. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Those who have walked the path of sādhana lose nothing at all, either in this world or in the next. O my dear friend, one who has done good never receives evil in return. This is the law of nature.\" Here Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as \"Pārtha\" — one who has known the far shore. One who has taken steps on the path of sādhana, even if he does not behold Bhagavān all at once, never drowns in the middle of the path. He surely reaches the shore beyond his sādhana. One who has done good, who has walked a good path, never has evil befall him. Some of us say: \"I have never done anything wrong in my life, I have never wished ill on anyone, so why has God done this to me?\" That we have not done wrong or wished ill \"in this life\" is limited only to this birth. But the course of the jīva is beginningless and endless — a stream flowing from birth to birth. If we experience some tragedy or hardship in this birth, there must certainly be some other action of ours behind it, in some other birth. One who does no wrong does not receive wrong. One who does good cannot fail to receive good. This is a truth for all time. There is no exception to it. Kṛṣṇa explains further ahead how this is so.",
  "6.41": "prāpya puṇyakṛtām lokān uṣitvā śāśvatīḥ samāḥ |\n\nśucīnām śrīmatām gehe yogabhraṣṭaḥ abhijāyate -- Such a person, fallen from yoga, attains the worlds of those who have earned merit, dwells there for a long time, and is then born in the household of the pure and the wealthy.\n\nFor one who has practiced yoga, striven for meditation on Bhagavān, and climbed some rungs of the ladder, but who, for some reason — mental distraction, or the end of his lifespan — has stopped there, his sādhana is never in vain. Why this is so, Kṛṣṇa has already explained in the previous chapter (Ch. 2, verse 40), and he explains it again here with emphasis. Kṛṣṇa says there is no trouble at all if sādhana stops midway. Because, after giving up the body, such a person attains the world attained by those who have earned merit. Having enjoyed happiness there for a very long time, he is then born again on earth in the household of the pure, wealthy, and learned in the Veda. No lack of worldly enjoyment will trouble him. He obtains the most excellent environment needed for the sādhana of yoga.",
  "6.42": "athavā yoginām eva kule bhavati dhīmatām |\n\netat hi durlabhataram loke janma yat īdṛśam -- Or else he is born in the very family of wise yogis. Such a birth as this is exceedingly rare to obtain in this world.\n\nContinuing, Kṛṣṇa says: if not so, he is born in the household of accomplished yogis. By this, he receives initiation into yoga from the very womb. Whatever rung of sādhana he had climbed in his previous birth, he easily reaches that same rung again and gets the opportunity to continue his sādhana from there onward. This is the special feature of spiritual sādhana. If one climbs two rungs in one birth, in the next birth one's foot lands directly on the third rung. For this reason no sādhana is ever in vain — there is never any need for despair on this account. Such a matured jīva, being born in the household of accomplished yogis, receives there the hereditary sāttvika pull (genetic forces) and the environment needed for it (environmental forces), and continues his sādhana there. Such a birth in the household of accomplished yogis is very rare in the world. This fortune belongs only to those who have risen high on the path of sādhana.",
  "6.43": "tatra tam buddhisaṃyogam labhate paurvadaihikam |\n\nyatate ca tataḥ bhūyaḥ saṃsiddhau kurunandana -- In this birth too, he regains the connection to the discernment of his previous birth. O joy of the Kurus, he then strives once again toward accomplishment.\n\nWhatever sādhana he had done in his previous birth lies hidden in his subconscious, and the moment he gets an environment suited to it, it awakens. The growth of this birth becomes the continuation of the growth of the previous birth. If there was not much growth in the previous birth, one must begin it here — there is no need to be alarmed about it. The saṃskāra of a previous birth is always with us, and with that saṃskāra we must continue on the path of sādhana. Meditation-yoga is not the sādhana of a single birth.",
  "6.44": "pūrvābhyāsena tena eva hriyate hi avaśaḥ api saḥ |\n\njijñāsuḥ api yogasya śabdabrahma ativartate -- By the force of his former practice, he is drawn along, even without his own awareness. Even one who merely desires to know yoga goes beyond the Veda-word to the supreme Brahman.\n\nEven if not born in the household of the Veda-knowing, still, by the fruit of the sādhana of a previous birth, our mind is drawn toward sādhana without our own awareness. The story of Prahlāda and Piṅgalā is a fine illustration of this. If there is, within, a constant yearning for the spiritual, an utterly intense concern for it, such people rise even beyond those who have studied the scriptures.",
  "6.45": "prayatnāt yatamānaḥ tu yogī saṃśuddha kilbiṣaḥ |\n\nanekajanmasaṃsiddhaḥ tataḥ yāti parām gatim -- The sādhaka who engages in meditation, striving through effort, sheds the grime of the mind, and, having attained accomplishment through many births, reaches Bhagavān, the highest abode.\n\nAs said before, our sādhana spans many births. First a desire is born within us; from that, Bhagavān grants, in the next birth, the environment for that desire to be fulfilled; there, through effort, the inner grime is washed away, and clean knowledge comes through sādhana. Through this knowledge and sādhana, after many births, it becomes possible to go beyond the Veda-word and behold Bhagavān. Thus a seeker (jijñāsu) with intense inward concern can reach mokṣa even beyond those learned in the scriptures.\n\nMost important in sādhana is the sādhana of the inward world. Whatever bathing, worship, homa, and havana one performs outwardly, greater than all of it is the inward world. Kṛṣṇa explains this in the next verse.",
  "6.46": "tapasvibhyaḥ adhikaḥ yogī jñānibhyaḥ api mataḥ adhikaḥ |\n\nkarmibhyaḥ ca adhikaḥ yogī tasmāt yogī bhava arjuna -- The meditation-yogi is greater than those who practice austerities; he is judged greater even than the merely learned; the meditation-yogi is greater than the karma-yogis too. Therefore, O Arjuna, become a meditation-yogi.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: whoever is able to meditate one-pointedly on Bhagavān inwardly is greater than one who performs mere austerity (tapas). Here tapas means the vows we observe — bodily mortification, the Ekādaśī fast, the observance of celibacy, and so on. One who meditates inwardly is dearer to Bhagavān than one who performs hundreds of outward vows, homas, and havanas. Kṛṣṇa says he is greater even than the merely learned — meaning, one who meditates is greater than one who merely knows about meditation and the scriptures. Today one finds many who talk about spirituality, but true meditation-yogis are exceedingly rare to find. As the American writer Thoreau observed in Walden, there are nowadays professors of philosophy, but not philosophers.\n\nSome of us are given to ritual action — performing yajñas, worshiping Bhagavān through the mouth of fire. This action is for the sake of beholding Bhagavān within. The same holds for image worship. We must have the knowledge that our worship is not confined merely to the image of God, but that it is a symbol that helps us behold Bhagavān within. Without mental worship, outward worship is not worship at all. Therefore the meditation-yogi who performs mental worship is greater than one who is merely a ritualist performing outward worship. \"Therefore become a meditation-yogi in life; engage in inward meditation. Once you are engaged in that meditation, your outward actions will not stain you. Then even war becomes worship of Bhagavān\" — this is Kṛṣṇa's message to Arjuna.",
  "6.47": "yoginām api sarveṣām matgatena antaḥ ātmanā |\n\nśraddhāvān bhajate yaḥ mām saḥ me yuktatamaḥ mataḥ -- Of all yogis, the one who worships me with his inner being fixed on me, with faith, is judged by me to be the greatest yogi of all.\n\nWhile meditating, we may fix the mind on anything at all and meditate. Some worship many minor deities, spirits, and ghosts. But Kṛṣṇa says: \"whoever meditates with the mind fixed on Bhagavān alone is the greatest yogi of all.\" If one meditates on something other than Bhagavān, it is of no use at all. Our astrologers generally say, \"for such-and-such planetary affliction, worship such-and-such deity.\" This means: see Bhagavān within that symbol. Whatever deity one worships, if one does not worship Bhagavān within that symbol, it does not become true yoga. The desire to see Bhagavān alone in meditation, and to see him alone within every symbol, is the most excellent yoga. When one lives in this way, one's very life becomes a yoga.\n\nThus ends the sixth chapter.\n\nHere ends the first ṣaṭka (the first six chapters) of the Bhagavad Gītā. In this ṣaṭka, Kṛṣṇa has explained the knowledge and the sāṅkhya of the worship of Bhagavān. The first ṣaṭka of the Gītā explains the method by which the worship of Bhagavān is to be performed with knowledge. Ahead comes the subject of Bhagavān's worship and the details of his glory.",
  "7.1": "mayi āsakta-manāḥ pārtha yogam yuñjan mat-āśrayaḥ |\nasaṃśayam samagram mām yathā jñāsyasi tat śṛṇu -- O Pārtha, with your mind fixed on me, taking refuge in me alone, practicing yoga — hear now how you shall know me fully, without any doubt.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: after taming the mind — which is like a rutting elephant or a wild bull — and ripening it through disciplined practice, after fixing it upon Bhagavān with unshaking, firm devotion, free of ego, and after complete surrender (with the constant awareness that we are, at all times, subject to Bhagavān), and after offering oneself entirely to Bhagavān through meditation-yoga — \"hear now how you may know Bhagavān to the full capacity of your understanding.\"",
  "7.2": "jñānam te aham sa-vijñānam idam vakṣyāmi aśeṣataḥ |\nyat jñātvā na iha bhūyaḥ anyat jñātavyam avaśiṣyate -- I shall now declare to you, fully and without remainder, this jñāna together with vijñāna — knowing which, nothing further remains to be known in this world.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"I will tell you that, knowing which, everything is known.\" There is a saying in the Upaniṣads: \"yasmin vijñāte sarvam idaṃ vijñātaṃ bhavati\" (Śāṇḍilya 2.2; Muṇḍaka 1.1.3) — \"knowing which, everything else becomes known.\" If we know Bhagavān, we have known this entire world; but one cannot come to know Bhagavān merely by knowing the world. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"I shall give you that vijñāna, knowing which nothing further remains to be known.\" Knowledge (jñāna) becomes complete (samagra) only when it becomes vijñāna. Vijñāna is 'vi' + 'jñāna' — 'vi' here is a prefix meaning distinctive, particular, or special. To know a matter penetrating to its very root is vijñāna. Kṛṣṇa is speaking here of vijñāna. \"God exists, God is all-capable, God is the indweller of all\" — this is jñāna; to know in detail how he is present in each and every thing, and in what special power (vibhūti) he stands within each thing — that is vijñāna.",
  "7.3": "manuṣyāṇām sahasreṣu kaścit yatati siddhaye |\nyatatām api siddhānām kaścit mām vetti tattvataḥ -- Among thousands of men, some rare one strives for perfection; and among those who strive and attain perfection, some rare one knows me in truth.\n\nWho can know Bhagavān to the very root? Kṛṣṇa says: \"Among thousands of human beings, a few strive for perfection. Among those who take up this yoga-striving and attain the goal, it is hard even to say that any one of them has truly understood me.\" Even among those who have risen to the heights of direct (aparokṣa) knowledge, those who know Bhagavān in his true nature (tattvataḥ) are exceedingly rare.",
  "7.4": "bhūmiḥ āpaḥ analaḥ vāyuḥ kham manaḥ buddhiḥ eva ca |\nahaṅkāraḥ iti iyam me bhinnā prakṛtiḥ aṣṭadhā -- Earth, water, fire, wind, space, mind, and intellect, and (with its latent awareness) ego — thus is this, my subordinate material nature (jaḍa-prakṛti), divided eightfold.\n\nThis verse presents, in summary, the cosmic form of Bhagavān's creation of the world. Before creation, this entire world lay in subtle form within Bhagavān's own being; afterward, Bhagavān brought forth this creation. What was formed first was this jaḍa-prakṛti — the five elements (earth, water, fire, wind, space) together with mind, intellect, and ego: eight in all. This entire gross world is a modification of these five elements; hence the five elements are the root substance of every material thing in this world.\n\nA question may arise here: what does it mean to say that space (ākāśa) itself was \"created\"? Fundamentally, the creation of space is not an absolute creation but a relative one. The unmanifest expanse (Space) already existed; what is called the \"creation of space\" is Bhagavān's manifesting his own form within it. Within the colorless expanse, an invisible blue-hued (Ultraviolet) space came into being — this, Madhvācārya notes, was already recorded in the thirteenth century. Vibration arose within it, and from that vibration wind was born; from the vibration of wind, fire; and from fire, water. This water, over time, solidified into earth. Thus from these five elements countless things were formed.\n\nFirst, from the five elements, the food-sheath (annamaya-kośa) was formed; then from fire, wind, and space, the vital-sheath (prāṇamaya-kośa); within that, from mind and intellect, the mind-sheath (manomaya-kośa); and then, from the faculty of memory — the ego-principle (awareness of self, citta, cetana) — the knowledge-sheath (vijñānamaya-kośa) came to be. To say \"mind was created\" means the presiding deity of mind was created; to say \"earth was created\" means, along with jaḍa earth, the presiding deity of earth was created. Likewise, the \"creation of wind\" means the creation of the forty-nine deities associated with wind — for, as is known, forty-nine layers of air envelop the earth, divided according to varying air-pressure at varying distances from the earth, each regulated by its own presiding deity. (Full detail on this, and on the science of aerial craft, can be found in the Bhāradvāja Saṃhitā.) Thus, under Bhagavān's governance, the eightfold akṣara-prakṛti took shape, and within this eightfold world Bhagavān filled it, denoted by the eight syllables (aṣṭākṣara).\n\nThe first creation of sound is the syllable Om, which itself has eight component sounds — from the navel to the end of utterance: a-kāra, u-kāra, ma-kāra, nāda, bindu, kalā, śānta, atiśānta. Through these eight sounds, Bhagavān filled this eightfold prakṛti in eight forms: viśva, taijasa, prājña, turīya, ātma, antarātma, paramātma, and jñānātma. Thus the whole world took on its eightfold modification. (In spirituality every number carries a Vedāntic significance; we have seen here the importance of the number eight in the process of creation — Bannanje's small work \"Aṅkeyalli Adhyātma\" [Spirituality in Numbers] sheds further light on this.)",
  "7.5": "aparā iyam itaḥ tu anyām prakṛtim viddhi me parām |\njīva-bhūtām mahābāho yayā idam dhāryate jagat -- This is the lower (nature); but know, O mighty-armed one, another, higher prakṛti of mine — the one that has become jīva, by which this world is sustained.\n\nBetween the two prakṛtis, this (jaḍa) one is the lower. There is another prakṛti under my governance that surpasses it: cit-prakṛti, Śrī-tattva — the eternal support of all jīvas. O mighty hero, it is this that bears up this world.\n\nFor an unconscious (acetana) creation, there is a nimitta-kāraṇa (efficient cause) and an upādāna-kāraṇa (material cause) alone. Jaḍa-prakṛti is the material cause of the material world, and through it Bhagavān is the efficient cause of the entire creation. Among conscious beings (cetana) there are two kinds: cara (mobile) and acara (immobile). For the creation of immobile cetana (e.g., trees and plants), one efficient cause and one material cause suffice, as stated above. But for the creation of mobile cetana (born from egg or womb), a single efficient cause is not enough — two efficient causes are needed, one father and one mother. One consciousness produces another consciousness — and this pattern begins already at the root creation itself. Thus this entire creation comes about through the union of two consciousnesses and one jaḍa. As said before, at the time of dissolution the entire world lay within Bhagavān's own being; so he alone is both father and mother. Yet, since the ongoing creation of jīvas in this world requires two consciousnesses called father and mother, Bhagavān established this arrangement already at the very beginning of creation. This creation is simply the continuation of that.\n\nIn this verse Kṛṣṇa says: \"The prakṛti I spoke of earlier is jaḍa-prakṛti; but there is another prakṛti, distinct from it and superior to it.\" That is cit-prakṛti, who has given the position of mother to this entire world. As the Veda says, jaḍa-prakṛti is 'akṣara'; the distinct cit-prakṛti is 'paramākṣara'; and Bhagavān is 'parataḥ-paramākṣara.' Thus, as mediator between Bhagavān and the world, there is Śrī-tattva (Śrī Lakṣmī) — for this reason Lakṣmī is called Lokamātā, the Mother of the Worlds. When creating this world, Bhagavān used jaḍa-prakṛti as the material cause for the body (piṇḍāṇḍa), while for the creation of jīva within that jaḍa body, he used cit-prakṛti — possessed of conscious power and having become jīva-bhūtā — as the cause. Thus the world's first jīva, Caturmukha (Brahmā), came into being through Śrī-tattva. Thus, at the very root of the world, we find two kinds of consciousness — prakṛti and puruṣa. Even in jīva-form itself there is the twofold division of male and female — this we know from the very beginning of creation. This creation came about through Bhagavān's two aspects, puruṣa and prakṛti. Jaḍa itself has no gender distinction, yet we identify certain jaḍa objects as feminine and others as masculine — this follows the gender of the object's presiding deity. Take the river, for example: most presiding deities of rivers are feminine; likewise the presiding deity of the earth is female. For this reason we regard the river, and the earth, in the form of a mother.\n\nThus the root fact of the world is Bhagavān (puruṣa), and, subordinate to him, jaḍa-prakṛti and cit-prakṛti. These are eternal — never destroyed. Only the form (vikṛti) is destroyed; the root substance is never created or destroyed in this world. Hence these are without kṣara — a-kṣara. From these three, the 'kṣara' world comes into being.",
  "7.6": "etat yonīni bhūtāni sarvāṇi iti upadhāraya |\naham kṛtsnasya jagataḥ prabhavaḥ pralayaḥ tathā -- Understand that all beings are formed from these two (prakṛtis); I am the source of the origin, and likewise the dissolution, of the whole world.\n\nJaḍa-prakṛti and cit-prakṛti are the root cause of every living thing in the entire world. Bhagavān stood behind all jīvas as father, and Lakṣmīdevī as mother, and thus created this world. Just as an embryo grows within the mother's womb, jaḍa-prakṛti underwent modification: cit-prakṛti gave form to jaḍa-prakṛti, held it, sustained it — and that became the world.\n\nThrough the impelling of Bhagavān's power, through jaḍa- and cit-prakṛti, Bhagavān is the cause of the birth of this world; in the same way he is the cause of the world's dissolution too. Kṛṣṇa says: \"I am the root cause of the origination of the entire world; I am the cause of its destruction.\" Bhagavān is the one who existed before this world's birth, who is the cause of its birth, the cause of its destruction, and who remains after the destruction.\n\nSome among us ask: jaḍa-prakṛti's regulating power is cit-prakṛti; and the one who impels cit-prakṛti and engages it in creation and destruction is Bhagavān, called Puruṣa. Then who controls this Bhagavān? Kṛṣṇa answers this in the next verse.",
  "7.7": "mattaḥ parataram na anyat kiñcit asti dhanañjaya |\nmayi sarvam idam protam sūtre maṇigaṇāḥ iva -- O Dhanañjaya, there is nothing else higher than me. All this is strung upon me, as rows of gems upon a thread.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"There is nothing else beyond me, greater than me, or distinct from me.\" Cit-prakṛti, jaḍa-prakṛti, the jīvas who take bodies formed from them, the brahmāṇḍa (macrocosm) they inhabit, this piṇḍāṇḍa (microcosm) — all of it is strung upon Bhagavān as gems are strung upon a thread. Bhagavān is the very thread (sūtra) that strings together the entire universe and all living beings.",
  "7.8": "rasaḥ aham apsu kaunteya prabhā asmi śaśi-sūryayoḥ |\npraṇavaḥ sarva-vedeṣu śabdaḥ khe pauruṣam nṛṣu -- O Kaunteya, I am the sap (rasa) in water; I am the radiance (prabhā) in the moon and the sun; I am the syllable Om (praṇava) in all the Vedas; the sound in space; the manly vigor (pauruṣa) in men.\n\nThe scriptures tell us that Bhagavān created this world and then entered into it. This raises a puzzle for us: \"Bhagavān is all-pervading — what does it mean for him to 'enter'?\" To understand this we must know that the all-pervading Bhagavān has infinite forms. He is present everywhere in one pervasive form, and he also enters into each individual thing as a special vibhūti-form, granting it a particular power. Thus many forms of Bhagavān may be present within a single object. The form of Bhagavān that grants a thing its special power is called his vibhūti-form — this is his līlā. Bhagavān enters an object in his vibhūti-form and manifests within it a particular power — this is called vibhūti-vijñāna.\n\nKṛṣṇa begins by explaining his vibhūti in water, the most vital of the five elements. In our worship, worshipping Bhagavān in water is especially significant: every rite (bathing, argha, tarpaṇa, etc.) requires water. When we say arghyaṃ samarpayāmi, gandhākṣataṃ samarpayāmi, puṣpaṃ samarpayāmi, naivedyaṃ samarpayāmi, tāmbūlaṃ samarpayāmi — all of these offerings, in essence, are a single offering: water. When we have nothing else, water stands as representative for everything, offered with devotion, because water is the root substance of the expanse of Bhagavān's creation.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa says \"rasaḥ aham apsu kaunteya\" — \"I am the rasa (sap/essence) within water.\" Rasa in Sanskrit has many meanings — the essential substance, flowing quality, and so on. The power of flow is water's distinctive quality; and this flowing power came to water because \"Hari\" — the flowing power itself — sits within it. This is important knowledge for worship. In technical, esoteric language water is called Viṣṇuḥ — \"viśanti atra iti viṣṇuḥ\": that within which one may enter and, from within, come back out.\n\nBhagavān sits in water as rasa (taste). If he had not sat as rasa in water and given us the experience of taste, our tongues would have been useless. Whatever is the essential, extraordinary power within each thing — that is Bhagavān's vibhūti. He is Rasaḥ: 'ra' means bliss (ānanda); 'sa' means knowledge (jñāna) — hence Bhagavān named Rasaḥ is full of knowledge and bliss.\n\nAfter water, Kṛṣṇa takes the example of the sun and moon: \"prabhā asmi śaśi-sūryayoḥ\" — \"Within sun and moon, I am he who shines as prabhā.\" Life-force flows to us from the sun and moon. Though human life depends especially on solar energy, all plant life has a special relationship with the moon; for this reason herbalists study which trees derive which powers from moonlight, and traditionally cut timber according to the phase of the moon — wood cut without regard to lunar phase does not last. Because Bhagavān constantly channels energy through sun and moon, this world of living beings survives; without the falling of solar and lunar power, we would have no life at all. For this Bhagavān is called Keśava — he who dwells within the rays of sun and moon and, through that radiance, through sunlight and moonlight, gives us life. Dwelling in the light of moon and sun, giving that shining power, and being himself the light within that light, Bhagavān sits denoted by the word Prabhā: 'pra' meaning excellent, 'bhā' meaning full of knowledge and bliss. Bhagavān, being that knowledge-and-bliss-filled light, dwells in sun and moon, continually pouring life-force into all beings.\n\nKṛṣṇa continues: \"In all the Vedas, I am present, denoted by the word Veda itself.\" Veda means \"that which gives knowledge.\" Bhagavān, self-complete, gives knowledge to all, is denoted by every word, and dwells within all the Vedas. The life of one who understands the meaning of the Veda is ever auspicious. The essence of such Veda is 'Oṃkāra' — that is the Praṇavaḥ.\n\nHere we should understand one thing: Om is said to be the essence of all the Vedas — how so? The Vedas are many: the Ṛgveda has 24 branches, the Yajurveda 101, the Sāmaveda 1,000, the Atharvaveda 12 — 1,137 saṃhitās in all, with their Brāhmaṇas, Āraṇyakas, and Upaniṣads — an immense body of Vedic literature. Yet fundamentally the Veda has three main divisions: verse-form (Ṛgveda), prose-form (Yajurveda), and song-form (Sāmaveda). Taking one syllable from each of these three Vedas produced Oṃkāra. \"Giving essence to Oṃkāra, and dwelling in Oṃkāra as the essential syllable denoted by 'Praṇavaḥ,' I abide,\" says Kṛṣṇa. This is a profound matter. The ancients distilled the three Vedas and produced from their essence a single sūkta of three sections — the Puruṣa-sūkta, the most exalted of all Vedic sūktas. Distilling this sūkta again produced the three-line Gāyatrī mantra (\"tat savitur vareṇyaṃ bhargo devasya dhīmahi dhiyo yo naḥ pracodayāt\"). \"Tat savitur vareṇyaṃ\" belongs to the Ṛgveda, \"bhargo devasya dhīmahi\" to the Yajurveda, \"dhiyo yo naḥ pracodayāt\" to the Sāmaveda — thus the essence of the three Vedas resides in the three lines of a single mantra, for which reason Gāyatrī is called Vedamātā. Distilling Gāyatrī further yields the three-worded vyāhṛti \"bhūḥ bhuvaḥ svaḥ\"; and the essence of these three words is the three-syllabled Om (a, u, ma).\n\nWithin Om, the syllable 'a' belongs to the Ṛgveda, 'u' to the Yajurveda, and 'ma' to the Sāmaveda. The Ṛgveda begins with 'a' (\"agnim īḷe purohitaṃ yajñasya devam ṛtvijam...\") and ends in 'i.' From there the Yajurveda continues, beginning with 'i' (\"iṣe tvorje tvā...\") and ending in 'u' (\"samudro bandhuḥ\"). Thus 'a' and 'u' within Om fully denote the Ṛgveda and Yajurveda in abbreviated form. Next comes the Sāmaveda, beginning with 'a' (\"agna ā yāhi...\") and ending in 'u' (\"bṛhaspatir dadhātu\") — so here 'ma' does not appear as a letter, yet, as we know, the Sāmaveda exists in the form of song (nāda); and within Om too, 'ma' is the syllable that emerges in the form of nāda — that is, music. Thus Om is the seed-syllable that abbreviates the Veda, our guide to recognizing the Veda; hence it is the most excellent of all the mantras used to praise Bhagavān. There is no greater word of praise in this world.\n\nThe Om placed at the end of a mantra is, in grammatical terms, called 'praṇavaḥ.' To show that every name, from beginning to end, refers ultimately to Bhagavān, Om is pronounced at the beginning and end of a mantra; for this reason the Om used at the beginning and end of a mantra should never be recited separately, but joined with what precedes or follows. For example, at the end of the Viṣṇu-sahasranāma, one should not say \"sarva-praharaṇāyudhaḥ Oṃ namaḥ iti\" but rather \"sarva-praharaṇāyudho'ṃ namaḥ iti\" — the visarga in āyudhaḥ is dropped and Om joined to it, the two pronounced as one.\n\n\"I am Praṇavaḥ, the essence of the Veda,\" says Kṛṣṇa. When Praṇavaḥ expands, it becomes vyāhṛti; vyāhṛti expanded becomes Gāyatrī; Gāyatrī expanded becomes the Puruṣa-sūkta; then the three Vedas; and from there the whole of the Veda. Thus Bhagavān dwells within Praṇava, within vyāhṛti, within Puruṣa-sūkta, within the entire Veda.\n\nBhagavān, who filled every sound born from the words of the Veda, filled every worldly sound and every worldly note — he created infinite sounds and infinite notes in space. Having given us infinite experience through sound, and having graced us with the special power to grasp sound and to reproduce what we have grasped, he gave humankind, through knowledge, the special power to know this Bhagavān denoted by sound. Bhagavān, who granted every kind of manly power (pauruṣa) to living beings, filled the manifold vigor of men, denoted by the word 'puruṣa.'\n\nBefore proceeding to the next verse, let us look once more, in a little more detail, at Oṃkāra, which we have briefly touched on above. As said before, the entire Veda declares which qualities of Bhagavān we must worship; and whatever qualities all the pronouns denoting Bhagavān express — all of this, gathered and distilled into its essence, is the seed-syllable Om. Om is formed from two roots, 'ava' or 'avi.' \"Avati iti Om\" — here 'avati' has many special meanings. From the root 'ava vyāpau' (to pervade), avati means \"that which fills everywhere\" — hence Om means all-pervading (sarvagata). 'Ava' also means knowledge; avati thus means \"one who knows everything\" — hence Om means all-knowing (sarvajña). There is another root, 'ava sāmarthye,' from which avati means all-capable (omnipotent). Thus Om declares three principal facets of Bhagavān: all-capable, all-pervading, all-knowing — the three fundamental qualities by which Bhagavān is recognized. If the jīva is an atomic point, Bhagavān is all-pervading; if the jīva is of limited knowledge, Bhagavān is all-knowing; if the jīva is of limited capacity, Bhagavān is all-capable. Thus Om reveals how utterly distinct that power, distinct from our own, truly is.\n\nThere is another root, 'ava praveśe' — here avati means \"to enter.\" Bhagavān is all-pervading, and therefore he is within and without (omnipresent) all — as the Nārāyaṇa-sūkta says, \"antar bahiś ca tat sarvaṃ vyāpya nārāyaṇaḥ sthitaḥ.\" There is yet another root, 'ava vade,' where avati means to protect, in one sense, and to destroy, in another; hence Om means the all-protector, the all-destroyer power. \"Bhagavān is he who destroys the ignorance, the sorrow, the fault within us — the destroyer of all fault.\" This is what we should keep in mind when worshipping Bhagavān as the \"power of destruction\" contained in Om. Om, born collectively from the root 'ava,' declares all the principal qualities of Bhagavān that we must worship — the all-destroyer, the all-fault-remover, the indweller of all, the all-inner-controller, the all-knowing, the all-capable. Its detailed unfolding is found throughout the entire Veda.\n\nThe second root is \"ūyate iti Om\" — one in whom the world is woven (ota) is Om; that is, he in whom the entire world is dependent, in whom all qualities are woven together, is Om — Bhagavān, all-supporting, full of every quality.\n\nHaving seen Om taken as a whole, let us now see what its three constituent letters (a, u, ma) individually express. Each syllable of the word Praṇava explains one syllable of Om. The first syllable of Om is 'a' — 'a' means supreme, Bhagavān who is supreme above all is 'a'; this is explained by the syllable 'pra' of Praṇava — 'pra' meaning excellent, the infinitely excellent power that is Bhagavān is 'a.' The second syllable of Om is 'u' — 'u' means excellent, full of knowledge and bliss, elevated; this is explained by the syllable 'ṇa' of Praṇava, meaning the strength of knowledge-bliss of the self; Bhagavān, all-capable, is thus knowledge-and-bliss itself, 'u.' The third syllable of Om is 'ma' — meaning of the nature of knowledge; this is explained by the syllable 'va' of Praṇava — 'va' meaning knowledge, giving the sense of all-knowing. Thus one facet of Om is explained by 'Praṇavaḥ.'\n\nAs the Upaniṣad says, \"neti neti ātmā agrahyaḥ na hi gṛhyate\" — the first thing to understand about Bhagavān is that he is not something that can be fully grasped! Understanding that it is impossible to know him completely is itself the first thing we must learn about Bhagavān. Om explains this. Here 'a' means \"not\" or \"is not\" — Bhagavān is not, Bhagavān is not [in the sense we imagine]! That is, Bhagavān is not something we can fully comprehend, because whatever is in the things we know does not exist in him. We are acquainted only with the threefold-guṇa world, but Bhagavān is beyond the three guṇas; our threefold-guṇa mind cannot grasp what is beyond the three guṇas. There is no fault whatsoever in Bhagavān. He is full of every good quality. Bhagavān is beyond our standpoint.\n\nBhagavān is 'u' — a thing loftier than any height our mind can rise to; our mind cannot rise to that height. And yet he is \"ma\" — meaning he can be known! That is, Bhagavān is like the ocean: we cannot bring the entire ocean and fill it into a pot in our home, but we can fill our pot with as much water as it can hold and bring it home. In the same way, we cannot grasp Bhagavān fully, but we can know him according to our own capacity. Om conveys to us this Vedic message: \"It is impossible to know Bhagavān fully, but strive to know him according to your own worthiness.\" Om teaches how we must meditate upon Bhagavān. Bhagavān is 'a' — that is, he is beyond (atīta): what existed before creation is 'a.' Bhagavān as he exists after our coming into being is 'u'; Bhagavān as he remains after all is destroyed is 'ma.' Bhagavān, who remains one and the same across the three times, is Om.\n\nBhagavān is 'a' — that is, Bhūḥ (the earth); he is 'u' — the space above the earth (Bhuvaḥ); he is 'ma' — the knowledge-granting heaven (Svaḥ). The power that regulates the three worlds is Bhagavān, Om. The infinite power that regulates our three states — 'a' (waking), 'u' (dream), 'ma' (deep sleep) — is Bhagavān, Om.\n\nThus, whatever quality the entire body of Vedic literature says we must worship in Bhagavān, whatever quality all the pronouns denoting the all-pronoun Bhagavān express — all of it, gathered, distilled, and expressed in essence, is the seed-syllable Om. This is the most excellent of all praises of Bhagavān. It is impossible to call anyone but Bhagavān \"Om.\" (For this reason, generally, no one uses \"Om\" as their own name, nor should they.)\n\nChanting Om with full contemplation of all the meaning behind it is exceedingly effective. Generally, renunciants (sannyāsīs) practice the japa of Om. It is not so fitting for a householder to continuously chant Om — at most ten times is proper. It is good for women who lead a householder's life not to chant Om, because chanting Om with full engagement and contemplation of its meaning has a powerful effect on the mind: it can absorb the mind and create the risk of dispassion (vairāgya) toward household life. But for the renunciant there is no such problem — he may chant Om as many times as his body can bear (up to 12,000 times).",
  "7.9": "puṇyaḥ gandhaḥ pṛthivyām ca tejaḥ ca asmi vibhāvasau |\njīvanam sarva-bhūteṣu tapaḥ ca asmi tapasviṣu -- I am the sweet fragrance in earth; I am the radiance in fire; I am the life in all beings; I am the austerity in the ascetics.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"Puṇyo gandhaḥ pṛthivyāṃ — the fragrance in soil is from me.\" Whatever fragrance delights our nostrils is 'puṇya-gandha' — here puṇya means pure or beautiful. Bhagavān, denoted by 'Puṇya,' gave fragrance to every object born upon this earth; in his special form denoted by 'Gandha' he has entered into that object, granting it a distinctive quality. Every plant, creeper, herb, flower, and fruit born upon this earth owes its variety of scent to Bhagavān. Dwelling within the presiding deity of earth (Pṛthvī-devatā), and residing within fragrance, denoted by the word Gandha, Bhagavān has taken his seat in the soil. The earth is the ground of everything, and because Bhagavān has grounded himself in the earth, everything that springs up from the earth carries its own distinctive fragrance. Thus Bhagavān has spread out before our very noses a world of beauty-laden, distinctive fragrance in his creation.\n\nIn the Himalayan Valley of Flowers there exists an infinite variety of flowers, each with a scent utterly distinct from the next — though all these plants grow from the very same soil, Bhagavān has filled each plant with its own exclusive quality. Some flowers have no discernible fragrance at all, and such flowers are generally not offered to God, because every act of worship should be filled with joy; when the pleasing fragrance of a flower strikes our nose during worship, we should awaken to the awareness of Bhagavān denoted by 'Puṇya' or 'Gandha.'\n\nHaving spoken of water, sun, moon, space, and earth, Bhagavān now goes on to explain his vibhūti-vijñāna in fire (Vibhāvasu), the chief seat of Bhagavān's worship upon earth. Vibhāvasu is the presiding deity of fire on earth — the fifth among the eight Vasus (Droṇa, Prāṇa, Dhruva, Arka, Agni/Vibhāvasu, Doṣa, Vastu, Dyuvasu), also known by many other names: Vaiśvānara, Vahni, Jātaveda, Hutāśana, Pāvaka, Anala, Dahana. Fire is a supremely important symbol of Bhagavān on this earth; the Veda itself begins with the name of Agni (\"agnim īḷe purohitaṃ yajñasya devam ṛtvijam\"). There is no better symbol for worshipping Bhagavān on earth than fire — fire is a form of light, fire is pure, and fire is the sole symbol of Bhagavān that accepts what is offered to it. Here Kṛṣṇa says \"tejaś ca asmi vibhāvasau\" — \"within such a fire-deity I am present as the burning, cooking, illuminating power, as tejas.\" If Bhagavān is present in the fire that blazes in the outer world, then the same fire-named Bhagavān (Agni-Nārāyaṇa) seated in our tongue gives us the light called speech — a great light within us in the form of language; and as Vaiśvānara he is the very one who regulates our digestion (this is explained further in the tenth chapter ahead).\n\nContinuing, Kṛṣṇa says: \"Filling the wind, becoming the breath of every living being, I stand giving the power of breath to life.\" The presiding deity of life-force is Prāṇa-devaru. We can live even if other sense-organs stop functioning, but we cannot live if the life-breath itself stops working. In sleep our other faculty-deities may rest, but if the Prāṇa-deity were to sleep, we could never wake again! Bhagavān, day and night, stands as 'Prāṇa' within life-giving Prāṇa and sustains us.\n\nHaving described the five elements, Kṛṣṇa now turns to the mental world. Those who live in the realm of the mind are the tapasvins (tapaḥ — deep contemplation). Deep reflection of the mind is called tapas; its presiding deity is Śiva. What we speak arises from what flashes in our mind; Bhagavān, who gave the mind such wondrous power of thought, standing within the mind-sheath, gives us the experience of bliss. Thus, across the five elements — from the food-sheath up to the bliss-sheath — Kṛṣṇa has explained, in these two verses (8 and 9), the wondrous divine science of how Bhagavān stands present in vibhūti-form.\n\nWe have thus seen, in brief, this contemplative worship (upāsanā) of how Kṛṣṇa, standing in piṇḍāṇḍa and brahmāṇḍa, fills distinctive power into different objects as his vibhūti. This theme of vibhūti will be expanded further in the next chapter; here Kṛṣṇa has summarized it, explaining how the contemplation of vibhūti, taken as a whole, ought to be conducted.",
  "7.10": "bījam mām sarva-bhūtānām viddhi pārtha sanātanam |\nbuddhiḥ buddhimatām asmi tejaḥ tejasvinām aham -- O Pārtha, know me to be the eternal seed of all beings; I am the intelligence of the intelligent, and I am the vigor of the vigorous.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"bījaṃ mām sarva-bhūtānām\" — \"I am the seed of all beings.\" When we hear the word \"seed,\" we usually think of the seed of a tree; when this seed is sown, it grows into a tree — the seed is the material (upādāna) cause of the tree. Is Bhagavān likewise the material cause of this world? This is the first question that arises before us. To understand this, we must first grasp the spiritual, etymological meaning of the word 'bīja.' Bīja comes from the root 'añj' (to manifest); here 'vi' is a prefix. Whatever is vyañjana (expressive) becomes abhivyañjana (manifestation) — that is, that by which something is made manifest is bīja. He who gave a visible form to that which existed (the root substance) but was not seen — that is Bhagavān. That is, he who gave manifest form to this unmanifest world that lay in subtle form — that is why he who granted manifestation to the entire host of beings in this world is bīja of all beings. That Bhagavān, who is both father and mother to this world, is bīja. At the time of dissolution, he who gave manifest form to this world — which lay unmanifest (avyakta), in subtle form — is \"sarva-bhūtānāṃ bīja.\"\n\nTo the entire host of living beings in the world — that is, Caturmukha, Prāṇa, Garuḍa, Śeṣa, Rudra, Indra, Kāma, all the deities, human beings, plants, and so on — to this whole host of jīvas Bhagavān gave body, gave form, gave manifestation, and stood behind the entire host of living beings as father. Whatever kind of living being exists in this world, behind its exclusive quality Bhagavān stands as the causal person, as the root-cause of the unfolding of its individuality (its manifestation). We have another conception of \"seed\" — that when it grows into a tree it loses its own form. But Bhagavān is 'sanātana' (eternal). Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"I remain myself, and I give form to the world.\" That is, Bhagavān never changes — but the world changes. Bhagavān gives the world different forms of manifestation, gives it new power; because of this the world keeps changing, keeps taking new form. Only Bhagavān, who is the causal person behind this, remains one and the same at all times. Thus Bhagavān is the seed of all beings (jaḍa, cetana, and liberated), and he is eternal.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"buddhir buddhimatām asmi tejas tejasvinām aham\" — \"whatever intelligence the intelligent possess is my gift; and to the vigorous I am their vigor.\" Bhagavān manifested the power of knowledge in knowledge-seeking jīvas, making them jñāna-yogīs who cultivated the lineage of knowledge in the world and attained liberation. Likewise, to karma-yogīs, whose life is dominated by action, Bhagavān stood as tejas and led them forward, granting them liberation \"as tejas.\" Buddhiḥ and Tejaḥ are both names of Bhagavān. Bhagavān, the vast and endless ocean of knowledge, is 'Buddhiḥ.' Bhagavān, who is all-capable, whose bodily strength and spiritual strength are both full and complete, is 'Tejaḥ.'",
  "7.11": "balam balavatām ca aham kāma-rāga-vivarjitam |\ndharma-aviruddhaḥ bhūteṣu kāmaḥ asmi bharata-ṛṣabha -- I am the strength of the strong, free of desire and attachment; and, O best of the Bharatas, I am that desire in beings which is not opposed to dharma.\n\nHaving summarized every facet of his vibhūti, Kṛṣṇa here explains the two fundamental faces of life. Kṛṣṇa mentions here the strength that lies in cetana (consciousness). Strength is of many kinds. As the Muṇḍakopaniṣad says: \"nāyam ātmā pravacanena labhyo na medhayā na bahunā śrutena | yam eva eṣa vṛṇute tena labhyaḥ tasya eṣa ātmā vivṛṇute tanūṃ svām || nāyam ātmā balahīnena labhyo na ca pramādāt tapaso vā api aliṅgāt | etaiḥ upāyaiḥ yatate yaḥ tu vidvān tasya eṣa ātmā viśate brahma-dhāma ||\" (3.2.3–4). \"Na ayaṃ ātmā balahīnena labhyaḥ\" means Bhagavān cannot be attained by the weak. The strength meant here is not bodily strength; it is the strength of Bhagavān's grace, together with the strength of one's own inherent worthiness and mental resolve. As this verse says, mental strength may grow through study and scriptural hearing, but without the strength of the self (ātma-bala), Bhagavān cannot be seen. As the Mahābhārata-tātparya-nirṇaya says: \"yasya deve parā bhaktir yathā deve tathā gurau | tasyaite kathitā hy arthāḥ prakāśante mahātmanaḥ ||\" (1.102) — only for such a great soul, in whom there is supreme devotion to Bhagavān and, correspondingly, discriminating devotion to his teachers as well, do things become truly clear. Strength of knowledge alone is not enough; the strength of devotion is needed. Also crucial is the strength of a guru's grace, which awakens devotion to Bhagavān within us. Thus the strength of one's own worthiness, the strength of practice, the strength of devotion, the strength of scriptural study, the strength of the guru's grace — all these are given to us by that Bhagavān. Standing in many forms named 'Bala,' he grants special strength according to each jīva's worthiness.\n\nOrdinarily there is a defect lurking behind strength: when strength arrives, it tends to give rise to bad desire in us. But Bhagavān is strength free of desire and attachment. Desire-free strength is, for us, almost unimaginable. Kṛṣṇa is not saying here that desire itself should be abandoned — desires such as \"I must know Bhagavān,\" \"I must study scripture with devotion\" are necessary and good for us. Wherever a desire serves as a sustaining power, there Bhagavān stands as its \"desire-free\" root. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"I stand behind that desire, in every living being, which is not opposed to dharma.\"\n\nIn this verse Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as 'Bharatarṣabha' — one who rides as a chariot-warrior in the realm of knowledge and bliss; Bharata (breath/wind) as elder brother (ṛṣabha).\n\nIn the verse above, Kṛṣṇa explained only the good-facing aspect of his vibhūti. Contemplating the Gītā in this light, some may raise a doubt: \"is it God who makes us do good, and some other, evil power that makes us do bad?\" — that is, the suspicion that Bhagavān is not the doer behind everything. Kṛṣṇa's answer to this doubt of ours is the next verse.",
  "7.12": "ye ca eva sāttvikāḥ bhāvāḥ rājasāḥ tāmasāḥ ca ye |\nmattaḥ eva iti tān viddhi na tu aham teṣu te mayi -- Whatever states of being are sāttvika, rājasa, or tāmasa — know that these too proceed from me alone; yet I am not dependent on them — they are dependent on me.\n\nThe world consists of the inner realm and the outer realm. In the inner realm are the feelings of the three guṇas; in the outer, the substances of the three guṇas. The confluence of sattva, rajas, and tamas is this world itself. Earth is the sign of tamoguṇa, water of rajoguṇa, and fire of sattvaguṇa; hence every single object we see in this world is a confluence of these three. Our own inner feelings, too, are a confluence of these three qualities. Even our varṇa system is founded on this threefold guṇa: one in whom sattva predominates, with rajas and tamas lesser, is the Brāhmaṇa varṇa; one in whom rajas predominates, followed by sattva and tamas, is the Kṣatriya; one in whom rajas predominates, followed by tamas and sattva, is the Vaiśya; and one in whom tamas predominates, followed by rajas and sattva, is the Śūdra. All these varṇas alike are fit for mokṣa. At the very dawn of creation, creation began already as a mixture of these three qualities — there is no such thing as \"pure sattva\" here. (Madhvācārya, in his Bhāgavata-tātparya-nirṇaya, has clearly explained, in mathematical precision, exactly how this world was constructed from the threefold guṇas — this is one of the great themes of cosmology.)\n\nThus every substance carries three components, and so does every feeling of ours. This intricate world of the three guṇas is strange indeed — so confusing that a human being cannot decide what is good and what is bad among them. But here Kṛṣṇa says: \"I, being beyond the three guṇas, am the one giving these three qualities\" — this is astonishing! Bhagavān gives to each jīva, in accordance with its own nature, exactly the disposition it needs. No action of anyone occurs anywhere without Bhagavān's impulsion. As explained earlier, the jīva has no independent action of its own will: \"na hi pratibimbasya kriyā | sa hi bimba-kriyaiva kriyāvān |\" — there is no action in a reflection apart from the action of that which is reflected. Under Bhagavān's governance, these three qualities pervade this world — this is the schoolhouse Bhagavān has built for our jīva to ripen and reach mokṣa. Bhagavān is beyond the three guṇas; he is not subject to their control. For this he is praised thus: \"trigunātīta vidāraka-parito tehi subhaktiṃ karuṇāpūrṇa para-prada-caritaṃ jñāpayamete\" — the whole world lies under the control of the three guṇas, but Bhagavān is beyond the three guṇas.",
  "7.13": "tribhiḥ guṇa-mayaiḥ bhāvaiḥ ebhiḥ sarvam idam jagat |\nmohitam na abhijānāti mām ebhyaḥ param avyayam -- Deluded by these very states composed of the three guṇas, this whole world fails to recognize me, who am beyond them, imperishable.\n\nBecause the entire world is deluded by the three guṇas, and because these three qualities pervade everywhere, thought that transcends the three guṇas is impossible for us. For this reason, the jīva cannot fully grasp that Bhagavān who is beyond the three guṇas and imperishable. To know Bhagavān, we must step outside the wheel of these three guṇas — but by what path can we step out? The next verse gives the answer.",
  "7.14": "daivī hi eṣā guṇamayī mama māyā duratyayā |\nmām eva ye prapadyante māyām etām taranti te -- This divine māyā of mine, composed of the three guṇas, is exceedingly hard to overcome; only those who take refuge in me alone cross beyond this māyā.\n\nThere is another facet to these three guṇa-composed states. The presiding lady of the three guṇas — Śrī Lakṣmī herself — is also threefold in aspect: Śrī, Bhū, and Durgā are the three forms that govern the three guṇas. This mother, guṇamayī, has filled the world as māyā. That māyā — mighty enough to govern the whole world — is under Bhagavān's control. To see Bhagavān we would need to draw aside this veil of māyā, but this is impossible for us on our own. For this reason the sole path to reach Bhagavān is complete surrender to Bhagavān; he alone can draw aside this veil of māyā and grant vision. As explained earlier, when one steps away from the deluding bonds of worldly life and surrenders to Nārāyaṇa, he carries us across this veil of māyā.\n\nWith the awareness that we have no existence apart from Bhagavān, we must offer ourselves entirely to him. Among the nine forms of devotion, this is the most excellent — it is called ātma-nivedana (self-offering). First knowing the Bhagavān within us, casting away ego and possessiveness, and offering ourselves completely to Bhagavān — this is when direct realization of Bhagavān (bhagavat-sākṣātkāra) occurs. What is chiefly required here is ekabhakti (devotion to one alone) and surrender to that One. Kṛṣṇa says: \"whoever surrenders to me alone certainly crosses over.\" Hearing this statement of Kṛṣṇa's, some questions may trouble us: \"why doesn't everyone surrender to Bhagavān? How does evil come to arise in people?\" and so on. Let us seek the answer to our question in the next verse.",
  "7.15": "na mām duṣkṛtinaḥ mūḍhāḥ prapadyante nara-adhamāḥ |\nmāyayā apahṛta-jñānāḥ āsuram bhāvam āśritāḥ -- The evil-doers, the deluded, the lowest of men — those whose knowledge has been stolen away by māyā, who have taken refuge in a demonic nature — do not surrender to me.\n\nIn this verse Kṛṣṇa speaks of the wicked (duṣkṛtins). There are two kinds among them: those who, caught by some prārabdha-karma or under the influence of their surroundings, proceed along a sinful path (e.g., Karṇa, Ajāmiḷa), and those who are wicked by their very nature (e.g., Duryodhana, Śakuni). Kṛṣṇa says: \"I release from māyā those who surrender to me; even so, not everyone surrenders to me.\" Those who are wicked by their own nature never accept Bhagavān — no matter how much good counsel is given, it makes no difference to them. Caught in the net of māyā, they live believing that all pleasure lies in the senses. For virtuous people who fall into māyā's net under external influence, there exists in creation a system by which they return to the right path; but if a jīva's very nature is base, such a one can never be reformed — such a one never attempts to know Bhagavān, and, taking wickedness itself as duty, becomes what Kṛṣṇa here calls \"narādhamaḥ,\" the lowest of men, ever enveloped by māyā, and never even aware of their own lack of understanding! Such people never surrender to Bhagavān and never reach him.\n\nFor those who fall into wickedness through external influence, māyā has enveloped their intelligence; but when time ripens, they slip free of the worldly influence of their surroundings and return to surrender at Bhagavān's feet. A fine example of this is the story of Ajāmiḷa. Ajāmiḷa was a Brāhmaṇa — simple, of few words, sāttvika, learned; he served his guru, guests, and elders, and loved the wife he had married. One day, in the forest, he saw a beautiful woman in a state of partial undress and became infatuated. Forgetting his wife and his parents, he lost himself entirely in the company of this forest-girl, doing every wrong thing to please her — he became a drunkard, a thief, a gambler. A household grew up with her in the forest, and children were born; the last son was named 'Nārāyaṇa.' One night, Ajāmiḷa beheld before his eyes the fearsome messengers of Yama, ropes of death in their hands! Truly terrified, he cried out for his son 'Nārāyaṇa' — and, instead of his son, the messengers of Viṣṇu appeared to him. Awakened, he became aware of the error of his ways. Bhagavān brought him, who had strayed from the path, back to the right way and uplifted him. In the days that followed he lived as a great soul and, through his own striving, attained mokṣa.\n\nOur sattva and knowledge come to us as the fruit of many births. When, for some evil cause, we go astray, the merciful Bhagavān, rather than punishing us, forgives us and uplifts us. Bhagavān is that embodiment of compassion who forgives any fault whatsoever. The suffering Bhagavān gives us is like the correction a mother gives her child; his punishment is the protection of his compassion. He hates no one. Bhagavān, who tolerates the faults of all, is forbearing.\n\nBhagavān lets each one's individuality unfold according to their own nature; he never changes the jīva's fundamental nature. Sinners reap the fruit of sin; the meritorious reap the fruit of merit. This arrangement never changes. For this reason we find every kind of person in this world.\n\nGenerally, devotees of Bhagavān and knowers of truth are a minority in this world; at no time are they ever found in large numbers.\n\nIn the Harikathāmṛtasāra, the Dāsas have beautifully sung of the sāttvikas, tāmasikas, and rājasikas in this manner: those who are sattva-sattva, sattva-rajas, or sattva-tamas — all three of these sāttvika-natured people are called devotees of Bhagavān; the eternally-bound souls, rajas-rajas types, arise on earth, in heaven, and in hell, and the rājasa and tāmasa souls wander about upon this earth; those who are tamas-sattva are called countless unnamed demons; those who are tamas-rajas are called the host of daityas; and one who is tamas-tamas — utterly full of fault, the vilest among the vile in every dharma — such a soul is called Kali.\n\nThe influence of māyā upon us varies according to the age. In the Sāttvika Age (Satya Yuga) sāttvika nature is so strong that even sāttvika souls remain pure without falling under tāmasika influence. But in Kali Yuga everyone falls easily under tāmasika influence. If, even so, the mind turns beyond this toward Bhagavān, that is supremely excellent — for this it is said, \"in Kali Yuga, if one but remembers Hari's name, whole lineages are uplifted.\" In Kali Yuga the punishment for a sin committed under tāmasika influence is lessened; likewise, the fruit of merit is a great reward. Having explained the wicked, Kṛṣṇa now explains, in the next verse, the kind that is the virtuous and good.",
  "7.16": "catur-vidhāḥ bhajante mām janāḥ sukṛtinaḥ arjuna |\nārtaḥ jijñāsuḥ artha-arthī jñānī ca bharata-ṛṣabha -- O Arjuna, four kinds of virtuous people worship me: the distressed, the seeker of knowledge, the seeker of wealth, and the knower — O best of the Bharatas.\n\nHaving first explained the wicked, Kṛṣṇa in this verse explains the virtuous. The path toward Bhagavān is like a straight line — it is not a forked path. Along this straight line, those who turn their back on Bhagavān and proceed are, by their very nature, the wicked. Those who, having turned their back on Bhagavān, later awaken and turn once more to face him, are wicked people who become virtuous. Those who proceed facing Bhagavān from the start are virtuous by nature — they are the ones who worship Bhagavān. Among these virtuous ones there are four kinds: (1) ārtaḥ, (2) jijñāsuḥ, (3) arthārthī, (4) jñānī.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"there are virtuous people who worship me in four ways.\" Here \"worship\" (bhajana) means to adore Bhagavān with devotion. The root is 'bhaj sevāyām.' To love Bhagavān above all else and more than anything, and to conduct oneself in accordance with that love, is bhajana. Among those who worship thus, the first are the ārta (the distressed).\n\nWhen a person falls into some difficulty, when overtaken by sorrow, he prays to God seeking relief — this is ārta-bhakti. Within this bhakti one may find the very lowest as well as the very highest degree. If one worships God as ārta when trouble comes but forgets him entirely when happiness comes — like the proverb \"in distress, Venkataramana!\" — that becomes the lowest grade of ārta-bhakti. A fine example of the highest grade of ārta-bhakti is Draupadī: when the crisis of her humiliation arose in the assembly hall, she called out to Kṛṣṇa in an anguished cry of devotion (and we must not forget that Draupadī was constantly devoted to Kṛṣṇa even before that). Gajendra's cry too, when the crocodile seized his leg, was ārta-bhakti.\n\nThere is a problem in ārta-bhakti. Generally, when happiness comes to us, we forget Bhagavān; we think all our wealth and prosperity are \"the fruit of our own labor\" — then God does not come to mind at all. But should some trouble arise, God is the very first thing we remember. Never thinking \"God alone has given me all this happiness,\" we begin, the moment trouble comes, to ask \"why has God done this to me?\" Generally, people go first to astrologers when trouble strikes. Astrologers suggest some remedy — worship of the nine planets, serpent-worship, Ganapati-worship, worship of the Goddess, and so on. Some astrologers, lacking scriptural knowledge and spiritual understanding, are prone to guide people wrongly, giving nonsensical statements such as \"the Goddess is angry because you worshipped Viṣṇu!\" and thereby leading people astray. When this happens, ordinary people worship all the deities separately and drift away from ekabhakti (devotion to the One). Because of this, there is a real risk that devotion becomes an exercise in fear — \"may no harm come to me from God\" — rather than in love. For all these reasons, continuous devotion and devotion to the One alone are quite likely to be absent among ārta devotees.\n\nWhile the ārta worship Bhagavān to be relieved of hardship, the jijñāsu (seekers of knowledge) worship Bhagavān for the sake of knowledge. Likewise the arthārthīs worship Bhagavān for worldly prosperity, wealth, or to obtain something.\n\nDifferent from these three types of devotee is a fourth kind: the jñānī. He does not worship Bhagavān because he wants or does not want something; rather, having fully understood Bhagavān's glory, he naturally and singularly loves Bhagavān. In him there is ekabhakti and unbroken devotion. He worships Bhagavān with no worldly expectation whatsoever.\n\nBefore moving to the next verse, we should be clear about what ekabhakti means. Ekabhakti does not mean ignoring all the deities and gurus and elders and worshipping Bhagavān alone; if we were to do this, it would be an insult to Bhagavān. For example, when trouble strikes us and an astrologer says a \"navagraha-śānti\" ritual will bring relief, it would be wrong for us to ignore that and say, \"I will worship only Bhagavān; what need is there to worship a deity subordinate to Bhagavān?\" The worship offered to Bhagavān seated together with his retinue of the nine planets is 'navagraha-pūjā' — it is worship of Bhagavān seated as the head of whatever caused the difficulty. Any worship performed while remembering the full glory of Bhagavān's entire retinue becomes the most excellent worship. Worshipping Bhagavān together with all the deities in his retinue is ekabhakti. Bhagavān is not solitary; he is the infinite Prajāpati. Therefore we should worship the other deities faithfully, holding Bhagavān as primary and regarding the deities as his retinue. This is the most excellent devotion, one that even the deities who guide us toward God themselves welcome. If, on the other hand, we forget Bhagavān and worship only a single deity, that deity may itself decline to accept our worship. For worship to be complete, ekabhakti must be shown toward Bhagavān alone.\n\nDevotion to one's guru is of the very same kind. In devotion to the guru, what we ought to see is the Bhagavān dwelling within the guru's inmost being. The jñānī sees the indwelling Bhagavān in everything, and, free of any worldly expectation, cultivates ekabhakti and continuous devotion.",
  "7.17": "teṣām jñānī nitya-yuktaḥ eka-bhaktiḥ viśiṣyate |\npriyaḥ hi jñāninaḥ atyartham aham saḥ ca mama priyaḥ -- Among these, the jñānī, ever united (with me), devoted to me alone, is distinguished as the best; for I am exceedingly dear to the jñānī, and he too is dear to me.\n\nAmong the four kinds of devotees described above, the jñānī is the best of all. Knowingly, with no expectation of reward, he knows Bhagavān and loves him naturally. As we have seen in earlier chapters, we receive exactly what we do; as we regard Bhagavān, so does Bhagavān regard us. For this reason, the jñānī, whose devotion is knowledge-founded, free of expectation, and devoted to Bhagavān alone, remains extremely close to Bhagavān.",
  "7.18": "udārāḥ sarve eva ete jñānī tu ātmā eva me matam |\nāsthitaḥ saḥ hi yukta-ātmā mām eva anuttamām gatim -- All of these are indeed noble; but the jñānī, I hold, is my very own self — for he, disciplined in mind, is established in me alone as the supreme goal.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"All these ways of walking the path of devotion are excellent — but the jñānī alone is exceedingly close to me and dear as my own self.\" For, as we know, devotion without knowledge cannot become complete devotion. One who practices devotion knowingly goes straight to Bhagavān and reaches him — hence it is the most excellent devotion.\n\nThe jñānīs may themselves be ārta, jijñāsu, or arthārthī (Arjuna, for instance, is an example). But not all ārtas, jijñāsus, and arthārthīs need be jñānīs. There is no devotion equal to that of the jñānī; the jñānī's mind is ever fixed on Bhagavān. Though all who walk the path of devotion are great, the jñānī alone is extremely intimate with Bhagavān.\n\nAs stated above, a jñānī may also be an arthārthī. This may seem a little puzzling; an episode from Prahlāda's life clarifies the point. We all know Prahlāda's story. He was a great jñānī, yet this very Prahlāda offers a prayer to Bhagavān in his Narasiṃha form, who had slain Prahlāda's father Hiraṇyākṣa: \"My father, without understanding, not knowing your greatness, insulted you. I know there can never be a remedy for that fault; yet forgive the wrong he committed out of ignorance.\" Here we see no self-interest at all — this is thus an excellent illustration of how a jñānī can also be an arthārthī.",
  "7.19": "bahūnām janmanām ante jñānavān mām prapadyate |\nvāsudevaḥ sarvam iti saḥ mahātmā su-durlabhaḥ -- At the end of many births, one endowed with knowledge surrenders to me, understanding \"Vāsudeva is all\" — such a great soul is exceedingly rare.\n\nTo become a jñānī in this life is no easy task. Kṛṣṇa says: \"bahūnāṃ janmanām ante jñānavān māṃ prapadyate\" — that is, \"through the striving, inquiry, and study of many births, knowledge arises, and such a knower naturally surrenders to me.\" As explained in earlier chapters, gaining knowledge is not the work of a single birth; yet the spiritual striving done in one birth is never lost. We are born already carrying the knowledge of our spiritual striving from previous births — this is Bhagavān's great gift to humankind. Knowledge is the accumulation of hundreds of births; it is not something one can acquire in a single lifetime. However much we may know, it is always insufficient — as Bhagavān himself says, no one but Bhagavān knows the Veda's full meaning.\n\nThus the one who becomes a jñānī through the striving of birth after birth offers everything to Vāsudeva and attains mokṣa. The name Vāsudeva has a special meaning here: Vāsu-deva. Bhagavān is Vāsu — that is, he who conceals himself. When, having gained knowledge, crossing the fifteen fences, descending deep from a state of samādhi, we behold the sixteenth — our own jīva-nature — direct realization of Bhagavān occurs. In this state, the concealed Bhagavān opens up, becoming Deva. To see Bhagavān we must first see ourselves; without our own self-awareness, seeing Bhagavān is impossible. When we behold our own jīva-nature, the Bhagavān who is directly revealed from within it is Devaḥ. While we remain veiled behind our five sheaths we call him Vāsu; when, through gaining knowledge, we come out beyond that veil and see our jīva-nature, Bhagavān who then gives darśan as Deva is Vāsudevaḥ.\n\nThus the jñānī — who through the striving of many births has gained knowledge, come to know Bhagavān, and surrendered to him — is exceedingly great. Kṛṣṇa says such people are very rare in this world.\n\nHere we have seen the four kinds of devotees, and the excellence of the jñānī among them. Kṛṣṇa now goes on to explain why those who fit none of these four kinds remain outside as a fifth category — the aspects of where a person strays from the true spiritual path — in the following portion of this chapter.",
  "7.20": "kāmaiḥ taiḥ taiḥ hṛta-jñānāḥ prapadyante anya-devatāḥ |\ntam tam niyamam āsthāya prakṛtyā niyatāḥ svayā -- Those whose knowledge has been carried away by this or that desire surrender to other deities, following this or that discipline, constrained by their own nature.\n\nKṛṣṇa here places renewed emphasis on what he has explained in earlier chapters. The root cause of a person straying from the spiritual path is their own mad desires — this is the great obstacle to our awareness. When one is unable to control one's desires, instead of pursuing the spiritual path one pursues one's desires, does whatever is necessary — however wrong — to fulfill them, and undergoes decline.\n\nOnce one pursues desires, desire begins to control one's awareness. At such a time, if someone says, \"go to this temple for the fulfillment of your wish,\" one goes there. Wherever word spreads that wish-fulfillment is possible, one begins visiting that place. Because of this, one abandons the root spiritual truth, drifts away from ekabhakti, and, wherever one's desire seems likely to be granted, begins worshipping that deity, begging for the fulfillment of one's material desires. One takes up whatever vow or observance is said to help. Because of this one drifts away from the true path of worshipping Bhagavān. Through the worship of the One, such petty desires are never granted — for \"yad anugraham icchāmi tasya vittaṃ harāmy aham\": Bhagavān's resolve is to take away, and test, the wealth of his devotees, and then uplift them. Bhagavān knows better than we do what we truly need; his grace is the great prasāda. Those who chase after their desires do not have this awareness, and thereby remain deprived of Bhagavān's grace.\n\nWhy is this so? Why does not everyone worship Bhagavān in the same way? To this Kṛṣṇa says: \"tam tam niyamam āsthāya prakṛtyā niyatāḥ svayā\" — \"every jīva has its own particular prakṛti (nature).\" (Here 'prakṛti' means both one's own jīva-nature and also the environment and inherited disposition of one's family lineage — genes and environmental force.) This jīva-nature of a person can never be changed by anyone. Each person's nature is of a different kind — for this reason there is no uniform nature in this world. So many times upon this earth Bhagavān and many great souls have incarnated and taught the truth, yet a single, uniform dharma was never established — because even when Bhagavān himself comes and speaks, only a jīva whose very nature is capable of understanding the truth can grasp it; the remaining jīvas simply lack the capacity to grasp that truth. This is entirely natural. Hence Kṛṣṇa says: \"even to accept the truth, a jīva needs the requisite worthiness.\" Thus, according to each jīva-nature's own worthiness, the worship of whichever deity that jīva finds pleasing proceeds in this world. For this reason, not everyone in this world can become a true devotee of Bhagavān — that is possible only for a sāttvika consciousness.",
  "7.21": "yaḥ yaḥ yām yām tanum bhaktaḥ śraddhayā arcitum icchati |\ntasya tasya acalām śraddhām tām eva vidadhāmi aham -- Whatever form a devotee, with faith, wishes to worship, that very faith of his I make unwavering.\n\nWithout the awareness that Bhagavān, this extraordinary power, governs this world together with his retinue of deities, and having abandoned ekabhakti in favor of the choice to worship, with faith, a single deity alone — to such people Kṛṣṇa says: \"whichever deity's devotion one has, I make that very devotion unshakeable!\"\n\nA human being is fashioned of faith. Whatever one has faith in grows within one. Whatever deity-power we worship with faith, that same conviction grows firmer within us, and that faith becomes unshakeable. It is very hard to change afterward. For this reason we must gain proper understanding about God. If we think of God and his retinue of deities as a group with a culture like that of humans — hating one another, harboring jealousy — that would be the height of our own folly. As said before, Bhagavān is a single power. He is always manifest together with his retinue of deities. When we worship Gaṇapati or any other deity with the awareness of this one power, that worship becomes a great worship. But if, setting that aside, we come to believe that Gaṇapati (or whichever deity) is separate, that he alone is God, and build our faith upon that alone, then we become trapped within the fence of that faith. This becomes no different from worshipping a stone image as God. In truth, what we worship is not the stone image itself but the symbol of Bhagavān within it. The stone image is merely the deity's symbol; the all-capable Bhagavān is indwelling within that image. Therefore, whenever we worship any deity-symbol, it is important to hold this contemplation: \"Bhagavān is one alone; he is indwelling within this deity's symbol, and, through his entire retinue of deities, he protects us.\" With this contemplation, whether we worship Gaṇapati, Vīrabhadra, Koṭi Cennayya, or any other deity, it becomes a great worship pleasing to the Paramātman. Our vows and observances too should be conducted in the same spirit — whether the fast of Ekādaśī or the fast of Saṅkaṣṭahara Caturthī, all of it should be for Bhagavān's pleasure. Bhagavān is denoted by every word — so whatever name we call him by is Bhagavān's name; Bhagavān is all-capable — so he may dwell within stone, within a pillar; Bhagavān is the indweller of all — the indweller of all deities, protecting us. Devotion conducted with this contemplation is called the most excellent devotion. Abandoning this and cultivating blind faith instead may only harden within us and drag us down to ruin!",
  "7.22": "saḥ tayā śraddhayā yuktaḥ tasya ārādhanam īhate |\nlabhate ca tataḥ kāmān mayā eva vihitān hi tān -- Endowed with that faith, he strives to worship that (deity), and thereby obtains the desired objects — which, in truth, are granted by me alone.\n\nWe see in this world people who, without observing the ekabhakti described above, worship some one deity steadfastly and obtain results. A natural question arises here: \"how can results be obtained even through an incorrect mode of worship?\" Kṛṣṇa says: \"Whomever you worship, in the end it is I alone who grant the fruit.\" As the scriptures say, \"sarva-deva-namaskāraḥ keśavaṃ prati gacchati\" — whatever worship is offered to any deity, it accrues, in the end, to the Bhagavān indwelling within that deity. But there is a single difference: when done knowingly, it yields \"eternal, complete fruit\"; when done unknowingly, out of desire for reward, it yields only a \"petty, material fruit.\"\n\nWhatever object of worship we worship, it is Bhagavān alone, indwelling within that object of worship, who grants the fruit. It is only that we lack the awareness that \"Bhagavān, indwelling within the deity, has given this.\" This awareness is exceedingly important in every one of our actions. We must conduct every one of our observances as an act of worship of Bhagavān, making it divine; otherwise we are deprived of the great fruit and receive instead some petty, trivial result. (Here \"mayaiva vihitān hi tān\" can also be read two ways: \"I myself, dwelling within the deity, grant the fruit,\" or \"you obtain from me the fleeting result that you desired, that was 'expedient' for you.\")",
  "7.23": "antavat tu phalam teṣām tat bhavati alpa-cetasām |\ndevān deva-yajaḥ yānti mat-bhaktāḥ yānti mām api -- But the fruit obtained by such small-minded ones is perishable; worshippers of the deities go to the deities, while my devotees come to me.\n\nHearing Kṛṣṇa's words, a feeling may arise in us: \"what does it matter to me who grants the fruit? It is enough that my desired object is fulfilled.\" For this reason Kṛṣṇa, in this verse, sounds a note of warning. Kṛṣṇa says: \"antavat tu phalaṃ teṣāṃ tad bhavaty alpa-cetasām\" — those who rejoice at obtaining some petty fruit are the small-minded. The fruit obtained in this manner is never eternal; it is fleeting. \"You reach whatever deity you worship — but not mokṣa,\" says Kṛṣṇa. No deity keeps us in their world for an endless time; hence, the moment the term of our karma is exhausted, we must return again to this mortal world. For this reason, mere worship of a deity, without awareness of Bhagavān, cannot grant eternal mokṣa.\n\nIf one fixes the mind on Bhagavān and worships him with ekabhakti, then one attains \"tad dhāma paramaṃ mama\" — the eternal mokṣa from which one never needs to return. Loving Bhagavān brings about true knowledge; through this one may attain mokṣa in the form of union with Bhagavān, which knows no sorrow ever again.\n\nHere some may be confused: \"why does God love flattery? Why does he uplift only those who praise him and not the rest?\" God loves not flattery, but truth. Within the thousand names of the Viṣṇu-sahasranāma lies the truth of what God is, what his greatness is (etymologically). Bhagavān loves those who know the truth. He has no personal likes or dislikes. What he loves is true, accurate knowledge. True knowledge exists in the form of devotion; and true devotion exists in true knowledge. Awareness of what is truly real is dear to Bhagavān, and this is the qualification needed to reach mokṣa. Hence Bhagavān does not uplift those who merely flatter him — he uplifts those who know the truth, and grants mokṣa to those with the requisite worthiness.\n\n\"Hold firm in the worship of the indwelling Bhagavān, who alone grants eternal fruit — and attain mokṣa\" — this is Kṛṣṇa's message here.\n\nKṛṣṇa now goes on, further in this chapter, to explain just how difficult it is to know Bhagavān, even for those who walk the path of devotion and knowledge seeking to know him.",
  "7.24": "avyaktam vyaktim āpannam manyante mām abuddhayaḥ |\nparam bhāvam ajānantaḥ mama avyayam anuttamam -- The unwise, not knowing my supreme, imperishable, unsurpassed nature, think that I, the unmanifest, have taken on manifestation.\n\nOrdinarily, gaining a correct understanding of Bhagavān is difficult, but forming a wrong conception is easy. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"In truth, I am unmanifest (avyakta).\" A jīva becomes manifest (vyakta) when it takes on a body composed of the five elements — hence whatever possesses a gross body is called vyakta. Bhagavān has no such gross, five-element body destined one day to perish — he is forever avyakta. Such an avyakta Bhagavān, in his descents (avatāras), of his own free will appears to everyone in a particular form. People who do not know Bhagavān's true nature, seeing the avatāra-form that appears before them, come to believe that Bhagavān too has a body composed of the five elements just like theirs, and that Bhagavān one day gives up that body. That is, they take Bhagavān, who has no manifest, gross, perishable, material body, to be one who is bound within such a gross body. \"Those who understand it in this way are utterly devoid of understanding,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "7.25": "na aham prakāśaḥ sarvasya yoga-māyā-samāvṛtaḥ |\nmūḍhaḥ ayam na abhijānāti lokaḥ mām ajam avyayam -- Veiled by my yoga-māyā, I am not manifest to all; this deluded world does not recognize me, the unborn, the imperishable.\n\nBhagavān does not appear before everyone. No one can gain a clear picture of his infinite qualities. Human thought has a limit, but Bhagavān is not confined within that limit; hence our intellect cannot grasp his infinite knowledge, his infinite power, his infinite forms. Bhagavān does not appear whenever we merely wish it; but when the time is ripe, according to his own will, Bhagavān becomes visible, to whatever extent possible, to a mind and to senses that are themselves of the nature of knowledge and bliss. In this world, the host of living beings caught in the delusion of ignorance can never grasp Bhagavān. Because of the veil of māyā called ignorance, standing between the jīva and Bhagavān, Bhagavān does not appear to us.",
  "7.26": "veda aham samatītāni vartamānāni ca arjuna |\nbhaviṣyāṇi ca bhūtāni mām tu veda na kaścana -- O Arjuna, I know the beings that are past, present, and yet to come; but me, no one knows.\n\nExplaining, with great clarity, the distinction between the jīva and Bhagavān, Kṛṣṇa says: \"I know all the beings of the past, all the beings of the present, and all the beings yet to come.\" The word bhūta here carries many meanings — the five elements, the universe formed from them, the entire host of objects formed from that universe, and the entire host of living beings — all of these are 'bhūtas.' He who knows the infinite universes created in the past, the universe existing now, the infinite universes yet to be created, the infinite hosts of living beings that existed in the past, exist now, and will come to exist in the future — that one alone, the lord of infinite universes, is Bhagavān. Kṛṣṇa says: \"mām tu veda na kaścana\" — \"no one but I knows this fully.\" No one has ever fully known Bhagavān, nor is it possible to know him fully. He is independent, complete, and eternally true. The jīva is incomplete, dependent, and only relatively true.\n\nIt is impossible to know Bhagavān fully. But why is it that not everyone can know him even to the extent of their own capacity? Kṛṣṇa explains this in the next verse.",
  "7.27": "icchā-dveṣa-samutthena dvandva-mohena bhārata |\nsarva-bhūtāni sammoham sarge yānti parantapa -- O Bhārata, O scorcher of foes, all beings, from the very moment of their birth into creation, fall into utter delusion, born of the confusion of the dualities arising from desire and hatred.\n\nAlready at birth, we are born carrying \"desire and hatred\" together with our very birth. As the body grows, this too grows, and from it comes the confusion of duality (dvandva). Not knowing what brings happiness and what brings sorrow, we mistake sorrow itself for happiness, chase after it, and when sorrow finally arrives, we cry out in dismay. Believing falsehood to be truth, confusing knowledge with ignorance, confusing truth with untruth — those caught in such duality-delusion are very difficult to correct. This is not mere delusion (moha), it is utter delusion (sammoha)! The stubbornness of \"what I know must be right,\" the insistence that \"my own view alone is correct\" — through this, a person, without knowing the truth, becomes ignorant. Thus from ignorance comes delusion, and from delusion, utter delusion. Failing to make the effort to learn what one does not know, one instead develops a perverse sort of \"knowledge\" and, afterward, the insistence that \"what I believe is right.\" This is generally the weakness we see in human beings. In this world, even the greatest fool believes himself wiser than everyone else! And so he measures the entire business of the world by the yardstick of his own intelligence, and goes astray. The Mahābhārata puts it thus: \"sarvo hi manyate loka ātmānaṃ buddhimattaram\" — every person in the world believes himself to be the most intelligent. Through this false conceit — \"I am intelligent, my judgment alone is correct, others do not have as much understanding as I do\" — a person proceeds toward utter delusion. How one may escape this danger is explained in the next verse.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna with the epithets 'Bhārata' and 'Parantapa.' To gain awareness of Bhagavān we must become Bhārata-Parantapas — proceeding as a charioteer (ratha) on the path of light (Bhārata), and constantly contemplating the supreme principle (Parantapa) — only then may we come to know even a shadow of Bhagavān. This is the special meaning contained in these epithets.",
  "7.28": "yeṣām tu antagatam pāpam jananām puṇya-karmaṇām |\nte dvandva-moha-nirmuktāḥ bhajante mām dṛḍha-vratāḥ -- Those virtuous people whose sin has come to its end, freed from the delusion of dualities, worship me with firm resolve.\n\nIn the previous verse Kṛṣṇa spoke of duality-delusion. There are, at root, two causes for this: one is the jīva's own nature (e.g., Duryodhana, Śakuni); the other is prārabdha-karma (e.g., Karṇa, Ajāmiḷa, Piṅgaḷā). If the jīva's very nature is of this fallen kind, there is no remedy for it whatsoever. But for a sāttvika jīva who, for some reason, has strayed onto a bad path, there is a way through knowledge. Sometimes, through some misfortune, even those with direct (aparokṣa) knowledge stray from the path. Until their sinful karma has been washed away, such people cannot return to the right path — but sometimes a tragedy or shock occurring in life brings them back to the path of truth. In such circumstances, the realization of their own fault dawns on them, and, in a spirit of remorse, a profound devotion to God arises in them.\n\nThere is no atonement more excellent than remorse. Even the gravest sin can be corrected through remorse. In our lives we commit wrongs, knowingly and unknowingly, all the time — mentally, physically, and verbally, faults occur without our even realizing it. But the remorse that arises from the heart, once the fault is recognized, is itself the atonement for such wrongs. There is a verse recited, in the Vedic tradition, after bowing down following the worship of a deity: \"pāpo'haṃ pāpa-karmāhaṃ pāpātmā pāpa-sambhava | trāhi māṃ kṛpayā deva sarva-pāpa-haro bhava || anyathā śaraṇaṃ nāsti tvam eva śaraṇaṃ mama | tasmāt kāruṇya-bhāvena rakṣa māṃ jagadīśvara |\" — in this verse we plead with Bhagavān: \"Forgive whatever wrong I have done, knowingly or unknowingly.\" We surrender to God, saying, \"I have carried sin from birth, and from it I commit wrongs — therefore forgive me, and deliver me from this sin.\" Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"te dvandva-moha-nirmuktā bhajante māṃ dṛḍha-vratāḥ\" — that is, once one has come out from such sinful karma, all duality-delusion is resolved, and a firm, deep devotion to Bhagavān becomes established. Those who, having become aware of their sin, turn to Bhagavān in remorse, proceed very swiftly along Bhagavān's path.",
  "7.29": "jarā-maraṇa-mokṣāya mām āśritya yatanti ye |\nte brahma tat viduḥ kṛtsnam adhyātmam karma ca akhilam -- Those who, taking refuge in me, strive for release from old age and death, come to know that Brahman, the whole of the adhyātma, and all karma as well.\n\nThose who turn toward Bhagavān take refuge in him and strive, seeking release from old age and death, seeking freedom from the bonds of this saṃsāra. Generally, it is excellent to desire nothing at all; but where desiring is the very nature of life, then to desire nothing else but mokṣa is the most excellent desire. In this verse Kṛṣṇa explains the wonderful subject of what effort the seeker of mokṣa must make, and how one must proceed on the path of knowledge.\n\nWhat must be understood in adhyātma-vidyā, Kṛṣṇa calls \"Brahma.\" The word Brahma comes from the root 'ava vṛddhau' (to grow great); avati means the greatest of all — that is Om. That is, Bhagavān, beginningless, eternal, full of every quality, all-pervading, all-capable, is Brahma — he himself is Om. The central point of this verse, \"te brahma tad viduḥ,\" means \"they come to know the Bhagavān who is present everywhere.\" Realization of Bhagavān's all-pervasiveness is Brahma-sākṣātkāra. Bimba-sākṣātkāra (realization of the archetype) refers to the realization of that Bhagavān who is subtler than the subtlest within us. This same Bhagavān, in his form as archetype (bimba), pervades and fills the entire universe — this is Brahma-sākṣātkāra. We cannot directly perceive Bhagavān's all-pervading form, but awareness of that pervasion is exceedingly important. He who, filling the sun's radiance, illuminates the entire world, is the very same one who dwells within me and illumines me — this awareness is ātma-sākṣātkāra.\n\nBali Cakravartī's experience was likewise. As is well known, Bhagavān in the form of Vāmana, the great giver, asked King Bali for a gift of three paces of land. Disregarding Śukrācārya's opposition, Bali resolved to give the gift to Vāmana. Then Vāmana, with his first step, covered the earth, and with his second, covered the sky; for the third step, Bali offered his own head, and, going down to Pātāla, attained upliftment. If we analyze this story a little more deeply, we discover the essence hidden within it. All of us, drowning in the ocean of saṃsāra, are, in a certain sense, Balis. To attain realization of Bhagavān, we must all become strong, mentally and spiritually. There are, principally, three steps in worship. The first is to worship a small (vāmana) image of Bhagavān as God. The second is to come, through continued worship, to know that Bhagavān is not confined to the image alone, but is the power pervading the entire world. The important third step is to know that Bhagavān is the indweller of all, that he fills me too, and to bow down before that supreme power. Then true realization of Bhagavān occurs for us, and Bhagavān uplifts us. Bali — knowing Bhagavān to be the indweller within himself (ātmani vedanam) — offered himself to Bhagavān (ātma-nivedanam), that is, gave his own head. This is the concept of the three steps.\n\nBhagavān is all-pervading, all-capable; he dwells within everything and does everything. When one knows the truth that this jīva is a reflection dependent on him, the mystery of all karma becomes known. Hence the first three matters to be understood in adhyātma-vidyā are: the jīva, who is Bhagavān's reflection; Brahma, who is complete and all-capable; and karma, which proceeds through that infinite capacity. This is Brahma-Karma-Adhyātma. Beyond these three subjects, Kṛṣṇa explains three further matters in the next verse.",
  "7.30": "sa-adhibhūta-adhidaivam mām sa-adhiyajñam ca ye viduḥ |\nprayāṇa-kāle api ca mām te viduḥ yukta-cetasaḥ -- Those who know me together with adhibhūta and adhidaiva, and together with adhiyajña, and who, with disciplined mind, remember me even at the time of departure — they truly know me.\n\nFor awareness of Bhagavān to arise, one must first have awareness of the physical science (adhibhūta). Awareness of the five elements, this body, the macrocosm, the microcosm, and the substances present here is the first step of spiritual practice. After adhibhūta comes awareness of adhidaiva. In this body of ours, composed of the five elements, how do actions such as \"seeing, hearing, experiencing\" proceed in an orderly, regulated manner? There is no action in the inert (jaḍa); rather, the presiding deities behind it, dwelling in each sense-organ, regulate each action — this is the understanding of adhidaiva. The overseer who governs these thousands of deities is that Bhagavān. Every single action that occurs in this world, or in the piṇḍāṇḍa, is a yajña; the one who conducts that yajña as its overseer is adhiyajña — the regulator of all the deities, Bhagavān.\n\nIn sum, we must clearly understand six matters: (1) adhibhūta — the physical world; (2) adhyātma — the individual jīva; (3) adhidaiva — the entire host of deities presiding over each principle; (4) adhiyajña — Bhagavān, the regulator of all; (5) Brahma-realization — the awareness that the archetypal Bhagavān, the indweller, fills every place; and (6) the truth that every action occurs under the governance of that Bhagavān. These six matters must remain firmly established in our minds even at the final moment of our life's journey — that is, when we give up the body. Whatever remains firmly fixed in our mind when we give up the body determines our next birth. For this reason, awareness of Bhagavān throughout one's lifetime is exceedingly important. \"One who, in his striving, never falls prey to delusion, and who, even at the moment of death, ever contemplates Bhagavān, reaches me\" — says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nThus, having opened before us in this chapter the splendor of knowledge, Kṛṣṇa goes on, in the following chapter, to explain in greater detail adhibhūta, adhidaiva, adhyātma, adhiyajña, Brahma, and karma.\n\nThus ends the seventh chapter.",
  "8.1": "Arjunaḥ uvāca:\n\nkiṃ tad brahma kim adhyātmaṃ kiṃ karma puruṣottama |\nadhibhūtaṃ ca kiṃ proktam adhidaivaṃ kim ucyate -- Arjuna asked: O Puruṣottama, what is that 'Brahman'? What is 'adhyātma'? What is 'karma'? What do they call 'adhibhūta'? What do they call 'adhidaiva'?\n\nArjuna asks Kṛṣṇa: \"What is tad brahma (that Brahman-principle)? What is adhyātma? And what is karma?\" Here Vyāsa captures the feeling behind Arjuna's question through the epithet \"Puruṣottama.\" Puruṣottama means: one who stands beyond kṣara (the jīva) and is greater even than akṣara (Śrī-tattva). \"You who stand beyond kṣara and akṣara are the Puruṣottama; there is no principle higher than you. Therefore, as I understand it, you are Brahman. I wish to hear this explained by you\" -- this is the sentiment Arjuna expresses through the epithet Puruṣottama. Arjuna then asks: \"What do they call adhibhūta? What do they call adhidaiva?\"",
  "8.2": "adhiyajñaḥ kathaṃ ko'tra dehe'smin madhusūdana |\nprayāṇakāle ca kathaṃ jñeyo'si niyatātmabhiḥ -- O Madhusūdana, who is the 'adhiyajña' in this body, and how? And how are you to be known by the self-controlled at the time of departure?\n\nContinuing, Arjuna asks: \"Who is the adhiyajña?\" Who is the adhiyajña within our body, and in what manner? What is the scope of the term adhiyajña's meaning? -- this is Arjuna's question. Here again Vyāsa uses the epithet \"Madhusūdana\" to convey the feeling behind Arjuna's question. The name \"Madhusūdana\" carries two principal meanings. \"Madhu\" means bliss (ānanda). \"Sūda\" means one who performs good deeds -- the sāttvika. So Madhusūdana means one who, dwelling within the sāttvika, gives them bliss. \"Sūda\" also carries another meaning -- destruction. So Madhusūdana is also one who destroys the joy of the wicked. \"You alone, seated within us, are the giver of joy, and you alone are the giver of sorrow. Seated within us, you govern every yajña. Therefore it is my understanding that you are the adhiyajña -- explain this to me\" -- this is the feeling of Arjuna that Vyāsa conveys through this epithet. Arjuna asks: \"At the final and greatest journey of our life, explain to me the manner and process of the jīva's departure from the body.\"\n\nLooking at the two epithets used in this verse -- Puruṣottama and Madhusūdana -- and their meanings, it becomes clear that Arjuna already knew the true meaning of these six matters (words). Yet, wishing to hear the detailed explanation from Bhagavān, he puts this question to Kṛṣṇa. Generally, those who already know still ask such questions with the intention of making it known to others. Here Arjuna asks this question of Kṛṣṇa both to confirm what he himself knows and to convey Bhagavān's explanation to all of humankind.\n\nWe might wonder: \"Why is Arjuna, standing on the battlefield, asking such questions? What need is there for this here?\" Generally, for those standing on a battlefield, nothing haunts them as much as death -- it awaits them at every moment. They remain acutely aware of death and of the state beyond death; this matters greatly to soldiers. Up to this point in the Kṛṣṇa-Arjuna dialogue, Kṛṣṇa has nowhere assured Arjuna of victory -- he has only said that fighting for dharma is Arjuna's duty. Therefore, for Arjuna standing on the battlefield, the question of death and what lies beyond it is of great importance. Bhagavān's answer to this question of Arjuna's forms the rest of this eighth chapter.",
  "8.3": "Bhagavān uvāca -- The Lord said:\n\nakṣaraṃ brahma paramaṃ svabhāvo'dhyātmam ucyate |\nbhūtabhāvodbhavakaro visargaḥ karmasaṃjñitaḥ -- The supreme imperishable is 'Brahman.' The svarūpa of the jīva [and the aggregate universe (piṇḍāṇḍa) related to the jīva -- the Lord's own inward disposition] is called 'adhyātma.' The Lord's manifold act of creation, the cause of the manifestation of the sentient and the insentient, is called 'karma.'\n\nKṛṣṇa says \"Brahma\" means \"paramaṃ brahma -- paramam akṣaram.\" Bhagavān is the greatest of all -- he is the supreme imperishable; that we must come to know such a Bhagavān is the meaning behind the word \"Brahma.\" In the Viṣṇu-sahasranāma, Bhagavān is described thus:\n\nparamaṃ yo mahat tejaḥ paramaṃ yo mahat tapaḥ |\nparamaṃ yo mahad brahma paramaṃ yaḥ parāyaṇam ||\n\nBhagavān is the form that gives light to every light; he is the final goal of all thought; he is the destination of all knowledge. Here Bhagavān is called \"parama mahā brahma.\" \"Brahma\" means the jīvas; \"parabrahma\" means the liberated jīvas; \"paramabrahma\" means Śrī-tattva -- the ever-liberated Śrī Lakṣmī. \"Parama mahā brahma\" means Nārāyaṇa. Bhagavān is supreme even over the parāyaṇas -- here \"parāyaṇas\" means the tattvābhimāni deities who are our support. Bhagavān is the master of all the deities. This is the meaning of \"Brahma,\" one of the six matters we must come to know.\n\nContinuing, Kṛṣṇa says: \"svabhāvo'dhyātmam ucyate.\" Here svabhāva means one's own nature -- the manner of the jīva's existence, the jīva's own nature. Before we come to know Bhagavān, we must know our own jīva-nature. What am I? What is the good within me (plus point)? What is the bad within me (minus point)? -- we must constantly strive to know ourselves this way [though we spend most of our time instead judging others!]. We are not even aware that, apart from the eyes, hands, legs, and other organs of this body, there is a thing called \"I.\" We must first come to know the existence of the jīva, and only then its nature. [Generally we do not know our own jīva-nature; we live instead under the sway of influence.] The jīva is an extremely subtle thing. It resides in a center of power called the anāhata-cakra at the middle of the heart-lotus. This jīva is like a small lamp lit in a room -- just as a lamp illumines the whole room, the jīva, though extremely subtle, pervades the entire body with its light. Such is the jīva and its nature -- this is \"adhyātma.\"\n\nKṛṣṇa continues: \"This world is bhūta and bhāva -- that is, the creation of the sentient and the insentient.\" We exist in this world. How was such a world created? When was it created? What primal substance lies behind it? Who is its instrumental and material cause? Why does the jīva take birth through a body? When we reflect on all this in order, one truth becomes clear to us -- this is the doctrine of karma. The primal power creating this multitude of jīvas and this insentient world is Bhagavān; his action alone is true action. To understand the source of this world's manifold creation is to understand true karma. All of us are but puppets made by Bhagavān -- he is the puppeteer, and our action is bound by that puppeteer's law. The entire wheel of the universe turns subject to Bhagavān; his action is the great action (mahākriyā). Our action is like an ant walking on a great turning wheel -- whichever direction the ant moves, its motion is really the wheel's own turning, not the ant's; the wheel does not turn because of the ant. In the same way, the world's course is not decided by our action -- it proceeds continually according to its own course. Hence there is no meaning in the pride of \"I did this,\" \"this happened because of me.\" Thus, that distinctive creative activity which causes the creation of the sentient and insentient worlds is true \"karma.\"\n\nSeen from another dimension, this verse opens up differently. Here the jīva does not enter at all -- all three matters speak of Bhagavān himself. \"Brahma\" means \"Bhagavān\"; \"svabhāva\" means Bhagavān's own independent nature. So the contemplation of Bhagavān's qualities and nature is itself \"adhyātma.\" Some among us ask: \"Why does Bhagavān have this nature -- joy for one, sorrow for another; poverty for one, wealth for another; one to hell, one to heaven -- why this discrimination?\" This is a childish question, and it is from such reasoning that atheism is born. The jīva is beginningless and eternal; Bhagavān does not create it. Bhagavān's nature is to create each jīva according to the qualities and worthiness already inherent in it -- so the question of discrimination does not even arise. Bhagavān's independence in creation, his impartiality, his being unaffected by any influence, his transcendence of the three guṇas -- these are Bhagavān's fundamental nature, and the awareness of this is \"adhyātma.\" When we know Bhagavān's nature, we understand what his creation is -- that is karma. In Bhagavān's action there are no three stages of knowledge-will-deed as there are for us; his willing is itself the action -- to know this is \"the understanding of karma.\" Thus, the understanding of Bhagavān's own form, his nature, and his action -- these are Brahma, adhyātma, and karma.",
  "8.4": "adhibhūtaṃ kṣaro bhāvaḥ puruṣaś cādhidaivatam |\nadhiyajño'ham evātra dehe dehabhṛtāṃ vara -- The perishable state [of birth and death, the aggregate universe made of embodied beings] is 'adhibhūta.' The tattvābhimāni deities who govern from within the body [the Śrī-tattva, called 'puruṣa,' the supreme driving power of the world] are 'adhidaiva.' O best among embodied beings, in this body I myself am the 'adhiyajña.'\n\nAs seen in the previous verse, adhyātma is the jīva imprisoned in the body. Every aspect of this five-element world, created for the enjoyment of this jīva, falls under adhibhūta. The root substance of the five elements, and the world built from them, is adhibhūta -- everything composed of the three guṇas, from inert nature up to the gross universe, is \"adhibhūta.\"\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"puruṣaś cādhidaivatam.\" There are deities within the body who regulate its various senses to assist the jīva dwelling there -- this is the science of adhidaivata. That is one facet. Seen another way, here Kṛṣṇa addresses \"puruṣa.\" There are four \"puruṣas\" within our body -- śarīra-puruṣa, chandaḥ-puruṣa, veda-puruṣa, and saṃvatsara-puruṣa. For this body to stand and move, the śiva-śakti that is śarīra-puruṣa is needed. For thought in the mind to emerge as speech in the forms of spandana, parāśari, paśyantī, madhyamā and vaikharī, Śeṣa, who is chandaḥ-puruṣa, is the cause. Garuḍa, who is veda-puruṣa, dwells in the mind-sheath (manomaya-kośa) and grants the power of vedāntic reflection and mental contemplation, causing us to attain the rare power of vedic speech. The one who governs the jīva's worthiness and the entire form of the jīva is Caturmukha-Brahma, the deity presiding over the jīva's faculties -- he is saṃvatsara-puruṣa [\"saṃ-vatsa-ra\" means the grandfather who rears his children well]. Brahma and Vāyu lift the jīva and set it on the path of sādhana. Thus these five deities are, after Bhagavān, the chief presiding deities within the body, and the entire universe governed by them is adhibhūta.\n\nKṛṣṇa continues: \"I dwell within every body in a particular form -- this form of Bhagavān is the 'adhiyajña' form.\" The one who presides over and governs the yajña, who is its enjoyer, its performer, its causer, and the giver of its fruit -- that Bhagavān is adhiyajña. Every action we perform, knowing it as an act of worship of Bhagavān, is yajña. \"Yaja devā pūjāyām\" -- every action of our life is worship of Bhagavān. Bhagavān, dwelling within our body and causing action through all the tattvābhimāni deities, is \"adhiyajña.\"\n\nSeen from a second dimension [as in the previous verse's analysis of Bhagavān, Bhagavān's nature, and his action], adhibhūta means Brahmā and all the deities. The sentient-insentient world, the jīvas, the body, Brahma, and the universe -- all of this is adhibhūta. The \"Śakti,\" Śrī-tattva, who is mother to Brahmā and all the deities and second only to Bhagavān, is adhidaiva. Bhagavān, who pervades everywhere and stands as the indwelling controller within every jīva, is adhiyajña.",
  "8.5": "antakāle ca mām eva smaran muktvā kalevaram |\nyaḥ prayāti sa madbhāvaṃ yāti nāsty atra saṃśayaḥ -- One who departs, remembering me alone even at the last hour, having given up the body, attains my own state [attains dwelling in me] -- of this there is no doubt.\n\nAnswering Arjuna's last question, Kṛṣṇa says: \"Whoever, remembering me alone even at the moment of death, leaves the body and departs, comes and dwells in me, becoming -- just as I am -- of the nature of knowledge and bliss. Never again do they fall into the turmoil of this ignorant and sorrowful worldly existence. Never doubt this.\" Here \"antakāle\" does not mean \"in old age,\" but rather the final moment when the jīva leaves the body. To the consciousness that departs remembering Bhagavān as it leaves the body, an immense light appears, and that consciousness attains liberation. [On the jīva's departure from the body, and its journey after leaving the body, the book \"Life After Life,\" which gathered the experiences of many who had died and come back to life, sheds light.]",
  "8.6": "yaṃ yaṃ vāpi smaran bhāvaṃ tyajaty ante kalevaram |\ntaṃ tam evaiti kaunteya sadā tadbhāvabhāvitaḥ -- O Kaunteya, whatever state one remembers as one leaves the body at the end, that alone one attains, drawn always by the saṃskāra rooted in that very state.\n\nWhy did Kṛṣṇa say that those who remember Bhagavān in life's last moment \"join me\"? This verse answers that. Here Kṛṣṇa reveals to us a remarkable psychology of the moment of death. There is a purpose, Kṛṣṇa says, to meditating on Bhagavān at the moment of death. Whatever we remember as we give up the body, that is what we obtain in the next birth -- so if we remember Bhagavān at that time, we join Bhagavān. The thought that arises in the final moment of death is the deciding factor for the next birth; hence, whatever one is to become in the next birth, that very thing comes to mind! Those who have no yoga (connection) for reaching Bhagavān never have Bhagavān come to mind at that hour. There are many illustrations of this in the scriptures. The story of Bharata, son of Ṛṣabhadeva -- after whom this land came to be called \"Bhārata\" -- makes this point clearly. (This Bharata is not the son of Duṣyanta; Duṣyanta's son Bharata is the founding figure of the \"Bharata lineage.\") The supremely righteous Bharata, once his son had come of age, handed over all his authority to him, took up vānaprastha, and lived in the forest with the sages, ever devoted to Bhagavān. One day he found in the forest a fawn whose mother had died. Bharata brought it home and began to raise it, and from then on spent his time entirely absorbed in caring for that deer. Because of this, his sādhana fell into decline. Before the fawn had even grown large, the occasion of his own death arrived -- and at that time, what haunted Bharata was not the thought of Bhagavān but anxiety for that fawn! Because of this he was born a deer in his next birth. This is the finest example that prārabdha does not spare even one who has attained aparokṣa-jñāna. This story is a vivid illustration of the very point Kṛṣṇa makes here.\n\nEven if we practice sādhana all our life, the thought of Bhagavān may still fail to arise at the moment of death. For this reason, a lifetime's sādhana -- or the sādhana of many lifetimes -- is necessary. Kṛṣṇa says \"sadā tadbhāvabhāvitaḥ.\" Here bhāva means absorption. One must think of Bhagavān continually, with a mind ever absorbed in him alone. For the thought of Bhagavān to arise at the last hour, one needs the saṃskāra of a mind constantly fixed on Bhagavān. Without this, if one squanders one's life thinking \"I can simply remember Bhagavān at the time of death,\" the thought of Bhagavān will not arise at all at that hour!\n\nHowever much Bhīṣmācārya thought of Bhagavān in his final days, what he actually saw was Kṛṣṇa wounded and bleeding from his own arrows! He did not attain the true vision of the blissful Bhagavān. What we must understand here is that we should never fix our mind deeply on any bad thing -- if we do, that is what we will see at the last hour! Throughout life we must never dwell on bad thoughts, nor hate anyone as wicked. To hate someone is a very bad thing -- it ruins our entire life. Keep away from the wicked, but do not hate them. Hating and reviling someone, and dwelling on it deeply, does the other person no harm at all -- but we ruin our own life by it. Whatever we hate settles itself deeply in our mind and drags us down; it is that very thing that finally rears its head at the time of death! So we must be watchful about this. We are not fit to criticize anyone, because we cannot see into another's heart -- we speak only from outward appearance, without the power to see \"who is what\" within. So Kṛṣṇa says: give up thinking of others, and think always of Bhagavān -- the one and only thing we should be thinking of is Bhagavān.",
  "8.7": "tasmāt sarveṣu kāleṣu mām anusmara yudhya ca |\nmayy arpitamanobuddhir mām evaiṣyasy asaṃśayaḥ -- Therefore remember me at all times, and fight. With your mind and intellect surrendered to me, you shall surely come to me alone.\n\nThere is no fixed hour for remembering God. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Remember me at all times.\" Be aware, whenever performing any action, that it is Bhagavān who is causing that action to be done. Even while fighting, the feeling that \"Bhagavān, seated within me, is causing this action -- this is his will\" transforms even war into the worship of God! Awareness of Bhagavān and surrender to him prevent even our unwitting mistakes from staining us with sin. There is no restriction of time for the remembrance of Bhagavān -- whenever the remembrance of Bhagavān arises, that is the sacred hour. To hold him in the mind is itself purity. So one must perform one's duty remembering him, as an offering of worship, without egotism.\n\nThe feeling present within an action determines whether it is dharma or adharma. Take the example of Dharmavyādha -- he lived selling meat, yet lived as a supremely righteous man, because his inner disposition (anusandhāna) was pure. He held the conviction: \"God has set me to this work; my duty in this birth is to make my living selling meat, and I must do it honestly and carry on my life.\" He conducted his trade only for the sustenance of himself and his family. Living honestly in this way, he rose to a height surpassing even the sages. So purity of feeling is the highest thing. Hence we must fix our mind and intellect in Bhagavān -- \"then you shall surely reach me without doubt,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "8.8": "abhyāsayogayuktena cetasā nānyagāminā |\nparamaṃ puruṣaṃ divyaṃ yāti pārtha anucintayan -- O Pārtha, with the mind made steady through the practice of yoga, going nowhere else, continually contemplating -- one attains that supreme, divine Puruṣa.\n\nPārtha, the Supreme Puruṣa -- whose creation is but a līlā -- is attained by one who, through the discipline of practice, with an inward disposition wanting nothing else, remembers him with devotion.\n\nTo make our mind stand continually fixed in Bhagavān, \"constant reflection\" (nirantara manana) is the single most important element. Ordinarily our mind wanders toward things we do not even want; we must train it to think only of Bhagavān, with nothing else drawing it away. Our mind, intellect, and inward faculty (citta) all work as we train them to work; we must train them in the practice of thinking of Bhagavān. Practice (abhyāsa) is the single means by which the mind's wandering nature is tamed. When this is done, the mind becomes fixed in Bhagavān -- and a mind fixed in Bhagavān beholds Bhagavān even at the last hour. This is not mere thought (cintana) but anucintana -- the true, exact contemplation of Bhagavān as described in the śruti. One cannot reach Bhagavān by contemplating what is false.\n\nKṛṣṇa says one must, with a one-pointed mind, contemplate the divine Supreme Puruṣa, Bhagavān who dwells in the middle of the heart-lotus. Kṛṣṇa's message here is: recognize in meditation that the greatest of the great, Bhagavān, dwells eternally and worthily within your heart. Bhagavān is all-pervading, all-capable, full of every quality, beginningless and eternal -- such a Bhagavān dwells in our heart-lotus and is divine (divya).\n\nThe word \"divya\" used here carries many meanings. Originally, \"divya\" or \"deva\" derive from the root \"div.\" In the ancient dhātu-pāṭha this root carries seven meanings: (1) dyuti (radiance) (2) vijigīṣā (the will to conquer) (3) kānti (splendor/desire) (4) stuti (praise) (5) vyavahāra (dealing) (6) krīḍā (play) (7) gatiḥ (motion/knowledge). Recently three more meanings -- moda, mada, and svapna -- have been added, though these are not found in the ancient dhātu-pāṭha. Let us briefly look at the seven meanings above:\n\n1) Dyuti: the nature of light. Bhagavān, who gives light to the sun, moon, and every source of radiance, fills us within with the light of knowledge.\n\n2) Vijigīṣā: Bhagavān stands higher than all, and is victory itself.\n\n3) Kānti: one who can create by mere will -- who gave us our own will and fulfills it according to each one's worthiness.\n\n4) Stuti: one praised by all; the one whom all praise is Bhagavān, denoted by every word.\n\n5) Vyavahāra: one who conducts all the dealings of the world.\n\n6) Krīḍā: creation-preservation-destruction are but play to Bhagavān; birth and death, every transaction, is his play.\n\n7) Gatiḥ: one who gives motion and knowledge -- who has gone everywhere, who knows everything, who dwells within all as the indwelling reflection (bimba) -- he is divya or \"deva.\"\n\nThus many meanings can be found in the word \"divya.\" When we say \"O God,\" if we once recall the meanings above, the joy that comes of it is boundless -- such is the power in Bhagavān's name. We remain with Bhagavān and yet are mentally far from him. Through constant anucintana of Bhagavān we can attain his direct realization.\n\nIn the remaining part of this chapter, Kṛṣṇa explains what it means to hold Bhagavān in anucintana. The following verses show what form a meditator should hold in meditating upon Bhagavān.",
  "8.9": "kaviṃ purāṇam anuśāsitāram\naṇor aṇīyāṃsam anusmared yaḥ |\nsarvasya dhātāram acintyarūpam\nāditya-varṇaṃ tamasaḥ parastāt -- One who remembers him who is all-knowing, the most ancient, the ruler of all, subtler than the atom, the sustainer of all, of inconceivable form, radiant as the sun, beyond all darkness -- attains him.\n\nWhen meditating on our indwelling Bhagavān, Kṛṣṇa here spells out, point by point, the matters one must hold in contemplation. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Worship Bhagavān as kavi.\" Here kavi means the all-knowing, the omniscient. What we need first is knowledge -- without knowledge there is no bliss -- so we must first seek awareness of Bhagavān. Second, Kṛṣṇa says, worship Bhagavān as purāṇam. Purāṇa means the one who existed before all else and remains ever the same at all times -- the one who, seated within our body (pura), governs us at every moment; this is the meaning to be held in contemplation.\n\nThird, Bhagavān is \"anuśāsitāram\" -- the one who ordains and governs everything. My own existence, and the existence of every object in the world, is subject to his control; Bhagavān governs according to the worthiness of each jīva's own nature -- knowing this, one must contemplate him. That same Bhagavān who fills the entire universe is present, subtler than the atom, within the jīva itself. \"Bhagavān, who bears everything, sits within us and sustains us; he is the power that upholds and nourishes the entire universe; he grants every desire to all\" -- with this understanding one must meditate on him.\n\nWe must know that Bhagavān is beyond the reach of our imagination -- his is a form that neither our speech nor our mind can grasp. If, in meditation, one looks upon Bhagavān with the eye of the jīva's own nature, he appears like the rising sun. His hue is not a color born of material nature; it is a color beyond the three guṇas, transcendent, imperishable. One who knows Bhagavān thus and contemplates him continually attains Bhagavān himself at the moment of leaving the body, remembering him alone.",
  "8.10": "prayāṇakāle manasācalena\nbhaktyā yukto yogabalena caiva |\nbhruvor madhye prāṇam āveśya samyak\nsa taṃ paraṃ puruṣam upaiti divyam -- At the time of departure, with an unwavering mind, endowed with devotion and the strength of yoga, having drawn the life-breath rightly to the point between the eyebrows -- he attains that supreme, divine Puruṣa.\n\nThere is another path to reaching Bhagavān -- haṭhayoga. This path is difficult; it is the way of reaching Bhagavān through yama-niyama, prāṇāyāma, and pratyāhāra. In this verse Kṛṣṇa explains what those who practice this path should do at the time of their death. Having achieved a steady body and a steady prāṇamaya-kośa through āsana and prāṇāyāma, having through pratyāhāra expelled the impurities filling oneself and barred any bad thought from entering, one attains one-pointed concentration -- this is a very long and difficult sādhana. Such practitioners must bring the jīva, which dwells with the life-force at the middle of the heart-lotus (anāhata-cakra), upward through the viśuddhi-cakra to a halt at the bhrū-madhya (the ājñā-cakra between the two eyebrows). This process is extremely difficult, and unwavering devotion is essential throughout it. If, in this way, the jīva departs from the bhrū-madhya through the brahmarandhra at the crown, it goes directly to Bhagavān.\n\nBecause this haṭhayoga is such a difficult sādhana, the ancients incorporated a simple, supporting practice into their daily life -- ūrdhvapuṇḍra is a fine example of this. It relates to the jīva's upward course, and is a simple method of fully awakening the centers of power within the body. [But today we do not even know why we apply this mark.]\n\nIt is dangerous to practice haṭhayoga without proper guidance. In the second skandha of the Bhāgavata, an entire chapter explains in detail the stages by which the jīva is carried upward. Thus one may reach Bhagavān either through the persistence of yoga, or -- as a child insists with its mother -- a devotee may reach him through the sheer insistence of bhakti.\n\nHaving explained this method of bringing the jīva to rest at the bhrū-madhya, Kṛṣṇa goes on, in the next three verses, to explain in still more detail the manner in which the jīva then departs through the gateway of the crown's power-center.",
  "8.11": "yad akṣaraṃ vedavido vadanti\nviśanti yad yatayo vītarāgāḥ |\nyad icchanto brahmacaryaṃ caranti\ntat te padaṃ saṅgraheṇa pravakṣye -- That which the knowers of the Veda call the imperishable, which ascetics free from passion enter, desiring which seekers practice brahmacarya -- that state I shall now declare to you in brief.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"The jīva, brought by yogic practice to rest at the bhrū-madhya, must from there be carried upward, in meditation, toward the sahasrāra.\" Speaking now of the form one must meditate upon during this process, Kṛṣṇa says: \"The knowers of the Veda call it the imperishable 'akṣara' [the all-denoting word whose meaning is expressed through the syllable Oṃkāra].\" Those who have won Bhagavān through great effort, who have crossed beyond worldly attachment, go and join that Bhagavān. \"They,\" says Kṛṣṇa, \"desiring to know such a Bhagavān, take up brahmacarya in their life.\" [Here \"brahmacarya\" does not mean the bodily celibacy given in the dictionary, but rather \"brahmaṇi caraṇam\" -- the settling of the mind in Bhagavān.] \"Desiring to reach him, they contemplate that great principle in this manner. I shall now tell you, in brief, this process of attaining mokṣa,\" says Kṛṣṇa, and he goes on to explain this process to Arjuna in the following verses.",
  "8.12": "sarvadvārāṇi saṃyamya mano hṛdi nirudhya ca |\nmūrdhny ādhāyātmanaḥ prāṇam āsthito yogadhāraṇām ||12||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and commentary for these two verses (8.12 and 8.13) are given together at the next verse (8.13).]",
  "8.13": "omityekākṣaraṃ brahma vyāharan mām anusmaran |\nyaḥ prayāti tyajan dehaṃ sa yāti paramāṃ gatim -- Restraining all the gateways [of the body], confining the mind within the heart, fixing one's own life-breath at the crown of the head, established in yogic concentration -- uttering the single imperishable syllable Oṃ, denoting Brahman, remembering me -- whoever departs, leaving the body thus, attains the supreme goal.\n\nRestraining all the nāḍī-gateways, holding the mind firmly fixed in Bhagavān alone, placing one's own life-breath at the crown, established in the practice of yoga, glorifying that supreme principle denoted by the single syllable \"Oṃ,\" remembering me -- one who leaves the body and ascends in this way reaches the place from which there is no return.\n\nWhen carrying the jīva toward the sahasrāra, all the other gateways of the body must be closed by the power of yoga, and one must open the closed door of the sahasrāra and go out through it. In doing this, the mind must be fixed upon that all-destroying Bhagavān. Using one's yogic practice to the fullest, restraining the breath, placing the mind in Bhagavān, one must meditate.\n\n\"One who, remembering me -- denoted by Oṃkāra -- by means of Oṃkāra, goes out through the door of the sahasrāra, gives up the body and ascends, reaches mokṣa (Bhagavān), the place to which there is never again a return.\"\n\nThis sādhana is possible only for haṭhayogis, and it is an extremely difficult practice. Not everyone can attain accomplishment through such yogic practice. Therefore, in the next verse, Kṛṣṇa explains the easier path.",
  "8.14": "ananyacetāḥ satataṃ yo māṃ smarati nityaśaḥ |\ntasyāhaṃ sulabhaḥ pārtha nityayuktasya yoginaḥ -- O Pārtha, for one who, with an undivided mind, remembers me constantly, day after day -- for that ever-steadfast yogī, I am easily attained.\n\n\"For the sādhaka who worships me, thinking of Bhagavān continually and eternally, remembering nothing else, I become effortlessly attainable,\" says Kṛṣṇa. One who is ever engaged in sādhana and has reached its culmination attains Bhagavān without any further effort.",
  "8.15": "mām upetya punarjanma duḥkhālayam aśāśvatam |\nnāpnuvanti mahātmānaḥ saṃsiddhiṃ paramāṃ gatāḥ -- Having attained me, great souls who have reached the supreme perfection do not again attain birth in this transient, sorrow-ridden world.\n\nJīvas who, through sādhana, attain siddhi and reach Bhagavān never again take birth in this transient world, which is but a dwelling place of sorrow. \"Those who come and join me are the truly blessed. In this world, in the pursuit of life, the highest attainment is the direct realization of Bhagavān. Those who attain it need never again struggle, caught in the bonds of worldly existence,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "8.16": "ābrahmabhuvanāl lokāḥ punarāvartino'rjuna |\nmām upetya tu kaunteya punarjanma na vidyate -- O Arjuna, all the worlds up to and including the world of Brahmā are subject to return. But, O Kaunteya, having attained me, there is no rebirth.\n\nFrom the world of Brahmā down through every other world, destruction awaits one day. Caturmukha Brahmā's own lifespan is 31,104,000,000,000 (thirty-one thousand, one hundred four thousand crore) years -- yet this is but a single day to Bhagavān! Bhagavān is beyond time. Therefore Kṛṣṇa says: \"Only upon attaining me is there no rebirth.\"",
  "8.17": "sahasrayugaparyantam ahar yad brahmaṇo viduḥ |\nrātriṃ yugasahasrāntāṃ te'horātravido janāḥ -- Those who know that Brahmā's day lasts a thousand yugas, and his night likewise a thousand yugas -- they are the ones who truly know day and night.\n\nAlthough Bhagavān is beyond time, for the sake of contemplating him, his period of creation is called Bhagavān's day, and his period of dissolution is called Bhagavān's night. One cycle of a thousand yuga-cakras makes one day of Brahmā... a yuga-cakra means the four yugas together: Kaliyuga, 432,000 years; Dvāpara yuga, 864,000 years; Tretā yuga, 1,296,000 years; Kṛta yuga, 1,728,000 years -- together, one yuga-cakra is 4,320,000 years. A thousand such yuga-cakras make one day of Brahmā -- that is, 4.32 billion years. So one full day-and-night of Brahmā is 8.64 billion years. Caturmukha Brahmā's lifespan, or the lifespan of Satyaloka, is 100 (of his) years -- that is, 8.64 billion × 360 × 100 = 31,104,000,000,000 (thirty-one thousand, one hundred four thousand crore) years. This is Bhagavān's period of creation (day). After that comes the mahāpralaya, whose duration is likewise 31,104,000,000,000 years (night). This is the reckoning of Bhagavān's creation and dissolution.\n\nThere are three kinds of pralaya: (1) manvantara-pralaya; (2) dina-pralaya; (3) mahā-pralaya. [Besides these three, many smaller dissolutions also occur, in which some portion of the earth may be destroyed.] In manvantara-pralaya the earth is not wholly destroyed, but civilization is destroyed. Dina-pralaya is Caturmukha Brahmā's night -- occurring once every 4.32 billion years -- and in this pralaya the earth is wholly destroyed. Mahāpralaya occurs once every 31,104,000,000,000 years, and in this dissolution every world, from Satyaloka down, merges into Bhagavān in the form of the subtlest of subtle atoms.",
  "8.18": "avyaktād vyaktayaḥ sarvāḥ prabhavanty ahar āgame |\nrātryāgame pralīyante tatraivāvyaktasaṃjñake -- From the unmanifest, at the coming of day, all manifest things arise; at the coming of night, they dissolve back into that very unmanifest.\n\nAt the time of mahāpralaya, this world -- manifest within the unmanifest Bhagavān -- merges into him in the form of atoms. Again, at the time of creation, it takes on gross form once more from its subtle form and becomes manifest.",
  "8.19": "bhūtagrāmaḥ sa evāyaṃ bhūtvā bhūtvā pralīyate |\nrātryāgame'vaśaḥ pārtha prabhavaty ahar āgame -- O Pārtha, that same host of beings, coming into existence again and again, dissolves helplessly into Bhagavān at the coming of night, and comes into being again at the coming of day.\n\nThe primal substance of creation never changes -- those same five elements revolve, again and again, in the cycle of creation and dissolution. In this cycle circle endless jīvas unfit for mokṣa. But the jīva who has attained Bhagavān stands outside this cycle.",
  "8.20": "paras tasmāt tu bhāvo'nyo'vyakto'vyaktāt sanātanaḥ |\nyaḥ sa sarveṣu bhūteṣu naśyatsu na vinaśyati -- But beyond that unmanifest, there is another, eternal unmanifest state, distinct and higher -- which, when all beings perish, does not perish.\n\nBhagavān, standing beyond everything, is unique and supreme above all else. He is sanātana (eternal) -- unchanging at all times, the one denoted by every Veda, the sole principle who, even when the entire sentient-insentient world is destroyed, is not destroyed but instead absorbs everything into himself.",
  "8.21": "avyakto'kṣara ity uktas tam āhuḥ paramāṃ gatim |\nyaṃ prāpya na nivartante taddhāma paramaṃ mama -- This unmanifest principle is called the imperishable ('akṣara'); they say it is the final refuge. Those who attain it never return. That is my supreme abode.\n\nThis unmanifest principle is the supreme destination to which all must go and join. It is the principal meaning of the word \"akṣara\" -- never destroyed, pervading everywhere, granting whatever anyone desires, all-capable, the giver of sensory experience, denoted by every word -- Bhagavān. Having reached him, one need never again take birth in this worldly existence. \"That is my supreme form,\" says Kṛṣṇa.\n\npuruṣaḥ sa paraḥ pārtha bhaktyā labhyas tv ananyayā --",
  "8.22": "yasyāntaḥsthāni bhūtāni yena sarvam idaṃ tatam || -- O Pārtha, that supreme Puruṣa, within whom all beings dwell and by whom this entire universe is pervaded, is attainable only through unwavering devotion.\n\n\"Ananyayā bhaktyā\" -- unwavering devotion -- this alone is the path. \"Yasyāntaḥsthāni bhūtāni\" -- he within whom all beings dwell. \"Yena sarvam idaṃ tatam\" -- by whom all of this is pervaded. This is the supreme conclusion of the chapter on the yoga of meditation.",
  "8.23": "yatra kāle tv anāvṛttim āvṛttiṃ caiva yoginaḥ |\nprayātā yānti taṃ kālaṃ vakṣyāmi bharatarṣabha -- O best of the Bharatas, I shall now tell you of those time-deities by which yogīs who have departed either do not return, or do return.\n\n\"I shall tell you which deities govern the jīva that has left the body (fallen under the sway of time) and lead it onward; by which path one who goes is born again on earth, and by which one reaches mokṣa directly,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Here some misunderstand the word \"kāla.\" The \"kāla\" Kṛṣṇa speaks of in this verse does not mean time -- it means the group of deities who govern the jīva that has left the body.",
  "8.24": "agnir jyotir ahaḥ śuklaḥ ṣaṇmāsā uttarāyaṇam |\ntatra prayātā gacchanti brahma brahmavido janāḥ -- Fire and light [the two sons of Vahni], the deity of day [together with the deity of midday], the deity of the bright fortnight [with the deity of the full moon], and the deities of the six months of uttarāyaṇa [with the deity of the solstice] -- those knowers of Brahman who have gone by this path reach Brahman; they do not return.\n\nThe jīva that has left the body, fit for mokṣa, is first welcomed by Agni and Jyoti, the sons of Vaiśvānara (the presiding fire). These deities honor the jīva and send it onward. Next it is welcomed by the deities of light and of midday. With this true experience of light, the jīva proceeds further and reaches the deity of the bright fortnight and of the full moon. After that, the deity of the solstice (saṅkramaṇa) and of uttarāyaṇa, together with the deities of uttarāyaṇa's six months, send the jīva onward to Bhagavān. Thus, whoever travels by this path never returns -- they reach the eternally true Bhagavān.",
  "8.25": "dhūmo rātris tathā kṛṣṇaḥ ṣaṇmāsā dakṣiṇāyanam |\ntatra cāndramasaṃ jyotir yogī prāpya nivartate -- The deity of smoke, the deity of night, the deity of the dark fortnight, the deities of the six months of dakṣiṇāyana, and the deity of dakṣiṇāyana -- the sādhaka who travels by this path reaches the world of lunar light and returns.\n\nWhen a jīva unfit for mokṣa leaves the body, it is welcomed by the deities of smoke and darkness. Then comes the deity of the dark fortnight; then the deities of dakṣiṇāyana and its six months bring the jīva to the world of Soma. There the jīva enjoys pleasure until its store of merit is exhausted, then rejoins the clouds, reaches the earth through rain, enters a female womb through a male body, and is born again on earth.",
  "8.26": "śuklakṛṣṇe gatī hy ete jagataḥ śāśvate mate |\nekayā yāty anāvṛttim anyayāvartate punaḥ -- These are the bright and dark paths of the world, held by scripture to be eternal. By one, a person does not return; by the other, one returns again.\n\nThus there is one bright path of light leading to mokṣa, and another dark path of darkness leading back to worldly existence. Both these paths are an arrangement that has existed equally for all jīvas since beginningless, endless time. One who goes by the path of light does not return; one who goes by the path of darkness returns again.",
  "8.27": "naite sṛtī pārtha jānan yogī muhyati kaścana |\ntasmāt sarveṣu kāleṣu yogayukto bhavārjuna -- O Pārtha, no yogī who knows these two paths is ever deluded. Therefore, O Arjuna, be established in yoga at all times.\n\nHaving correct understanding of both these paths, and knowing which path you must take, you must proceed onward together with the respective presiding deities. These deities come at their respective stages and lead the jīva onward. For this reason, contemplation of the path and of the presiding deities is essential in one's final remembrance. Knowing this, and practicing it with proper understanding, no sādhaka need ever be confused. Therefore Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna: \"At all times, proceed knowing this method.\"",
  "8.28": "vedeṣu yajñeṣu tapaḥsu caiva\ndāneṣu yat puṇyaphalaṃ pradiṣṭam |\natyeti tat sarvam idaṃ viditvā\nyogī paraṃ sthānam upaiti cādyam -- Whatever merit is declared to come from studying the Vedas, performing sacrifices, practicing austerities, and giving gifts, the sādhaka who knows this transcends all of it and reaches that first and highest abode.\n\nBringing this teaching to its conclusion, Kṛṣṇa says: the highest knowledge of all is the awareness of this final hour. Whatever merit is declared throughout the scriptures through yajña, dāna, and tapas, the awareness of the secret of this final moment of death surpasses all of that merit. Therefore the greatest sādhana of all is reflection on the final hour -- for this reason, more contemplation is needed on death than on life.\n\nDeath is not inauspicious. That death is an experience of sorrow is our own delusion. While within the body, every torment of the physical body clings to the jīva. Death is simply release from this gross body -- so the jīva, dwelling in its subtle body, comes out of its cage like a bird flying freely in the sky. For this reason, casting aside the fear of death, the contemplation of the deities who auspiciously bear one upward at the final moment must remain constant in our life.\n\nThus ends the eighth chapter.",
  "9.1": "Bhagavān uvāca -- The Lord said:\n\nidaṃ tu te guhyatamaṃ pravakṣyāmy anasūyave |\njñānaṃ vijñānasahitaṃ yaj jñātvā mokṣyase'śubhāt -- This most secret of all things I shall now declare to you, since you are free of envy -- knowledge together with realization, knowing which you shall be released from all evil.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"I shall speak to you again of the truth of Bhagavān; I am unveiling before you a matter of the deepest secrecy.\" This is not a matter to be laid open before everyone. It is to be spoken only before those who have an unwavering yearning for the worship of Bhagavān, whose minds want nothing besides Bhagavān, and who long for him alone -- this is the most secret of confidences. \"That I am telling you,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Because Arjuna has both the earnest curiosity and the worthiness to know this matter, Kṛṣṇa speaks this secret teaching to him. This is knowledge to be given only to one who has both the worthiness and the desire to hear it -- it cannot be given to everyone.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa uses the word \"anasūyave.\" The quality one must have before studying Vedānta, and must retain even after studying it, is egolessness. If one studies Vedānta while still full of ego, it is of no use whatsoever. Before studying Vedānta, ego must not be present. If one knows \"I am nothing before Bhagavān,\" ego does not arise. But if, having studied Vedānta, the ego \"I am a great scholar\" arises, then in gaining that learning one has, in effect, lost God. Yet today we all remain caught in one form of ego or another -- the ego of knowledge, the ego of authority, the ego of wealth, the ego of beauty, the ego of youth -- so many such things trouble us. Only when we cast these off do we become worthy to understand Vedānta. As long as ego fills us, Vedānta is of no use to us.\n\nOf all forms of ego, the ego of knowledge is the most dangerous. Other forms of ego have an end, but the ego of knowledge has none. If the thought \"I am a scholar\" enters one's head, that person becomes truly impoverished! The offspring of ego is envy (asūyā). This envy is a fire that burns us; that is why in Kannada it is called \"hoṭṭekiccu\" (stomach-fire). It destroys our very character. To such a person, teaching Vedānta is useless. \"You have no envy -- that is why I am telling you this secret,\" says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nFor one standing on a battlefield, to be free of envy is an exceedingly great thing. Hatred, envy, and ego are forever surging on a battlefield. One who has conquered these and stands above them is truly the finest. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"Knowing this, your mind will be able to become pure and seek Bhagavān.\" When knowledge comes, the impurity within us is washed away and the mind becomes clean. Our greatest wealth is a cheerful, clean mind. Bhagavān reveals himself in such a mind. A mind free of ego and envy is called \"Vasudeva,\" and the Bhagavān who is seen by such a mind is \"Vāsudeva.\" From a pure mind comes knowledge, from knowledge comes bliss, and from this blissful state comes release from the five-element body and from worldly existence -- mokṣa.\n\nWe have three sheaths made of the five elements. Within these three sheaths lies the ātman, whose nature is knowledge and bliss. The subtle causal body (liṅga-śarīra), the subtle body (sūkṣma-śarīra), and the gross body (sthūla-śarīra) are the coverings that envelop the ātman. Casting off all three of these garments, being released from all inauspiciousness, and standing before Bhagavān in one's true, essential state -- this is what Kṛṣṇa here briefly calls \"mokṣyase'śubhāt.\" [When we remove our upper garment before the deity in a temple, its deeper meaning is this: \"casting off every covering placed before Bhagavān, becoming bare before God.\" We must remove every mask we have placed before Bhagavān and lay ourselves open to him -- then he, in turn, opens himself to us.] Let us now see, in the verses that follow, how Bhagavān explains to Arjuna, as a whole, this knowledge and realization of release from the five-element body and attainment of mokṣa.",
  "9.2": "rājavidyā rājaguhyaṃ pavitram idam uttamam |\npratyakṣāvagamaṃ dharmyaṃ susukhaṃ kartum avyayam -- This is the king of knowledge, the king of secrets, the highest of the pure; it grants direct realization, it is righteous, most easy to practice, and imperishable.\n\nSpeaking of the spiritual science he is about to teach, Kṛṣṇa says: \"This is the foremost among all forms of knowledge; it is the most secret and the most sacred of teachings.\" It is the knowledge that makes Bhagavān -- denoted by the word \"pratyakṣa\" (the regulator of the senses) -- directly visible; the knowledge that makes the sustaining power of the universe, Bhagavān, a direct object of realization; knowledge whose subject is Bhagavān himself, seen face to face. Knowing this teaching, winning Bhagavān's favor becomes very easy, because what is most dear to Bhagavān is knowledge. No other path pleases Bhagavān as easily as knowledge does. \"This is a path that never perishes,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Therefore the path of knowledge is never in vain.",
  "9.3": "aśraddadhānāḥ puruṣā dharmasyāsya parantapa |\naprāpya māṃ nivartante mṛtyusaṃsāravartmani -- O Parantapa, those without faith in this teaching, failing to attain me, return again into the path of worldly existence that is death's own snare.\n\nHaving earlier spoken of envy, Kṛṣṇa now speaks of another exceedingly important quality -- śraddhā, faith. To one who has no faith, nothing that is told is of any use. Whatever we have no faith in, our mind simply refuses to accept. To grasp any matter, we must first have faith in that matter. \"There may well be things I do not know, because there are a great many things of which I am unaware\" -- to realize this is the first step of faith. (I must know that I do not know.) We must be aware of our own ignorance. Only then does the faith arise to come to know what we do not yet know. First we must know that we do not know; then we must be aware of the possibility that what we do not know may itself be true; and then we must have the faith to come to know it. Coming to know in this way, the truth that there exists something we do not know becomes clear to us.\n\nA seeker walking the path of knowledge must, at the most fundamental level, have faith. Nothing said to one who does not believe is of any use. This is exactly what Kṛṣṇa says here: \"For one without faith, there is no truth.\" Those who have no faith in Bhagavān, the sustainer of the world, do not reach Bhagavān. \"Such people fall into a darkness in which the self is destroyed, or else remain caught, revolving in the whirlpool of worldly existence,\" says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nThe epithet \"Parantapa\" is used in this verse. Its surface meaning is \"the hero who scorches his enemies.\" In the battle of our life, our enemies are the ignorance, delusion, desire, and anger that dwell within us. Every seeker, placing faith in the Supreme, must become a fire that burns such enemies -- must become a Parantapa who blazes by consuming them.",
  "9.4": "mayā tatam idaṃ sarvaṃ jagad avyaktamūrtinā |\nmatsthāni sarvabhūtāni na cāhaṃ teṣv avasthitaḥ -- By me, in my unmanifest form, this entire universe is pervaded. All beings dwell in me, yet I do not dwell within them.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"I am the unmanifest form that fills the entire universe.\" In the phrase \"avyaktamūrti\" used here, \"avyakta\" and \"mūrti\" are mutually opposite terms. Avyakta means that which is unseen, that which has no shape. Mūrti means form and shape. Bhagavān is unmanifest, pervading everywhere like space, yet he is also the form, full of knowledge and bliss, filling everyone within. He fills each and every object in a distinct form, possessing infinite forms. Kṛṣṇa says: \"I am the support of every single object; this entire universe depends upon me. Yet I remain without any support, pervading everywhere.\"",
  "9.5": "na ca matsthāni bhūtāni paśya me yogam aiśvaram |\nbhūtabhṛn na ca bhūtastho mamātmā bhūtabhāvanaḥ -- Yet beings do not dwell in me -- behold my divine, sovereign power! I sustain all beings, yet I do not dwell in beings; my very self brings all beings into existence.\n\nWe must attend carefully to what Kṛṣṇa says in this verse and the previous one. On the surface these appear to be contradictory, riddle-like statements. What Kṛṣṇa says is this: \"Know my capacity -- I am present in everything, yet present in nothing. Everything is in me, yet nothing is in me.\" This is the contemplation of Bhagavān's inconceivable power. As the Bhāgavata says: \"What we in this world call 'contradictory' becomes consistent, within the realm of auspicious dharma, when applied to Bhagavān.\" By stating this opposing pair of qualities, what Bhagavān is telling us here is -- everything is in me, yet no attachment (mamakāra) is in me. That is, this indicates Bhagavān's utter non-attachment. He clings to nothing. He depends on nothing, and no trace of anything adheres to him. Kṛṣṇa says: \"This is my divine power\" -- this is Bhagavān's all-capability.\n\nKṛṣṇa explains this once more, crisply, as \"bhūtabhṛn na ca bhūtasthaḥ.\" Here the word \"na ca\" is used between the two phrases like a lamp placed at a threshold. Its meaning is this: \"I am bhūtabhṛt -- I bear all created beings -- and na-ca-bhūtabhṛt -- I bear nothing; I am bhūtasthaḥ -- all beings are in me -- and I am na-ca-bhūtasthaḥ -- I am in nothing.\"\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"mama ātmā bhūtabhāvanaḥ.\" That is, the creation, sustenance, dissolution, governance, knowledge, ignorance, bondage, and liberation of this world -- all of it comes from Bhagavān. He is of the nature of knowledge and bliss. Bhagavān is not one thing and his body another; his qualities and his actions are not separate from him. All of this is undivided. We cannot conceive of this within the bounds of the five elements and the three guṇas.\n\nIn the next verse Kṛṣṇa explains, through an illustration, how Bhagavān fills everywhere.",
  "9.6": "yathākāśasthito nityaṃ vāyuḥ sarvatrago mahān |\ntathā sarvāṇi bhūtāni matsthānīty upadhāraya -- Just as the mighty wind, moving everywhere, forever remains within space, so know that all beings likewise dwell within me.\n\nThe wind is everywhere -- it is within us and outside us alike. The wind is not visible to us, yet it fills this space. In the same way, Bhagavān fills us both within and without. Yet no trace of anything adheres to him. Every single existing thing in this world exists within Bhagavān.",
  "9.7": "sarvabhūtāni kaunteya prakṛtiṃ yānti māmikām |\nkalpakṣaye punas tāni kalpādau visṛjāmy aham -- O Kaunteya, at the end of creation all beings return into my own primal nature; and at the beginning of creation, I send them forth again.\n\n31,104,000,000,000 years after creation begins comes the mahāpralaya -- this is a hundred years of Caturmukha Brahmā, called kalpakṣaya. In this mahāpralaya, everything from Satyaloka downward loses its gross form and, in subtle form, merges into the primal nature (prakṛti) that is subject to Bhagavān. Every class of living being likewise loses its gross form and merges into Bhagavān's own being. Then follows another 31,104,000,000,000 years of dissolution. After that, creation is formed anew -- that is, everything once more takes on gross form from its subtle form.",
  "9.8": "prakṛtiṃ svām avaṣṭabhya visṛjāmi punaḥ punaḥ |\nbhūtagrāmam imaṃ kṛtsnam avaśaṃ prakṛter vaśāt -- Resting upon my own primal nature, I send forth again and again this entire host of beings, helpless and subject to the sway of that nature.\n\nAt the time of dissolution, Bhagavān takes both the inert and the living beings that have merged into him in subtle form, and creates them anew using his own subordinate primal nature (prakṛti). This cycle of creation and dissolution by Bhagavān is continuous. Countless such universes have been created and destroyed in the past, and this will continue through endless time to come. It is for this reason that Bhagavān is called the lord of infinite crores of universes. (We have already discussed this in detail in the seventh chapter.)\n\nDoes karma, then, not adhere to Bhagavān, who thus creates and destroys without end? Let us see this in the verses that follow.",
  "9.9": "na ca māṃ tāni karmāṇi nibadhnanti dhanañjaya |\nudāsīnavad āsīnam asaktaṃ teṣu karmasu -- O Dhanañjaya, those actions do not bind me, for I remain seated as one indifferent, unattached to those very actions.\n\nBhagavān is unattached; he does not act for his own sake. No action can ever bind the independent Bhagavān. (If such action could bind him, would he still be called independent?) \"I do nothing for myself, and karma does not adhere to me\" -- this Kṛṣṇa states plainly.\n\nThe epithet \"Dhanañjaya\" is used in this verse. Taken in its ordinary sense, this name means: \"one who defeats wicked kings who plunder the wealth of the people and rule unjustly, wins that wealth, and puts it to good use for his subjects -- he is Dhanañjaya.\" [One who attacks another for his own selfish gain in order to acquire wealth is called a \"robber,\" not Dhanañjaya. In the Mahābhārata, Ekalavya belonged to this second class -- and for this reason his thumb was severed, and Kṛṣṇa himself later brought about his destruction.] Spiritually, true wealth is Bhagavān himself. One who has gained the knowledge of such a Bhagavān is Dhanañjaya. Only when each of us, like Arjuna, gains the knowledge of Bhagavān and becomes a Dhanañjaya can we attain mokṣa.",
  "9.10": "mayādhyakṣeṇa prakṛtiḥ sūyate sacarācaram |\nhetunānena kaunteya jagad viparivartate -- Under my supervision, primal nature gives birth to all that moves and does not move. By this cause alone, O Kaunteya, the universe revolves and changes.\n\nThe root substance needed to create the universe is inert primal nature (jaḍa-prakṛti), and the one who gives it shape is Lakṣmī, the sentient primal nature (cetana-prakṛti), the presiding consort of inert nature. Bhagavān, standing as the overseer of all creation, as the power behind every action, brings about this creation through both inert and sentient prakṛti. Bhagavān fills every single object in this world as a distinct power within it.",
  "9.11": "avajānanti māṃ mūḍhā mānuṣīṃ tanum āśritam |\nparaṃ bhāvam ajānanto mama bhūtamaheśvaram -- Fools disregard me when I have taken on a human form, not knowing my higher nature as the great Lord of all beings.\n\nIn this verse Bhagavān states a matter about his own nature that no one else could ever state. Bhagavān has no body made of the five elements. Bhagavān, appearing in an avatāra form, merely appears to us as though he possesses a body like our own. Those who see that visible body and conclude that Bhagavān's body is perishable like ours, a body that will one day fall away -- such people, says Kṛṣṇa, are by nature tāmasic, or fools under the sway of tamas, who fail to recognize Bhagavān's true greatness.\n\nHere the epithet \"bhūtamaheśvara\" is used. This is a special word describing Bhagavān's greatness. Taken as a whole, this word means: \"bhūtas\" refers to all living beings; their overseers (\"īśas\") are Brahmā and the other deities (\"bhūteśas\"); and she who is greatest among these deities is Śrī Lakṣmī (\"bhūtamaheśā\"). Bhagavān, the consort of such a Lakṣmī, is \"Bhūtamaheśvara.\" Breaking the same word apart differently, we get bhūta-mahān-īśvara -- that is, the all-capable, supreme Bhagavān who is greater than and governs all beings, from Brahmā downward. In yet another reading: bhūta-mahat-īśvara. Here \"bhūta\" means that which stands at the very pinnacle of greatness, beginningless and eternal, free from birth and death. \"Mahat\" means the principle that is complete in time, place, and quality. \"Īśvara\" means the all-capable Bhagavān. Thus this epithet proclaims Bhagavān's qualities in many forms at once.",
  "9.12": "moghāśā moghakarmāṇo moghajñānā vicetasaḥ |\nrākṣasīm āsurīṃ caiva prakṛtiṃ mohinīṃ śritāḥ -- Their hopes are in vain, their actions are fruitless, their knowledge comes to nothing, their minds are bewildered -- they cling to a deluding nature that is demonic and fiendish.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa stresses the point he made in the previous verse -- that Bhagavān is of the nature of knowledge and bliss and has no body made of the five elements. \"The very thought that God has a body made of the five elements, like ours, is itself the path of sin,\" says Kṛṣṇa. This is because those who think in this way are never sāttvika; they can never be true devotees of Bhagavān. Every hope of spiritual attainment withers away for those who think in this manner. The deities never love those who declare Bhagavān to be perishable. Whatever sacrifices and rituals such people perform are in vain. In short, in the pursuit of spiritual life, not one of their hopes is fulfilled in the world beyond.\n\nThose without sāttvika reflection, or those under the sway of tamas, think of Bhagavān in this flawed way. They lack the capacity for true reflection. No scripture they read is of any use to them; their minds cannot grasp spiritual truth. \"Such people are demons (rākṣasas) or fiends (asuras),\" says Kṛṣṇa. Rākṣasas are those who are tāmasic by nature; asuras are the rājasic, those who see the purpose of life only in sensory enjoyment. Even those who, through such influence, have lost the capacity for reflection think in this same way. True, natural reflection never comes to them; they are forever thinking in ways opposed to what is right.",
  "9.13": "mahātmānas tu māṃ pārtha daivīṃ prakṛtim āśritāḥ |\nbhajanty ananyamanaso jñātvā bhūtādim avyayam -- But, O Pārtha, great souls of a divine disposition worship me single-mindedly, knowing me as the imperishable origin of all beings.\n\nHaving described in the previous verse those of demonic and fiendish nature, Kṛṣṇa here describes those of divine nature (daivī prakṛti). Those of divine nature always walk the path of knowledge, light, and truth. They have the good fortune to come to know Bhagavān. Bhagavān calls them \"mahātmās\" -- great souls. Bhagavān has boundless love for them. Kṛṣṇa says: \"They worship me with an undivided mind.\" True worship is our loving God, and God loving us in turn. Those of divine nature know the truth that everything besides Bhagavān is perishable, and that Bhagavān alone is the eternal truth. In this world, only Bhagavān and the knowledge of him are imperishable; everything else -- wealth, property, gold, power, and so on -- is subject to decay. The one wealth that never perishes is Bhagavān and the knowledge of him. Those who reflect on Bhagavān in this way are the mahātmās.\n\nThe epithet used in this verse is \"Pārtha.\" On the surface, Kṛṣṇa is simply addressing Arjuna as \"son of Pṛthā.\" But etymologically, this epithet carries a different, spiritual meaning. It comes from the root \"prath,\" meaning \"to spread.\" That which has spread, that which fills everywhere, is \"pratha.\" This indicates Bhagavān's all-pervasiveness. Those who, knowing the truth that Bhagavān is all-pervading, see him everywhere are \"Pārthas.\" In another sense, those who continually drink in the meaning of the Vedas -- Bhagavān, who is the very object denoted by the Vedas, and his qualities -- are \"Pārthas.\" All of us should become Pārthas by drinking in the meaning of the Vedas in this way; the message behind this epithet is that we must recognize, within ourselves too, that all-pervading power that has spread everywhere.\n\nIn what various ways do those of divine nature worship Bhagavān? Kṛṣṇa explains this in the following verse.",
  "9.14": "satataṃ kīrtayanto māṃ yatantaś ca dṛḍhavratāḥ |\nnamasyantaś ca māṃ bhaktyā nityayuktā upāsate -- Ever glorifying me, striving with firm resolve, bowing to me with devotion, ever united with me, they worship me.\n\n\"Those of divine nature glorify me continually,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Bhagavān's presence is deeply felt in their every activity. In another sense, those who are of divine nature are those who, holding a stringed instrument and plucking its strings, sing continually in praise of Bhagavān's qualities (as the sage Nārada does) and remain ever joyful. They worship continually, with firm resolve, until they reach their goal, with unwavering love. Kṛṣṇa goes on, in the following verse, to describe another kind of devotee among them.",
  "9.15": "jñānayajñena cāpy anye yajanto mām upāsate |\nekatvena pṛthaktvena bahudhā viśvatomukham -- Others, too, worship me through the sacrifice of knowledge, adoring me who face every direction -- as one, as distinct, or in manifold forms.\n\nSome others remain immersed in the world of Bhagavān's inner knowledge. In meditation they have no awareness whatsoever of the outer world; their worship consists precisely in seeing Bhagavān, in meditation, in his many dimensions. Their worship is a meditative sacrifice (dhyāna-pūrvaka yajña). It is very difficult to recognize such people outwardly as mahātmās. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Such people see me as one, as distinct, and as many.\" Here \"ekatvena\" means worshipping with the understanding that Bhagavān is one -- not that there is a separate god for each country, each caste, each faith, but that God is one for all; this is ekatvena. \"Pṛthaktvena\" means seeing the Bhagavān who is unlike anything else in his many distinct forms; such people see Bhagavān in meditation in his many colors. Thus they worship the all-facing Bhagavān as one, as the four-fold form (caturmūrti), as manifold forms, and in manifold hues.\n\nHaving given this understanding of how Bhagavān should and should not be contemplated, Kṛṣṇa now goes on to explain how Bhagavān's glory (vibhūti) should be contemplated in various distinct symbols. In the rest of this chapter, Kṛṣṇa explains the contemplation of Bhagavān who fills every atom of the universe, and the particular manner of worshipping him through each. Come, let us hear this rare and special knowledge from Bhagavān himself, and be fulfilled by it.",
  "9.16": "ahaṃ kratur ahaṃ yajñaḥ svadhāham aham auṣadham |\nmantro'ham aham evājyam aham agnir ahaṃ hutam -- I am the ritual, I am the sacrifice, I am the offering to ancestors, I am the healing herb; I am the sacred formula, I am the clarified butter, I am the fire, I am the oblation offered.\n\nI am the sacrificial rite [since my action is my own nature, I am \"kratu\"]. Through me is the act of oblation performed [\"yajña,\" since I know all]. Through me is the food of the ancestors given [I am \"svadhā,\" since I am my own support]. Through me comes the food of human beings [\"auṣadha,\" since I am the support of the afflicted]. The sacred formula (mantra) is mine [it is \"mantra\" because it protects through understanding]. The clarified butter (ghee) is mine [it is \"ājya\" because I am greater than all]. The fire is mine [it is \"agni,\" since I alone give motion to a motionless universe]. The oblation offered in the fire is mine [it is \"huta,\" because it is I who am called upon by devotees].\n\nThe teachers of scripture describe three symbols for the worship of Bhagavān -- found throughout the Veda -- namely the fire-symbol, the wind-symbol, and the sun-symbol. On the earthly plane, Bhagavān is worshipped through the fire-symbol; in the intermediate region, through the sun; and inwardly, through the symbol of the wind. On this earth, human beings are generally more attached to the path of action than the path of knowledge, and in such people action is emphasized over knowledge. Some perform worship from morning till evening, yet have no real knowledge of what they are performing -- for them, the acquisition of knowledge is secondary. This is a human weakness: we do not place as much emphasis on knowledge as we do on ritual purity! Rather than wasting time on outward observances performed without understanding, gaining even a little knowledge through study and discourse is the greater worship, pleasing to God.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa explains what worship of Bhagavān should look like for those whose emphasis is on action. Kṛṣṇa says: \"ahaṃ kratur ahaṃ yajñaḥ.\" The process of fire-worship performed for the worship of Bhagavān is \"kratu.\" When making an offering to Bhagavān through yajña, we speak these words as we offer the oblation: \"Agnaye svāhā -- agnaye idaṃ na mama\" (to Agni, svāhā -- this is not mine, it is Agni's); \"Prajāpataye svāhā -- prajāpataye idaṃ na mama\"; \"Indrāya svāhā -- indrāya idaṃ na mama\"; \"Sūryāya svāhā\"; \"Vāyave svāhā\"; \"Varuṇāya svāhā\" -- and so on. Offering oblations to Bhagavān through these various symbols is yajña. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"I am the regulator of the ritual you perform.\" Whatever symbol we worship through, it is received by the Bhagavān who indwells that deity -- for example, in the gaṇahoma, caṇḍikāhoma, navagraha-homa, and so on. Whatever deity's symbol we perform the sacrifice through, that sacrifice is received by Bhagavān -- this understanding must be held firmly in mind. Though Bhagavān manifests in many forms of power, the one who regulates all is Bhagavān alone. [Astrologers, to remove some calamity befalling us, may instruct us to worship a particular symbol of Bhagavān's power -- not because Bhagavān is many.] Only when the Bhagavān within a deity is pleased is that offering received by both the deity and Bhagavān together. We should not, therefore, perform an action merely to please one deity on one day and another on another; our worship must be reserved for the all-indwelling Bhagavān.\n\nWithin our body dwell the tattvābhimāni deities. All deities occupy the position of a guru; they are Bhagavān's own family, and Bhagavān stands governing them. Worshipping the indwelling Bhagavān of these tattvābhimāni deities, so that by his grace they may guide us on the righteous path, is yajña.\n\nIf the worship we perform daily at our household shrine is yajña directed toward the tattvābhimāni deities, then śrāddha is the yajña directed toward the pitṛ-devatās (ancestral deities). Many of us hold a mistaken idea -- that śrāddha is performed \"for the departed soul.\" This is not correct. Śrāddha is performed for the \"pitṛ-devatās\" who govern the jīva that has left the gross body and now dwells in the subtle body. There are, in particular, ten Viśvedevas (Kratu, Dakṣa, Vasu, Satya, Kāma, Kāla, Dhuri, Locana, Purūravas, Ārdrava); of these, Purūravas and Ārdrava hold the office that governs the ancestral company. The group of pitṛ-devatās numbers ten (foremost among the hundred pitṛs): Yama, Soma, and Kavyavāha (the three lords of the pitṛs), the Somasadas, Agniṣvāttas, Barhiṣadas, Somapas, Havirbhujas, Ājyapas, and Sukālins. We do not offer the piṇḍa to satisfy the hunger of the departed jīva. Our elders go to heaven or hell purely by the fruit of their own past actions, not because of the śrāddha we perform. The purpose of performing śrāddha is this: the jīva under the sway of the pitṛ-devatās may, owing to the influence of some misdeed, encounter obstacles along its path; and at that time, the subtle body it inhabits has no ritual means of removing them. For this reason, śrāddha is the worship of the pitṛ-devatās, seeking their help to free the jīva from such obstacles and let it proceed onward. In sum, śrāddha is the remembrance of the day our elders passed, an expression of gratitude to them, and worship of the pitṛ-devatās.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"svadhāham.\" Mantras function like addresses -- \"svāhā\" is addressed to the tattvābhimāni deities, \"svadhā\" to the pitṛ-devatās. Kṛṣṇa says that śrāddha reaches the pitṛ-devatās through him.\n\nAfter the tattvābhimāni deities and the pitṛ-devatās come human beings. The chief food of human beings is \"auṣadhi\" -- in Sanskrit, a plant that dies after bearing fruit is called an auṣadhi; its very fruiting is its death. All the grains that human beings eat are auṣadhis, and the collective community of such auṣadhis is \"auṣadha.\" Kṛṣṇa says: \"ahaṃ auṣadham.\" The grain we eat, too, is offered to Bhagavān, who sits within us as Vaiśvānara. It is Bhagavān who gives medicine, who through it removes disease, and who removes hunger and protects.\n\nWe offer oblations with various mantras. As said before, a mantra is like an address. If every oblation offered ultimately reaches Bhagavān, why the different addresses? Kṛṣṇa said: \"to me, in these various addresses!\" Bhagavān is one, but his forms are many. Whatever mantra we send an offering through, it reaches the Bhagavān who is denoted by every mantra, by every word. Thus, though we worship different forms of power through different divine symbols, every single name ultimately speaks of Bhagavān alone. Bhagavān is the essence of everything. A fine illustration of this is the coconut: the coconut with its husk is like a burning fire; the nut within the husk is like the indwelling deity of fire, Agni; and the kernel within the shell is like the Bhagavān who indwells Agni. We call the whole thing simply \"coconut,\" or simply \"fire.\" Just as the true essence within the coconut is the kernel, the true essence within fire is, in the end, the indwelling Bhagavān. This is what Kṛṣṇa means here by \"ahaṃ agniḥ.\"\n\nTaking one especially important item of offering as an example, Kṛṣṇa says: \"aham eva ājyam.\" Ājya is ghee. Ghee holds a special power to satisfy the deities. \"I am present as that special power within the ghee,\" says Kṛṣṇa, and adds, \"I myself am the oblation of the sacrifice (ahaṃ hutam).\"\n\nHavis is the sacrificial substance offered to Bhagavān. The special power within that havis comes from Bhagavān himself. The havis we offer is never in vain. The havis offered into the fire, through the seven colors emanating from the fire, joins the sun's rays in the atmosphere and yields its fruit for society. Bhagavān, who receives the offered portion, is Havirhariḥ. \"Sarva-deva-namaskāraḥ Keśavaṃ prati gacchati\" -- Bhagavān is denoted by every word, so whatever word is uttered ultimately reaches him alone. Thus Bhagavān, who receives the havis and removes the sins of his devotees, is Havirhariḥ.\n\nViewed from another dimension, we can find in each and every word of this verse a distinct contemplation of Bhagavān's own qualities:\n\nKratuḥ: Bhagavān himself is the principal meaning of the word \"kratu.\" \"Kratu\" conveys the meanings of action, knowledge, and so on. Of the nature of action, of the nature of knowledge, Bhagavān is kratuḥ -- he who gives us faith, will, the power of action, and every facility, stands behind us conducting our activities, is of the nature of knowledge, and frees us from the bondage of karma.\n\nYajñaḥ: means the all-knowing one. Bhagavān, of the nature of knowledge, present everywhere, is the indwelling spirit of the yajña that all must worship through every action.\n\nSvadhāḥ: means the all-regulating one who rests upon himself alone.\n\nAuṣadhaḥ: Bhagavān is \"auṣadhaḥ\" -- the support and giver of mokṣa to those tormented, floundering in the suffering of worldly existence.\n\nMantraḥ: Bhagavān, who protects those who contemplate him with understanding, is \"mantraḥ.\" [A mantra protects us only when we recite it with understanding; otherwise it may yield only a slight result, never its true, great fruit.]\n\nĀjyaḥ: Bhagavān is the greatest of all things, hence he is \"ājyaḥ.\"\n\nAgniḥ: Bhagavān, who gives motion to that which is itself motionless, is \"agniḥ.\"\n\nHutaḥ: Bhagavān, who is called upon (offered to) by all devotees, is \"hutaḥ.\"",
  "9.17": "pitāham asya jagato mātā dhātā pitāmahaḥ |\nvedyaṃ pavitram oṃkāra ṛk sāma yajur eva ca -- I am the father of this universe, its mother, its sustainer, its grandfather; I am that which is to be known, the sacred syllable Oṃ, and the Ṛk, Sāma, and Yajus themselves.\n\nI am the father, mother, sustainer, and grandfather of this world [I am \"pitṛ\" since I nourish; \"mātṛ\" since I bring into being (know/give birth to); \"dhātṛ\" since I cause growth; \"pitāmaha\" since I am greater than the father]. I am that which the whole world must come to know; I am that which sanctifies the world; I am denoted by Oṃkāra and by the Ṛgveda, Sāmaveda, and Yajurveda [I am \"Oṃkāra\" because I am called by \"Oṃ\"; I am \"ṛk\" since I am worthy of worship; \"sāma\" since I am equal to all; \"yajus\" since I am worshipped through sacrifices].\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa explains the worship of Bhagavān in relation to the bonds of life. Kṛṣṇa says: \"pitāham asya jagato mātā dhātā pitāmahaḥ\" -- that is, \"I alone am the father, I alone am the mother, I alone am the sustainer and the grandfather.\" As we know it, one person may become a father, or a mother, but one and the same person cannot be both father and mother. This is possible only in Bhagavān. Bhagavān is father to all and mother to all -- the power that fills as \"father\" within every father and as \"mother\" within every mother. We praise this in these words:\n\ntvam eva mātā ca pitā tvam eva, tvam eva bandhuś ca sakhā tvam eva |\ntvam eva vidyā draviṇaṃ tvam eva, tvam eva sarvaṃ mama deva deva ||\n\nThis same understanding is present at the root of the Gāyatrī. In \"tat savitur vareṇyaṃ bhargaḥ,\" Bhagavān is spoken of as the father who created the universe. \"Savitṛ\" means the one who gave birth to the world. At the time of dissolution, Bhagavān bore the world within his own womb, and at the time of creation, he gave birth to it. She who gives birth and nurtures is \"mother\" -- so Bhagavān is mother too. Not only that: dwelling within every mother, awakening her motherhood, filling her as \"mother\" within, and dwelling within every father, awakening fatherhood within him, Bhagavān is the father. If we examine the derivation of the word \"pitā,\" \"father\" is merely its conventional meaning. The śruti says:\n\nyo naḥ pitā janitā yo vidhātā dhāmāni veda bhuvanāni viśvā |\nyo devānāṃ nāmadhā eka eva taṃ sampraśnaṃ bhuvanā yanty anyā ||\n\nHere \"pitā\" and \"janaka\" are spoken of separately: he is our pitā, he is our janaka -- that is, he alone is the one who begets, and he alone is the protector. In this world, man is not woman, and woman is not man -- but Bhagavān is the reflected image (bimba) of every being. Hence Bhagavān is of both genders -- he is strī-puṃsaka, neither male nor female in the ordinary sense, yet he has none of the flaws present in a woman or in a man. Therefore he is also beyond gender. [This can be seen in the many names of Bhagavān.]\n\nBhagavān alone is \"dhātā\" -- from beginningless, endless time, he sustains and nourishes this world. Dwelling within Caturmukha Brahmā, he is the one denoted by the word \"pitāmaha\" (grandfather).\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa has spoken of only the principal relationships, meaning that it is the indwelling Bhagavān who awakens this feeling within every relationship. Behind every relationship stands a connection to Bhagavān -- though we are unaware of this. Relationships arise according to each jīva's own accumulated karma (prārabdha); the love, affection, and enmity found within them are all Bhagavān's own doing. When we know this, our life becomes smooth. There is no need to feel diminished when someone hates us, nor to feel elated when someone loves us -- everything is Bhagavān's play (līlā), unfolding according to our own nature and our prārabdha karma; this awareness is of the greatest importance. A fine illustration of this appears in the Bhāgavata, in the story of the battle between Vṛtra and Indra: because Tvaṣṭā, the father of Vṛtra (who had set out to kill Indra), mispronounced a single note in his mantra, it was Indra, instead, who came to kill Vṛtra. In that battle, Indra hesitated to kill the greatly learned Vṛtra. Then Vṛtra said to him: \"Do not hesitate, Indra -- kill me. For in this play, Bhagavān has given you the role of the slayer, and me the role of the slain. We must simply perform it.\" With this awareness, we need not be troubled when another reviles or hates us. When we know that everything is Bhagavān's arrangement, everything appears as one and the same; hatred and love naturally fall away. There is neither diminishment when a bond breaks nor elation when it is formed -- this is a very exalted state. In sum, what Kṛṣṇa says here is: behind every relationship stands Bhagavān. He fills every being, present within all as \"everyone.\" In one form or another, within each person, he causes the corresponding action to be performed. This is the science we must come to contemplate.\n\nHaving explained action and relationships, Kṛṣṇa now explains knowledge. Kṛṣṇa says: \"vedyaṃ pavitram oṃkāra ṛk sāma yajur eva ca.\" \"Oṃ\" is the root of every name in this world of named things. Kṛṣṇa says: \"I am Oṃkāra.\" Every sound in the world is born from Oṃkāra; hence Bhagavān is denoted by every word. Every word in Sanskrit, too, has arisen from Oṃkāra. Let us briefly see how.\n\nOṃkāra, the root syllable of the Vedic corpus, has eight components: akāra, ukāra, makāra, nāda, bindu, kalā, śānta, atiśānta. From this arises the eight-syllabled name of Nārāyaṇa: \"Oṃ namo nārāyaṇāya.\" From Oṃkāra's eight root sounds arose the eight classes of the alphabet:\n\n1) Vowels: a, ā, i, ī, u, ū, ṛ, ṝ, ḷ, ḹ, e, ai, o, au, aṃ, aḥ\n2) Ka-group: ka, kha, ga, gha, ṅa\n3) Ca-group: ca, cha, ja, jha, ña\n4) Ṭa-group: ṭa, ṭha, ḍa, ḍha, ṇa\n5) Ta-group: ta, tha, da, dha, na\n6) Pa-group: pa, pha, ba, bha, ma\n7) Semivowels: ya, ra, la, va\n8) Final six: śa, ṣa, sa, ha, ḷa, kṣa\n\nIn all, fifty-one letters from \"a\" to \"kṣa\" (hence the word \"akṣara\" -- from a to kṣa). Every word in the entire world is a combination of these letters. Since every word is born from Oṃkāra, and Bhagavān is denoted by Oṃkāra, Bhagavān is denoted by every word. Hence the fundamental, sacred meaning of every word ultimately speaks of Bhagavān -- even words denoting a flaw, when applied to Bhagavān, carry only a sacred meaning. For example, \"duḥkhī\" (sorrowful), when applied to Bhagavān, means \"master over sorrow.\"\n\nCorresponding to these fifty-one letters (mātṛkās) that denote Bhagavān are fifty-one forms of Bhagavān: (1) Aja, (2) Ānanda, (3) Indra, (4) Īśa, (5) Ugra, (6) Ūrja, (7) Ṛtambhara, (8) Ṝgha, (9) Ḷśa, (10) Ḹji, (11) Ekātma, (12) Aira, (13) Ojobhṛt, (14) Aurasa, (15) Anta, (16) Ardhagarbha, (17) Kapila, (18) Khapati, (19) Garuḍāsana, (20) Gharma, (21) Ṅasāra, (22) Cārvāṅga, (23) Chandogamya, (24) Janārdana, (25) Jhūṭitāri, (26) Ñama, (27) Ṭaṅki, (28) Ṭhakala, (29) Ḍaraka, (30) Ḍhari, (31) Ṇātma, (32) Tāra, (33) Thabha, (34) Daṇḍi, (35) Dhanvi, (36) Namya, (37) Para, (38) Phali, (39) Bali, (40) Bhaga, (41) Manu, (42) Yajña, (43) Rāma, (44) Lakṣmīpati, (45) Vara, (46) Śāntasaṃvit, (47) Ṣaḍguṇa, (48) Sārātma, (49) Haṃsa, (50) Ḷāḷuka, (51) Nṛsiṃha [Nṛharyakṣa].\n\nThe three root syllables of Oṃkāra -- a, u, m -- correspond to poetry, prose, and song, and speak of the three forms of the Veda and of the Bhagavān they proclaim. \"A\" belongs to the Ṛgveda, \"u\" to the Yajurveda, and \"m\" to the Sāmaveda. The Ṛgveda begins with the letter \"a\" -- \"agnim īḷe purohitaṃ yajñasya devam ṛtvijam, hotāraṃ ratnadhātamam...\" -- and ends with the letter \"i\": \"...samānam astu vo mano yathā vaḥ susahāsati.\" From there the Yajurveda continues, beginning with \"i\" -- \"iṣe tvorje tvā...\" -- and ending with \"u\": \"...samudro bandhuḥ.\" Thus \"a\" and \"u\" within Oṃkāra form an abbreviation fully denoting the Ṛgveda and Yajurveda. Next comes the Sāmaveda, which begins with \"a\" -- \"agna ā yāhi...\" -- and ends with \"u\": \"svasti no bṛhaspatir dadhātu.\" Here the syllable \"m\" does not appear directly, but as we know, the Sāmaveda exists in the form of musical sound (nāda), and within Oṃkāra too, \"m\" is the syllable that emerges in the form of sound -- that is, music. Thus Oṃkāra is the seed-syllable that abbreviates the entire Veda; it is our guide for recognizing the Veda. For this reason, Oṃkāra is the most exalted of all mantras that glorify Bhagavān -- there is no greater word of praise in this world. For this reason, Oṃkāra is Bhagavān's own exclusive name, the one we use to call upon him.\n\nWhen Oṃkāra expands, it becomes the vyāhṛtis; when the vyāhṛtis expand, they become the Gāyatrī; when the Gāyatrī expands, it becomes the Puruṣa-sūkta, and from there the three Vedas, and from there all the Vedas entire. Thus Bhagavān resides within the Praṇava, within the vyāhṛtis, within the Puruṣa-sūkta, and within all the Vedas. Many further details about Oṃkāra have already been explained specially in the seventh chapter (verse 8).\n\nBhagavān, who fills every word of the world born from the words of the Veda, and every worldly sound, created infinite sounds and infinite tones within space. He gave us boundless experience through sound, and graced us with the special power to grasp that sound and to reproduce it once grasped. The special power to come to know this Bhagavān, denoted by sound, through knowledge, is Bhagavān's own special gift to humankind.\n\nBhagavān is \"ṛk\": that through which we worship Bhagavān is ṛk. We worship through mantras. He who is worshipped is the Bhagavān named \"ṛk\" -- worshipped through every mantra, the Bhagavān worthy of praise is \"ṛk.\"\n\nBhagavān is \"sāmaḥ\": he who equalizes all things. Because he is equal in every one of his forms, in qualities such as knowledge and bliss, and because he gives life equally according to each one's own worthiness, Bhagavān is \"sāmaḥ.\"\n\nBhagavān is \"yajuḥ\": offered through every sacrifice, ever present within the Vedas, denoted by every word, Bhagavān is \"yajuḥ.\"",
  "9.18": "gatir bhartā prabhuḥ sākṣī nivāsaḥ śaraṇaṃ suhṛt |\nprabhavaḥ pralayaḥ sthānaṃ nidhānaṃ bījam avyayam -- I am the goal, the sustainer, the lord, the witness, the abode, the refuge, the true friend; the origin, the dissolution, the ground, the resting place, the imperishable seed.\n\nI am the one who must be known for liberation. I am the one who bears every burden. I am the master of all. I am the one who directly witnesses everything. I am the resting place of all. I am the refuge even of the liberated. I am the friend who needs no reason to be a friend. I am the cause of creation, sustenance, and dissolution. In dissolution I bear the world within my own womb; I am also the cause of its manifestation once more. I am imperishable.\n\nHaving earlier spoken of relationships, Kṛṣṇa now describes their many forms of attachment. In society, people receive help from one another in various ways -- protection from a king, refuge in times of danger, comforting one in distress, friendship, and so on. All of these bonds, says Kṛṣṇa, are Bhagavān's own dharma.\n\nOne who says \"I am here\" and protects someone who has no ground to stand on, no chance to live, is called their \"gati\" (refuge/goal). In truth, gati means the final destination. Bhagavān, who is the refuge not only in worldly life but in mokṣa itself, is gatiḥ; he is the good destiny (sadgati), and when we err, he is also the bad destiny (durgati) that follows. Bhagavān is the Lord (prabhu) who bears every burden and sees what each person in this world does, and what is good or bad for them. He knows all our inner and outer being. Pervading everywhere, able to see everything, he is the true witness (sākṣī).\n\nKṛṣṇa continues: \"aham nivāsaḥ.\" As the Īśāvāsya Upaniṣad says:\n\nīśāvāsyam idaṃ sarvaṃ yat kiñca jagatyāṃ jagat |\ntena tyaktena bhuñjīthā mā gṛdhaḥ kasya svid dhanam ||\n\nWe dwell in Bhagavān's own house. He who built this house called the universe, gave us shelter within it, and arranged everything we need to live, is \"nivāsaḥ.\" He is the refuge of all. Such a Bhagavān is also the intimate friend (suhṛt) who protects us (śaraṇa) in times of danger. There are two kinds of friends -- sakha and suhṛt. \"Sakha\" means a companion, one who is always by our side. \"Suhṛt\" means one who, whether near or far, responds to our heart and holds our welfare at heart. Bhagavān is the sakha who dwells within the center of our heart-lotus, and the suhṛt who forever responds to the longing of our heart.\n\nHaving spoken of these bonds, Kṛṣṇa now, moving beyond human power, explains divine power. He says: \"prabhavaḥ pralayaḥ sthānaṃ nidhānaṃ bījam avyayam.\" Some among us know creation as Brahmā's work, sustenance as Viṣṇu's, and destruction as Śiva's. Here Kṛṣṇa says clearly: \"I alone am prabhavaḥ, pralayaḥ, sthānam.\" Bhagavān is the root cause of the world's creation. At the end of creation, this world is destroyed; and throughout the period from creation to destruction, Bhagavān alone is fundamentally the cause of its continuance.\n\nAt the time of dissolution, that Bhagavān nurtures every class of living being in subtle form within his own womb. He is the imperishable one who sows that subtle seed so that it may sprout, blossom, and grow again into a great tree.\n\nHaving thus explained every action of the world -- worldly, transactional, and transcendent -- along with relationships and bonds, Kṛṣṇa now goes on to explain the things that occur in the course of time.",
  "9.19": "tapāmy aham ahaṃ varṣaṃ nigṛhṇāmy utsṛjāmi ca |\namṛtaṃ caiva mṛtyuś ca sad asac cāham arjuna -- I give heat; I withhold and I send the rain; I am both immortality and death; I am being and non-being, O Arjuna.\n\nBhagavān, present within the sun, sends solar energy to the earth and gives it life. He alone causes the rain to fall in its proper season, and he alone withholds it from falling out of season. Rain failing to come when it is needed, and rain coming when it should not, both come from Bhagavān. Bhagavān is the causal power behind every visible act.\n\nBhagavān is the nectar (amṛta) that gives us the breath of life. When our lifespan ends, it is he who stops that breath and calls us back. In sum, Kṛṣṇa says: \"I am the power that governs this entire universe, both what is visible (sat) and what is invisible (asat).\" This is Kṛṣṇa's concise, comprehensive picture given for the complete contemplation of Bhagavān. Here lies, in its fullest measure, everything needed for the contemplation of Bhagavān.\n\nNext, Kṛṣṇa goes on to describe, among his devotees (the wise), two principal kinds -- the traividyas and the bhāgavatas.",
  "9.20": "traividyā māṃ somapāḥ pūtapāpā yajñair iṣṭvā svargatiṃ prārthayante |\nte puṇyam āsādya surendralokam aśnanti divyān divi devabhogān -- Those versed in the three Vedas, drinkers of soma, cleansed of sin through sacrifices, worship me and pray for the path to heaven. Attaining that merit, they reach the realm of Indra and enjoy the divine pleasures of the gods in heaven.\n\n\"Traividyas\" means those who follow a triple path. They are the ones who divide their devotion between Bhagavān and other things. They place worldly desires and the wish for heaven before Bhagavān as they perform their devotion. For them, the karma-kāṇḍa portion of the Veda is what matters most. Performing sacrifices such as the jyotiṣṭoma solely from a desire for heaven, they seek not Bhagavān but heaven itself. As the fruit of such devotion, they reach Indra's realm, heaven, and enjoy pleasures there.",
  "9.21": "te taṃ bhuktvā svargalokaṃ viśālaṃ kṣīṇe puṇye martyalokaṃ viśanti |\nevaṃ trayīdharmam anuprapannā gatāgataṃ kāmakāmā labhante -- Having enjoyed that vast heavenly world, when their merit is exhausted, they enter the world of mortals once more. Thus, following the dharma of the three Vedas, driven by desire after desire, they obtain only a coming and a going.\n\nThe traividyas who perform meritorious acts out of desire for heaven reach heaven, enjoy its manifold pleasures there, and as soon as the fruit of their good deeds is exhausted, are born once more in this mortal world. For this reason they remain deprived of the greatest of all attainments -- mokṣa and Bhagavān himself. They remain forever chasing desires, caught in the revolving wheel of coming and going. Utterly different from their course is that of the Bhāgavatas:",
  "9.22": "ananyāś cintayanto māṃ ye janāḥ paryupāsate |\nteṣāṃ nityābhiyuktānāṃ yogakṣemaṃ vahāmy aham -- For those who, thinking of nothing else, worship me constantly with single-minded devotion -- for those ever devoted to me, I carry the burden of their yoga and kṣema (attainment and preservation of what they need).\n\nThe Bhāgavatas desire nothing else at all. Besides the contemplation of Bhagavān, they have no other thought. They desire Bhagavān alone, exclusively. They do not perform worship bound to any desire. They do not offer oblations to, or worship, any deity other than Bhagavān as their primary object. Within every deity, they see only Bhagavān. This is single-pointed devotion (ekabhakti) -- the path one must follow to reach the Supreme and attain mokṣa.\n\nWhen we have worship performed, we make a saṅkalpa (a statement of intent). We must never place our worldly desires before Bhagavān in this saṅkalpa. Only knowledge, devotion, and mokṣa should be our resolve. When we have worship performed while placing perishable desires before us, we lose out on the far greater fruit available to us. What we truly need and do not need is known to Bhagavān far better than it is known to us, and he never abandons us. When worship is performed purely for the love of Bhagavān, and single-minded devotion is planted firmly in him, Bhagavān loves us in return. Kṛṣṇa says: \"teṣāṃ nityābhiyuktānāṃ yogakṣemaṃ vahāmy aham\" -- \"The burden of the yoga and kṣema of such devotees is mine to carry.\" What greater assurance could one need than this?\n\nIf we believe, deeply and firmly, that \"Bhagavān will surely protect us,\" he will indeed protect us without fail. We must never waver in our faith in Bhagavān -- it must be firm. Our goal must be fixed solely on reaching Bhagavān. We must accept whatever Bhagavān gives as his greatest grace (mahā-prasāda) and surrender ourselves to him completely. In such a state, we shall never know sorrow; life becomes filled with bliss. Whatever happens in life, we must cultivate the disposition of accepting it joyfully, knowing it to be \"as Bhagavān has willed.\" This is the great gateway to mokṣa.",
  "9.23": "ye'py anyadevatā-bhaktā yajante śraddhayānvitāḥ |\nte'pi mām eva kaunteya yajanty avidhipūrvakam -- O Kaunteya, even those who, as devotees of other deities, worship them with faith, worship me alone -- though by a mistaken method.",
  "9.24": "ahaṃ hi sarvayajñānāṃ bhoktā ca prabhur eva ca |\nna tu mām abhijānanti tattvena ataś cyavanti te -- For I alone am the enjoyer and the lord of all sacrifices; but they do not know me in truth, and therefore they fall away.\n\nSome, as discussed earlier, worship deities as their primary object -- distinct from Bhagavān -- seeking the fulfillment of their desires. We might wonder what happens when this is done. Kṛṣṇa has already said: \"I am present in everything.\" Whomever one worships primarily, that worship is ultimately received by Bhagavān himself. The trouble here lies in the worshipper's lack of this awareness. This leads to a flawed inner orientation. Devotion performed without this awareness can never be complete devotion. Worship performed without consciousness of the Supreme can never elevate us. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Instead of gaining merit through such worship, one merely falls, even while performing meritorious acts.\" Therefore, whatever worship is performed, it bears fruit only when performed with awareness of the Supreme. Whomever we worship, that worship ultimately reaches Bhagavān alone, because Bhagavān is the enjoyer of all sacrifices. This is the secret of the Yajña-puruṣa behind every sacrifice. As the Puruṣa-sūkta says: \"yajñena yajñam ayajanta devāḥ\" -- through sacrifice, the deities worship the Bhagavān named Yajña. Through worship of Bhagavān -- the enjoyer of all sacrifices, the lord of all sacrifices, denoted by the word \"yajña\" -- we may attain him.\n\nEven so, we see in society many people who worship deities in various ways, placing their faith solely in the worship of that particular deity. What becomes of those who worship in this manner? Kṛṣṇa answers this in the verse that follows.",
  "9.25": "yānti devavratā devān pitṝn yānti pitṛvratāḥ |\nbhūtāni yānti bhūtejyā yānti madyājino'pi mām -- Those devoted to the deities go to the deities; those devoted to the ancestors go to the ancestors; those who worship spirits go to spirits; and those who worship me reach me alone.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"You attain whomever you worship.\" Those who worship the deities attain the deities; those who worship the ancestors attain the ancestors; those who worship spirits (the bhūta-preta class) attain spirits. But those who, through single-pointed devotion, see Bhagavān in everything and worship him with faith -- \"they reach me\" -- this is the assurance Kṛṣṇa gives here. One may attain heaven by worshipping the deities, but not mokṣa, which is freedom from rebirth. Whatever worship one performs, true worship consists in worshipping with awareness of Bhagavān present behind every action. Worship performed without this awareness never leads us toward mokṣa. If one worships Bhagavān together with the entire family of deities, from Brahmā downward, as belonging to him, then one attains Bhagavān. This is the arrangement laid down by scripture.\n\nBefore moving to the next verse, we must be clear about one matter. In our daily life we perform worship in the form of gaṇahoma, navagraha-homa, deity worship, saṅkaṣṭi, and so on. Is this wrong? If we worship Bhagavān through the symbol of Gaṇapati or some other form, do we not attain mokṣa? What is right, what is wrong? What is the traividhya-dharma, and what is the bhāgavata-dharma?\n\nAmong us today, bhāgavata-dharma is fading away. Worshipping the indwelling Bhagavān of any symbol is not wrong; what is wrong is the saṅkalpa (intention) behind it. Take navagraha-homa, for example. If we perform it desiring no fruit at all, purely for the attainment of mokṣa, for the pleasure of Nārāyaṇa who indwells the navagrahas together with the whole family of deities -- there is nothing wrong in this. But if our saṅkalpa is \"may Nārāyaṇa indwelling the navagrahas be pleased and thereby remove my navagraha-doṣa,\" that becomes traividhya-dharma. When we make our saṅkalpa without mixing in any desire, performing whatever action solely for the pleasure of Bhagavān, that is bhāgavata-dharma. If Bhagavān is well pleased, everything is accomplished. Enjoy happily what he gives; do not desire what he withholds. Do not worship Bhagavān asking him to remove some difficulty that has come upon you -- instead, accept whatever comes as prasāda, and pray continually to Bhagavān for knowledge, dispassion, devotion, and mokṣa. The burden of your protection belongs to Bhagavān; do not trouble your mind over it. Nothing that happens in your life is accidental -- it is Bhagavān's will. You worship Bhagavān not to remove it but to reach Bhagavān himself. When we transform the inner orientation behind our saṅkalpa in this way, it becomes bhāgavata-dharma. A traividhya bows to his teacher saying \"my guru\"; a bhāgavata bows knowing \"Bhagavān, seated within that teacher, is teaching me.\" A bhāgavata sees Bhagavān in every one of his actions. In all his activity he desires nothing but mokṣa and Bhagavān; his worship carries no desire but the desire for mokṣa. Reaching this height is not impossible for us -- it requires only effort. The more we act purely for the love of Bhagavān, the closer we draw to mokṣa. Never ask Bhagavān \"give me this\" or \"remove this.\" Bhagavān knows what you need; leave that to him. Act only for the love of Bhagavān and for the attainment of mokṣa. Do not evade this with the thought \"this is only for Bhīmasena/Vāyudeva\" -- worship Bhagavān with full devotion and no desire for fruit. The only thing you have the right to ask for in worship is mokṣa and Bhagavān himself; devote your effort to attaining that. All the ups and downs of life are perishable -- without dwelling on them, strive to detach from worldly existence and reach the deathless state of mokṣa. This is the essential message here.\n\nWhat is needed to worship Bhagavān is not wealth or gold, but pure, desireless devotion alone. How simple the worship of Bhagavān truly is -- Kṛṣṇa explains this in the following verse:",
  "9.26": "patraṃ puṣpaṃ phalaṃ toyaṃ yo me bhaktyā prayacchati |\ntad ahaṃ bhaktyupahṛtam aśnāmi prayatātmanaḥ -- Whoever offers me, with devotion, a leaf, a flower, a fruit, or water -- that offering of devotion, from one whose heart is pure, I accept.\n\nWhat is fundamentally needed for the worship of the exalted Bhagavān is complete self-involvement and worship with a heart full of feeling. If we offer even a tulasī leaf or a flower with a mind full of devotion, Bhagavān lovingly accepts it. No golden pedestal, no silver tray, no five-vessel offering set is needed here. Offer whatever is available in your home, in your garden. If there is no milk in the house, offer even a glass of water with devotion -- so long as purity of feeling lies behind it. Kṛṣṇa says: \"yo me bhaktyā prayacchati.\" What Bhagavān desires is our inner love and heartfelt sincerity, not outward splendor. \"Tad ahaṃ bhaktyupahṛtam aśnāmi prayatātmanaḥ\" -- \"Whatever you offer with devotion, having fixed your mind in me, I accept it,\" says Kṛṣṇa. The story of Śabarī in the Rāmāyaṇa is a fine illustration of this teaching of Bhagavān.",
  "9.27": "yat karoṣi yad aśnāsi yaj juhoṣi dadāsi yat |\nyat tapasyasi kaunteya tat kuruṣva madarpaṇam -- O Kaunteya, whatever you do, whatever you eat, whatever you offer in sacrifice, whatever you give, whatever austerity you perform -- do it as an offering to me.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"You need do nothing extra for my sake -- simply offer to me whatever you do for your own sake.\" In our daily life there are many unavoidable duties, the work needed to sustain our livelihood. We must perform these knowing them to be worship of Bhagavān. A fine illustration of this is the story of Dharmavyādha. He made his living selling meat, and held this resolve: \"Selling meat is a trade that has come to me by heredity; therefore I sell meat, and I carry out this trade that has fallen to my lot for my own sustenance and to support my parents. From it I earn only what I need for my life; I never exploit people in my dealings.\" With this resolve he sold meat, and through it earned the title of \"Dharmavyādha, the great sage\" -- an occasion arose where even great ṛṣis and munis came to learn from him. The root cause of this was simply that he performed whatever he did as worship of God. Thus it is not our occupation itself that matters, but the inner orientation of performing it as worship of Bhagavān.\n\nOffer to Bhagavān whatever your daily duty may be; perform it as worship of Bhagavān. There is no need to prepare special delicacies for Bhagavān -- offer to him whatever you yourself eat. Doing this, the worship of Bhagavān need never be a separate or difficult matter. There is no need for some special fire-ritual for Bhagavān's sake -- whatever fire-offering you make, offer it saying \"agni-antargata-nārāyaṇāya idaṃ na mama.\" If you see a hungry person, know that he carries within his stomach the Bhagavān in the form of Vaiśvānara, and honor that person so that the Vaiśvānara within him may be pleased. This itself is the greatest worship of Bhagavān.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"yat tapasyasi kaunteya tat kuruṣva madarpaṇam.\" Here tapas means contemplation, or the observance of a vow. For example, when observing Saṅkaṣṭi, do so with the thought that it be offered to the Bhagavān who indwells Gaṇapati. Hold no desire for fruit, no ego, in doing this. When any observance whatsoever is performed knowing it to be ultimately offered to the Supreme Person, Bhagavān, it becomes true tapas. Here Kṛṣṇa gives Arjuna the message: \"Perform even this war, which has come to you as your duty, as worship of Bhagavān.\" What fruit comes from following bhāgavata-dharma, Kṛṣṇa explains in the verse that follows.",
  "9.28": "śubhāśubhaphalair evaṃ mokṣyase karmabandhanaiḥ |\nsannyāsayogayuktātmā vimukto mām upaiṣyasi -- Thus you shall be freed from the bonds of karma, whether their fruits be good or bad; with your self united in the yoga of renunciation, liberated, you shall come to me.\n\nWhen we perform any action bound by the desire \"let such-and-such a fruit come to me,\" and with the ego \"I did this action,\" that action clings to us. For this reason, in earlier times, when making the resolve \"I shall perform this action,\" people would say:\n\nbhagavato balena bhagavato vīryeṇa bhagavataḥ tejasā |\nbhagavatā karmaṇā karma kariṣyāmi bhagavato vāsudevasya ||\n\n\"The action I perform is not my action; it is the action of the Bhagavān within me. All the power and capacity behind it belong to Bhagavān; nothing belongs to me.\" This is the saṅkalpa of bhāgavata-dharma. No action performed for the love of Bhagavān becomes a bondage to us. In the Brahma-sūtras, Vedavyāsa states this: \"When the knowledge of Bhagavān becomes firm and immediate within us, that fire of knowledge burns away all the sins of our past desire-driven actions.\" Therefore knowledge is the highest of all. We see this in the dialogue between Yama and Naciketas in the Kaṭha Upaniṣad. There, when Naciketas asks Yama for the boon of knowledge, Yama says to him: \"Ask instead for as much wealth as you desire; if you wish, I shall make you emperor of a great kingdom.\" To this Naciketas replies: \"All these are but means to hell; I want none of them. I wish to know of Bhagavān.\" Thus the sole path to washing away our sinful karma entirely and reaching mokṣa is knowledge of Bhagavān. When action is performed with the mindset of offering it to Bhagavān, freedom from the yoke of good and bad karma becomes possible. Kṛṣṇa says: \"sannyāsayogayuktātmā vimukto mām upaiṣyasi.\" Here the \"sannyāsa\" spoken of is a yoga (a means) -- the practice of the sāttvika, who offer everything to Bhagavān and act with the outlook of worship, without desire for fruit. Sannyāsa means offering one's desire and resolve to Bhagavān. \"Kāma\" means wanting to do something; \"saṅkalpa\" means resolving to do it. Sannyāsa is the freedom from desire-driven resolve. If bhāgavata-dharma is thus followed, one may easily reach Bhagavān -- \"they shall surely reach me,\" is Kṛṣṇa's assurance.",
  "9.29": "samo'haṃ sarvabhūteṣu na me dveṣyo'sti na priyaḥ |\nye bhajanti tu māṃ bhaktyā mayi te teṣu cāpy aham -- I am the same toward all beings; none is hateful to me, none dear. But those who worship me with devotion are in me, and I am in them.\n\nBhagavān holds neither hatred nor favoritism toward anyone. What he loves is not the individual, but the knowledge and devotion within that individual. Those who are worthy win Bhagavān's favor through devotion. To true devotees, Bhagavān becomes wholly devoted in turn.",
  "9.30": "api cet sudurācāro bhajate mām ananyabhāk |\nsādhur eva sa mantavyaḥ samyag vyavasito hi saḥ -- Even one of thoroughly bad conduct, if he worships me with undivided devotion, should be considered righteous, for he has resolved rightly.\n\nTrue devotees are, as a rule, never of bad conduct. If, by chance, a sāttvika person becomes ill-behaved in one birth owing to some prārabdha karma, he remains fundamentally good, because he has practiced devotion to Bhagavān across many births. This may not be visible to ordinary people like us, but it is visible to Bhagavān; in Bhagavān's eyes, he remains a good soul. For this reason we should never look down upon anyone. If someone walks a bad path, there may be many reasons for it -- he may well be sāttvika at heart, yet his prārabdha karma may be driving him along that path. One who is fundamentally good, Kṛṣṇa says in the following verse, does not remain on that path forever.",
  "9.31": "kṣipraṃ bhavati dharmātmā śaśvacchāntiṃ nigacchati |\nkaunteya pratijānīhi na me bhaktaḥ praṇaśyati -- Swiftly he becomes righteous of soul and attains lasting peace. O Kaunteya, declare it boldly: my devotee never perishes.\n\nThose who have thus strayed from the path owing to prārabdha karma soon become aware of the wrong they are doing, in some circumstance or other, and return to the right path -- and they attain the imperishable mokṣa. This is certain. \"Declare this as an oath,\" says Kṛṣṇa. A fine example of this is the story of Ajāmila. Though Ajāmila did everything one should never do in life, he was fundamentally a great soul. Bhagavān, at the very end of his life, showed such a man the path of truth and redeemed him. In his final hour he undertook true sādhana and attained mokṣa.\n\nWhy does Kṛṣṇa tell Arjuna here, \"you make this vow\"? Because Bhagavān may break his own vow for the sake of his devotees, but never the vow of a devotee. An illustration of this occurs in the Mahābhārata war: Kṛṣṇa had vowed before the war that he would not take up arms. Yet in the midst of the battle, when Arjuna hesitated to fight against Bhīṣma, Kṛṣṇa, seized with fury, seized a chariot wheel and rushed forward as if to slay Bhīṣma himself! Seeing this, Bhīṣma folded both his hands and said: \"O Bhagavān, I had vowed that I would make you take up a wheel in your hand and fight -- you had said you would not take up a wheel. But now, to fulfill my vow, you have taken up the wheel. You have forgotten yourself, but not the vow of your devotee. Who else but you is our refuge?\" This incident reveals just how deeply devoted to his devotees Bhagavān is.",
  "9.32": "māṃ hi pārtha vyapāśritya ye'pi syuḥ pāpayonayaḥ |\nstriyo vaiśyās tathā śūdrās te'pi yānti parāṃ gatim -- For, O Pārtha, even those born of a sinful womb -- women, vaiśyas, and śūdras -- who take refuge in me, attain the supreme destination.\n\nThis verse has confused even some commentators. Before Madhvācārya wrote his commentary on this verse, most held a mistaken understanding of it. Read superficially, it carries the risk of being understood to mean \"women, vaiśyas, and śūdras are sinners.\" But Kṛṣṇa is not speaking here about the female jīva as such -- women are not sinners. Rather, \"strī-pāpayoni\" refers to a male who, owing to some sin or curse, has taken birth as a woman -- not to the female jīva who is simply a woman. Kṛṣṇa says: \"One may have come to a lowly birth owing to some sin, but even such a person, by worshipping me, may become free of every flaw.\" A fine illustration of this is the sixteen thousand one hundred women held captive by Narakāsura. These were sons of Agni who, desiring to become the consorts of Bhagavān, gave up their higher stature and were born as women on earth, only to be caught and made to suffer in Narakāsura's prison. Kṛṣṇa married them and redeemed them.\n\nNandagopa and Yaśodā were, at root, portions of the divine. The Vasu named Droṇa became Nandagopa, and his wife, the Vasu named Dharā, became Yaśodā. They were born on earth as vaiśyas. Bhagavān, in his avatāra, grew up in their household and redeemed them.\n\nThe great soul Vidura was himself Dharmarāja. Though the son of Vedavyāsa, owing to the effect of a curse he had to live his life being called \"the son of a servant woman.\" When Kṛṣṇa came for the peace negotiations, he did not go to the houses of Duryodhana, Bhīṣma, or Droṇa, but went directly to Vidura's house, accepted his simple hospitality there, and was pleased with him. Here it becomes clear that what Bhagavān desires is not outward show but love. What matters to him is the inner self, not the outer mask. Thus Kṛṣṇa states clearly: \"Those born into a lowly womb through some sinful act or curse, when they take refuge in me, are freed from that sin and reach me.\"",
  "9.33": "kiṃ punar brāhmaṇāḥ puṇyā bhaktā rājarṣayas tathā |\nanityam asukhaṃ lokam imaṃ prāpya bhajasva mām -- How much more, then, the pure brāhmaṇas and devoted royal sages! Having attained this transient, joyless world, worship me.\n\n\"Need I even mention, then, those born through the fruit of merit as royal sages or knowers of Brahman, who worship Bhagavān?\" Kṛṣṇa asks. The burden of protecting every single person who takes refuge and shelter in Bhagavān belongs to Bhagavān himself. For this reason, Kṛṣṇa gives us this counsel here: \"Do not thrash about, caught in this world, thinking it to be everything -- for this is 'anityam asukhaṃ lokam.' There is no happiness in it; it is not everlasting. Therefore take refuge in me to attain everlasting bliss. Surrender to me. The burden of your redemption is mine.\"",
  "9.34": "manmanā bhava madbhakto madyājī māṃ namaskuru |\nmām evaiṣyasi yuktvaivam ātmānaṃ matparāyaṇaḥ -- Fix your mind on me, be my devotee, worship me, bow to me. Having thus united your very self with me, wholly devoted to me, you shall come to me alone.\n\nAs it is said: \"tvan mā bhajasva padmākṣi, yena saukhyaṃ labhāmy aham\" -- \"I worship you; you worship me too\" -- here bhajana means loving with devotion.\n\nWhatever worship you perform, let it be the worship of Bhagavān. See Bhagavān in all your love. Let every bow you make be a bow to Bhagavān. Doing this, fix your mind in Bhagavān and make your entire life full of him -- let Bhagavān be the greatest thing to you, the very essence of your life, the object of your constant contemplation. Let every single action of your life become the worship of Bhagavān. Then, says Kṛṣṇa, \"you must surely reach me.\" Thus Kṛṣṇa has explained the worship that accomplishes mokṣa, and with this the ninth chapter comes to its conclusion.\n\nThus ends the ninth chapter.",
  "10.1": "Chapter Ten\n\nHaving explained to Arjuna the qualities of Bhagavān that we must worship, and the knowledge and realization behind them, Kṛṣṇa now, recognizing the thirst for knowledge and complete sincerity that has blossomed in him, explains in this chapter the anusandhāna to be specially adopted in worship -- Bhagavān's own vibhūti (glory).\n\nA teacher does not ordinarily reveal priceless knowledge easily. Scripture holds that such a matter should be disclosed only when the disciple has shown complete sincerity toward the teacher and possesses the worthiness for it. Hence a teacher never imparts precious knowledge to a disciple without first testing him. A fine illustration of this appears in the Praśnopaniṣad. To the six great scholars who came to Pippalāda, he said:\n\nbhūya eva tapasā brahmacaryēṇa śraddhayā saṃvatsaraṃ saṃvatsyatha; yathākāmaṃ praśnān pṛcchata; yadi vijñāsyāmaḥ sarvaṃ ha vo vakṣyāma iti |\n\n\"Live for one year in austerity, in brahmacarya, and with faith. After a year, ask whatever questions you wish; if I know the answer, I shall tell you.\" Here the mutual testing of teacher and disciple stands out plainly. But when a teacher recognizes complete sincerity in a worthy disciple, he pours out to him, joyfully, the knowledge within him even without being asked. It is precisely this complete sincerity that Kṛṣṇa has, at this stage, recognized in Arjuna.\n\nBhagavān uvāca -- The Lord said:\n\nbhūya eva mahābāho śṛṇu me paramaṃ vacaḥ |\nyat te'haṃ prīyamāṇāya vakṣyāmi hitakāmyāyā -- O mighty-armed one, hear once more my supreme word, which, since you delight in hearing it, I shall speak to you, desiring your welfare.\n\nDissatisfaction in giving knowledge to a disciple arises from the disciple's own talent and special qualities. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"I shall tell you still more.\" For, he says, what he is about to tell is \"paramaṃ vacaḥ.\" Here \"paramam\" means the very highest among the matters we ought to know -- the supreme knowledge that grants realization of the ultimate reality beyond all else. It is something that benefits the entire world; there is nothing beyond it. \"You stand here on this battlefield, delighting to hear this -- that joy is welling up within you. For that reason, and for the good of the world, I shall speak; listen,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "10.2": "na me viduḥ sura-gaṇāḥ prabhavaṃ na maharṣayaḥ |\naham ādir hi devānāṃ maharṣīṇāṃ ca sarvaśah -- Neither the hosts of gods nor the great sages know my origin [the manner in which I fashion the universe], for I alone am, in every way, the origin of the gods and of the great sages.\n\nKṛṣṇa here reveals a fundamental matter. He says: \"Neither the great sages nor the gods, from Brahmā downward, know my prabhava.\" The word \"prabhava\" is used here in three senses: prabhava as origin or birth; prabhava as capacity or greatness; prabhava as the coming-into-being of the world. \"My origin, my greatness, and the birth of this world that has come from me -- none of this is known even to the great knowers who have realized me directly, nor even to Brahmā and the other gods who possess immediate knowledge of me,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "10.3": "yo mām ajam anādiṃ ca vetti lokamaheśvaram |\nasammūḋhaḥ sa martyeṣu sarvapāpaiḥ pramucyate -- One who knows me as unborn, without beginning, the great Lord of the worlds -- that one, undeluded among mortals, is freed from all sins.\n\nSome of us are troubled by a certain question: \"If Bhagavān created every living being, then who created him?\" If someone else had created Bhagavān, we could not call him God at all. Kṛṣṇa knows what our doubt is, and in this verse he answers it. Kṛṣṇa says: \"yo mām ajam anādim.\" Here \"aja\" means unborn. Bhagavān stands beyond birth and death. It is precisely for this reason that, to be freed from birth and death ourselves, we must worship him and reach him. Bhagavān is anādi (an-ādi) -- meaning \"the power that impelled creation even before it began, and, having created, governs every transaction within this creation, the supreme power.\"\n\nBhagavān is \"lokamaheśvaraḥ.\" All the gods, from Brahmā downward, are \"lokeśas.\" Lakṣmī, the mother who bore them, is \"lokeśvarī.\" Bhagavān, greater even than her, is \"lokamaheśvara.\" There is no principle greater than him. He stands beyond birth and death.\n\nIt is that same Bhagavān who created the great sages and the gods, all of them; hence none of them has seen the furthest edge of his glory. The Ṛgveda says at one place: \"na te viṣṇo jāyamāno na jāto devo mahimnaḥ paramantam āpa\" -- neither those born in the past nor those yet to be born have known, or can know, the utmost limit of Bhagavān's glory, because it is infinite.\n\nHow this universe itself came to be created is likewise unknown to anyone -- because during the time of dissolution everything lay asleep within Bhagavān's own womb, and afterward, at the time of creation, Bhagavān created Caturmukha first of all. Hence no one has witnessed this act of creation, which began even before Caturmukha's own birth. For this reason the Ṛgveda says: \"If anyone knows the secret of this creation, surely it is some wise son who would know it\" -- and that very one is the first living being of Bhagavān's creation, Caturmukha Brahmā. For all these reasons, we ordinary humans can never come to know the full extent of Bhagavān's glory. The ācāryas have expressed this thus: \"bahu citraṃ jagat bahu-dā-kāraṇāt para-śaktir ananta-guṇaḥ paramaḥ\" -- if this world, created by Bhagavān, is itself so wondrous, how wondrous indeed must its creator be! Therefore, knowing the truth that Bhagavān is infinite, and worshipping him accordingly, is the sole path to mokṣa. One who has come to know this truth about Bhagavān is \"asammūḋha\" -- undeluded. Falling prey to no illusion, such a person loves everyone always and sees Bhagavān within everyone. \"The seeker who has realized this truth is freed from all sins\" -- this is Kṛṣṇa's assurance.",
  "10.4": "buddhir jñānam asammohaḥ kṣamā satyaṃ damaḥ śamaḥ |\nsukhaṃ duḥkhaṃ bhavo'bhāvo bhayaṃ cābhayam eva ca -- Discernment, understanding, freedom from delusion, forbearance, truthfulness, control of the senses, calmness of mind, pleasure and pain, birth and its absence, fear and fearlessness -- all these arise from me alone.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa explains a matter essential to our worship of Bhagavān. It is Bhagavān who gives us buddhi, jñāna, asammoha, kṣamā, satyam, damaḥ-śamaḥ -- all of it. Some are under the illusion that they themselves are very intelligent, unaware of the truth that it is Bhagavān who bestows that very power of intellect. Kṛṣṇa says: \"It is I who give you buddhi, and I who give you jñāna.\" Here \"jñāna\" means the awareness of a particular object; \"buddhi\" means the discernment of what should and should not be done. Only when both buddhi and jñāna are present in us is our understanding complete. If jñāna is present but buddhi is absent, the Veda departs from us: \"ācārahīnaṃ na punanti vedāḥ\" -- if a person is without proper conduct, all the Vedas abandon him, just as fledglings whose feathers have grown fly away from their nest, leaving him behind. To know that one ought to have good conduct is jñāna; but the discernment of what is and is not proper conduct is buddhi. Along with buddhi and jñāna, the capacity to grasp the truth without any doubt whatsoever is \"asammoha.\" These are the three chief pillars that sustain us in life; knowing this, we must worship Bhagavān.\n\nThe fourth quality is \"kṣamā\" -- an exceedingly great quality. If someone criticizes us, and there is something in that criticism by which we may correct ourselves, we should correct it. But if it is merely envious carping, letting the matter rest there without any retaliation is kṣamā.\n\nThe fifth quality is \"satyam\" -- honesty. Today we have forgotten true integrity and live behind its mask. Dishonesty has come to pervade our lives. However rich our outward conduct, without honesty it is of no use. Speaking one way to a person's face and another behind their back is dishonesty. If we dislike something about another person, we may either tell them so directly or keep our distance from them. Until honesty grows within us, no scripture can redeem us. We must worship Bhagavān asking him to grant us such honesty.\n\nSixth is \"damaḥ,\" pertaining to the senses. Our senses do not listen to us; they drag us toward what we should not want. Because of this we see what should not be seen and say what should not be said. Such mastery over the senses is damaḥ. The one and only path to mastering the senses is surrender to Bhagavān.\n\nThe seventh quality is \"śamaḥ\" -- our mind remaining tranquil (to be in a blissful state) is śamaḥ. Today all of us live in a state of restlessness (Tension)! The root cause is that we keep our mind centered on worldly concerns. When we set worldly concerns aside and let the mind flow toward Bhagavān, the mind becomes at ease. One who holds the firm faith that \"God exists, and he will never abandon me\" whenever a problem arises in life can attain this state. In sum, the essential message here is: we must worship with the full realization that everything in our inner world is Bhagavān.\n\nContinuing, Kṛṣṇa says: \"pleasure-pain, birth-death, fear-courage...",
  "10.5": "ahiṃsā samatā tuṣṭis tapo dānaṃ yaśo'yaśaḥ |\nbhavanti bhāvā bhūtānāṃ matta eva pṛthagvidhāḥ -- Non-violence, equanimity, contentment, austerity, giving (and its opposite), fame and infamy -- these various states of being that arise in living beings arise from me alone.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"Non-violence, equanimity, contentment, austerity, giving (and its opposite), fame, ill-fame\" -- all these come from me. Here non-violence and equanimity are especially noteworthy, because violence itself sometimes becomes non-violence, and inequality itself becomes equality. For this reason Kṛṣṇa specially raises them here.\n\nFirst, non-violence: living in such a way that we cause no harm to another is ahiṃsā. Here ahiṃsā is not merely physical; we must achieve non-violence in mind and speech as well. For example, taunting someone with a barbed word, or mentally reviling or cursing another, is also violence. Sometimes violence itself becomes non-violence -- for instance, killing a man-eating tiger, if it saves the lives of many villagers, is itself an act of non-violence. (We see this kind of non-violence in many of Kṛṣṇa's own deeds.) A small violence performed to prevent a greater violence is non-violence. We must conduct ourselves through speech, action, and mind so as to cause no harm to another.\n\nThe second quality is equanimity (samatā). Equanimity here does not mean seeing everyone as identical. It is equanimity in proportion to worth. For instance, giving equal marks to a student who has studied diligently and to another who has been idle and undisciplined is not equality. Equality is awarding marks according to each one's merit, without hatred, without distinguishing between enemy and friend, native and foreigner. It is seeing each person as they ought to be seen -- treating a father as a father, a mother as a mother, a teacher as a teacher. Honoring equals equally, whoever they may be, is equanimity.\n\nThe third quality is contentment. The chief cause of every human sorrow is discontent. In today's society, even when we have all we truly need at home, we live in perpetual discontent -- lured by advertisements in the media, or competing with our neighbors. When we have no job we desire one; once employed, however much we earn, discontent remains. To be content with whatever you have, without pining for what you lack, is true contentment.\n\nFourth is austerity (tapas), of three kinds: kāyā-vācā-manasā tapaḥ. \"Kāya\" means the body. Austerity through the body generally means not letting the body have its own way, through restraint or moderation of food. When food is regulated, the mind comes under control; when the mind is controlled, the body can remain steady. For this purpose various forms of vow-observance serve as means of restraint. In sum, the quality of yielding without ego or envy comes from bodily austerity. Speaking words that are true, natural, and beneficial to others; listening to and reflecting on the scriptures, mastering them through one's own study and discourse, digesting them, then teaching them to others and passing them on to the next generation -- this is vācā-tapaḥ. Constant, deep mental contemplation is manasā-tapaḥ.\n\nFifth is (a)dāna. The word \"dāna\" has two meanings in Sanskrit -- one is \"to give,\" the other \"to sever.\" If our giving severs our sin, that is dāna; if instead it severs our merit, that is a-dāna.\n\nGiving away something useless to us merely to dispose of it is not dāna. If we possess something essential that another lacks, and we share what we have to relieve their want, that is true dāna. If a person is struggling without even one meal, and we have grain enough for two meals, giving that person one meal's worth without worrying about our own tomorrow is true dāna.\n\nIn giving food, clothing, and shelter, there is no restriction of who is worthy or unworthy to receive them -- a place to stay for the homeless, clothing for the unclothed, food for the hungry, may be given to anyone. But when giving other things, one must consider who truly needs and deserves them, and give accordingly. Giving for the sake of publicity is a-dāna. Giving with no desire at all, purely out of sincere duty, in a worthy place, at a worthy time, to a worthy person -- that is true dāna.\n\nFinally, Kṛṣṇa mentions fame and infamy (yaśaḥ-ayaśaḥ). This is a strange matter indeed. One person may do something wicked and still win fame as a good man; another may spend a lifetime doing good and still meet with unwanted slander and ruin. This is determined by the course of our own life. For this reason we should not trouble our minds over it. Kṛṣṇa himself, in his own conduct, showed us how one should bear false accusation -- the episode of the Śyamantaka jewel, when Kṛṣṇa was accused of theft, is a fine illustration of this.\n\nThus Kṛṣṇa says: \"All these states of mind arise in living beings from me alone.\" Which state should arise in whom, and when, is Bhagavān's own determination. Every movement of our mind's condition is subject to Bhagavān. This is a fundamental truth we must know in our worship -- a wondrous portrait of mental worship that Kṛṣṇa gives us here.",
  "10.6": "maharṣayaḥ sapta pūrve catvāro manavas tathā |\nmadbhāvā mānasā jātā yeṣāṃ loke imāḥ prajāḥ -- The seven great sages of the first age, and likewise the first four Manus, born of my own mind, came into being from me; and all these peoples of the world are their descendants.\n\nKṛṣṇa here explains a chief matter we must know in worship. He mentions the seven sages (saptarṣis) of the first manvantara [Marīci, Atri, Aṅgiras, Pulastya, Pulaha, Kratu, and Vasiṣṭha] and the first four Manus [Svāyambhuva, Svārociṣa, Uttama, and Raivata], the mind-born sons of Caturmukha -- all of these, he says, came into being from him alone. [All the gods, appointed as regulators of the four varṇas and grouped into four classes on account of their special understanding, called \"Manus,\" came into being from me, born by my own will.] All the peoples of this world are their descendants.\n\nWe already know that the entire universe was created by Bhagavān. Here Kṛṣṇa explains a chief point we must know in our worship. He speaks of the saptarṣis of the first manvantara and the first four Manus. Each manvantara has its own distinct saptarṣis. In the hierarchy of the gods, these saptarṣis of the first manvantara, ranked in the sixteenth tier, are the very greatest among the community of sages. Looking at the Purāṇas and the Mokṣadharma-parvan of the Mahābhārata, we find that \"maharṣayaḥ sapta pūrve\" also refers to the seven Brahmās who assist Caturmukha, the cause of creation. [Bhṛgu and Dakṣa should be included here by implication. The tenth mind-born son, Nārada, is a lifelong celibate, and so his name is not included in the expansion of creation... These seven sages together with Bhṛgu and Dakṣa are called the Prajāpatis.] Bhagavān created Caturmukha, and through him created the saptarṣis. In creation, the human lineage first spread through Svāyambhuva Manu; from him the line of Manus grew. For all these reasons, the rulers of the first four manvantaras and the saptarṣis are primarily those who preceded us and are to be worshipped. All these peoples of the world are their descendants.\n\nThe word \"manu\" has another meaning too. The gods who form Bhagavān's own family are also called \"manus.\" Among the tattvābhimāni deities there are chiefly four kinds; these are the deities governing the four natures of humankind (brāhmaṇya, kṣātra, vaiśya, śūdra). Each of us carries all four of these natures within us; whichever nature predominates in us determines which class we are called by. Besides this, the classes of living beings denoted by the word \"manu\" are four: all the gods; the sages (humans) created by the gods; the \"human-humans\" who descend from the lineage of sages; and the humans who descend from this lineage in turn. This is a point we must know in worship.",
  "10.7": "etāṃ vibhūtiṃ yogaṃ ca mama yo vetti tattvataḥ |\nso'vikampena yogena yujyate nātra saṃśayaḥ -- One who truly knows this glory and yogic power of mine [the manner of my forms that grant greatness through their very presence] becomes established in unwavering, pure meditative union -- of this there is no doubt.\n\nIf we come to know truly the greatness of Bhagavān, his various forms, and their depth, surrender arises naturally within us. Knowledge of Bhagavān gives birth to firm faith within us. A mind filled with faith and devotion becomes firmly established in Bhagavān.",
  "10.8": "ahaṃ sarvasya prabhavo mattaḥ sarvaṃ pravartate |\niti matvā bhajante māṃ budhā bhāvasamanvitāḥ -- Those who know \"I am the origin of all, and from me everything proceeds\" -- understanding this, the wise worship me with heartfelt devotion.\n\nThe wise, knowing the truth that \"Bhagavān is the regulator of all things, everything has been created by Bhagavān, everything is governed by Bhagavān,\" worship him with devotion. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Those who possess such unwavering devotion toward Bhagavān and the highest knowledge worship me knowing that their entire mental world is under my control, that I am the cause of all things, that everything is born from me, everything is governed by me, everything is subject to me.\"",
  "10.9": "maccittā madgataprāṇā bodhayantaḥ parasparam |\nkathayantaś ca māṃ nityaṃ tuṣyanti ca ramanti ca -- With their minds fixed on me, their very life devoted to me, enlightening one another and speaking of me always, they find contentment and joy.\n\nThose with true knowledge and devotion always keep their thoughts filled with the contemplation of Bhagavān. Their mind becomes wholly imbued with him; they see Bhagavān in everything. They live with the awareness that Bhagavān stands behind every event, and that everything unfolds according to his will. Their mind stays in harmony with Bhagavān. They speak of Bhagavān constantly, examining every single matter as though marveling, \"How wondrous is Bhagavān's creation!\" They accept every event of their life as Bhagavān's own prasāda. The life of such people is filled with bliss. No evil thought stirs within them; their mind remains ever pure. Setting aside worldly talk, they remain ever joyful, speaking only of Bhagavān.",
  "10.10": "teṣāṃ satatayuktānāṃ bhajatāṃ prītipūrvakam |\ndadāmi buddhiyogaṃ taṃ yena mām upayānti te -- To those ever united with me, who worship me with love, I grant that yoga of the intellect by which they come to me.\n\nIn this verse, the word \"yukti\" occurring in \"satatayuktānām\" carries many meanings in Sanskrit. Living with the utmost righteous discipline, without straying or slipping from the path, is yukti. Grasping the study of the Veda that supports the contemplation of Bhagavān is yukti. Fixing the mind in Bhagavān is yukti. Here Kṛṣṇa says: \"I myself grant the intellect by which reaching Bhagavān becomes possible, the mind that contemplates on such matters, the discernment that one must contemplate with devotion, and the certain knowledge.\" When one surrenders to Bhagavān and serves him with love, Bhagavān himself reveals the path by which he is reached.",
  "10.11": "teṣām evānukampārtham aham ajñānajaṃ tamaḥ |\nnāśayāmy ātmabhāvastho jñānadīpena bhāsvatā -- Out of compassion for them alone, dwelling within their own being, I destroy, with the shining lamp of knowledge, the darkness born of ignorance.\n\nBhagavān redeems us not for any selfish purpose of his own, but out of compassion. It is out of tender regard for the devotee who worships him with single-minded devotion that he redeems us. He does not merely give us intellect; he is also the one who removes the veil of tamas (ignorance) that ever covers our intellect. Without Bhagavān's help we cannot ourselves remove the veil of ignorance covering our mind and intellect. Only Bhagavān, who is beyond the three guṇas, can dispel this darkness. Through this we can grasp reality as it truly is. Bhagavān fills our disposition and devotion, awakens our true nature, casts his gracious glance upon us, gives us light in every way, and enables us to see reality. For this we must surrender to him, saying: \"Bhagavān, dispel the darkness within me.\" However much we study while harboring darkness within, it is of no use. To grasp the true meaning when we read scripture, surrender to Bhagavān is essential.\n\nAt this point Arjuna, listening to Kṛṣṇa's teaching, becomes utterly absorbed. He forgets even that he is standing on a battlefield, and begins to see none other than Kṛṣṇa alone. In the four verses that follow, we see Arjuna give voice to his contemplation of the supremacy of Bhagavān's infinite power.",
  "10.12": "Arjuna uvāca --\nparaṃ brahma paraṃ dhāma pavitraṃ paramaṃ bhavān |\npuruṣaṃ śāśvataṃ divyam ādidevam ajaṃ vibhum || -- \"You are the supreme Brahman, the supreme abode, and the supreme purifier. You are the eternal, divine Person, the primeval God, unborn and all-pervading.\"\n\nThis is Arjuna's exclamation after hearing of Bhagavān's glories. \"Paraṃ brahma\" -- the supreme truth. \"Ādidevam\" -- the God of the gods. \"Aja\" -- unborn. \"Vibhu\" -- all-pervading. According to the Mādhva tradition, \"Paraṃ brahma\" here means Nārāyaṇa.",
  "10.13": "āhus tvām ṛsayaḥ sarve devarṣir nāradas tathā |\nasito devalo vyāsaḥ svayaṃ caiva bravīṣi me -- O worshipful one, you are the highest principle beyond the highest, the greatest refuge, the purifier even of the pure. All the sages who know you praise you as the one full of every quality, the supreme Person, the unchanging, ever engaged in divine play, the very first among all the gods, unborn, and abiding everywhere in manifold forms. The divine sage Nārada, Asita and Devala, and the sage Vyāsa have said so; and you yourself now tell me this.\n\nHere Arjuna addresses Kṛṣṇa: \"You are Paraṃbrahma, Paraṃdhāma, the supremely pure.\" That which is greater than everything is Brahma. The word \"brahma\" is used in many other senses too, and so, for clarity, he says \"Paraṃbrahma\" here. The undertone of this address is: \"You, who are greater than everything and who raise up those who have faith in you, are complete. To see the fullness of my own being, I need your grace.\" Then, \"Paraṃ dhāma\": \"dhāma\" means refuge. There is no single thing in this world that does not take refuge in Bhagavān. \"You are wholly independent, and all of us are subject to you; you are the giver of refuge, and we are the sheltered\" -- in this sense Arjuna calls Kṛṣṇa \"Paraṃdhāma.\" The tattvābhimāni deities give us refuge, each in their own form; but Bhagavān, who is the giver of refuge even to such deities, is Paraṃdhāma. Bhagavān, who redeems every living being throughout their endless journey by being their refuge, is the refuge of all refuges.\n\nBhagavān is \"Paramapavitra\" -- supremely pure. We worship such a Bhagavān through the symbol of fire, itself the emblem of purity. Fire is itself pure and never becomes soiled; whatever touches it, fire purifies of its impurity. Bhagavān is the supreme power that purifies both us and the entire world.\n\nHere Arjuna, transcending all his physical relationships, beholds that Parabrahman standing before him. \"I am full of impurity, full of envy and intolerance. By your grace, by taking refuge in you, I must become pure. Through you I must rise to that height and attain my own completeness\" -- this longing lies within Arjuna's address.\n\nSometimes we speak in exaggerated terms out of excitement. Here Arjuna makes certain that he is not speaking out of mere excitement. This is a very important point for a seeker in the worship of God, because whatever conception we form of God is merely our own imagination, not direct realization. Our imagination is shaped by the state of our own mind. When the mind is filled with enthusiasm, all manner of new fancies arise; not all of them need be true. Thus each person's imagination is only as great as their own intellectual capacity -- it need not match how Bhagavān actually is. For this reason, Arjuna here makes sure that what has come to him is not merely a fancy of his own intellect but the actual state of things. He recalls here what the sages and the divine sage Nārada have said.\n\nArjuna says: \"These great souls have declared you to be Puruṣaḥ, Śāśvataḥ, Divyaḥ, Ādidevaḥ, Ajaḥ, Vibhuḥ.\" In each of these words used here lies hidden one of Bhagavān's infinite qualities. We find all these words as names of Bhagavān in the Viṣṇusahasranāma. Come, let us briefly contemplate the meanings of these words:\n\nPuruṣaḥ: Here \"puruṣa\" does not mean \"male.\" One who dwells in the \"pura\" (city/body) is \"puruṣaḥ.\" The Puruṣa-sūkta, the crown jewel among all the essence-bearing hymns of the Vedas, calls Bhagavān \"sahasra-śīrṣā puruṣaḥ.\" Bhagavān, who existed even when nothing else existed at the time of creation, and who built this creation, is Puruṣaḥ. Bhagavān, who burned away everything at the time of dissolution, is Pura-uṣa. Bhagavān, who fills this universe infinitely, through infinite time, with infinite capacity, and infinite qualities, is Pura-saha. Existing when nothing existed at the time of dissolution, and at the time of creation bringing forth, one by one from his own navel, the elements of creation -- Bhagavān first created \"Mahattattva.\" Mahattattva means the awakening of the collective consciousness of the entire universe. Having given the memory of all past creation to Brahmā-Vāyu, the presiding deities of citta, he built the world in subtle form. Then came the creation of the gods and the five elements. Bhagavān filled himself within this gross universe thus created. Entering as the reflected image within each individual body (piṇḍāṇḍa), in one form or another, causing that body to perform its appointed task, and granting mokṣa -- such a Bhagavān is Puruṣaḥ. Thus, before creation, at the time of creation, within the created object, in the pursuit of that object, and from that pursuit all the way to liberation -- everywhere present, dwelling in the center of the heart-lotus, Bhagavān is complete with the six full attributes, of the nature of knowledge and bliss. Pu-ru-ṣaḥ -- puruṣa: here \"pu\" means the supremely pure one who purifies us. \"Ru\" means \"ruvanti\" -- the one denoted by every word in the world. \"Ṣaḥ,\" or \"saha,\" means the principle that indwells and pervades every single thing. Thus the name \"Puruṣaḥ\" is a name that contemplates Bhagavān's qualities in many ways at once.\n\nŚāśvataḥ: Bhagavān, of the nature of bliss, undergoing no change whatsoever across beginningless, endless time, ever eternal, is Śāśvataḥ.\n\nDivyaḥ: The word \"divya\" has many meanings. Fundamentally, the words \"divya\" and \"deva\" derive from the root \"div.\" In the ancient lexicon of roots, this root carries seven meanings: 1) dyuti (radiance), 2) vijigīṣā (the wish to conquer), 3) kānti (loveliness/will), 4) stuti (praise), 5) vyavahāra (transaction), 6) krīḍā (play), 7) gatiśu (motion/knowledge).\n\n1) Dyuti: means the nature of light. Bhagavān, who gives light to the very sources of light such as the sun and moon, fills us within with the light of knowledge.\n\n2) Vijigīṣā: Bhagavān stands higher than all, and is himself of the nature of victory.\n\n3) Kānti: One who can create by mere will alone. He who gave us will, and who fulfills it according to each one's own worthiness.\n\n4) Stuti: One praised by all; he whom everyone praises is Bhagavān, denoted by every word.\n\n5) Vyavahāra: One who conducts every transaction of the world.\n\n6) Krīḍā: Creation, sustenance, and dissolution are but a game (krīḍā) to Bhagavān.\n\n7) Gatiśu: One who gives motion and knowledge. One who has gone everywhere, who knows everything, who dwells within everyone as the reflected image, is \"divya\" or \"deva.\"\n\nĀdidevaḥ: One for whom the creation, sustenance, and dissolution of this world are but play is Ādidevaḥ. Bhagavān exists both when this universe exists and when it does not. He stands higher than all. He conducts every transaction of the world at every time. Thus, dwelling within everything and conducting all activity, Bhagavān is Ādidevaḥ.\n\nAjaḥ: \"na jāyate iti ajaḥ\" -- \"ja\" means birth; \"a-jaḥ\" -- ajaḥ -- means one who is not born. Caturmukha, born from the navel of the unborn one, is called \"Aja.\" Bhagavān, present within such a Caturmukha and creating through him, himself unborn, creating and destroying everything -- is Ajaḥ. All are Bhagavān's children, yet he is born of no one, for birth and death do not apply to him. In another sense, \"ajaḥ\" also means one who pervades everywhere. A-ja-Ajaḥ: here \"a\" means \"not\" or \"no\" -- that is, he is not any object that we know of. There is no flaw whatsoever in him. In yet another sense, \"ajaḥ\" means one who is born of no one -- and yet is born within everyone! You say this is contradictory? Indeed -- Bhagavān is not an object born from someone else. But dwelling as the reflected image within every womb, he is born, at one and the same time, in countless forms. Bhagavān, present as the reflected image within a pregnant woman's womb, takes birth.\n\nVibhuḥ: \"vividha-bhavati\" -- meaning one who assumes infinite forms of manifold kinds. Bhagavān, all-capable, filling the entire universe everywhere, is Vibhuḥ.\n\nHere Arjuna specially mentions the names of the divine sage Nārada, Asita-Devala, and Vyāsa, because Arjuna had seen them himself, and had heard them speak of Bhagavān. We know that Nārada is a supremely learned sage. In the hierarchy of the gods, he occupies the fourteenth tier. He is the sage of the divine realm who came and gave his blessing when Bhagavān's foremost worship took place in Dharmarāya's court. Nārada, who destroys the ignorance present in human society and gives the knowledge of Bhagavān, is the deity of intuition who reveals the truth to us. When our ājñā-cakra is awakened, we attain the vision of Nārada. It is Nārada who answers, through inner insight, what should and should not be done. Asita and Devala were sages of the Vedic age, seers of mantras. Arjuna had seen them come to Dharmarāya's court and give him instruction in dharma. As for Vyāsa: the avatāra of Bhagavān who divided the Veda and gave us the eighteen Purāṇas, the Mahābhārata, and the fifth Veda. \"What all these great souls have said, that is exactly what you are telling me,\" says Arjuna. Thus Arjuna makes certain that the experience coming to him is not a mere illusion.",
  "10.14": "sarvam etad ṛtaṃ manye yan māṃ vadasi keśava |\nna hi te bhagavan vyaktiṃ vidur devā na dānavāḥ -- O Keśava, I believe all this that you tell me is true. O Bhagavān, neither the gods nor the demons know the manner of your manifestation.\n\n\"I know that there is no exaggeration whatsoever in what you say. Neither the gods nor the demons know the manifestation of your own awareness,\" says Arjuna. The gods have not been able to measure the full extent of Bhagavān's qualities; the demons have not succeeded in finding any flaw in him, because Bhagavān is infinitely full of qualities and has no flaw whatsoever. To understand him completely is impossible. Only he can speak of himself; no one else can describe him. Setting aside understanding Bhagavān in full, it is difficult even to fully understand a single avatāra of his, or even a single līlā within that avatāra. We cannot understand the manner in which Kṛṣṇa conducted himself in his own avatāra; it appears strange to us. Coming to know him is not so easy a task. Behind each and every one of his acts lies a message.",
  "10.15": "svayam evātmanātmānaṃ vettha tvaṃ puruṣottama |\nbhūtabhāvana bhūteśa devadeva jagatpate ||\nvaktum arhasy aśeṣeṇa divyā hy ātmavibhūtayaḥ |\nyābhir vibhūtibhir lokān imāṃs tvaṃ vyāpya tiṣṭhasi -- O Puruṣottama, you alone, by your own power, know yourself. O creator of all beings, O lord of all beings, O God of gods, O lord of the world -- you alone should describe, without omission, the divine glories of your own being, by which forms you stand pervading all these worlds.\n\nArjuna says: \"Your measure of qualities cannot be gauged even by the gods. Therefore you alone know about yourself. It must be told by you yourself, and by no one else, for you are Puruṣottama.\" The epithet \"Puruṣottama\" is used here. Puruṣottama means one standing beyond kṣara (the perishable jīva), greater even than akṣara (the imperishable Śrī-tattva). This address reveals that Arjuna expresses the sentiment: \"Besides you, who stand beyond both kṣara and akṣara, there is no other principle greater than you. Therefore this explanation must be known from you.\"\n\nHere Arjuna addresses Kṛṣṇa as Bhūtabhāvana, Bhūteśa, Devadeva, and Jagatpate. Bhūtabhāvana means: one who created the universe made of the five elements, and who is the cause of the creation, sustenance, and dissolution of every class of living being. Bhūteśa means: the all-regulating Lord who governs all. Devadeva means: one who is God even to all the gods; the primal light that gives light even to the sun and the other luminaries. In \"jagatpati,\" \"pati\" means protector. Bhagavān is the one who gives birth, and Bhagavān is the one who brings death. He alone gives the mokṣa free of birth and death. He is the protector of the entire world. When Bhagavān gives us \"saṃsāra-bandha\" (worldly bondage), that too is but one facet of his protection and his discipline. In sum, through this Arjuna expresses the sentiment: \"Without you, we are nothing; you alone are the protector of all.\"",
  "10.16": "[In Bannañje's commentary, verses 10.15 and 10.16 are explained together. The explanation is contained entirely within the commentary on verse 10.15.]",
  "10.17": "kathaṃ vidyām aham yogin tvāṃ sadā paricintayan |\nkeṣu keṣu ca bhāveṣu cintyo'si bhagavan mayā -- O all-capable one, how may I come to know you, ever contemplating you in manifold ways? O Bhagavān, in which and which objects should I contemplate you?\n\nBehind every distinct quality present in every single object of this world lies hidden Bhagavān's own vibhūti. Here Arjuna prays to Bhagavān: \"You, who are all-capable, know where and in what form you exist -- I do not. When I see any object, the memory of the divine power behind it must arise in me. I must come to know in which and which objects I may worship you.\"",
  "10.18": "vistareṇātmano yogaṃ vibhūtiṃ ca janārdana |\nbhūyaḥ kathaya tṛptir hi śṛṇvato nāsti me'mṛtam -- O Janārdana, tell me again, at greater length, your own yogic power and your glory, for I never find satisfaction in this nectar, however much I hear of it.\n\n\"Tell me still more of your vibhūti,\" Arjuna asks Kṛṣṇa. Bhagavān's vibhūti is chiefly of four kinds: (1) Sākṣāt-vibhūti: here Bhagavān appears to us on earth in a distinct, direct form -- for example, Bhagavān's own avatāras. (2) Vijātīya-śreṣṭha-prabha: for example, the moon, king of the stars. Here the moon does not belong to the class of the stars, yet he is their king. (3) Svajātīya-śreṣṭha-prabha: for example, Indra among the gods, the sun among light-bearing things. (4) Svajātīya-ekadeśīya-śreṣṭha-prabha: for example, Arjuna among the Pāṇḍavas. Thus, in these four ways, Bhagavān's forms of vibhūti are infinite. We cannot worship all of them here. For this reason Arjuna asks Kṛṣṇa to describe, at length, his vibhūti in a manner conducive to our worship.\n\nArjuna, having heard Kṛṣṇa's message, floats now upon an ocean of bliss. He says: \"I am drinking nectar, and here there is no saying 'enough.'\" This is a matter that grants mokṣa, that is a delight to the ears.",
  "10.19": "Bhagavān uvāca -- The Lord said:\nhanta te kathayiṣyāmi divyā hy ātmavibhūtayaḥ |\nprādhānyataḥ kurusreṣṭha nāsty anto vistarasya me -- Well then! O best of the Kurus, I shall tell you some of my chief divine forms of glory. There is no end to my expanse.\n\nIn Sanskrit, the word \"hanta\" is used to express delight and wonder. Seeing Arjuna's keen interest, Kṛṣṇa praises him: \"Well done!\"\n\nToday no one wishes to hear Vedānta. Most people slip away saying \"we have no time.\" Looked at psychologically, \"no time\" is a phrase used to conceal the fact that one simply has no interest! If we truly wish for something, time is found. Here Kṛṣṇa praises Arjuna's eagerness and says: \"I will certainly tell you what you have desired to hear.\" Bhagavān's vibhūti is divine, and it is infinite; it cannot be told in its full extent. Here Kṛṣṇa gives us, in condensed form, the chief vibhūtis of Bhagavān that we must primarily worship.\n\nHere the epithet \"Kuruśreṣṭha\" is used. Seekers who have engaged themselves on the path of Bhagavān's worship are \"Kurus.\" This is the minimum qualification needed for spirituality. The sentiment behind this address is: \"You have risen high in the practice of spirituality. You have earnest devotion to this practice. For that reason I shall certainly tell you.\"",
  "10.20": "aham ātmā guḍākeśa sarvabhūtāśayasthitaḥ |\naham ādiś ca madhyaṃ ca bhūtānām anta eva ca -- O Guḍākeśa, I am the ātman, the abode of every quality, dwelling in the heart of every being. I am the beginning, the middle, and the very end of all beings.\n\nThe overall essence of vibhūti is this: \"Bhagavān fills within everyone.\" Kṛṣṇa says: \"I fill, as the ātman, within every single being. I am the ātman of all living beings.\" Some mistakenly understand this verse to mean that \"jīvātman and Paramātman are one.\" But what Kṛṣṇa says here is that \"I fill as the ātman of jīvātmans,\" not \"I myself am the jīvātman.\" This describes the reflected-form Bhagavān who dwells as antaryāmī within every single jīva. Bhagavān is \"sarvabhūtāśayasthitaḥ\" -- he stands as the indwelling spirit in the cave of the heart of every class of living being, dwelling in the center of the heart-lotus. This is Bhagavān's greatest vibhūti. Bhagavān manifests in infinite forms of vibhūti within infinite jīvas. For this reason we see, in each and every living being, a distinct excellence of power. Bhagavān, standing as the regulator within every living being even before its birth, being the cause of its creation, protecting it, and preserving everything even at the time of dissolution -- is the power that stands within and protects in every state.\n\nHere the epithet \"Guḍākeśa\" is used. This too is an indication of vibhūti. \"Guḍāka\" means sleep. \"Guḍākeśa\" means one who has conquered sleep. Arjuna possessed this special capacity, and for that reason he was called by this name. The undertone of this address is: \"I too am the power filling you as the one who conquers sleep within you.\" In the rest of this chapter Kṛṣṇa goes on to describe his own distinctive vibhūti.",
  "10.21": "ādityānām ahaṃ viṣṇuḥ jyotiṣāṃ ravir aṃśumān |\nmarīciḥ marutām asmi nakṣatrāṇām ahaṃ śaśī -- Among the twelve sons of Aditi, I am Viṣṇu [named so since I spread and fill everything]. Among the sources of light, I am Ravi, the greatest, radiant with rays [ravaḥ from \"ravaḥ\"-sound and \"i\"-knowability -- as \"ravi\" I dwell in the sun]. Among the descendants of the Maruts, I am Marīci. Among the stars, I am the moon, lord of the stars.\n\nDescribing his own vibhūtis, Kṛṣṇa first tells us the svarūpa-vibhūti most essential to our daily worship. Among the twelve sons of Aditi (the dvādaśādityas -- Vivasvān (Sūrya), Aryamā, Pūṣā, Tvaṣṭā, Savitā, Bhaga, Dhātā, Vidhāta, Varuṇa, Mitra, Indra, and Viṣṇu in the form of Vāmana), one is Bhagavān's own direct vibhūti -- namely Vāmana-rūpa Viṣṇu himself. For this reason Kṛṣṇa says: \"Among the twelve ādityas, I am Viṣṇu.\" \"Ādityas\" also carries the meaning of all the gods together; among all the gods, the greatest of gods, \"Viṣṇu,\" is that very Bhagavān.\n\nEvery single name occurring in this chapter is a vibhūti-name of Bhagavān. Hence behind each name lies a rich etymological tradition. The first vibhūti-name occurring in this verse is \"Viṣṇuḥ.\" Let us briefly see what this name conveys. Looking at the Purāṇic sense given in the Purāṇas and the Mahābhārata, the word \"Viṣṇuḥ\" is derived from three roots: (1) viṣal -- vyāpto (to pervade), (2) viśati -- praveśe (to enter), and (3) veti -- prajanayati, gati, kānti, kādaneṣu, gacchati. Taken in the sense of these roots -- Viṣṇuḥ means one who fills everywhere (vyāpta), entering both outside and inside of everything -- because he fills thus, he is Viṣṇuḥ. The Nārāyaṇa-sūkta says this as: \"antar bahiśca tat sarvaṃ vyāpya nārāyaṇa sthitaḥ.\" Then, \"veti iti -- viṣṇuḥ\": he in whom all take refuge is Viṣṇu, the giver of refuge to all. \"Veti-prajanayati\" -- one who created the entire universe is Viṣṇu. \"Veti-gati\" -- Bhagavān, the protector of all, who protects everyone, is Viṣṇu. \"Veti-kānti (icchā)\" -- one whose true resolve is to protect and redeem the entire world is Viṣṇu. \"Veti-kādaneṣu\" -- one who destroys the entire world is Viṣṇu. \"Veti-gacchati\" -- one who, as Trivikrama, pervades the three worlds in three strides is Viṣṇu. Taken all together, \"Viṣṇu\" gives the meaning \"God.\" These are some of the meanings we must contemplate through the word \"Viṣṇuḥ.\" This is stated at one place in the Mahābhārata in the etymological explanation of names -- an explanation given by Kṛṣṇa himself to Arjuna in the Bhārata. In sum, Kṛṣṇa has here explained his own svarūpa-vibhūti as: \"I am Viṣṇu -- the refuge of all, the cause of the creation, sustenance, and dissolution of all, filling both inside and outside of everything, who measured the three worlds in three strides.\"\n\nHaving spoken of his svarūpa-vibhūti, Kṛṣṇa next explains his vibhūti in \"Sūrya,\" the greatest symbol for the worship of Viṣṇu. We worship the Viṣṇu who is the object of the Gāyatrī in the sun. This is the worship of Bhagavān in solar energy. This is the second tier of Bhagavān's vibhūti. For this reason, after Viṣṇu, Kṛṣṇa mentions \"Ravi.\" The sun is, among all luminaries visible to our eyes, the greatest luminary. Bhagavān, filling the sun as \"Ravi,\" pervades the universe as solar energy.\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Raviḥ.\" Rava-i -- one known through \"rava\" (sound) -- is Ravi. \"Rava\" means sound. He who is knowable through the Gāyatrī \"rava\" is Raviḥ -- Bhagavān, who gives light to every source of light, is present, as the object of the Gāyatrī, named \"Ravi,\" within the sun and within the sun's rays.\n\nThe next vibhūti Kṛṣṇa mentions is Marīci, of the lineage of the Maruts who regulate the different layers of the atmosphere in the sky. Fundamentally there are forty-nine Maruts, of whom ten regulate our own individual body (piṇḍāṇḍa). The one who regulates these Maruts is Pravaha-vāyu. Nowhere in the Purāṇas is a name \"Marīci\" found among these forty-nine Maruts. So it is difficult to say that this Marīci is one of these forty-nine. But it is clear that he is the son of Pravaha-vāyu. Reconciling Mādhvācārya's commentary on the Aitareya Upaniṣad with the Gītā-tātparya, Marīci is the Bhūta-vāyu deity in the eighteenth tier. He is thus of the same rank as the deity presiding over space (Gaṇapati). Bhagavān, named Marīci, stands as vibhūti within the presiding deity of Bhūta-vāyu.\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Marīci.\" \"Mara\" means water. \"Mari\" means a cloud full of water. Bhagavān, present within the wind that carries this cloud wherever it should go, giving Marīci the power to bring rain, and seated within him in vibhūti form, is \"Marīci.\"\n\nHaving spoken of his vibhūti in the sun and the wind in the sky, Kṛṣṇa next explains his vibhūti in the moon, king of the stars. This is the vijātīya-śreṣṭha-prabha vibhūti. The moon, lord of the stars: Bhagavān, denoted by the word \"śaśi,\" sits within the moon as the power that regulates all the stars, and gave that power to it.\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Śāśiḥ.\" One possessing \"śaśa\" is \"śaśi.\" \"Śa\" means bliss. \"Śaśa\" means the bliss of bliss -- supreme bliss. Bhagavān, possessing such supreme bliss, sat within the moon as the very embodiment of bliss. For this reason moonlight gives us bliss.",
  "10.22": "vedānāṃ sāmavedaḥ asmi devānām asmi vāsavaḥ |\nindriyāṇāṃ manaś cāsmi bhūtānām asmi cetanā -- Among the Vedas, I am the Sāmaveda [being \"Sāma,\" equal everywhere, and of the nature of Vedic knowledge, I am called \"Sāmaveda\" and dwell in it]. Among the gods, I am Indra [Since I dwell in every abode (vāsa), I am called \"Vāsava\" and dwell in Devendra]. Among the senses, I am the mind [Since I give awareness, I am called \"manas\" and dwell in the mind]. I am the sentience (caitanya) of living beings [Since I loosen the knot of memory of every living being (\"cetas\"-\"nā\"), I am called \"cetanā\" and dwell in the power of sentience].\n\nHaving spoken of his vibhūti in the sky -- the sun, the moon, the stars, the wind -- Kṛṣṇa now speaks of the special quality of the sky, sound. The vibhūti of sound is the Veda. Among the Vedas, the special Veda is the Sāmaveda. You may well ask why, though all the Vedas are equally wondrous, the Sāma is specially mentioned. There is no hierarchy in the Veda, but there is a hierarchy among those who preside over the Veda. The presiding deity of the Sāmaveda is Prāṇadeva (Vāyu). As scripture states, it is Prāṇadeva who sings the Sāma-song in the sweetest manner of all. The Sāma-song of Prāṇadeva, \"Sāmagāna,\" is exceedingly dear to Bhagavān.\n\nThe distinguishing feature of the Sāmaveda is \"nāda\" (musical sound). However a word is broken apart, however it is recited word by word, whatever tone is filled into it, the Sāmaveda is the practice by which it becomes the naming of Bhagavān. While the Ṛgveda and Yajurveda praise Bhagavān through mantras and words, the Sāmaveda has the rare, distinctive quality (unique quality) of reconciling nāda, together with word, into Bhagavān. Since the Veda that contemplates Bhagavān through the sound behind words is the Sāmaveda, Bhagavān is specially present within Sāma. Therefore \"Sāma\" is a fitting name of Bhagavān. If the Ṛgveda shows that all words are directed toward Bhagavān, the Sāmaveda shows that not only words but all sounds whatsoever speak of Bhagavān.\n\nBhagavān is \"Sāmaḥ\" and he is \"Vedaḥ.\" Here \"Sāmaḥ\" means one who is equal everywhere. \"Vedaḥ\" means of the nature of knowledge. Bhagavān, filling the Sāmaveda as nāda, made it his vibhūti.\n\nAfter the Veda comes the deities who are the subject of the Veda. Chief among the deities are the tattvābhimāni deities. Among the deities who, as the seat of Bhagavān, regulate the senses, the chief is \"Vāsava\" (Indra). He is the greatest among the deities presiding over the external senses. He is especially distinguished among the five jñānendriyas (eye, ear, nose, tongue, skin) and the five karmendriyas (hands, feet, mouth, anus, genitals) -- most notably in the hand.\n\nBhagavān, dwelling within us, sat as the very power of grasping that turns what the mind perceives into awareness -- that receives its object. Through this the power to grasp the world came to the jiva. Bhagavān, the wonder who grasps and pervades the entire universe through his own awareness, is \"Cetanaḥ.\" \"Cetas\" means our subconscious. Every single experience of ours is stored in our subconscious. Bhagavān, named \"Cetanaḥ,\" who gave citta the power to grasp everything, is this vibhūti of Bhagavān.",
  "10.23": "rudrāṇāṃ śaṅkaraś cāsmi vitteśaḥ yakṣarakṣasām |\nvasūnāṃ pāvakaś cāsmi meruḥ śikhariṇām aham -- Among the Rudras [eleven of them], I am the chief, śaṅkara [Since I bestow (kara) happiness (śaṃ), I am called \"Śāṅkara\" and dwell in śaṅkara]. Among the lords of the Yakṣas and Rākṣasas, I am Kubera [Since I am lord (\"īśa\") of wealth (\"vitta\"), I am called \"Vitteśa\" and dwell in Kubera, giving him lordship over the Yakṣas and Rākṣasas]. Among the [eight] Vasus, I am Agni [Since I purify, I am called \"Pāvaka\" and dwell in fire]. Among the towering mountains, I am Meru [Since no other impels me (\"īru\"-\"mā\"), I am called \"Meru\" and dwell in Mount Meru].\n\nHaving spoken of his vibhūti in the mind, Kṛṣṇa now explains his vibhūti in Śiva, the deity presiding over the mind. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Among the Rudras, I am Śāṅkara.\" The Rudras are eleven in number: Raivata, Oja, Bhava, Bhīma, Vāmadeva, Ugra, Vṛṣākapi, Ajaikapāt, Ahirbudhnya, Virūpākṣa, and Mahādeva as the foremost among them. (Each of these eleven Rudras has many further names; different sources mention different names at different places.) The \"Rudra\" who regulates our mind is Mahādeva or Śiva. His name is Śaṅkara. \"Rudra\" carries one meaning as \"rodayati iti rudraḥ\" -- one who gives sorrow. Bhagavān gives sorrow only to the wicked. In another sense: \"rujaṃ drāvayati iti rudraḥ\" -- one who removes our disease and gives happiness. Disease is fundamentally mental. If our mind is firm, sickness does not come. If sickness does come, and our mind remains firm that we can face it, the mind itself can cure that disease.\n\n\"Śāṅkara\" means one who gives happiness and joy. A healthy body is itself a very great happiness in life. A healthy body is needed to pursue any meritorious deed. \"Rudra\" is the deity of the physical body (śarīra-puruṣa). For this body to stand and move, the power of Śiva as the body's presiding deity within is the cause. Rudra sits within our mind, dispels mental turmoil, gives a healthy body, and grants bliss. In the Ṛgveda's hymn to Śiva, Śiva is praised as \"bhiṣaktamāṃ tvā bhiṣajam\" -- meaning \"you are the physician of physicians.\" For this reason Śiva is also called \"Vaidyanātha.\" There are many reasons why Rudra is called Mahādeva. Here \"deva\" means indriya (devaṃ tīti devam indriyam). Śiva is the greatest of all the deities presiding over the senses. In the hierarchy of the gods, while ten of the eleven Rudras occupy the eighteenth tier, Śiva stands far higher, in the fifth tier. Śiva is a very great power who assists Vāyudeva as a component in Bhagavān's act of dissolution. Bhagavān stands within Śiva named \"Śāṅkara.\" It is the Rudra-deity who leads us onto the path of Bhagavān's worship, and readies our mind for the direct realization of Bhagavān. For this reason, this is a very great vibhūti of Bhagavān.\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Śāṅkaraḥ.\" \"Śaṃ\" means happiness, \"kara\" means one who bestows. Being himself of the nature of bliss, denouncing this sorrowful worldly existence, and granting us liberation -- such is Bhagavān, \"Śāṅkaraḥ.\"\n\nHaving spoken of Śiva, the presiding deity of our inner wealth, Kṛṣṇa next explains his vibhūti in Kubera, who gives us external wealth. Kubera presides over our body and over external wealth. Under his administration are the Yakṣas and Rākṣasas. Yakṣas are those who mercilessly devour human flesh. Rākṣasas are somewhat better than Yakṣas, but they too are cruel. We must protect ourselves from them.\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Vitteśaḥ.\" \"Vitta\" means wealth, \"īśa\" means lord. Bhagavān, named \"Vitteśa,\" dwelt within Kubera, gave him lordship over the Yakṣas and Rākṣasas, and made him the lord of external wealth. This is Bhagavān's special vibhūti in Kubera.\n\nWhen we consider the classes of deities that occur in the Veda, the Āditya, Vasu, and Rudra groups always occur together. The offering made to Bhagavān in the three daily sacrifices (savana) is likewise offered to the Bhagavān present in this group of deities. To the Bhagavān present among the eight Vasus, the offering in the morning savana is given through the eight-syllabled Gāyatrī mantra. The midday savana pertains to the eleven Rudras; for this reason the eleven-syllabled \"Triṣṭup\" is used there. The evening savana pertains to the twelve Ādityas; for this reason the twelve-syllabled \"Jagatī\" is used there. Kṛṣṇa has spoken of the Ādityas and the Rudras. Now, speaking of the Vasus, he says: \"Among the Vasus, I am Agni.\" There are eight Vasus: Droṇa, Prāṇa, Dhruva, Arka, Agni, Doṣa, Vastu, and Vibhāvasu -- each with many further names. The fifth, Agni, bears the further names Vaiśvānara, Vahni, Jātavedas, Hutāśana, Pāvaka, Anala, and Dahana. While the other Vasus occupy the eighteenth tier in the hierarchy of the gods, Agni stands higher, in the fifteenth tier. Fire is the foremost symbol for worshipping Bhagavān on the earthly plane. Bhagavān, the power that purifies the entire universe, sat within Agni named \"Pāvakaḥ\" and gave him this exalted station.\n\nBhagavān's radiance is present within fire. Within that radiance is the power of light, the power to give heat, and also the power to burn. In addition, this radiance holds the power of digestion. Whatever comes into contact with fire becomes purified. Bhagavān, who dwelt within Agni and gave Agni this radiance, is Pāvakaḥ, who purifies the world. Bhagavān, who purifies all, is the protector (avaka) even of the deities.\n\nHaving spoken of Kubera, lord of wealth, Kṛṣṇa next explains his vibhūti in Mount Meru, the earthly seat of wealth. Mount Meru is called Hemādri (the golden mountain). It is the abode of the Vasus. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Among mountains, I am Meru.\" Swāmi Rāma has shared, in his own book, his personal experience of the wealth -- unknown to ordinary humans -- found within the mountain range of the Himalayas. The Mahābhārata tells us that when Dharmarāya's entire treasury was destroyed in war, he had wealth hidden among the mountain ranges brought forth and performed his yajña. This matter is known even today to certain yogis who perform tapas in the Himalayas. Meru is such a seat of wealth.\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Meruḥ.\" Mā-īru-Meru. Bhagavān stands as the impeller even of Mother Mahālakṣmī, greatest among all sentient beings. There is no other who impels such a Bhagavān. For this reason \"Meruḥ\" is a fitting name of Bhagavān.",
  "10.24": "purodhasāṃ ca mukhyaṃ māṃ viddhi pārtha bṛhaspatim |\nsenānīnām ahaṃ skandaḥ sarasām asmi sāgaraḥ -- O Pārtha, know me as Bṛhaspati, greatest of household priests [Since I am lord (\"pati\") over the great (\"bṛhat\"), I dwell in \"Bṛhaspati\"]. Among generals, I am Skanda [Having drained (skanna) the vigor of foes, I am called \"Skanda\" and dwell in the general Skanda]. Among reservoirs of water, I am the ocean [I am called \"Sāgara\" since I take in (gara) the essence (sā), and I dwell in the ocean].\n\nHaving told us the symbol of the Bhagavān we worship, Kṛṣṇa now speaks of the purohitas (priests), essential above all else. Kṛṣṇa says: \"In the priests is my presence.\" As the first hymn of the Ṛgveda says: \"agnimīḷe purohitaṃ yajñasya devam ṛtvijam, hotāraṃ ratnadhātamam...\" The \"hotā\" spoken of here is the Ṛgveda's priest. In earlier times, sixteen priests would be present when performing a yajña. For the Ṛgveda: the \"Hotā\" is chief, with three assistants (Maitrāvaruṇa, Acchāvāka, Grāvastut). For the Yajurveda: the Adhvaryu is chief, with three assistants (Pratiprasthātā, Neṣṭā, Unnetā). For the Sāmaveda: the Udgātā is chief, with three assistants (Prastotā, Pratihartā, Subrahmaṇya). Overseeing all of them, ensuring no defect whatsoever occurs in the yajña, is the priest versed in all the Vedas, called \"Brahmā\" -- he too has three assistants (Brāhmaṇācchāṃsi, Agnīdhra, Potā). Thus sixteen priests, each carrying Bhagavān's special presence, together conducted the yajña. The position of the priest was once exceedingly exalted in society. Today's priests, however, do not command the same standing in society as before. Priests are those who, knowing beforehand what is good for us even before we ourselves know it, correct us and guide us onto that path. They are advisors in the position of a guru, giving us the foresight to act in ways conducive to our own progress, and guiding us so that we may flourish. They are guides to the entire society. Priesthood is a very responsible task. When doubt arises over a religious observance, the priest's responsibility as guide to society is very great. If any lapse or error occurs while reciting a mantra, the priest is held responsible for it. Such is the responsible work of priesthood. Hence Bhagavān's special presence is there. Two priests on this earth are greater than all others: Śukrācārya, priest of the daityas, and Bṛhaspatiācārya, priest of the gods. Bṛhaspati occupies the tenth tier in the hierarchy of the gods. He is the advisor to the gods and to Devendra in the divine realm. In him, as priest to the gods, Bhagavān has a special presence. Indra, who occupies the eighth tier, bows to Bṛhaspatiācārya, who occupies the tenth tier, and follows his guidance -- and the reason for this is precisely the presence of Bhagavān within Bṛhaspati. To remind us of this, Kṛṣṇa here addresses Arjuna, the avatāra of Devendra, as \"Pārtha.\"\n\nSeen from another angle, in the Vedas \"Bṛhaspati\" means Prāṇadeva. Bṛhaspati is the husband of Sarasvatī-Bhāratī -- these are none other than Brahmā-Vāyu. They are the priests to all the gods. Bhagavān resides within Prāṇadeva as the special vibhūti of gāna (the glory of voice).\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Bṛhaspati.\" \"Bṛhat\" means the great, \"pati\" means lord. Bhagavān, lord over Brahmā and all the gods, and priest even to the priests, is \"Bṛhaspati.\" Having spoken of the priests, spiritual warriors, Kṛṣṇa now explains his special vibhūti among the warriors who bring victory and protection in battle. When the wealth the gods had earned under Bṛhaspatiācārya's guidance was seized by the asuras, it was Skandaḥ who fought against them and won it back. Bhagavān, named \"Skandaḥ,\" stands within the general and leads him. Bhagavān is the antaryāmī principle seated within, who gave Skanda, as commander of the army, the power to destroy the demon Tārakāsura. When speaking of generals, we may recall Karnataka's own celebrated son who won fame as a general, \"Kāriyappa.\" It is by Bhagavān's special presence that such special power comes to a commander.\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Skandaḥ.\" \"Skandaḥ\" means the power that destroys the entire world. \"Having drained the vigor of enemies, I am called 'Skanda' and dwell in the general Skanda,\" Kṛṣṇa has said. Continuing, Kṛṣṇa says: \"sarasām asmi sāgaraḥ.\" If Mount Meru is the site of the earth's boundless wealth, the ocean is the greatest of all bodies of water rich in essence (rasa). \"Within it, I am present as vibhūti,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Wherever on earth one digs a well, one finds sweet water. Yet the water of the boundless ocean is salty. We do not know why. The famous Russian psychologist Velikovsky says in his book \"Worlds in Collision\": \"We are under the illusion that we have unraveled all the wonders of the universe, but we know nothing.\" Even today we do not know why seawater is salty and well-water sweet. Bhagavān placed every other essence within fruit -- but the essence of essences, salt, he placed nowhere in any fruit, but in the water of the ocean alone. The ocean is a wonder. Greater diversity than the diversity of creatures on land exists within the ocean. Bhagavān, having created the ocean, fills it, denoted by the word \"Sāgaraḥ.\"\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Sāgaraḥ.\" Sāra-gara-Sāgara. Bhagavān, who accepts what is essence, is \"Sāraḥ.\" Bhagavān, who fills salt -- the greatest of all essences -- is \"Sāgaraḥ.\" \"Sāraṃ garati dadātīti sāgaraḥ\" -- Bhagavān, who gives the world the very essence and totality of life, is \"Sāgaraḥ.\"",
  "10.25": "maharṣīṇāṃ bhṛgur ahaṃ girām asmy ekam akṣaram |\nyajñānāṃ japayajño'smi sthāvarāṇāṃ himālayaḥ -- Among the great sages, I am Bhṛgu [Having scorched away the wicked, I am called \"Bhṛgu\" and dwell in the sage Bhṛgu]. Among words, I am the single imperishable syllable Oṃ [being, above all, the \"One\" and, being imperishable, the \"Akṣara,\" I am denoted by Oṃkāra]. Among sacrifices, I am japa-yajña [Since I nourish (pa) everything that is born (ja), I am called \"japa\"; and since I receive everyone's every worship, I am called \"yajña\" -- I dwell in japa-yajña]. Among immovable things, I am the Himālaya [Since I am the abode (\"ālaya\") of Śrī in her form as \"Hrī\" and as \"Mā,\" I am \"Himālaya\" and dwell in the snowy mountain].\n\nWe earlier saw the divine sage Bṛhaspati, who is connected to the divine realm. Setting him aside and looking at the other great sages, Bhṛgu Maharṣi is the foremost among the distinguished seers of Bhagavān. While Viśvāmitra and Vasiṣṭha occupy the sixteenth tier in the hierarchy, Bhṛgu-muni stands higher, in the fifteenth tier, equal to Agni. He is one who made a new discovery among the ranks of sages on this earth. According to a story in the Bhāgavata, Bhṛgu was the great sage sent as arbiter to determine which of the Trimūrti was greatest, and it was he who proclaimed to the world that \"Nārāyaṇa is supreme.\" As the story tells it, as chief arbiter, Bhṛgu first went to Śiva, then to Brahmā. Receiving no honor there, Bhṛgu went, in anger, to Nārāyaṇa. There, when Viṣṇu too failed to notice him, Bhṛgu, in fury, kicked Viṣṇu in the chest. Then Nārāyaṇa, ever devoted to his devotees, said: \"Had you called me, I would have come. From kicking me this way, how much your foot must have hurt!\" -- and began to rub his foot. Bhṛgu, realizing his mistake, then surrendered to Nārāyaṇa. Seeing this story, we might ask whether these sages did not know Bhagavān's greatness. Certainly they knew Bhagavān's full greatness. This was merely an act performed to convey the matter to ordinary people through action. It is likewise Bhṛgu who cursed Bhagavān: \"May you take ten incarnations on this earth.\" To honor that curse, Bhagavān took ten incarnations on earth. Lakṣmī was born as Bhārgavī, a daughter to Bhṛgu-muni. Thus we see many distinctive things about Bhṛgu. The reason for all of this is Bhagavān's special presence within Bhṛgu.\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Bhṛguḥ.\" \"Bharjanāt iti bhṛguḥ\" -- Bhagavān, who scorches everything at the time of dissolution, and who chiefly scorches away the sins of his devotees, is Bhṛguḥ. Bhagavān, who scorches away the wicked forces that obstruct the lives of his devotees, who scorches the wicked and unrighteousness, and establishes dharma, is denoted by the word Bhṛguḥ. Making Bhṛgu attain the very highest station among sages, Bhagavān is present within him, denoted by the name Bhṛgu.\n\nSpeaking of sages, the question arises for us: how did the sages come so close to Bhagavān? The reason for this is upāsanā and success in mantra-japa. Even Viśvāmitra, a kṣatriya, became a Brahmarṣi through success in the japa of Vedic mantras. To become a sage, to attain ṛshitva, one must attain the direct realization of Bhagavān through the worship of Oṃkāra. The most exalted and greatest word in existence is Oṃkāra. Bhagavān, named \"Akṣaraḥ,\" has a special presence within this Oṃkāra. Oṃkāra is Bhagavān's exclusive name. [Why Kṛṣṇa gave Oṃkāra such importance, and what the extraordinary distinctiveness of Oṃkāra is, we have already seen in detail in the seventh chapter (Ch. 7, verse 8).] Bhagavān, ever indestructible (a-kṣara), is the sole principle greater than all. Hence he is Ekaḥ.\n\nAfter Oṃkāra, Kṛṣṇa here explains japa-yajña. Kṛṣṇa says: \"yajñānām japayajño'smi.\" The greatest of all yajñas is japa-yajña. As said before, worship performed only through the face of fire is not the only yajña. Giving, gathering together for good works such as worship (saṃgatīkaraṇa), giving knowledge, giving food, sharing our surplus with those who lack it and living so -- all of these are yajña. Generally there are two kinds of yajña: inner yajña and outer yajña. Inner: the continuous worship of Bhagavān (devapūjā); uniting our citta and mind with Bhagavān (saṃgatīkaraṇa); and offering ourselves to Bhagavān -- this is the perfect mānasa-yajña. Such japa-yajña performed in the mind is supremely excellent. The reason for this is Bhagavān's vibhūti present within japa-yajña.\n\nThe vibhūti-names of Bhagavān occurring here are \"Japaḥ\" and \"Yajñaḥ.\" Bhagavān, who sustains every single living being born into this world, is \"Japaḥ.\" Such a Bhagavān, since he receives everyone's every act of worship, is called \"Yajña\" and dwells within japa-yajña.\n\nHaving spoken of japa, Kṛṣṇa now speaks of the greatest immovable place on earth bearing sāttvika vibration. In former times, when building a house, people would construct the shrine-room at the spot with the greatest sāttvika vibration. In a town, temples were built at the spot with the greatest sāttvika vibration. On this earth, the one place with the highest sāttvika vibration is the Himālaya. Wherever one sits here, our mind is very easily attuned to Bhagavān. This power came to the Himālaya through Bhagavān's special presence. Bhagavān resides here, named \"Himālayaḥ.\" This vibhūti-name of Bhagavān carries a special meaning. Hrīmā-ālaya-Himālaya. \"Hrī\" means Śrī Lakṣmī, \"Mā\" means Bhūdevi. Bhagavān, the abode of Śrīdevi and Bhūdevi, is Himālayaḥ. Through this special vibhūti of Bhagavān, every particle of the Himālaya is filled with spiritual wonder. The Himālaya has the special power to transform a person's mind and turn them toward spirituality. The Himālaya is the abode of Vedavyāsa, Nara-Nārāyaṇa, and Ācārya Madhva. It is the mysterious abode of many deities. Bhagavān's vibhūti has brought this wondrous power to this land.",
  "10.26": "aśvatthaḥ sarvavṛkṣāṇāṃ devarṣīṇāṃ ca nāradaḥ |\ngandharvāṇāṃ citrarathaḥ siddhānāṃ kapilo muniḥ -- Among all trees, I am the Peepal [dwelling in the form of a horse (aśva), I am called \"Aśvattha\" and dwell in the Peepal tree]. Among the sages of the divine realm, I am Nārada [Since I give (da) all that men (nāra) desire, I am called \"Nārada\" and dwell in Nārada]. Among the Gandharvas, I am Citraratha, their king [Since I travel (ratha) in a wondrous (citra) chariot, I am called \"Citraratha\" and dwell in the Gandharva king Citraratha]. Among the perfected siddhas, I am the sage Kapila [being of the nature of bliss (\"ka\"), while sustaining (\"pi\") and dissolving (\"la\") all].\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"Among all trees, I am the Aśvattha.\" Seen superficially, the Peepal tree today seems useless for anything. Had Bhagavān not given this tree such importance, we would know nothing at all about it. This tree gives us no edible fruit, no fragrant flower. Furniture cannot be made from this tree. Growing widely and encroaching on its surrounding area, this tree seems, on the surface, only a nuisance to people! Yet here Kṛṣṇa says: \"Among the community of trees, the Aśvattha is the foremost tree.\" Not knowing why this tree has such prominence, some of us worship it today, while others neglect it. There are those who, believing that planting this tree brings merit, plant the Peepal somewhere far from home, at a temple. Today most people do not know its importance. In earlier times, people gave great importance to three trees: the banyan, the Peepal, and the Atti (fig). [Bendāvare recalls Bannãñje quoting the poet Bendṛe, who, seeing these three trees sprung up in his own garden, said with a full heart: \"Bhagavān has come into our garden in three forms.\"] In earlier times, when performing yajña, people churned Śami and Araṇi (Peepal) sticks together to strike a spark of fire and create Agni. The Peepal stick is chiefly used in yajña. When the Aśvattha's wood is used as fuel in yajña, a distinctive power emanates in the fire's blazing flame. Just as the sun's rays hold seven colors, so does fire hold seven colors. The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad names the seven colors emanating from the flame of fire as Kālī, Karālī, Manojavā, Sulohitā, Sudhūmravarṇā, Sphulinginī, and Viśvarucī. Yajña is precisely the reconciling of these seven colors of fire with the seven colors of sunlight, bringing their effect into the atmosphere. The Aśvattha stick is a crucial means for this process.\n\nToday many people circumambulate the Aśvattha tree, believing that the presence of a deity resides within it. When couples are childless, astrologers advise them to circumambulate the Aśvattha. People who have no notion of why this is so dismiss all of it as mere superstition. The Aśvattha is the tree that gives more oxygen than any other tree in the world. Science today accepts that breathing such pure air may resolve certain womb-related defects. In earlier times, sages sat at the foot of the Aśvattha tree to study the Vedas. Thus Bhagavān's special vibhūti within the Aśvattha tree has given that tree many such special powers.\n\nThe name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Aśvatthaḥ.\" Haya-grīva is Bhagavān's special avatāra who taught the Veda, having the form of a horse. Bhagavān, who imparted the teaching of the Veda while having the form of a horse (aśva), is called \"Aśvatthaḥ.\"\n\nContinuing, Kṛṣṇa says: \"devarṣīṇāṃ ca nāradaḥ.\" Nārada is one of the Gandharvas. But through Bhagavān's special presence, he rose to a great height and became a divine sage (devarṣi). Nārada attained Bhagavān through the greatest sādhana. In an earlier Brahma-kalpa, Nārada was born into a poor family. His mother lived by working in the āśrama of sages and munis. As a small child, he grew up listening to the spiritual conversations of the sages. When he was five years old, his mother died from a snakebite. As the wise say: \"So that we know God alone is our nātha (master/support), Bhagavān first makes us anātha (masterless).\" Thus orphaned, from his fifth year he began sādhana with the knowledge he had gained from the sages. Yet he did not attain the vision of Bhagavān in that birth. But he \"heard\" the voice of Bhagavān: \"Do not try to see me in this birth. In the next kalpa you will see me always.\" Later, he was born as Nārada in this Brahma-kalpa and became the recipient of Bhagavān's great grace.\n\nNārada is the presiding deity of our intuition. As we see in stories of former times, whenever some good work was to happen, Nārada would come, speak, and go. For example, it was Nārada who told Vālmīki to write the Rāmāyaṇa. Here a question arises for us: why does Nārada not come now, why do we not receive guidance? If we call Nārada, he will certainly come even today! \"Nārada\" means our own inner voice. If we lend our ear to the call of our own ātman, Nārada speaks there. If we practice listening to our own inner message, our ājñā-cakra opens. Through this comes the message \"do this\" -- a divine message. This is precisely the disembodied voice, the inner voice. This is Nārada. Though he cannot be seen directly, he can be heard. All of Nārada's distinctive power came from Bhagavān's special presence.\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Nāradaḥ.\" Bhagavān, who removes the ignorance of men (nāra-dhyati) and pours out to them what they desire (nāra-dadāti), is Nāradaḥ.\n\nAfter Nārada, Kṛṣṇa explains his special vibhūti among the Gandharvas. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Among the Gandharvas, I am Citraratha.\" The Gandharvas are a class of sub-deities who attend upon the divine realm. They are minor deities. The king of this group is Citraratha. As the saying goes, \"the king is a visible deity\" -- Bhagavān is present as special vibhūti within Citraratha. The vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Citrathaḥ.\" Bhagavān, possessing a wondrous chariot, is Citrarathaḥ. Bhagavān's vehicle is Garuḍa, distinctive above every other vehicle. Bhagavān, who travels in such a chariot, is Citrarathaḥ.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"siddhānāṃ kapilo muniḥ.\" Two sages named Kapila occur in history. Both of them taught Sāṅkhya. But one of them did not believe in God. So the Kapila-muni referred to here is Kapila Vāsudeva -- the direct avatāra of Bhagavān who originally gave Sāṅkhya to the world. He is Bhagavān's avatāra born of the union of Devahūti, daughter of Svāyambhuva Manu, and Kardama Prajāpati. Vedavyāsa too has presented before us exactly what Kapila taught. Kapila-avatāra is the avatāra of Bhagavān that gave the world something new.\n\nThe vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Kapilaḥ.\" Ka-pi-la: Bhagavān, who sustains (pi) the entire world and dissolves it (la), of the nature of bliss (ka), is Kapila. Bhagavān, who ever drinks bliss, and who through the wondrous Sāṅkhya unveiled the secret of the universe, is \"Kapilaḥ.\"\n\nHaving spoken of the ṛshi, Brahmarṣi, devarṣi, and maharṣi -- of brahma-tejas and of the Vedic study, tapas, and yajña they perform -- Kṛṣṇa, in the next verse, speaks of kṣātra-tejas (martial radiance).",
  "10.27": "uccaiḥśravasam aśvānāṃ viddhi mām amṛtodbhavam |\nairāvataṃ gajendrāṇāṃ narāṇāṃ ca narādhipam -- Among horses, know me as Ucchaiḥśravas, born from the nectar [in the churning of the ocean; Since I attained supreme fame, I am called \"Ucchaiḥśrava\" and dwell in Ucchaiḥśravas]. Among great elephants, I am Airāvata [Since I am the protector of \"Airā\" -- Lakṣmī -- I am called \"Airāvata\" and dwell in Airāvata]. Among men, I am the king [Since I am lord over all men, I am called \"Narādhipa\" and dwell in the king].\n\nIn former times, kings used horses and elephants as chief components of their army. In this verse Kṛṣṇa speaks of his special vibhūti in the horse, the elephant, and the king. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Know me as the horse 'Ucchaiḥśravas' and the great elephant 'Airāvata,' born together with the nectar in the churning of the ocean.\" These two creatures are exceedingly special creatures. Horses and elephants are creatures renowned for loyalty to their master. They never betray the master who lovingly raises them. Among men, the greatest is \"Narādhipa.\" Bhagavān has a special presence among leaders who stand as heads of humankind, and who, righteous themselves, protect their people. We may recall here kings of former times such as Dharmarāja and Parīkṣit, who ruled with such righteousness.\n\nThe vibhūti-names of Bhagavān occurring here are \"Ucchaiḥśravas,\" \"Airāvataḥ,\" and \"Narādhipaḥ.\" \"Śravas\" means fame, knowledge, and action. Bhagavān, of far-spreading fame, supremely wise, forever performing this great work of creation, sustenance, and dissolution, is \"Ucchaiḥśravas.\" \"Airā\" means Lakṣmī as present within Bhūdevi. Bhagavān, the consort of such a Lakṣmī, is \"Airāvataḥ.\" Bhagavān, the lord over men, over every class of living being, and present within kings, is \"Narādhipaḥ.\"",
  "10.28": "āyudhānām ahaṃ vajraṃ dhenūnām asmi kāmadhuk |\nprajanaś cāsmi kandarpaḥ sarpāṇām asmi vāsukiḥ -- Among weapons, I am the thunderbolt [being free of all enemies, I am called \"Vajra\" and dwell in the thunderbolt]. Among cows, I am Kāmadhenu [Since I yield (dhuk) whatever is desired (kāma), I am called \"Kāmadhuk\" and dwell in Kāmadhenu]. I am the god of procreation, Kandarpa [Since I nourish (pa) manifold (dara) pleasures (kaṃ), I am called \"Kandarpa\" and dwell in the god of love, having given him the power of the pull of birth]. Among serpents, I am Vāsuki [Since I dwell everywhere (vāsu) and grant happiness (ki), I am called \"Vāsuki\" and dwell in Vāsuki].\n\nThe supreme weapon among all weapons is the Sudarśana, borne by Bhagavān himself. Setting aside Bhagavān's own weapon, the finest weapon is the Vajrāyudha, the thunderbolt. Kṛṣṇa says: \"Among weapons, I am the Vajrāyudha.\" This thunderbolt is Indra's weapon. Bhagavān gave it the power to control the three worlds. It was fashioned by invoking, on Bhagavān's own command, the complete fruit of the tapas of the sage Dadhīci, in order to destroy Vṛtra. The thunder and lightning seen when it rains is said to be the symbol of the Vajrāyudha. Bhagavān is present within this weapon, named \"Vajra.\" \"Varjanāt iti vajraḥ\" -- Bhagavān, who wards off (varjana) enemies and every fault, is Vajraḥ.\n\nHaving spoken of weapons, Kṛṣṇa next speaks of the Kāmadhenu of sage Vasiṣṭha, which destroyed all of Viśvāmitra's weapons. Among animals, the greatest animal is the cow (dhenu). The cow is Bhagavān's distinctive vibhūti, nourishing us with its milk our whole life long. For this reason Kṛṣṇa, even as a boy, established cow-worship. Not even the cow's dung and urine are to be discarded as waste. Skin diseases that heal from nothing else may be cured by bathing with cow-dung and mṛtikā (red clay). Sweeping cow-dung before one's house drives away insects and pests. There is a story in the Mahābhārata stating that Lakṣmī's presence resides in cow-dung. Thus, countless uses come from the cow. Bhagavān, named \"Kāmadhuk\" -- giving whatever is desired -- stood as vibhūti within Kāmadhenu.\n\nAmong the living beings of this world, that which stands as the pull of birth (sexual desire) between male and female is the god of procreation, Kāmadeva. Within this Kāmadeva (Kandarpa) lies Bhagavān's distinctive power. For the act of procreation to occur in creation, Bhagavān filled everyone with an irrepressible desire, and sat there. For this reason, without Bhagavān's grace, no one can freely escape this power. It cannot be forcibly abandoned. Bhagavān, named \"Kandarpaḥ,\" fills Kāmadeva. Bhagavān, who gives humans desires of many kinds, satisfies them through those desires, and sways them in the cradle of strange pleasures, is \"Kandarpaḥ.\"\n\nAfter Kāmadeva-Manmatha, Kṛṣṇa explains the serpent, symbol of desire. A reptile that moves by winding its belly is called a serpent. The king of all such reptiles is Vāsuki. Bhagavān, named \"Vāsukiḥ,\" stood within him and gave Vāsuki this station. There is no place where Bhagavān is absent. Bhagavān, who dwells everywhere, is \"Vāsukiḥ.\"",
  "10.29": "anantaś cāsmi nāgānāṃ varuṇo yādasām aham |\npitṛṇām aryamā cāsmi yamaḥ saṃyamatām aham -- Among the great serpents, I am Śeṣa [Being imperishable, I am called \"Ananta\" and dwell in Śeṣa]. I am Varuṇa, lord of aquatic creatures [Since I am great (\"var\") and of the nature of bliss (\"ṇa\"), I am called \"Varuṇa\" and dwell in Varuṇa, having given him lordship over aquatic creatures]. Among the pitṛs, I am Aryamā [Since I am both that which is to be known (\"arya\") and the one who knows (\"mā\"), I am called \"Aryaman\" and dwell in the Āditya named Aryamā, lord of the pitṛs]. Among those who punish, I am Yama [Since I regulate (niyama), I am called \"Yama\" and dwell in Yama, having given him the charge of punishing sinners].\n\nAmong the class of serpents, those with a hood are called \"Nāgas.\" Among the Nāgas, the greatest is Śeṣa. Śeṣa is the very great divine power who, as Balarāma, as Lakṣmaṇa, ever serves as Bhagavān's own bed. Śeṣa is Saṃkarṣaṇa, the earth's power of attraction (Gravity). For this reason it is said that Śeṣa bears the earth. Bhagavān has a special presence within such a Śeṣa. Bhagavān, who filled Śeṣa as the root power of all hooded serpents, ever-imperishable, reclining upon Śeṣa, is \"Anantaḥ.\"\n\n\"Among aquatic creatures, I am their lord, 'Varuṇa,'\" says Kṛṣṇa. Bhagavān filled Varuṇa, who gives life to many aquatic creatures within the covering of water, as his special vibhūti. The vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is Varuṇaḥ. Being of great, blissful nature, Bhagavān, dwelling within Varuṇa and giving him lordship over aquatic creatures, is \"Varuṇaḥ.\"\n\nThe divine power that regulates jivas who leave this earth having given up the body is the pitṛ-devatās. Bhagavān stood as their special power in their leader, Aryamā, one of the twelve Ādityas. \"Since I am that which is to be known (arya) and the one who knows (mā), I am called 'Aryaman,' dwelling in Aryamā, the Āditya who is lord of the pitṛs,\" says Kṛṣṇa.\n\nYama regulates jivas who have committed sin. Bhagavān has a special presence within Yama. Bhagavān, dwelling within Yama and regulating him, is \"Yamaḥ.\" In the science of yoga, the word \"yama\" is used in a distinctive sense: it names five rules of what we should not do -- (1) violence, (2) falsehood, (3) theft, (4) excessive desire, and (5) stretching out one's hand before another. Bhagavān, who gave us these five rules to be given up on the path of Bhagavān's worship, is \"Yamaḥ.\" Bhagavān, who regulates the entire world and regulates even Yama-Dharma himself, is \"Yamaḥ.\"",
  "10.30": "prahlādaś cāsmi daityānāṃ kālaḥ kalayatām aham |\nmṛgāṇāṃ ca mṛgeṃdro'haṃ vainateyaś ca pakṣiṇām -- Among the descendants of Diti, I am Prahlāda, the foremost [Since I am of the nature of the greatest bliss, I am called \"Prahlāda\" and dwell in Prahlāda]. Among reckoners, I am Time [Since I destroy, I am called \"Kāla\" and dwell in the deity of time]. Among beasts, I am the lion [Since I am lord (\"indra\") to devotees who seek Bhagavān (\"mṛga\"), I am called \"Mṛgendra\" and dwell in the lion]. Among birds, I am Garuḍa [Since I am the refuge of those who take shelter in me (\"vinata\"), I am called \"Vainateya\" and dwell in Garuḍa].\n\nBhagavān's presence is found among the good, and also among the wicked. It is Bhagavān who gives the daityas their strength. Generally daityas ruin the lives of others. But Prahlāda, born at the very origin of the daitya lineage, is different from all others. The reason for this is that Bhagavān had a special presence within him. Prahlāda, born into the daitya lineage, shed that very nature and rose to a great height. For this reason he is a special vibhūti even among daityas. Bhagavān's special grace was upon Prahlāda. While still in the womb, he had the good fortune to receive spiritual teaching through the divine sage Nārada. Like Dhruva, he was a seeker who beheld God at the age of five. Through Prahlāda, Bhagavān gave the world a very important message: \"Bhagavān is not fearsome; if one goes to Bhagavān with love, he regards us with just such love in turn. Worship of Bhagavān is not a transaction; we should not worship God merely to achieve some desire of ours; our desireless devotion is what pleases Bhagavān; by worshipping Bhagavān in this way we can remain ever blissful\" -- this truth Prahlāda showed the world. The vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Prahlādaḥ.\" Bhagavān, the complete embodiment of bliss, ever free of sorrow, is Prahlādaḥ.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"kālaḥ kalayatām aham.\" In this world, across beginningless, endless time, every single birth and death that occurs is predetermined. No one can change this. Here the word \"kāla\" carries many meanings: reckoning, death, Śiva, Durgā, kālāgni, and so on. Within all of these lies Bhagavān's special vibhūti. Bhagavān fills \"kāla,\" named \"Kālaḥ.\" \"Kāla\" is a word derived from the root \"kala.\" He who has gathered (kale) every quality, every capacity, within himself is Kālaḥ. Bhagavān is the principle perfectly complete with all good qualities. When, failing to recognize this principle, we become egotistical, that very principle becomes the \"kāla-puruṣa\" and is the cause of our destruction. Our redemption sometimes lies \"in life,\" sometimes \"in death\"; sometimes \"in honor,\" sometimes \"in dishonor\"; sometimes \"in knowledge\" our redemption lies, and sometimes \"in ignorance\" our redemption lies. But there is never redemption \"in ego.\" When the world becomes filled with ego-laden ignorance, Bhagavān rises and stands as the \"kāla-puruṣa.\" \"Kāla\" has another meaning: \"time.\" Behind every single action, \"time\" is the universal cause. Bhagavān, the regulator of time, is Kālaḥ.\n\nThrough his knowledge-bestowing avatāras (Vyāsa, Kapila, Dattātreya, Nara-Nārāyaṇa, and so on), Bhagavān is \"Kāla\" (destroyer) to ignorance; through his strength-bestowing avatāras (Naraśiṃha, Varāha, Rāma, Paraśurāma, and so on), he is \"Kāla\" to ego. Thus Bhagavān, complete with every quality, the destroyer of all, and the regulator of time, is Kālaḥ.\n\nHaving spoken of his vibhūti as destructive power, Kṛṣṇa now explains his vibhūti in the fiercest and mightiest of animals. The strongest and largest animal on earth is the elephant. But even such an elephant can be slain by the king of the forest, the lion, Mṛgendra. Bhagavān sat within the lion as the vibhūti that is called the foremost among beasts. The name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Mṛgendraḥ.\" Bhagavān, the supreme guide (mṛga) who stands as guide to those established in the sādhana for Bhagavān, is \"Mṛgendraḥ.\"\n\nAfter the animal, Bhagavān's vibhūti among birds. The greatest of all birds is \"Garuḍa.\" Garuḍa is a bird of the eagle family with a white-bellied form. Bhagavān's vehicle, Garuḍa, is the son of Vinatā; for this reason he is called Vainateya. Being the refuge of the devotees (vinata) who surrender to him, Bhagavān, called \"Vainateya,\" is present within Garuḍa. Garuḍa, who bears Bhagavān upon his shoulder and travels, is Bhagavān's special vibhūti.",
  "10.31": "pavanaḥ pavatām asmi rāmaḥ śastrabhṛtām aham |\nruṣāṇāṃ makaraś cāsmi srotasām asmi jāhnavī -- Among moving things, I am the greatest, the wind [Since I am to be sought (van) by the very gods who sustain the world (pam), I am called \"pavana\" and dwell in the wind]. Among warriors who fight with weapons, I am [since I am of the nature of bliss (ram), boundless (ama), and delight the world] renowned as Rāma. Among fish, I am the whale [Since I give (kara) understanding (ma), I am called \"Makara\" and dwell in the whale]. Among rivers, I am the Gaṅgā [Since I am the protector (avi) of those who have given up (ja) all desires, I am called \"Jāhnavī\" and dwell in the Gaṅgā].\n\nContinuing, Kṛṣṇa says: \"pavanaḥ pavatām asmi.\" The well-known meaning of the word \"pavana\" is \"wind\" or Vāyudeva. Among the powers that move through the sky, \"Vāyu\" is a great vibhūti. Bhagavān, specially present within Vāyudeva who regulates our very life, is Pavanaḥ. \"Pavate iti pavanaḥ\" -- meaning that which moves without ceasing. Bhagavān, indwelling within everyone, remains in each object in its own form and gives it constant motion. The instant his motion ceases within our body, we become a corpse (śava). In the Upaniṣads, Bhagavān is called \"Vanaḥ.\" He is \"Pa-vanaḥ\" -- here \"pa\" means \"protection\" -- the power that protects us and all the gods, from Brahmā downward. \"Vanaḥ\" means that which everyone must worship, that which must be adored with love, that which everyone must take refuge in. Bhagavān, in a thousand forms, dwelling within a thousand living beings, ever protecting them, is Pavanaḥ.\n\n\"Among warriors who take up arms and fight, I am Rāma,\" he explains, describing his own svarūpa-vibhūti. Kṛṣṇa... More than in any of Bhagavān's other avatāras, in the Rāma-avatāra he, bow in hand, made full use of weaponry and subdued his enemies.\n\nThe name \"Rāma\" carries many meanings in scripture. \"Rā-amaḥ\" means one of boundless, blissful nature who shares that bliss with all. This quality of Bhagavān is clearly evident in the Rāma-avatāra. In the Rāma-avatāra, Bhagavān never conducted himself in a way that caused anyone pain. Even when everyone was angry at Kaikeyī, the very cause of his being sent to the forest, Rāmacandra never once spoke a harsh word. Instead, he said: \"If I have committed any fault, forgive me,\" and went to the forest joyfully. This is the unparalleled avatāra that showed sacrifice for another's happiness. Bhagavān, husband of Sitā, lord of Rama (Lakṣmī), showed in this avatāra, in his own person, how the married life between man and woman should be, how the love between elder and younger brothers should be, how the bond between parents and children should be. This is the vibhūti of Bhagavān's boundless-bliss nature that gave the world such joy.\n\nAmong the fish living in water, the mightiest and largest in form is the whale. Kṛṣṇa says: \"ruṣāṇāṃ makaraŜ cāsmi.\" Here \"makara\" means the whale. Bhagavān, denoted by the word \"Makara,\" gave this power to the whale as his special vibhūti within it. Bhagavān, who gives (kara) understanding (ma), is Makaraḥ.\n\n\"Among rivers, I am the Gaṅgā,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Among rivers, the Gaṅgā is supreme. No other medicine equals this river's water. Bathing in the Gaṅgā can purify our mind. No impurity there attaches to us. The Gaṅgā's water never spoils even when stored. The vibhūti-name of Bhagavān, who gave the Gaṅgā this great power, is \"Jāhnavī.\" \"Jahnu\" means to renounce. Bhagavān, who protects those who, renouncing petty material desires, walk the path of Bhagavān, is \"Jāhnavī.\" This is a feminine symbolic name of Bhagavān. Bhagavān, who nurtures like a mother, is worshipped in feminine form as well. Jāhnavī is a feminine-form name of Bhagavān.",
  "10.32": "sargāṇām ādir antaś ca madhyaṃ caivāham arjuna |\nadhyātmavidyā vidyānāṃ vādaḥ pravadatām aham -- O Arjuna, I am the cause of the birth, death, and continuance of all beings. Among knowledge, I am the knowledge of adhyātma [Since I am lord over jivas, I am called \"Adhyātma,\" and, being of the nature of knowledge, \"Vidyā\" -- I dwell in adhyātma-vidyā]. Among the debates of disputants, I am reasoned discourse (vāda) [conducted for the sake of understanding truth; being denoted by every name, I am called \"Vāda\" and dwell in vāda].\n\nBhagavān's vibhūti lies hidden within every single object in this world. Even in an object that seems useless to us on the surface, there lies hidden a distinctive quality (Exclusive Quality) not found in any other object. Hence everything, from a single blade of grass to Caturmukha Brahmā, is Bhagavān's vibhūti. Here Kṛṣṇa has merely spoken of the distinctive vibhūti within that particular group. That Bhagavān existed before the jiva's birth, is the cause of the jiva's continued existence, and finally stands as the power of destruction. Thus Kṛṣṇa says: \"I am present at the birth, during existence, and at the end as well.\" In the sense that \"you, having attained knowledge on this matter, being sāttvika, already know this,\" Kṛṣṇa here specially addresses Pārtha as \"Arjuna.\"\n\n\"Among forms of knowledge, I am the knowledge of adhyātma,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Paramātman fills the adhyātma-vidyā that touches Bhagavān. Bhagavān, greater than all souls, is the \"adhika-ātmā\" (adhyātma). To attain adhyātma-vidyā, we must engage in dialogue with one another. This is the scholarly discussion undertaken to increase our understanding and quench our thirst for knowledge. Within such discussion, Bhagavān is present, denoted by the word \"vādaḥ.\" Bhagavān, denoted by every name, is Vādaḥ.",
  "10.33": "akṣarāṇām akāro'smi dvandvaḥ sāmāsikasya ca |\naham eva akṣayaḥ kālo dhātāham viśvatomukhaḥ -- Among letters, I am the first letter, \"a\" [Since I am called \"a,\" I am denoted by the letter \"a\" and dwell as \"akāra\"]. Among compound-groups, I am the dvandva-compound [Since I exist both inside and outside through two forms, I am called \"dvandva\" and dwell in the dvandva-compound]. I alone, imperishable, am [dwelling in time] the devourer of all. Filling everywhere, I alone am also the sustainer of all.\n\nHaving earlier spoken of Oṃkāra among words, Kṛṣṇa here explains his vibhūti in the syllable, saying: \"akṣarāṇām akāro'smi.\" \"A\" is the distinctive letter that fully expresses every one of Bhagavān's qualities. \"A\" means \"is not.\" Bhagavān \"is not\" -- meaning, no object known to us is him. He is unlike anything in the world. Such a Bhagavān \"has no\" flaw whatsoever. He is complete with every quality. Thus Bhagavān, wholly unlike anything, free of every flaw, complete with every quality, denoted by \"a,\" fills within the letter \"a.\"\n\nAfter the syllable, speaking of compound-forms, Kṛṣṇa says: \"dvandvaḥ sāmāsikasya.\" As we know, for any compound to form, two words are needed there (or one word and one suffix). In every other compound, one word, or both words (bahuvrīhi), may lose their own primacy. But in the dvandva-compound, both words remain equally important. In the dvandva-compound, where both words retain their primacy, Bhagavān fills it, named \"Dvandvaḥ.\" Bhagavān, who fills both within us and outside us in two forms, is Dvandvaḥ.\n\nBhagavān, the kāla-puruṣa who destroys all, is \"Dhātā\" -- one who sustains all and nourishes all. Such a Bhagavān is Viśvatomukhaḥ. He fills everywhere. There is no place where he is absent. There is no spot he does not see.",
  "10.34": "mṛtyuḥ sarvaharaś cāham udbhavaś ca bhaviṣyatām |\nkīrtiḥ śrīr vāk ca nārīṇāṃ smṛtiḥ medhā dhṛtiḥ kṣamā -- I am the death-deity who devours all [being the one who kills, I am named \"Mṛtyu,\" and dwell within Death, the deity of Yama's family, having given him the power to devour all]. I am also the source that brings forth those yet to be. Among women, I am the goddess of fame; I am Śrīdevī; I am the goddess of speech. I am the goddess of memory; I am the goddess of retentiveness; I am the goddess of endurance; I am the goddess of forgiveness [Being worthy of praise (kīrtanīya), I am called \"Kīrti\" and dwell in the goddess of fame; being the refuge of all, \"Śrī,\" I dwell in Śrīdevī; being the speaker, \"Vāk,\" I dwell in the goddess of speech; being that which is remembered, \"Smṛti,\" I dwell in the goddess of memory; being the very embodiment of understanding, \"Dhṛti,\" I dwell in the goddess of retentiveness; being the one who forgives faults, \"Kṣamā,\" I dwell in the goddess of forgiveness].\n\nBhagavān is also the death-deity who devours everything at the time of dissolution. It is he, too, who reconstructs creation anew after the dissolution. Speaking of his vibhūti in feminine form, Kṛṣṇa here explains his vibhūti chiefly within Lakṣmī, Sarasvatī, and Bhāratī. Fame, fortune, beautiful speech, memory, intellectual power, firmness, and patience -- the presiding goddesses of these are chiefly Lakṣmī, Sarasvatī, and Bhāratī. Within them, Bhagavān stands as vibhūti in feminine form.",
  "10.35": "bṛhatsāma tathā sāmnām gāyatrī chandasām aham |\nmāsānām mārgaśīrṣo'ham ṛtūnām kusumākaraḥ -- Among the songs of the Sāma, I am the song called Bṛhatsāma [Since I am great (\"bṛhat\"), the very essence (\"sa\"), and beyond the reach of understanding (\"ama\"), I am called \"Bṛhatsāma\" and dwell in this song]. Among the meters, I am the Gāyatrī [Since I protect (\"trī\") singers (\"gāya\"), I am called \"Gāyatrī\" and dwell in the Gāyatrī meter]. Among the months, I am Mārgaśīrṣa [Since I stand at the end of the path, I am called \"Mārgaśīrṣa\" and dwell in that month]. Among the seasons, I am spring [Since I give (\"kara\") right understanding (\"mā\") -- turning away from bad (\"ku\") toward good (\"su\") -- I am called \"Kusumākara\" and dwell in the spring season].\n\nThere are many kinds of Sāma-songs. Singing using the seven svaras occurs only in the Sāmaveda; this is absent in the Ṛgveda and Yajurveda. Not all Sāma-songs use the seven svaras. Among such seven-svara Sāmas, Kṛṣṇa says: \"I am the song called Bṛhatsāma.\" The name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Bṛhatsāmaḥ\" -- Bhagavān, who is great, of the nature of essence, and boundless beyond our understanding, is \"Bṛhatsāmaḥ.\"\n\nChandas (meter) is that which holds within a poetic form a rare meaning, a message of the spirit. It is the method by which our thoughts are expressed in rhythmic form. There are chiefly seven meters: the 24-syllable Gāyatrī, the 28-syllable Uṣṇik, the 32-syllable Anuṣṭup, the 36-syllable Bṛhatī, the 40-syllable Paṅkti, the 44-syllable Triṣṭup, and the 48-syllable Jagatī. Among these meters, the one holding Bhagavān's special vibhūti is \"Gāyatrī.\" This is also itself a name of Bhagavān.\n\n\"Gāyataṃ trāyate iti gāyatrīḥ\" -- whoever praises Bhagavān through this mantra, he protects. The Gāyatrī meter is the first among the seven meters. The Gāyatrī, of eight syllables in three feet, is like the very foundation (Foundation) of everything. Most mantras in the Ṛgveda are in the Gāyatrī meter. The Gāyatrī mantra, celebrated as the \"mother of the Vedas\" (vedamāta) among all the Vedas, exists in the \"Gāyatrī\" meter. Thus Gāyatrī holds a mother's position for the other meters. Gāyatrī is the mantra that holds the fundamental face of spirituality. It is that which channels the solar energy present in the sun to us, praising Bhagavān, the principal of our life, present within the solar system. The Gāyatrī is the mantra of collective contemplation that says: \"Bhagavān is the power that, regulating the entire universe, comes as solar energy, and impels the power of 'dhī' within our ātman, in our heart-lotus.\" Gāyatrī is used distinctly (distinctly) as a Vedic meter; it has no use in ordinary, worldly composition. This distinctive station of the Gāyatrī came from Bhagavān's special vibhūti. [We should know one matter here: the Aitareya Upaniṣad calls the Bṛhatī meter the greatest. The reason for this is: seen in the hierarchy of presiding deities, the presiding deities of the Gāyatrī are Lakṣmī, Sarasvatī, Bhāratī, Garuḍa, and Agni's consort Svāhā. Looking at the Bṛhatī meter, its presiding deities are Lakṣmī, Sarasvatī, Bhāratī, Garuḍa, and Tārā, consort of Bṛhaspati. Since Tārā stands higher than Agni's consort Svāhā in the hierarchy of the gods, keeping this hierarchy foremost in view, it was said thus there. When we know this, there is no confusion here.]\n\n\"Among the months I am Mārgaśīra, among the seasons, spring,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Mārgaśīra is the first among the months; Kārtika is the last month. For this reason, Dīpāvalī is celebrated in Kārtika (just before Mārgaśīra). Seeing off a year with gratitude is the significance of Dīpāvalī. Mārgaśīra is also called Agra-hāyaṇa. \"Hāyaṇa\" means month, \"agra\" means first. We celebrate Gītā-jayantī in this month. From this we may say that the month in which Kṛṣṇa gave the teaching of the Gītā to Arjuna was also Mārgaśīra (further research on this is needed). Seated as vibhūti within this month, the vibhūti-name of Bhagavān is \"Mārgaśīrṣa.\" Bhagavān, standing at the very end of the path we must travel on the path of sādhanā, is \"Mārgaśīrṣa.\" \"I am 'Kusumākaraḥ' in the spring season, when flowers bloom and trees and plants sprout anew,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Bhagavān, who gives base understanding to the wicked and the highest knowledge to the good, is \"Kusumākaraḥ.\"",
  "10.36": "dyūtaṃ chalayatām asmi tejas tejasvinām aham |\njayaḥ asmi vyavasāyaḥ asmi sattvaṃ sattvavatām aham -- I am the dice-game of cheats [Being of the nature of play, I am called \"Dyūta\" and dwell in gambling]. I am the vigor of heroes [Being of the nature of radiance, I am called \"Tejas\" and dwell in vigor]. I am victory. I am effort. I am the dignity of the great [Being the treasury of good qualities, I am called \"Sattva\" and dwell in the dignity of great men].\n\nThe visible cause of the war between the Kauravas and Pāṇḍavas was gambling -- the highest art in deceit! Bhagavān is present in gambling, named \"Dyūta.\" The name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Dyūta.\" \"Divu\" means play. Bhagavān, ever engaged in the play of creation, sustenance, and dissolution, is denoted by the word \"Dyūta.\" Bhagavān is the vigor of those possessing strength of soul. Named \"Jayaḥ,\" Bhagavān is present in victory. Bhagavān is the impeller of every victory in the world. Named \"Vyavasāyaḥ,\" Bhagavān is the one who makes us engage enthusiastically in any undertaking. As the regulator of strength, he sits within the strong, named \"Sattvaḥ.\"",
  "10.37": "vṛṣṭīnāṃ vāsudevo'smi pāṇḍavānāṃ dhanañjayaḥ |\nmunīnām apy ahaṃ vyāsaḥ kavīnām uśanā kaviḥ -- Among the royal Vṛṣṇi clan, I have incarnated as Vāsudeva [being called \"Vasu\" for encompassing and dwelling within all, and \"deva\" for being of divine form]. Among the Pāṇḍavas, I am Arjuna [Since I have won every treasure, I am called \"Dhanañjaya\" and dwell in Arjuna]. Among the sages, I am Vyāsa [Since I am, above all (\"vi\"), everywhere (\"ā\"), the one who fills (\"sa\")]. Among the wise, I am Uśanas, the great scholar [Being of the nature of will, I am called \"Uśanas\" and dwell in Śukra, guru of the daityas].\n\nSpeaking of his direct vibhūti, Kṛṣṇa says: \"vṛṣṭīnāṃ vāsudevo'smi.\" The vibhūti-name of Bhagavān occurring here is \"Vāsudevaḥ.\" Bhagavān, present everywhere yet unseen, who becomes directly visible by bestowing immediate knowledge, is Vāsudevaḥ. \"Among the Pāṇḍavas, I am 'Dhanañjayaḥ,'\" says Kṛṣṇa. This is a localized (ekadeśa) vibhūti. Bhagavān's special presence dwelt within Arjuna. Bhagavān stood within him named \"Dhanañjayaḥ.\" In \"Dhanañjaya,\" \"dhana\" means knowledge. Bhagavān's own awareness, and the mokṣa attained through it, is eternal wealth. Bhagavān, who gives this, is Dhanañjayaḥ.\n\n\"Among sages, Vyāsa\" -- this is Bhagavān's direct vibhūti. Vyāsa divided the Veda into 1137 saṃhitās and gave it to us through the wise. The Vedas are chiefly four: the Ṛgveda, the poetic anthology; the Yajurveda, the prose anthology; the Sāmaveda, for song; and the Atharvaṇa Veda, which the sage Atharvan received as teaching from Brahmā and wrote down. Fundamentally there are three Vedas, but through the tradition of sages, four Vedas came into being. Vedavyāsa taught this to four disciples (Paila, Vaiśampāyana, Sumantu, and Jaimini), and these four sages further divided the Veda into many branches (saṃhitās). The Ṛgveda has 24 saṃhitās; the Yajurveda has chiefly two branches -- one is the Śukla Yajurveda and the other the Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda. The Śukla Yajurveda has 15 saṃhitās, and the Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda has 86 saṃhitās -- 101 in total. The Sāmaveda has 1000 branches, a thousand kinds of musical tradition! But today only three (Jaiminīya, Rāṇāyanīya, and Kauthuma) singing traditions remain current. The Atharva Veda has 12 branches in total. Thus Vyāsa is the sage who divided and expanded the Veda into 1137 (24+101+12+1000) saṃhitās. Bhagavān, filling everywhere, who opened before us the very ocean of knowledge of the wondrous diversity of human nature and intellect, is Vyāsaḥ. Bhagavān stood within Śukrācārya, guru of the daityas, named \"Uśana.\" For this reason Śukra attained a station among the planets. Bhagavān, who accomplishes every task by will alone, is \"Uśana.\"",
  "10.38": "daṇḍaḥ damayatām asmi nītiḥ asmi jigīṣatām |\nmaunam caiva asmi guhyānāṃ jñānaṃ jñānavatām aham -- I am the rod of the chastisers [Since I chastise, I am called \"Daṇḍa\" and dwell in the science of chastisement]. I am the statecraft of those who seek victory [Since I lead toward victory, I am called \"Nīti\" and dwell in statecraft]. I am silence among secrets [Since I am praised by munis, I am called \"Mauna\" and dwell in silence]. I am the understanding of the wise [Being the very embodiment of understanding, I am called \"Jñāna\" and dwell in understanding].\n\n\"I am the power of chastisement that subdues the wicked,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Bhagavān sits among heroes as this power of chastisement. \"Daṇḍa\" means punishment. In Sanskrit, \"śikṣā\" (punishment/discipline) also carries the meaning of instruction. But here daṇḍa means punishment (Punishment). There is no forgiveness in the kingdom of God for wrongdoers. We must ourselves experience the karma we have performed. Bhagavān, who gives ruthless and stern punishment to wrongdoers, is \"Daṇḍaḥ.\" Bhagavān, as the power of victory who regulates all, is \"Nīti.\" Bhagavān, standing as the power of reflection, is \"Maunaḥ.\" Bhagavān, the understanding of the wise, is \"Jñānaḥ.\"\n\nFundamentally, the expanse of vibhūtis ends here. Let us see the summation of vibhūti in the remaining part of this chapter.",
  "10.39": "yac cāpi sarvabhūtānāṃ bījaṃ tad aham arjuna |\nna tad asti vinā yat syān mayā bhūtaṃ caram acaram -- O Arjuna, whatever distinctive quality any living being possesses, in all of them I myself am present, giving that power. Excepting me, there is nothing in this world, moving or unmoving, that exists of itself.\n\n\"I am the one who gives the manifesting form and power fundamental to every single object (inert or sentient) existing in this world,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Thus Bhagavān stands as the seed-person for the entirety of creation. He is the one who gives a visible body to the invisible jiva. Bhagavān, present within the male seed, joining the jiva through the seed into the woman's body, giving it a form there, and illuminating it -- this is Bhagavān. Thus Bhagavān is the one who gives birth to everything in this world, sentient and insentient, moving and unmoving. \"Apart from me, no object in this world exists independently,\" says Kṛṣṇa. This is the direct answer Kṛṣṇa gives to a confusion present in the Vedic tradition. The jiva and Bhagavān are not one. The jiva is the reflection of Bhagavān. Everything exists, but nothing has independent existence apart from Bhagavān.",
  "10.40": "nānto'sti mama divyānāṃ vibhūtīnāṃ parantapa |\neṣa tūddeśataḥ prokto vibhūter vistaro mayā -- O Parantapa, there is no end to my divine glories. I have told you here only a portion of the extent of my glory, by way of example.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"My vibhūti is present in every single object in this world. There is no end to telling of it.\" Bhagavān's vibhūti, unlike anything in this world, is endless. It cannot be told to completion. Here Kṛṣṇa has told us only what is most essential -- what we must worship continually, what we must keep in our daily contemplation.",
  "10.41": "yad yad vibhūti mat sattvaṃ śrīmad ūrjitam eva vā |\ntat tad evāvagaccha tvaṃ mama tejo'ṃśasambhavam -- Whatever is glorious, whatever is prosperous, whatever stands mightily above others in any group -- know all of it to have sprung from a mere spark of my splendor.\n\nIn sum, whatever distinctive quality exists in one person, in one object, that is not found in another, is a single spark of Bhagavān's own glory. It is a mere fragment of Bhagavān's infinite splendor.",
  "10.42": "atha vā bahunā etena kiṃ jñātena tava arjuna |\nviṣṭabhyāham idaṃ kṛtsnam ekāṃśena sthito jagat -- Or, O Arjuna, what use have you for all this detailed knowledge? Here is the essence: with a single fragment of myself, I stand pervading and sustaining this entire universe.\n\nTo know and speak of each and every thing separately is impossible. Nor is it necessary to do so. Bhagavān exists within everything as one distinctive power. Within everything lies hidden Bhagavān's own vibhūti. Kṛṣṇa says: \"I stand pervading the entire universe with a single fragment of my own capacity.\" As the Puruṣa-sūkta says:\n\npādo'sya viśvā bhūtāni | tripād asyāmṛtaṃ divi |\ntripād ūrdhva udait puruṣaḥ | pādo'syāihābhavat punaḥ |\ntato viṣṭvyaṅkrāmat | sāśanānāśane abhi |\n\nOnly one quarter of Bhagavān pervades this world. The remaining three-quarters, of the nature of nectar, rest in the divine realm (the realm of the liberated). \"Filling both inside and outside of every object in the world, I am of universal form,\" said Kṛṣṇa -- and with this, this chapter on Bhagavān's vibhūti comes to its conclusion.\n\nThus ends the tenth chapter.",
  "11.1": "arjuna uvāca --\n\nmad-anugrahāya paramaṁ guhyam adhyātma-saṁjñitam |\nyat tvayoktaṁ vacas tena moho 'yaṁ vigato mama --\n\nmat anugrahāya paramam guhyam adhyātma sañjñitam | yat tvayā uktam vacaḥ tena mohaḥ ayam vigataḥ mama -- To show grace upon me, you have spoken this supreme secret called adhyātma. By that utterance, this delusion of mine has gone.\n\nArjuna says: \"Out of grace toward me, you have told me this supreme secret matter called adhyātma.\" Here \"anugraha\" (grace) is the wish of elders to bring those younger than themselves onto the right path and raise them to a higher state. Arjuna is expressing the feeling: \"I was about to stray, and you brought me back to the right path and uplifted me.\" The word \"mat\" is used here. \"Mat\" does not mean merely \"of me\" or \"for me\". \"Mat\" functions like \"yat\". \"Mātīti mat\" -- meaning \"all seekers of knowledge\". Therefore \"mad-anugraha\" means \"for the upliftment of all seekers of knowledge.\" To deliver this rare message to all humankind, Bhagavān taught the Gītā to Arjuna, and Himself, in the form of Vyāsa, recorded it and gave it to us -- not merely for Arjuna's sake alone.\n\nIn this world, the knowledge that ought to be kept most secret is adhyātma-vidyā. A rare and precious matter is always kept confidential, because it should not be misused, and it should not reach those who do not understand its importance. Adhyātma is the science of the mind, the science of the soul (jīva), and the science of the Supreme (Paramātman). For having opened up such rare knowledge before him, Arjuna praises Bhagavān and says: \"By this, all my mental confusion has left and my delusion has cleared.\" ",
  "11.2": "bhavāpyayau hi bhūtānāṁ śrutau vistaraśo mayā |\ntvattaḥ kamala-patrākṣa māhātmyam api cāvyayam --\n\nbhava apyayau hi bhūtānām śrutau vistaraśaḥ mayā | tvattaḥ kamala-patra akṣa māhātmyam api ca avyayam -- I have heard from you, in detail, the coming-to-be and passing-away of beings; O lotus-petal-eyed one, I have also heard your imperishable glory.\n\nArjuna says: \"I have heard from you the detailed account of the arising and dissolution of beings.\" Here \"bhava\" and \"apyaya\" do not merely mean birth-death or creation-destruction; there is another, quite fascinating, meaning. The Vedic-age seers used these words in a distinct sense. The Upaniṣad speaks of \"prabhavāpyayau bhūtānām\" -- the attainment of happiness is \"bhava,\" and the cessation of sorrow is \"apyaya.\" This is the ancient seers' meaning. Arjuna is saying: \"You have told me, from your own mouth, the root formula by which happiness may be gained and by which one may be freed from the sorrow of life -- and I have heard it with my own ears.\"\n\nHere the epithet \"kamala-patrākṣa\" (lotus-petal-eyed) is used. There is something special in Arjuna's use of this word. Behind this epithet lies the feeling: \"You have poured upon me a stream of compassion, O Kṛṣṇa.\" \"Your glory is an imperishable, endless treasure. You have given me that great knowledge. You have told me of your glory, which is exceedingly hard to attain,\" says Arjuna.",
  "11.3": "evam etad yathātha tvam ātmānaṁ parameśvara |\ndraṣṭum icchāmi te rūpam aiśvaraṁ puruṣottama --\n\nevam etat yathā ātmā tvam ātmānam parameśvara | draṣṭum icchāmi te rūpam aiśvaram puruṣottama -- O Parameśvara, all that you have said of yourself is exactly so. O Puruṣottama, I long to see that sovereign form of yours which rules the universe.\n\nArjuna says: \"I have no doubt at all in what you have said.\" Here Arjuna addresses Kṛṣṇa as \"Parameśvaraḥ.\" Īśa means one who is capable/competent. The deities who govern us are the \"īśas.\" The inner faculty and the deities who preside over the vital force -- Garuḍa, Śeṣa, Rudra, and Brahma-Vāyu -- are the \"īśvaras.\" Bhagavān, who controls these presiding deities, standing higher than all, the master of all, is \"Parameśvaraḥ.\" \"I have no confusion about what you have said, because you are Parameśvaraḥ; but I am longing to see that form of yours filled with your total sovereign power,\" says Arjuna, placing before Kṛṣṇa his wish to \"behold Bhagavān's wondrous form.\"\n\nHere Arjuna addresses Bhagavān as \"Puruṣottamaḥ.\" Kṛṣṇa explains the special meaning of this word in detail in the fifteenth chapter (Ch. 15, verses 16-18). There are two kinds of \"puruṣa\" in the world -- kṣara-puruṣa and akṣara-puruṣa. All jīvas beginning with Brahmā are kṣara-puruṣas. The sentient Prakṛti (Śrī) that holds together this entire mass of the moving and unmoving is called Akṣara-puruṣa (Ch. 15, v. 17). He who transcends both of these is Puruṣottama, who is called Paramātman. That imperishable Parameśvara dwells within and sustains all three worlds (Ch. 15, v. 17). That Nārāyaṇa, who stands beyond the kṣara and is greater even than the akṣara, is \"Puruṣottama\" (Ch. 15, v. 18). \"I wish to behold that Puruṣottama-form of yours\" -- this is the longing Arjuna places before Kṛṣṇa.",
  "11.4": "manyase yadi tac chakyaṁ mayā draṣṭum iti prabho |\nyogeśvara tato me tvaṁ darśayātmānam avyayam --\n\nmanyase yadi tat śakyam mayā draṣṭum iti prabho | yogeśvara tataḥ me tvam darśaya ātmānam avyayam -- O Lord of the universe, O Lord of yogis, if you deem it possible for me to see it, then show me your imperishable self.\n\nArjuna continues: \"If you think it is possible for me to see that form of yours, then show me your form.\" Here Arjuna addresses Kṛṣṇa as \"Prabho.\" \"Prabhu\" means capability. Behind this address is the feeling: \"Though I lack the capability to see it, you have the capability to bestow that capability and show it to me.\" \"For you are Yogeśvara,\" says Arjuna. \"Yoga\" means the means/method. \"You alone know the means by which your form ought to be shown to me. You are the master of all means (yogas). Therefore show me that imperishable, eternal form of yours\" -- this is what Arjuna asks of Kṛṣṇa.",
  "11.5": "śrī-bhagavān uvāca --\n\npaśya me pārtha rūpāṇi śataśo 'tha sahasraśaḥ |\nnānā-vidhāni divyāni nānā-varṇākṛtīni ca --\n\npaśya me pārtha rūpāṇi śataśaḥ atha sahasraśaḥ | nānā vidhāni divyāni nānā varṇa ākṛtīni ca -- Behold, O Pārtha, my forms -- by hundreds and by thousands; of many kinds, divine, of many colours and shapes.\n\nWhere Arjuna asked in the singular, \"show me your form,\" Kṛṣṇa answers in the plural, \"behold my forms.\" All of Bhagavān's forms are one; there is no difference between the original form and the incarnate forms. \"Behold my hundreds and thousands of most wondrous forms, never seen by you before -- of many kinds, radiant, of many colours and shapes,\" Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna. Here Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as \"Pārtha.\" Those who, having understood the true meaning of the Veda, have set out on the journey toward Bhagavān who is the very substance of that meaning, are the \"pārthas.\"",
  "11.6": "paśyādityān vasūn rudrān aśvinau marutas tathā |\nbahūny adṛṣṭa-pūrvāṇi paśyāścaryāṇi bhārata --\n\npaśya ādityān vasūn rudrān aśvinau marutaḥ tathā | bahūni adṛṣṭa pūrvāṇi paśya āścaryāṇi bhārata -- Behold the Ādityas, the Vasus, the Rudras, the two Aśvins, and the Maruts. O Bhārata, behold many wonders never seen before.\n\n\"Behold all the Ādityas (the twelve Ādityas), the Vasus (the eight Vasus), the Rudras (the eleven Rudras), the Aśvins (the two), the Maruts (the forty-nine) -- behold them all. Behold the most wondrous marvel you have never before seen or heard of,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Here Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as \"Bhārata.\" In the Aitareya Brāhmaṇa there occurs the saying \"vāyur vāva bharataḥ\" -- Vāyu-deva, who is ever engrossed in Bhagavān, is called \"Bharata.\" Hence here \"Bhārata\" carries the meaning \"the younger brother of Bhīma (the presiding deity of prāṇa).\" [Devotion to Hanumān, or to the principle of prāṇa, who is ever nearest to Bhagavān, is an easy means of coming to know Bhagavān.]",
  "11.7": "ihaikasthaṁ jagat kṛtsnaṁ paśyādya sacarācaram |\nmama dehe guḍākeśa yac cānyad draṣṭum icchasi --\n\niha eka sthama jagat kṛtsnam paśya adya sa cara acaram | mama dehe guḍākeśa yat ca anyat draṣṭum icchasi -- Here now, behold, in my body, the entire universe together with all that moves and does not move. O Guḍākeśa, whatever else you wish to see -- behold all of it.\n\n\"Behold the entire universe, resting upon my body. Whatever exists within the cosmic egg is here -- sentient and insentient, moving and unmoving, all of it. Behold whatever your mind wishes to see,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Here Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna as \"Guḍākeśa.\" One who has conquered ignorance, sloth, and sleep is \"Guḍākeśa.\"\n\nHere, in each verse, a different epithet has been used. Looking at the meaning behind each of these epithets, what becomes clear is this -- for us to come to know Bhagavān, we too must become Pārtha, Bhārata, and Guḍākeśa. That is: the journey (Pārtha) undertaken having understood the true meaning of the Veda toward Him who is that very meaning; devotion to the principle of prāṇa (Bhārata); and conquering ignorance and sloth to contemplate Bhagavān (Guḍākeśa) -- when these are accomplished, that form of Bhagavān becomes known to us.",
  "11.8": "na tu māṁ śakyase draṣṭum anenaiva sva-cakṣuṣā |\ndivyaṁ dadāmi te cakṣuḥ paśya me yogam aiśvaram --\n\nna tu mām śakyase draṣṭum anena eva sva cakṣuṣā | divyam dadāmi te cakṣuḥ paśya me yogam aiśvaram -- But you are not able to see me with this eye of yours alone. I give you a divine eye; behold my sovereign power.\n\nBhagavān's form cannot be seen with this outer eye of ours. That Bhagavān, who is of the nature of one's own true self, can be seen only through the eye that is of the nature of one's own true self. Here Kṛṣṇa grants Arjuna the good fortune of beholding the all-capable Bhagavān through his own true-self eye.",
  "11.9": "sañjaya uvāca --\n\nevam uktvā tato rājan mahā-yogeśvaro hariḥ |\ndarśayām āsa pārthāya paramaṁ rūpam aiśvaram --\n\nevam uktvā tataḥ rājan mahā-yogeśvaraḥ hariḥ | darśayām āsa pārthāya paramam rūpam aiśvaram -- O King, having spoken thus, Hari, the great Lord of yogis, then showed Pārtha his supreme, sovereign form.\n\nSañjaya, who has received the divine sight from Vyāsa and is describing to Dhṛtarāṣṭra the events unfolding on the battlefield, likewise beholds Bhagavān's cosmic form. He tells Dhṛtarāṣṭra: \"O King, having spoken thus, the great Yogeśvara Hari showed his supreme form.\" The word \"tataḥ\" (then), used here, carries a special sense in this verse: because Arjuna was endowed with divine sight, because he was qualified to behold the vision of the divine form, Bhagavān, who fills all places, showed him his cosmic form -- this is the sense the word conveys. Sañjaya here addresses Bhagavān, the Lord of Brahmā and all deities, as \"Mahā-yogeśvaraḥ.\" The word \"Hariḥ\" used here is a word describing Bhagavān's all-pervasiveness. Whoever offers oblation into whichever deity's mouth, it is Bhagavān who first receives it -- He is \"Hariḥ.\" In the following verse Sañjaya describes the cosmic form of Bhagavān that he beheld.",
  "11.10": "anekavaktra-nayanam anekādbhuta-darśanam |\naneka-divyābharaṇaṁ divyānekodyatāyudham --\n\n[The word-split, meaning, and explanation for these two verses (11.10 and 11.11) are given together in the next verse (11.11).]",
  "11.11": "divya-mālyāmbara-dharaṁ divya-gandhānulepanam |\nsarvāścarya-mayaṁ devam anantaṁ viśvato-mukham --\n\naneka vaktra nayanam aneka adbhuta darśanam | aneka divya ābharaṇam divya aneka udyata āyudham | divya mālyā ambara dharam divya gandha anulepanam | sarva āścarya-mayam devam anantam viśvataḥ-mukham -- A form of countless mouths and eyes, of countless wondrous sights; a form of countless divine ornaments, holding countless divine weapons raised aloft; a form clad in garments with divine garlands, anointed with divine fragrance; the source of all wonders, endless, facing every direction.\n\nKṛṣṇa, who till now appeared to us all in human form, at this moment showed, wherever one looked, only the face of Kṛṣṇa -- within and without every being gathered there, everywhere only the face of Kṛṣṇa was seen. Sañjaya did not see there elephants, horses, or soldiers; instead, he beheld that Bhagavān who fills every place and every being. This form of Bhagavān is impossible to describe -- it can only be witnessed and experienced. It is a wonder. Radiant with every ornament, holding divine weapons, clad in garments set with garlands of flowers, anointed with shining fragrance, of the nature of every wonder, the very form of astonishment -- Bhagavān revealed his cosmic form on the battlefield.",
  "11.12": "divi sūrya-sahasrasya bhaved yugapad utthitā |\nyadi bhāḥ sadṛśī sā syād bhāsas tasya mahātmanaḥ --\n\ndivi sūrya sahasrasya bhavet yugapat utthitā | yadi bhāḥ sadṛśī sā syāt bhāsaḥ tasya mahātmanaḥ -- If the light of a thousand suns were to blaze up at once in the sky, that would perhaps match the splendour of that great being.\n\nSañjaya says: \"Bhagavān appeared as radiant as a thousand suns rising in the sky all at once.\" To yogis, Bhagavān appears, in the inner eye, as brilliant as a thousand suns rising together would be. Yet Bhagavān, who is \"of moonlike essence,\" is equally cool and soothing. Bhagavān is scorching like the sun, yet cool like the moon. No one can ever behold this so radiant form of Bhagavān with their outer eye.",
  "11.13": "tatraikasthaṁ jagat kṛtsnaṁ pravibhaktam anekadhā |\napaśyad deva-devasya śarīre pāṇḍavas tadā --\n\ntatra eka stham jagat kṛtsnam pravibhaktam anekadhā | apaśyat deva-devasya śarīre pāṇḍavaḥ tadā -- The son of Pāṇḍu then beheld there, gathered together in one place in the body of the God of gods, the entire universe divided into its many kinds.\n\nThe cosmic form Bhagavān showed Arjuna on the battlefield is exceedingly distinctive. As Madhvācārya explains, when Arjuna received this vision of the cosmic form, this same darśana was in fact granted to everyone in the world qualified to receive it. [It will be said later in this chapter that this special vision was granted to every qualified being in the three worlds.] What appeared before Arjuna here was not merely Bhagavān filling every place, but also the entire universe held within Him. [To see the universe within Bhagavān here means to see, in meditation, the Paramātman and the creation brought forth by Him, as one sees them together. Arjuna (Pāṇḍava), whose mind was purified by sattva, saw with his own divine sight the entire universe gathered within Bhagavān, quite distinctly.]",
  "11.14": "tataḥ sa vismayāviṣṭo hṛṣṭa-romā dhanañjayaḥ |\npraṇamya śirasā devaṁ kṛtāñjalir abhāṣata --\n\ntataḥ saḥ vismaya āviṣṭaḥ hṛṣṭa-romā dhanañjayaḥ | praṇamya śirasā devam kṛta-añjaliḥ abhāṣata -- Then he, overcome with wonder, his hair standing on end, Dhanañjaya, bowing his head to the Deva, spoke with joined palms.\n\nHaving beheld this wondrous form of Bhagavān, Arjuna stood transfixed, his hair standing on end with joy, astonishment, and bewilderment all at once. He who had gained this most rare treasure (dhana) of vision of Bhagavān (hence Dhanañjaya), bowed down full-length with devotion, joined his two hands in salutation, and began to praise Bhagavān.",
  "11.15": "arjuna uvāca --\n\npaśyāmi devāṁs tava deva dehe sarvāṁs tathā bhūta-viśeṣa-saṅghān |\nbrahmāṇam īśaṁ kamalāsana-sthaṁ ṛṣīṁś ca sarvān uragāṁś ca divyān --\n\npaśyāmi devān tava deva dehe sarvān tathā bhūta viśeṣa saṅghān | brahmāṇam īśam kamala āsana stham ṛṣīn ca sarvān uragān ca divyān -- I see, O Deva, in your body, all the gods, and likewise the assemblies of the various kinds of beings; Brahmā, the lord, seated on the lotus-throne, and all the sages, and the divine serpents.\n\nArjuna is beholding Kṛṣṇa's most wondrous form. To him all the gods are visible within that Bhagavān -- gods, demons, asuras, gandharvas, apsaras, yakṣas, human beings, animals, all of them. Chiefly, Arjuna sees within Bhagavān the four-faced Brahmā, Śiva, the great sages, and the divine serpents born of a portion of the divine, present within that Bhagavān who reclines on Śeṣa.",
  "11.16": "aneka-bāhūdara-vaktra-netraṁ paśyāmi tvāṁ sarvato 'nanta-rūpam |\nnāntaṁ na madhyaṁ na punas tavādiṁ paśyāmi viśveśvara viśva-rūpa --\n\naneka bāhu udara vaktra netram paśyāmi tvām sarvataḥ ananta rūpam | na antam na madhyam na punaḥ tava ādim paśyāmi viśva-īśvara viśva-rūpa -- I see you, of countless arms, bellies, mouths, and eyes, of endless form on every side. Neither your end, nor your middle, nor even your beginning do I see, O Lord of the universe, O form of the universe.\n\nI see you everywhere with countless arms, bodies, mouths, and eyes, in endless forms. O Lord of the earth, O form of the universe, I do not see your end; nor your middle; nor even your beginning.\n\nWherever one looks, a face; wherever one looks, eyes; wherever one looks, arms; wherever one looks, a belly. This verse serves as a commentary on the Puruṣa-sūkta. As the Puruṣa-sūkta declares: \"oṁ sahasra-śīrṣā puruṣaḥ, sahasrākṣaḥ sahasra-pāt\" -- Bhagavān has a thousand heads, a thousand eyes, a thousand feet. Wherever Arjuna wished to see something, there he beheld exactly what he sought. This is a strange experience. It is impossible to explain; it can only be experienced. The form of the infinitely-formed Bhagavān has neither an end nor a base. Bhagavān, the master of the entire universe, is at once smaller than the smallest atom and vaster than the vastest whole. To behold this form of Bhagavān in meditation is exceedingly difficult. As the authors of scripture say, one who sits down intending to behold Bhagavān's entire body in meditation may end up seeing nothing at all. It is therefore better to focus one's meditation on a single small finger of his flute-playing hand, or on the glance of his compassion-filled eyes.",
  "11.17": "kirīṭinaṁ gadinaṁ cakriṇaṁ ca tejo-rāśiṁ sarvato dīptimantam |\npaśyāmi tvāṁ durnirīkṣyaṁ samantād dīptānalārka-dyutim aprameyam --\n\nkirīṭinam gadinam cakriṇam ca tejaḥ-rāśim sarvataḥ dīptimantam | paśyāmi tvām durnirīkṣyam samantāt dīpta-anala-arka-dyutim aprameyam -- Crowned, bearing the mace, and the discus, a mass of splendour blazing on every side, I see you, hard to gaze upon on all sides, of the brilliance of blazing fire and sun, immeasurable.\n\nOn one side is your enchanting form adorned with every ornament; on another, the fearsome form of the enemy, holding the discus and mace. So all of it appears as a single mass of light. It is the experience of clearly seeing what cannot be seen. Whether it is blazing fire or the radiant sun, I cannot tell. It is a form that cannot be measured or fully known, most wondrous and rare.",
  "11.18": "tvam akṣaraṁ paramaṁ veditavyaṁ tvam asya viśvasya paraṁ nidhānam |\ntvam avyayaḥ śāśvata-dharma-goptā sanātanas tvaṁ puruṣo mato me --\n\ntvam akṣaram paramam veditavyam tvam asya viśvasya param nidhānam | tvam avyayaḥ śāśvata-dharma-goptā sanātanaḥ tvam puruṣaḥ mataḥ me -- You are the imperishable, the supreme object to be known; you are the supreme resting place of this universe; you are the changeless guardian of eternal dharma; you are, I hold, the everlasting, primeval person.\n\nArjuna says: \"You are akṣaraṁ paramam.\" Here akṣara means the all-pervading, indestructible reality, the granter of every wish, the meaning conveyed by every word, and so on. Bhagavān is the supreme among all that we ought to come to know. Arjuna says: \"You are the creator of the entire universe, its giver of refuge, the ultimate shelter of the whole world. You are the protector of eternal dharma across beginningless, endless time. It has become clear to me that you are that which all the Vedas seek to establish.\" [The special meanings of the several distinctive words appearing in this verse -- akṣaraḥ, puruṣaḥ, avyayaḥ, śāśvata-dharma, and so on -- have already been analyzed in earlier chapters, so their explanation is not repeated here.]",
  "11.19": "anādi-madhyāntam ananta-vīryam ananta-bāhuṁ śaśi-sūrya-netram |\npaśyāmi tvāṁ dīpta-hutāśa-vaktraṁ sva-tejasā viśvam idaṁ tapantam --\n\nanādi madhya antam ananta vīryam ananta bāhum śaśi sūrya netram | paśyāmi tvām dīpta hutāśa vaktram sva-tejasā viśvam idam tapantam -- Without beginning, middle, or end; of endless valour; of endless arms; with the sun and moon as your eyes; I see you, with your mouth a blazing fire, scorching this entire universe with your own splendour.\n\nYour qualities have no beginning, middle, or end. You are of endless valour -- your prowess is without beginning or end. Endless, imperishable your limbs. The sun and moon born of your eyes; the flame of blazing fire in your mouth. This whole universe seems to be scorched and burning by your radiance.",
  "11.20": "dyāv ā-pṛthivyor idam antaraṁ hi vyāptaṁ tvayaikena diśaś ca sarvāḥ |\ndṛṣṭvādbhutaṁ rūpam ugraṁ tavedaṁ loka-trayaṁ pravyathitaṁ mahātman --\n\ndyāv ā pṛthivyoḥ idam antaram hi vyāptam tvayā ekena diśaḥ ca sarvāḥ | dṛṣṭvā adbhutam rūpam ugram tava idam loka-trayam pravyathitam mahātman -- This entire space between heaven and earth is pervaded by you alone, and so are all the directions. Having seen this wondrous, terrible form of yours, O great being, the three worlds tremble.\n\nYour form pervades all the space between earth and sky. I see your form extending endlessly in every direction. All who have beheld your form in the three worlds are stricken with terror upon seeing this fierce form of yours. The world is shaking. Bewildered, Arjuna thus expresses to Bhagavān his own experience.",
  "11.21": "amī hi tvāṁ sura-saṅghā viśanti kecid bhītāḥ prāñjalayo gṛṇanti |\nsvastīty uktvā maharṣi-siddha-saṅghāḥ stuvanti tvāṁ stutibhiḥ puṣkalābhiḥ --\n\namī hi tvām sura-saṅghāḥ viśanti kecit bhītāḥ prāñjalayaḥ gṛṇanti | svasti iti uktvā mahā-ṛṣi-siddha-saṅghāḥ stuvanti tvām stutibhiḥ puṣkalābhiḥ -- These hosts of gods indeed enter into you; some, in fear, with joined palms, invoke you; saying \"may there be well-being,\" the assemblies of great sages and perfected beings praise you with abundant hymns.\n\nSome are entering into you, others are coming forth. Some, with joined palms, are singing your praise. The wise, beholding this wondrous form of yours, are blessing the world with \"may there be auspiciousness for the world.\" The great sages and seers are extolling you with many hymns of praise.",
  "11.22": "rudrādityā vasavo ye ca sādhyā viśve 'śvinau marutaś coṣmapāś ca |\ngandharva-yakṣāsura-siddha-saṅghā vīkṣante tvāṁ vismitāś caiva sarve --\n\nrudra ādityāḥ vasavaḥ ye ca sādhyāḥ viśve aśvinau marutaḥ ca uṣmapāḥ ca | gandharva yakṣa asura siddha saṅghāḥ vīkṣante tvām vismitāḥ ca eva sarve -- The Rudras, the Ādityas, the Vasus, and the Sādhyas, the Viśvedevas, the two Aśvins, the Maruts, and the Uṣmapas, the assemblies of Gandharvas, Yakṣas, Asuras, and Siddhas -- all of them gaze upon you, utterly astonished.\n\nThe eleven Rudras, the twelve Ādityas, the eight Vasus, the Sādhyas, the Viśvedevas, the Aśvins, the Maruts, the Pitṛs, the Gandharvas, the Yakṣas (who have entered the assemblies of the gods), the Asuras, the Siddhas -- all of these, Arjuna says, are gazing at you, utterly astounded, gathered together.",
  "11.23": "rūpaṁ mahat te bahu-vaktra-netraṁ mahā-bāho bahu-bāhūru-pādam |\nbahūdaraṁ bahu-daṁṣṭrā-karālaṁ dṛṣṭvā lokāḥ pravyathitās tathāham --\n\nrūpam mahat te bahu-vaktra-netram mahā-bāho bahu-bāhu-ūru-pādam | bahu-udaram bahu-daṁṣṭrā-karālam dṛṣṭvā lokāḥ pravyathitāḥ tathā aham -- O mighty-armed one, seeing your vast form, of many mouths and eyes, of many arms, thighs, and feet, of many bellies, terrible with many tusks -- the worlds are struck with terror, and so am I.\n\nArjuna is beholding how the act of creation is proceeding within the very limbs of Bhagavān. As the Puruṣa-sūkta declares: \"brāhmaṇo 'sya mukham āsīt, bāhū rājanyaḥ kṛtaḥ; ūrū tad asya yad vaiśyaḥ, padbhyāṁ śūdro ajāyata\" -- that is, from Bhagavān's mouth arose the creation of the wise (Wisdom), from His arms the creation of the kṣatriyas (Protection), from His waist or thighs the creation of the vaiśyas (Production), and from His sacred feet the creation of the śūdras (Service). These four classes are the four supporting pillars of society. Arjuna sees this fourfold creation taking place within Bhagavān's very body. Alongside this, he also sees, filling that body, the many terrible tusks that devour everyone (the act of dissolution). Beholding this form of Bhagavān, all are struck with wonder. \"I too am terrified,\" says Arjuna.",
  "11.24": "nabhaḥ-spṛśaṁ dīptam aneka-varṇaṁ vyāttānanaṁ dīpta-viśāla-netram |\ndṛṣṭvā hi tvāṁ pravyathitāntarātmā dhṛtiṁ na vindāmi śamaṁ ca viṣṇo --\n\nnabhaḥ-spṛśam dīptam aneka-varṇam vyātta-ānanam dīpta-viśāla-netram | dṛṣṭvā hi tvām pravyathita-antarātmā dhṛtim na vindāmi śamam ca viṣṇo -- Touching the sky, blazing, of many colours, with mouths gaping wide, with blazing, wide eyes -- having seen you thus, my inner self is shaken; I find neither steadiness nor peace, O Viṣṇu.\n\nYour endless form pervading the earth and sky -- a mass of light untouched by any darkness. Within you, many are praising you with many scriptural utterances, with the Vedas. I see your form of unearthly hues. I have seen your wide, gracious, compassionate eyes as well. Seeing all this, my mind is thrown into confusion. \"O Viṣṇu, I cannot comprehend this strange form of yours,\" says Arjuna. [The distinctive meaning of the word \"Viṣṇu\" was analyzed in the previous chapter.]",
  "11.25": "daṁṣṭrā-karālāni ca te mukhāni dṛṣṭvaiva kālānala-sannibhāni |\ndiśo na jāne na labhe ca śarma prasīda deveśa jagan-nivāsa --\n\ndaṁṣṭrā-karālāni ca te mukhāni dṛṣṭvā eva kāla-anala-sannibhāni | diśaḥ na jāne na labhe ca śarma prasīda deveśa jagat-nivāsa -- Having merely seen your mouths, terrible with tusks, resembling the fire of universal dissolution, I know not the directions, nor do I find any comfort; be gracious, O Lord of the gods, O refuge of the universe.\n\nHaving seen your roaring mouths, terrible with tusks, like the fire of the final dissolution, I have lost all sense of direction. My peace is gone. O Lord of the gods, O refuge of the world, be gracious to me. Above all, seeing your face -- gaping-mouthed with terrible tusks like Narasiṁha -- I am utterly disoriented. Seeing you pervading like the fire of dissolution, my joy is vanishing in terror. I am losing my composure. O Lord of all the gods, you who are the refuge of the universe, show me grace.",
  "11.26": "amī ca tvāṁ dhṛtarāṣṭrasya putrāḥ sarve sahaivāvani-pāla-saṅghaiḥ |\nbhīṣmo droṇaḥ sūta-putras tathāsau sahāsmadīyair api yodha-mukhyaiḥ --\n\n[The word-split, meaning, and explanation for these two verses (11.26 and 11.27) are given together in the next verse (11.27).]",
  "11.27": "amī ca tvāṁ dhṛtarāṣṭrasya putrāḥ sarve sahaivāvani-pāla-saṅghaiḥ |\nbhīṣmo droṇaḥ sūta-putras tathāsau sahāsmadīyair api yodha-mukhyaiḥ ||\nvaktrāṇi te tvaramāṇā viśanti daṁṣṭrā-karālāni bhayānakāni |\nkecid vilagnā daśanāntareṣu sandṛśyante cūrṇitair uttamāṅgaiḥ --\n\nkecit vilagnāḥ daśana-antareṣu sandṛśyante cūrṇitaiḥ uttama-aṅgaiḥ -- Bhīṣma, Droṇa, and Karṇa, along with the chief warriors of our own side too -- I see them rushing headlong into your terrible, tusk-filled mouths. Some are seen caught between your teeth, their heads crushed to powder.\n\nArjuna is beholding, within Bhagavān, events that will occur in the future. In this world everything is predetermined; Arjuna is simply seeing this with his divine sight. Bhīṣma, Droṇa, and Karṇa are all entering into your mouth, along with the leaders of our own army. Some of them, I see, are caught by your fearsome tusks and being crushed to pieces.",
  "11.28": "yathā nadīnāṁ bahavo 'mbu-vegāḥ samudram evābhimukhā dravanti |\ntathā tavāmī nara-loka-vīrā viśanti vaktrāṇy abhivijvalanti --\n\nyathā nadīnām bahavaḥ ambu-vegāḥ samudram eva abhimukhāḥ dravanti | tathā tava amī nara-loka-vīrāḥ viśanti vaktrāṇi abhivijvalanti -- As the many rushing waters of rivers flow onward, facing toward the ocean alone, so do these heroes of the world of men enter your blazing mouths.\n\nArjuna is describing how meritorious souls are entering Bhagavān's mouth. Just as all river waters reach the ocean, so these heroes of this world are joining you, says Arjuna. This is an auspicious simile; there is nothing fearsome in this image.",
  "11.29": "yathā pradīptaṁ jvalanaṁ pataṅgā viśanti nāśāya samṛddha-vegāḥ |\ntathaiva nāśāya viśanti lokās tavāpi vaktrāṇi samṛddha-vegāḥ --\n\nyathā pradīptam jvalanam pataṅgāḥ viśanti nāśāya samṛddha-vegāḥ | tathā eva nāśāya viśanti lokāḥ tava api vaktrāṇi samṛddha-vegāḥ -- Just as moths, in swelling haste, rush into a blazing fire to their destruction, so too do these people, in swelling haste, rush into your mouths to their destruction.\n\nDescribing how the wicked are entering Bhagavān's mouth, Arjuna says: \"Just as moths fall into a blazing fire and are destroyed, they are rushing in headlong and being destroyed.\"",
  "11.30": "lelihyase grasamānaḥ samantāl lokān samagrān vadanair jvaladbhiḥ |\ntejobhir āpūrya jagat samagraṁ bhāsas tavogrāḥ pratapanti viṣṇo --\n\nlelihyase grasamānaḥ samantāt lokān samagrān vadanaiḥ jvaladbhiḥ | tejobhiḥ āpūrya jagat samagram bhāsaḥ tava ugrāḥ pratapanti viṣṇo -- O one who is present everywhere, licking up and devouring all the worlds on every side with your blazing mouths, filling the whole universe with your splendour, your fierce radiances scorch it, O Viṣṇu.\n\nYou are licking and swallowing everything with your tongue; everything is entering your belly. This whole world is becoming a single morsel for you. That radiance of your body is filling every corner of the universe. Filling everyone within and without, you appear as the destroyer of all (Viṣṇu).",
  "11.31": "ākhyāhi me ko bhavān ugra-rūpo namo 'stu te deva-vara prasīda |\nvijñātum icchāmi bhavantam ādyaṁ na hi prajānāmi tava pravṛttim --\n\nākhyāhi me kaḥ bhavān ugra-rūpaḥ namaḥ astu te deva-vara prasīda | vijñātum icchāmi bhavantam ādyam na hi prajānāmi tava pravṛttim -- Tell me, who are you, of this terrible form? Salutations to you, O best of gods; be gracious. I wish to know you, the primeval one; I do not understand your purpose.\n\nBeholding this terrifying form of yours, I understand nothing at all. I have become unable to grasp your glory. I have come to you for refuge; uplift me. I wish to know you, who have taken on this fearsome form -- thus Arjuna pleads and beseeches Kṛṣṇa.",
  "11.32": "śrī-bhagavān uvāca --\n\nkālo 'smi loka-kṣaya-kṛt pravṛddho lokān samāhartum iha pravṛttaḥ |\nṛte 'pi tvāṁ na bhaviṣyanti sarve ye 'vasthitāḥ praty-anīkeṣu yodhāḥ --\n\nkālaḥ asmi loka-kṣaya-kṛt pravṛddhaḥ lokān samāhartum iha pravṛttaḥ | ṛte api tvām na bhaviṣyanti sarve ye avasthitāḥ prati-anīkeṣu yodhāḥ -- I am Time, grown mighty, the agent of the world's dissolution, engaged here in gathering in the worlds. Even without you, none of these warriors arrayed in the opposing ranks shall live on.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"I stand here as Kāla-puruṣa.\" The word \"kāla,\" derived from the root \"kal,\" yields several meanings -- the destroyer of all, the one full of all qualities, the all-knowing, and so forth. Here Kṛṣṇa stands specifically as the power of destruction. \"I stand here as your charioteer only to finish off these men who are a burden upon the earth,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Looking at the Mahābhārata war, it was indeed a great war; about five million people died in it -- roughly a tenth of the country's population at the time. As the Bhāgavata says: standing as Kāla-puruṣa, Kṛṣṇa, without lifting any weapon, drew out everyone's lifespan with his very glance. Hence Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna: \"Even without you, all these men -- on both sides -- will die. Their death has already been determined.\"",
  "11.33": "tasmāt tvam uttiṣṭha yaśo labhasva jitvā śatrūn bhuṅkṣva rājyaṁ samṛddham |\nmayaivaite nihatāḥ pūrvam eva nimitta-mātraṁ bhava savyasācin --\n\ntasmāt tvam uttiṣṭha yaśaḥ labhasva jitvā śatrūn bhuṅkṣva rājyam samṛddham | mayā eva ete nihatāḥ pūrvam eva nimitta-mātram bhava savyasācin -- Therefore rise up, gain glory; having conquered your enemies, enjoy a thriving kingdom. These have already been slain by me alone; be merely the instrument, O Savyasācin (ambidextrous archer).\n\nKṛṣṇa is encouraging Arjuna. Rise up, prepare for battle. You will be victorious in this war. Destroy these enemies, for I have already slain them. Here you are merely the instrument. Fulfil the duty that has fallen to your lot, and experience the good fortune that is destined for you.",
  "11.34": "droṇaṁ ca bhīṣmaṁ ca jayadrathaṁ ca karṇaṁ tathānyān api yodha-vīrān |\nmayā hatāṁs tvaṁ jahi mā vyathiṣṭhā yudhyasva jetāsi raṇe sapatnān --\n\nmayā hatān tvam jahi mā vyathiṣṭhāḥ yuddhasva jetā asi raṇe sapatnān -- Slay Droṇa, and Bhīṣma, and Jayadratha, and Karṇa, and other heroic warriors too -- all already slain by me. Do not be distressed; fight; you shall conquer your enemies in battle.\n\nI have already drawn out the lifespan of all those gathered here -- Droṇa, Bhīṣma, Jayadratha, Karṇa. Therefore you need not grieve over this. This wave of life is inevitable; no one can stop it. Therefore rise and fight. You will be victorious in this righteous war. My full grace and protection are with you -- thus Kṛṣṇa gives his blessing.\n\nHaving earlier spoken with arrogance, \"sthāpaya me acyuta rathaṁ senayor ubhayor madhye,\" Arjuna had then, seeing his elders standing before him, lost heart and refused to fight. Both these states were decisions made without regard for the true state of affairs. For this reason Kṛṣṇa, across these ten chapters, has taught Arjuna and made him understand that war, here, is dharma. Now, having granted him the vision of his cosmic form, he has said: \"You are merely the instrument.\" What Arjuna did upon hearing Kṛṣṇa's fearsome form and these words, Sañjaya describes in the following verse.",
  "11.35": "sañjaya uvāca --\n\netac chrutvā vacanaṁ keśavasya kṛtāñjalir vepamānaḥ kirīṭī |\nnamaskṛtvā bhūya evāha kṛṣṇaṁ sa-gadgadaṁ bhīta-bhītaḥ praṇamya --\n\netat śrutvā vacanam keśavasya kṛta-añjaliḥ vepamānaḥ kirīṭī | namaskṛtvā bhūyaḥ eva āha kṛṣṇam sa-gadgadam bhīta-bhītaḥ praṇamya -- Having heard this speech of Keśava, Arjuna, joining his palms, trembling, having bowed once more, spoke again to Kṛṣṇa in a choked voice, utterly overcome with fear, prostrating himself.\n\nHaving heard Kṛṣṇa's words, Arjuna joined his hands in devotion to Bhagavān. In this state he stood shaken and trembling. Prostrating with his entire body to Bhagavān, he spoke, his voice choked with emotion.\n\nHere Sañjaya addresses Bhagavān as \"Keśava\" and \"Kṛṣṇa.\" Keśava means the one who engages and controls Brahmā and Rudra, the powers at the root of the universe's creation and destruction, in that very work of creation and destruction. Bhagavān, present within every living being and controlling them, the indweller of all, is Keśava. \"Kṛṣṇa,\" too, is Bhagavān's own root name -- the one who draws away (karṣaṇa) our ego and ignorance, who draws the whole world toward himself, and who draws us out of saṁsāra, is \"Kṛṣṇa.\"",
  "11.36": "arjuna uvāca --\n\nsthāne hṛṣīkeśa tava prakīrtyā jagat prahṛṣyaty anurajyate ca |\nrakṣāṁsi bhītāni diśo dravanti sarve namasyanti ca siddha-saṅghāḥ --\n\nsthāne hṛṣīkeśa tava prakīrtyā jagat prahṛṣyati anurajyate ca | rakṣāṁsi bhītāni diśaḥ dravanti sarve namasyanti ca siddha-saṅghāḥ -- It is fitting, O Hṛṣīkeśa, that the world rejoices and is delighted by your fame; the demons flee in terror in every direction; and all the hosts of the perfected ones bow to you.\n\nAs said before -- when Arjuna received the vision of the cosmic form, many wise people and even the gods, throughout the entire universe, also received this rare vision of Bhagavān. Arjuna is describing what he perceives within himself. Beyond this, external reality too is now visible to him within Bhagavān, and he describes it here: \"The sages and the wise are singing your praises. They are thrilled, extolling you. Wicked powers flee in fear at the sight of you. The virtuous and the perfected leap and dance for joy. All of this is exactly fitting.\"",
  "11.37": "kasmāc ca te na nameran mahātman garīyase brahmaṇo 'py ādi-kartre |\nananta deveśa jagan-nivāsa tvam akṣaraṁ sad-asat tat paraṁ yat --\n\nkasmāt ca te na nameran mahātman garīyase brahmaṇaḥ api ādi-kartre | ananta deveśa jagat-nivāsa tvam akṣaram sat-asat tat param yat -- And why should they not bow to you, O great being, greater even than Brahmā, the primordial creator? O endless one, Lord of the gods, refuge of the universe, you are the imperishable, the being and the non-being, and that which is beyond both.\n\nHow could those who know you fail to bow to you? You are the perfect self. You are the father even of the four-faced one who is the father of this world. It is impossible not to bow to such a one as you. You are endless in place, time, and quality. You are the abode of the universe, filling everyone within and without; you are the definite... and flawless embodiment of knowledge and bliss (sat). You who are of unmanifest form (asat) we cannot see with our outer eye. You are Akṣaraḥ, you are Deveśa, you are the cause of causes, you are the refuge of the world.",
  "11.38": "tvam ādi-devaḥ puruṣaḥ purāṇas tvam asya viśvasya paraṁ nidhānam |\nvettāsi vedyaṁ ca paraṁ ca dhāma tvayā tataṁ viśvam ananta-rūpa --\n\nvettā asi vedyam ca param ca dhāma tvayā tatam viśvam ananta-rūpa -- You are the primal god, the ancient person; you are the supreme resting place of this universe; you are the knower and the object to be known, and the supreme abode; by you, O infinite-formed one, this universe is pervaded.\n\nYou are the Ādi-devaḥ, you are the Purāṇa-puruṣaḥ, the ultimate refuge in this world. The one all must come to know, who himself knows all, the supreme light beyond all else. You, of infinite form, stand pervading the entire universe.",
  "11.39": "vāyur yamo 'gnir varuṇaḥ śaśāṅkaḥ prajāpatis tvaṁ prapitāmahaś ca |\nnamo namas te 'stu sahasra-kṛtvaḥ punaś ca bhūyo 'pi namo namas te --\n\nnamaḥ namaḥ te astu sahasra-kṛtvaḥ punaḥ ca bhūyaḥ api namaḥ namaḥ te -- You are Vāyu, Yama, Agni, Varuṇa, the moon, Prajāpati, and the great-grandfather. Salutations, salutations to you, a thousand times over; and again, and yet again, salutations, salutations to you.\n\n[Vāyu is so called from \"va,\" strength, and the knowledge of \"āyā\"; \"Yama\" because he regulates everything; \"Agni\" because he gives motion to a universe that would otherwise be motionless (from \"aga,\" unmoving); \"Varuṇa\" because he chooses (varaṇa) his devotees; \"Śaśāṅka\" because he is marked (aṅka) by supreme bliss (śaśa); \"Prajāpati\" because he is the protector (pati) of creatures (prajā).] You are the great-grandfather of the world. A thousand times I bow to you. Again and yet again, salutations, salutations.",
  "11.40": "namaḥ purastād atha pṛṣṭhatas te namo 'stu te sarvata eva sarva |\nananta-vīryāmita-vikramas tvaṁ sarvaṁ samāpnoṣi tato 'si sarvaḥ --\n\nananta-vīrya amita-vikramaḥ tvam sarvam samāpnoṣi tataḥ asi sarvaḥ -- Salutations to you from the front, and salutations to you from behind; salutations to you from every side indeed, O All. Of endless valour, of immeasurable prowess, you pervade everything, and therefore you are all.\n\nIn Bhagavān's cosmic form, how is one to distinguish which side is the front and which the back? This is impossible. Hence Arjuna says: salutations to you from the front, salutations from behind, salutations from every side to you who fill every place. You are of endless valour. You are of immeasurable prowess. O All, salutations to you. Salutations to this endless power, the cause of the creation of every living being in the world, the regulator of the universe filling every place. Everything is you.",
  "11.41": "sakheti matvā prasabhaṁ yad uktaṁ he kṛṣṇa he yādava he sakheti |\najānatā mahimānaṁ tavedaṁ mayā pramādāt praṇayena vāpi --\n\n[The word-split, meaning, and explanation for these two verses (11.41 and 11.42) are given together in the next verse (11.42).]",
  "11.42": "sakheti matvā prasabhaṁ yad uktaṁ he kṛṣṇa he yādava he sakheti |\najānatā mahimānaṁ tavedaṁ mayā pramādāt praṇayena vāpi |\nyac cāvahāsārtham asat-kṛto 'si vihāra-śayyāsana-bhojaneṣu |\neko 'thavāpy acyuta tat-samakṣaṁ tat kṣāmaye tvām aham aprameyam --\n\nArjuna is repentant about the way he has previously behaved toward Kṛṣṇa. Without the awareness that Kṛṣṇa is the root-power of the universe, he has treated him casually, and for this he now begs Kṛṣṇa's forgiveness. He entreats Bhagavān to forgive whatever wrongs he has committed knowingly or unknowingly, carelessly or out of love. The word \"eka\" occurring here carries a special meaning. Ekaḥ means the supreme among all and the doer of all (\"eṣa eva karoti -- ekaḥ\"). \"I have mocked and spoken casually with you, who are the supreme doer, the supreme among all; you are Acyuta -- there is no lapse in your capability, quality, or form. My own lapsed intellect failed to grasp this. I ought not to have behaved this way. For the offence I have committed, I beg forgiveness of you. You are aprameya -- pervading all time and place, beyond our comprehension. Only now has my error become clear to me. I have come to understand that I was acting wrongly out of ignorance.\"",
  "11.43": "pitāsi lokasya carācarasya tvam asya pūjyaś ca gurur garīyān |\nna tvat-samo 'sty abhyadhikaḥ kuto 'nyo loka-traye 'py apratima-prabhāva --\n\nYou are the father of this world, moving and unmoving; you are its object of worship, its most venerable teacher of teachers. There is none equal to you; how then could there be anyone greater, even in the three worlds, O one of unequalled glory?\n\nYou are the father of the world, having created and sustaining all. I regarded you, who are the teacher of the world, with far too much familiarity. There is no one else equal to you. There is no other comparison by which to measure you. In this entire world there is no other power that surpasses you. [Here \"the three worlds\" means the group of worlds below the earth, the earth itself, and the group of worlds above the earth.] You are the cause of all causes. Where else could there be anything greater than you?",
  "11.44": "tasmāt praṇamya praṇidhāya kāyaṁ prasādaye tvām aham īśam īḍyam |\npitāsyaiva sakhyeva sakhyuḥ priyaḥ priyāyārhasi deva soḍhum --\n\nTherefore, prostrating and laying down my body, I beg your grace, O Lord, worthy of praise. As a father bears with a son, as a friend with a friend, as a lover with a beloved, so, O Lord, you ought to bear with me.\n\nBefore you, praised by all, the lord of all, I abase myself, falling at your feet, entreating you: O Lord, you should forgive the wrongs done by me, your favoured one, for your own favoured one's sake, just as a father forgives a son's fault, or a friend a friend's fault.\n\nI cannot bear to stand before you. I feel I must fall prostrate and roll upon the ground. I plead before you, who are praised by all. Just as a father forgives a son's wrong, just as a friend forgives a friend's wrong, so may you, the father of the world, the intimate friend of everyone's heart in this world, forgive me.",
  "11.45": "adṛṣṭa-pūrvaṁ hṛṣito 'smi dṛṣṭvā bhayena ca pravyathitaṁ mano me |\ntad eva me darśaya deva rūpaṁ prasīda deveśa jagan-nivāsa --\n\nI am delighted to have seen what was never seen before, and yet my mind is shaken with fear. Show me, O Deva, that very form again; be gracious, O Lord of the gods, O refuge of the universe.\n\nI am overjoyed to have beheld this wondrous form of yours, never seen before. Yet I am afraid, having seen your fierce form that destroys the entire universe. On one side is joy, on the other, terror. I am utterly bewildered by this. Therefore show me that \"Kṛṣṇa\" form of yours again.",
  "11.46": "kirīṭinaṁ gadinaṁ cakra-hastam icchāmi tvāṁ draṣṭum ahaṁ tathaiva |\ntenaiva rūpeṇa catur-bhujena sahasra-bāho bhava viśva-mūrte --\n\nI wish to see you just as before, crowned, with the mace, the discus in hand. O thousand-armed one, O form of the universe, appear again in that same four-armed form.\n\n\"Show me your form as you appeared before -- four-armed, crowned, holding the mace and discus,\" Arjuna, joining his palms, entreats the thousand-armed, cosmic-formed Bhagavān. Here Arjuna says to Kṛṣṇa: \"Show me your four-armed form.\" Kṛṣṇa had shown his four-armed form to his intimate devotees on other occasions too, and Arjuna had seen this as well. It is recorded in the Bhāgavata that Kṛṣṇa, as a child, showed his four-armed form: \"tam adbhutaṁ bālakam ambujekṣaṇaṁ catur-bhujaṁ śaṅkha-gadādy-udāyudhaṁ, śrīvatsa-lakṣmaṁ gala-śobhi-kaustubhaṁ pītāmbaraṁ sāndra-payoda-saubhagam\" (Bhāg. 10.3.9). This verse confirms that Kṛṣṇa had shown a few chosen souls his four-armed form, unseen by ordinary people. Here Arjuna, having had enough of beholding the cosmic form, entreats Bhagavān to show him instead his four-armed form.\n\nThe important point to understand here is this: devotion to Bhagavān's cosmic form is, for ordinary people like us, altogether impossible. Even Arjuna, a man of the highest wisdom, could not bear to look upon the cosmic form itself. If that is so, is it possible for us to absorb it in our meditation? For this reason, rather than meditating upon Bhagavān's cosmic form, it is more beneficial to worship him -- being mindful of Bhagavān's cosmic power -- in his avatāra form. Let us see, in the verses that follow, how Kṛṣṇa granted this request of Arjuna's.",
  "11.47": "śrī-bhagavān uvāca --\n\nmayā prasannena tavārjunedaṁ rūpaṁ paraṁ darśitam ātma-yogāt |\ntejo-mayaṁ viśvam anantam ādyaṁ yan me tvad anyena na dṛṣṭa-pūrvam --\n\nO Arjuna, out of my own delight, by my own yogic power, this supreme form has been shown to you -- made of splendour, universal, endless, primeval, which none other than you has ever seen before.\n\nKṛṣṇa consoles Arjuna, who has been terrified by the sight of Bhagavān's form of destruction. Kṛṣṇa says: \"I showed you the cosmic form only out of delight in your devotion, not to frighten you.\" Kṛṣṇa had shown his cosmic form on several occasions during his incarnation. When Yaśodā asked the child Kṛṣṇa, who had eaten mud, to open his mouth, she was shown the cosmic form within it. On another occasion, when the infant Kṛṣṇa yawned after drinking milk, Yaśodā again beheld Bhagavān's cosmic form within his mouth. When Kṛṣṇa came to Dhṛtarāṣṭra's court for the peace negotiations and Duryodhana tried to have him seized, Kṛṣṇa showed his special form there too. Beyond this, Gargācārya and the sage Udaṅka also received the vision of Kṛṣṇa's cosmic form. But the cosmic form Kṛṣṇa showed Arjuna here surpasses all of these. \"No one on this earth has ever seen this before,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Bhagavān's cosmic form is a mass of light; it is an ocean of qualities. Arjuna has received this vision of the endless cosmic form.",
  "11.48": "na veda-yajñādhyayanair na dānair na ca kriyābhir na tapobhir ugraiḥ |\nevaṁ-rūpaḥ śakya ahaṁ nṛ-loke draṣṭuṁ tvad anyena kuru-pravīra --\n\nNot by study of the Vedas, nor by sacrifices, nor by gifts, nor by rites, nor by fierce austerities can I, in this form, be seen in the world of men by anyone other than you, O hero of the Kurus.\n\nIt is not possible through the learning that seeks out the ritual portions of the Vedas. Not through gifts. Not through observances and vows. Not through rigorous austerities. No one who has not gained Bhagavān's favour can attain this vision of his cosmic form. Through Vedic study, sacrifice, charity, and such acts alone, it is not possible to attain the vision of the supreme, altogether unique Bhagavān in his cosmic form. \"You, devoted to your duty (Kuru-pravīra), have beheld such a form of mine,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "11.49": "mā te vyathā mā ca vimūḍha-bhāvo dṛṣṭvā rūpaṁ ghoram īdṛṅ mamedam |\nvyapeta-bhīḥ prīta-manāḥ punas tvaṁ tad eva me rūpam idaṁ prapaśya --\n\nLet there be no distress in you, and no bewilderment, at having seen this terrible form of mine. Free from fear, with a delighted mind, behold once again that very form of mine.\n\nDo not be troubled or bewildered at having seen this frightening form of mine. Look once more upon that same, earlier form of mine -- with fear cast away, and your heart filled with affection.\n\nDo not be alarmed at seeing this form of mine that destroys the enemy. Cast aside all your fear and look upon my four-armed form, which is dear to you, says Krishna.",
  "11.50": "sañjaya uvāca --\n\nity arjunaṁ vāsudevas tathoktvā svakaṁ rūpaṁ darśayām āsa bhūyaḥ |\nāśvāsayām āsa ca bhītam enaṁ bhūtvā punaḥ saumya-vapur mahātmā --\n\nHaving thus spoken to Arjuna, Vāsudeva showed him again his own form; and having once more assumed his gentle form, the great being consoled him, who was afraid.\n\nVāsudeva, having spoken thus, showed Arjuna once more his Kṛṣṇa-form. The great-souled Kṛṣṇa, assuming his gentle form once again, consoled the frightened Arjuna.\n\nIn this verse Sañjaya addresses Bhagavān as \"Vāsudeva.\" This name of Bhagavān has many meanings. Vāsu-deva -- Vāsudeva. Bhagavān is \"Vāsu,\" meaning the one who has concealed himself. When we cross the fifteen fences of worldly bondage and, descending deep from the state of samādhi, behold the sixteenth, the true form of the jīva, then Bhagavān's direct realization occurs. In this state, Bhagavān, who had been concealed, opens himself as \"Deva.\" To behold Bhagavān we must first behold ourselves. Without self-awareness it is impossible to see Bhagavān. When we behold our own true jīva-nature, the Bhagavān who becomes directly realized from within it is \"Devaḥ.\" When present within our five sheaths (the food-sheath, the vital-sheath, the knowledge-sheath, the mind-sheath, and the bliss-sheath) as if behind a veil, he is \"Vāsu\"; and when he comes forth from behind that veil and grants vision of himself upon our beholding our true jīva-nature, appearing as \"Deva\" -- that Bhagavān is \"Vāsudevaḥ.\"",
  "11.51": "arjuna uvāca --\n\ndṛṣṭvedaṁ mānuṣaṁ rūpaṁ tava saumyaṁ janārdana |\nidānīm asmi saṁvṛttaḥ sa-cetāḥ prakṛtiṁ gataḥ --\n\nHaving seen this gentle, human form of yours, O Janārdana, I have now returned to myself, my mind composed, restored to my natural state.\n\nO Janārdana, having seen this gentle form of yours in human shape, life has returned to me. I am now recovering and returning to my natural state.\n\nHere Arjuna addresses Bhagavān as \"Janārdana.\" Jana-ardana -- Janārdana. Here \"ardana\" carries the meaning \"the one who brings to an end.\" The word \"jana\" has several meanings. \"Jana\" can mean the wicked; Janārdana then means \"the destroyer of the wicked.\" \"Jana\" can also mean those subject to birth; Janārdana then means \"the one who liberates from birth,\" that is, the bestower of liberation. \"I was terrified, seeing that form of yours which stands ready to destroy the wicked. Responding to the call of your devotees, and beholding this gentle form of yours which bestows liberation upon your devotees, I have recovered\" -- this is the feeling behind this address.",
  "11.52": "su-durdarśam idaṁ rūpaṁ dṛṣṭavān asi yan mama |\ndevā apy asya rūpasya nityaṁ darśana-kāṅkṣiṇaḥ --\n\n[The word-split, meaning, and explanation for these two verses (11.52 and 11.53) are given together in the next verse (11.53).]",
  "11.53": "śrī-bhagavān uvāca --\n\nsu-durdarśam idaṁ rūpaṁ dṛṣṭavān asi yan mama |\ndevā apy asya rūpasya nityaṁ darśana-kāṅkṣiṇaḥ ||\nnāhaṁ vedair na tapasā na dānena na cejyayā |\nśakya evaṁ-vidho draṣṭuṁ dṛṣṭavān asi māṁ yathā --\n\nThis form of mine that you have seen is exceedingly hard to behold; even the gods are ever longing for a sight of this form. Not by the Vedas, nor by austerity, nor by charity, nor by sacrifice can I be seen in this manner, as you have seen me.\n\nThis most rare form of mine that Bhagavān showed Arjuna is exceedingly difficult to attain. Even the gods wait eagerly, forever, for a sight of such a rare form. Kṛṣṇa here again emphasizes and repeats what he said earlier: \"It is not possible to see this special form of mine through one's own effort\" -- that is, through Vedic study, sacrifice, charity, austerity, and such acts, says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "11.54": "bhaktyā tv ananyayā śakya aham evaṁ-vidho 'rjuna |\njñātuṁ draṣṭuṁ ca tattvena praveṣṭuṁ ca parantapa --\n\nBut by unswerving devotion alone, O Arjuna, can I, in this form, be truly known, and seen, and entered into, O scorcher of foes.\n\nThe root formula for beholding Bhagavān is \"devotion.\" Through one-pointed, undivided devotion to Bhagavān alone, it becomes possible to reach him. Bhagavān, who is greater than all, is subject to his devotee alone.",
  "11.55": "mat-karma-kṛn mat-paramo mad-bhaktaḥ saṅga-varjitaḥ |\nnirvairaḥ sarva-bhūteṣu yaḥ sa mām eti pāṇḍava --\n\nHe who does all his work for me, holds me as the supreme goal, is devoted to me, is free of attachment, and bears enmity toward no being -- he comes to me, O Pāṇḍava.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa explains the most essential ingredient, alongside devotion, for beholding Bhagavān. Without the pride of thinking \"I have done this\" for the work we do -- with the constant remembrance that Bhagavān has caused it to be done through our hands; with an attitude of surrender; with devotion free of any desire for its fruit; with surrender to Bhagavān free of hatred and retaliation; with a detached spirit, loving him having understood the truth that Bhagavān is supreme -- through this alone is it possible to behold Bhagavān.\n\nThus ends the eleventh chapter.",
  "12.1": "The Lord created this world and placed the jīva within this saṁsāra bound by fifteen fences. These fifteen fences are:\n\n1. The fence of faith (śraddhā): one fence that cannot be uprooted -- faith that comes from the jīva's own nature; and one fence that must be uprooted -- faith that comes from the influence of one's family, household, and the environment one grew up in.\n\n2-6. The fence of the five elements: earth-water (annamaya-kośa), fire-air-ether (prāṇamaya-kośa).\n7. The fence of the senses: the five sense-organs (jñānendriyas) and the five organs of action (karmendriyas).\n8. The fence of the inner faculty (antaḥkaraṇa): the mind that thinks ill.\n9. The fence of food (anna): sustenance.\n10. The fence of vitality (vīrya): energy.\n11. Tapaḥ: the thought \"it should become this way, it should become that way\" (like a beggar's dream).\n12. Mantra: the speech uttered to make one's dream come true.\n13. Karma: the action performed to accomplish that dream.\n14. The worlds (lokāḥ): movable and immovable possessions -- \"what I have done,\" \"what is mine.\"\n15. The fence of name (nāma): reputation.\n\nCaught inside these fifteen fences is the sixteenth -- the jīva called kṣara -- to whom this fence, like a garment, has been fitted and who has been sent into this world by the seventeenth, conscious Prakṛti, who is Lakṣmī. She is Akṣara-Puruṣā. Beyond both kṣara and akṣara is the Lord, the eighteenth, Puruṣaḥ -- Puruṣottama. Up to this point in the Gītā, the Lord has chiefly emphasized: \"One who worships me with devotion is freed from this bondage of saṁsāra and reaches me.\" Here the question that arises for us is -- why should we not, instead of the Lord, worship the seventeenth, Śrī-tattva? Isn't the mother more compassionate than the father? Wouldn't it be easier to attain mokṣa by worshipping the mother who placed us in the bondage of saṁsāra? Which path is more fitting? -- This is the question that naturally occurs to everyone.\n\nIt has been declared in praise that liberation is possible through the worship of Śrī-tattva. For example, these lines from the Śrī-sūkta, recited by everyone while performing maṅgalārati after worshipping the Lord:\n\nśriye jātaḥ śriya āniryāyaḥ śriyaṁ vayo janitṛbhyo dadhātu |\nśriyaṁ vasānāḥ amṛtatvam āyan bhajanti sadyas samidhā mitadyūn ||\n\nHere it says: \"We are born for the worship of Śrī-tattva. To one who praises Śrī-tattva she grants wealth and long life. Those who worship Śrī-tattva attain immortality. Through the worship of Śrī-tattva by couples, their cherished desires are fulfilled. She is the bestower of mokṣa.\" Since this is stated, it becomes clear that mokṣa can indeed be attained through the worship of Śrī-tattva as well. So which path is more fitting -- worship of Puruṣottama, the Lord, or worship of Śrī-tattva, the akṣara? This is the question of all of us. Our representative Arjuna begins this chapter by placing this very question of ours before the Lord. Being the last chapter of the second ṣaṭka, it contains only twenty verses. Though small, this chapter contains extraordinary matters. If we incorporate what the Lord says here into our own lives, our life becomes worthwhile. Come, let us learn the extraordinary message of devotion given in this chapter.\n\nevaṁ satata-yuktā ye bhaktās tvāṁ paryupāsate |\nye ca api akṣaram avyaktaṁ teṣāṁ ke yoga-vittamāḥ --\n\nThose devotees who, ever disciplined, worship you constantly, and those who worship the imperishable, unmanifest principle -- of these two, who are the more accomplished in yoga?\n\nOn the surface this verse looks like it asks which is better: worship of the Lord's own specific form, or worship of the formless, unmanifest Lord who is not revealed through any form. But Madhvācārya explains that Arjuna's real question here is something else altogether.\n\nNot everyone who contemplates the Lord need be a devotee (bhakta) of the Lord. The essential ingredient needed for contemplating the Lord is love; love grounded in knowledge is bhakti. In earlier times, every household kept an image in its shrine-room -- whichever chosen deity suited that family's worship. If we examine this practice, we can understand why people installed and worshipped an image in this way. First we must understand how our own mind works: the mind does not perceive what the eye has not seen. For example, if we know the Lord as \"supremely beautiful\" and sit in meditation with closed eyes, our mind ends up showing us some beautiful person we have actually seen! For this reason the ancients carved an image of the Lord with hands and feet like ours, yet unlike any human being. Holding this image in memory and meditating with closed eyes on the Lord's qualities, only that image appears to us -- not anyone we happen to know. This makes meditation far easier: the mind, instead of dwelling on some person, begins to perceive the Lord in the form of the image. For this reason a form is needed for contemplating the Lord.\n\nAs we know, Mother Śrī Lakṣmī is called by many names -- Śrī-tattva, Akṣarā, Avyaktā, Prakṛti, Hrī, and so on -- and many such references occur throughout the Bhagavad Gītā itself. As the Kaṭhopaniṣad says, \"mahataḥ param avyaktam, avyaktāt puruṣaḥ paraḥ\" -- the unmanifest principle is higher than the mahat-tattva, and the puruṣa, the Lord, is higher still than the unmanifest principle. Here it becomes clear that \"avyaktā\" and \"akṣarā\" refer to Mother Śrī Lakṣmī.\n\nConstantly holding in mind the qualities you have declared, learning your greatness and special forms through the scriptures, cultivating devotion in that, and worshipping you in this way -- is that good? Or is worship of Śrī-tattva, who is beyond the perishable (akṣara), better? Which path is more fitting -- this is Arjuna's question.",
  "12.2": "bhagavān uvāca -- The Blessed Lord said:\n\nmayi āveśya manaḥ ye māṁ nitya-yuktāḥ upāsate |\nśraddhayā parayā upetāḥ te me yukta-tamāḥ matāḥ -- Those who fix their mind on me and, ever disciplined, worship me endowed with supreme faith -- these I consider the most accomplished in yoga.\n\nKṛṣṇa answers Arjuna's question: \"Those who worship me with faith are the foremost among the seekers of the Lord.\" Here śraddhā means faith/trust. Faith is of the utmost importance in sādhanā; worship done without faith is of no use. This is a matter of the mind's own working. A worldly example: the patient and the physician. If a patient has no trust in the physician who examines him and prescribes medicine, the medicine he takes will not work on him. If his trust is deep, then whatever medicine the physician gives for whatever illness, the disease is cured! This has even been scientifically demonstrated. Therefore, worship done merely with closed eyes and no faith is useless. Meditating on the Lord's form and qualities with firm faith, having understood him, is what is called the highest devotion. With full faith one may worship the Lord through any form, through any name whatsoever. There is no form that is not the Lord's form, no name that is not the Lord's name. When faith is complete, name and form present no difficulty. Thus the Lord says: \"Whoever worships my form and special qualities with faith is the foremost among seekers.\"",
  "12.3": "ye tu akṣaram anirdeśyam avyaktam paryupāsate |\nsarvatra-gam acintyam ca kūṭa-stham acalam dhruvam --\n\n[The word-split, meaning, and explanation for these two verses (12.3 and 12.4) are given together in the next verse (12.4).]",
  "12.4": "ye tu akṣaram anirdeśyam avyaktam paryupāsate |\n\nsarvatra-gam acintyam ca kūṭa-stham acalam dhruvam ||\n\nsanniyamyendriya-grāmaṁ sarvatra sama-buddhayaḥ |\n\nte prāpnuvanti mām eva sarva-bhūta-hite ratāḥ -- the unmanifest, imperishable principle -- indescribable in\nwords, ungraspable in thought, one that pervades everywhere as if seated in the sky, unmoving, undecaying,\nthe supreme changeless principle. Restraining the group of senses, seeing with equanimity everywhere, and\nwishing well for all beings -- those who worship this unmanifest principle in this way also reach me alone.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa explains the worship of the unmanifest principle, Lakṣmī. There is one difference between worldly\nconduct and the worship of the Lord. In the world, generally, the mother always forgives her children's\nfaults while the father punishes them. But in the worship of Mother Śrī Lakṣmī and the worship of the Lord,\nthis is reversed. If there is any lapse or fault in worship, the Mother does not forgive it, but the Lord\ndoes forgive. For this reason, worship of the unmanifest, imperishable one is difficult. Here Kṛṣṇa explains\nwhat one must understand in order to worship Mother Lakṣmī, using several epithets in this verse. She whose\nbody never perishes is Akṣarā; the Prakṛti that our intellect cannot grasp is Anirdeśyā; Lakṣmī, who is\nnever manifest, is Avyaktā. All these qualities apply to the Lord as well.\n\nLakṣmī, the foundational principle of creation, dwells in the sky and is the cause of the entire universe's\nexpanse. Constant and eternal, she is forever devoted to serving the Lord's feet. Worshipping such a\npowerful deity demands rigorous control of the senses. What is essential here is to see everything with\nequanimity and to worship without the slightest lapse. [Take, for example, the recitation of the Lalitā\nSahasranāma -- even a small error there brings about an ill result.] In this way, through such strict\ndiscipline, one can reach the Lord through the worship of the unmanifest principle. But this, says Kṛṣṇa,\nis not the easy path.",
  "12.5": "kleśaḥ adhika-taraḥ teṣām avyakta-āsakta-cetasām |\navyaktā hi gatiḥ duḥkham deha-vadbhiḥ avāpyate -- Greater is the toil for those whose minds are attached to the unmanifest principle; for the embodied, the path through the unmanifest is attained only with difficulty.\n\nFor those who worship Śakti, she is pleased and grants mokṣa out of grace. But for those who worship the unmanifest, \"adhika-tara kleśaḥ\" -- the worship is very difficult. It is like travelling toward the Lord by a roundabout path instead of the direct one. Seekers must accomplish this only with great difficulty.\n\nIn the verses that follow, Kṛṣṇa explains how easy the direct worship of the Lord is. This is the easy path of bhakti that the Lord has shown us.",
  "12.6": "ye tu sarvāṇi karmāṇi mayi sannyasya mat-parāḥ |\nananyenaiva yogena māṁ dhyāyanta upāsate --\n\n[The word-split, meaning, and explanation for these two verses (12.6 and 12.7) are given together in the next verse (12.7).]",
  "12.7": "ye tu sarvāṇi karmāṇi mayi sannyasya mat-parāḥ |\n\nananyenaiva yogena māṁ dhyāyanta upāsate ||\n\nteṣām ahaṁ samuddhartā mṛtyu-saṁsāra-sāgarāt |\nbhavāmi na cirāt pārtha mayy āveśita-cetasām -- There are some who offer up everything they do to me alone; my devotion is their sole aim; they remember and serve me alone. O Pārtha, for those who have fixed their minds on me alone, I very soon free them from the ocean of the saṁsāra of death.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"The easy means of reaching me is that whatever action you perform for the maintenance of your life, offer it up to me.\" What Kṛṣṇa is saying here is the anusandhāna that should underlie every one of our actions -- the thought that \"the Lord has caused this action to be done through my hands; let it be offered up to him.\" When we hold this attitude we can become worthy of the Lord's full grace. The most essential requirement here is worshipping the Lord single-mindedly, without any other object. There may be shortfalls in our sādhanā, control of the senses may be difficult, wishing well for all beings may not always be achieved, there may be lapses in our contemplation, one-pointedness may not come easily. But Kṛṣṇa's assurance here is: \"Surrender to me, fix your mind on me -- I will lift you up.\" When we establish the Lord in our consciousness, the Lord takes on the responsibility of carrying us across this ocean of birth and death.",
  "12.8": "mayi eva manaḥ ādhatsva mayi buddhim niveśaya |\nnivasiṣyasi mayi eva ataḥ ūrdhvam na saṁśayaḥ -- Fix your mind on me alone. Establish your understanding in me. As a result, hereafter you will dwell in me alone -- of this there is no doubt.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: place your mind in me. \"Ādhāna\" means sowing a seed, or kindling a spark. One must kindle the spark of devotion to the Lord in the mind and make it blaze; one must nurture the seed of bhakti so that it grows; one must become firmly settled in a certain, decisive understanding. When the mind is thus constantly established in the Lord, one can reach the Lord without any toil. This is the explanation of how much easier worship of the Lord is compared to the worship of Śakti. There are very few restrictions on the worship of the Lord. For example, the recitation of the Viṣṇu-sahasranāma has no fixed time restriction whatsoever -- anyone, at any time, may recite the Viṣṇu-sahasranāma. The Viṣṇu-sahasranāma is a remedy for every ailment. Lapses that occur unknowingly in one's contemplation of the Lord are forgiven.\n\nWorship of Nārāyaṇa means worship of Nārāyaṇa together with Lakṣmī. Those who worship Nārāyaṇa need not separately worship Lakṣmī or the other deities. What is most pleasing to Lakṣmī and to all the deities is the worship of the Lord. All the deities help the seeker who worships Nārāyaṇa and cause him to rise higher on his path of sādhanā... Knowing this, we can easily achieve one-pointed devotion in our worship, and become firmly established on the path of bhakti.",
  "12.9": "atha cittam samādhātum na śaknoṣi mayi sthiram |\nabhyāsa-yogena tataḥ mām icchā āptum dhanañjaya -- O Dhanañjaya, if you are unable to fix your inner mind firmly on me, then through the practice of repeated discipline, cultivate the desire to attain me.\n\nThere is devotion to the Lord in the mind, there is the sprout of knowledge-filled devotion in the intellect. And yet steady remembrance does not come, the mind does not settle firmly -- what should one do then? As the śāstra says:\n\nsmartavyaḥ satataṁ viṣṇur vismartavyo na jātucit |\nsarve vidhi-niṣedhās syur etayor eva kiṅkarāḥ ||\n\nThat is: \"Viṣṇu must be remembered constantly; he must never be forgotten.\" All the injunctions and prohibitions [of the scriptures] serve only this end. Without the mind constantly resting on the Lord, this unbroken remembrance is not possible. Kṛṣṇa explains what to do in such a case: \"Pray to me: give me the strength to bring my mind to one-pointedness. Whatever one wishes for on the spiritual path, it is my responsibility to fulfil it\" -- this is Kṛṣṇa's assurance. With practice and with the will, one-pointedness is certainly attainable. Just as we train and teach children through repeated practice, so too must we coax and coax our own mind, counsel it, and make it practise. When this is done, the mind takes to it as habit. First we must give up chasing after worldly wealth and become Dhanañjaya -- winners of the priceless wealth that is the Lord.",
  "12.10": "abhyāse api asamarthaḥ asi mat-karma-paramaḥ bhava |\nmat-artham api karmāṇi kurvan siddhim avāpsyasi -- If even this practice of meditation is beyond you, then engage yourself only in actions that are a form of my worship. Even by performing action as an offering to me, you shall attain accomplishment.\n\nFor some, no matter how much they practise, one-pointed devotion does not come. In sādhanā on the spiritual path, a single lifetime's practice is not always enough for the mind; sometimes one-pointedness does not come even after an entire lifetime of practice. To such people the compassionate Kṛṣṇa says: \"Do not perform the action you undertake for your own selfish sake, but perform it with the anusandhāna that it is worship of the Lord.\" By offering every one of one's daily actions in a spirit of surrender, as worship of the Lord, one can attain accomplishment on the spiritual path.",
  "12.11": "atha etat api aśaktaḥ asi kartum mat-yogam āśritaḥ |\nsarva-karma-phala-tyāgam tataḥ kuru yata-ātmavān -- If you are unable to do even this, then take refuge in my discipline and, having steadied your mind, renounce the fruit of all actions.\n\nIf it is not possible to offer up all one's action to the Lord in the manner described above, if it is not possible to perform everything as worship of the Lord, then Kṛṣṇa says: worship however you can -- but ultimately, without any desire for the fruit, offer all your actions to the Lord. Here Kṛṣṇa places emphasis both on renunciation of the fruit of action and on offering the action itself.",
  "12.12": "śreyaḥ hi jñānam abhyāsāt jñānāt dhyānam viśiṣyate |\ndhyānāt karma-phala-tyāgaḥ tyāgāt śāntiḥ anantaram -- Better than practice without understanding is knowledge; better than mere knowledge is meditation grounded in that knowledge; better than mere meditation is meditation freed from any bond to the fruit of action. Following such meditation, the very next stage is liberation.\n\nKnowledge is superior to meditation without knowledge; knowledge is a fundamental necessity in spiritual sādhanā. Meditation or action performed without knowledge is of no use. Faith without understanding becomes blind faith; likewise, understanding without faith becomes mere arrogance. Therefore one must first come to know, and having known, one must believe. Understanding alone is not enough. Better than knowledge alone is meditation accompanied by knowledge. Greater than all of this is renunciation of the fruit of action. Therefore, first come to know, and having known, practise. But let your meditation be meditation free of any bond to the fruit of action. Through such a way of life, the attainment of mokṣa becomes possible.\n\nHaving explained up to this point how worship of the Lord ought to be, Kṛṣṇa now goes on to explain how we must shape our lives, and what our conduct and daily practice should be, if we are to live a life that pleases the Lord.",
  "12.13": "adveṣṭā sarva-bhūtānām maitraḥ karuṇaḥ eva ca |\nnirmamaḥ nirahaṅkāraḥ sama-duḥkha-sukhaḥ kṣamī -- one who bears no hatred toward any being, a friend to all, one whose heart melts on seeing suffering, one free of the pride of \"I\" and \"mine,\" one who is neither elated by pleasure nor cast down by pain, one who is unfailingly forbearing --\n\nHere in the Gītā begins the series of verses describing the qualities of the devotees who are most dear to the Lord. \"Adveṣṭā\" -- hating no one. \"Maitraḥ karuṇaḥ\" -- friendliness and compassion. \"Nirmamaḥ nirahaṅkāraḥ\" -- free of possessiveness and pride.",
  "12.14": "santuṣṭaḥ satatam yogī yata-ātmā dṛḍha-niścayaḥ |\nmayi arpita-manaḥ-buddhiḥ yaḥ mat-bhaktaḥ saḥ me priyaḥ -- one who is ever content, ever engaged in the discipline of meditation, one who has reined in his own mind, one whose mind is made firm by his settled conviction in the truth, one who has offered his mind and understanding to me -- such a devotee of mine is dear to me.\n\nDescribing the human conduct the Lord approves of, Kṛṣṇa first says: \"adveṣṭā sarva-bhūtānām\" -- hating no one. This is the first quality a devotee of the Lord must have. Hatred is of two kinds: hating the good (on seeing their advancement) and hating the wicked (on seeing their wickedness). Hatred arises from intolerance and envy -- being pained at another's flourishing is hatred. Whether the person is good or wicked, there should be no envy, no intolerance. Wherever there is goodness, love it and cultivate friendship with it; keep away from the wicked, but love goodness and good people. Melt at seeing another's suffering. Give up the pride and possessiveness that says \"I,\" \"mine,\" \"by me.\" \"īśāvāsyam idam sarvam\" -- the Lord is the master of all this; let the attitude be that whatever exists, exists because he has given it... Just as pride is not good, so too is excessive possessiveness not good. Let there be, as with the poet-saint Purandara Dāsa, a detachment even within love. Purandara Dāsa had a son whom he raised with great love. One day the beloved son died. He then lived on in detachment, singing, \"The parrot is not in the cage -- the parrot you gave has returned to you.\" We must achieve balance in how we regard pleasure and pain. When happiness comes, do not soar; when sorrow comes, do not collapse -- meet what comes. Let there be a spirit of forgiveness toward others; no retaliation. [Judges and officials may punish wrongdoers -- that is their duty-dharma; there is no retaliation in that.]\n\nContinuing, Kṛṣṇa says: \"santuṣṭaḥ satatam yogī yata-ātmā dṛḍha-niścayaḥ.\" The state of mind a person needs in order to be at ease is \"freedom from discontent.\" The cause of all our sorrows in life is discontentment. Instead of being discontented about what one lacks, be content with what one has. Practise being satisfied with whatever one has. Always be a yogī. There are many meanings to the word \"yogī\" here. Practising various postures is not yoga. The process that must be undertaken to bring the mind to one-pointedness is yoga. Holding still the mind that runs about like a monkey is like holding still the wind -- it is a very strange thing; we only realise the mind has slipped away after it has already gone. To hold such a mind still, one must first practise sitting steadily with the spine erect -- āsana (any posture will do). Then, through prāṇāyāma, awakening inner contemplation is yoga. Seating the Lord in a purified mind is yoga; carrying out, with faith and honesty, the path one has found for the conduct of one's life is yoga. Through such practice, one must keep the mind under one's own control. Practise not viewing anything with an evil disposition. Let there be an unwavering resolve on the spiritual path. Whatever comes, proceed with firm resolve. Let the mind yearn for the Lord; let the intellect come to know the Lord. \"Whoever loves me (as a devotee) in this way is most dear to me,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "12.15": "yasmāt na udvijate lokaḥ lokāt na udvijate ca yaḥ |\nharṣa-amarṣa-bhaya-udvegaiḥ muktaḥ yaḥ saḥ ca me priyaḥ -- One by whom the world is not agitated, and who is not agitated by the world; one free of exultation, resentment, fear, and agitation -- he too is dear to me.\n\nOne who causes no one pain, one who does not drive others to distress through pride, one who does not flare up in anger, one who is not afraid, one who is not shaken -- such a person is very dear to me.\n\nOthers should not be agitated on seeing us, nor should we agitate others. No one should feel intolerance or dissatisfaction on seeing us, and we should not feel intolerance on seeing others. This is achievable when we love the world unconditionally. When we do what pleases the Lord, the Lord loves us; and one whom the Lord loves is loved by the whole world.\n\nWe must free ourselves from exultation, resentment, fear, and agitation. Rejoicing in doing what ought not to be done is exultation; feeling envy on seeing another do what they ought to do is resentment. A seeker must be free of exultation and resentment. Do not do anything that should be feared, and when you have done nothing wrong, do not fear. If we look upon another with suspicion, they too look upon us with suspicion; when we do not trust others, they do not trust us either -- and from this comes agitation (tension). Agitation brings many kinds of disease to settle in our body. We must learn to live selflessly and carefree, trusting in the Lord. \"The seeker who loves everyone in life, the seeker who has conquered exultation, resentment, fear, and agitation, is dear to me,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "12.16": "anapekṣaḥ śuciḥ dakṣaḥ udāsīnaḥ gata-vyathaḥ |\nsarva-ārambha-parityāgī yaḥ mat-bhaktaḥ saḥ me priyaḥ -- one who desires nothing, one who is clean and alert, one who does not become attached to anything, one who does not lose composure, one who does not engage in any undertaking other than the worship of the Lord [one who, having offered everything to the Lord, has abandoned the pride of \"I\" and \"mine\"] -- such a devotee of mine is very dear to me.\n\nIn this verse Kṛṣṇa explains six qualities that a devotee of his must have, qualities that support devotion.\n\n(1) Anapekṣaḥ: not desiring \"I need this, I need that.\" Not chasing after whatever brings momentary pleasure, but walking on the path of eternal mokṣa; being content with whatever one gets -- this is anapekṣaḥ.\n\n(2) Śuciḥ: being pure and untroubled. This is true purity (maḍi). Bad speech in the mouth, bad thoughts in the mind, dirt on the body -- these are impurity. We must first wash the dirt from our mind; external purity supports this. For this, in the śānti-pāṭha we say: \"Oṁ bhadraṁ karṇebhiḥ śṛṇuyāma devāḥ, bhadraṁ paśyema-akṣabhir yajatrāḥ\" -- let us see what is good, speak what is good, think what is good. This is true purity.\n\nThere are two very well-known verses about purity:\n\napavitraḥ pavitro vā sarvāvasthāṁ gato'pi vā |\nyaḥ smaret puṇḍarīkākṣaṁ sa bāhya-abhyantaraḥ śuciḥ ||\n\nand\n\nati-nīla-ghana-śyāmaṁ nalina-āyata-locanam |\nsmarāmi puṇḍarīkākṣaṁ tena snāto bhavāmy aham ||\n\nHere it is said that one should remember Puṇḍarīkākṣa. This is a special name of the Lord. Puṇḍarīkākṣa means one whose eyes are like a red lotus. The Lord's eyes are always blossoming and radiant like the petals of a red lotus. Ordinarily our eyes widen when we feel great joy. But the Lord is an ever-flowing fountain of joy. The Lord, his eyes wide with love, is looking at me. I am bathing in the stream of grace flowing from his eyes; his gracious, compassionate gaze has flowed over me, and I have bathed in it -- this anusandhāna is śuci. Thus, by the Lord's grace, we must become pure both inwardly and outwardly.\n\n(3) Dakṣaḥ: we must have the skill to direct the mind toward the Lord. He cannot be won over by our outward effort alone; we must go toward him from within. Outward action is only supportive of our inner sādhanā. We must have the understanding, the discernment, of how the Lord may be reached.\n\n(4) Udāsīna: being the indweller (antaryāmin). No emphasis or insistence is needed on any outward action. For example -- someone in your family may be having some worship performed to fulfil some desired result. In such a case it is not good to oppose it, insisting that worship of the Lord done with desire for a result is wrong. Whatever it may be, it is service to the Lord; pray that it may reach the Lord. Learn to live with everyone without any insistence.\n\n(5) Gata-vyathaḥ: being always joyful, unperturbed no matter what happens.\n\n(6) Sarva-ārambha-parityāgī: not engaging in any undertaking that runs contrary to the Lord's supremacy. Give up doing every undertaking with desire for its fruit; offer everything, in the end, to the Lord with \"kṛṣṇārpaṇam astu.\"\n\n\"Whoever loves me possessing these qualities is a devotee dear to me,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "12.17": "yaḥ na hṛṣyati na dveṣṭi na śocati na kāṅkṣati |\nśubha-aśubha-parityāgī bhaktimān yaḥ saḥ me priyaḥ -- one who neither exults nor hates, neither grieves nor desires, one who has renounced [attachment to the outcomes of] both the auspicious and the inauspicious -- such a devoted one is dear to me.\n\nHere some points already said earlier are repeated. Generally, a matter is repeated to make it clearer and to give it greater emphasis. Here Kṛṣṇa says: do not exult when you accomplish something good, and do not feel disgust on seeing another. Do not feel excessive joy on getting something or sorrow or hatred on not getting it. In life, knowledge, devotion, and dispassion are auspicious; there is no need to analyse the good and bad of every other matter. Nothing in this world happens by accident. Behind every event that occurs here there is a reason unknown to us. For that reason no one is competent to judge it as auspicious or inauspicious. Living with this understanding is what makes a devotee dear to me, says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "12.18": "samaḥ śatrau ca mitre ca tathā māna-apamānayoḥ |\nśīta-uṣṇa-sukha-duḥkheṣu samaḥ saṅga-vivarjitaḥ -- one who makes no distinction between foe and friend; one who regards honour and dishonour, cold and heat, pleasure and pain, alike; one who is not attached to anything --\n\nThe sequence describing the qualities of the devotee dear to the Lord continues. \"Samaḥ śatrau ca mitre ca\" -- making no distinction between enemy and friend. \"Mānāpamānayoḥ\" -- equanimity in honour and dishonour. \"Saṅga-vivarjitaḥ\" -- having renounced attachment.",
  "12.19": "tulya-nindā-stutiḥ maunī santuṣṭaḥ yena kenacit |\naniketaḥ sthira-matiḥ bhaktimān me priyaḥ naraḥ -- one who regards praise and blame alike; one who is thoughtful and restrained in speech; one who is content with whatever he has; one who has no fixed dwelling [one who is fixed in the Lord]; one whose understanding of the Lord has grown firm -- such a devoted person is dear to me.\n\nOrdinarily we habitually form the notion, in worldly terms, of \"enemy\" or \"friend.\" Those who praise us are our friends; those who point out our faults are our enemies. This is our unenlightened conduct. Behind friendship there may be hidden enmity, and behind enmity there may be hidden friendship. We may recall here what the poet-saint Purandara Dāsa sang -- that \"there should be those who reproach us.\" By reproaching us, those who reproach take upon themselves the fruit of the reproachable faults within us. Those who praise, by praising, take a share in the fruit of the merit of the goodness within us. Therefore, on the path of sādhanā, those who reproach are more helpful than those who praise. For this reason, do not make the distinction \"enemy, friend\" and go on to criticise anyone or nurse hatred. Stay far from bad qualities. Love good qualities. Wherever there is goodness, keep company with it (mैत्री); melt on seeing suffering (karuṇā); rejoice on seeing another's joy (muditā); stay detached from wickedness (upekṣā). Fundamentally, there should be no division into \"enemy\" and \"friend.\" See the Lord in everyone. Give up the worldly notion of enemy and friend.\n\nDo not swell with pride when honoured. Do not shrink when insulted. Conquer both honour and dishonour. What another says of us is not what matters; what matters is what our own inner self is. When another praises us, no quality we do not actually have comes and joins us. Likewise, when another criticises us, we do not actually go lower. We must be watchful about praise, because it is more likely to lead us astray. From insult there is a chance that we may leave the wrong path and, walking the right path, rise higher.\n\nRegard pleasure and pain alike. No object gives us pleasure or pain by itself; it is the disposition with which we view that object that gives us pleasure or pain. Therefore cultivate the habit of regarding pleasure and pain alike, just as cold and heat are regarded alike.\n\nIt may seem to us that all these tendencies are simply natural to human beings. But this is not our natural dharma. It is through our own attachment that these unwanted qualities fill us. Therefore do not react at once to praise and blame. Be restrained in speech (maunī). Reflect on the matter. Do not act rashly. Speak as little as you possibly can. Speak having thought it over. Whatever you have, experience it with contentment. Do not go chasing after movable and immovable possessions. Live joyfully and free of anxiety in whatever the Lord has given you. When we surrender to the Lord in devotion, we can rise to this height on our path of sādhanā. \"The devotee who has such qualities is dear to me,\" says Kṛṣṇa.",
  "12.20": "ye tu dharma-amṛtam idam yathā-uktam paryupāsate |\nśraddadhānāḥ mat-paramāḥ bhaktāḥ te atīva me priyāḥ -- Those who thus practise, in the manner described here, this immortal dharma [that is the means to dharma and mokṣa], who, holding faith in my greatness and walking accordingly, have placed their devotion in me -- they are exceedingly dear to me.\n\nHaving explained what our devotion should be like, and what qualities a devotee must possess, Kṛṣṇa, describing this immortal dharma (the means to mokṣa) here, states the fruit of it: \"Those who worship in the manner explained here, who have cultivated these qualities of dharma, who bring it into practice through devotion -- such devotees are supremely dear to me.\"\n\nThus ends the twelfth chapter.",
  "13.1": "bhagavān uvāca -- The Blessed Lord said:\n\nidam śarīram kaunteya kṣetram iti abhidhīyate |\netad yaḥ vetti tam prāhuḥ kṣetrajñaḥ iti tad-vidaḥ -- This entire moving and unmoving universe, which is like the Lord's own body, is called \"kṣetra\" (the field). One who knows this, those who know call \"kṣetrajña\" (the knower of the field).\n\nThe word \"kṣetra\" has no single fixed meaning. In this verse Kṛṣṇa gives us the clue as to how the word kṣetra should be understood here. Kṛṣṇa says kṣetra means \"idaṁ śarīram\" -- this body. So here kṣetra means the dwelling-house in which the jīva lives -- everything from the piṇḍāṇḍa (the individual body) up to the brahmāṇḍa (the whole universe). Calling this brahmāṇḍa \"kṣetra\" and \"śarīra\" is an apt name (anvartha-nāma). Whoever knows this kṣetra in its entirety is the kṣetrajña. Who is this? Let us see in the next verse... kṣetrajñaṁ ca...",
  "13.2": "kṣetrajñam ca api mām viddhi sarva-kṣetreṣu bhārata |\nkṣetra-kṣetrajñayoḥ jñānam yat tat jñānam matam mama -- O Bhārata, know that it is I alone who dwell in all fields, and who am the \"kṣetrajña.\" The knowledge of both the field and the knower of the field, that alone is what I hold to be true knowledge.\n\nThe Lord, filling everything within and without, present in every field, is the kṣetrajña. The jīva is merely kṣetra-stha (situated in the field). True knowledge means awareness of the universe -- awareness of the Lord who fills the universe. How do we stand within this universe? How does this universe bind us? How do we cast off this bondage and reach that universal Self, the Lord? This very awareness is knowledge. As stated in the previous chapter, we are in a prison of fifteen fences. But this is not punishment -- it is education. To be freed from this bondage, one must come to know the kṣetrajña who liberates us from saṁsāra. This very vigilance is true knowledge.",
  "13.3": "tat kṣetram yat ca yādṛk ca yat vikāri yataḥ ca yat |\nsaḥ ca yaḥ yat prabhāvaḥ ca tat samāsena me śṛṇu -- What is that kṣetra? What is it like? What are its transformations? Whence do they come? Who is its propelling power? What is he like? What are his glories? Hear this from me in brief.\n\nFirst, what is kṣetra? How and from what are the brahmāṇḍa and piṇḍāṇḍa constructed? What are its properties? In what various ways does it transform? How do these changes occur? By whom do they occur? Hear all of this from me in the form of a brief, condensed formula.",
  "13.4": "ṛṣibhiḥ bahudhā gītam chandobhiḥ vividhaiḥ pṛthak |\nbrahma-sūtra-padaiḥ ca eva hetumadbhiḥ viniścitaiḥ -- This has been sung by the sages in many ways, through various different mantras -- and so too through the reasoned, decisive words of the Brahma-sūtra.",
  "13.5": "Even the wise speak of this matter each in their own way, limited by their own experience. What is stated in the Brahma-sūtra is not easily understood. Therefore I, who am myself the kṣetrajña, will tell you the truth directly -- listen.",
  "13.6": "mahā-bhūtāni [mahān bhūtāni] ahaṅkāraḥ buddhiḥ avyaktam eva ca |\nindriyāṇi daśa ekam ca pañca ca indriya-gocarāḥ ||\n\nicchā dveṣaḥ sukham duḥkham saṅghātaḥ cetanā dhṛtiḥ |\netat kṣetram samāsena savikāram udāhṛtam -- Earth [Pṛthivī], water [Varuṇa], fire [Agni], air [Marīci, son of Vāyu], and sky [the deity of space, Vināyaka-Gaṇapati who destroys obstacles] -- these are the five great elements. The principle of ego [Brahma-Vāyu and Śiva], the principle of intellect [Pārvatī], primal matter [Śrī], the ear [Candra], the skin [Marut, son of Vāyu], the eye [Sūrya], the tongue [Varuṇa], the nose [the Aśvins], the mouth [Agni], the hands [the two sons of Vāyu], the feet [Indra's sons Yajña and Śambhu], the anus [Yama], the organ of generation [the deity Śiva for men, Svāyambhuva Manu for women] -- thus ten senses. The eleventh is the principle of mind [Indra, Kāma, and Aniruddha], and the five objects of the senses -- sound, touch, form, taste, and smell [Rudra's sons Prāṇa, Apāna, Vyāna, Udāna, and Samāna] -- these twenty-four together constitute the kṣetra, in brief.\n\nDesire [Śrī and Bhāratī], aversion [Dvāpara-Śakuni], pleasure [Mukhya-prāṇa-Vāyu-Bhīma], pain [Kali-Duryodhana], the composite body [the presiding jīva within the body; all the presiding deities together], the range of memory [Śrī], and fortitude [Sarasvatī and Bhāratī] -- these seven are the special activities called the transformations (vikāra). Thus, along with its transformations, the kṣetra has been stated in brief.\n\nIn these two verses Kṛṣṇa has explained the kṣetra and the transformations of the kṣetra. Here Kṛṣṇa has explained, in condensed, formulaic form, the constituent elements of the world that lie within the single unbroken unit called kṣetra. First, the five great elements (pañca-mahābhūta). From these five, this piṇḍāṇḍa (the individual body) and this brahmāṇḍa (the universe) are constructed. For this reason the universe is called \"pra-pañca\" (\"composed of the five\"). These five great elements are the basic substance of the world. Within the piṇḍāṇḍa, these five great elements are called the food-sheath (annamaya) and the vital-sheath (prāṇamaya). The visible gross body is made of the earth and water portions. Within this gross body lies the invisible prāṇamaya-kośa: the body's heat (fire), the breath (air), and the space within for the movement of blood and air (ether). Beyond this lies the mind-sheath (manomaya-kośa), comprising the mind and intellect; beyond that lies the vijñānamaya-kośa, the faculty of memory. Hence, prior to the intellect come ego and consciousness (citta). Here, ego (ahaṅkāra) means \"the awareness of one's own existence.\" This principle of ego is called citta in the individual body, and mahat-tattva in the universe as a whole.\n\nThe root of this manifest world is the unmanifest. The primal state of creation is the ocean of dissolution -- that is, the unmanifest world scattered in atomic form. Within such a world, the four-faced Brahmā created the mahat-tattva. The very arising of the awareness \"I\" in the beings who, within the ocean of dissolution, had no awareness of their own existence -- that itself is the creation of the mahat-tattva. The scope of citta is cetanā (consciousness) -- this is the extent of our memory.\n\nIn this body there are five sense-organs of knowledge, five organs of action, and the eleventh sense, which is the mind. The ear grasps sound, the eye grasps form, the nose grasps smell, the tongue grasps taste, the skin grasps touch (air). Likewise, the mouth is for speaking, the hands for working, the feet for walking. These eight are called the \"graha\" (sense organs). Then come the anus and the organ of generation (for excretion and reproduction). The mind, the eleventh sense, is what subjects all of these to thought. All of these are the constituent elements of the kṣetra; taken together, this is the kṣetra. [Each of the units described here also has its own presiding deity, as already briefly given above.]\n\nThis world is always changing. For this reason the world is also called aśvattha. The horse (aśva) is a creature in constant motion; it is never still. Since this world too is likewise always in motion, the ancients called it aśvattha. Our body is a physical transformation (vikāra); the mental world's every turmoil arising within it is a mental transformation. In particular, our desire (icchā), aversion (dveṣa), pleasure and pain (sukha-duḥkha), the range of our memory (cetanā), and fortitude (dhṛti) -- this is Kṛṣṇa's formulaic explanation of the kṣetra and its transformations. We may analyse this matter as extensively as we wish; Kṛṣṇa has explained everything here in condensed form.\n\nIf one is to walk the path of knowledge, there are certain codes of conduct we must follow in our own behaviour. We must follow these. Kṛṣṇa explains, in the next five verses, the twenty virtues we must adopt in our lives. The more we incorporate each of these virtues into our lives, the further we can walk the path of knowledge. Otherwise, we will have to walk the path of ignorance -- Kṛṣṇa gives us this warning here too.",
  "13.7": "amānitvam adambhitvam ahiṁsā kṣāntiḥ ārjavam |\nācāryopāsanam śaucam sthairyam ātma-vinigrahaḥ ||7||\n\namānitvam adambhitvam ahiṁsā kṣāntiḥ ārjavam |\nācārya-upāsanam śaucam sthairyam ātma-vinigrahaḥ -- Giving up self-importance, not putting on a show of greatness, not causing hurt, forbearance, straightforwardness of conduct and speech, service of the teacher, purity of body and mind, unwavering judgment, firmness of resolve --\n\n(1) Amānitvam: To gain knowledge we must give up the desire for honour and esteem. One who expects to be honoured, to receive praise, never attains knowledge. For this, we must first give up the love of publicity. Give up all self-promotion and pursue knowledge -- then knowledge is attained. If anyone approves of you, that is their generosity -- remain humble in that.\n\n(2) Adambhitvam: Learn to live as the smallest before everyone. No display of grandeur. Never veer from honesty on the path of knowledge. Do not try to show that you have what you do not have.\n\n(3) Ahiṁsā: Never hurt another. If we wound another's mind, there is no medicine that can heal it again. Therefore never cause another loss of face. Not causing trouble to another is ahiṁsā.\n\n(4) Kṣāntiḥ: Not retaliating. When someone does us wrong, we should not go about doing wrong back to them. There is a system for punishing wrongdoers. Worldly speaking, the responsibility for punishing a wrongdoer belongs to the police and the judicial system, not to us. If we set out to punish a wrongdoer ourselves, that becomes the crime of taking the law into our own hands. In the same way, in the Lord's kingdom there is a separate arrangement for punishing wrongdoers. Therefore you have no right either to take retribution or to curse.\n\nThe finest illustration of the above qualities is Jaḍa-Bharata and the sage Raikva. If we read their life stories, we learn how they upheld this quality in their own lives.\n\n(5) Ārjavam: A life without masks. Straightforward conduct and speech. An honest life, with nothing different inside from what is shown outside.\n\n(6) Ācāryopāsanam: An ācārya is one who has thoroughly understood what is right and what is wrong, and who brings that into practice -- one who has seen the truth and lives it out before society. Serve such people. The wise are not easily found, and they generally do not impart knowledge without first testing the seeker. Whatever happens, do not give up pursuing the wise. If the thirst for knowledge within you is deep, they will certainly impart knowledge to you. If you want knowledge, you must wait until the wise open themselves to you. The finest illustration of this is the story of Śvetaketu, and of Pippalāda in the Praśnopaniṣad.\n\n(7) Śaucam: Śauca means purity. We have already looked at this subject to some extent in the previous chapter. In Kannada, śaucam means \"maḍi\" (ritual purity). We must give up merely performing maḍi and instead learn to become maḍi. If you bathe, put on wet clothes, and touch no one, that alone does not make you maḍi. Purity is fundamentally a matter of the mind; bodily purity supports it. Forgetting worldly concerns and establishing the mind firmly in the Lord -- that is maḍi. We must become maḍi in body, speech, and mind. If a bad thought arises in us, if bad words come to our mouth -- then we are impure. One who is truly pure in body, speech, and mind, if seated among the impure, can make everyone around them pure. This is what was once called \"paṅkti-pāvana\" (one who purifies the row he sits in). The row in which a fully pure person sits becomes pure. Purity without the knowledge of scripture is not true purity. When remembrance of the Lord is in the mind and the Lord's name is on the lips, we are maḍi.\n\nTo purify the mind, there is ācamana and prāṇāyāma. With the contemplation \"O Lord, by the stream of your compassion, bring nectar down from my sahasrāra, make my entire body sacred, let all the sins and impurities within me burn away and be washed clean\" -- one should meditatively dispel the sin-personified (pāpa-puruṣa). Here we should actually experience the stream of nectar descending from our head.\n\nSthairyam: Steadfastness of mind is sthairya. We need a resolve of our very own. We should not fall into confusion, changing our decision because someone said so. We must be capable of thinking for ourselves. Whatever question arises for us, the capacity to find its answer lies within our own head. \"Wherever the question is, the answer is right there too.\" Study the scriptures well and find the answer to your own problem yourself, and work with resolve. Achieve steadfastness without being thrown into confusion by hearing this person and that.\n\n(9) Ātma-vinigrahaḥ: Control of the senses (self-control). Our senses must remain under our own control. For the senses to remain under control, we must keep our mind under control. Do not become a monkey in the hands of your own mind -- control the mind. For this, establish your mind firmly in the Lord. Thus, through control of the mind, achieve control of the senses.",
  "13.8": "indriya-artheṣu vairāgyam anahaṅkāraḥ eva ca |\njanma mṛtyu jarā vyādhi duḥkha doṣa anudarśanam -- Not losing oneself in the objects of the senses, freedom from arrogance, and an ever-watchful awareness of the flaws of a life burdened with birth, death, old age, and disease --\n\n(10) Indriyārtheṣu vairāgya: Restraining the senses from flowing outward toward the external world, and establishing the mind firmly in the Lord. This is called śama-dama. Preventing the senses from flowing toward worldly matters is dama, and making them flow toward the Lord is śama. When one truly knows the Lord, the senses no longer flow toward anything else. Cultivate dispassion toward worldly sound, touch, form, taste, and smell. For example, the tongue is fickle. Some among us, no matter how food is cooked at home, keep finding fault -- this isn't right, that isn't right, and so on. Give up this kind of fickleness. Here, without any selecting of what one likes or dislikes, learn to accept whatever is served as the Lord's prasāda. In this way, achieve śama-dama.\n\n(11) Anahaṅkāra: This is the finest quality of all, superior to every other. Whatever quality we may possess becomes worthless before our own ego. Ego (attachment) destroys every quality within us. Wherever ego exists, no other quality can truly exist. For this reason Kṛṣṇa emphasises it here with \"eva ca.\" Thoughts like \"I am learned, I am disciplined, I am pure\" -- ego haunts us in this way in every single matter. If we indulge in this kind of ego, our discipline ceases to be discipline at all. Wherever ego takes hold, sādhanā becomes futile. Wherever ego exists, the Lord is not present there -- meaning that because of ego, we move away from the Lord. For this reason, give up the pride of thinking \"I did this, this happened because of me.\" Show gratitude to the Lord, recognising that he caused it to be done through your hands.\n\n(12) Janma-mṛtyu-jarā-vyādhi-duḥkha-doṣa-anudarśanam: See life with insight. This life is a stream flowing between the two banks called birth and death. In this stream flows an unbroken chain of waves called disease, old age, sorrow, and grief. Reflect deeply on what the purpose of our life is, why the Lord brought us into being, and how a human birth ought to be made worthwhile. Life is not merely food, sleep, fear, and procreation -- even animals experience these. Therefore, study the scriptures and cultivate a true vision in keeping with the truth. Give up living merely in expectation of worldly pleasure -- live instead for the realisation of the truth, for reaching the Lord. All worldly pleasure and wealth are without substance. Come to know the substantial Lord who lies beyond all this, and take your steps toward him.",
  "13.9": "asaktiḥ anabhiṣvaṅgaḥ putra dāra gṛha ādiṣu |\nnityam ca sama-cittatvam iṣṭa aniṣṭa upapattiṣu ||\n\n[The word-division, meaning, and explanation of these three verses (13.9, 13.10, and 13.11) are given together in the next verse (13.11).]",
  "13.10": "mayi ca ananya-yogena bhaktiḥ avyabhicāriṇī |\nvivikta-deśa-sevitvam aratiḥ jana-saṁsadi |\n\n[The word-division, meaning, and explanation of these three verses (13.9, 13.10, and 13.11) are given together in the next verse (13.11).]",
  "13.11": "asaktiḥ anabhiṣvaṅgaḥ putra dāra gṛha ādiṣu |\nnityam ca sama-cittatvam iṣṭa aniṣṭa upapattiṣu ||\n\nmayi ca ananya-yogena bhaktiḥ avyabhicāriṇī |\nvivikta-deśa-sevitvam aratiḥ jana-saṁsadi |\n\nadhyātma-jñāna-nityatvam tattva-jñāna-artha-darśanam |\netat jñānam iti proktam ajñānam yat ataḥ anyathā -- Not becoming attached, not clinging excessively to children, wife, home, and the like; unfailing equanimity of mind when the wished-for and the unwished-for occur; unwavering devotion to me alone, never turning elsewhere; dwelling in solitude; not mixing in crowds; constant abidance in spiritual awareness; study of scripture for the sake of direct realisation [the direct vision of the Lord who is the very object of that spiritual knowledge] -- these are knowledge and the means to knowledge. Whatever is other than this is ignorance and the path of ignorance.\n\n(13) Asaktiḥ: \"Sakti\" means clinging or becoming attached. We have discussed this matter many times before. The root cause of all our sorrow is \"sakti\" (attachment). Therefore do not become attached to anything. At birth, death brought nothing; at death, one takes nothing away. Therefore keep your distance from this worldly attachment. Accept what comes as the Lord's prasāda, and be content when it departs, knowing it to be the Lord's will.\n\n(14) Anabhiṣvaṅgaḥ putra-dāra-gṛha-ādiṣu: Abhiṣvaṅga means over-attachment. Kṛṣṇa says do not become excessively attached to home, spouse, children, and the like. To not be attached to one's home and family does not mean abandoning everything and going to the forest. It only means giving up the clinging that says, \"I cannot live without this.\" Watch whatever comes in life as a witness. Life itself is a drama; in it you are both the actor and the spectator. The Lord is the director. Do not carry the excessive clinging that says, \"My children alone are my life, my spouse alone is my life, my earnings alone are my life, I have no existence apart from this.\" Love -- but do not become so attached that you are consumed with grief or fall into despair. When one learns to live this way, life becomes beautiful. If our children become distant from us, if our spouse becomes distant, if we lose our wealth (home, property, and so on), there is no sorrow or despair. Living this way, whatever tragedy comes in life, we are able to accept it with the feeling that it is merely our part to play -- there is no shock.\n\n(15) Nityam ca sama-cittatvam iṣṭa-aniṣṭa-upapattiṣu: In life, welcome events may occur, and unwelcome events may occur. Learn to remain even-minded at all times, without losing your mental balance when the welcome and unwelcome arrive. What we consider unwelcome need not actually be a misfortune, nor need what we desired actually be good for us. Therefore, learn to accept whatever comes without labelling it welcome or unwelcome, with the thought, \"I am not the doer, Hari is the doer.\" Every event that occurs in our life is a practical training that the Lord has designed for us. Live knowing the truth that the Lord gives us sorrow in order to make us wise.\n\n(16) Mayi ca ananya-yogena bhaktiḥ avyabhicāriṇī: Let there be constant, unmixed devotion to the Lord. The Lord is one; his names are many. Hold unswerving devotion toward him. Here, one-pointed devotion is very important. Worshipping a different deity each day, or worshipping many deities out of the fear that worshipping one might anger another -- this becomes infidelity (vyabhicāra). Therefore Kṛṣṇa emphasises here: \"let your devotion be unswerving and free of infidelity.\" Let there be in you the conviction that the Lord is all-capable, all-powerful, the cause of the world's origin and all else. Without faith in the Lord, reading any scripture is of no use. Only through complete faith in the Lord is the pursuit of knowledge possible.\n\n(17) Vivikta-deśa-sevitvam: As far as possible, try to live in a place of the highest sāttvika vibration, among sāttvika people. A place with a fully unbroken sāttvika vibration is called a place of pilgrimage (puṇya-kṣetra). Live in such a place, among good people who speak well and think well. If this is not possible, practise solitude as much as you can. Do not waste your precious birth in idle chatter with a group of people.\n\n(18) Aratiḥ jana-saṁsadi: Wherever crowds gather for unwanted matters, stay away from such crowds.\n\n(19) Adhyātma-jñāna-nityatvam: Remain constantly in spiritual awareness. First know the piṇḍāṇḍa and the brahmāṇḍa; then know the extraordinary wonder of the mind that comes to know these; then know what \"I\" actually means (awareness of self). Having known this, know the Supreme Self who governs this self. This is spiritual knowledge (adhyātma-jñāna). Let the goal of your study be this spiritual knowledge.\n\n(20) Tattva-jñāna-artha-darśanam: Let your study be devoted to knowing the fundamental truth of the world. Through all the qualities above, attain the direct realisation of the Lord who is the very subject-matter of all the scriptures and who is expounded by all the scriptures.\n\nThe qualities described here are themselves knowledge and the means to knowledge. Whatever is other than this is ignorance, and the goal of ignorance.\n\nHaving explained the means to knowledge, Kṛṣṇa, in the next six verses, places before us a description of the Lord who lies beyond the limits of words. Kṛṣṇa here beautifully describes what the Lord is like and how his form should be contemplated. In these verses we may notice many things that appear, one after another, to contradict each other. For example: the Lord is nirguṇa (without qualities), yet he is complete with every quality; the Lord is unmoving, yet he moves; the Lord is formless, yet he has form; the Lord is impossible to know, yet he can be known; the Lord has no senses, yet he has senses; he is far away, yet he is near -- and so on. Such a description can be given of none but the Lord. The explanation of all of this can be seen in the verses that follow.",
  "13.12": "jñeyam yat tat pravakṣyāmi yat jñātvā amṛtam aśnute |\nanādi mat-param brahma na sat tat na asat ucyate -- I will now tell you of that which is to be known, knowing which one attains the deathless. The beginningless Brahman, subordinate to none but me [without a birth even from a body], is that which is to be known. It is said to be other than the visible earth-water-fire, and other than the invisible air and space too.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa, describing the object of knowledge (jñeya) that is to be known through the means of knowledge, says: \"That which words cannot state directly, the knowledge by knowing which you will taste immortality -- that I will tell you.\" The Lord is anādi-mat -- beginningless. He has no form at all made of the perishable five elements. He is of the very nature of knowledge and bliss. Every quality and every action of the Lord is likewise beginningless. The Lord, full of every good quality, is the one who gives us completeness. He is the protector of all; there is no principle greater than him. Such a Lord we cannot know in full -- he is beyond the reach of our comprehension. And yet, we can know him truly, to the extent of our own capacity.",
  "13.13": "sarvataḥ pāṇi-pādam tat sarvataḥ akṣi śiraḥ mukham |\nsarvataḥ śruti-mat loke sarvam āvṛtya tiṣṭhati -- Everywhere it has hands and feet; everywhere eyes, heads, and mouths; everywhere it has ears; it stands pervading everything within the world.\n\nThe Lord is full everywhere. He has hands and feet everywhere; ears, face, mouth, and eyes everywhere. (In Sanskrit, mukha means both \"face\" and \"mouth\" -- in this rich language there is no separate word for \"mouth\" alone.) For this reason we can perform no action without the Lord's knowledge. He is omniscient, all-pervading, all-capable. Scripture (śruti) certifies all of this. It was the Lord who uttered the Veda at the beginning. He stands pervading everything, within and without. Within the undivided Lord are his infinite forms. Every one of his forms has senses that are of the very nature of knowledge and bliss, intrinsic to his own being. He stands filling every one of our senses and directs them.",
  "13.14": "sarva-indriya-guṇa-ābhāsam sarva-indriya-vivarjitam |\nasaktam sarva-bhṛt ca eva nirguṇam guṇa-bhoktṛ ca -- It is that which illumines all the senses and all their objects; yet it possesses no senses of its own. Unattached to anything, yet bearing everything. Unconnected to the three guṇas, yet the very ground of every good quality.\n\nAll the living beings in this world grasp the objects of their senses through their senses only because of the Lord. Such a Lord has no senses at all that are made of the five gross elements, that are perishable, that will one day come to an end like ours, or that are different from his own essential nature. That Lord who bears this world as its father is male. He who gave birth to this world is also its mother. Physically he is neither male nor female. In his essential nature he is both male and female. The Lord fills everyone, yet he is not attached to anything. He bears everything, yet... he has no bond of attachment to it. The Lord is beyond the three guṇas; he has no connection with the three guṇas. Yet he is the very ground of all good qualities. He is both formless and possessed of form.",
  "13.15": "bahiḥ antaḥ ca bhūtānām acaram caram eva ca |\nsūkṣmatvāt tat avijñeyam dūra-stham ca antike ca tat -- It is outside living beings; it is inside them too; and yet it moves everywhere. It is subtler than the atom; for this very reason it is beyond the reach of comprehension. Though far away, it is near to everyone.\n\nBhūta here means the entire universe, all living beings (piṇḍāṇḍa). The Lord fills this universe within and without. As the Nārāyaṇa Upaniṣad says: \"antarbahiścha tat sarvaṁ vyāpya nārāyaṇaḥ sthitaḥ\" -- the Lord, Nārāyaṇa, stands filling and pervading everything, within us and outside us. From the tiniest living being up to the four-faced Brahmā, the Lord fills everyone within and without. Within us he stands as the antaryāmin (indweller), present in image-form, and directs us; and standing outside us he sustains us. The Lord who reveals himself in the inner being of the wise is, to the ignorant, \"bahiḥ\" (very far away). The all-pervading Lord is unmoving, yet his infinite forms are always in motion. The Lord fills and stands within every moving and unmoving living being. The Lord fills even the atom, as an atom. The Lord is knowable (jñeya), yet not fully knowable (vijñeya). We can know something of the Lord's detail, but we cannot know him in full. We are like a tiny fish in the ocean; just as that fish cannot know the vastness of the ocean, no living being in this world can fully know the Lord -- because he is too subtle and too vast for our mind to grasp.",
  "13.16": "avibhaktam ca bhūteṣu vibhaktam iva ca sthitam |\nbhūta-bhartṛ ca tat jñeyam grasiṣṇu prabhaviṣṇu ca -- It exists as one and undivided in all beings, and yet stands as if divided into many. Know that it alone is what sustains all beings, what devours them, and what brings them into being.\n\nThe Lord dwells in every living being of this world in image-form (bimba-rūpa). He is present in each and every living being in many forms. Here every living being is a separate, divided thing (vibhakta), but the Lord alone is undivided (avibhakta), unbroken. The Lord who is present everywhere is one, yet he appears to us as if he were different in each place. The finest example of this is the Lord's own avatāras -- Paraśurāma, Veda-Vyāsa, and Śrī Kṛṣṇa. Here the Lord appeared to us in three forms at one and the same time. Though one, he appeared to us as three. He dwells within every living being, yet he alone bears every living being. The Lord is the creator of all, and he alone is also the one who will one day dissolve his own creation. And after the dissolution, he alone is the one who re-creates the creation.",
  "13.17": "jyotiṣām api tat jyotiḥ tamasaḥ param ucyate |\njñānam jñeyam jñāna-gamyam hṛdi sarvasya viṣṭhitam -- It is the light of all lights, said to be beyond all darkness. It is the very form of knowledge. It knows itself. It is that which can be attained through knowledge. It dwells established in the heart of everyone.\n\nWhat Kṛṣṇa has explained so far is difficult for us to grasp in our meditation. For this reason Kṛṣṇa, in this verse, describes himself in an emotionally accessible way. The Lord is light without any darkness. That light has no boundary; it is infinite. The Lord is the great luminary (mahā-jyoti) who gives light to all other lights. When we sit with closed eyes seeking to see the Lord in meditation, we may see some object or person we love. At that point we must try to see the Lord within that person or object (because the Lord is the indweller of all -- he fills everything within and without). Next, we should contemplate: \"The Lord is present in the sun; solar energy is his gift.\" Then we should try to see the Lord's feet at the centre of the solar system. We must experience in meditation how the light springing from the tip of that foot enters and illumines our heart. This is the process by which, step by step in meditation, we come to see the Lord. The Lord is the one who has given light to every light in this world. The capacity to illumine our inner being belongs to the Lord alone. The light that is the Lord is beyond darkness; it is the light of the very nature of knowledge and bliss. The Lord alone knows the Lord in full. We must make the effort to know him to the extent of our own capacity. The Lord dwells established in our very heart. Utterly unlike anything else, subtler than the subtlest, he dwells at the centre of our heart-lotus. Such a Lord can be known only through knowledge accompanied by devotion.",
  "13.18": "iti kṣetram tathā jñānam jñeyam ca uktam samāsataḥ |\nmat-bhaktaḥ etat vijñāya mat-bhāvāya upapadyate -- Thus \"kṣetra,\" \"jñāna,\" and \"jñeya\" have been stated in brief. My devotee, having come to understand this, becomes established in my very being.\n\nIn this verse Kṛṣṇa, summing up what has been said so far, says: \"Up to now, the kṣetra, the means of knowledge, and the Lord who is to be known through these means of knowledge, have been explained in a nutshell. Once one knows creation, the created world, the manner of the world's creation, and the Lord who is the creator, nothing further remains to be known. The devotee who comes to know this with complete faith and devotion reaches me,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Knowledge of the Lord together with devotion is capable of leading us toward mokṣa.\n\nIn the next verse Kṛṣṇa explains the concept of primal creation and its contemplation. This verse is Kṛṣṇa's answer to every confusion that exists in the spiritual world regarding creation. Kṛṣṇa places all of this before us, in condensed form, through this verse.",
  "13.19": "prakṛtim puruṣam ca eva viddhi anādī ubhau api |\nvikārān ca guṇān ca eva viddhi prakṛti-sambhavān -- Know that both Prakṛti [insentient prakṛti and sentient prakṛti] and Puruṣa [the jīva and the Lord] are without beginning. Know that the gross forms and the three guṇas are the workings of Prakṛti.\n\nThe Lord has not created any jīva, nor has he created Prakṛti. Insentient prakṛti, sentient prakṛti, the Supreme Puruṣa (the Lord), and the subordinate puruṣas (the jīvas) -- all of these are beginningless and eternal. \"The world\" means the transformation (vikāra) of prakṛti. When a child is born, what occurs there is not the creation of a jīva; it is the creation of a form for the jīva. The jīva, by way of the father's body, enters the mother's womb and takes on a form there. In just this way, the Lord creates a form for the jīvas through prakṛti. At the very root of creation too, Lakṣmī-Nārāyaṇa stood as mother and father and set the creation of the world in motion. At the beginning of creation, the jīvas resided within the womb of Mother Lakṣmī. Mother Lakṣmī resided within the womb of the Supreme Puruṣa, the Lord. At the root of creation, emerging from the crown of Lakṣmī's head, and by way of the Lord's navel, the four-faced Brahmā was born. For this reason the four-faced Brahmā is called one who was born from Lakṣmī's head and from the Lord's navel. In sum: \"The insentient prakṛti that is the cause of the world is eternal; the jīva, given form from that insentient prakṛti and thus created, is also eternal; Lakṣmī-Nārāyaṇa, who are the causal puruṣa for this insentient prakṛti and for the jīva, are also eternal; the world means the transformation of prakṛti.\" This is the essential matter we must understand here.\n\nFirst of all, the power needed for the expansion of creation was created. That itself is the subtlest transformation of prakṛti -- the three guṇas. By the vibration of this power, the entire world came into being in its unmanifest form. It was Mother Lakṣmī who gave it form; it was the jīvas who received form; it is Nārāyaṇa who gives form. For this reason, from the four-faced Brahmā down to every single deity and jīva, all are the children of Lakṣmī-Nārāyaṇa.\n\nIn the verse above, Kṛṣṇa spoke of Puruṣa and Prakṛti. [Puruṣa means the individual soul and the Supreme Soul; Prakṛti means insentient prakṛti and sentient prakṛti.] Kṛṣṇa places before us, in the next three verses, an explanation of these four roles in the process of creation. In the first of these verses (verse 20), Kṛṣṇa explains jīvātmā and Paramātmā...",
  "13.20": "kārya-kāraṇa-kartṛtve hetuḥ prakṛtiḥ ucyate |\npuruṣaḥ sukha-duḥkhānām bhoktṛtve hetuḥ ucyate -- In the making of effects and instruments [the body and the senses], insentient prakṛti [Śrī-tattva, who is sentient prakṛti] is said to be the cause. Puruṣa [the Supreme Self] is said to be the cause in the experiencing of pleasure and pain.\n\nIn this verse Kṛṣṇa lays out two distinct matters for us. We have a body. Within the body there are five senses for experiencing the enjoyment of pleasure and pain. Thus: the body, the senses within the body, and the enjoyment of pleasure and pain through the senses -- this is life. For the jīva, the agency behind the effects and their instruments accords with the karma it performed before. That is, in accordance with our karma, the Lord gives us a form in this birth. The instrumental cause for the shaping of our body is our father and mother. Our body is made of insentient prakṛti. It is the world-mother, Śrī Lakṣmī, who gives us this form within our mother's womb; Lakṣmī fashions the body as, so to speak, a limb of the Lord. In this way, the jīva, having received the body's organs and senses, experiences through them the enjoyment of pleasure and pain. The Supreme Puruṣa, the Lord, gives the jīva the experience of pleasure and pain. Thus, while sentient and insentient prakṛti are both involved in the making of the body, it is the Lord who, standing as the indweller himself, grants the jīva the experience of being the enjoyer.",
  "13.21": "puruṣaḥ prakṛti-sthaḥ hi bhuṅkte prakṛti-jān guṇān |\nkāraṇam guṇa-saṅgaḥ asya sat asat yoni janmasu -- The jīva, situated in prakṛti, experiences the guṇas born of prakṛti. The bond with the guṇas is the cause of its birth in a good or a bad womb.\n\nThe jīva, caught in the bonds of prakṛti, experiences the workings of prakṛti such as pleasure and pain. The bond with the three guṇas alone is the reason a jīva comes to be born in a good womb or a bad one.\n\nWithin the body constructed by prakṛti dwell both the Lord and the deities. But it is the jīva who dwells in the body and experiences the enjoyment of pleasure and pain. The body is constructed out of the quality of prakṛti. The jīva receives a body in this birth in accordance with its own nature and in accordance with the fruit of the karma of its previous birth.\n\nAs explained before, whatever the jīva remembers intensely at the moment it leaves its body determines its next birth accordingly. The bond with the three guṇas alone is the reason for a jīva's birth in a superior or an inferior womb.",
  "13.22": "upadraṣṭā anumantā ca bhartā bhoktā maheśvaraḥ |\nparamātmā iti ca api uktaḥ dehe asmin puruṣaḥ paraḥ -- Standing above all and seeing everything as he wills; the one who directs everything; the one who bears everything; the one who tastes the essence of everything -- the all-powerful Paramātmā. He alone is the Supreme Puruṣa who dwells together with the jīva in this body.\n\nWhen sentient prakṛti constructs a body out of insentient prakṛti, and the jīva, dwelling in that body, experiences pleasure and pain through the senses, the Lord -- the Supreme Puruṣa -- is the one who, as the mover behind everything, standing higher than everything, sees everything directly and near at hand. He knows our karma. He knows what ought to come of what kind of karma. Because he sits within and impels us, this body stands; if he departs, the body falls. He, dwelling within us untouched by any sorrow, is ever the enjoyer of bliss.",
  "13.23": "yaḥ evam vetti puruṣam prakṛtim ca guṇaiḥ saha |\nsarvathā vartamānaḥ api na saḥ bhūyaḥ abhijāyate -- One who thus knows Puruṣa and Prakṛti together with the guṇas, however he may live in the world, is not born again.\n\nOne who knows, together with their guṇas, beginningless and eternal prakṛti (both insentient and sentient), the Paramātmā who is the Puruṣa and the cause of everything, and the living beings who are subordinate to him, is a jñānī. However such a jñānī may appear outwardly, through the realisation of the truth he attains mokṣa. Here Kṛṣṇa emphasises how much more important knowledge is than outward observance. A life lived behind a mask is of no use whatsoever. Superior to that is the realisation of the truth. When the realisation of the truth comes, one comes to know on one's own how meaningless the worldly world is. There is no other sacred means superior to knowledge. Even if there is an accidental lapse in the performance of one's duties, it is no obstacle for one who walks the path of knowledge; knowledge itself brings him back onto the right path, and he attains mokṣa.\n\nKṛṣṇa has said that knowing prakṛti and puruṣa together with the guṇas is the path to mokṣa. What, then, is the way to know this? Kṛṣṇa explains this in the next two verses. There are many ways of reaching the Lord. Not every way suits everyone, because what one person has understood cannot simply be taken up by another in the same way. Come, let us listen to Kṛṣṇa's explanation on this matter.",
  "13.24": "dhyānena ātmani paśyanti kecit ātmānam ātmanā |\nanye sāṅkhyena yogena karma-yogena ca apare -- Some, through meditation, see the Paramātmā within themselves with an inward eye. Some do so through the awareness that comes from scriptural knowledge. Others do so through karma-yoga.\n\nTo undertake sādhanā, we first need a body fit for sādhanā. If one is born into a body that has not attained fullness, in an inferior womb, sādhanā is not possible. We must first become human beings (become puruṣas established in a body that has attained fullness). For this reason the dāsas have sung: \"A human birth is a great thing; do not waste it, you fools!\" Without the body needed for sādhanā, no sādhanā at all is possible.\n\nSome are born with knowledge from birth itself, through their own inherent worthiness and through the Lord's grace. For these, knowledge is clear. Such people, through the worthiness of their own soul, can see the Lord present in image-form within their own soul's essential nature. Such people, without undertaking any study at all, ever seeing the Lord in their inner being, reach the Lord. Brahmā and all the deities, and those born of a portion of the divine, are able to see the Lord in this way with closed eyes, and having seen him, are able to reach him.\n\nSome first come to know the Lord through the science of the pure nature of the self. Knowing the jīva and knowing the Paramātmā -- this itself is Sāṅkhya-yoga. Having gained knowledge in this way, and having gained the flash of realisation of that knowledge, these people see the Lord through meditation and reach the Lord.\n\nYet others achieve purity of body through their continual performance of their duties, and by this means become fit recipients of the Lord's favour, gain knowledge, see the Lord in meditation, and reach the Lord.",
  "13.25": "anye tu evam ajānantaḥ śrutvā anyebhyaḥ upāsate |\nte api ca atitaranti eva mṛtyum śruti-parāyaṇāḥ -- Still others, who do not know in this way, worship having heard it from those who know. Devoted to what they have heard, they too cross beyond death.\n\nOrdinary people, who are unable to undertake sādhanā in any of the above ways, can climb the first step of sādhanā by hearing and learning from another. Even though this is the very lowest step on the path of sādhanā, such people, through continual hearing (śravaṇa), reflection (manana), and sustained contemplation (nididhyāsana), without ego, acquire knowledge, and through that knowledge come to see the Lord in meditation. This is the path to mokṣa that Kṛṣṇa has shown for the ordinary human being. Thus, through hearing and worship, they too can conquer death and reach the Lord.",
  "13.26": "yāvat sañjāyate kiñcit sattvam sthāvara jaṅgamam |\nkṣetra kṣetrajña saṁyogāt tat viddhi bharata-ṛṣabha -- O bull among the Bharatas, whatever being comes into existence, whether unmoving or moving, know that it comes about only through the union of the kṣetra [Śrī-tattva] and the kṣetrajña, the Lord.\n\nKnow that everything that comes into being in this world -- every moving and unmoving living being, the entire insentient world, and Prakṛti-devī who presides over it -- is together called kṣetra. The one who knows all of this is the Lord, the kṣetrajña. Therefore kṣetra means the entire living and insentient world constructed by the Lord. All of creation came about through the union of Lakṣmī and Nārāyaṇa. They are the first couple of this world. Therefore the father and mother of all are Nārāyaṇa and Śrī Lakṣmī. \"When you know this, you become a true bharata-ṛṣabha,\" says Kṛṣṇa. One who is devoted to the Lord, one who has complete devotion to the Lord, is a bharata-ṛṣabha.",
  "13.27": "samam sarveṣu bhūteṣu tiṣṭhantam parameśvaram |\nvinaśyatsu avinaśyantam yaḥ paśyati saḥ paśyati -- One who sees the all-powerful Lord standing identically in all beings, undying even as they perish, he alone truly sees.\n\nThe all-powerful Lord, who stands identically within every living being, is Parama-Īśa-Vara. Whose command we cannot help but obey; whose obedience is our duty; the unknown power that dwells within us, unbeknownst to us, ensuring that obedience -- that is \"Īśa-vara,\" meaning the deities who preside over each principle. The Lord who governs even such presiding deities is Parameśvara. Such a Lord is not seated somewhere directing us from afar. He dwells within us and controls us. Dwelling within us in complete freedom, he bears us and carries us forward. [In earlier times, people conveyed this message to the public through the tattirāya at the temple.]... The Lord is the governor of both the liberated and the unliberated. It is the Lord who, present within, at all times, for everyone, impels each according to their own worthiness and in keeping with the nature of each thing. There is no high or low in the Lord's power. The Lord present in an elephant and the Lord present in an ant are not different. But because there is a difference in his mode of presence, a difference arises in the basis of that presence. The Lord present in image-form within our body is imperishable. He undergoes no destruction of body, no destruction of his own essential nature. He experiences no sorrow. He is not incomplete. One who knows this truth possesses true knowledge. Kṛṣṇa goes on to unfold this matter further and explain it in the next verse.",
  "13.28": "samam paśyan hi sarvatra sama-avasthitam īśvaram |\nna hinasti ātmanā ātmanam tataḥ yāti parām gatim -- Seeing thus, that the all-powerful Lord is present identically everywhere, without disparity, one does not injure oneself by one's own self. Thereafter one attains the supreme state.\n\nWe are situated within our own body. But the Lord is \"saṁsthita\" -- established beyond it. We, who dwell within this body, do not even have knowledge of this body. We do not even know why we exist. We remain within, experiencing joy and sorrow. But the Lord is a form of bliss untouched by sorrow or ignorance. He stands identically present, at a height above all the deities presiding over each principle within us. The Lord, untouched by sorrow and ignorance, looks upon no one with a sense of high or low. He looks upon everyone equally, according to their own karma and their own worthiness of soul. The Lord, standing as the ruler of the universe within everything sentient and insentient, guides us in keeping with our own nature as a jīva and in accordance with our karma. \"When you know this truth, you can live without doing violence to yourself,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Living without awareness of yourself, without awareness of the Lord, is itself a form of self-violence. Once one knows the true state of affairs -- what \"I\" means, and what the Lord dwelling within me means -- there is no more self-violence. To live without knowing this is a life of ignorance and sorrow. If we understand every single thing that happens in life as \"the fruit of karma I performed before, which the Lord has caused to happen,\" we can live joyfully, without sorrow. It is our own mind that gives us sorrow. It is the same mind that gives us joy. Our sorrow comes from our own understanding and from nothing else. For example, a wealthy person loses all his wealth for some reason. If, at that time, he understands: \"This event happened in keeping with the karma of my past birth. Everything is the Lord's play; he is the one who gives, he is the one who takes away, he is the one who sustains; why then should I worry over this?\" -- he can remain joyful. If, instead of this, one behaves as though the whole universe has fallen upon one's head, that is doing violence to oneself. We must practise seeing everything as a spectator. Such a peaceful life, lived with awareness of the Lord, can carry us toward the supreme state, mokṣa.\n\nKṛṣṇa places before us, in the next verse, another contemplation on the Lord. If we look carefully at this verse, the truth becomes clear to us. A confusion present in all of us is this: \"Do I perform the action, or does the Lord cause me to perform it? If the Lord causes it, why does he make me do bad deeds?\" Kṛṣṇa gives a condensed answer to this question in this verse. We need to look at this verse bearing in mind what Kṛṣṇa has said before.",
  "13.29": "prakṛtyā eva ca karmāṇi kriyamāṇāni sarvaśaḥ |\nyaḥ paśyati tathā ātmānam akartāram saḥ paśyati -- All actions, in every way, are being carried out by prakṛti alone [the Lord himself engages and drives forward every action in keeping with the jīva's karma]. One who knows this, and who knows that he himself is never independent in doing anything at all [the Lord has no other causal agent], he alone truly knows.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"One who knows that 'I' am not the doer is the one who truly knows.\" Therefore we must first give up the ego that says \"I am doing this.\" \"I am doing\" is merely a transactional truth, not the ultimate truth. For example: \"I am speaking.\" For me to speak, I must first not be mute -- I must have a human body with the proper organs for it. To speak, my mind must think through matters correctly in time and transmit the message. That is, I need the proper power of the brain for this. I need the courage to speak. Now let us ask ourselves: who gave me the human body capable of speech? Who designed and graciously gave me the brain capable of thought? Who transmitted the message from this brain at the right time? Who granted me the wondrous art of speaking? From all these questions we come to know this truth: \"The Lord within me made the sound -- I merely spoke.\" Kṛṣṇa explains this very point here, saying: \"prakṛtyaiva cha karmāṇi kriyamāṇāni sarvaśaḥ.\" In keeping with each one's own jīva-nature (jīva-svabhāva), through the influence of prakṛti (the three guṇas), Prakṛti-Puruṣa (Lakṣmī-Nārāyaṇa) cause action to be performed through us. Therefore: the jīva's body is created in keeping with the jīva-nature that has come to it, beginningless and eternal; in keeping with that, comes the influence of the three guṇas present in the surrounding environment; in keeping with the influence of the three guṇas, comes every kind of action the jīva performs. The one who causes all of this -- Nārāyaṇa together with Lakṣmī, the lord of all the deities.",
  "13.30": "yadā bhūta pṛthak bhāvam eka-stham anupaśyati |\ntataḥ eva ca vistāram brahma sampadyate tadā -- When one sees that the diverse multitude of living beings is established in the one Lord, and that this diverse expansion comes from him alone, one then attains the Lord himself.\n\nThe jīva is beginningless and eternal. Creation and dissolution are merely the visible form of things. Every single living being is a separate, distinct entity (pṛthak-bhāva); no two living beings are a hundred percent alike. This vast community of many living beings exists within the one Lord. The living beings that emerge from the Lord's womb and take on form at the time of creation return, in subtle form, to the Lord's womb at the time of dissolution. We must grasp this in keeping with the actual state of affairs. Whoever is able to grasp this truth is able to attain mokṣa.",
  "13.31": "anāditvāt nirguṇatvāt paramaḥ ātmā ayam avyayaḥ |\nśarīra-sthaḥ api kaunteya na karoti na lipyate -- O Kaunteya, the Paramātmā is never born; the guṇas do not touch him; for this reason he undergoes no destruction. Though dwelling within the body, he does nothing; he is tainted by nothing. [This imperishable Paramātmā is neither a jīva bound to saṁsāra, nor -- needless to say -- is he insentient matter. He does everything; he is tainted by nothing.]\n\nThe one substance that is free of all transformation is the Lord. He was never born from any particular cause whatsoever. The Lord is free of the three guṇas. For this reason he never undergoes change or transformation. Though present as image-form within every jīva, he has no bondage to the body. He causes us to do everything, yet he is tainted by nothing. He does everything without attachment, yet no residue of it clings to him. To help us understand how this can be, Kṛṣṇa gives an example and explains it in the next verse.",
  "13.32": "yathā sarva-gatam saukṣmyāt ākāśam na upalipyate |\nsarvatra avasthitaḥ dehe tathā ātmā na upalipyate -- Just as space, all-pervading yet so subtle that it presents no obstruction to anything, is tainted by nothing, so too the Self, though present everywhere within the body, is tainted by nothing.\n\nSpace exists everywhere. There is no place where it is absent. Just as this all-present space is tainted by nothing at all, so too the Lord, though present within every body, remains untainted.",
  "13.33": "yathā prakāśayati ekaḥ kṛtsnam lokam imam raviḥ |\nkṣetram kṣetrī tathā kṛtsnam prakāśayati bhārata -- O Bhārata, just as a single sun illumines this entire earth, so too the Lord, who is the kṣetrajña, alone illumines the entire world that is the kṣetra.\n\nJust as a single sun illumines the whole universe, so too the Lord, the kṣetrajña, illumines the entire kṣetra. We must first come to know the Lord's light (knowledge); only then does the light within us become intelligible to us.",
  "13.34": "kṣetra kṣetrajñayoḥ evam antaram jñāna-cakṣuṣā |\nbhūta prakṛti mokṣam ca ye viduḥ yānti te param -- Those who, with the eye of knowledge, know this distinction between kṣetra and kṣetrajña, and know the path of knowledge that leads to release from the five elements and from prakṛti [release from sentient prakṛti, for the jīvas], attain the Supreme.\n\nThis verse is the summation of this chapter. What is kṣetra, what is kṣetrajña, what is the difference between kṣetra and kṣetrajña; what qualities conducive to mokṣa a living being must have in order to be released from insentient matter -- those who come to know all of this and bring it into their own practice and life reach the Lord, who is supreme above all.\n\nThus ends the thirteenth chapter.\n\n[In Bannanje's commentary, the thirteenth chapter concludes with 34 verses. Verse 13.35 does not appear separately in his text; it is already contained within the preceding verse.]",
  "13.35": "[In Bannanje's commentary, the thirteenth chapter concludes with 34 verses. Verse 13.35 does not appear separately in his text; it is already contained within the preceding verse.]",
  "14.1": "That is, \"Salutations to Narayana, husband of Lakshmi, by the power of whose glance Lakshmi carries out the entire creation, sustenance, and dissolution.\" Here, having given a full description of Lakshmi in one hymn, salutations are offered to Narayana, the husband of such Lakshmi. Therefore the praise of Lakshmi-Narayana together is Lakshmi-stuti, not merely praise of Lakshmi alone. This has its root in the twelfth, thirteenth, and fourteenth chapters of the Gita. In the twelfth chapter there is the warning that worshipping Lakshmi alone will certainly not win her favor; in the thirteenth chapter there is the teaching that this world was formed through the union of Lakshmi and Narayana — therefore all of us are their children. How Lakshmi is joined with the Lord in the process of creation is what the fourteenth chapter explains. Since Lakshmi, in her three forms of Shri, Bhu, and Durga, is the governing power of sattva, rajas, and tamas, her children are of three kinds — sattvika, rajasa, and tamasa. This matter is stated for the first time in this chapter; in no earlier chapter has the detail of the three-fold nature been given so clearly. By analysing these three gunas, correcting the rajas and tamas within us, and seeing how effort can be made along the sattvic path — this is what we find in this chapter.\n\nThe Lord said:\n\nparam bhūyaḥ pravakṣyāmi jñānānāṃ jñānam uttamam |\nyaj jñātvā munayaḥ sarve parāṃ siddhim ito gatāḥ ||1||\n\nparam bhūyaḥ pravakṣyāmi jñānānām jñānam uttamam |\nyat jñātvā munayaḥ sarve parām siddhim itaḥ gatāḥ — I will tell you further of the highest knowledge, the supreme among all things to be known; knowing which, all the sages attained the highest state from this very world.\n\nKrishna says: \"I will tell you more.\" This is a very important statement. Ordinarily, even if a disciple insists, a teacher does not speak. The teacher must see the disciple's thirst for knowledge and feel moved to speak, and must speak joyfully. Only then does that knowledge bear fruit for the disciple. Knowledge spoken by a guru joyfully, on seeing such thirst, is true knowledge. Here Krishna himself, unprompted, is telling Arjuna knowledge of an extraordinary kind. Krishna says: \"I will explain in still greater detail the kṣetra (Prakriti-Lakshmi) and kṣetrajña (Narayana) spoken of earlier, because it is the most important of all things to be known.\" Behind the creation of this world stand Lakshmi and Narayana as mother and father. Therefore, rather than remembering only the father, or only the mother, it is better to know and remember both parents together. Krishna says: \"I will tell you this matter in still greater detail, because it is something a person striving for liberation must know.\"\n\nLiberation is impossible without contemplating Lakshmi-Narayana as the source of creation. \"Even the sages who were born on this field of striving and attained realization of Brahman attained the supreme accomplishment of life — liberation — only by knowing this,\" says Krishna. This very point is explained with great clarity in the next verse.",
  "14.2": "idam jñānam apāśritya mama sādharmyam āgatāḥ |\nsarge api na upajāyante pralaye na vyathanti ca — Having gained this knowledge and cast off all sorrow, those who resemble Me are not born at the time of creation, nor do they suffer at the time of dissolution.\n\nThe word \"apāśritya\" occurring in this verse carries a special meaning. \"anyat sarvam apahāya āśrayaṇam — apāśrayaṇaḥ\": taking refuge in the Lord alone, thinking \"there is no other refuge for me but You\" — this taking of refuge is what is called \"apāśritya\" here. As the Dvādaśa-stotra says:\n\nkuru bhukṣva ca karma nijaṃ niyataṃ hari-pāda-vinamra-dhiyā satataṃ |\nharir eva paro harir eva guruḥ harir eva jagat-pitṛ-mātṛ-gatiḥ ||1||\n\nThis verse explains the root meaning of the word \"apāśritya.\" Here Krishna says: \"Those who worship Me with the knowledge that Lakshmi-Narayana are the mother and father of the world attain a divine nature like Mine.\" Such people, having shed every fault, reach the state beyond the three gunas, join the Lord, and abide in Him. This world is created from Lakshmi-Narayana, and it is to them that it returns as well. One who knows this can cross beyond birth and death. He is never again caught in the bonds of this worldly existence. The word \"upajāyate\" is used here. In Sanskrit, \"upa\" means \"exalted, elevated.\" Therefore \"upajāyate\" means an exalted birth. To take up this body and be born for the sake of realizing the Lord is a lofty birth. Birth that rises upward through spiritual striving is \"upajanana.\" One who, in this very lifetime, has gained this knowledge of the origin of creation is never born again. He attains the supreme state of dwelling eternally in the Lord.",
  "14.3": "mama yonir mahad brahma tasmin garbhaṃ dadhāmy aham |\nsambhavaḥ sarvabhūtānāṃ tato bhavati bhārata ||3||\n\nmama yoniḥ mahat brahma tasmin garbham dadhāmi aham |\nsambhavaḥ sarvabhūtānām tataḥ bhavati bhārata — Conscious Prakriti is My own consort. In her I place the seed of creation. O Bharata, the birth of all beings takes place through her.\n\nHere Krishna states a very remarkable thing. To understand this verse we must first become \"Bhā-rata.\" \"Rata\" means devoted absorption. We must be absorbed in the Lord; absorbed in the path of knowledge; be a vessel filled and overflowing with devotion; and be humble. Only then does this secret of creation become clear.\n\nFor anything at all to be created, more than one cause is required. In the case of an insentient object, two causes are needed — for instance, for a pot to be made, the raw material is needed, and the potter who gives it form is needed. The creation of plant life happens through the Lord joining life to matter. But for the \"sentient creation\" that is born through a womb, both a male and a female are needed to bring it about along with insentient matter. From the union of the two, a third comes into being.\n\nIn this verse, in the phrase \"mama yoniḥ mahat brahma,\" the words \"yoni\" and \"brahma\" are words that carry many meanings. We must understand which meaning fits which context in order to understand the verse. \"Yoni\" can mean cause, source of origin, wife, and so on. Likewise \"Brahma\" can mean the individual soul, Chaturmukha (four-faced Brahma), Lakshmi, the Lord, and so on. In this verse, \"yoni\" means wife; \"mahat brahma\" means Shri Lakshmi, who is mother even to Chaturmukha. Krishna says: \"I place the seed of creation in My wife, conscious Prakriti (Lakshmi). That seed grows, and from it all classes of living beings are created.\" This is new to us, because the Rigveda describes the creation of Chaturmukha in this way:\n\ntam id garbhaṃ prathamaṃ dadhra āpo yatra devāḥ samagacchanta viśve |\najasya nābhāv adhy ekam arpitaṃ yasmin viśvāni bhuvanāni tasthuḥ || 10.82.06 ||\n\nHere it is said that \"Brahma came forth from the Lord's lotus navel.\" The creation of living beings took place through Chaturmukha. So we also know that Chaturmukha is called the father of the world. Elsewhere in the Veda (the Ambhriṇī-sūkta) it is said:\n\nahaṃ suve pitaram asya mūrdhan mama yonir apsv antaḥ samudre |\ntato vi tiṣṭhe bhuvanānu viśvo tām dyāṃ varṣmaṇopa spṛśāmi || 7 ||\n\nThis is Lakshmi's own utterance: \"I gave birth to Chaturmukha, the father of this world, from my own crown; the Lord, who is the cause even of me, resides in the ocean.\" Elsewhere it is said that the whole universe was created by Shiva. If one does not know the method of scripture, all of this appears to be mere confusion. Those who strive for liberation must analyse this subtly — then the truth becomes manifest.\n\nThe soul, Prakriti, and the Lord are without beginning and without end. Before creation, the entire world existed in subtle form within the Lord's own being. Looked at yet more subtly — the entire universe existed within the being of Goddess Lakshmi, and Lakshmi existed within the being of the Lord. The Lord placed the seed of all classes of living beings within Lakshmi's being. The entire universe, in the form of a lotus, sprang forth from the Lord's navel through Lakshmi's crown. Within it, along with Chaturmukha, all the gods and all classes of living beings were born in unmanifest form. For this reason, Lakshmi-Narayana are the mother and father of the entire world. One important point we must understand here is that the act of creation proceeded step by step. What was produced through the union of Lakshmi-Narayana was only the unmanifest creation. Thereafter, Chaturmukha Brahma gave this subtle, unmanifest world (the realm of living beings) its first gross form and became known as the grandfather of the world. After that, this world received its complete gross form through Shiva. If we understand this process, there is no confusion here at all. It is true that Chaturmukha was born from Lakshmi's crown; it is also true that he was born from the Lord's navel; it is true that he is the father of the world; it is also true that this gross world was fashioned by Shiva; and it is equally true that Lakshmi-Narayana are the mother and father of all classes of living beings. A detailed explanation of this is given clearly in the Mahabharata Tatparya Nirnaya.\n\nFor the creation of any sentient being possessing \"bhūti\" (growth, elevation), the union of a male and a female is necessary. This is the tradition that has continued since the very beginning of creation. Behind every creation in this world, Lakshmi-Narayana stand as mother and father. Krishna explains this matter still more clearly in the next verse.",
  "14.4": "sarvayoniṣu kaunteya mūrtayaḥ sambhavanti yāḥ |\ntāsāṃ brahma mahad yonir aham bījapradaḥ pitā — O Kaunteya, whatever forms take shape in whatever wombs — for all of them, conscious Prakriti is the mother; I alone am the father who sows the seed.\n\nNot merely the gods and human beings — behind the birth of every class of living being stand Lakshmi-Narayana as mother and father. The mother of all is conscious Prakriti, Lakshmi. The father who sows the seed is Narayana. As the Mundaka Upanishad says:\n\npumān retaḥ siñcati yoṣitāyāṃ bahvīḥ prajāḥ puruṣāt samprasūtāḥ || 2.2.5 ||\n\nHere \"pumān\" means the Lord who is especially present within the male, and \"yoṣitā\" means Lakshmi who is especially present within the female. Just as a seed sown in a field takes on its true form there, so too the Lord bestows the seed within Shri Lakshmi, who presides over insentient Prakriti. Mother Lakshmi gives that seed its shape. How grand this coordinated working is! For this very reason, the ancient authors of the scriptures honoured a pregnant woman's body as a temple, the place where the womb resides as the sanctum, and the Lord who is present within, nurturing the life growing there, as the deity Himself. This is the grandeur with which our ancients contemplated the Lord in every act. A woman becomes a temple when she is pregnant. And so a woman's highest longing is to become a mother; the final goal of her desire is motherhood. Lakshmi is present in her in full measure when she is pregnant. For this reason, married life is the worship of Lakshmi-Narayana. In married life, the union of husband and wife is the union of Lakshmi-Narayana. Here our great fortune lies in this: that the Lord uses us as instruments through which He creates. Krishna gives an elaborate account of the raw material needed for creation to take place in this way in the verses that follow.",
  "14.5": "sattvaṃ rajas tama iti guṇāḥ prakṛtisambhavāḥ |\nnibadhnanti mahābāho dehe dehinam avyayam — O Mahabaho, sattva, rajas, and tamas are the qualities that arise from insentient Prakriti. These bind the imperishable soul within the body.\n\nPrakriti, the mother of this world and of every class of living being, presides over the three gunas. Therefore every living being, at the very moment of its birth, is born together with the three qualities of sattva, rajas, and tamas, and grows under their influence. No being is ever created purely, with a single quality alone and without the three. Even in insentient matter we see these three qualities. Fire, which always gives light in an upward direction, is sattva; water, the seat of unclarity, is rajas; and earth, which always draws downward, is tamas. Every object in the world is a combination of these three. Take rice, for example: we sow it in the earth (earth—tamas); we water it (water—rajas); it absorbs the sun's light (sun—sattva), sprouts, grows, and yields a harvest. We harvest that crop and turn it into rice. We add water (rajas) to the rice (tamas) and cook it with heat (sattva) into cooked food. Whether it is raw rice or cooked food, everything is a combination of these three — earth, water, fire. Thus this entire creation is built out of the three gunas of sattva, rajas, and tamas. These three qualities bind every living being that is born. The proportion of these three qualities differs from person to person. Even in the most sattvic soul, one fit for liberation, these three qualities are present. No one has a hundred percent pure sattva quality. Looking at insentient matter — fire is sattva, but along with it there is smoke, which is rajas, and firewood, which is tamas. Sattva manifests itself only through rajas and tamas. Thus, bound by these three gunas, the soul — which by its own nature has neither birth nor death — takes on a gross body and is born, and loses that body and dies. To attain the liberation that is free of birth and death, we must conquer these three inner enemies that dwell with us.\n\nHere Vyasa has used the epithet \"Mahābāhu.\" Behind this epithet lies the resonance: \"Become the mighty-armed one who conquers these three gunas that bind you and make you dance to their tune; reject what is base, and become a hero of the spirit.\"\n\nThis entire universe, this life of ours, everything, is a transformation of the three gunas. Our mind, our thought, our decisions, our reading, our beliefs — all of it proceeds from the influence of these qualities. It is the three gunas that lead us onto the right path, and it is these same three gunas that lead us astray. If we are to gain the awareness needed to judge which of the three paths, when followed, leads to good, we must first be aware of which path we are actually walking on. For this reason, awareness of the three gunas is very important for us.\n\nThere are four things that govern a person. One is \"svabhāva\" — the innate nature that has been joined to the soul, as part of its own form, from beginningless time. Along with this are the three \"prabhāvas\" — sattva, rajas, tamas. Svabhāva never changes. We must recognize our own svabhāva. But we never come to know what our svabhāva actually is! This is because \"prabhāva\" sits like a lid over this svabhāva. We constantly mistake the influence of this lid for our own true nature. But that is not our real nature — it is prabhāva. Throughout an entire lifetime we may never come to know what our svabhāva is. Not knowing our svabhāva, we remain caught and struggling within this prabhāva. If we do not know how the influence of these three gunas works upon us, we cannot correct ourselves. Today we have pushed our own svabhāva and our sattva quality into a corner, and are living under the control of rajas and tamas.\n\nWe must first correct ourselves — not society. We must know how much sattva we have, how much rajas, how much tamas, and try to use the sattvic element more in our conduct and increase its share. We must reflect honestly on where, in past events, we have gone wrong under the influence of rajas and tamas, and be watchful not to let this happen again in the future. If we live bearing this understanding in mind, we can govern ourselves and live in great joy.\n\nIn the verses that follow, Krishna explains what a life of sattva looks like, what a life of rajas looks like, and what a life of tamas looks like. If living beings caught in the bondage of the three gunas come to know how they behave under the influence of each bond, they can make the effort to walk the path of truth. Krishna gives a very beautiful analysis of this in the verses that follow.",
  "14.6": "tatra sattvaṃ nirmalatvāt prakāśakam anāmayam |\nsukhasaṅgena badhnāti jñānasaṅgena ca anagha — Among these, sattva is the purest. Therefore it is the one that grants the light of knowledge and removes ailments. O Anagha, it binds through attachment to happiness and attachment to knowledge.\n\nOf the three, sattva is thoroughly pure. Therefore it grants the light of knowledge and removes afflictions. Sit with your eyes closed and look within yourself. Observe the mental developments, thoughts, and feelings taking place within you. If you are under the influence of sattva, you will see within yourself a clean, pure, unburdened, glowing light. Our inner world is fully wired for electricity, with a lamp installed and many switches to control that lamp. There is a constant supply of current here. But we do not know where this switch is. Therefore, even though everything is present, darkness continually pervades us. We simply never use it. When sattva awakens within us, we find this switch. From it, a light of knowledge free of any flaw glows within us.\n\nWhen the quality of sattva awakens, an inner joy overflows within us. Most people have had this experience. For example, we sometimes say: \"For some reason I feel very happy today.\" There is no special reason for it. It is simply that we are experiencing the inner joy welling up. This is the awakened state of sattva. All happiness that comes from knowledge is sattvic.\n\nHere Krishna gives a warning. For sattva to awaken within us, we must become \"anagha\" — sinless. If the touch of sin is upon us, sattva will not awaken. Therefore we must stay away from sin. The more the touch of sin, the further sattva recedes.\n\nEven though sattva is an instrument in the pursuit of liberation, it is nonetheless one of the three gunas. Therefore sattva too can become a bond. It can bind us through attachment to happiness and attachment to knowledge. For instance: the pursuit of knowledge is a sattvic quality. But if we fall into attachment to the pursuit of knowledge on matters that are not about the Lord, then it becomes a bond. If we are absorbed in some research or inquiry, that will not carry us toward liberation. We may become caught in the sattvic bondage of attachment to the pleasure that comes from the results of that research. In the pursuit of liberation, we must cross beyond sattva as well, because liberation is beyond the three gunas. Krishna here calls the binding of sattva \"badhnāti\" — because this bond is one that can be shed easily. It never drags us downward. [But Krishna refers to the binding of rajas and tamas as \"nibadhnāti\" in the verses that follow.] Whatever sattva keeps us circling in bondage without leading us toward the Lord — from that sattva we must free ourselves.",
  "14.7": "rajo rāgātmakaṃ viddhi tṛṣṇāsaṅgasamudbhavam |\ntan nibadhnāti kaunteya karmasaṅgena dehinam — Know rajas to be of the nature of attachment, arising from craving and clinging. O Kaunteya, it binds the soul through attachment to action.\n\nRajas means \"rāga\" — strong liking for an object, an unbroken chain of desires. Thus, the desire of all five senses is what arises from the influence of rajas. This is the cause of craving and clinging. Here, craving means the thirst of \"want, want.\" Clinging means continual association with an object. Rajas gives rise to desire; from that desire comes association, and from association comes attachment. In this way, rajas step by step subjects us to a bondage that becomes impossible to break free of (\"nibadhnāti\"). Under its influence, we begin to engage in action devoid of any desire for knowledge. For example: struggling for money, devoting one's entire life to money, fighting as though money were the whole world, and so on. This is the effect of the deep bondage of rajas.",
  "14.8": "tamas tu ajñānajaṃ viddhi mohanaṃ sarvadehinām |\npramādālasyanidrābhis tan nibadhnāti bhārata — Know tamas to be born of ignorance, the deluder of all embodied beings. O Bharata, it binds through negligence, laziness, and sleep.\n\nTo be utterly without knowledge is tamas. Ignorance is born from tamas. It is so powerful that it can prevent even the knowledge that \"I have no knowledge, I am ignorant\" from arising. This is extremely frightening. That we do not even know that we know nothing is itself due to tamas. This is the quality that traps everyone in delusion. Tamas binds us with three knots we cannot break free of. These are sleep, laziness, and negligence. To live carelessly even while awake is negligence (pramāda). To make a mistake is not itself wrong, but to make a mistake and fail to correct it is negligence. The root cause of all this is the tamas that dwells within us.",
  "14.9": "sattvaṃ sukhe sañjayati rajaḥ karmaṇi bhārata |\njñānam āvṛtya tu tamaḥ pramāde sañjayaty uta — Sattva binds one to happiness. O Bharata, rajas binds one to action. Tamas veils knowledge and binds one to negligence.\n\nIn this verse Krishna sums up the three qualities together in brief. The basic nature of sattva is that it causes a natural joy to well up within us. Rajas, saying \"one must do something, or else there is no happiness in life,\" keeps a person constantly fighting for money and wearing away his life in that pursuit. Tamas covers over the knowledge that lies within us. For example: the goodness of wishing to help someone in difficulty, taking joy in giving — this is sattva. Living always in pursuit of one's own happiness and, in that pursuit, doing what ought not to be done — this is rajas. Laziness, sleep, ignorance, and the negligence that follows from them — all of these are the effect of tamas. This is the supreme psychology that Krishna has given us through the Gita.\n\nWe have seen how Krishna has described the way these three gunas take control of us and make us dance to their tune. Here a doubt may arise for us. When these three qualities work together at the same time, how is it possible for purely sattvic thought, purely rajasic thought, or purely tamasic thought to occur at all? When the three qualities are present intermingled within one and the same body, how is it possible for any one quality to act at all? When these three qualities are blended together within us, how can we identify a distinct sattvic nature, rajasic nature, or tamasic nature? Krishna explains this in the next verse.",
  "14.10": "rajas tamaś ca abhibhūya sattvam bhavati bhārata |\nrajaḥ sattvam tamaś caiva tamaḥ sattvam rajas tathā — O Bharata, sattva grows strong by overpowering rajas and tamas; rajas by overpowering sattva and tamas; and likewise tamas by overpowering sattva and rajas.\n\nThe three gunas possess a special power: any one of the three can take over the control of our mind and suppress the other two. One of the three qualities takes possession of our mind, and then our mind begins to act in accordance with that quality. Lakshmi presides over the three gunas. She grants to whichever quality it may be the power to act by overriding the other two. In this way, one quality suppresses the other two and begins to act.\n\nWe must recognize which quality is awake within us. Then we can avoid the harm that comes from the influence of that quality. Krishna explains the method for recognizing this in the next three verses.",
  "14.11": "sarvadvāreṣu dehe asmin prakāśaḥ upajāyate |\njñānam yadā tadā vidyāt vivṛddham sattvam iti uta — When light in the form of knowledge arises through all the senses in this body, one should know that sattva has grown strong.\n\nWhen the quality of sattva awakens, we experience an inner fullness of light. This flow of light emerges through all the senses. Joy born of knowledge fills every sense. Above all, in our mind, fresh new insights (novel ideas) begin to dawn. From this comes boundless happiness. This is the awakened state of sattva.",
  "14.12": "lobhaḥ pravṛttir ārambhaḥ karmaṇām aśamaḥ spṛhā |\nrajasy etāni jāyante vivṛddhe bharatarṣabha — O Bharatarshabha, when rajas grows strong, these traits appear: greed, feverish enthusiasm, engaging in undertakings, restlessness, and unquenched craving.\n\nWhen rajas awakens, the first thing to appear in us is greed — thoughts of how to make money, a stinginess that will not give even a small coin to another, and so on. For example, some people spend lakhs on their own household celebrations, but if someone starving for food comes and asks, they will not give that person even a single paisa in charity. This is greed. Bhavabhūti speaks of such misers in the Nīti-śataka:\n\ndānaṃ bhogo nāśaḥ tisro bhavanti gatayo vittasya |\nyo na dadāti na bhuṅkte tasya tṛtīyā gatir bhavati || 158 ||\n\nWealth has three possible destinations: giving, enjoyment, and ruin. Misers neither give their money to others nor enjoy it themselves; in the end it is lost to the jackals and the dogs. This is the effect that follows from the influence of rajas. Under the influence of rajas, a person constantly wants to be engaged in some undertaking or other. All kinds of dealings for the sake of making money; however deeply one is involved, there is never satisfaction there; even if God has given enough for one's own food and clothing through one's own dealings, there is no satisfaction; even though one's own salary is enough to live happily, one pines because it is less than the neighbor's salary, and so on. Such a person's daily mantra is \"want, want.\" When rajas rises up, trampling sattva underfoot, all of these characteristics appear.\n\nHere Vyasa has used the epithet \"Bharatarṣabha.\" To escape rajas, we must become \"Bharatarṣabhas.\" There is a saying in the Aitareya Brahmana: \"vāyur vāva bharataḥ\" — the name \"Bharata\" belongs to Vayudeva, who is always absorbed in the Lord. Taking refuge in Pranadeva can carry us across rajas.",
  "14.13": "aprakāśaḥ apravṛttiḥ ca pramādaḥ mohaḥ eva ca |\ntamasy etāni jāyante vivṛddhe kurunandana — O Kurunandana, when tamas grows strong, these traits appear: darkness, lack of enthusiasm for action, negligence, and delusion.\n\nWhen tamas awakens, everything is dark, both within and without. Always sleeping, making mistakes, living in ignorance — these come from the influence of tamas. Tamas will not let one rise before sunrise in the morning; this quality pushes us to always be sleeping. This quality makes us always long for darkness. To escape its influence, we must become \"Kurunandanas.\" We must engage ourselves in some task and make it a settled habit. For example: cultivating the habit of rising before sunrise in the morning. Once such a habit is formed, the mind will not consent to giving it up.\n\nSome among us say: \"I have no time for spiritual reflection.\" But most of those who say this spend their holidays, their leisure time, from morning to evening, lazily sleeping and wasting time! In this way, tamas invades and controls us without our even being aware of it. By engaging ourselves in some duty instead of being lazy, we can escape the influence of tamas.",
  "14.14": "yadā sattve pravṛddhe tu pralayaṃ yāti dehabhṛt |\ntadā uttamavidāṃ lokān amalān pratipadyate — When sattva has grown strong and the embodied one meets death, he attains the stainless worlds of those who know the highest truth.\n\nAs we know, the last moment when the soul leaves the body determines that soul's future course. If, at the time of death, one is able to give up the breath with sattvic thoughts, under the influence of sattva, then in the next birth one is born as a flawless human being in a community of the wise. There, dwelling among the wise, one is able to grow in knowledge and rise again to greater heights on the path of knowledge. Only if we live our whole life giving importance to sattva is it possible for us to die established in sattva. If instead we live thinking \"when the time comes to die, we will think of sattvic thoughts then,\" it is not possible to die under the influence of sattva.",
  "14.15": "rajasi pralayaṃ gatvā karmasaṅgiṣu jāyate |\ntathā pralīnaḥ tamasi mūḍhayoniṣu jāyate — Dying in rajas, one is born among those attached to action. Dying dissolved in tamas, one is born in degraded wombs.\n\nIf one lives performing action that does not support knowledge, living always under the influence of rajas thinking \"money, money,\" and dies under that same influence, then in the next birth one must be born clinging to action. And if one lives and dies under the influence of tamas, in the next birth one must be born in a degraded womb. Here, \"degraded womb\" can even mean being born as a human being. Living as a human being with no refinement, no cleanliness, no knowledge — merely possessing a human shape with not a trace of true humanity — this too is a degraded birth. Even birth as an animal is far better than this. This is the result that follows from dying in tamas. [We can understand how dying under the influence of rajas or tamas at the final moment affects the next birth from the story of Indradyumna, who was born as the elephant Gajendra, and Emperor Bharata, who was born as a deer. To learn about recent research into rebirth, one may look into the relevant researcher's books and the details of the research on this subject.]",
  "14.16": "karmaṇaḥ sukṛtasyāhuḥ sāttvikaṃ nirmalaṃ phalam |\nrajasas tu phalaṃ duḥkham ajñānaṃ tamasaḥ phalam — The wise say that the fruit of good action is sattvic and free of sorrow. The fruit of rajasic action is sorrow. The fruit of tamasic action is ignorance.\n\nIf, throughout life, one performs deeds that help others, that is good action (sukṛta). If, when we have wealth, we live stingily thinking \"this is mine, this is mine,\" a life filled with sorrow becomes our own. When we use what we have earned to help others in their difficulty, it carries us toward the sattvic fruit. Madhvacharya emphasizes this point in his Gītā-tātparya, saying: \"nānā janasya śuśrūṣā kartavyā karavan mite\" — serving those who are in difficulty is one facet of the pursuit of liberation. Some of us, seeing someone in difficulty, say: \"That is his own accumulated karma; let him experience it. Why should I trouble myself over it?\" When we speak this way, it amounts to disdaining the Lord who dwells within that person in difficulty. Such people never attain realization of the Lord. In our spiritual practice, prayer and austerity alone are not enough; service to people in difficulty is also a principal element here. Madhvacharya asserts that it is the Gita's command that a practitioner should carry out this service alongside his daily observances. Good action is measured by how much the food cooked in your house satisfies the hunger of how many hungry people. For this reason there was a tradition in earlier times: after cooking, one would offer the food to God, then wait about ten minutes at the main door, and only then eat. This was because, if any hungry person came along the street of the house during this time, the custom then was to serve that person food first, and only afterward would the head of the household eat. Not only this — before even this, they used to perform \"balihārana.\" Giving food to the animals and birds around the house is called \"balihāraṇa.\" If one does good deeds throughout one's life in this way, the fruit of it becomes sattvic. Because of this, even if one is unable to attain knowledge in this birth, that opportunity presents itself in the next birth. But if, despite possessing knowledge, one lives doing wrong, one must again be born in a degraded womb.\n\nPerforming fire-rituals, charity, gifts to brahmins — doing all of this, if the pride \"I am greater than everyone\" arises, that becomes an action of rajas. Its fruit is greed; a life mixed with sorrow. Because of this, even having performed a meritorious act, one must experience sorrow. Action performed out of ignorance, or under pressure from someone else, never allows knowledge to grow. Ignorance itself is the fruit of tamasic action.\n\nKrishna explains, in summary form, in the next verse, how it can be known which quality is fully awake within us.",
  "14.17": "sattvāt sañjāyate jñānaṃ rajasaḥ lobha eva ca |\npramādamohau tamasaḥ bhavataḥ ajñānam eva ca — From sattva, understanding becomes clear. From rajas, greed grows. From tamas arise negligence, delusion, and ignorance.\n\n\"sattvāt sañjāyate jñānam\" — this line of the Gita carries a very important message. When a person reaches a firm resolve that knowledge matters more than everything else, and that he is prepared to give up anything for its sake — then we can know beyond doubt that this is the full awakening of sattva. A fine example of this is the conversation between Yama and Nachiketa found in the Kathopanishad:\n\netat tulyaṃ yadi manyase varaṃ vṛṇīṣṭa vittaṃ cirajīvikāṃ ca |\nmahābhūmau nachiketas tvam edhi kāmānāṃ tvā kāmabhājaṃ karomi || 1-24 ||\nye ye kāmā durlabhā martyaloke sarvān kāmāṃś chandataḥ prārthayasva |\nimā rāmāḥ sarathāḥ satūryā na hīdṛśā lambhanīyā manuṣyaiḥ |\nābhir matprattābhiḥ paricārayasva nachiketo maraṇaṃ mānuprākṣīḥ || 1-25 ||\n\nHere, when Nachiketa asked Yama to teach him knowledge of the Self, Yamaraja said: \"You are still young; you do not need that learning. Instead I will make you emperor of the whole earth; I will grant whatever you desire; I will give you long life and prosperity.\" Nachiketa then replied:\n\nśvobhāvā martyasya yad antake tat sarvendriyāṇāṃ jarayanti tejaḥ |\napi sarvaṃ jīvitam alpam eva tavaiva vāhās tava nṛtyagīte || 1-26 ||\nna vittena tarpaṇīyo manuṣyo lapsyāmahe vittam adrākṣma cet tvā |\njīviṣyāmo yāvad īśiṣyasi tvaṃ varas tu me varaṇīyaḥ sa eva || 1-27 ||\najīryatām amṛtānām upetya jīryan martyaḥ kva-adhaḥsthaḥ prajānan |\nabhidhyāyan varṇaratipramodān atidīrghe jīvite ko rameta || 1-28 ||\nyasmin nidaṃ vicikitsanti mṛtyo yat sāṃparāye mahati brūhi nas tat |\nyo'yaṃ varo gūḍham anupraviṣṭo nānyaṃ tasmān nachiketā vṛṇīte || 1-29 ||\n\n\"Whatever you give me is trifling. However long a life one gets, one must die one day. Therefore I want none of this. Even if you offered me the entire world, I would not want it. I want knowledge. Teach me that very knowledge of the Self.\" This is true sattva, unswayed by any temptation. A fine, recent example of how those with a genuine hunger for the pursuit of knowledge become ready to lose everything and walk the path of knowledge in this way is Swami Rama (1925–1996). We can read and learn of his life story in his book. Even after studying scripture, for the true meaning of scripture to arise within us, sattva must be present within us. Knowledge of the Lord, denoted by the syllable \"A,\" arises only when the quality of sattva is present. When sattva is fully awakened within us, true knowledge becomes clear to us, and we come to know the truth that, before this knowledge, all other worldly matters are as nothing.\n\n\"rajaso lobha eva ca\" — greed is the exclusive quality of rajas. It cannot be seen in sattvic people. Desire for some object, anger when that object is not obtained, and greed the moment it is obtained. Such a person neither uses it himself nor gives it to another. Charity is a very distant thing for such people! That we should live sharing whatever we have is sattva. This is stated very beautifully in the Ishopanishad:\n\nīśāvāsyam idaṃ sarvaṃ yat kiñca jagatyāṃ jagat |\ntena tyaktena bhuñjīthā mā gṛdhaḥ kasya svid dhanam || 1 ||\n\nThe Lord is the master of everything. We should enjoy what He has given us. We should not covet wealth that is not ours. The Lord is the owner of all the wealth of this world. Therefore it is not right to think of the wealth He has given as \"mine\" and live greedily. But this greed is a very significant flaw of rajas. It makes a person, without any compassion for others, hoard what he has earned without giving it to another, and sometimes even without eating it himself. This greed is a defect so bad that it is generally not even found among animals. If the trait of greed is found in us, we should recognize that this is the awakened state of rajas.\n\n\"pramādamohau tamaso bhavato'jñānam eva ca\" — just as greed comes from rajas, so negligence and delusion, and from these, ignorance, come from tamas. Becoming excessively attached to someone, forgiving the wrongs that person does because of that attachment, dancing to their tune — and so on. This is so powerful an influence that it can cover over our very knowledge and lead us onto the wrong path. A fine example of this is the story of Ajamila in the first chapter of the sixth canto of the Bhagavata. He was a sattvic brahmin. He was married. Once, when he went into the forest to gather flowers and tulasi for worship, a scene he saw there had a deep effect on his mind. He began to be infatuated with a young woman he saw in the forest. For her sake he took to liquor and meat. To satisfy her desires he took to theft. A brahmin who had been sattvic became fully tamasic. Looking at this story from a psychological point of view, we can understand why such an event took place. Ajamila was married, but in his household, because of the many ritual observances of purity and impurity, he had forcibly suppressed the desire within him. If we forcibly hold back our desire, it will one day burst forth and come out. For this reason the authors of the scriptures say: \"before undertaking spiritual practice, if you have infatuation with worldly pleasure, experience it, come to know its emptiness, and then practice becomes easy.\" Ajamila had studied scripture, and yet he fell victim to infatuation. Because of this, all his knowledge was forgotten. Tamas made him do a wrong he should never have done. This is the effect that occurs upon us when tamas becomes powerful within us.\n\nIn this verse Krishna has clearly outlined which quality is at work within us. The summary of this verse is this: if there is thirst for knowledge, that is a sattvic quality; if there is greed of the kind \"I alone should have it, others should not get it,\" that is rajas; if excessive attachment and infatuation develop toward some physical object, that is the work of tamas.\n\nThere is another aspect to sattva, rajas, and tamas. In worship, after the nine powers are named, before beginning contemplation of the Lord, a mantra is recited: \"saṃ sattvāya namaḥ — raṃ rajase namaḥ — taṃ tamase namaḥ.\" Some also recite this mantra as: \"saṃ sattvāya śriyo namaḥ — raṃ rajase bhuve namaḥ — taṃ tamase durgāyai namaḥ.\" [This latter is not the mantra itself; the original form of the mantra is: \"saṃ sattvāya namaḥ — raṃ rajase namaḥ — taṃ tamase namaḥ.\"] Here, \"saṃ sattvāya namaḥ\" does not mean offering salutation to the quality of sattva itself — it means offering salutation to Shri Devi, who presides over sattva. \"raṃ rajase namaḥ\" means salutation to Bhu Devi, who presides over rajas. \"taṃ tamase namaḥ\" means salutation to Durga Devi, who presides over tamas. These are the three forms of Lakshmi. Lakshmi's original form is called \"Mahalakshmi.\" From this original form come three forms of special manifestation (āveśa).\n\nHere, the one who awakens sattva is Shri Devi; the one who awakens rajas is Bhu Devi; and the one who awakens the quality of tamas is Durga Devi, who presides over tamas. \"sattvāt sañjāyate jñānam\" means: Lakshmi, who presides over the quality of sattva, is the giver of knowledge. For Narayana's knowledge to arise, one needs the grace of Shri Devi. Everyone knows that \"Shri\" bestows wealth. But few know that it is Shri Devi who bestows the great wealth called knowledge. Money is indeed wealth, but knowledge is the great wealth — and she who gives it is \"Shri.\" For knowledge to awaken within us, we must first worship the mother in her Shri form, and beg her to grant us knowledge of the Lord.\n\nAs the authors of scripture say, before the mind can flow toward the Lord, we must first pray to the deity presiding over the mind. Shiva is the deity presiding over the mind. Those who seek knowledge have their mind's tamas, its darkness, driven away and filled with light by Shiva and Parvati. For this reason, we must first pray to the principle of Shiva for the sake of knowledge. After Shiva grants knowledge, for it to remain in the mind as retained memory, we must pray to the life-force (Brahma-Vayu, Sarasvati-Bharati). The chief power that keeps the knowledge that has come to us from fading (making it stable) is the life-force (Anjaneya). And she who carries what has thus become stable further upward to reach the Lord is Lakshmi Devi, who is ever with the Lord. Such a Lakshmi does not grant awareness in all her forms. It is in her Shri form that she bestows the wealth that takes the form of knowledge. \"Shri\" is one of Mahalakshmi's manifestations — the form that has taken a vow alongside Vishnu, governing sattva, for the protection of the world. Therefore, for those walking the path of knowledge, the grace of Lakshmi's Shri aspect is of chief importance.\n\nHere we have analysed how, by the grace of Shiva, Pranadeva, and Shri Devi, one can advance on the path of knowledge and reach the Lord. Before we go on to analyse the nature of the elements, let us briefly consider the significance of the peace mantra and the prayer to Ganapati. Before reading any scripture, we recite the peace mantra: \"Om shantih shantih shantih.\" The meaning of this peace mantra is: \"O Lord, who art the very culmination of knowledge and bliss, lead me on the path of knowledge and bliss (peace).\" In the same way, before every worship we first pray to Ganapati. This is because Ganapati, son of Shiva-Parvati, is the deity of space (ākāśa). This space was created from the Lord's navel. Therefore Ganapati is the deity of our own navel.\n\nnābhya āsīd antarikṣaṃ śīrṣṇo dyauḥ samavartata |\npadbhyāṃ bhūmir diśaḥ śrotrāt tathā lokān akalpayan\n\nThe three chakras near our own navel are the Muladhara, Svadhishthana, and Manipura. Above the navel, in the lotus of the heart, lies the Anahata chakra, the true seat of spiritual practice. To pass beyond the Muladhara, Svadhishthana, and Manipura chakras and enter the heart, Ganapati must open the door of the navel. For this reason we pray first of all to Ganapati. Before every ritual sacrifice, a Ganapati mandala is drawn and worshipped, and only then does the sacrifice begin. When Ganapati opens the door of the navel, the heart-lotus opens of its own accord. This state is called \"vṛṣāha.\" The Lord accepts our worship as \"vṛṣāhī\" in this state. Such a Lord is also called \"Vṛṣāhī.\"\n\n\"rajaso lobha eva ca\" — rajas means Bhu Devi, who presides over the quality of rajas. Here, Bhu Devi does not mean the deity in the eighteenth orbit who governs the earth; rather it is \"Bhu,\" the manifestation of Lakshmi who governs even that deity, and who is the cause of creation. Enchantment, attraction, pull, infatuation — all of these are faces of rajas. And so, at the root of one person loving another, what is needed is precisely this enchantment. The mutual attraction of male and female is the very cause of creation. The element that gives, for the sake of creation, the pull needed for creation, and that same pull that leads one astray, both lie within rajas. It is Bhu Devi who bestows this nature of rajas. Infatuation such as \"my property, my house, my earnings, my money\" and so on comes from rajas.\n\n\"pramādamohau tamasaḥ bhavato'jñānam eva ca\" — Durga is the one who presides over the destructive power of tamas, which physically leads us toward ruin. She grants the intellect that inclines toward destruction. Taking wrong for right and doing wrong, slipping because of it, excessive attachment to some notion, and the absence of knowledge — all of these come from the influence of tamas. Thus Shri-Bhu-Durga, the three manifest forms of Conscious Prakriti, presiding over sattva, rajas, and tamas, bind us through the three gunas. Therefore the three forms of the consciousness of Prakriti are precisely sattva, rajas, and tamas. [There is a hint in the Gita itself as to why this meaning must be understood here — this can be seen in verse 19 of this very chapter, further ahead. For more information about Mahalakshmi's Shri-Bhu-Durga forms, one may read the \"Sarga-anusarga-laya-prādurbhāva-nirṇaya\" section of the Mahabharata Tatparya Nirnaya.]",
  "14.18": "ūrdhvaṃ gacchanti sattvasthāḥ madhye tiṣṭhanti rājasāḥ |\njaghanyaguṇavṛttisthāḥ adhaḥ gacchanti tāmasāḥ — Those established in sattva rise upward. The rajasic remain in the middle. The tamasic, established in the lowest quality's activity, sink downward.\n\nHaving described the nature of the three gunas, Krishna here explains the three levels of their effect. Those established in sattva rise mentally upward. There is no elevation at all for one in rajas. One established in tamas experiences a mental downfall. Here the word \"ūrdhva\" has a special meaning. As the Kathopanishad says:\n\nūrdhvamūlo avākśākhaḥ eṣo aśvatthaḥ sanātanaḥ || 2-3-1 ||\n\n\"Ūrdhva\" means the Lord, who is above everything. For one established in sattva to go upward means moving with one's face turned toward the Lord. As he moves forward, he grows closer and closer to the Lord. But one established in tamas moves with his back turned to the Lord. One in rajas continues in the same place, in an unsettled state.\n\nThe power that keeps the gods in bondage is Shri Devi; the power that keeps human beings in bondage is Bhu Devi; and the power that keeps the demons in bondage is Durga Devi. The sattvic, becoming objects of the grace of Shri Devi, who presides over sattva, attain the higher worlds [the six subtle worlds above the earth, and finally liberation]; the rajasic continue on within the bondage of Bhu Devi [earth and the intermediate space]; and the tamasic reach the tamasic world under the control of Durga Devi [the seven subtle worlds below the earth].",
  "14.19": "nānyaṃ guṇebhyaḥ kartāraṃ yadā draṣṭā anupaśyati |\nguṇebhyaś ca paraṃ vetti mad bhāvaṃ so'dhigacchati — When the seer perceives that there is no doer other than the three gunas [that the Lord, distinct from the three gunas, is the one root cause of everything], and knows the Lord as beyond the gunas [he is the true human being], he attains My own state.\n\nWhatever happens in our lives is entirely the working of these three gunas. We ourselves do nothing of our own accord; rather, everything proceeds under the influence of the three gunas. Coming from Prakriti, they work while covering over our true nature. For this reason, it is not possible to know a person's true nature from his outward behavior alone. For this reason we must practice looking at the events that take place in the world without being disturbed by them.\n\nA mind attuned to sattva grasps only what accords with sattva. Such a person is never deluded into thinking \"I did this.\" It is said here that there is no doer other than the gunas. Insentient matter is never called a doer. Therefore \"guna\" here does not mean a merely insentient quality; the message in this verse is to recognize the conscious presiding power behind it [we discussed this in verse 17]. There are many kinds of agency. Wherever there is a willed act, that is a \"doer.\" This exists only in conscious beings. But true agency — \"independent, willed action\" — exists only in the Lord. The well-known saying \"nāhaṃ kartā hariḥ kartā\" — \"I am not the doer, Hari is the doer\" — is already known to us. This is stated in the Mokshadharma Parva in this way:\n\nnāhaṃ kartā na kartā tvaṃ kartā yas tu sadā prabhuḥ |\nso'yaṃ pacati kālo mām vṛkṣe phalam ivāgatam || 12-220-84 ||\n\n\"Neither have you done it, nor have I done it — 'He' has made us do it.\" When this awareness is present, the pride of agency cannot arise in us. From this comes freedom from hatred and freedom from possessiveness. \"I did it, it happened because of me\" is merely delusion. Whatever happens in this world happens under the influence of these three gunas. Beyond that, I myself, on my own, have done nothing. This awareness must come to us.\n\nThe influence of the three gunas exists both in insentient matter and in sentient beings. Action proceeds under its influence. If someone scolds you, it means that in that person, at that moment, rajas is awake and at work. We should conduct ourselves in that moment with this awareness. Shri Ramachandra demonstrated this in his own life. As Valmiki says in the Ramayana:\n\nsa hi nityaṃ praśāntaṃ mṛdupūrvaṃ ca bhāṣate |\nucyamāno'pi paruṣaṃ nottaraṃ pratipadyate || Ayodhya-1.15 ||\n\nWhenever anyone confronted Rama with a quarrel, he never grew angry. He would simply say \"Let us speak again later\" and walk away. This is because, when rajas is at work in one person, an occasion arises for us too to have to respond with rajas toward them. This is very dangerous. Therefore, do not become rajasic yourself toward one who is under the sway of rajas. Remain neutral and stand apart. Then their rajas subsides of its own accord. If one remains silent before those who grow angry, their anger dissolves very quickly. When we recognize the influence of these qualities in this way, the very course of our life changes.\n\nIt is not enough merely to know what has been said above. The one who stands beyond these three gunas is the Lord. When you know Him, it becomes possible for you to stand beyond the three gunas as well. We must understand the truth that, to reach that Lord, I must come out from under the influence of these three gunas. \"Such a person's mind becomes established in Me, and because of this, he goes on to come to Me and to abide in Me alone,\" says Krishna.\n\nThere is another facet to this verse. \"guṇebhyaḥ anyaṃ kartāraṃ yadā draṣṭā anupaśyati tadā saḥ 'naraḥ' bhavati.\" Here \"guṇebhyaḥ anyam\" means a power beyond even Mahalakshmi, who presides over the three gunas, who is denoted by the very word \"guna,\" and who bears the names Shri-Bhu-Durga. He alone is the true doer. Who is he? As the Mundakopanishad says:\n\nyadā paśyaḥ paśyate rukmavarṇaṃ kartāram īśaṃ puruṣaṃ brahmayonim || 3-1-3 ||\n\nHe who is the father even of Chaturmukha, who governs even conscious Prakriti, who is denoted by all the Vedas — he alone is the doer. One who knows this is the true human being.\n\nThe body that has the facility to undertake every spiritual practice is the human body alone. He who makes use of such a body is \"nara\" (man). To be freed from dharma, artha, and kama means to be freed from sattva, rajas, and tamas and to reach the Lord. This is the goal of human life. Only a human being has the awareness: \"these three gunas have been working within me; I must shed this influence and rise above it.\" Because of this awareness, \"madbhāvaṃ so'dhigacchati\" — devotion and knowledge fill his mind, and he attains liberation.",
  "14.20": "guṇān etān atītya trīn dehī dehasamudbhavān |\njanmamṛtyujarāduḥkhair vimukto'mṛtam aśnute — When the embodied one crosses beyond these three gunas that arise in the body, he becomes free even from the sorrows of birth, death, and old age, and attains immortality.\n\nWe must cross beyond these three gunas that cover our true nature and exert such a deep effect upon us. [Here, crossing beyond sattva among the three gunas means crossing beyond gross sattva and reaching subtle sattva.] Thus, having crossed the three gunas and become one who is beyond the three gunas, we can then cross beyond all the birth, death, disease, old age, and sorrow of this worldly existence, reach the Lord, and become immortal. From this it becomes possible to attain liberation, the state of bliss in which there is never again birth or death.\n\nHere Krishna has said that crossing beyond the three gunas leads to the attainment of liberation. Having said this, a question naturally arises for us. Arjuna places that very question before Krishna in the next verse.",
  "14.21": "Arjuna said:\n\nkaiḥ liṅgaiḥ trīn guṇān etān atītaḥ bhavati prabho |\nkim ācāraḥ katham ca etān trīn guṇān ativartate — O Lord, by what signs is one known to have crossed beyond these three gunas? What is his conduct? How does he cross beyond these three gunas?\n\nOn behalf of all of us, Arjuna places this question before Krishna. He asks: \"If there is one who has crossed beyond the three gunas, how is he to be recognized? What are his signs? What is his conduct like? How does he cross beyond these three gunas?\"\n\nHere Arjuna addresses Krishna as \"Prabho\" — \"Lord.\" Behind this address lies the resonance \"You alone can surely answer my questions\" together with complete surrender. The Lord answers Arjuna's question in detail in the verses that follow.",
  "14.22": "The Lord said:\n\nprakāśaṃ ca pravṛttiṃ ca moham eva ca pāṇḍava |\nna dveṣṭi sampravṛttāni na nivṛttāni kāṅkṣati — O Pandava, he neither hates the light of worldly understanding, nor the vigor of activity, nor delusion when they are present; nor does he long for them when they are absent.\n\nOne who has crossed beyond the three gunas does not hate whatever comes to him that he did not seek; nor does he long for what he sought when it does not come. He carries within him the detachment of one who thinks: \"the Lord knows better than I do what is needed and what is not; why then should I trouble my mind over it?\" Whatever comes into his life, he welcomes it. Vyasa says in the Shanti Parva of the Mahabharata:\n\nevam apy aśubhaṃ karma na bhūtaṃ na bhaviṣyati || 12-32-18 ||\n\n\"What ought not to happen has not happened in the past, nor will it happen in the future. Whatever is to happen will surely come to pass. What is the use, then, of hating it and calling it unwanted?\" Therefore one who has crossed beyond the three gunas does not hate what comes, calling it \"unwanted,\" nor does he long for what is absent. He looks upon everything without agitation, living like a filled vessel that does not spill over.\n\nIn this world, some people are drawn to sattva, others merely to activity, and yet others have no interest at all — living in darkness without even knowing it as darkness. This is a perfectly ordinary sight. Those who stand beyond the three gunas see this without their minds becoming disturbed. Knowing that \"everything proceeds according to each person's own governing quality,\" they live as unattached witnesses. They speak what they see as true, but they never build up argument and dispute over it. They accept everything, thinking: \"All of this is the Lord's play, an arrangement He has made.\" When Krishna described, in the twelfth chapter of the Gita, what the \"true devotee\" is like, he gave the very same description found in this verse. This means that true devotion is precisely this standing beyond outward quality.\n\nHere Vyasa has used the epithet \"Pāṇḍava.\" \"Paṇḍā\" means a wise person; \"Pāṇḍava\" means one who recognizes knowledge and bows his head to it. Behind this epithet lies the message: \"before you can become one beyond the three gunas, you must first become a Pandava.\"",
  "14.23": "udāsīnavat āsīnaḥ guṇaiḥ yaḥ na vicālyate |\nguṇāḥ vartanta iti evam yaḥ avatiṣṭhati na iṅgate — He remains as if indifferent, unmoved by the pull of the gunas. Knowing simply that \"the gunas are acting,\" he remains still and does not stir to their rhythm.\n\nIn worldly dealings, such a person remains as if indifferent. Our own wants and aversions arise from the three gunas. One beyond the three gunas does not wish \"it must happen this way, it must happen that way.\" He carries the faith that \"the Lord will bring about whatever ought to happen, exactly as it ought to happen.\" [Here we must understand one thing: the events that take place in this world are not under our control. What we wish should not happen may happen; what we wish should happen may not happen. Given this, there is no sense in needlessly agitating the mind by insisting \"it must happen this way.\"] Living in this manner, there is no anger when what is desired does not come to pass, and no elation when it does. The mind remains serene.\n\nThe senses exist within the gunas, and behind the senses lie the gunas. But one beyond the three gunas, knowing all of this and not letting his mind be disturbed by it, lives without restlessness even in the midst of any action [even a passing, momentary action].",
  "14.24": "samaduḥkhasukhaḥ svasthaḥ samaloṣṭāśmakāñcanaḥ |\ntulyapriyāpriyaḥ dhīraḥ tulyanindātmasaṃstutiḥ — He looks upon pleasure and pain equally. He abides within himself. Clod, stone, and gold are the same to him. Made steadfast through practice, the pleasant and the unpleasant are the same to him; blame and praise are the same to him.\n\nOne beyond the three gunas looks upon all pleasure and sorrow equally. This is because what appears to us on the surface as pleasure need not actually be pleasure; and the same is true of sorrow. When one comes to understand this, the feeling arises within the mind that all of this is the Lord's play. Then the capacity to see everything equally grows within us.\n\nJust as we watch a scene unfolding in a play as spectators, so too we should practice watching the events that occur in the world as spectators. It may be full of joy, it may be full of sorrow — but we should relish each and every action of the Lord. The temporary sorrow that comes in life is itself the cause of our lasting joy. It is only that we do not know this. Those who are beyond the three gunas, understanding this truth, look upon pleasure and sorrow equally and live in bliss.\n\nKrishna here repeats again the very words he used earlier when describing the characteristics of the person of steady wisdom and of the devotee. To one beyond the three gunas, earth, precious gem, and gold are all the same. Neither gold nor gem appears precious to him. For him, knowledge alone is precious. For this reason he shows no craving whatsoever for money, pearls and gems, or gold. And so he lives in happiness. A verse in the third canto of the Bhagavata says this:\n\nyaś ca mūḍhatamo loke yaś ca buddheḥ paraṃ gataḥ |\ntāv ubhau sukham edhete kliśyaty antarito janaḥ || 3-7-17 ||\n\nOne who is utterly deluded and one who has gone beyond intellect — these two alone can live happily in this world, because neither has any infatuation with money. But those who stand between these two, not knowing the meaning of life, fall victim to infatuation and suffer sorrow.\n\nA fine example of how the wise incorporate this principle into their own lives is the life story of Ramakrishna Paramahamsa. Another fine illustration is the way of life lived by Vishvapriya Tirtha (Vrindavanacharya), who was the swami of the Sode Matha in the time of the Maharaja of Mysore.\n\nLooking at this verse from another angle, we come to know a further special message. The devotion to the Lord of a true devotee (one beyond the three gunas) is not thrown off balance by the pleasures and sorrows that arise in his life. Whether in poverty or in wealth, he remains constantly engaged in devotion to the Lord. The love or hatred of others is never the measure of his devotion. In this way, those beyond the three gunas live with equanimity in every condition.",
  "14.25": "mānāpamānayoḥ tulyaḥ tulyaḥ mitrāripakṣayoḥ |\nsarvārambhaparityāgī guṇātītaḥ sa ucyate — Honor and dishonor are the same to him; the party of friends and the party of foes are the same to him. He renounces all undertakings. Such a one is called \"beyond the gunas.\"\n\nOne beyond the three gunas may be honored by one person, who speaks of him as \"a great sage\"; another may revile him as \"half mad.\" But none of this has any effect upon him. He has neither the infatuation of thinking \"these are my friends,\" nor the hatred of thinking \"these are my enemies.\" He looks upon everyone according to their own worth, living without engaging in any activity opposed to devotion to the Lord. He does not engage himself in any long-term undertaking of his own.\n\nThus, in the four verses above, Krishna has beautifully described the complete picture of the nature of one beyond the gunas. We can recognize those possessing this nature as being beyond the gunas.\n\nHaving thus explained the three gunas and the nature of one beyond the three gunas up to this point, Krishna explains in the final two verses how we ourselves may become beyond the three gunas. Since all our activity lies within the bounds of these three gunas, standing beyond the three gunas is, for us, beyond imagining. And so here Krishna explains the one and only path by which we may become beyond the three gunas.",
  "14.26": "māṃ ca yaḥ avyabhicāreṇa bhaktiyogena sevate |\nsaḥ guṇān samatītya etān brahmabhūyāya kalpate — One who serves Me with unswerving devotion, tearing apart the bonds of these gunas, becomes fit for the Lord's devotion, like conscious Prakriti herself. [He attains conscious Prakriti.]\n\nIf we try, of our own effort, to become beyond the three gunas, we cannot possibly find success in that effort, because all our thinking revolves entirely within the three gunas. This is a wheel spinning in circles. To be delivered from this cycle, the very holder of that wheel must come to our aid. As the Dvadasha-stotra says: \"trigunātīta vidhāraka parito dehi subhaktim\" — it is the Lord who upholds those who are beyond the three gunas. Therefore the one and only path to becoming beyond the three gunas is to take refuge in that Lord. By offering unwavering, exclusive devotion to that Lord together with Lakshmi, one can shed all these bonds — including death, which came into this world wearing the fifteen-fold fence of bondage from Lakshmi — and return once more to the mother's own lap.",
  "14.27": "brahmaṇo hi pratiṣṭhā aham amṛtasya avyayasya ca |\nśāśvatasya ca dharmasya sukhasya aikāntikasya ca — I alone am the support and foundation of the imperishable, unchanging conscious Prakriti; of eternal dharma; and of liberation, which is bliss alone.\n\nWhen one stands beyond the three gunas, the soul's own natural, eternal dharma becomes manifest. In this state, mention is made of conscious Prakriti, the principle of Brahman, because in these very verses lies the essence of the Acharya's entire doctrine.\n\nThere was a tradition in earlier times: when one sat down to eat, before beginning the meal, one would recite this verse of this chapter, and only then eat. The reasoning behind that tradition was: \"before the leaf on which we eat, we have thereby contemplated the essence of the entire scripture.\" In the thirteenth chapter, Krishna explained about kṣetra and kṣetrajña. In this chapter, Krishna explains the very same subject of the universe and the Lord who governs the universe still more clearly, from a further angle. Prakriti and the created universe, the way of crossing beyond that universe, and finally a summary of the entire philosophy — all of this is contained in this chapter. Krishna has given us, through this chapter, the special message of understanding that we need to know.\n\nThe sentient-insentient universe is \"kṣara.\" Beyond that lies conscious Prakriti, \"akṣara.\" Beyond that lies the Lord who governs all, \"Puruṣottama.\" As explained at the start of the twelfth chapter, the soul is the sixteenth, enclosed within fifteen fences. She who binds these fifteen tightly around the sixteenth is the seventeenth, Shri Lakshmi. He who frees us from this fence and grants us liberation is the eighteenth, Purushottama. [To learn in detail about these fifteen fences, see the sixth question of the Prashna Upanishad: śraddhāṃ khaṃ vāyuḥ jyotiḥ āpaḥ pṛthivī indriyaṃ manaḥ | annam annādi vīryaṃ tapo mantrāḥ karma lokāḥ lokeṣu ca nāma ca || 6-4 || — these are the fifteen fences.]\n\n\"Eighteen\" is a number that lays before us the sum total of philosophy. Through eighteen verses in this chapter, Krishna has laid open before us the essence of the entire scripture. Come, let us hear and learn this remarkable chapter from Krishna.",
  "15.1": "Śrī Bhagavān uvāca - The Lord said:\n\nŪrdhvamūlam adhaḥ śākham aśvattham prāhuḥ avyayam |\n\nchandāṃsi yasya parṇāni yastaṃ veda sa vedavit -- Bhagavān above, and Prakṛti, are the root of the universe. The living beings and inert matter below are its branches and twigs. This is the ever-existing [aśvattha - what stood today does not stand tomorrow], [aśvattha - like a horse, ceaselessly in motion] peepal tree. The Vedas are its leaves. One who knows this knows the Vedas.\n\nThat the Bhagavad Gītā is the essence of the Upaniṣads is proved by this very verse. The Kaṭhopaniṣad says: \"ūrdhvamūlo'vākśākha eṣo'śvatthaḥ sanātanaḥ\" — here it is put as \"ūrdhvamūlam adhaḥ śākham aśvattham prāhuḥ avyayam.\" This is a line packed with meaning. Every single word here conceals many philosophical ideas.\n\nTranslated directly, this line becomes: \"This universe itself is a peepal tree. Its root is above, its branches and twigs below.\" Read at face value, this makes no sense to us at all. An \"upside-down peepal tree\" — what is this? And of all trees, why did Kṛṣṇa pick the aśvattha — a tree that gives no fruit, sheds no flowers, and is not even useful for building a house? To understand this we must dig beneath the word 'aśvattha', peeling it layer by layer.\n\n(1) The coarse meaning of aśvattha is the peepal tree. This tree differs from every other tree in the world. When there is not even a stir of wind and all other trees stand still, the faintest breath of air sets the aśvattha's leaves trembling and rustling. In Sanskrit it is also called 'caladalaḥ' — the tree with ever-moving leaves. This is exactly the resemblance between the world and the aśvattha tree: the world too is forever in motion, forever changing.\n\n(2) Aśvattha: here 'aśva' means horse. The world is like a horse. Observing a horse makes this meaning clear. There is one difference between a horse and other animals — every other animal can stand perfectly still while resting, but a horse never stands motionless, not even for an instant. This world too is like the horse — it never stands still.\n\n(3) A-śvaḥ-tha — here 'śvaḥ' means tomorrow, and 'tha' means that which remains standing. Aśvattha means \"na śvaḥ tiṣṭhati\" — it will not stand tomorrow. It is here today, but there is no guarantee it will be here tomorrow — that is, this world is momentary. In the Kaṭhopaniṣad, when Yama offers Naciketas any pleasure he desires, Naciketas replies: \"śvobhāvā martyasya yadantakaitat… sarvendriyāṇāṃ jarayanti tejaḥ\" (1.26). \"In this ever-changing, momentary world, I do not want the pleasures that consume us — give me knowledge instead,\" Naciketas asks. Change is the very law of this world.\n\n(4) Looking at the strict etymological meaning of aśvattha — aśu-vā-ta-tha. Here 'aśu-vā' means that which moves faster than everything else — that is Bhagavān. For this reason Bhagavān is also called 'Aśva'. In His horse-faced Hayagrīva form, Bhagavān gave the entire body of Vedic knowledge to the world. Having known everything before anyone else, Bhagavān sits (tha) under the name 'Aśva' to regulate this world, within and without. He pervades (tata) this entire world, and in the end the whole world becomes His food (thaṃ — the food that consumes the entire world; this describes pralaya, the dissolution).\n\nThese are the meanings we can find packed into the single word 'aśvattha'. No other language can pack this many ideas into one word — which is why Sanskrit is called a 'pregnant' (meaning-laden) language. This same tree-image and description is found in the Bible too, where they call the tree \"the tree of the knowledge of good and evil\" in \"the Garden of Eden,\" and say: \"but of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, you shall not eat of it; for in the day that you eat of it you will surely die.\" That is: \"this tree bears fruit; eat this fruit and you will die.\" Here the fruit is the fruit of action (karma-phala). One who desires the fruit of his own action cannot attain deathless liberation — he circles endlessly through the wheel of birth and death. This is the inner meaning of that line.\n\nIn \"prāhuḥ avyayam,\" 'prāhuḥ' means \"having known, they declared.\" \"The sages of the Vedic age knew this world to be an aśvattha, and declared it so,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Having said \"this world is perishable, destined one day to be destroyed,\" Kṛṣṇa immediately turns and says — \"it is avyaya\"! That is, indestructible! To understand this, we must go and sit at the ocean's shore and watch the waves come in. Every single wave arrives with a roar. Not one wave there is permanent — every wave is momentary. But the ocean itself is never, for a single instant, without a wave. That is avyaya. In the very same way, one world is destroyed and another world is born. The world may perish, but the flow of the world is unbroken. After one dissolution comes another creation; after creation comes dissolution — this goes on without end. Hence the world is avyaya; the jīva is beginningless and endless; birth and death are continuous. The coming-together of the living being and inert matter is birth; their parting is death. Everything here shows nothing but ceaseless change. In sum, this world is the imperishable aśvattha.\n\nHere it is said \"this tree's root is above (ūrdhva-mūla).\" 'Above' means the One greater than all — Bhagavān. Bhagavān is the root of this tree called the world. At the very root of the world, at the highest point, stand Lakṣmī-Nārāyaṇa as mother and father. From inert Prakṛti this tree develops. From Bhagavān come Caturmukha Brahmā (the mahat-tattva), Vāyu, Garuḍa-Śeṣa-Rudra (the ahaṅkāra-tattva), Indra and the other gods, the lesser deities, the gandharvas, human beings, animals, and birds — branch after branch was created in this way, and all of these together became one tree. Here \"adhaḥ śākham\" means growth that proceeds from above downward — this points to the gradation (tāratamya) that exists among the deities.\n\nAfter the tree's branches and twigs come its leaves. \"This tree's leaves are the Vedas,\" says Kṛṣṇa. As we know, the world has two aspects — one is the world of names (nāmātmaka), the other is the world of forms (rūpātmaka). Here the world of forms is the branches and twigs, while the world of names is the tree's leaves. The very first thing created in the world of names was the Vedas. It is the Vedic words within the Vedas that gave things their names.\n\n\"sarveṣāṃ tu sa nāmāni karmāṇi ca pṛthak pṛthak, vedaśabdebhya evādau pṛthak saṃsthāś ca nirmame\" || Manusmṛti 1.21 ||\n\nWhatever words exist in the Veda are, at root, names of Bhagavān. These same names were then applied to worldly things — this is how words came into being. That is chandas, Vedic meter. The Vedic mantras — the whole body of Vedic literature — are the leaves of this tree.\n\nWhy was the Veda compared here to a leaf? Because for a tree to bear flowers and fruit, it is the leaf that carries the power to yield fruit. In the same way, it is the Vedas that yield for us the fruit of spiritual practice. For this reason Kṛṣṇa compared the tree to the universe, and the tree's leaf to the Vedas. At the time of dissolution the tree itself is destroyed, but the tree's leaf is not destroyed. This is why it is said, \"at the time of dissolution Bhagavān lies upon a banyan leaf.\" The Veda is eternal — it cannot be destroyed. Bhagavān lying upon the leaf means He lies there as denoted by the Veda (veda-vācya). This tells us that the Vedas existed even during the dissolution. Here a question may occur to us — when not a single thing existed, what does it mean to say the Vedas existed? In the line \"agnimīḷe purohitam…\" there was no fire and no priest either —\n\nno priest at all. If so, then who recites the Veda, and to whom do the Vedas speak, at the time of dissolution?\n\nAt the time of dissolution, the only meaning left for the Vedas is Bhagavān Himself — there is no other meaning. So every single word in the Veda speaks of Bhagavān. \"nāmāni sarvāṇi yamā viśanti tam vai viṣṇum parama udāharanti\" — He into whom all names enter, He is Viṣṇu. He is denoted by every word. Viṣṇu means \"viśanti sarvāṇi nāmāni atra\" — all names enter into Him here. As the Aitareya Āraṇyaka says: \"sarve ghoṣāḥ sarve vedāḥ sarvāḥ ṛcaḥ ekaiva vyāhṛtiḥ…\" — every sound in nature, every resonance, speaks of Bhagavān. Thus, at the time of dissolution, Lakṣmī, the presiding deity of the Vedas, sings the Veda (śrutigīta), and Bhagavān listens. This is the concept of the world of names. Kṛṣṇa says: \"whoever comes to know what has been said here knows the entire Veda.\"",
  "15.2": "Adhaḥ ca ūrdhvaṃ prasṛtāḥ tasya śākhāḥ guṇa pravṛddhāḥ viṣaya pravālāḥ |\n\nadhaḥ ca mūlāni anusaṃtatāni karma anubandhīni manuṣyaloke -- Fattened by the three guṇas, its branches spread both below and above. The objects of the senses are their sprouts. Its roots too spread downward, into the earth, following each being's own karma.\n\nHere the description of the tree is developed further. That this tree's roots spread both downward and upward brings to mind, unmistakably, the description of the banyan tree. The branches called the five elements pervade from above to below. Looking at our own body — Bhagavān sits in our sahasrāra. From above to below the five elements stand as branches, and the three guṇas — sattva, rajas, tamas — stand as branches too. Here sound, touch, form, taste, and smell are like the tender shoots. That is, the objects made of the five elements are this tree's shoots — and these shoots are what perpetually attract us. Bhagavān's infinite form fills our limbs from top to bottom, and His activity there follows our own karma. Bhagavān's root is above, yet He has spread Himself from above to below — thus He stands pervading everywhere.",
  "15.3": "Na rūpam asya iha tathā upalabhyate na antaḥ na ca ādiḥ na ca sampratiṣṭhā |\n\naśvattham enam su virūḍha mūlam asaṅgaśastreṇa dṛḍhena chittvā ||3||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and commentary for these two verses (15.3 and 15.4) are given together at the next verse (15.4).]",
  "15.4": "Na rūpam asya iha tathā upalabhyate na antaḥ na ca ādiḥ na ca sampratiṣṭhā |\n\naśvattham enam su virūḍha mūlam asaṅgaśastreṇa dṛḍhena chittvā ||3||\n\ntataḥ padaṃ tat parimārgitavyam yasmin gatāḥ na nivartanti bhūyaḥ |\n\ntam eva ca ādyam puruṣam prapadye yataḥ pravṛttiḥ prasṛtā purāṇī ||4|| -- Its true nature is not to be grasped as it merely appears here; its end is not seen, nor its beginning, nor even its resting-place. Cutting down this strongly-rooted peepal with the sharp sword called detachment, one should then go seeking that Supreme Reality. Those who reach Him never return again. From Him alone this ancient journey began. One must take refuge in that Supreme Puruṣa who exists before all things.\n\nNowhere else has the universe been described as clearly and completely as it is here. Elsewhere we find only fragments of description here and there, but Kṛṣṇa has given the complete picture here. Having explained this much, Kṛṣṇa now issues a caution. Kṛṣṇa says: \"living within this world and looking at the world, the unbroken vision I have described is not possible.\" We are never able to know any single thing fully and completely, because without knowing the whole universe, even one tiny particle of the universe cannot be understood. Just as a frog sitting on the ocean's shore cannot know the ocean's depth, breadth, and vastness, in the same way we cannot come to know this world fully.\n\nHow did this world come into being? Where does it go when it is destroyed at the time of dissolution? How do all the planets and spheres stand unsupported in this sky? If we go on asking questions like this, we have no answer among ourselves. Even science has no clear answer to this question, because science stands on nothing but the tower of imagination.\n\nThe Ṛgveda, describing the state of things at the time of dissolution, says: \"nāsadāsīnno'sadāsīttadānīṃ nāsīdrajo no vyomā…\" (10.129.1) — meaning, neither what can be seen with the eye existed, nor what cannot be seen; even these worlds did not exist, at the time of dissolution. So then, was this world created out of nothing at all? The Chāndogya Upaniṣad, refuting the notion that 'everything was born from nothing,' says: \"kathamasataḥ sajjāyeta…\" (6.2.2). We have all heard that at the time of dissolution Bhagavān lies in the ocean of dissolution. When this very world does not exist, what ocean, what water is there? How are we to make sense of all this?\n\nTo put it briefly: at the time of dissolution, everything shatters and takes on the form of atoms — this itself is the pralaya-samudra, the ocean of dissolution. In this age, all classes of living beings shed their gross bodies and, in their subtle bodies, remain within Bhagavān's own being, without even the awareness of their own existence. At the time of creation, this world takes shape stage by stage. It is Bhagavān who does all of this. Such a world is so deep that its head, foot, and middle cannot be known at all. For this reason, we must first come to know that one thing, knowing which everything can be known. In the Chāndogya Upaniṣad, Uddālaka, addressing his son Śvetaketu — who had just returned after finishing twelve years of study at the gurukula and stood before his father stiff with pride — asks him: \"uta taṃ ādeṣṭaṃ aprākṣaḥ; yenāśrutaṃ śrutaṃ bhavati, amataṃ mataṃ avijñātaṃ vijñātaṃ…\" (6.1.3) — \"Have you asked for that teaching by which the unheard becomes heard, the unthought becomes thought, the unknown becomes known?\" Here Kṛṣṇa says the same thing: \"surrender to that Supreme Power in order to know that, knowing which everything is known.\" The Upaniṣad too says exactly this: \"yasmin vijñāte sarvamidaṃ vijñātaṃ bhavati\" (Śāṇḍilya 2.2; Muṇḍaka 1.1.3).\n\nGranting that knowing Bhagavān means knowing everything — but by what means is He to be known? How is one to reach the root of this tree, so unbroken and so deeply rooted? To this Kṛṣṇa answers: \"sharpen the sword called knowledge on the whetstone of detachment (detachment) and worship, cut down the deeply-rooted tree, and go seeking the Supreme Reality.\" We must seek out that place from which, having gone, there is no returning here again. Only the knowledge that comes from realising Bhagavān's limitlessness can serve as this axe. Here too, we cannot reach the root by our own effort alone — we do not have that power. Hence the one path open to us is surrender to that Supreme Power. Only by His grace can we reach that Puruṣottama.",
  "15.5": "Nirmānamohāḥ jita saṅga doṣāḥ adhyātma nityāḥ vinivṛttakāmāḥ |\n\ndvandvaiḥ vimuktāḥ sukha duḥkha saṃjñaiḥ gacchanti amūḍhāḥ padam avyayam tat -- Free of pride, past delusion, having conquered the taint of attachment, ever fixed on the Self, their desires withdrawn entirely, freed from the pairs of opposites known as pleasure and pain — such wise ones reach that imperishable abode.\n\nThe matter Kṛṣṇa speaks of in this verse has come up here and there in many earlier chapters, and each time it arose we discussed it in some detail. Here Kṛṣṇa states it all together, as a whole. This verse describes how the course of our life ought to be as we move forward begging for surrender to Bhagavān. \"Before anything else, one must give up māna and moha,\" says Kṛṣṇa — that is, the two must first be abandoned: 'ahaṅkāra', the sense of 'I', and 'mamakāra', the sense of 'mine'. We arrive in this world naked, and we leave it naked too — so falling into 'I' and 'mine', ahaṅkāra and mamakāra, has no meaning whatsoever here. The taint of attachment (saṅga-doṣa) works many harmful effects upon us; hence we must conquer this taint. Excessive attachment to a thing, excessive possessiveness over it, is never good. To live with such detachment, we must first engage ourselves in spiritual reflection. Giving up desires, neither soaring when pleasure comes nor shrinking when sorrow comes, we must learn to live beyond the pairs of opposites. Defeat is the stepping-stone to victory, just as sorrow is the stepping-stone to joy. We must accept hardship and happiness equally, as we accept day and night. Worldly expectation is the root of disappointment. Those who chase after it are fools who bring trouble upon themselves by their own doing. But the wise, who have grasped this secret, reach Bhagavān's realm without effort — this is certain.\n\nEven among believers in God there are many differences of opinion about God. Each person feels their own view is the correct one. For this reason Kṛṣṇa gives further explanation on this matter in the verses that follow. The ideas contained in the verses now before us may seem to us somewhat unclear; these verses will open up to us in accordance with our own effort and our own worthiness. Here Kṛṣṇa has explained, most engagingly, the concept of this trinity — jīva, jagat, and Bhagavān.\n\nFor those who are earnest about reflecting on Bhagavān, the remainder of this chapter becomes very important. Kṛṣṇa explains here the whole essence of how we ought to practise in our lives, and how we ought to reflect on Bhagavān.",
  "15.6": "Na tat bhāsayate sūryaḥ na śaśāṅkaḥ na pāvakaḥ |\n\nyat gatvā na nivartante tat dhāma paramam mama -- The sun cannot illumine That; nor can the moon; nor fire either. Those who go to That do not return again. That is My highest abode.\n\nAn explanation of exactly this kind appears in the Kaṭhopaniṣad, whose essence Kṛṣṇa states here. The Kaṭhopaniṣad says:\n\nna tatra sūryo bhāti na candratārakaṃ nemā vidyuto bhānti kuto'yamagniḥ |\n\ntameva bhāntamanubhāti sarvaṃ tasya bhāsā sarvamidaṃ vibhāti ||15||\n\n\"The sun does not illumine Bhagavān, nor does moonlight, nor the light of fire,\" says Kṛṣṇa. Here Kṛṣṇa takes as His example the very symbols through which we worship Bhagavān. We worship the Gāyatrī in the sun; we worship Bhagavān, present at the centre of the lunar orb, as Dhanvantari; on this earth, Bhagavān is worshipped through the face of fire. The warning Kṛṣṇa is giving here is this — all of these are merely symbols of light. They cannot directly show us Bhagavān. To see Bhagavān we must make use of the inner light (antaḥ-jyoti) that is our own true nature. In the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (4th chapter), this matter is laid out with great clarity in the dialogue between Janaka and Yājñavalkya:\n\n\"Yājñavalkya, kiṃ jyotirayaṃ puruṣaḥ iti | āditya jyotiḥ, samrāṭ… ||4.2||\" — Here Janaka asks Yājñavalkya: \"in this world, what is the light that helps living beings carry out their tasks?\" To this, Yājñavalkya answers directly: \"the light of the sun.\"\n\n\"astamita āditye Yājñavalkya, kiṃ jyotirevāyaṃ puruṣaḥ iti | candramevāsya jyotir bhavati… ||4.3||\" — When there is no sunlight, by what light do living beings carry out their tasks — asked this, Yājñavalkya says: \"by moonlight.\"\n\n\"astamita āditye, Yājñavalkya, candramasyastamite, kiṃ jyotirevāyaṃ puruṣa iti | agnirevāsya jyotirbhavati… ||4.4||\" — Asked what light there is when both sun and moon are absent, Yājñavalkya answers: \"by the light of fire.\"\n\n\"astamita āditye, Yājñavalkya, candramasi astamite, śānte'gnau, kiṃ jyotirevāyaṃ puruṣa iti | vāg evāsya jyotir bhavati vācaivāyaṃ jyotiṣāste palyayate karma kurute vipalyayeti tasmāddhaika, samrāṭ, api yatra pāṇirna vinirjāyate, atha yatra vāguccarati upaiva tatra nyetīti | evamevaitad Yājñavalkya ||4.5||\" — Suppose there is no sun, no moon, no fire, and no stars —\n\nno light of any kind — how do people then manage? Asked this, Yājñavalkya answers: \"by speaking to one another.\"\n\n\"astamita āditye: Yājñavalkya, candramasyastamite, śānte'gnau, śāntāyāṃ vāci, kiṃ jyotirevāyaṃ puruṣa iti | ātmaivāsya jyotirbhavati, ātmanaivāyaṃ jyotiṣāste palyayate karma kurute vipalyayet iti ||4.6||\" — When all light is gone, and no one else is present, what light then is our strength — asked this, Yājñavalkya answers: \"the light of the Self, ātma-jyoti.\"\n\nLooked at on the surface, this dialogue tells us nothing at all. But see how much thought the ancients have packed into it. Without acquaintance with the inner ātma-jyoti within us, if we go on searching for Bhagavān only in the physical light of the outer world, He will never be found. No physical light can illumine Him — He is the light of all lights. To see Him, we must make use of the ātma-jyoti that is our own true nature. Using the sun, moon, and fire as symbols of Bhagavān, we must awaken the inner light within us.\n\nThat place where Bhagavān is forever visible to the wise is called \"paramadhāma\" — that itself is mokṣa. There, neither the sun, nor the moon, nor fire is the light. It is from this paramadhāma alone that the sun, the moon, and fire receive their own light. One who recognises the ātma-jyoti and discovers the Paramātman within the ātman reaches this paramadhāma, from which there is never any return.",
  "15.7": "Mama eva aṃśaḥ jīvaloke jīvabhūtaḥ sanātanaḥ |\n\nmanaḥ ṣaṣṭhāni indriyāṇi prakṛti sthāni karṣati -- The imperishable jīva dwelling in this body is like a fragment of Me — [My distinct portion; My reflection]. He draws to himself the five senses residing in the body, together with the mind as the sixth, toward their objects.\n\nThe jīva is Bhagavān's eternal fragment. Here 'aṃśa' (fragment/portion) is of two kinds: svarūpa-aṃśa — forms such as Matsya and the other avatāras, which share Bhagavān's very nature of knowledge and bliss — and bhinna-aṃśa, the distinct fragment, which is the jīva, a reflection of Bhagavān.\n\nThe jīva draws toward itself the mind and the five senses that exist within Prakṛti. The relationship of the jīva with Bhagavān is eternal.",
  "15.8": "Śarīram yat avāpnoti yat ca api utkāmati īśvaraḥ |\n\ngṛhītvā etāni saṃyāti vāyuḥ gandhān iva āśayāt -- In truth, when the jīva enters this body and when it departs from it, it is the all-powerful Bhagavān who comes along, governing these senses — just as the wind carries along a flower's fragrance. [When Bhagavān enters and leaves this jīva's body, He holds the five senses, together with the mind as the sixth, under His own control. When He gives the jīva an experience of sense-objects, He carries these along with Him — just as the wind carries along a fragrance from a flower.]\n\nThis verse is one that explains both Bhagavān and the jīva together. Kṛṣṇa says something here that may seem a little confusing to us. Unless we understand it clearly from its several angles, everything here will seem unclear. On the surface, Kṛṣṇa says: \"every jīva is My own fragment.\" Here 'aṃśa' (fragment) has two aspects. One is svarūpa-bhūta aṃśa, and the other is bhinnāṃśa. Bhagavān is of the nature of knowledge; this jīva too is of the nature of knowledge. If Bhagavān is the great light, the jīva is a spark of that light. If Bhagavān is the bimba (the original), the jīva is the pratibimba (the reflection). This is eternal — beginningless and endless. That the jīva is Bhagavān's reflection means it is Bhagavān's bhinnāṃśa. Within this jīva, He who dwells as its very support, sustaining it as 'jīva' and giving it its breath, is Bhagavān in His bimba-form (reflected-original form) — that is His svarūpa-aṃśa. In this way, the jīva, together with the five senses that experience the outer five elements and the sixth, the mind, which conveys this experience to the inner jīva, and under the sustaining presence of Bhagavān in His bimba-form, enters the gross body taking shape in the mother's womb (Bhagavān causes it to enter). To the jīva that has thus entered, Bhagavān in His bimba-form, present in every sense-organ, gives experience through the presiding deities of the senses. By Bhagavān's grace the jīva begins to experience sense-objects.\n\nA single jīva has, in total, three bodies. One is the visible gross body; within it, the subtle body (the aniruddha-body); and within that, the liṅga-body. The subtle-bodied jīva that enters this gross body possesses all its senses. For this reason, this jīva enters the womb carrying within itself the memory of its past births. The spiritual practice of a past life remains fully preserved in the mind of this subtle jīva. This is why it is said, \"spiritual practice is never wasted — it stays with us always, even accompanying us in death.\" For death belongs only to the gross body, not to the subtle body. It is for this reason that under hypnosis it becomes possible to know of a jīva's past births. Sometimes, when we see a person and feel as though they had once been closely familiar to us — the memory of a past birth may well be one reason for this. This memory remains awake only within the inner subtle body — it does not remain awake in the gross senses. Thus the jīva, on entering the gross body, draws the senses along with it, and on leaving, draws them away with it as well. In this action the jīva is not independent — it is Īśvara who causes this to happen. Here Īśvara means Bhagavān, who is all-capable. Śaṅkarācārya, in his own commentary, says: \"īśvaraḥ īśanaśīlo nārāyaṇaḥ\" — here 'īśanaśīlaḥ' means all-capable.\n\nThere is a play on words in this verse. In 'avāpnoti', 'ava' is a prefix. The jīva's entry into a body with its fifteen enclosures is the jīva's bondage — a kind of 'adhaḥpāta' (descent, downfall). But this is a descent that precedes the jīva's ascent, because the jīva needs precisely this instrument-body in order to reach mokṣa. In the same way, the jīva's departure from the body is called 'utkāmati'. Death means Bhagavān leading the jīva out from the gross body — and this is not inauspicious. That is why the prefix 'ut' is used here. And Bhagavān's act of bringing the jīva along is called here 'saṃyāti'. 'Saṃ' means: of His own accord, by His own will, in His own independence. Everything is under Bhagavān's control — the jīva is not independent; only Bhagavān is independent. This is what this word indicates. The jīva has no freedom either to be born or to die — everything proceeds strictly according to Bhagavān's ordinance.\n\nKṛṣṇa explains, through the illustration of a flower's fragrance, how the jīva, together with its senses, comes in and goes out along with Bhagavān. Just as the fragrance of a blossoming flower comes and enters our nose, so too is the course of this jīva — it cannot be seen. That the fine particles of a flower's fragrance enter our nose is known to us only through the scent itself, not through any physical sight of it. This entire course of the jīva is exactly the same — it cannot be seen. Kṛṣṇa explains this with still greater clarity in the next three verses.",
  "15.9": "Śrotram cakṣuḥ sparśanam ca rasanam ghrāṇam eva ca |\n\nadhiṣṭhāya manaḥ ca ayam viṣayān upasevate -- Standing established in the ear, the eye, the skin, the tongue, the nose, and the mind, 'He' savours the sense-objects.\n\nBhagavān stands as the regulator within every one of our senses, present in each in its own form; He grasps the sense-objects without any taint of sorrow, and gives us our experience of them. Because this verse speaks of Bhagavān, two special words are used here — 'upa-sevate' and 'adhiṣṭhāya'. 'Upa' means above, elevated. Whereas we, plunged in pleasure and pain, enjoy sense-objects, Bhagavān stands beyond all of it and experiences everything untouched by any taint of sorrow.\n\nThe word 'adhiṣṭhāya' is used because Bhagavān is present in each sense-organ in a special form, near at hand, and regulates it. 'Eva' and 'ca' are both used together in this verse. This reflects Bhagavān's special quality: a+ū+va+ca = eva-ca (a set of abbreviations) — meaning Bhagavān, free of every flaw, replete with every excellence, of the very nature of knowledge and bliss.",
  "15.10": "Ut krāmantam sthitam vā api bhuñjānam vā guṇa anvitam |\n\nvimūḍhāḥ na anupaśyanti paśyanti jñāna cakṣuṣaḥ -- Fools cannot know Him, whether He is departing from the body, dwelling within it, or savouring the essence of what is good. Those whose eyes of knowledge have opened can know Him.\n\nThe word 'guhānvitam' used in this verse tells us clearly that this verse speaks of Bhagavān. Bhagavān, who brings the jīva into the body and who, across the three states of waking, dream, and deep sleep, gives the jīva its experience of sense-objects, is Himself the One who ever partakes only of auspicious enjoyment. Whether going out of the body, or remaining within it — it is that Bhagavān who causes everything to happen. Though He dwells within us, the utterly deluded cannot know this. Only those whose eye of knowledge has opened can know it. Kṛṣṇa uses two special words here — 'vimūḍha' and 'jñānacakṣuṣaḥ'. 'Mūḍhāḥ' means those who know the truth wrongly — they live entirely in distorted knowledge, true understanding having slipped from them. 'Vi-mūḍha' means those who never recognise their own error, forever taking their error to be correct, forever living in distorted knowledge. Such people can never, under any circumstance, come to know Bhagavān. Those endowed with the capacity to see Bhagavān are the 'jñānacakṣuṣaḥ' — that is, those who have opened the inner eye of understanding through the study of scripture. Such people are able to know Bhagavān, and are able to see Bhagavān with their own true, essential eye.",
  "15.11": "Yatantaḥ yoginaḥ ca enam paśyanti ātmani avasthitam |\n\nyatantaḥ api akṛta ātmānaḥ na enam paśyanti acetasaḥ -- Earnest sādhakas who strive --\n\ncome to see 'this One' dwelling within themselves. The unwise, lacking understanding, cannot see Him however hard they strive.\n\nTo see Bhagavān, mere study of scripture is not enough. One must gain knowledge and, through that knowledge, sustain unceasing practice. When one makes ceaseless effort to see Bhagavān through dhyāna-yoga, ātma-sākṣātkāra (realisation of the Self) comes about [we have already discussed dhyāna at some length in the sixth chapter]. Along with our own effort, His grace too is needed to see Bhagavān. Without that, no amount of great striving can bring one to see Bhagavān. For example — we may plough a field well, prepare it thoroughly, manure it, and sow the finest seed in it — but what if the rain does not come as it should? What if too much rain comes and washes everything away? All of this is divine grace. This is why, along with ceaseless effort, surrender to Bhagavān is of the highest importance. One must pray to Bhagavān: \"purify my mind, and come and dwell within this purified mind.\" A pure, sāttvika mind is called 'Vasudeva'; the Bhagavān who becomes visible to that Vasudeva is 'Vāsudeva'. However much scripture one has read, if one's mind remains impure (akṛtātmā — of impure understanding), no matter how much effort one makes, Bhagavān will not be won over. This is precisely what is meant by 'maḍi' (ritual purity) — we must strive while being pure in mind. \"See, through unceasing, knowledge-grounded effort, the Bhagavān who is nearest of all to you, dwelling within your own inner being, within your own true nature as jīva\" — this is what Kṛṣṇa says. In the previous verse Kṛṣṇa said 'ayam asau' (this One standing before you); here He says 'enam' (this One who is nearest of all to you). We need not go searching for Bhagavān anywhere else — He is established within our very own jīva-nature, in His bimba-form. When we come to know our own jīva-nature, which is His seat, He becomes visible to us.",
  "15.12": "Yat ādityagatam tejaḥ jagat bhāsayate akhilam |\n\nyat candramasi yat ca agnau tat tejaḥ viddhi māmakam -- The light dwelling in the sun that illumines the whole universe, and that in the moon, and that in fire as well — know that to be My own light.\n\nEarlier Kṛṣṇa said that neither the sun, nor the moon, nor fire can illumine Him; here He says that the shining of the sun, the shining of the moon, the shining of fire — all of it comes from Him. Bhagavān is the source-light of every light. The sources of light are the sun, moon, stars, lightning, electricity, and fire —\n\nit is Bhagavān who gives light to all these masses of light. That is to say — from the sun onward, whether it is the stars of the moon, or the lightning flashing in the sky, or the burning fire, every one of these masses of light shines by the source-light called Bhagavān. Whatever light stands within our own nature, subtler than the subtlest, guiding us — that very same light, vaster than the vastest, illumines the whole universe. In sum, the source-radiance of every light is Bhagavān. The authors of the Upaniṣads, describing this great light, say: \"…yadetad vidyuto vyadyutadā iti\" (Kena 4.4). There they call this light \"ā-iti\" — meaning, this is a wonder (vismaya). Astonishment until it is known; bliss once it is known.\n\nLooking at this verse from the standpoint of upāsanā: the radiance in the sun is Nārāyaṇa's radiance; the radiance in the moon is Dhanvantari's radiance; the radiance in fire is Paraśurāma's radiance. In this way, we may worship, through this very verse, Bhagavān as the source-light of every single light.",
  "15.13": "Gām āviśya ca bhūtāni dhārayāmi aham ojasā |\n\npuṣṇāmi ca oṣadhīḥ sarvāḥ somaḥ bhūtvā rasātmakaḥ -- Entering the earth, I Myself bear all beings by My own strength. Becoming the moon, of the nature of nectar-essence, [dwelling within the moon under the name 'Soma', on account of My gentle form] I nourish all the plants.\n\nHere 'gām' means 'pṛthivī' (the earth). It is Bhagavān who, in the form of the power of saṃkarṣaṇa (the power of Śeṣa — the power of attraction — Gravitational force), bears the earth so that it stands unsupported in the sky. This describes Bhagavān's Varāha avatāra. The story is that Bhagavān, in His Varāha form, lifted up the earth as it was sinking down and about to be destroyed. Here, the earth 'sinking down' means it slipping from its own orbit. If the earth were to leave its orbit, it would be destroyed within a fraction of a second. It was the demon Hiraṇyākṣa of the tāmasa realm who dislodged the earth from its orbit. We may recall here Newton's own saying: \"give me a place to stand and a lever, and I can move the earth out of its orbit.\" In just this way, when the demon of the tāmasa realm dislodged the earth from its orbit, Bhagavān set it back in its orbit and protected it. To the sages of the Vedic age, 'Bhagavān bearing up the earth' appeared in the form of Varāha. For this reason we see the form of Bhagavān, the bearer of the earth,\n\nin the Varāha form — the foremost among boons. In the Gāyatrī, in the word 'vareṇyam', there is contained the very thought of Bhagavān's Varāha form, the bearer of the earth. The Russian scientist Velikovsky says in his book \"Worlds in Collision\": \"It is scientifically true that the earth has slipped from its orbit twice, but we do not know why.\" There, citing the Bhāgavata, he says: \"the sages of India knew of this matter — that one evil power dislodged the earth from its orbit, and another, divine power set it back in its orbit.\" This is exactly what Kṛṣṇa says in this verse as \"gām dhārayāmi.\" Bhagavān, the bearer of the earth, dwells within the earth, and dwells within every single class of living being, protecting us. As the Upaniṣad says: \"ya pṛthivī antaro yamayati eṣa ta ātmā antaryāmī amṛtaḥ\" — meaning, \"though Bhagavān, dwelling within the earth, is protecting us, we do not know it.\" This power of Bhagavān that thus bears and protects the earth is called here 'ojas' — that is, the power that overcomes every obstacle.\n\nAmong us, when performing sandhyāvandanam, there is a step called the 'bathing of the mind'. We mentally experience and bathe in the stream of nectar flowing down from the pot of nectar in the hand of Dhanvantari, who sits within the lunar orb in our own sahasrāra. Reciting 'Oṃ-vaṃ' twenty-four times, we let that nectar-fluid of the lunar orb flow and purify us from within. When this is done with the mind fixed one-pointedly on Bhagavān, mentally experiencing it, the mind becomes pure. The greatest filth within us is the pāpapuruṣa dwelling in our own mind — bad feeling, bad thought, envy, all of this filling our mind. For this to leave, one must bathe in this nectar-fluid that flows from the pot of nectar of the lunar orb. This is exactly what Kṛṣṇa means here by \"somo bhūtvā rasātmakaḥ.\" Bhagavān fills the moon in the form of Soma. He, the regulator of water, having become Soma within the moon, fills the plant-life with life-sap. Whatever it is that we eat and live by — He who protects and nurtures that plant is Bhagavān in the form of Soma, filling the moon.",
  "15.14": "Aham vaiśvānaraḥ bhūtvā prāṇinām deham aśritaḥ |\n\nprāṇa apāna samāyuktaḥ pacāmi annam caturvidham -- Having become 'Vaiśvānara', dwelling within the bodies of living beings, joined with prāṇa and apāna, I digest [what is chewed, sucked, licked, and drunk] the four kinds of food.\n\nHere 'pacāmyannaṃ caturvidham' means \"I digest the four kinds of food.\" 'Anna' means every kind of food. \"Prāṇinām dehamāśritaḥ\" means the food that living beings ('prāṇins') are able to eat. The food of living beings may be divided into four kinds — bhakṣya, bhojya, lehya, and coṣya. Bhakṣya is that fit to be chewed and bitten and eaten (eating); bhojya is that fit to be eaten without chewing, simply swallowed (dining); lehya is that fit to be licked and eaten; coṣya is that fit to be drunk. Bhagavān, in His form as Vaiśvānara, dwells in the fire of digestion in the stomach of every living being that partakes of these four kinds of food, dividing the four kinds of food into three grades (subtle, middling, gross), feeding the body, and thereby arranging for our very survival.\n\nThe food we eat is a mixture of earth, water, and fire. Of the earth in this food, the subtle portion goes to the brain, the middling portion to flesh and skin, and the gross portion is expelled as excrement. In the same way, of the water, the subtle portion becomes breath, the middling portion becomes blood, and the gross portion is used up as urine. And of the fire, the subtle portion becomes the power of speech, the middling portion becomes bone-marrow, and the gross portion becomes teeth and bone. It is Bhagavān who, dwelling within us, performs this task — harder than separating gold from a gold mine — in a perfectly ordered way, and who thereby gives us life. The servants for this task of Bhagavān's are the two forms of Prāṇadevaru known as prāṇa and apāna. As the Kaṭhopaniṣad says:\n\nūrdhvaṃ prāṇamunnayati apānaṃ pratyagasyati.\n\nmadhye vāmanamāsīnaṃ viśve devā upāsate ||2.2.3||\n\nHere, the two forms of Prāṇadevaru — prāṇa and apāna — and, in the middle (in the heart), Bhagavān's Vāmana-form. This is a form of upāsanā distinct from the worship of Bhagavān in His fivefold form. Dwelling at the centre of the heart, digesting the food we have eaten and thereby giving us life, is Bhagavān in His Vāmana-form. In this manvantara, Vāmana is 'Upendra', the protector of Devendra. Hence the worship of Bhagavān in His Vāmana-form, dwelling in our own hearts, is of great importance in this manvantara. This very Bhagavān, who is in Vāmana-form within us, is the same One who, as Trivikrama, fills the earth and the sky.",
  "15.15": "Sarvasya ca aham hṛdi sanniviṣṭaḥ mattaḥ smṛtiḥ jñānam apohanam ca |\n\nvedaiḥ ca sarvaiḥ aham eva vedyaḥ vedāntakṛt vedavit eva ca aham -- I am established in the hearts of all. Memory, understanding, and forgetting all come from Me. I alone am that which is to be known through all the Vedas. I am the one who composed the Vedānta-sūtras, and I alone am the one who knows the innermost meaning of the Vedas.\n\n\"Sarvasya ca ahaṃ hṛdi\" — Bhagavān, denoted by the word 'aham' (I), fills us from within. Bhagavān's form outside us is called 'ahaḥ'; His form within us is called 'ahaṃ'. Bhagavān within us is ever with us, protecting us. Even as we depart the gross body in death, Bhagavān remains with us — He does not leave us for even a single instant. His seat is the true Self within our own liṅga-śarīra; this very Self is 'ahaṃ'. If we hold this awareness of Bhagavān, the ahaṅkāra of \"I did this\" will never trouble us.\n\nAs the Kaṭhopaniṣad says: \"aṅguṣṭhamātraḥ puruṣo'ntarātmā sadā janānāṃ hṛdaye sanniviṣṭaḥ…\" (2.3.7). Bhagavān, of the size of a thumb, dwells within every jīva, ever detached, ever free of the taint of sorrow. Madhvācārya, writing his commentary on the Kaṭhopaniṣad, says:\n\nnamo bhagavate tasmai sarvataḥ paramāya te |\n\nsarvaprāṇihṛdisthāya vāmanāya namo namaḥ || Sarvamūla ||\n\nHere we find a clue that the form of Bhagavān who is the antaryāmī of every heart is the 'Vāmana' form. Hence in upāsanā we ought to worship the Bhagavān within us in His Vāmana-form.\n\n\"Mattaḥ smṛtir jñānam apohanaṃ\" — it is Bhagavān who gives us the power of memory. Knowledge too comes from Bhagavān, and the retention of that knowledge in memory also comes from Bhagavān. As the Kenopaniṣad says: \"athādhyātmaṃ yaddhaitadgacchatīva ca mano'nena caitadupasmaratyabhīkṣṇaṃ saṅkalpaḥ\" (4.5). This is the upāsanā of Bhagavān's Aniruddha-form, which gives us the power of memory. Just as He gives the power of memory, He is also the one who makes us forget! Sometimes even those with direct spiritual realisation (aparokṣa-jñānins) forget Bhagavān — because Bhagavān makes them forget. The illustration for this is the story of Gargācārya found in the Bhāgavata. Gargācārya, who had named Bhagavān 'Kṛṣṇa' and was Himself a devotee of Kṛṣṇa, in some evil circumstance forgot everything and performed austerities praying: \"let a son be born to me who will kill Kṛṣṇa\"! What an irony. But all of this too is Bhagavān's līlā. Behind His līlā lies not hatred, but only compassion.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"I am the one who is to be known through all the Vedas.\" The Brahma-sūtra states clearly that every single word of the Veda ultimately refers to Bhagavān. Some say Vedānta means Upaniṣad. But Vedānta actually means \"the final verdict of the Veda\" — that is the Brahma-sūtra itself, the text that reconciles and explains the Veda as a coherent whole. The Veda is beginningless and eternal; the one who fully knows the innermost meaning of the Veda is Bhagavān alone.\n\nIn \"vedaiśca sarvairahameva vedyo,\" both the particle 'ca' and the particle 'eva' are used. Because of 'ca', this line carries two messages — one primary, one secondary.\n\nThe primary meaning is: \"Bhagavān alone is to be known through the Vedas.\" The secondary meaning is that Bhagavān is also to be known through the texts regarded as the fifth Veda — the Purāṇas, the Vedānta, the Mahābhārata, the Rāmāyaṇa, and so on. There are also the Smṛtis, but these cannot be accepted as an authoritative proof (pramāṇa) in the same way, because Smṛtis were composed by scholars at the bidding of the kings of their respective times, in keeping with the needs of those times — they are like a constitution, carrying the social dharma of that particular age along with them, and there is no reason they must be eternally true for all time. The one eternal, sanātana truth valid for all time is the Veda alone.\n\nIn \"ahameva vedaḥ,\" the primary meaning is: \"I alone am the one who is to be known through the Vedas.\" Secondarily, this also states matters that support Bhagavān's supremacy. By saying here \"I alone am the one to be known through the Vedas,\" Kṛṣṇa firmly rejects polytheism (bahudevatā-vāda). The Veda declares emphatically that Bhagavān is one alone — no other proof is needed for this.\n\nIn \"vedāntakṛd vedavideva cāham,\" Kṛṣṇa says: \"the complete meaning of the Veda is known to Me alone.\" In the Uddhava-gītā found in the Bhāgavata, Kṛṣṇa says:\n\nkiṃ vidhatte kimācaṣṭe kimanūdya vikalpayet |\n\nityasyā hṛdayaṃ loke nānyo madveda kaścana || 11.21.42 ||\n\nmāṃ vidhatte'bhidhatte māṃ vikalpyāpohyate tvahaṃ |\n\netāvānsarvavedārthaḥ śabda āsthāya māmbhidāṃ |\n\nmāyāmātramanūdyānte pratiṣidhya prasīdati || 11.21.43 ||\n\nKṛṣṇa explains here, quite clearly, that every single word of the Veda speaks of Bhagavān. But when we look at the Veda on the surface, it seems that the Veda is 'karma-bodhaka' (concerned with prescribing action), as though every word were merely a 'karma-vācaka' (an action-word). But to understand it this way is a mistake. Through the performance of ritual action, the mind becomes purified; and a purified mind takes to knowledge very quickly. Through this, one becomes able to know Bhagavān. So every single action prescribed in the Veda ultimately speaks of nothing but Bhagavān. In one place the Veda says \"perform this action,\" and in another place it says \"do not perform this other action.\" For instance, the Veda says: \"drink soma — do not drink surā (liquor).\" The meaning of this is: soma-drinking prepares the mind for spiritual reflection, through which Bhagavān can be known; drinking surā, on the other hand, corrupts the intellect and takes us further away from Bhagavān. In this way, every single action spoken of in the Veda ultimately speaks only of Bhagavān. In some places the Veda speaks of creation and dissolution; it speaks of the order of creation. These are lines that speak of Bhagavān Himself, saying \"Bhagavān performs the act of creation in this manner.\"\n\nVedic sūktas name a different deity in different places — in one place Agni, in another Indra, in yet another Sarasvatī, and so on. On the surface this may appear to us to be polytheism. But Kṛṣṇa has said \"abhidhatte mām\" — \"it speaks of Me.\" Take for example \"ātmanaḥ ākāśaḥ sambhūtaḥ\" — from the tattva of ahaṅkāra was born the deity of space, Gaṇapati. The creation of space means the creation, within the existing intermediate expanse, of one enclosing sheath (an atmosphere). Gaṇapati was born as the presiding deity of that. Bhagavān, filling space, incarnated within Gaṇapati under the name of 'Ākāśa'. In this way every single Vedic verse, in the end, speaks of nothing but Bhagavān.\n\nWe may see, through this example, how every single word occurring in the Veda speaks of Bhagavān. Example: 'agni'. A-ga-ni = agni. He who gives motion (ni) to that which is without motion (aga) — aga-ni. Since Bhagavān gives motion to the entire universe, He is Agni.\n\nEvery single letter in the Veda is a name of Bhagavān. For example, 'a'. A = Aja (the unborn); A = Ananta (the undying). Likewise 'ā'. Ā = Ādi (He who exists before all things); Ā = Ānanda; and so on. In the Varāha Purāṇa, Veda Vyāsa says: \"yāvanti paṭhitāni tāvanti harināmāni paṭhitāni na saṃśayaḥ\" — as many syllables as are recited, that many names of Hari are recited, without a doubt. The Ṛgveda contains 4 lakh 32 thousand syllables. Hence, according to the ancients, reading the Ṛgveda even once amounts to reciting 4 lakh 32 thousand names of Bhagavān. In sum: Bhagavān is to be seen in the letter of the Veda; He is to be seen in the word; He is to be seen in the sentence; and He is to be seen in the topic as a whole. It is only when we take the Veda apart in this way that the message contained within it becomes known to us. Otherwise, everything appears merely worldly. But we can never come to know Him fully (nānyo madveda kaścana) — He alone, that Bhagavān, knows Himself completely.\n\nThe next three verses are the essence of the whole method of thought in Indian philosophy. These are the verses that explain the inquiry into the world and the Self of the world.",
  "15.16": "Dvāvimau puruṣau loke kṣaraḥ ca akṣaraḥ eva ca |\n\nkṣaraḥ sarvāṇi bhūtāni kūṭasthaḥ akṣaraḥ ucyate -- In this world there are two kinds of puruṣa: kṣara-puruṣa and akṣara-puruṣa. All beings, from Brahmā downward, are kṣara-puruṣas. That conscious Prakṛti who binds together this entire aggregate of moving and unmoving things is called the akṣara-puruṣa.\n\nIn this world of sentient beings there are two kinds of puruṣa. One is the kṣara-puruṣa, the other the akṣara-puruṣa. [Here 'puruṣa' does not mean 'male' — we have already discussed this point specifically many times before.] Those who possess a body destined one day to fall are kṣara-puruṣas. All jīvas, Brahmā and the rest, are kṣara-puruṣas. Even all the gods, from Brahmā downward, cannot go to mokṣa together with their bodies. Caturmukha Brahmā's lifespan is one kalpa (31,104 thousand crore human years). After that, his body too turns to ash. The Īśopaniṣad explains this thus: \"vāyuranilam amṛtam athedaṃ bhasmāntaṃ śarīraṃ\" (17) — \"Prāṇadevaru, who stands as Bhagavān's support, is amṛta (deathless); yet even so, one day his body too must turn to ash.\" Thus all the gods, all classes of living beings, are kṣara-puruṣas.\n\nŚrī Lakṣmī, whose body is imperishable, of the very nature of knowledge and bliss, is the akṣara-puruṣa. All kṣara-puruṣas become akṣara upon reaching mokṣa. But Lakṣmī is eternally akṣara. Here Lakṣmī is described as 'kūṭasthā'. 'Kūṭa' means 'joining together'. She, the mother Śrī Lakṣmī, is the cause of the joining of jīva and body. Ever detached, without any taint whatsoever, the power that has ordered the universe — the mother of the universe, Śrī Lakṣmī — is the akṣara-puruṣa.",
  "15.17": "Uttamaḥ puruṣaḥ tu anyaḥ parama ātmā iti udāhṛtaḥ |\n\nyaḥ lokatrayam āviśya bibharti avyayaḥ īśvaraḥ -- He who is greater than both of these is Puruṣottama. He is called the Paramātman. That imperishable Parameśvara alone dwells within the three worlds and sustains them.\n\nThe Supreme Puruṣa, standing beyond both kṣara and akṣara, is that Bhagavān. He alone is called 'Paramātman'. He who, filling the three worlds — bhūḥ, bhuvaḥ, svaḥ, or the entire cosmos, which is also spoken of as divided into seven or fourteen worlds — sustains and protects them, is that all-powerful (Īśvara) Bhagavān.",
  "15.18": "Yasmāt kṣaram atītaḥ aham akṣarāt api ca uttamaḥ |\n\nataḥ asmi loke vede ca prathitaḥ puruṣa uttamaḥ -- I stand beyond the kṣara, and I am greater even than the akṣara. For this very reason I am renowned, both in the world and in the Veda, as 'Puruṣottama'.\n\nIn the previous verse Kṛṣṇa said \"he who stands beyond both kṣara and akṣara is Puruṣottama\"; here He states directly and clearly: \"that distinct Puruṣottama is Myself.\" Madhvācārya, in the maṅgalācaraṇa of his Viṣṇutattvanirṇaya, says: \"sadāgamaika vijñeyaṃ, samatīta kṣarākṣaraṃ, nārāyaṇaṃ sadā vande, nirdoṣa aśeṣa sadguṇaṃ\" — here he calls Bhagavān, who stands beyond kṣara and akṣara, \"samatīta kṣarākṣaraṃ.\"\n\nIn this verse it is said Bhagavān is \"atīta\" (beyond) kṣara, and \"uttama\" (greater) than akṣara. Here 'atīta' effectively means 'uttama' as well. Even so, both words — atīta and uttama — are used. This carries a special meaning. The scope of the kṣara-puruṣa's existence lies within this cosmos. But Lakṣmī-Nārāyaṇa stand beyond this cosmos altogether — this is what the word 'atīta' explains. And Bhagavān is greater even than the akṣara Lakṣmī. In this verse, 'api' and the particle 'ca' are used together. This declares Bhagavān's supremacy above all — there is no principle greater than Him.\n\nThe full meaning of the word 'Puruṣottama' applies to Bhagavān alone. Kṛṣṇa says: \"it is I who am called 'Puruṣottama' both in the world and in the Veda.\" Here 'loka' means the Purāṇic texts composed by human authors (paurūṣeya) — for example the Rāmāyaṇa, Mahābhārata, Pañcarātra, the Purāṇas, and so on. Thus the term 'Puruṣottama', in its fullest and most essential sense, applies only to Bhagavān.",
  "15.19": "Yaḥ mām evam asammūḍhaḥ jānāti puruṣottamam |\n\nsaḥ sarvavit bhajati mām sarvabhāvena bhārata -- One who, free of delusion, thus knows Me as Puruṣottama, knows everything. O Bhārata, he worships Me in every way.\n\nFor those who ask, \"what benefit do we gain from knowing what has been said here?\" Kṛṣṇa's answer is in this verse. Kṛṣṇa says: \"one who knows Me as Puruṣottama without ahaṅkāra, with a purified mind, having conquered anger, delusion gone — knows everything.\" For\n\nBhagavān's existence to become intelligible to us, we must first understand that a consciousness distinct from inert matter, called 'I', exists within this body (by awareness of self). Once this ātma-sākṣātkāra has occurred, Bhagavān's bimba-sākṣātkāra follows. Whoever comes to know Bhagavān is able to know everything. Hence we must live as jñānins, distinct from mere animals. Living in this way, our unwavering devotion toward God becomes firm. One who is truly wise, even upon losing everything he has, accepts it saying, \"that is Bhagavān's grace.\" This firm devotion is not found in the ignorant. For such firm devotion to arise, we must become 'Bhārata' — an unceasing thirst for knowledge, devotion among the wise, and devotion toward Prāṇadevaru, the foremost among Bhagavān's devotees, can make us truly 'Bhārata'.",
  "15.20": "Iti guhyatamam śāstram idam uktam mayā anagha |\n\netat buddhvā buddhimān syāt kṛtakṛtyaśca bhārata -- O Anagha, I have laid open before you a teaching that ought to be kept most secret. One who understands this comes to know Bhagavān; there remains nothing more for him to accomplish.\n\nThis is the most supreme of all the secrets that ought to be told to those inquiring into spiritual matters. Those with an interest in spiritual life must be told this in keeping with the depth of their own interest. The subject-matter of the scriptures should never be laid open in the open street, because it must neither be wasted nor turned to harm. It should be told only to those who have the capacity to grasp it, who have interest, faith, and devotion. This is the most closely-guarded of all secrets. One who knows the knowledge of the kṣara-puruṣa, the knowledge of the akṣara-puruṣa, and the knowledge of Puruṣottama is one who truly knows the śāstra. Śāstra means that which the knowing must teach to those who do not know.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"this is a matter fit for instruction, to be most secretly and most carefully guarded — there is no teaching greater than this. I have told you this most secret matter because you are 'Anagha' — for that very reason.\" 'Anagha' means one without any flaw whatsoever. We must first become free of the faults and failings that obstruct knowledge. If our mind is stained by evil thoughts, then the Gītā cannot become intelligible to us. When, without any prejudice or agitation, one surrenders completely to Bhagavān, the mind can become free of sin.\n\nKṛṣṇa says: \"this is the very root of all understanding. One who knows this is kṛtakṛtya\" — one whose task is accomplished. He who knows Bhagavān in his life\n\nis able to live a truly worthwhile life. To live with the awareness of Bhagavān is itself the fulfilment of life.\n\n|| Thus ends the fifteenth chapter ||",
  "16.1": "Ahiṃsā satyam akrodhaḥ tyāgaḥ śāntiḥ apaiśunam |\n\ndayā bhūteṣu alolutvaṃ mārdavaṃ hrīḥ acāpalam ||1||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and commentary for these three verses (16.1, 16.2, and 16.3) are given together at the next verse (16.3).]",
  "16.2": "Tejaḥ kṣamā dhṛtiḥ śaucam adrohaḥ nātimānitā |\n\nbhavanti sampadaṃ daivīm abhijātasya bhārata ||2||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and commentary for these three verses (16.1, 16.2, and 16.3) are given together at the next verse (16.3).]",
  "16.3": "Śrī Bhagavān uvāca - The Lord said:\n\nAbhayam sattvasaṃśuddhiḥ jñānayoga vyavasthitiḥ |\n\ndānam damaḥ ca yajñaśca svādhyāyaḥ tapaḥ ārjavam |\n\nahiṃsā satyam akrodhaḥ tyāgaḥ śāntiḥ apaiśunam |\n\ndayā bhūteṣu alolutvaṃ mārdavaṃ śrīḥ acāpalam ||\n\ntejaḥ kṣamā dhṛtiḥ śaucam adrohaḥ na atimānitā |\n\nbhavanti sampadaṃ daivīm abhijātasya bhārata -- O Bhārata, one born with a share of divine wealth [good conduct] is endowed with these qualities: fearlessness, purity of mind, steadfastness on the path of knowledge, generosity, mastery over the senses, worship of the gods, study of the Vedas, austerity, uprightness, non-injury, truthfulness, freedom from anger, renunciation, tranquillity, freedom from slander, compassion for living beings, freedom from craving, gentleness, modesty, steadiness, forbearance that pardons wrong, vigour, external and internal purity, absence of malice, and freedom from arrogance.\n\nKṛṣṇa here describes the twenty-six virtues that ought to be present in spiritual practice. We must trace out, one by one, which of these qualities we already have and strengthen them, and cultivate whichever we lack.\n\n(1) Abhayam (fearlessness): This is the very first quality demanded of one walking the spiritual path. Spirituality is not meant for cowards. As the well-known verse about Hanumān says:\n\nbuddhirbalaṃ yaśo dhairyaṃ nirbhayatvaṃ arogatā |\n\najāḍyaṃ vāk paṭutvaṃ ca hanumat smaraṇāt bhavet ||\n\nFor fearlessness to arise in us, we must worship Prāṇadevaru, who is even beyond death. Reading Vedānta without courage never brings firm conviction. When we are engaged in upāsanā, someone or other will come along and say something discouraging — and then our mind fails to stand firm in God. Whatever anyone says, only when we hold the firm conviction \"I will not abandon the path I have taken; God will protect me\" can we ever find success.\n\nIn these times there are far too many people who frighten others by saying \"do this and disaster follows, do that and disaster follows.\" Such people have no real acquaintance with scripture at all — they carry the spirit of Hiraṇyakaśipu. For example, some frighten women by saying \"women must not recite the Viṣṇu-sahasranāma.\" But the great irony is that the very name of God exists for our fearlessness. Turning away from it out of an unfounded fear is a loss entirely our own. God is never fearsome. He becomes fearsome only to those possessed by the spirit of Hiraṇyakaśipu, never to those possessed by the spirit of Prahlāda. We must become like Prahlāda — unafraid of the Hiraṇyakaśipus who try to frighten us — and praise the Bhagavān who grants fearlessness.\n\nThe spiritual practitioner is never a coward, because one thing is settled for him beyond doubt: \"there is no sense in my being afraid, because until Bhagavān protects me nothing in this entire world can touch me, and once Bhagavān lets go of me, no one else can protect me.\" This firm conviction must grow within us.\n\n\"rakṣatītyeva viśvāsaḥ\" — Bhagavān never abandons His devotees. Hence 'abhaya' is the very first qualification we must possess. The more firmly this trust grows in us, the more protection the devotee receives from Bhagavān.\n\n(2) Sattvasaṃśuddhi (purity of mind): The purity of our own mind is of the highest importance in spiritual practice. Ordinarily our mind is impure, whether from hereditary causes or from the influence of our surroundings. We should note here what an ācārya said as far back as the thirteenth century: \"daurlabhyāt śuddha buddhīnāṃ\" — those with a pure mind are exceedingly rare in society.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa does not simply say sattva-śuddhi, but says \"sattva-saṃ-śuddhi.\" Our mind is sometimes pure — in sleep, everyone's mind is pure. But what Kṛṣṇa speaks of here is a mind that remains pure even in the waking state, at all times. The Bhāgavata puts this as \"sattvaṃ viśuddhaṃ vasudevaśabditaṃ\" (4.30.23) — meaning, \"to a mind that is ever pure, Vāsudeva becomes visible.\" Hence for spiritual practice, a mind free of every impurity, free of every preconception, is of the greatest importance.\n\n(3) Jñānayoga-vyavasthitiḥ (fixing the mind steadily in the yoga of knowledge): Upāsanā-yoga is a supremely important yoga, with three faces — bhakti, jñāna, and karma-yoga — none of which can exist apart from the others. Jñāna-yoga means knowing Bhagavān's greatness, performing action as an act of worship of Him, and thereby cultivating devotion toward Him. 'Vyavasthitiḥ' means an unshaken state — fixing the mind unwaveringly on Bhagavān and steadily anchoring it in continuous reflection upon Him. Through this the mind becomes pure.\n\n(4) Dānam (giving to others what is one's own): When we possess something another lacks, and they are in real need of it — even if we ourselves might need it later — giving it without a second thought, in a spirit of offering, is dāna. Giving away things one no longer needs at home, or keeping the better portion for oneself while giving away the lesser, is not dāna. When giving, there should be no attachment whatsoever in the mind toward the thing given; there should be no pride of \"I am giving\" or \"I have given.\" One must give in a spirit of complete offering. What is given may be knowledge, grain, or fearlessness (giving courage to another) — whatever the act may be, what matters more than the act itself is the feeling behind it (this is explained further at chapter 10, verse 5).\n\n(5) Dama and (6) Yajña: Kṛṣṇa states these two together using the particle 'ca' (damaśca, yajñaśca), to show that, alongside all the other conduct listed here, these two are especially important. 'Dama' means abstaining from doing wrong — restraining our senses from wrongdoing. Yajña means offering every action we perform to Bhagavān. We must maintain these two forms of conduct constantly in our life; without them, no other virtue can be attained.\n\n(7) Svādhyāyaḥ: Svādhyāya is of three kinds. There is the comprehensive study of the Vedas concerning the independent Bhagavān — this is svādhyāya proper; if that is not possible, the study of the Veda-branch (svaṃ) belonging to one's own lineage is also called svādhyāya. The third kind is reflecting on the spiritual matters we have heard from our teachers, expounding them for ourselves, and, once that matter has become clear to our own self, sharing it with others. But this third kind of svādhyāya is vanishing today. Patañjali says: \"vedaṃ adhitya tvarita vaktāro bhavanti\" — today's student is tomorrow's teacher (vaktā), with no interval of svādhyāya in between. \"Tvarita adhītya\" — everything in a hurry!\n\n(8) Tapaḥ: The tapas Kṛṣṇa speaks of here is not the same as what has already been discussed under the name tapas earlier. Controlling the mind so that it does not go astray is 'dama'; the continuous study of scripture that follows from that is 'svādhyāya'. Between these two there are two things that trouble us: the craving of the tongue and sexual attraction. These are the root enemies of study. Controlling these is tapas. This is why, in the old days, people would leave behind some favourite food of theirs when visiting a place of pilgrimage — the underlying purpose being to shed one craving or another under some pretext.\n\n(9) Ārjavam (honesty): Straightforwardness in conduct and speech — saying one thing and doing another is not honesty; living with integrity is.\n\n(10) Ahiṃsā: We have already analysed this to some extent in chapter 10, verse 5. Causing pain to another out of hatred, intolerance, or envy is hiṃsā. Killing another life out of unavoidable necessity for food is not hiṃsā (for example, eating plants, or a kṣatriya's eating of meat); unknowingly causing the death of tiny creatures while walking or breathing is not hiṃsā; a soldier defending his country, killing an enemy soldier in fulfilment of his duty, does not commit hiṃsā; a war fought out of unavoidable necessity to prevent a still greater violence is not hiṃsā (for example, the Mahābhārata war); killing one person in order to save thousands is not hiṃsā (for example, Kṛṣṇa's arranging for the death of Droṇācārya, who had vowed to kill ten thousand soldiers a day and fought day and night toward that end); an act a physician performs to save a patient's life is not hiṃsā. In sum, any unavoidable act carried out without hatred, intolerance, envy, or self-interest is not counted as hiṃsā.\n\nLiving so as not to trouble another is ahiṃsā. If we wound another's mind, there is no medicine anywhere that can heal it again. So never humiliate another. For example: mocking someone with a barbed remark, mentally reviling another, or cursing someone. We must conduct ourselves such that no violence comes from us — through speech, through action, or through the mind — to another.\n\n(11) Satyam: This is another face of honesty. Satya here does not mean simply saying things exactly as they are. Speech that benefits the good and virtuous people in society is satya. Sometimes even a falsehood spoken for the world's benefit becomes satya; and even a truth that causes injustice to the world becomes falsehood. Kṛṣṇa demonstrated this message in a revolutionary way in the Mahābhārata itself. This matter is specifically addressed in the Mahābhārata, where it says:\n\nna narma yuktaṃ vacanaṃ hinasti na strīṣu rājan na vivāha kāle\n\nprāṇātyayai sarvadhanāpahāre pañcānṛtānyāhur apātakāni ||1.77.16||\n\nScripture condemns using falsehood out of jealousy to break off another's marriage or to spoil a betrothal already arranged. A falsehood spoken to save one's life when life is in danger is not a falsehood at all.\n\n(12) Akrodhaḥ: Never becoming angry at another, under any circumstance. Anger may well arise when an unwelcome event occurs — but never hand yourself over to the grip of anger. If, when anger comes, one observes silence until it subsides, the harm that anger would otherwise cause is avoided. Kṛṣṇa warns us repeatedly throughout the Gītā about kāma and krodha — these are fatal to a peaceful life. The life of Śrī Rāmacandra is the finest example of how we ought to learn tranquillity. In the Rāmāyaṇa, Vālmīki says: \"uccyamāno'pi paruṣam nottaraṃ pratipadyate\" (Ayodhyā 1.15). Even when someone stood ready to quarrel with him, Rāma, it is said, never lost his temper.\n\n(13) Tyāgaḥ: On the surface, dāna and tyāga may appear the same. But tyāga means living with the conviction that even what one possesses is not \"mine\" but Bhagavān's. Tyāga takes many forms — renunciation of pride of ownership, renunciation of the fruit of action, renunciation of ahaṅkāra and mamakāra, and so on. Let there be love, but stand apart from the feeling of \"mine.\" Receive gladly when something comes; remain unattached when it departs. Nothing on this journey of life is permanent. Knowing that Bhagavān alone is eternal, journey on with Him as your goal.\n\n(14) Śāntiḥ: The very first thing tyāga yields is peace of mind. When one accepts everything without the slightest agitation, everything becomes calm. Such peace can arise only when the mind is anchored in Bhagavān. Rather than spending life caught up in petty worldly matters — competition, rivalry, quarrel, and discord — it is far better to anchor the mind in Bhagavān and find peace.\n\n(15) Apaiśunam: 'Paiśunam' means sowing discord through slander. This is an exceedingly base quality. Slandering others to the powerful for one's own selfish ends, thereby ruining the peace of society, is a great sin. So Kṛṣṇa says: be apaiśuna. Do not slander, and do not lend your ear to slander.\n\n(16) Dayā bhūteṣu: Let there be compassion toward every single being possessing consciousness — whether plant, animal, bird, or human. Let there be kindness toward all. In earlier times this was observed in a special way — watering the tulasi first thing in the morning, going to the cowshed to feed the cows, feeding the dog at home before eating oneself, feeding the birds around the house, satisfying the Vaiśvānara-fire in the belly of a hungry visitor, and so on. Beyond this, showing kindness to someone in trouble, without distinguishing friend from foe, is a very great quality. We see this quality above all in Dharmarāya in the Mahābhārata.\n\n(17) Alolutvam: Excessive attachment to any one thing is lolatva. There should be no excessive fondness for anything. Clinging is the cause of sorrow.\n\n(18) Mārdavam (gentleness): Courtesy and a gentle disposition are called mārdavam here. Some among us speak to those who work for them, or to colleagues who report to them, without any respect — dealing with them arrogantly as \"my servants.\" This is an exceedingly base quality. In doing this we not only wound that person's heart, but effectively defy the Bhagavān dwelling within them. This is another face of ahaṅkāra. That is why Kṛṣṇa says: \"let your conduct have courtesy and gentleness.\"\n\n(19) Hrīḥ (a sense of shame): Feeling ashamed when one does something wrong. This is a very great quality. As the Taittirīya Upaniṣad says: śriyā deyaṃ, hriyā deyaṃ, bhiyā deyaṃ, saṃvidā deyaṃ (11.3). Scripture says: give even out of the shame that others might otherwise criticise you.\n\n(20) Acāpalam: The steadiness to take one's own decisions. Having examined a matter and understood what is right and what is wrong, standing firm in what one has determined is one's own path — this is acāpalam.\n\n(21) Tejaḥ: The authors of scripture call this brahma-varcas — a certain radiance filling the face, a certain brilliance in the eyes. This quality belongs only to those wise ones whose minds are pure and who walk steadily on the path of practice.\n\n(22) Kṣamā (forgiveness): Not harbouring a spirit of revenge even toward one who has wronged us. A desire for revenge should never arise in our mind at all. As scripture says: \"even if another does you harm, forgive them.\" Pray, \"God, grant them good sense\" — but never harbour a wish for revenge. Forgiveness is possible only when we are free of the pride that demands \"everyone must approve of me.\"\n\n(23) Dhṛtiḥ (steadiness of mind regardless of what happens): When there is true self-confidence, no one can touch us at all. A truly wise person is never a coward and never loses his composure. Fretting over \"this might happen\" is not living; rather, dhṛti is the firm inner strength of knowing that Bhagavān will give us the power to face whatever comes. Facing adverse circumstances and living through them — that is life. Unforeseen misfortune is an ordinary part of life. Knowing this, live with self-confidence. What is not meant to happen in this world never happens, and what is meant to happen no one can prevent. The burden of our protection belongs to the all-regulating Bhagavān. Why, then, fear anything?\n\n(24) Śaucam: We have discussed this matter several times in earlier chapters. Śauca means being pure both within and without. We must not cause trouble to others in society through any fault of our own. We must see to it that our body and mind remain clean. For this, people in earlier times wore fresh, spotless clothes every day, bathed with clay to avoid any bodily odour, and applied sandal-paste to their bodies. We must never behave in a way that makes another wrinkle their nose in disgust at us. Our mind should be clean.\n\nWearing fine clothes but with the mind full of impurity, we corrupt the atmosphere around us without even realising it. For this reason, we must be pure both within and without.\n\n(25) Adrohaḥ: Never harbouring thoughts of betrayal toward another. Never plot deceit against anyone. Never wish, even in a dream, for harm to come to another.\n\n(26) Nātimānitā: 'Atimāna' means an excessive, self-conjured pride in oneself. Kṛṣṇa says: \"never carry an excessive ahaṅkāra about yourself.\" Śrī Rāmacandra's life is the finest illustration of this quality. In the Vālmīki-Rāmāyaṇa it is said: \"vīryavān na ca vīryeṇa mahatā svena vismitaḥ\" (Ayodhyā 1.13). Rāma, though possessed of extraordinary valour, never carried the pride of \"I am a great hero,\" and lived his life like an ordinary human being. This is a very great quality.\n\nHaving thus described these twenty-six qualities we should cultivate in life, Kṛṣṇa says: \"bhavanti sampadaṃ daivīm abhijātasya bhārata.\" Here 'abhi' carries the sense of 'desiring'. \"These qualities belong to one born desiring divine wealth\" — meaning, those who are born already carrying, from birth itself, a longing for a sāttvika life naturally possess these qualities. These are the divine wealth — the mental wealth a human being must cultivate.",
  "16.4": "Ḍambhaḥ darpaḥ abhimānaḥ ca krodhaḥ pāruṣyam evaca |\n\najñānam ca abhijātasya pārtha sampadam āsurīm -- O Pārtha, one born with a share of demonic wealth [bad conduct] has these qualities: hypocrisy, arrogance, conceit, irascibility, harshness, and ignorance.\n\nAgainst the twenty-six divine qualities described above, Kṛṣṇa here names six demonic qualities:\n\n(1) Ḍambha (making a show of what one does not have): Displaying an amiability one does not truly possess, hiding the wickedness within and putting on the appearance of a thoroughly good person — this and the like is ḍambha. This is the opposite quality to the virtue of ārjava (uprightness) described above.\n\n(2) Darpa: A disposition that hesitates at no wrongdoing whatsoever. This is the opposite of the virtue of hrī (shame) described above.\n\n(3) Atimāna: An excessive, self-conjured notion of oneself — deluding oneself into thinking \"I am a great man\" and showing disrespect to others; expecting everyone to fall at one's feet — these amount to atimāna.\n\n(4) Krodha: Anger is a vice a person cannot shed even when he wishes to. It is a fault that leads us astray without our even realising it.\n\n(5) Pāruṣya: Harsh, offensive speech; addressing another with contempt — these are examples of pāruṣya.\n\n(6) Ajñāna: This ignorance is the emperor over all the vices listed above. A truly wise person never becomes a slave to these bad qualities.\n\nAll these demonic qualities, once they have made a home within us, end up driving us out of ourselves. So we must be the first to drive these vices out. The Qur'an puts this beautifully: \"drive them out from where you have been driven out.\" Drive out first these vices that have taken residence within you and are ruining you, is the message. This is exactly what the Veda says: \"yo asmān dveṣṭi yām vayam dviṣṭiḥ\" (Atharva 3.27.2) — meaning, \"drive out that enemy who hates us, and whom we hate.\" Here the 'enemy' refers to these inner enemies of ours — these very vices.\n\nIn this verse the epithet 'Pārtha' is used. Behind this epithet is the message: \"we must stand apart from these vices, walk the path of knowledge, and become true Pārthas, living a truly worthwhile life.\"",
  "16.5": "Daivī sampat vimokṣāya nibandhāya āsurī matā |\n\nmā śucaḥ sampadam daivīm abhijātaḥ asi pāṇḍavā -- Good conduct is the path to liberation. Bad conduct is the path to bondage in darkness. Pāṇḍava, do not grieve. You are born endowed with the path of goodness.\n\nKṛṣṇa explains why we ought to cultivate divine wealth, saying: \"divine wealth becomes the cause of vimokṣa, while demonic nature becomes the cause of nibandha.\" Here Kṛṣṇa deliberately uses the terms 'vi-mokṣa' and 'ni-bandha'. Vi-mokṣa means the development of one's personality across various levels, each in accordance with one's own effort, culminating in mokṣa. 'Ni-bandha' does not mean the bondage of ordinary worldly life — it is a base, degraded bondage, a bondage that thrusts us down into the darkness of tamas.\n\nKṛṣṇa says to Arjuna: \"you are the full manifestation of divine wealth. It is for this very reason that you sought out and were born into a house of divine wealth — your own nature and the influence of the house of your birth complement one another perfectly. Do not trouble yourself over this.\"",
  "16.6": "Dvau bhūtasargau loke asmin daiva āsura eva ca |\n\ndaivaḥ vistaraśaḥ proktaḥ āsuram pārtha me śṛṇu -- In this world there are two kinds of created beings: the divine [of good conduct] and the demonic [of bad conduct]. The divine kind has now been described at length; hear from me now, Pārtha, of the demonic in detail.\n\nThere are, fundamentally, two kinds of human beings in this world: those of divine conduct, who move with their face turned toward Bhagavān, and those of demonic conduct, who move with their back turned to Bhagavān. Of these two divisions, we have so far seen the divine nature described at length. Having given us a thorough picture of our own inner and social conduct, Kṛṣṇa now goes on to describe the conduct and speech of those of demonic nature. Through this, Kṛṣṇa teaches us \"how we ought not to be.\"",
  "16.7": "Pravṛttim ca nivṛttim ca janāḥ na viduḥ āsurāḥ |\n\nna śaucam na api ca ācāraḥ na satyam teṣu vidyate -- Those of demonic conduct do not know which path to take up and which to abandon. There is no purity in them; no good conduct; no truthful speech.\n\nKṛṣṇa here describes the social conduct and speech of those of demonic nature. Those of demonic nature have no awareness of what ought to be done and what ought not to be done. As a result they end up doing exactly what should not be done. Justice and injustice mean nothing to them. Earning money and attaining a position of prominence are their basic desires — and because of this, they carry no awareness at all that the earning of money ought to have a face of justice to it.\n\nFundamentally, those of demonic nature possess no inner cleanliness. Good speech, good thought, a clean body — none of this is found in them. They live putting on an outward act of great purity. They are never honest — whatever they do, it is for show. For every single quality Kṛṣṇa describes in the Gītā, we find a living example in the Mahābhārata itself — which is precisely why the Gītā is called the essence of the Mahābhārata. The finest example of demonic nature is \"Duryodhana.\"\n\nHow these people of demonic nature become a menace to society, and what their understanding is — Kṛṣṇa explains this at length in the remaining part of this chapter.",
  "16.8": "Asatyam apratiṣṭham te jagat āhuḥ anīśvaram |\n\naparaspara sambhūtaṃ kim anyat kāmahaitukam -- They say this universe is not the gift of the truly-real Bhagavān [it is a bundle of falsehood]. They say it rests on nothing [it has no true existence at all]. They say there is no all-powerful being who governs it. They do not even accept that one thing arises from another in orderly succession. What else, then? In their view it is something quite different — a child of desire, a chance illusion.\n\nThere are two kinds among these demonic people. One kind holds the atheistic view that the world has no other truth, no other foundation, no other ruling power — they believe there exists no force (satya) that subjects the world to creation, sustenance, and dissolution. They argue that since there is no God as the ground of this world, there is no master governing it, and that everything is illusion. Their belief is that the cycle of creation is nothing but a foolish doctrine.\n\nThe other kind of demonic people are those who hold that whatever we perceive in this world is merely our own illusion — a doctrine of nihilism (śūnyavāda). They believe that this world grows out of our own desires alone, and by no other power.",
  "16.9": "Etām dṛṣṭim avaṣṭabhya naṣṭa ātmānaḥ alpabuddhayaḥ |\n\nprabhavanti ugrakarmāṇaḥ kṣayāya jagataḥ ahitāḥ -- Clinging to such a view, these lost souls of little understanding, harmful and violent in conduct, become the cause of the world's destruction.\n\nPeople who cultivate a demonic disposition bring ruin upon their own lives and become, by their own doing, the cause of their own destruction. Without even the awareness that their own beliefs are destroying them, that they are walking a path of darkness, they become extremists (ugrakarmāṇaḥ). As a result they become enemies of the world and the cause of the world's ruin. Those among us and among you who carry this disposition are the true asuras.",
  "16.10": "Kāmam āśritya duṣpūraṃ ḍambha māna mada anvitāḥ |\n\nmohāt gṛhītvā asat grāhān pravartante aśuci vratāḥ -- Chasing after insatiable desire, given over to hypocrisy, conceit, and arrogance, these people of impure resolve go about, in their delusion, clinging to false views.\n\nThe desires of those of demonic nature have no end. However much they get, they only want more. For example: when there is no job at all, the desire is for a job; once a job is found, the desire is for a higher salary; once the salary comes, it seems less than the neighbour's salary — so the desire is for still more, then for a bigger post, and so on — no matter how much is gained, there is never satisfaction. This thirst can never be quenched. As the Purāṇas say, just as the netherworld can never be filled up and covered over with earth, so too can a person's desires never be satisfied. However much money we get, we add a zero to the right of it and grieve that it has become less — and in doing so we forget our happiness. In the Kaṭhopaniṣad, when Yama says, \"I will give you as much wealth as you want,\" Naciketas replies: \"na vittena tarpaṇīyo manuṣyaḥ\" — meaning, \"no one can ever satisfy a human being's desire for wealth.\" \"Enough\" is the divine disposition; \"I want more\" is the demonic disposition. Thus, chasing after desires, those of demonic nature do whatever should not be done in order to satisfy that insatiable want. Their whole life becomes hypocrisy — deceiving others through arrogance, excessive pride, and a theatrical existence. In this way their conduct and speech become an unclean life devoid of any purity.",
  "16.11": "Cintām aparimeyāṃ ca pralayāntām upāśritāḥ |\n\nkāma upabhoga paramāḥ etāvat iti niścitāḥ -- Given over to immeasurable anxiety that ends only with death, for whom the itch of desire is the whole of life, convinced that life is nothing more than this.\n\nThere is no end to their brooding. They spend their entire life fretting over acquiring what they do not have. They become slaves to anxiety. Their anxiety is pralayāntaka — it hounds them right up until death, never letting go. Enslaved to a life of enjoyment, unable to live without it, in this state of utter dependence, they live believing that life is nothing more than this.",
  "16.12": "Āśāpāśa śataiḥ baddhāḥ kāmakrodhaparāyaṇāḥ |\n\nīhante kāmabhogārtha anyāyena arthasañcayān -- Bound by hundreds of nooses of hope, given over wholly to desire and anger, they strive to amass wealth by unjust means for the sake of the enjoyment of their desires.\n\nFor the sake of enjoyment, they entangle themselves in the snare of their own desires and live in bondage. Selling themselves to desire and anger, they live wholly given over to kāma and krodha. The finest illustration of this is Pauṇḍraka Vāsudeva, who was possessed by the desire \"I myself must become God\" — and out of this desire he grew furious at the philosophers who tried to tell him this was wrong.\n\nTo satisfy their own pleasure and enjoyment, they strive to amass wealth by unjust means. Twisting the teachings of scripture to suit themselves, they engage themselves entirely in achieving their own desired ends.",
  "16.13": "Idam adya mayā labdham imam prāpsye manoratham |\n\nidam asti idam api me bhaviṣyati punardhanam -- \"This I have gained today; this desire I shall fulfil next; this much I already have; and this wealth too shall be mine\" —\n\nSuch is the thinking of those of demonic nature — the constant thought that one must always be amassing wealth. Whatever they get, the hoarding instinct says: \"today I got this much, tomorrow I shall earn that much more.\"",
  "16.14": "Asau mayā hataḥ śatruḥ haniṣye ca aparān api |\n\nīśvaraḥ aham aham bhogī siddhaḥ ahaṃ balavān sukhī -- \"This enemy I have slain, and the rest too I shall finish off; I am all-powerful; I alone am the enjoyer; I alone have gotten all I ever wished for; I am strong and I am happy\" —\n\nThis is the portrait of the arrogance of those of demonic nature — believing themselves all-powerful. This very arrogance — \"I myself am the lord,\" \"I myself am the enjoyer\" — is the root of their downfall.",
  "16.15": "Āḍhyo abhijanavān asmi kaḥ anyaḥ asti sadṛśaḥ mayā |\n\nyakṣye dāsyāmi modiṣye iti ajñāna vimohitāḥ -- Out of their folly they ramble on like this: \"I am rich, I am of noble birth, who else is there to equal me? I shall perform sacrifices, I shall give in charity, I shall rejoice\" —\n\nKṛṣṇa describes, across these three verses, how those of demonic nature think. They are forever obsessed with amassing wealth. Whatever they get, the itch is \"today I got this much, tomorrow I shall earn more.\" Hoarding money becomes an addiction to them. The thought is: \"I finished off this enemy, I will finish off the others too. I alone am all-capable — what is there in this world I cannot do? I was born to enjoy, not to suffer; I will do whatever it takes for my own pleasure, and finish off anyone who stands in my way. I alone am accomplished;\n\nI alone am responsible for my own success. I was born for enjoyment, and I have all the people, wealth, and power that requires; I act exactly as I please; I can buy anything with money; who in this world is my equal?\" — these and the like are their thoughts. Performing sacrifices and giving charity purely for show is their constant activity. This is nothing but the theatre of ignorance. They carry not the slightest awareness that this is the great gateway to their own total ruin — they live believing this very thing to be greatness, to be true refinement.",
  "16.16": "Aneka citta vibhrāntāḥ mohajāla samāvṛtāḥ |\n\nprasaktāḥ kāmabhogeṣu patanti narake aśucau -- Confused by countless thoughts, wrapped in the net of delusion, absorbed in the enjoyment of their desires, they fall and rot in a filthy hell.\n\nThey spend their lives in constant confusion, endlessly recalling past events. They never once feel remorse for their own wrongs. The sāttvika feel remorse and correct their faults; but these people, forever caught in the terrible net of delusion called ignorance and possessiveness, only fall into greater confusion — they never correct themselves. They devote themselves to enjoying whatever they desire, and, never satisfied with it, to hoarding still more and guarding it jealously. In time, they must fall and thrash about in a filthy hell.",
  "16.17": "Ātmasambhāvitāḥ stabdhāḥ dhana mānam mada anvitāḥ |\n\nyajante nāmayajñaiḥ te ḍambhena avidhi pūrvakam -- Self-glorifying, obstinate, intoxicated with the pride of wealth and status, such people too perform sacrifices — but only in name, hypocritically, breaking every rule of scripture.\n\nThey are full of praise for themselves. As it is said, \"avidyāyāṃ antare vartamānāḥ svayaṃ dhīrā paṇḍitaṃ anyamānā\" — they have no idea whether others actually respect them or not, but they cultivate for themselves a sense of their own greatness and swell with arrogance. The cause of their arrogance is not knowledge at all, but the intoxication of the money in their hand, and the deference people show them for the sake of that money. They perform sacrifices and rituals purely for hypocrisy, purely to display their own grandeur. There is no regard for scriptural rule in any of it — this is worship performed with no intention of pleasing Bhagavān, forgetting entirely the Bhagavān who dwells within as antaryāmī.",
  "16.18": "Ahaṅkāraṃ balaṃ darpaṃ kāmaṃ krodhaṃ ca saṃśritāḥ |\n\nmām ātma paradeheṣu pradviṣantaḥ abhyasūyakāḥ -- Given over to arrogance, forcefulness, conceit, desire, and irascibility, they deny and hate Me, dwelling as I do within their own body and the bodies of others, and are forever envious of another's rise.\n\nTheir basic possessions are arrogance, conceit, desire, and anger. \"Who is there to surpass me?\" — this arrogance of \"I alone am a great man.\" Its support is wealth and the strength of position. From this comes the conceit that looks upon others with contempt. Endless desires, and the anger that comes when they go unfulfilled — this is the whole of their existence. Kṛṣṇa says: \"they deny and reject the 'Me' who dwells within everyone.\" This is not some temporary, circumstantial denial of God — it is a denial of Bhagavān at the level of one's very nature. Such people are forever consumed with envy at the sight of another's knowledge or wealth.",
  "16.19": "Tān aham dviṣataḥ krūrān saṃsāreṣu nara adhamān |\n\nkṣepāmi ajasram aśubhān āsurīṣu eva yoniṣu -- Such vile human beings — hateful of the highest truth, merciless, and vile — I cast, endlessly, into ill-fated wombs of demonic birth within the ocean of saṃsāra.\n\nBhagavān does not create the jīva; we have already learned that the jīva and the jīva's own nature are, fundamentally, beginningless and eternal. Demonic nature is, at root, a jīva's own nature. Just as bitterness is intrinsic to the bitter gourd, so is the nature of these demonic people tāmasika by their own jīva-nature. Bhagavān does not alter that nature. Instead, the course of the jīva's destiny is shaped in keeping with that jīva's own nature. Bhagavān casts these merciless ones — who hate Bhagavān, who hate mankind, who hate the world itself — endlessly into the ocean of saṃsāra. As a result, they are born again and again into base households.",
  "16.20": "Āsurīṃ yonim āpannāḥ mūḍhāḥ janmani janmani |\n\nmām aprāpya eva kaunteya tataḥ yānti adhamāṃ gatim -- O Kaunteya, those fools who, birth after birth, take rebirth in wombs of demonic nature, never reach Me, and from there descend to an even lower state.\n\nThese wicked ones, born again and again into base households in this way, grow ever more deluded with each birth. With every birth their hatred of Bhagavān reaches new heights. Given over beyond measure to arrogance, intoxication, conceit, desire, and anger, they bring their own downfall upon themselves. They never reach Bhagavān. In keeping with the course of their own jīva-nature, they come to inhabit the tāmasika worlds.\n\nKṛṣṇa has beautifully described the nature of demonic people — their conduct, speech, thought, and action. From this we come to understand why there are troublemakers in this world, and what fate awaits them in future. When demonic qualities assail us without our knowing it, this chapter helps us take refuge in Bhagavān through the awareness of knowledge and wish for release from them. The jīva's own nature never changes. The jīva has no independent agency of its own; but the jīva does possess willed action. In keeping with this, we must make the effort to cultivate an unceasing divine nature and thereby reach Bhagavān. Whether the fruit of that effort is granted or withheld is Bhagavān's will — we ought not trouble ourselves over that. Wish for what is good, and live content with having been good. The burden of your protection belongs to Bhagavān; He will surely protect you.",
  "16.21": "Trividham narakasya idaṃ dvāraṃ nāśanam ātmanaḥ |\n\nkāmaḥ krodhaḥ tathā lobhaḥ tasmāt etat trayam tyajet -- These three are the path to the ruin of the self, the gateway to hell: desire, anger, and greed. One should therefore give up all three.\n\nThis verse carries a very important message. It also occurs in the Vidura-nīti. The Mahābhārata refers to it from both a social and a spiritual standpoint. Here Kṛṣṇa describes the three things that cause the ruin of the self: kāma, krodha, and lobha. Kṛṣṇa calls these \"the gateway to a human being's hell.\" Anger is the offspring of desire. When anger arises in us, it means many desires lie buried within us; when they go unfulfilled, we grow angry. There is a story in the Anuśāsana-parva of the Mahābhārata. A brāhmaṇa woman named Gautamī had an only son. One day he was bitten by a snake and died. A snake-charmer, seeing this, at once caught that very snake, brought it before her, and asked: \"tell me, in what manner would you like this snake, which bit and killed your son, to be killed?\" She answered: \"na brāhmaṇānāṃ kopo'sti kṛtaḥ kopācca yātanā…\" (13.1.20) — \"We are brāhmaṇas; a brāhmaṇa does not become angry, and if he does, he is no brāhmaṇa at all.\" Killing the snake will not bring my dead son back to life. My son died in accordance with his own karma. Anger over this brings only sorrow — let that snake go,\" she said. Those who have this understanding, who are free of desires, never grow angry. They alone are true brāhmaṇas. \"Kāma, krodha, and lobha are the three gateways to hell that thrust us into ruin — stay far from them,\" Kṛṣṇa warns us here. These are the root enemies that ruin our character. All other vices are their offspring. A person creates his own hell through these three vices alone. Give up these three, and there is happiness both in this world and the next.",
  "16.22": "Etaiḥ vimuktaḥ kaunteya tamaḥ dvāraiḥ tribhiḥ naraḥ |\n\nācarati ātmanaḥ śreyaḥ tataḥ yāti parāṃ gatim -- O Kaunteya, a man freed from these three gateways of darkness becomes able to do what is truly good for himself, and thereby attains the highest state.\n\nA human being who has escaped these three gateways to hell is a blessed soul. The path to mokṣa becomes smooth for him. He attains the highest state. Here Kṛṣṇa uses the word 'vimukta'. This does not mean giving up every desire whatsoever — it means giving up specifically the rājasika and tāmasika desires that harm our life. For example, the desire to reach Bhagavān is itself a desire — but it is not a gateway to hell, and need not be given up. There is no life without desire at all. So what we must give up here is only the rājasika and tāmasika element within desire. To indicate exactly this, the word used here is not 'mukta' but 'vi-mukta'.\n\nHere the epithet 'Kaunteya' is used. Behind this epithet is the message: \"if we are to walk the path of knowledge, we must become true Kaunteyas, who reject this base trio of kāma, krodha, and lobha.\"\n\nSome of us wonder why the rules of scripture are needed at all — is it not enough to live by the voice of one's own conscience? Kṛṣṇa explains, in the verse that follows, the importance of observing the discipline of scripture.",
  "16.23": "Yaḥ śāstravidhim utsṛjya vartate kāmakārataḥ |\n\nna saḥ siddhim avāpnoti na sukhaṃ na parāṃ gatim -- One who disregards the discipline of scripture and acts as he pleases attains neither accomplishment, nor happiness, nor the highest state.\n\nWe cannot live honestly by our own lights alone, casting aside the discipline of scripture, because we have no true way of knowing what is right and what is wrong. We simply take whatever we happen to like as being right. It is scripture that gives us true discernment. Mere self-will and honesty are not enough for us to incorporate the world's many truths into our practice. We absolutely need scripture to decide what is right and what is wrong. From our own experience alone,\n\nwe cannot grasp the entire world. Our intellect is limited, our reasoning is limited, our conscience is limited, our honesty is limited. Hence, with our limited experience, we cannot come to know the truth of the whole universe. For this reason, we must learn the truth from Bhagavān — from the Veda and the other scriptures He has given the world — and from the wise, who understand that scripture and have made it known to us. Without this, no true accomplishment is possible.",
  "16.24": "Tasmāt śāstram pramāṇaṃ te kārya akārya vyavasthitau |\n\njñātvā śāstra vidhāna uktam karma kartum iha arhasi -- Therefore let scripture be your authority in determining what should and should not be done. Knowing what scripture has enjoined, you ought to perform your action here accordingly.\n\nScripture alone is the authority for all our questions of what to do and what not to do. Hence we must first trust what scripture says — only then does it become possible to truly understand it. Those who tell us \"do this, do not do that\" in scripture are the wise, who have directly realised for themselves the consequences that follow from such action. Hence Kṛṣṇa says: know the discipline of action, and act accordingly.\n\nHere 'scripture' means the four Vedas, together with their supporting texts — the Pañcarātra, the Mahābhārata, the original Rāmāyaṇa, and the eighteen Purāṇas. Apart from these, nothing else can be held as a fully valid authority.\n\n|| Thus ends the sixteenth chapter ||",
  "17.1": "Arjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna asked:\n\nYe śāstravidhim utsṛjya yajante śraddhayā anvitāḥ |\n\nTeṣāṃ niṣṭhā tu kā kṛṣṇa sattvam āho rajaḥ tamaḥ -- Some people, without knowing the injunctions of Śāstra, still act with faith. What is their footing, Kṛṣṇa? Is it sattva, rajas, or tamas?\n\nWe have not studied the Vedas, we do not know the injunctions of Śāstra, but there is remorse for not knowing Śāstra; there is faith in Śāstra, a wish to live by it. The wise say: \"yadeva vidyayā karoti śraddhayā\" -- \"whatever you do, do knowingly. Karma done with knowledge succeeds; otherwise it is fruitless.\" Given this, is the karma of one who acts with faith, but without this knowledge, fruitless? This is Arjuna's question.\n\nHere \"śraddhā\" means the faith we hold in the transcendental, imperceptible power that governs us -- that is: faith in Bhagavān, faith in Śāstra, faith in sin and merit, faith that there is a supreme power governing everything. As the Mahābhārata says: \"the belief that karma bears fruit is the very pillar of human life.\" Given this, if someone has such faith without the knowledge of Śāstra, what is their footing? \"O Kṛṣṇa, you who draw everyone toward you -- where do you draw such people? Is their conviction sattvic, rajasic, or tamasic?\" -- Arjuna asks Kṛṣṇa this question that troubles us all.",
  "17.2": "Bhagavān uvāca -- The Lord said:\n\nTrividhā bhavati śraddhā dehinām sā svabhāvajā |\n\nSāttvikī rājasī ca eva tāmasī ca iti tāṃ śṛṇu -- This faith, born of one's own nature, is threefold: sāttvikī, rājasī, and tāmasī. Hear about it.\n\nLiving beings in the disciplined body are basically threefold by nature -- sāttvika, rājasa, and tāmasa. Beyond this, we also develop, under the influence of environment and upbringing, a further threefold faith at the level of the mind. So even one who is sāttvika by inherent nature may, at the mental level, become rājasa or tāmasa. For example, we have earlier seen the story of Gargācārya, who was sāttvika by inherent nature but, coming under tāmasa influence, performed austerities wishing for a son who would defeat Kṛṣṇa. This mind-level, externally-acquired faith is not carried across births, but the faith belonging to one's own nature (svarūpa-śraddhā) accompanies the soul in every birth without fail. To make this clear, the verse uses \"eva\" and \"ca\" together: the soul's own nature -- sāttvika-eva, rājasa-eva, or tāmasa-eva -- does not change; the external faith layered over it -- sāttvika-ca, rājasa-ca, tāmasa-ca -- arises from influence and is confined to, at most, a single birth. \"Hear of these three kinds of faith,\" Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna.",
  "17.3": "Sattva anurūpā sarvasya śraddhā bhavati bhārata |\n\nŚraddhāmayaḥ ayam puruṣaḥ yaḥ yat śraddhaḥ saḥ eva saḥ -- Bhārata, everyone's faith conforms to their own nature. This soul is nothing but faith in essence. Whatever a person's faith is, that is what he is.\n\nThe root faith conforms to the soul's own nature, but sometimes it does not become manifest. When it fails to manifest, whatever external influence governs the mind becomes that person's faith. Man is faith's puppet. One whose own-nature faith is sāttvika is sāttvika; one whose own-nature faith is rājasa is rājasa; one whose own-nature faith is tāmasa is tāmasa. Hence there are three kinds among souls: the soul with sāttvika faith is a sāttvika soul; with rājasa faith, a rājasa soul; with tāmasa faith, a tāmasa soul. When, through meditation, we still the mind and reach the turīya state, the external faith vanishes and only the own-nature faith becomes manifest.",
  "17.4": "Yajante sāttvikāḥ devān yakṣarakṣāṃsi rājasāḥ |\nPretān bhūtagaṇān ca anye yajante tāmasāḥ janāḥ -- The worship of the sāttvikas reaches the devatās; that of the rājasas reaches the yakṣas and rākṣasas. The worship of the tāmasas reaches the pretas and bhūta-gaṇas.\n\nTaken at face value this verse makes no sense -- it seems to say sāttvikas worship devatās, rājasas worship yakṣa-rākṣasas, and tāmasas worship ghosts and spirits. But the real message hidden here is different. As we know, no one -- sāttvika, rājasa, or tāmasa -- consciously worships anyone but Bhagavān or the devatās; only those given to black magic worship ghosts, spirits, or demons. So to grasp the true message we must analyze the verse more deeply. Here \"devān\" means \"the many forms of Bhagavān and his retinue of devatās.\" \"Yajante sāttvikā devān\" means: the worship a sāttvika offers with awareness of Bhagavān reaches the devatās. Likewise \"yakṣarakṣāṃsi rājasāḥ\" means: the worship a rājasa offers reaches the yakṣas and rākṣasas. Rājasas generally worship without awareness of Bhagavān's supremacy, out of fear, with the sentiment of a vow paid off so that the devatās will not trouble them; when worship is offered this way, it is yakṣas and rākṣasas bearing the devatās' names who come and receive what was meant for Bhagavān. The third class, tāmasas, worship purely to display their own status -- for example, as Duryodhana performed the Pauṇḍrika-yāga to outdo the Pāṇḍavas' Aśvamedha. Such ill-intentioned worship by tāmasas is received not by devatās but by bhūtas and pretas bearing their names.\n\nWhat matters in worship is not merely faith but what kind of faith it is. Even without knowing Śāstra, if one prays with sāttvika faith -- \"Bhagavān, I know nothing, I worship you with devotion, forgive my faults\" -- surrendering in this way, Bhagavān himself comes and accepts that worship. But rājasa or tāmasa worship, done from selfishness, ego, or hypocrisy, is accepted by neither Bhagavān nor his retinue of devatās.\n\nOur karma should be free of kāma (niṣkāma) -- here kāma means base desire. There should never be selfishness in desire; we must give up the narrow wish that only good come to us.\n\nWe should not worship with some narrow motive in mind -- for instance, performing a yajña so that one's business prospers, or worshipping in hope that a daughter gets a good match. We should not hand Bhagavān such a list of our wants: he alone knows what should happen, what is good and what is bad, and he gives accordingly. When we instead cling to such a narrow list of desires while acting, the outcome is grim. We have worship done with some desire in mind, we spend money on it, but the desire is not fulfilled -- and then we resent Bhagavān and feel the money was wasted. When this happens our worship has become a transaction, and it goes to bhūtas and pretas. Worship of Bhagavān is not a transaction.",
  "17.5": "Aśāstravihitaṃ ghoraṃ tapyante ye tapo janāḥ |\n\nḌambhāhaṅkārasaṃyuktāḥ kāmarāgabalānvitāḥ ||5||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and commentary for these two verses (17.5 and 17.6) are given together at the next verse (17.6).]",
  "17.6": "Aśāstra vihitaṃ ghoraṃ tapyante ye tapaḥ janāḥ |\n\nḌambha ahaṅkāra saṃyuktāḥ kāma rāga bala anvitāḥ\n\nKarśayantaḥ śarīrasthaṃ bhūtagrāmam acetasaḥ |\n\nMāṃ ca eva antaḥ śarīrasthaṃ tān viddhi āsuraniścayān -- Some people, transgressing the injunctions of Śāstra, practice severe austerities out of hypocrisy and pride, driven by craving and passion. Such foolish people ignore the assembly of deities [governing their body] residing within their own body; they disregard my greatness dwelling within. Know them to be of demonic disposition.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa explains what the lowest kind of faith -- tāmasa faith -- looks like. Some practice austerities transgressing the injunctions of Śāstra; the intention behind such austerity is dire. Here tapas does not mean only the austerities of ṛṣis and munis -- any action done with earnestness can become tapas [more on this in the coming verses, 14-19, of this chapter]. When practicing tapas, one may do so wishing good for oneself, or wishing good for the world. Take the Gāyatrī: in \"dhiyo yo naḥ pracodayāt\" there is the sāttvika faith of \"grant us all good understanding, O Bhagavān.\" Tapas performed wishing good for everyone in the world is sāttvika tapas. Apart from this, worship done wishing \"let good come to me alone\" is rājasa; worship done wishing \"let the other person be ruined, let good come to me\" is tāmasa. These latter are dire, world-harming forms of faith.\n\nŚāstra-sanctioned tapas means: recognizing Bhagavān's supremacy, harboring no ill will toward others, and practicing austerity wishing good for all in the world. \"sarvotkarṣe deva devasya viṣṇo mahā-tātparyam\" -- the essential purport of all the scriptures is that Bhagavān is supreme. Not accepting this is contrary to Śāstra. Even one who accepts this, if the wish arises in the mind that ill should befall the world, that too becomes aśāstra-vihita. Whatever is not sanctioned by Śāstra is not dear to Bhagavān. Kṛṣṇa gives the warning here that karma done selfishly, wishing ill on another, is a \"dire act.\" Behind such action lie hypocrisy and ego. This is never accepted by the devatās.\n\nSome practice austerity with the wicked intention of overpowering others by physical strength. Whoever obstructs their desires, whoever does not accommodate their whims -- the thought of finishing off such people occupies their minds. Those who act this way -- from hypocrisy, ego, narrow craving, selfishness, and brute force -- are fools. They punish their own bodies while performing austerity, but remain unaware of the assembly of deities [the group of devatās governing the body] residing within their own body. Disregarding the entire assembly of devatās, and disregarding even Bhagavān who dwells within those devatās, punishing their own bodies while performing their observances -- such people are called demonic. They hold the view: \"sensory action alone is everything; there is nothing beyond it.\"\n\nSeeing Kṛṣṇa's explanation here, we come to know how terrifying this demonic -- blind faith bred by influence -- truly is. We must recognize and correct this in every one of our own actions. For this, in the coming verses, Kṛṣṇa explains what the sāttvika, rājasa, and tāmasa varieties look like in food, tapas, dāna, and the rest.",
  "17.7": "Āhāraḥ tu api sarvasya trividhaḥ bhavati priyaḥ |\nYajñaḥ tapaḥ tathā dānaṃ teṣāṃ bhedam imam śṛṇu -- The food everyone favors is also threefold; so too are yajña, tapas, and dāna. Hear of this distinction among them.\n\nThe food we eat is also of three kinds -- sāttvika, rājasa, and tāmasa food. As the Chāndogyopaniṣad (5.1) says, \"annam aśitaṃ tredhā vidhīyate\" -- the prāṇa and vaiśvānara within us sort the food we consume into three grades (subtle, middling, and gross) and distribute it to the various parts of the body. There is a direct connection between our food and our mind. Our ancients understood this, and so they forbade foods that affect the mind -- garlic, for instance, is a medicinal substance, but its use in daily food is forbidden because it does not support the growth of the intellect. Similarly with drumstick, ivy gourd, and the like -- these are forbidden for students (though they may be used medicinally). In this way, Kṛṣṇa was the first in the world to teach which kind of food suits the human intellect.\n\nAlongside food, this same threefold pattern can be identified in our worship of Bhagavān, our austerity, our manner of thinking, our charity, our conduct in life, and our speech. In the coming verses Kṛṣṇa explains how to recognize these three kinds in food, yajña, tapas, and dāna.",
  "17.8": "Āyuḥ sattva bala ārogya sukha prīti vivardhanāḥ |\n\nRasyāḥ snigdhāḥ sthirāḥ hṛdyāḥ āhārāḥ sāttvika priyāḥ -- These are the foods a sāttvika favors: those which increase lifespan, goodness, strength, health, happiness, and affection; which remain pleasing for long; which give joy when eaten; which are tasty, full-bodied, of lasting effect, and repeatedly appealing.\n\nIn this verse Kṛṣṇa describes sāttvika food. Whatever food supports the growth of our lifespan, gives us a gentle nature, nourishes the body, and brings health, is sāttvika food. This food does not create an unpleasant impression when we see it -- it is full of flavor and tasty. Eating it fills the stomach without causing immediate hunger again; it is pleasing to look at and memorable when eaten.\n\nWhatever true sāttvikas eat is sāttvika food. Naturally occurring, flavorful, tasty fruits, tubers, pulses, and roots are generally sāttvika food. Pure ghee, which increases lifespan (when used in moderation), is also sāttvika food.",
  "17.9": "Kaṭu-amla-lavaṇāty-uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa-rūkṣa-vidāhinaḥ |\n\nĀhārā rājasasyeṣṭā duḥkha-śokāmaya-pradāḥ ||9||\n\nKaṭu āmla lavaṇa ati uṣṇa tīkṣṇa rūkṣa vidāhinaḥ |\n\nĀhārāḥ rājasasya iṣṭāḥ duḥkha śoka āmaya pradāḥ -- These are the foods a rājasa favors: excessively pungent, sour, salty, very hot, sharp, dry, and burning foods, which bring on sorrow, grief, and disease.\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa describes rājasa food. Excessively pungent, excessively sour, excessively salty foods are rājasa food. Drinking things that are too hot, eating harsh and dry food, consuming heartburn-inducing food -- these mark the rājasa. Eating this kind of food increases mental and physical distress; it is food that can bring on disease.\n\nEating raw green chillies directly, overeating tamarind, always using excess salt, drinking throat-scalding coffee, using excessive mustard and spice, eating tasteless food -- these are some examples of the rājasa pattern of eating. [If such foods are prescribed by a physician for treating an illness, they then count as sāttvika food for the patient.]",
  "17.10": "Yātayāmaṃ gatarasaṃ pūti paryuṣitaṃ ca yat |\nUcchiṣṭam api ca amedhyaṃ bhojanaṃ tāmasa priyam -- This is what a tāmasa favors as food: food cooked long before and gone stale, that has lost its flavor, that has turned rancid, that is left over from the previous day, that is another's leavings, and that is unclean.\n\nFor a tāmasa, the greatest thing in life is eating itself. They consume food without offering it to Bhagavān. Bland, stale, foul-smelling, flavorless food is what a tāmasa favors. They also favor eating another's leavings [for example, several people sharing and eating leftovers from the same plate].\n\nHaving thus explained how a person can be identified by their food, Kṛṣṇa goes on to explain how a person's nature can be recognized through their conduct -- yajña, tapas, and dāna.",
  "17.11": "Aphala ākāṅkṣibhiḥ yajñaḥ vidhi-dṛṣṭaḥ ya ijyate |\nYaṣṭavyam eva iti manaḥ samādhāya saḥ sāttvikaḥ -- The yajña performed strictly as enjoined by Śāstra, without any craving for its fruit, with the mind resolved simply that \"this ought to be offered\" -- that is a sāttvika yajña.\n\nNot everyone who performs a yajña is sāttvika. There is a threefold distinction in yajña too -- sāttvika, rājasa, and tāmasa. In this verse Kṛṣṇa explains what a sāttvika yajña looks like. Yajña does not mean only worship performed in the fire-pit -- worshipping Bhagavān before an image is also yajña, and mentally meditating on Bhagavān is also yajña. Worship performed strictly according to injunction, without desire for any fruit, wishing only that Bhagavān be pleased and that his gracious gaze fall upon oneself -- this is sāttvika yajña. Worship performed mechanically, without understanding the meaning of the mantras, with some narrow desire for fruit, does not become sāttvika yajña.\n\nHere the phrase \"vidhi-pūrvakam\" (\"according to injunction\") is used -- a very important point. For example, when we worship we ring the bell, we blow the conch. Whatever the mode of worship, we need to understand, before performing it, why we are doing it. The bell must not be struck harshly and monotonously; when rung, it should let out the sound of Oṃkāra. When the sound of Oṃkāra emerges this way, every evil force in the region where that sound is heard flees, and this sound draws in divine forces -- creating the pure atmosphere needed for worship. It is essential to draw out the sound of Oṃkāra from the bell with the earnest prayer that evil forces depart and divine forces arrive. A bell should never produce a discordant note; it has a proper pitch -- there are seven bells for the seven notes -- and it should be struck true to pitch. This is a wondrous process of purifying the atmosphere through Oṃkāra -- and this is only one example. In the same way, we must do every one of our actions knowingly and with full presence of mind; there must be joy in the doing. Only when done this way does it count as sāttvika yajña.",
  "17.12": "Abhisandhāya tu phalaṃ ḍambha artham api ca eva yat |\nIjyate bharataśreṣṭha tam yajñaṃ viddhi rājasam -- O best of the Bharatas, know that the yajña performed for the sake of its fruit, or for show, is rājasa.\n\nA yajña performed selfishly, seeking a particular fruit, out of hypocrisy, is a rājasa yajña. Any yajña, to truly reach Bhagavān, must be performed with devotion. We must act as the best among those devoted to Bhagavān (bharataśreṣṭha). Without this, a yajña performed for the sake of publicity becomes a rājasa yajña.",
  "17.13": "Vidhihīnam asṛṣṭa-annam mantra-hīnam adakṣiṇam |\nŚraddhā-virahitaṃ yajñaṃ tāmasaṃ paricakṣate -- A yajña not conducted according to Śāstra, without the giving of food, without mantras, without dakṣiṇā, and without faith in the act being performed -- such a yajña is called tāmasa.\n\nReciting mantras incorrectly, out of sequence, or with faulty pronunciation -- all this is vidhihīna. A yajña performed this way is a tāmasa yajña. Just as worship in any yajña proceeds through an image and through fire, the worship of living, breathing forms of Bhagavān [i.e. feeding people] is equally essential -- offering food is worship of Bhagavān dwelling within every being, this is caramūrti-ārādhana. A yajña performed without this food-offering, and without dakṣiṇā [the service we render toward the livelihood of those who officiate], is a tāmasa yajña. More important than all of this is faith -- whatever yajña is performed without unwavering, supreme faith in Bhagavān becomes, in every case, a tāmasa yajña.\n\nAfter food and yajña, Kṛṣṇa explains the three kinds of tapas in the coming verses. Fundamentally there are three kinds of tapas: bringing discipline to our conduct is tapas; deep contemplation is tapas; and bodily austerity undertaken for purity of mind is also tapas. One need not go to the forest to practice tapas -- it can be practiced while living at home. Before explaining sāttvika, rājasa, and tāmasa tapas, Kṛṣṇa here gives, across the next three verses, an account of the three fundamental kinds of tapas -- of body, speech, and mind.",
  "17.14": "Deva-dvija-guru-prājña-pūjanaṃ śaucam ārjavam |\n\nBrahmacaryam ahiṃsā ca śārīraṃ tapaḥ ucyate -- Reverence for the devatās, the twice-born, one's teachers, and the wise; purity; uprightness; celibacy; and non-violence -- these are called the tapas of the body.\n\nBowing wherever the symbol of Bhagavān is seen, showing respect to the learned, bowing to one's guru, honoring the prājñas -- the realized ones who have directly experienced Bhagavān through bhagavad-bhūti -- all of this is śārīra-tapas. Alongside this: maintaining cleanliness, honest conduct and speech, observance of brahmacarya, and living so as not to trouble others -- this too is tapas of the body. [The full explanation of śaucam, ārjavam, and ahiṃsā mentioned in this verse is given in the opening verses of the sixteenth chapter.] Here, \"observance of brahmacarya\" means fixing the mind on Bhagavān; toward that end, the study of the Vedas, and toward that, bodily brahmacarya. Complete bodily brahmacarya applies only to sannyāsins and brahmacārins -- householders cannot live this way. So for a householder, observing brahmacarya means not becoming a slave to unrestrained lust, but living within the bounds of gṛhastha-dharma. Thus: surrender to Bhagavān; showing respect to the wise, to gurus, to prājñas; maintaining cleanliness; honest conduct and speech; observing brahmacarya; and living by non-violence -- this is śārīrika tapas. We need this tapas to live as decent members of society. These are the physical disciplines a human being ought to follow.",
  "17.15": "Anudvega-karaṃ vākyaṃ satyaṃ priya-hitaṃ ca yat |\n\nSvādhyāya-abhyasanaṃ ca eva vāṅmayaṃ tapaḥ ucyate -- Speech that does not agitate, that is truthful, pleasing, and beneficial, together with the regular study of scripture -- this is called the tapas of speech.\n\nAs discussed in the previous chapter, truth is not simply saying things exactly as they are. Kṛṣṇa gives us, through this verse, an explanation of what truth actually means. Speaking in a way that favors and supports genuine goodness, wherever it exists, without hurting another, in a manner beneficial to them -- this is tapas of speech. Speaking words that are both pleasing and beneficial to another is very difficult, because what is pleasing is, in most cases, not beneficial. In such situations, silence is best. Reading the stories of Kṛṣṇa, we often find places where he appears to have lied or deceived -- but when we observe the compassion and well-wishing behind such action, we come to know it was no lie. In our daily speech, we must carry the awareness Kṛṣṇa gives us in this verse. It is fine to remain silent without speaking, but there is no use speaking something and then regretting it afterward -- \"a word once spoken cannot be taken back; we have no medicine to heal a wounded heart.\" So one should never speak words that hurt another. Rather than spend one's days in idle chatter, engaging in svādhyāya -- reflection on the meaning of the Vedas, contemplation of the Gītā, recitation of the Viṣṇusahasranāma, and the like -- this is tapas of speech.",
  "17.16": "Manaḥ prasādaḥ saumyatvaṃ maunam ātmavinigrahaḥ |\nBhāva-saṃśuddhiḥ iti etat tapaḥ mānasam ucyate -- Serenity of mind, gentleness, contemplative silence, self-restraint, and purity of feeling -- this is called tapas of the mind.\n\nBeyond the two kinds of tapas described above, the greater tapas is tapas of the mind -- tapas of body and of speech serve it. Our mind should always be at ease, never filled with bad thoughts or defiled; it should be gentle, free of cruelty. Before arriving at any judgment on a matter, one must reflect deeply -- one should never react rashly to any issue or any person. Reflect, and speak only after reflecting. To reflect in this way requires self-restraint (see chapter 13, verse 7 for more). A mind that is clean and free of cruelty has command over the senses; no evil thought occurs to such a mind. Even if someone speaks ill of us, we feel no hatred or anger toward them. This mastery of the mind is tapas of the mind.\n\nEach of these three kinds of tapas described here is again threefold -- sāttvika, rājasa, and tāmasa. This is explained in the next three verses.",
  "17.17": "Śraddhayā parayā taptaṃ tapaḥ tat trividhaṃ naraiḥ |\nAphala-ākāṅkṣibhiḥ yuktaiḥ sāttvikaṃ paricakṣate -- When these three kinds of tapas are practiced by people with supreme faith, without craving for fruit, and with an attitude of offering to Bhagavān, that is called sāttvika tapas.\n\nIf supreme faith and sincerity lie behind a tapas, it becomes sāttvika tapas. Every act performed with awareness of Bhagavān's supremacy, with the resolve that he be pleased, is sāttvika. For example, when bowing to a guru, the resolve that one is bowing to Bhagavān indwelling that guru -- this is the right attitude. There should be this spirit of offering to Bhagavān behind every one of our actions. This is exactly what the mantras convey: when we say \"anena bhagavān gopāla-kṛṣṇaḥ prīyatām -- śrī-kṛṣṇārpaṇam astu,\" there must be a full spirit of offering within us as well. Such tapas is called sāttvika tapas.",
  "17.18": "Satkāra-māna-pūjā-arthaṃ tapaḥ ḍambhena ca eva yat |\nKriyate tat iha proktaṃ rājasaṃ calam adhruvam -- Tapas performed hypocritically, for the sake of honor, respect, and homage, which has no firm foundation and no lasting result -- this is declared to be rājasa.\n\nAny tapas performed without awareness of Bhagavān, out of a desire that people honor oneself, seeking recognition, is rājasa tapas. Tapas performed with this kind of hypocrisy does not avail one in the world beyond; even in this world, the fruit of an act done from fickleness is not lasting.\n\nIn this verse \"eva\" and \"ca\" occur together, carrying a special message. If an act is done for the sake of honor, respect, and hypocrisy, it is rājasa; but if honor comes unsought while acting with the spirit of offering to Bhagavān, or if one publicizes something with the intention of sharing knowledge so that others may understand -- that does not become rājasa. To convey exactly this distinction, \"ca\" and \"eva\" are used here.",
  "17.19": "Mūḍha-grāheṇa ātmano yat pīḍayā kriyate tapaḥ |\n\nParasya-utsādana-arthaṃ vā tat tāmasam udāhṛtam ||19||\n\nMūḍha grāheṇa ātmanaḥ yat pīḍayā kriyate tapaḥ |\n\nParasya utsādana arthaṃ vā tat tāmasam udāhṛtam -- Tapas performed by tormenting one's own body out of misguided obstinacy, or with the aim of destroying another -- this is declared to be tāmasa tapas.\n\nTapas performed out of misunderstanding, out of delusion, without awareness of the Supreme, with the ill will that another should be ruined, without awareness of Bhagavān dwelling within one's own body, by punishing the body -- this is tāmasa tapas.",
  "17.20": "Dātavyam iti yat dānaṃ dīyate anupakāriṇe |\nDeśe kāle ca pātre ca tat dānaṃ sāttvikaṃ smṛtam -- Charity given, not for reciprocation, but from a sense of duty, to a worthy person in a fitting place and time -- this is remembered as sāttvika dāna.\n\nGiving without any expectation of return, with an inwardly joyful heart, wholeheartedly, lovingly, to a worthy person, at a fitting time, in a fitting place -- with the sense \"this is my duty\" -- this is sāttvika dāna. [More explanation: the opening verse of chapter sixteen.]",
  "17.21": "Yat tu pratyupakāra-arthaṃ phalam uddiśya vā punaḥ |\nDīyate ca parikliṣṭaṃ tat rājasam udāhṛtam -- Charity given for the sake of a return favor, or with an eye on some fruit, and given reluctantly -- this is declared to be rājasa.\n\nGiving so that another will do us a favor in future, or in return for a favor received in the past; giving because one feels obliged to give -- giving with the desire for fruit -- this is rājasa dāna.",
  "17.22": "Adeśa-kāle yat dānam apātrebhyaḥ ca dīyate |\nAsat-kṛtam avajñātaṃ tat tāmasam udāhṛtam -- Charity given at an unfitting place and time, to unworthy persons, without respect, given with contempt -- this is declared to be tāmasa.\n\nGiving something merely to be rid of what we do not want, or giving to unworthy people with scolding, mockery, and insult -- this is tāmasa dāna.\n\nWhat follows next in this chapter is the arthavāda -- the exposition further elaborating the importance of sāttvika karma that Kṛṣṇa has taught so far. Arthavāda means an explanatory passage impressing upon us the significance of sāttvika karma.",
  "17.23": "Oṃ tat sat iti nirdeśaḥ brahmaṇaḥ trividhaḥ smṛtaḥ |\n\nBrāhmaṇāḥ tena vedāḥ ca yajñāḥ ca vihitāḥ purā -- \"Oṃ, Tat, Sat\" -- this is remembered as the threefold designation of Brahman. By that same Bhagavān, the brāhmaṇas, the Vedas, and the yajñas were established of old.\n\nOṃ, Tat, and Sat are the three names of Bhagavān. The wise brought this practice of remembrance into use to remember and invoke Bhagavān. Bhagavān's name is unlike our own names -- his names are words describing his very qualities. \"Oṃ\" is a name reserved exclusively for Bhagavān; in three syllables (a, u, m) it expresses the whole of human life's every aspect. He in whom the entire universe is woven and interlaced -- he is \"Oṃ\". Oṃkāra is used to declare that every action we perform is offered to him. To begin with \"hariḥ oṃ\" is to say: \"everything I am about to do is offered to Bhagavān.\" [For more on Oṃkāra, see chapter 7, verses 8-9.]\n\n\"Tat\" means the all-pervading one. Bhagavān is the ground of every quality; being full of every quality, he pervades the entire Veda -- hence he is \"Tat\". And \"Sat\" means one free of every flaw. Thus Oṃ-Tat-Sat are the three names from Vedic times. What we today call \"acyutāya namaḥ,\" \"anantāya namaḥ,\" \"govindāya namaḥ\" carry this very same meaning. A-cyuta means one who never falls from any quality -- without him, this world would have no existence; he is the foundation of the entire universe. The second name, Ananta, means endless -- one whose qualities have no limit is Ananta. The third name, Govinda -- \"go\" means the Vedas; Bhagavān, established by the entirety of the Vedas, is Govinda. In sum, briefly, Oṃ-Tat-Sat means \"Bhagavān, who is the foundation of the universe, full of every quality, established by all the Vedas, and free of every flaw.\" This Bhagavān created the Veda, and from it created the Vedajñas -- \"become the knowers\" -- and created yajña, enjoining that karma be performed with knowledge.\n\nSome among us argue the Veda is \"pauruṣeya\" and others that it is \"apauruṣeya.\" The Veda uttered by the Supreme Person prior to creation is pauruṣeya; and since it is uttered by Bhagavān anew in every kalpa across beginningless, endless time, it is also apauruṣeya. Hence this dispute is pointless.",
  "17.24": "Tasmād om ity udāhṛtya yajña-dāna-tapaḥ-kriyāḥ |\n\nPravartante vidhānoktāḥ satataṃ brahmavādinām ||24||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning, and commentary for these two verses (17.24 and 17.25) are given together at the next verse (17.25).]",
  "17.25": "Tasmāt om iti udāhṛtya yajña dāna tapaḥ kriyāḥ |\n\nPravartante vidhāna uktāḥ satatam brahmavādinām -- Therefore the yajña, dāna, and tapas of the knowers of Brahman, performed as enjoined by Śāstra, always begin together with the remembrance of Bhagavān's name \"Oṃ\".\n\nHere Kṛṣṇa explains the importance of using Oṃkāra in karma. As we know, to become a knower of the Veda one must first receive instruction in Oṃkāra; Oṃkāra must be uttered at the beginning and end of a yajña; Oṃkāra must open and close Vedic recitation. All this is a practice handed down from antiquity. Whatever work we undertake, the essential purpose is to begin it with the resolve \"I do this as an act of worship of Bhagavān,\" and to close it saying \"may Bhagavān accept what I have done.\" This is why the practice of uttering Oṃkāra before performing karma has grown up over time. With this remembrance, any pārāyaṇa, homa, havana, yajña, tapas, or dāna we perform comes to count as sāttvika karma.\n\nTat iti anabhisandhāya phalaṃ yajña-tapaḥ-kriyāḥ |\nDāna-kriyāḥ ca vividhāḥ kriyante mokṣa-kāṅkṣibhiḥ -- Those who desire mokṣa perform manifold acts of yajña, dāna, and tapas for the sake of Bhagavān called \"Tat\", without setting their sights on any fruit.\n\nWe have seen earlier that \"Tat\" refers to Bhagavān, denoted by the entirety of the Veda. Here Kṛṣṇa explains what \"Tat\" signifies in the karma we perform. \"Tat\" is the abbreviated form of the Vedamātā Gāyatrī (its first and last syllables), and so it is a principal name of Bhagavān denoted by the Gāyatrī. \"Tat\" signifies, in karma, the fruit Bhagavān bestows. \"Tat\" means: karma performed without any desire for worldly fruit, purely for the love of Bhagavān, who releases us from bondage and grants mokṣa -- whether that karma be yajña, tapas, or dāna. Even when the wise give in charity, they give without seeking any worldly fruit. Take annadāna, for instance: Lakṣmī presides over food; annadāna is the offering of Lakṣmī to Nārāyaṇa in the form of Vaiśvānara dwelling within the recipient. Kanyādāna is similar: when giving a daughter in marriage, her parents do not give looking at the groom's wealth, position, or salary -- rather, they give with the resolve that the Lakṣmī within the girl be offered to the Nārāyaṇa within the boy. In this way, those who desire mokṣa act without any worldly craving for fruit, with the thought: \"he who makes everything happen is Bhagavān -- this worship is for his pleasure alone.\" This is \"Tat\".",
  "17.26": "Sat-bhāve sādhu-bhāve ca sat iti etat prayujyate |\nPraśaste karmaṇi tathā sat-śabdaḥ pārtha yujyate -- O Pārtha, the word \"sat\" is used in the sense of \"birth\" and \"goodness\"; it is also applied in the sense of \"praiseworthy karma.\"\n\n\"Sat\" carries this one further meaning: sad-bhāva means being of good character -- this is how the word is applied to a person in ordinary usage. When applied to karma, it becomes sat-karma. Karma performed with the spirit of offering to Bhagavān denoted by Oṃkāra, and without any desire for fruit (Tat), is \"sat\"-karma. Whatever karma is performed in relation to Bhagavān should be praśasta -- praiseworthy; that is, if done for selfish ends, people will not approve of it, whereas karma performed with the spirit of tyāga is what is called praśasta karma. Kṛṣṇa's message is that we should come to know the meaning of every such word and live by it, and thereby become true Pārthas. Kṛṣṇa explains the meaning of the word \"sat\" still further in the next verse.",
  "17.27": "Yajñe tapasi dāne ca sthitiḥ sat iti ca ucyate |\nKarma ca eva tat arthīyaṃ sat iti eva abhidhīyate -- Steadfastness in yajña, tapas, and dāna is also called \"sat\"; and karma performed for the sake of that Bhagavān is likewise called \"sat\".\n\n\"Sat\" means worshipping, through yajña, dāna, and tapas, the Bhagavān denoted by the word \"sat\" itself. Thus Bhagavān is \"sat\"; karma performed in relation to him is also \"sat\" (sat-karma); and one who performs karma in relation to him is also \"sat\" (a sajjana). All karma we perform with awareness of Bhagavān, without desire for fruit, in a spirit of offering to him, wishing that he be pleased -- all such karma is sat-karma.",
  "17.28": "Aśraddhayā hutaṃ dattaṃ tapaḥ taptaṃ kṛtaṃ ca yat |\n\nAsat iti ucyate pārtha na ca tat pretya no iha -- O Pārtha, whatever is offered in sacrifice, given in charity, undertaken as austerity, or performed as any other action without faith -- performed under compulsion, or out of fear, without complete conviction -- is called \"asat\". It bears fruit neither in this world nor in the next.\n\nKarma performed without faith in the act itself, without faith in Bhagavān, done under someone's compulsion, or out of fear, without complete conviction -- such action is fruitless. Dāna, tapas, yajña, or any other karma performed without faith bears no fruit here, and none hereafter.\n\nIti saptadaśo'dhyāyaḥ\nThus ends the seventeenth chapter.",
  "18.1": "Chapter Eighteen\n\nIn the previous seventeen chapters Krishna explained what our sādhanā should look like; here he lays that entire message before us in summary form. Along with this, the chapter also completes the account of the threefold guṇas left unfinished in the seventeenth chapter's treatment of sādhanā. This is the conclusion of the Gītā. The chapter opens with Arjuna's question.\n\nArjuna uvāca |\nsaṃnyāsasya mahābāho tattvam icchāmi veditum |\ntyāgasya ca hṛṣīkeśa pṛthak keśiniṣūdana ||1||\n\narjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna said:\n\nsaṃnyāsasya mahābāho tattvam icchāmi veditum |\ntyāgasya ca hṛṣīkeśa pṛthak keśiniṣūdana -- O Mahābāho, I wish to know the truth of saṃnyāsa; and of tyāga, O Hṛṣīkeśa -- their difference. O Keśiniṣūdana, tell me this truth.\n\nKrishna had earlier spoken of saṃnyāsa; then in the previous chapter he spoke of the actions called yajña, dāna and tapas. This leaves us a little confused. So Arjuna asks Krishna what the difference is between karma-tyāga and karma-saṃnyāsa. Though tyāga and saṃnyāsa appear at first glance to mean the same thing, they are in fact different. What is the essential meaning of saṃnyāsa and of tyāga -- how is each to be understood separately? This is Arjuna's question.\n\nHere Arjuna addresses Krishna by three divine names -- Mahābāho, Hṛṣīkeśa and Keśiniṣūdana. This shows the devotion he bears his teacher, and it is clear that he asks this question understanding that Krishna is not merely Vasudeva's son but is Bhagavān himself. \"Mahābāhu\" on the surface means one with long, strong arms that face down enemies. Its inner meaning is the world-protector who restrains the wicked and shelters the virtuous, who dispels the ignorance within everyone. \"Hṛṣīka\" means the senses; the Lord who dwells within and governs all the senses is Hṛṣīkeśa. \"Keśi\" means a ray of the sun -- Vāyudeva.\n\nDwelling in the solar sphere, and in Prāṇadeva, his innermost devotee, fulfilling our deepest wishes and filling us with strength -- that is Bhagavān \"Keśiniṣūdana\". He is the great power who, dwelling within both the brahmāṇḍa and the piṇḍāṇḍa, governs the entire universe. (In most places the word appears as \"Keśiniṣūdana\", though the correct form is Keśiniṣūdana.)",
  "18.2": "Bhagavān uvāca -- Bhagavān said:\n\nkāmyānāṃ karmaṇāṃ nyāsaṃ saṃnyāsaṃ kavayo viduḥ |\nsarvakarmaphalatyāgaṃ prāhus tyāgaṃ vicakṣaṇāḥ -- Giving up desire-driven actions is what the wise call \"saṃnyāsa\". Giving up only the fruit of all actions is what the discerning call \"tyāga\".\n\nGiving up actions performed out of desire -- this the wise call saṃnyāsa. Giving up only the fruit of every action -- this the discerning call tyāga. The saṃnyāsa spoken of here applies to every seeker, including householders, not only to formal renunciates. Saṃnyāsa is of two kinds: giving up the fruit of action, and giving up action itself when it is driven by desire. Here tyāga does not mean giving up action -- rather, whatever action one performs, it means performing it without desiring any fruit at all. Karma-tyāga therefore does not mean giving up Veda-study, giving up sandhyāvandana, giving up vows and observances. It means performing right action without desiring its fruit. Whatever the action, perform it for the Lord's pleasure -- not out of desire for fruit.\n\nTwo special words are used here -- kavayaḥ and vicakṣaṇāḥ. Those who know the connection between word and meaning are \"kavis\"; those who know that meaning and, having realized it, live it out are \"vicakṣaṇas\". \"The kavis and the vicakṣaṇas give this account of saṃnyāsa and tyāga,\" says Krishna.",
  "18.3": "tyājyaṃ doṣavat iti eke karma prāhuḥ manīṣiṇaḥ |\n\nyajñadānatapaḥkarma na tyājyam iti ca apare -- Some wise men say that action, being tainted with fault, should be given up. Others say that the actions called yajña, dāna and tapas should not be given up.\n\nRegarding karma-tyāga, one scripture seems to say one thing and another something else -- so it appears to us. But the inner substance of every scripture is one. This becomes clear once we understand what tyāga truly means. Learned men of understanding say: \"Action should be given up.\" Certain followers of the Veda say: \"Yajña, dāna and tapas should be performed.\" \"As long as you have breath, worship the Lord through agnihotra,\" says the Yajurveda. To properly grasp what is said here, we must look at the meaning of the word tyāga from various angles. In the verses that follow, Krishna gives a beautiful account of this.",
  "18.4": "niścayam śṛṇu me tatra tyāge bharatasattama |\ntyāgaḥ hi puruṣavyāghra trividhaḥ samprakīrtitaḥ -- O Bharatasattama, hear from me my decision on this matter of tyāga. O Puruṣavyāghra, tyāga has been declared to be of three kinds.\n\n\"I shall tell you my settled view on the matter of tyāga,\" says Krishna. Just as yajña, dāna and tapas are each of three kinds, so too is tyāga of three kinds; this Krishna explains to Arjuna. Here Krishna addresses Arjuna as Bharatasattama and Puruṣavyāghra. On the surface, Arjuna is Bharatasattama because he is eminent among the line of kings of the Bharata dynasty. The inner meaning of this address is: one devoted to Bhagavān and to knowledge, full of bhakti, is Bharatasattama. Likewise Puruṣavyāghra means the foremost among puruṣas -- those who let their mind flow toward the Lord are puruṣas, and the foremost among them is puruṣaśreṣṭha, meaning the foremost among the wise. We too must become Bharatasattamas and Puruṣavyāghras in our lives; only then does the Lord's message reach us.",
  "18.5": "niścayam śṛṇu me tatra tyāge bharatasattama |\ntyāgaḥ hi puruṣavyāghra trividhaḥ samprakīrtitaḥ -- O Bharatasattama, hear from me my decision on this matter of tyāga. O Puruṣavyāghra, tyāga has been declared to be of three kinds.\n\n\"I shall tell you my settled view on the matter of tyāga,\" says Krishna. Just as yajña, dāna and tapas are each of three kinds, so too is tyāga of three kinds; this Krishna explains to Arjuna. Here Krishna addresses Arjuna as Bharatasattama and Puruṣavyāghra. On the surface, Arjuna is Bharatasattama because he is eminent among the line of kings of the Bharata dynasty. The inner meaning of this address is: one devoted to Bhagavān and to knowledge, full of bhakti, is Bharatasattama. Likewise Puruṣavyāghra means the foremost among puruṣas -- those who let their mind flow toward the Lord are puruṣas, and the foremost among them is puruṣaśreṣṭha, meaning the foremost among the wise. We too must become Bharatasattamas and Puruṣavyāghras in our lives; only then does the Lord's message reach us.\n\nyajñadānatapaḥkarma na tyājyaṃ kāryam eva tat |\n\nyajño dānaṃ tapaścaiva pāvanāni manīṣiṇām ||5||\n\nyajña dāna tapaḥ karma na tyājyam kāryam eva tat |\nyajñaḥ dānam tapaḥ ca eva pāvanāni manīṣiṇām -- The actions called yajña, dāna and tapas are not to be given up; they must be performed. Yajña, dāna and tapas purify the wise.\n\nThe yajña, dāna and tapas that are always forms of worshiping the Lord must never be given up. They must always be performed. There is no more auspicious a thing than these. All three of them are acts that sanctify our life. There is no act more sacred than these anywhere in the world. Every other act is contained within these three acts. So every single act in our life must become yajña-dāna-tapas.",
  "18.6": "etāni api tu karmāṇi saṅgam tyaktvā phalāni ca |\nkartavyāni iti me pārtha niścitam matam uttamam -- But, Pārtha, these actions must be performed giving up attachment and giving up the longing for fruit -- this is my settled and highest conviction.\n\nThe root fault that arises when we perform an action is the ego-sense of \"I did it, it happened because of me.\" Along with this is presenting the Lord with our own list of desired fruits and performing action out of that longing for fruit. These are the great faults within action. If action is done with ego, it is wasted, for the Lord does not come to where there is ego. Then there is our performing action out of desire for fruit. If we perform action from desire for fruit and, by chance, the fruit we wanted does not come about, our interest in that action recedes; we come to feel hostility toward God. This is the calamity that comes of performing action out of longing for fruit. \"This is my settled view. What you must give up is not action, but the fruit of action and the interest-driven ego that says 'I did it,'\" says Krishna. This is the supreme conclusion of the doctrine of karma. Giving up ego, giving up kāmya-karma (action done purely for its fruit), and giving up the longing for fruit within action -- this alone is true tyāga, not the giving up of niyata-karma (prescribed, obligatory action). This is the settled doctrine.",
  "18.7": "niyatasya tu saṃnyāsaḥ karmaṇo nopapadyate |\nmohāt tasya parityāgas tāmasaḥ parikīrtitaḥ ||7||\n\nniyatasya tu saṃnyāsaḥ karmaṇaḥ na upapadyate |\nmohāt tasya parityāgaḥ tāmasaḥ parikīrtitaḥ -- Renouncing prescribed action is never right. Giving it up out of delusion is declared to be tāmasa tyāga.\n\nWe must never at any time abandon our niyata-karma, our prescribed duty. If, from wrong understanding, we abandon prescribed action, that is called tāmasa tyāga. Here \"niyata-karma\" means whatever action is fixed for one's own capacity as a jīva. As we know, not everyone may perform every action. Take yajña, for example: ascetics may not offer oblations into fire; brahmacārins may not perform full agnihotra. But ascetics may perform jñāna-yajña -- that is their niyata-karma. The yajña that everyone, at all times, may perform is nāma-yajña, the remembrance of the Lord's name; this is the niyata-karma applicable to all in the matter of yajña. As for dāna, brahmacārins cannot give kanyādāna -- that must be done by a householder; for a householder, kanyādāna is niyata-karma. The dāna that everyone may give is abhaya-dāna and jñāna-dāna. Third is tapas: not everyone can perform every tapas -- some require Vedic initiation. But the tapas everyone may perform is fasting and similar vows and observances; any human being may perform this tapas. Thus whatever action is niyata for a person, that action must be performed. In niyata-karma there is no question at all of desiring fruit. Krishna gives us this caution through this verse -- that abandoning niyata-karma out of wrong understanding is called tāmasa tyāga.",
  "18.8": "duḥkham ity eva yat karma kāyakleśa bhayāt tyajet |\nsa kṛtvā rājasaṃ tyāgaṃ naiva tyāgaphalaṃ labhet ||8||\n\nduḥkham iti eva yat karma kāya kleśa bhayāt tyajet |\nsaḥ kṛtvā rājasam tyāgam na eva tyāga phalam labhet -- Whatever action one gives up out of fear of the fatigue it brings the body, thinking \"this is troublesome\" -- having performed such a tyāga, one is rājasa-tyāga, and one gains no fruit of tyāga at all.\n\nKnowing full well that we must perform our niyata-karma, giving up action because the mind or body would be pained by it is called rājasa-tyāga. For example, giving up an action out of the mind's intense craving to watch some television serial; not performing sandhyāvandana because it is cold -- these and the like are rājasa-tyāga. If one performs such rājasa-tyāga, one does not gain the true fruit of tyāga.",
  "18.9": "kāryam ity eva yat karma niyataṃ kriyate arjuna |\nsaṅgaṃ tyaktvā phalaṃ ca eva saḥ tyāgaḥ sāttvikaḥ mataḥ -- O Arjuna, the niyata-karma performed simply because it is a duty, with attachment and the longing for fruit given up -- that is held to be sāttvika tyāga.\n\nOur mind must reach the point where it feels it is not possible to leave niyata-karma undone. When sattva-guṇa fills the mind to this degree, one does not feel like giving up any action at all -- there the longing for fruit does not exist at all. Performing action always with the feeling of offering it to the Lord, joyfully, with no ego whatsoever, having understood the significance of every action, performing action as niyata -- this is sāttvika-tyāga. Here what is given up is not action but the fruit of action.\n\nThus Krishna gives the simplest answer to our confusions. In sum: performing niyata-karma without longing for fruit, with ego given up, is sāttvika-tyāga; giving up niyata-karma out of laziness, thinking it troublesome, is rājasa-tyāga; and giving up niyata-karma from wrong understanding, thinking \"it is all superstition,\" is tāmasa-tyāga.",
  "18.10": "na dveṣṭi akuśalam karma kuśale na anuṣajjate |\ntyāgī sattvasamāviṣṭaḥ medhāvī chinnasaṃśayaḥ -- He neither hates action that brings no joy, nor clings excessively to action that brings joy. Such a one is a sāttvika tyāgī. He is wise, and his doubts are cut away.\n\nThis verse tells how a sāttvika tyāgī views his own actions. If some past action of ours produces a bad result today, or some other past action produces a good result, the sāttvika tyāgī does not trouble himself over it. He does not exult saying \"good has come,\" nor does he sink saying \"bad has come.\" He does not even hate an action that brings sorrow. Just as medicine is bitter, when good action becomes somewhat painful he does not worry over it.\n\nThe sāttvika tyāgī's mind is filled with sattva-guṇa. Tāmasa and rājasa concerns never arise for him. Surrendering everything to the all-knowing, all-powerful Lord, he performs his actions; through this his mind remains wholly absorbed in the Lord. Being wise (medhāvī), he is watchful of every step of his life. The mind of such a one, settled in the Lord, is never touched by any confusion. Outwardly the conduct of such people looks ordinary, but the intention behind their action is entirely different. They perform their actions with the understanding: \"Lord, You are making me act -- I am [only] acting.\"",
  "18.11": "nahi dehabhṛtā śakyaṃ tyaktuṃ karmāṇi aśeṣataḥ |\nyaḥ tu karma phala tyāgī saḥ tyāgī iti abhidhīyate -- As long as one bears a body it is not possible to give up all actions entirely. He who gives up the fruit of action is the one truly called a tyāgī.\n\nHaving been born human, we must perform action from birth until death; it is not possible to live giving up action altogether. What we must give up is not action -- it is the fruit of action. One who performs action in this way, and gives up the fruit of action, is called a karma-tyāgī.",
  "18.12": "aniṣṭam iṣṭaṃ miśraṃ ca trividhaṃ karmaṇaḥ phalam |\nbhavati atyāginām pretya na tu saṃnyāsinām kvacit -- The fruit of action is of three kinds -- desired, undesired, and a mixture of the two. This falls to those who do not give up the fruit of action, after death. It never touches those who have renounced it.\n\nPerforming action may lead to happiness, or to sorrow, or to a fruit that mixes happiness and sorrow. For one who performs action without giving up the fruit of action, this falls to him after death; he becomes entangled in action and its fruit, and is caught again in the bond of saṃsāra. But for one who has no longing at all for the fruit of action, this never touches him. Whatever comes to him is the Lord's prasāda; for him there is no distinction of desired and undesired. This is the path of mokṣa. For example: one who forgets the Lord and wages war out of desire for kingdom and power becomes the cause of the loss of life that occurs in that war; he must bear this burden of sin after death. But one who wages war purely out of duty, without any self-interest, offering everything to the Lord, is touched by no trace of sin at all.",
  "18.13": "pañca etāni mahābāho kāraṇāni nibodha me |\nsāṅkhye kṛta ante proktāni siddhaye sarva karmaṇām -- O Mahābāho, learn from me these five causes, declared in the doctrine of Sāṅkhya, for the accomplishment of every action.\n\nMany of us have a question: \"What is wrong in saying 'I did' whatever work we did?\" To find the answer to this question we must know the five causes that lie behind the happening of any act. Krishna explains to Arjuna these five causes declared in the Vedic Sāṅkhya-śāstra, the doctrine of knowledge.\n\nHere Krishna addresses Arjuna as Mahābāho. \"Mahā-ba\" means great desires; one who has given up those is Mahābāhu. We too, in our lives, must give up the fruit of action and, performing action solely with the feeling of offering it to the Lord, become Mahābāhus -- this is the resonance behind this address.",
  "18.14": "adhiṣṭhānaṃ tathā kartā karaṇaṃ ca pṛthagvidham |\nvividhāḥ ca pṛthak ceṣṭāḥ daivaṃ ca eva atra pañcamam -- The seat [of action], likewise the doer [Bhagavān], the various instruments, the diverse supporting efforts, and, fifth, destiny [Bhagavān] -- these are here.\n\nEvery action has five causes for its occurrence:\n\n(1) Adhiṣṭhāna: here adhiṣṭhāna means the seat, the ground. Our body is our adhiṣṭhāna. Without this body given for sādhanā, no action can take place at all. This body given for sādhanā that you possess is itself the Lord's prasāda.\n\n(2) Kartā: seated within the body, the one who governs our actions is the Kartā -- Bhagavān. He is independent. The jīva, who is dependent upon him, is not independent, but the jīva does possess willed action.\n\n(3) Karaṇas: our senses. Our senses are governed by the devatās. The lord of the senses is Bhagavān (Hṛṣīkeśa). The senses are themselves the Lord's great prasāda to us.\n\n(4) The various supporting means: for any action to occur, many supporting means suited to it are needed.\n\n(5) Daivam: every single one of our actions depends upon the Lord's saṅkalpa. This may also be called adṛṣṭa, destiny.\n\nLet us look at the above with an example -- take an excellent speaker. He speaks so engagingly, so full of substance. For him to speak like this he must not be dumb; having a mouth is not enough -- sound must issue from the mouth. Not mere sound -- the very sound suited to what is to be spoken must come. For that sound to come, speech needs a feeling behind it. That feeling must first flash in the mind; a word suited to that flashed feeling must come; that word must emerge distinctly from the mouth. Space and air are the medium by which sound is born from feeling. Only if all this proceeds smoothly can he speak well. Who is it that causes feeling and word to flash in the mind? Suppose the word suited to what must be said fails to flash? Suppose some switch in our brain simply does not work at that moment? Suppose one were born mute from birth? Suppose one were born into a family with no tradition of learning at all? What role do we play in any of this? When this is understood, the ego \"I did it\" does not arise. With this awareness one can live among all, like all, without becoming attached.",
  "18.15": "śarīra vāk manobhiḥ yat karma prārabhate naraḥ |\nnyāyyaṃ vā viparītaṃ vā pañca ete tasya hetavaḥ -- Whatever action a person undertakes with body, speech and mind -- whether right or wrong -- these five are its causes.\n\nWhatever action we perform with body, speech and mind has behind it the five causes named above. That action may be good or it may be bad. But action occurs only through these five causes.",
  "18.16": "tatra evaṃ sati kartāram ātmānaṃ kevalaṃ tu yaḥ |\npaśyati akṛta buddhitvāt na saḥ paśyati durmatiḥ -- This being so, one who -- from a corrupted understanding -- sees himself, who does nothing, as the sole \"doer\" is a fool. Such fools understand nothing at all.\n\nWhen these five causes are thus the true actors in our action, to remain unaware of this and take pride saying \"I did it\" is meaningless. Those who think in this way are fools. Such fools have understood nothing.",
  "18.17": "yasya na ahaṃkṛtaḥ bhāvaḥ buddhiḥ yasya na lipyate |\nhatvā api sa imān lokān na hanti na nibadhyate -- One who has no sense of \"I did it,\" whose understanding is not tainted -- even if he kills these people, he kills no one; he is not bound.\n\nWhatever action one who has given up the fruit of action performs, it does not bind him. When we perform action without clinging to \"it was done by me,\" without any longing for fruit whatever, the fruit of that action never touches us. If we look at the situation of the Mahābhārata war -- war came upon the Pāṇḍavas as something unavoidable. They never desired that war should happen; they never thought of winning the war and seizing power. But the situation of having to fight came and stood before them, unavoidably. They performed it, thinking \"this is the Lord's will,\" without reflecting on the consequences of the war, with the feeling of offering it to the Lord. In this war that thus came upon them, they had to kill countless warriors. But they performed their dutiful action without longing for fruit, without ego. \"Thus, even though the occasion arose to kill many people on the battlefield with a detached mind, the sin of that killing does not touch the killer,\" says Krishna. But the crucial point here is this: not for a single moment, mentally, should distrust of the Lord arise in us; with complete surrender, with settled conviction, we must perform whatever action has fallen to our lot without longing for fruit. When we perform action offering everything at the Lord's feet, with the awareness that \"I am an instrument in the Lord's hand,\" the bondage of action does not touch us. It is only because the jīva believes \"I did it\" that the fruit of action strikes him.\n\nHere a question may arise for us. The jīva has no independent doership; action proceeds according to the Lord's will. This being so, why is there injunction and prohibition for the jīva at all? Why does scripture say \"do this, do not do that\"? Krishna answers this question in the next verse.",
  "18.18": "jñānaṃ jñeyaṃ parijñātā trividhā karma codanā |\nkaraṇaṃ karma kartā iti trividhaḥ karmasaṃgrahaḥ -- The purpose behind the injunction of action is threefold: the awareness that something must be done, the action to be known in order to do it, and the doer who knows and acts. [The Lord's own prompting, which is the cause of action, is his very nature; it is thus, being forward-facing, in the form of awareness, that which all must know, and that which knows all.] The cause of action, too, is comprised of three: the instrument, the doing, and the doer.\n\nInjunction and prohibition exist for our knowledge. The injunction of action exists so that \"I\" should come to know the Lord. Action is performed not for the sake of action but for the sake of knowledge. Behind the method of action lies compassion. To a world without knowledge, the Lord's knowledge -- and action, in support of that knowledge. The prompting behind action is threefold: jñāna, jñeya and jñātā -- the awareness that an act must be done; the action that must be known in order to perform that act; and the one who, having known the action, performs it. That we must come to know the Lord is the root prompting behind action. We act in order to shed our own ignorance and gain knowledge; to grow our awareness; so that that awareness may turn toward the Lord. The scriptures gave us the injunction and prohibition of action solely so that we might become possessed of this awareness.\n\nThe one who prompts the jīva with the urge that action must be performed is the Lord. The jīva acts accordingly. What any given jīva is meant to become is a matter tied to that jīva's own inherent nature; but the jīva itself has no power to manifest it. The Lord prompts within him according to the fitness of that jīva's nature. Through the Lord's prompting the jīva's fitness becomes manifest, and accordingly the jīva engages in action. Here everything is, at root, the Lord's own nature. The Lord's knowledge is the Lord's nature; the Lord's action is the Lord's nature; the Lord's prompting is the Lord's nature. The Lord's own nature is working directly upon us. He himself is the jñāna, he himself is the jñeya, he himself is the jñātā. Therefore his prompting is of the nature of jñāna; his prompting is of the nature of jñātā; his prompting is jñeya. Thus, through the prompting of the Lord who is of the nature of knowledge, the jīva acts, and everything proceeds under his control.\n\nIn sum, the causes of action, stated concisely, are of three kinds: the instruments such as the body and senses; the doing (karma); and the doer (the jīva, the Lord, and destiny).\n\nHaving earlier given ample detail about the three guṇas, Krishna here explains the science of the threefold guṇas that is so essential to our life.",
  "18.19": "jñānaṃ karma ca kartā ca tridhā eva guṇabhedataḥ |\nprocyate guṇasaṃkhyāne yathāvat śṛṇu tāni api -- Knowledge, action and the doer are each declared, according to the difference of guṇa, to be of three kinds in the reckoning of the guṇas [in the Vedic Sāṅkhya-śāstra]. Listen to these as they truly are.\n\nThe three principal elements present in everyone's life are jñāna (awareness), karma (action) and kartā (the doer). Whatever we do, we do it out of some understanding -- that is jñāna; the \"doing\" that follows from that understanding is karma; that which is dependent upon jñāna and karma is the kartā. All three of these fall under the threefold division of the guṇas, and each is again of three kinds. Wherever the guṇas are examined in scripture, this matter is stated -- and it is stated in the supreme Sāṅkhya that the Lord, as Kapila-Vāsudeva, gave to the world. This is the most excellent of the truths first taught by Kapila-Vāsudeva, existing since ancient times, that had to be told to the world. Without the three guṇas there is no life at all. If we do not understand this triple guṇa-nature that pervades every facet of our life, we cannot correct our life, nor can we advance toward the Lord who is beyond the three guṇas. \"I shall tell you exactly this most ancient, most fundamental truth -- listen to it attentively,\" says Krishna to Arjuna.",
  "18.20": "sarvabhūteṣu yena ekaṃ bhāvaṃ avyayam īkṣate |\navibhaktaṃ vibhakteṣu tat jñānaṃ viddhi sāttvikam -- That by which one sees, in all beings, one unchanging reality, undivided among the divided -- know that awareness to be sāttvika.\n\nAs the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (4.5.11) says: \"asya mahato bhūtasya niḥśvasitam\" -- meaning, the entire world is the breath-exhalation of that Great Being. The Viṣṇu Sahasranāma says: \"eko viṣṇur mahad bhūtaṃ pṛthag bhūtāny anekaśaḥ\" -- meaning, Viṣṇu alone is the one great consciousness (mahā-bhūta); the lesser consciousnesses (bhūtas) are many. In this verse of the Gītā, Krishna, speaking of sāttvika awareness, says: \"In this mutually distinct, no-one-alike, singular world of living beings (sarva-bhūteṣu), free of all distinction, an undivided truth abides, wholly present within the full play of the guṇas. That awareness to which this truth becomes visible -- the Lord, one within infinity, free of the three guṇas -- is sāttvika.\"",
  "18.21": "pṛthak tvena tu yat jñānaṃ nānābhāvān pṛthagvidhān |\nvetti sarveṣu bhūteṣu tat jñānaṃ viddhi rājasam -- The awareness that sees difference in the Lord's power, that understands that various divine powers are working separately in different beings [awareness that recognizes only the differences among all beings, that knows one is not like another, but lacks the consciousness of the Lord -- mere material fact-awareness] -- know that to be rājasa.\n\nBelief in many gods, without unified devotion, is rājasa awareness. Such people lack the consciousness of the one undivided ultimate reality. They worship different deities on different occasions. Today's astrology, too, is prompting people down this wrong path -- worshipping one deity for one planetary affliction and another for another, without the awareness of the one undivided Lord, is rājasa awareness. Whatever the worship, if it is performed with the awareness of the all-controlling Lord, that worship becomes sāttvika. When, instead, one worships various deities as separate, that same worship becomes rājasa.",
  "18.22": "yat tu kṛtsnavat ekasmin kārye saktam ahaitukam |\natattvārthavat alpaṃ ca tat tāmasam udāhṛtam -- Clinging to some trivial matter as though it were everything [taking the unchanging jīva itself to be the unchanging Lord, thinking the whole universe a single jīva's fantasy], baseless, grasping no real truth [grasping that the universe is unreal], petty understanding -- this is declared to be tāmasa.\n\nTāmasa nature has many faces. Taking what is seen as many to actually be one; refusing to accept a Lord distinct from the jīva; holding that no real object exists at all in this world; ignorance, and, from that, inverted knowledge; imagining oneself a great knower despite having no real understanding; knowing some one matter and thinking \"I have understood everything\" -- these and the like constitute tāmasa awareness. We must know the limits of our own knowledge and make constant effort, every moment, to learn a truth beyond it -- otherwise we become tāmasa. For the Dvaita doctrine to be correctly understood by us, we must also have read Advaita and Viśiṣṭādvaita. For Hindu thought to be understood, we need the awareness of what Islam and Christianity teach. Apart from this, the deluded understanding of one who thinks \"what I believe alone is true, everything else is false\" is called tāmasa. A constant search for truth, a thirst for knowledge -- this alone can carry us toward sāttvika-ness. The foolish understanding that confines knowledge to one's own worldly, transactional life; that fails to recognize the sāttvika knowledge present in another; that makes one live out one's practice devoid of the awareness of the Paramātman -- this is tāmasa.",
  "18.23": "niyatam saṅgarahitam arāga dveṣataḥ kṛtam |\naphalaprepsunā karma yat tat sāttvikam ucyate -- Prescribed action performed without attachment, without either love or hate, by one with no desire for fruit -- that is called sāttvika.\n\nWhen we perform the prescribed action that has come to us in the form of worship of the Lord, it becomes sāttvika action. In this action there is no attachment or aversion whatever; no longing for fruit; no clinging of any kind. Action performed with faith and devotion, with detachment, discharging duty without deceit, with the feeling of offering it to the Lord, is sāttvika action.",
  "18.24": "yat tu kāma īpsunā karma sa ahaṅkāreṇa vā punaḥ |\nkriyate bahula āyāsam tat rājasam udāhṛtam -- Action performed out of desire for fruit, or from ego, wearying and taxing the body -- this is declared to be rājasa.\n\nThe work being done may itself be a good work. But when many desires and the ego of \"I did it\" enter behind it, it becomes rājasa action. Acting to solve some problem of ours, acting with a longing for many kinds of fruit while performing it, taking pride afterward in \"I did it\" -- these are the marks of rājasa action. Action performed by chasing some desire one's whole life, longing for tomorrow's happiness while forgetting today's happiness that already exists, wearying the body -- this is rājasa action.",
  "18.25": "anubandhaṃ kṣayaṃ hiṃsām anapekṣya ca pauruṣam |\nmohāt ārabhyate karma yat tat tāmasam ucyate -- Action begun out of delusion, without regard for consequence, loss, harm, or one's own capacity -- this is called tāmasa.\n\nAction begun without knowing one's own capacity, without any thought of what will follow, out of longing for fruit, rashness, ego, and hatred -- action harmful to society, performed forgetting the Lord -- this is tāmasa action. The best illustration of this is the actions performed by Duryodhana. Action performed without thought for the disaster that will follow, out of the ill will of wishing harm upon another, is tāmasa. Even good action, when done with a bad intention, becomes tāmasa.\n\nHaving explained the threefold classification of jñāna and karma, Krishna next explains the threefold classification of the kartā.",
  "18.26": "mukta saṅgaḥ anahaṃvādī dhṛti utsāha samanvitaḥ |\nsiddhi asiddhyoḥ nirvikāraḥ kartā sāttvikaḥ ucyate -- One free from attachment, who does not boast \"I did it,\" steadfast and full of enthusiasm, unmoved by success or failure -- such a doer is called sāttvika.\n\nThe sāttvika doer clings to nothing. Just as it is customary to offer at the close of any worship \"śrī kṛṣṇārpaṇam astu,\" so too the sāttvika performs every action of his life with devotion to the Lord, offering it to him. In him there is no ego of \"I did it\" whatever. Whatever the task, he performs it with the utmost enthusiasm. When a task he has undertaken does not succeed, he does not sink; when it succeeds, he does not soar. He performs everything unmoved. This is what the Ācāryas have beautifully expressed in the Dvādaśa-stotra:\n\nkuru bhuṅkṣva ca karma nijaṃ niyataṃ haripādavinamradhiyā satataṃ |\nharir eva paro harir eva guruḥ harir eva jagatpitṛmātṛgatiḥ ||\n\nPerform your own share of work; partake of what comes; let remembrance of Hari's feet never fail -- this is the root mantra of a sāttvika's life.",
  "18.27": "rāgī karmaphala prepsuḥ lubdhaḥ hiṃsā ātmakaḥ aśuciḥ |\nharṣa śoka anvitaḥ kartā rājasaḥ parikīrtitaḥ -- Attached, longing for the fruit of action, greedy, harmful by nature, unclean, given to elation and grief -- such a doer is declared rājasa.\n\nThe rājasa person performs good work, but it is filled with \"elation and grief.\" When his work succeeds he soars to the sky, and when it fails he sinks to the depths. The root cause of this is that the rājasa performs every one of his actions purely out of longing for fruit. He clings to action and discharges his duty in expectation of the fruit of action. Performing his task with greed, without faith, without mental cleanliness while performing action -- these are the nature of the rājasa person.",
  "18.28": "ayuktaḥ prākṛtaḥ stabdhaḥ śaṭhaḥ naikṛtikaḥ alasaḥ |\nviṣādī dīrghasūtrī ca kartā tāmasaḥ ucyate -- Undisciplined, worldly [lacking devotion to the Lord], obstinate, arrogant, deceitful, lazy, given to despair, and a chronic procrastinator -- such a doer is called tāmasa.\n\nThe tāmasa is of many kinds:\n\n(1) Ayuktaḥ: having no faith or conviction in the work one does; having no mental engagement at all with the Lord who governs action, while performing action.\n\n(2) Prākṛtaḥ: having no faith in God whatever; interested only in the material world.\n\n(3) Stabdhaḥ: one who, even when mistaken in the action he performs, does not correct himself, and, without understanding, carries on holding to his wrong belief.\n\n(4) Śaṭhaḥ: one who, even knowing something is wrong, has no wish to correct it -- possessed of the attitude \"why should I bow my head?\"\n\n(5) Naikṛtikaḥ: one who, wearing the mask of goodness, deliberately deceives another for gain.\n\n(6) Alasaḥ: one who knows what is good but is too lazy to do it.\n\n(7) Viṣādī: one who performs a task thinking \"alas, I have to do this,\" and continues to express displeasure even after doing it.\n\n(8) Dīrghasūtrī: one who, thinking \"it can be done some other time,\" postpones work for a long while; one who fails to see another's good qualities and instead brings up some old fault of theirs again and again.\n\nIf a person possesses even one of the qualities named here, he is called tāmasa. For example, the person possessing all these qualities is Duryodhana of the Mahābhārata.",
  "18.29": "buddheḥ bhedam dhṛteḥ ca eva guṇataḥ trividham śṛṇu |\nprocyamānam aśeṣeṇa pṛthaktvena dhanañjaya -- O Dhanañjaya, hear now, in full and each apart, the threefold division, according to guṇa, of buddhi (understanding) and of dhṛti (steadfastness).\n\nIt is in human beings that the body and senses are developed to their fullest measure; hence the human body is called \"pura\" (a city/fortress). For the puruṣa (jīva) dwelling in this body, buddhi and dhṛti are matters of the greatest importance. We must live our lives as people of understanding, not as cowards. This buddhi and dhṛti are each of three kinds -- sāttvika, rājasa and tāmasa. We must not follow the rājasa and tāmasa in our lives. For that, we must know what is rājasa and what is tāmasa. \"I shall tell you the three kinds of buddhi and dhṛti fully, each apart -- listen attentively,\" Krishna says to Arjuna. That we should conquer rājasa and tāmasa dhṛti and become sāttvika, becoming true Dhanañjayas, is the root purpose of Krishna's message here.",
  "18.30": "pravṛttim ca nivṛttim ca kārya akārye bhaya abhaye |\nbandham mokṣam ca yā vetti buddhiḥ sā pārtha sāttvikī -- O Pārtha, the understanding that knows engagement and withdrawal, what should and should not be done, what should and should not be feared, and the path of bondage and the path of release -- that understanding is sāttvika.\n\nThe chief problem we face in life is the confusion of whether or not to do a given thing. Deciding what to take up and what to let go; what is good work and what is bad; knowing what one's own duty is; dharma and adharma; sin and merit; fear and fearlessness; the path of bondage and the path of release -- a firm decision on all of this belongs to the sāttvika mind. This is a consciousness with no trace of self-interest whatsoever; a consciousness that comes when one descends into the depth of knowledge; a consciousness that comes when the intellect (Pārtha) is expanded. It is the discerning consciousness that, having analyzed rightly, leads us to decide upon the right path. Our own conscience never deceives us. Every person's conscience gives a firm decision, and that decision is sāttvika.",
  "18.31": "yayā dharmam adharmam ca kāryam ca akāryam eva ca |\nayathāvat prajānāti buddhiḥ sā pārtha rājasī -- O Pārtha, the understanding that cannot rightly determine what is dharma and what is adharma, what should and should not be done -- that understanding is rājasa.\n\nSometimes mistaking dharma itself for adharma, or adharma for dharma; sometimes falling into confusion over what is right and what is wrong -- this is rājasa understanding. This kind of understanding, useless in life, cannot properly decide what ought and ought not to be done.",
  "18.32": "adharmam dharmam iti yā manyate tamasā āvṛtā |\nsarva arthān viparītān ca buddhiḥ sā pārtha tāmasī -- O Pārtha, the understanding, covered in darkness, that takes adharma to be dharma, and thus grasps every matter inverted -- that understanding is tāmasa.\n\nThe understanding that fails to grasp a thing as it truly is, grasping it instead as the opposite, is tāmasa. Such people grasp sorrow not as sorrow but as happiness -- for example, deluding themselves that drinking is a joy and thereby becoming addicted to it. Understanding that takes knowledge itself for ignorance, holds its own inverted knowledge to be everything, and refuses to listen to what anyone else says, is tāmasa understanding.\n\nWhoever has good understanding also has dhṛti. From ignorance -- fear; from awareness -- fearlessness. Vedānta exists precisely for the sake of fearlessness -- to fill the mind with courage and free a person from fear.\n\nThe surface meaning of \"dhṛti\" is courage, self-confidence, boldness and the like. Its full meaning is far broader. It is the \"dhāraṇā\" spoken of in the eight limbs of yoga (yama, niyama, āsana, prāṇāyāma, pratyāhāra, dhyāna, dhāraṇā and samādhi) -- grasping a matter and holding it fast in the mind is dhṛti. This dhṛti, too, is of three kinds -- sāttvika, rājasa and tāmasa. A beautiful account of this can be found in the next three verses.",
  "18.33": "dhṛtyā yayā dhārayate manaḥ prāṇa indriya kriyāḥ |\nyogena avyabhicāriṇyā dhṛtiḥ sā pārtha sāttvikī -- O Pārtha, the steadfastness by which one holds firm, through unswerving bhakti-yoga, the workings of mind, life-breath and senses so that they do not go astray -- that dhṛti is sāttvika.\n\nThe dhṛti by which mind, body and senses, working in mutual support without going astray, function within bhakti-yoga -- that dhṛti is sāttvika. The mind grasps things as they truly are; the senses and body, in support of that, work with devotion. Here \"yoga\" means the means by which one grasps reality in support of devotion to the Lord -- and that is itself our self-confidence. This has many facets. For example, the courage to call a wrong a wrong. Ordinarily there are many obstacles to declaring a wrong to be wrong -- the first obstacle being excessive deference to others. Whatever supports devotion to the Lord we must state without mercy toward wrongdoing. We must boldly resist what is wrong. Speaking the truth sternly, without misperceiving, without treading a wrong path, without acting contrary to devotion to the Lord, is sāttvika dhṛti. The mind not thinking evil, the body not moving toward evil, the senses not grasping at evil -- everything that supports devotion to the Lord in this way is sāttvika dhṛti.\n\nOrdinarily we can control our body and senses, but controlling the mind so that it does not think evil is a very hard task. Achieving this requires long practice. Knowing all this, we come to see how far we stand from sāttvika dhṛti, and this helps us correct ourselves and move toward it.",
  "18.34": "yayā tu dharma kāma arthān dhṛtyā dhārayate arjuna |\nprasaṅgena phala ākāṅkṣī dhṛtiḥ sā pārtha rājasī -- O Pārtha, the steadfastness by which one, out of excessive attachment, holds fast to dharma, kāma and artha, longing for their fruit -- that dhṛti is rājasa.\n\nBhakti is loving the Lord the way a mother loves her child, selflessly. If longing for fruit enters this love, it is no longer called bhakti but self-interest. The rājasa may act, as far as possible, within the framework of dharma to accomplish his own desired ends. But he holds fast to his purpose out of a longing for fruit -- whether for earning money or from excessive attachment. This is rājasa dhṛti. That we should be free of every trace of the rājasa quality, becoming pure and white (Arjuna) -- purely sāttvika -- is Krishna's message.",
  "18.35": "yayā svapnam bhayam śokam viṣādam madam eva ca |\nna vimuñcati durmedhā dhṛtiḥ sā pārtha tāmasī -- O Pārtha, the steadfastness of the foolish, by which one cannot let go of sleep, fear, grief, despondency and arrogance -- that dhṛti is tāmasa.\n\nOne with a tāmasa disposition in perception is dull-witted, imprudent. He never arrives at good perceptions. When a good matter is being discussed he is dozing. He is always in fear (for example, taking bribes, and then living in fear one's whole life because of it). There is no liveliness, no enthusiasm, no triumph in his life. The unscriptural resolve \"I am the best, everyone else is inferior\" is tāmasa dhṛti.",
  "18.36": "sukham tvidānīm trividham śṛṇu me bharatarṣabha |\nabhyāsād ramate yatra duḥkhāntam ca nigacchati ||36||\n\n[The padaccheda, meaning and explanation of these two verses (18.36 and 18.37) are given together in the next verse (18.37).]",
  "18.37": "sukham tu idānīm trividham śṛṇu me bharatarṣabha |\nabhyāsāt ramate yatra duḥkhāntam ca nigacchati |\n\nyat tat agre viṣam iva pariṇāme amṛta upamam |\ntat sukham sāttvikam proktam ātma buddhi prasādajam -- O Bharatarṣabha, hear from me now of the threefold happiness: that which delights through habituation, that which brings an end to sorrow, that which is at first unpleasant like poison and in the end brings comfort like nectar -- such happiness is sāttvika. This is the happiness that comes through the Lord's grace when one's own nature has been clarified.\n\nA human being, taken as a whole, desires happiness in life; happiness is the final goal of his life. But what is true happiness? Happiness too is of three kinds -- sāttvika, rājasa and tāmasa. \"At the close of my account of the threefold nature, hear the threefold account of happiness,\" says Krishna. We should note the many messages Vyāsa has given us behind the epithet \"Bharatarṣabha\" used here. When one becomes rata -- devoted -- in bhakti to the Lord, one becomes eminent; when, like Bharata, without any desire for worldly happiness, one loves Śrī Rāmacandra fully; when, holding guru-bhakti toward Vāyu (Hanumān), foremost among those devoted to the Lord, one discharges one's action with the feeling of offering it to the Lord -- then we become \"Bharatarṣabhas\". One who is a Bharatarṣabha alone can come to know what true happiness is and experience it.\n\nIf any matter appears pleasant from the very outset, it is not sāttvika happiness. What is gained without effort, without hardship, is the happiness of delusion. Sāttvika happiness is at first in the form of hardship. The excellent illustration of this is the churning of the ocean (samudra-manthana). There, what came first was not nectar but poison; the true nectar was obtained only at the end. As the saying goes, \"muddy the hands and the curds come to the mouth\" -- effort brings happiness. The medicine we drink is bitter like poison, but the health that comes from it is sweet like nectar.\n\nKrishna here uses a rare and remarkable epithet -- \"ātma buddhi prasādajam\". Ātma-prasāda means the Lord's grace. Sāttvika happiness does not come simply because we wish for it; it is something that comes through the Lord's grace. To obtain it we must have a transparent, pure mind. It does not come from outside but wells up from within. Thus, when the Lord's grace and the mind's clarity are present together, one is able to experience the happiness of one's own inherent nature.",
  "18.38": "viṣaya indriya saṃyogāt yat tat agre amṛta upamam |\npariṇāme viṣam iva tat sukham rājasam smṛtam -- Happiness that arises from the contact of sense-object and sense, that seems at first like nectar, exciting, and in the end proves like poison, dangerous -- such happiness is called rājasa.\n\nWhatever gives great pleasure when first experienced, and whose consequence afterward is sorrowful, is rājasa happiness. This is external happiness -- happiness gained momentarily through the contact between external objects and our senses. Most of the crime we see reported today is the consequence of pursuing this kind of momentary happiness. There is nothing wrong in experiencing the happiness of sense-objects, but it is very important to experience it and then rise above it. The ancients declared two things above all must be conquered: one is addiction to food, the other, sexual craving. In sum, conquering the momentary happiness of the senses is of great importance.",
  "18.39": "yat agre ca anubandhe ca sukham mohanam ātmanaḥ |\nnidrā ālasya pramāda uttham tat tāmasam udāhṛtam -- Happiness that stupefies the self, both at the beginning and throughout, arising from sleep, sloth and heedlessness -- that is declared to be tāmasa.\n\nThat which pushes a person out of the real world into delusion, casts him into the net of infatuation, makes him a fool, and creates the illusion of happiness -- that is tāmasa happiness. In it there is no happiness at the beginning, and none at the end. Examples include drinking, smoking, and every other bad habit. The happiness of laziness, the happiness of sleep, the happiness of heedlessness -- these are external happiness gained by killing the inner mind. This is tāmasa. Take sleep, for instance. When a person is an infant, twenty hours of sleep and four hours of waking are needed. But for a mature person, four hours of sleep suffice; with practice, one may remain awake for twenty. But tāmasa happiness creates the delusion that sleeping the whole day is the highest happiness, and thereby leads a person astray.",
  "18.40": "na tat asti pṛthivyāṃ vā divi deveṣu vā punaḥ |\nsattvaṃ prakṛtijaiḥ muktaṃ yat ebhiḥ syāt tribhiḥ guṇaiḥ -- There exists no being at all -- not on earth, nor among the gods in heaven -- free from these three guṇas born of prakṛti.\n\nHaving given a detailed picture of the three guṇas, Krishna now describes the nature of the jīva. Whether on this earth or in any realm of the gods up to Satyaloka, every living being is subject to these three guṇas born of prakṛti. Every single jīva is subject to these three natures, and every jīva is a mixture of the three guṇas. No one is a hundred percent sāttvika, rājasa or tāmasa. The entire world is a mixture of the three.\n\nThe nature of the jīva cannot be directly divided into three simple categories of sāttvika, rājasa and tāmasa. For this reason Krishna explains it through the four varṇas instead. As explained earlier, cāturvarṇya is not a \"division of jāti (caste)\" -- it is a division of varṇa. Neither the Bhagavad Gītā nor the Veda lays emphasis on caste division. Caste division appears in certain dharma-śāstras, not in the Veda or the Gītā. Jāti is a division made for the ordering of society; varṇa, by contrast, is something stated on the basis of understanding the jīva's nature from the standpoint of psychology.\n\nBroadly speaking: the devas are sāttvika, human beings are rājasa, and the daityas (demons) are tāmasa. But this division does not end there -- within each of these classes there is again a threefold division. For example, among the tāmasa: the lowest of men are tāmasa-sāttvikas; the demon clans are tāmasa-rājasas; and demons like Kālanemi who govern them are tāmasa-tāmasas.\n\nAmong humans, the rājasa-tāmasas are the lowest of men. Rājasa-rājasas are ordinary worldly people who go on forever in worldly life -- they never go toward either dark bondage or toward mokṣa; they simply revolve endlessly in this wheel of saṃsāra. The third group, the rājasa-sāttvikas, are fit for mokṣa. Cāturvarṇya applies only to this third group. So among the mokṣa-worthy rājasa-sāttvikas, there are four kinds: mokṣa-worthy brāhmaṇa, mokṣa-worthy kṣatriya, mokṣa-worthy vaiśya, and mokṣa-worthy śūdra. This is the fourfold division based on the jīva's own nature. There is no distinction here of higher or lower -- all are fit for mokṣa. Depending on the proportion of sattva, rajas and tamas, many further subdivisions can be seen within cāturvarṇya:\n\n(1) Paramahaṃsas (90-part sāttvika): those who have established themselves wholly in the Lord. In them the portion of rajas and tamas is only 10.\n\n(2) Haṃsas (85-part sāttvika): next after the paramahaṃsas. In them the proportion of rajas and tamas is 15-part.\n\n(3) Bahūdakas (80-part sāttvika): they have no fixed dwelling, residing in a different place each day. In them the sāttvika portion is 80, and the rajas-tamas portion is 20.\n\n(4) Kuṭīcakas (75-part sāttvika): like the Bahūdakas, but they reside settled in one dwelling. In them the portion of rajas and tamas is 25.\n\n(5) Vānaprasthas (70-part sāttvika): householders who, once their children have reached maturity, go to the forest to practice sādhanā. In them 30 parts are rajas-tamas, the remaining 70 sāttvika. They still perform agni-karma (fire-rites) but have given up union with women.\n\n(6) Brahmacārins (65-part sāttvika): in them 35 percent is rajas and tamas. Union with women is forbidden to them.\n\n(7) Gṛhasthas (householders): in them the sāttvika portion is 60, the remaining 40 rajas and tamas. They may lead a householder's life.\n\nThe above seven kinds belong to the brāhmaṇa class. In sum, a mokṣa-worthy human being with at least a 60-part sāttvika measure is called a brāhmaṇa.\n\n(8) Kṣatriyas (55:35:10): in them the sāttvika portion is 55 and the rajas portion 35. They are interested in governance; their duty is to protect society by the strength of their arms.\n\n(9) Vaiśyas (40:40:20): in them the sāttvika and rajas portions are equal (40). They are interested in agriculture, dairying, and trade.\n\n(10) Śūdras (35:35:30): they are the foundation of the whole of society. They possess a disposition of service.",
  "18.41": "brāhmaṇa kṣatriya viśām śūdrāṇām ca parantapa |\nkarmāṇi pravibhaktāni svabhāva prabhavaiḥ guṇaiḥ -- O scorcher of foes, the actions of the brāhmaṇa, kṣatriya, vaiśya and śūdra are divided according to the guṇas born of their own nature.\n\nIn the mokṣa-worthy human being, there are, according to the jīva's nature, four kinds of varṇa. Every person's conduct and speech, custom and outlook, accord with their nature; from the difference in nature comes the difference in guṇa and karma. Since this is entirely a matter concerning the jīva's own nature, it has no relation whatever to caste. Caste pertains to the body; varṇa pertains to the very nature of the jīva. [Further detail on cāturvarṇya is given in Chapter 4, Verse 13.] Krishna explains the natural, inherent actions of these four varṇas in the verses that follow.",
  "18.42": "śamaḥ damaḥ tapaḥ śaucaṃ kṣāntiḥ ārjavam eva ca |\njñānaṃ vijñānam āstikyaṃ brahmakarma svabhāvajam -- Steadiness in the Lord, control of the senses, observances such as fasting, purity, forbearance toward even one who has wronged, straightforwardness of conduct and speech, learning, depth of enquiry, and faith in the Lord's existence -- these are the natural, inherent actions of the brāhmaṇa nature.\n\nKrishna explains in this verse what the natural actions of one whose jīva-nature is \"brāhmaṇa\" look like. The dispositions of this varṇa are as follows:\n\n(1) Śamaḥ: the mind being settled in the Lord.\n\n(2) Damaḥ: control over the senses.\n\n(3) Tapaḥ: deep mental reflection upon a matter; vows and observances that support the mind in thinking good thoughts; constant reflection upon scripture.\n\n(4) Śaucam: purity of mind, and, in support of it, outward cleanliness.\n\n(5) Kṣāntiḥ: a mind that forgives even those who have done wrong, that does not become angry.\n\n(6) Ārjava: a clean, untroubled mind; straightforward conduct and speech.\n\n(7) Jñāna: knowledge concerning the Lord.\n\n(8) Vijñāna: deep awareness of the Lord, distinctive knowledge of the Lord's qualities.\n\n(9) Āstikya: proceeding forward with complete confidence in the Lord's existence.\n\nAll these qualities ought to be present in people of every varṇa. But they are present in the greatest measure in those of the brāhmaṇa varṇa. If even one of these qualities is present in lesser measure, one is not called a brāhmaṇa. If, on the other hand, these qualities are found in a kṣatriya to a greater degree than in brāhmaṇas, he is called a rājarṣi (royal sage) -- for example, King Janaka.",
  "18.43": "śauryaṃ tejaḥ dhṛtiḥ dākṣyam yuddhe ca api apalāyanam |\ndānam īśvara bhāvaḥ ca kṣātraṃ karma svabhāvajam -- Valour, majesty, steadfastness, skill, not fleeing even in battle, generosity, and the disposition of a ruler -- these are the natural, inherent actions of the kṣatriya nature.\n\nHere Krishna explains the natural actions of one who is a kṣatriya.\n\n(1) Śauryam: the power to subdue one's opponents.\n\n(2) Tejas: a majesty before which opponents bow their heads the moment they see it.\n\n(3) Dhṛti: the spirit of daring that presses forward undaunted even when life is in danger.\n\n(4) Dākṣyam: skill and cunning in mastering one's enemies.\n\n(5) Yuddhe ca api apalāyanam: the valour to fight without turning one's back, without fleeing, on the battlefield.\n\n(6) Dānam: the giving of charity, performing dāna-dharma.\n\nThese six qualities exist to a great degree in the kṣatriya; they exist to a lesser degree in the other varṇas. Besides these, there is one special quality reserved for kṣatriyas alone: (7) Īśvara-bhāvaḥ: the authority to give fitting punishment to a wrongdoer. This authority does not belong to the other varṇas.",
  "18.44": "kṛṣi gorakṣya vāṇijyaṃ vai śyakarma svabhāvajam |\nparicaryātmakaṃ karma śūdrasya api svabhāvajam -- Agriculture, cattle-rearing and trade are the natural actions of the vaiśya's nature. Service-natured work is likewise the natural action of the śūdra's nature.\n\nThe natural, inherent work of the vaiśya is agriculture, go-rakṣā (cattle-protection, dairying) and trade. Bhīṣmācārya, teaching Dharmarāja the duties of kingship, said: \"The land from which farmers and traders depart for another country, unable to bear the tax-burden, cannot prosper.\" Agriculture, trade and dairying are the backbone of a country. This work is the natural, inherent action of the vaiśya.\n\nIn śūdras, the quality of serving others is of great importance. One without this quality is not truly human. No one belongs to a single varṇa alone; every disposition exists in everyone. Whichever disposition predominates in us, to that varṇa we belong. The śūdra disposition is so essential that without it no other disposition has any worth. Whatever action we perform, we must always perform it with a spirit of service. For instance: a brāhmaṇa should give the gift of knowledge with the feeling of service to society. Without this, it does not deserve to be called a gift of knowledge.\n\nKrishna has given a most fascinating account of varṇa here. We find that in society, and in some ancient texts, the word \"jāti\" is sometimes used for varṇa, and the word \"varṇa\" for jāti. In such contexts we must not confuse ourselves, but understand, in each case, in which sense the word is used. For example, when Dharmarāja asked, \"How should I observe the varṇa system in my rule?\" Bhīṣmācārya told him: \"There is no division of varṇa at all -- all are children of God; a king has no authority to discriminate by varṇa.\" Here \"varṇa\" is used in the sense of \"jāti\".\n\nOrdinarily, in society, children follow the same disposition their parents had. For example, a brāhmaṇa's son finds it easy to follow the brāhmaṇa disposition, and grows up following it. When this happens, it becomes difficult to identify one's true varṇa. It was this difficulty in identifying varṇa that gave rise to the jāti system. The best illustration that jāti and varṇa are distinct is Vedavyāsa, born to a fisherwoman's daughter, whom the whole world acknowledges as a great brāhmaṇa.\n\nToday society no longer follows the custom of identifying one's own jīva-nature and acting accordingly. Instead, parents impose their own wishes upon their children. This is an entirely unscientific custom. Failing to recognize the true talent hidden within children, and insisting that children must learn what the parents themselves have decided, is a tradition contrary to the varṇa system. Each should learn according to their own jīva-nature; then they become skilled in that matter, and society benefits from them.",
  "18.45": "sve sve karmaṇi abhirataḥ saṃsiddhiṃ labhate naraḥ |\nsvakarmaniratah siddhiṃ yathā vindati tat śṛṇu -- A person devoted to his own natural work attains perfection. Hear how one devoted to his own work attains that perfection.\n\nKrishna calls action suited to one's own nature \"svakarma\". \"Do your own action\" means: do the action suited to your own nature. There is no greater dharma than sincerity and devotion to one's own natural work. Living honestly, performing the action natural to one's own nature with the feeling of offering it to the Lord, is the highest dharma of all.\n\nSome of us may ask: \"What is wrong in doing whatever work brings in the most money, regardless of what it is?\" Here Krishna declares this to be wrong. Doing just any work is not living -- it is torment. Living within the work natural to your own nature -- that alone is true living; that alone is the path to mokṣa. Your education should suit your nature; your work should suit your education. When this is so, there is abhirati (complete contentment and joy) in one's work. Performing action joyfully, offered to the Lord in this way, one may attain mokṣa, the final perfection of life. Krishna explains in the verses that follow how perfection comes through action natural to one's own nature.",
  "18.46": "yataḥ pravṛttiḥ bhūtānāṃ yena sarvam idaṃ tatam |\nsvakarmaṇā tam abhyarcya siddhiṃ vindati mānavaḥ -- By worshipping, through his own natural work, the Lord from whom the activity of all beings arises and by whom all this is pervaded, a person attains perfection.\n\nWhen we perform our own natural work as worship of the Lord, it becomes spiritual practice. There is no greater worship than this. Here \"arcanā\" (worship) does not mean the ritual worship performed through a priest in a temple. It is the self-performed worship comprised of sense-control, śama, dama, and all such things together. Thus our own natural work already includes sense-control, devotion to the Lord, and the feeling of offering to him. All of these together make every single act of our own natural work an act of worship of the Lord. Living in this way, our whole life becomes worship of God. When we offer our action to the Lord who gave us our very will and impulse to act, our action itself becomes an act of worship.",
  "18.47": "śreyān svadharmaḥ viguṇaḥ paradharmāt su anuṣṭhitāt |\nsvabhāva niyataṃ karma kurvan na āpnoti kilbiṣam -- One's own dharma, even flawed, is better than another's dharma well performed. Performing the action fixed by one's own nature, one incurs no sin.\n\nPerforming our own svadharma, natural to our own nature, brings no sin even if there is fault in that action. To abandon this and perform paradharma is not right. When Dharmarāja, after the war, sat resolving \"I cannot ascend the throne, I want nothing, I shall go to the forest,\" Vedavyāsa counselled him wisely: \"Just as it would be inappropriate for you to abandon your own natural dharma and go to the forest, it would be equally inappropriate for me to abandon my tapas and come rule a kingdom.\" From this we must understand: rather than performing another's dharma, performing our own natural work, even with its flaws, is the most excellent dharma.",
  "18.48": "sahajaṃ karma kaunteya sadoṣam api na tyajet |\nsarvārambhāḥ hi doṣeṇa dhūmena agniḥ iva āvṛtāḥ -- O Kaunteya, one should not abandon one's inborn work even though it has fault, for all undertakings are covered by fault as fire is covered by smoke.\n\nHowever much fault there may be in the action natural to our own nature and circumstance, we must not abandon that action. Here the Pāṇḍavas' natural dharma is to fight against injustice. In fighting thus, death and injury may occur. But that is not sin. There is not a single action in this world free of fault. Where there is fire there is always smoke. There may be violence in an action, but when it is one's natural dharma, it cannot be set aside. When violence is unavoidable within one's natural dharma, one should not trouble oneself over it. Performing the action that falls to our lot desirelessly, with the feeling of offering it to the Lord, is dharma. This is untouched by sin.\n\nBut merely acting according to one's nature does not by itself grant liberation. Certain further practices are needed behind it; certain further disciplines of mind are needed. Krishna explains these in the verses that follow.",
  "18.49": "asaktabuddhiḥ sarvatra jitātmā vigataspṛhaḥ |\nnaiṣkarmyasiddhiṃ paramāṃ saṃnyāsena adhigacchati -- One whose understanding clings nowhere, who has conquered the self, who is free of longing -- such a one attains, through this \"saṃnyāsa,\" the supreme perfection that grants mokṣa [the perfection that is the destruction of undesirable karma].\n\nTo reach the perfection that is the means to mokṣa [the state free of sinful karma], it is not enough merely to engage in one's own action; we must, beyond that, control our mind, act without any longing for fruit, give up the ego of \"it was accomplished by me,\" and act with the feeling of offering it to the Lord. Ego is the great enemy of spiritual life; we must be watchful of this. In sum: giving up everywhere the ego of \"it was done by me,\" conquering the mind, clearing away the longing for fruit through the understanding, renouncing desires, and offering everything to the Lord while performing one's own action -- this brings one the perfection needed to attain mokṣa.",
  "18.50": "siddhiṃ prāptaḥ yathā brahma tathā āpnoti nibodha me |\nsamāsena eva kaunteya niṣṭhā jñānasya yā parā -- O Kaunteya, hear from me in brief the manner in which one who has attained that perfection reaches the Brahma-tattva [the Śrī-tattva]. This perfection is the final stage of knowledge.\n\nWhen a conduct of life untouched by any wrongdoing (naiṣkarmya-siddhi) takes shape, the Lord comes and settles in our mind. Through this, one may reach Mother Śrī Lakṣmī, and finally reach the Lord. How one reaches the Brahma-tattva, Krishna states next in concise form. Naiṣkarmya-siddhi is the final stage of a person's pursuit of knowledge. To reach this stage we must become Kaunteyas -- persevering in spiritual life the way Kuntī persevered.",
  "18.51": "buddhyā viśuddhayā yuktaḥ dhṛtyā ātmānaṃ niyamya ca |\nśabda ādīn viṣayān tyaktvā rāgadveṣau vyudasya ca -- Endowed with a purified understanding, restraining the self with steadfastness, giving up sense-objects such as sound, and casting aside attraction and aversion --\n\nIn spiritual practice our mind should never think evil. Our understanding must be pure. We must control the mind with great courage, and with extraordinary boldness drive out every evil thought from the mind. The senses are the instruments that stir up the mind. Therefore the cravings of hearing, seeing, touching, smelling and eating must first be given up. One must remain with a mind as clean, as free of attraction and aversion, as a child's.",
  "18.52": "viviktasevī laghu āśī yata vāk kāya mānasaḥ |\ndhyāna yoga paraḥ nityaṃ vairāgyaṃ samapāśritaḥ -- dwelling in solitude, eating lightly, controlled in speech, body and mind, ever devoted to dhyāna-yoga, and firmly grounded in vairāgya --\n\nOne should not mingle in crowds where idle gossip takes place, and should practice, as far as possible, dwelling in solitude. In spiritual practice one should choose as pure a place as possible and practice there. When one overeats, the mind does not function well; therefore one should eat prescribed food at a prescribed time. Controlling speech, controlling bodily activity, controlling the movement of the mind, one should meditate. In life one should love everything while clinging to nothing.",
  "18.53": "ahaṅkāraṃ balaṃ darpaṃ kāmaṃ krodhaṃ parigraham |\nvimucya nirmamaḥ śāntaḥ brahmabhūyāya kalpate -- Giving up the ego of \"I\", coercive force, arrogance, desire, anger and possessiveness, becoming free of \"mine\" and at peace, one becomes fit to attain the Brahma-nature. [Such a one reaches the Śrī-tattva.]\n\nGiving up the ego of \"I did it,\" which runs contrary to remembrance of the Lord; giving up imposing one's own opinion upon another; giving up pride in one's own status; giving up desire and anger; giving up the mentality of holding one's hand out before another -- giving up all these, and giving up the possessiveness of \"I\" and \"mine,\" fixing the mind one-pointedly on the Lord, one may reach the Brahma-tattva. [The mind becomes settled in the Lord; receiving Caturmukha Brahmā's teaching, one reaches Mother Lakṣmī.]",
  "18.54": "brahmabhūtaḥ prasannātmā na śocati na kāṅkṣati |\nsamaḥ sarveṣu bhūteṣu mat bhaktiṃ labhate parām -- One who has become brahma-bhūta [settled in the Śrī-tattva], with a clarified mind, neither grieves nor desires. Equal toward all beings, he attains supreme devotion to me.\n\nOne whose mind is fixed on the Lord has no wants or aversions whatsoever. His mind is clear. In this state, the very highest bhakti grows. He sees the Lord, full of all qualities, present in every single object in the world. Because of this, there is in him no discrimination, no hatred at all.",
  "18.55": "bhaktyā mām abhijānāti yāvān yaḥ ca asmi tattvataḥ |\ntataḥ mām tattvataḥ jñātvā viśate tat anantaram -- Through bhakti he comes to know exactly how great I am, and what I truly am. Having thus known me in truth, he enters into me immediately thereafter.\n\nOne established in this supreme state of bhakti has no wrong understanding whatever. Through the presence of Lakṣmī, he comes to know, in full measure suited to his own jīva-capacity, exactly what the Lord is, what his glory is, what his power is, and the extent of his infinite qualities. \"Having thus known the Lord in truth as he is, he comes and unites with me through the most purified knowledge and bhakti,\" says Krishna.",
  "18.56": "sarva karmāṇi api sadā kurvāṇaḥ mat vyapāśrayaḥ |\nmat prasādāt avāpnoti śāśvataṃ padam avyayam -- Knowing that \"I alone\" am everything, and performing all actions continuously while depending on me, one reaches, through my grace, the eternal, imperishable abode.\n\nOne who knows the Lord to be everything; who, whatever action he performs, keeps the Lord as the goal of that action; and who performs action with the feeling of complete offering to the Lord, reaches, through the Lord's grace, a mokṣa that is never subject to change.",
  "18.57": "cetasā sarvakarmāṇi mayi saṃnyasya mat paraḥ |\nbuddhiyogam upāśritya mat cittaḥ satataṃ bhava -- With inner intent, surrendering all actions unto me, holding me as the highest, and taking refuge in buddhi-yoga, keep your mind fixed on me at all times.\n\nHere Krishna says: \"Know me, offer all action unto me, and through jñāna-yoga keep your mind fixed on me continuously.\" It is a matter of great importance that we perform every action we do having offered it to the Lord. Today this kind of practice in action has all but disappeared. The root cause is ignorance. We perform worship out of longing for some fruit. Sometimes astrologers say there is some planetary affliction -- for instance, \"kuja-doṣa\" (the affliction of Mars). Ordinarily, when astrologers say there is kuja-doṣa, what first occurs to us is \"this planet is troubling me.\" But in truth kuja-doṣa does not mean a fault of Mars -- it is our own fault, our own prārabdha-karma; the planet merely indicates it. We mistakenly think Mars itself is pursuing us. The planets do not exist to trouble us; they never trouble us at all. Rather, they indicate to us the trouble that will come from our own prārabdha. It is the planets' role to forewarn us of coming difficulty, so that we may prepare ourselves mentally to face that misfortune. When we thus learn that prārabdha-karma exists, taking refuge in the Lord to face that problem and press forward on the path of sādhanā is prāyaścitta (expiatory) worship -- it is not a bribe offered to the planets. The navagraha-pūjā, for instance: since the planets indicate our prārabdha-karma, praying to the Lord who is the antaryāmī (indwelling controller) of those planets is navagraha-pūjā.\n\nEven a devotee of Nārāyaṇa is not spared prārabdha-karma. We must experience our own planetary influence according to our own prārabdha. Even the wise encounter hardship. When we lack the capacity to face that sorrow, it is not wrong to pray to the Lord. What is of great importance here is prayer to the Paramātman without any longing for fruit. The disposition behind such worship should be: \"As the fruit of action I performed in a former birth, I have received this misfortune today. You, standing as the antaryāmī of the planets, have shown me this planetary trial before me. My countless salutations to you. Forgive whatever wrong I have unknowingly done; show me the way to face this misfortune, advance on the path of knowledge, and reach you; use me as your instrument to perform your own worship through me; I have taken refuge in you.\" This disposition should underlie such worship. Thus, with the awareness that the Lord is supreme above all, even while worshipping all the various devatās, one should recognize the Lord who is their indwelling controller, and mentally offer one's action to him. This is the true manner in which action ought to be performed.\n\nIn sum: we must keep our mind from wandering elsewhere and settle it firmly in the Lord; we must hold the awareness, settled firmly in our mind, that every action we perform is a form of worship of the Lord and that the Lord himself is causing it to be done through our hands. Our practice and our inner discipline should be full of joy. There should be no pressure, no fear, no longing for fruit in it.",
  "18.58": "mat cittaḥ sarvadurgāṇi mat prasādāt tariṣyasi |\natha cet tvam ahaṅkārāt na śroṣyasi vinaṅkṣyasi -- With your mind fixed on me, you will cross over all difficulties through my grace. But if, out of ego, you will not listen, you will perish.\n\nWhen we direct the mind toward the Lord, fix the Lord in our understanding, make the Lord steady in our chitta, and make remembrance of the Lord permanent even in the subconscious, then all our actions become worship of the Lord. Through this, complete sincerity is expressed in all our dealings, and we gain the capacity to face the difficult situations, hardships and privations that arise in life. Thus the wise person passes effortlessly beyond all the calamities that inevitably arise on his life's journey and moves forward. Living in this way, the Lord protects us from every misfortune that arises in our life. His protective shield always surrounds us. Through this we are saved from every calamity that comes in life. But if, instead, one takes the arrogant view that \"Vedānta is mere talk, I have no faith in it,\" one ruins one's own future with one's own hands and comes to destruction.",
  "18.59": "yat ahaṅkāram āśritya na yotsye iti manyase |\nmithyā eṣaḥ vyavasāyaḥ te prakṛtiḥ tvām niyokṣyati -- If, resting on ego, you think \"I will not fight,\" this resolve of yours is false. Your own nature [the Lord's will] will compel you to it.\n\nWe saw in Chapter One how Arjuna sank down in his chariot saying \"I will not fight.\" Here Krishna says: \"'I will not fight' is a delusion born of your ego. You will end up doing the very thing you say you will not do.\" This is because the power that governs us is prakṛti. Here our merely physical resolve is futile. Action proceeds inevitably according to our own jīva-nature, the hereditary influence that follows from it, the influence of environment, and, governing all of these, the will of the Lord.",
  "18.60": "svabhāvajena kaunteya nibaddhaḥ svena karmaṇā |\nkartuṃ na icchasi yat mohāt kariṣyasi avaśaḥ api tat -- O Kaunteya, you are bound by your own action, born of your own nature. Whatever you, out of delusion, do not wish to do, you will do it anyway, helplessly.\n\nThat we think, out of ego, \"I must do this, I must do that\" is itself a product of our own ignorance. Whatever we may resolve, in the end what we do accords entirely with our own jīva-nature. Its control does not lie with us. It proceeds, without our even realizing it, according to the Lord's will.",
  "18.61": "īśvaraḥ sarvabhūtānām hṛt deśe arjuna tiṣṭhati |\nbhrāmayan sarvabhūtāni yantra ārūḍhāni māyayā -- O Arjuna, the all-powerful Lord dwells in the seat of the heart of every being, whirling all beings, mounted upon the machine [of the world], through his māyā [his own will].\n\nThe all-powerful Lord dwells within the very core of the heart of every jīva. It is that Lord who keeps this wondrous machine called piṇḍāṇḍa and brahmāṇḍa in constant motion. He alone is the master of the brahmāṇḍa and the piṇḍāṇḍa. He is the great consciousness dwelling within all, giving them experience and governing them. All action proceeds according to his will, by the Lord's own glory.",
  "18.62": "tam eva śaraṇaṃ gaccha sarvabhāvena bhārata |\ntat prasādāt parāṃ śāntiṃ sthānaṃ prāpsyasi śāśvatam -- O Bhārata, take refuge in him alone, with your whole being. Through his grace you will attain the supreme peace and the eternal, imperishable abode.\n\nWe must offer ourselves to the all-capable Lord. There should be surrender to the Lord at every stage of our life -- not merely in times of hardship. When, having cultivated sattva-guṇa within us and dispelled darkness, we offer ourselves to the Lord, we come to a vision of the true, complete reality. Once that Lord has opened his eyes and shown grace, no further means is needed for knowledge to come afterward. Truth reveals itself of its own accord, and the very highest bliss becomes ours. Through this, one may reach mokṣa, the very pinnacle of the bliss of knowledge.",
  "18.63": "iti te jñānam ākhyātaṃ guhyāt guhyataraṃ mayā |\nvimṛśya etat aśeṣeṇa yathā icchasi tathā kuru -- Thus have I declared to you this knowledge, more secret than all secrets. Having deliberated on this fully, do as you wish.\n\nKrishna had concluded his teaching of knowledge in the fifteenth chapter. Here, having given the entire teaching of upāsanā (worship/practice), he concludes it, saying: \"This is the secret of secrets in the world. I have told you the innermost secret of spiritual life. Deliberate fully on what I have said and act as you become convinced.\" By telling Arjuna here, \"act as your own jīva-nature and your own conscience tell you,\" Krishna underscores the social principle that we must never impose our own opinion upon another.",
  "18.64": "sarva guhyatamam bhūyaḥ śṛṇu me paramaṃ vacaḥ |\niṣṭaḥ asi me dṛḍham iti tataḥ vakṣyāmi te hitam -- Hear once more from me my supreme word, the most secret of all secrets. Because you are dearly loved by me, I shall tell you what is good for you.\n\nI am telling you once more, O sāttvika one, the essence of the sādhanā I described earlier -- the innermost secret among the matters we ought to know. You, my devotee, are a knower. Knowers are dear to me above all; that is why I am telling it to you once again -- listen:",
  "18.65": "mat manāḥ bhava mat bhaktaḥ mat yājī mām namaskuru |\nmām eva eṣyasi satyaṃ te pratijāne priyaḥ asi me -- Fix your mind on me, be devoted to me, worship me, bow to me. You will come to me alone; I promise you this truth. You are dear to me.\n\nExplaining the essence of sādhanā here, Krishna again states, in the form of a conclusion, what he had explained earlier, gathering it into its essence. Krishna says: \"Fix your mind on me.\" Our exclusive contemplation in sādhanā must be a single, undivided devotion to the Lord alone. With full knowledge that the Lord is supreme, we must remember him. Let there be \"pure love\" for the Lord. Every action we perform should be offered to the Lord out of love. Knowing the truth that \"all salutations to all the gods reach Keśava\" (sarva-deva-namaskāraḥ keśavaṃ pratigacchati), worship the Lord who indwells all. When we see the Lord in this way in every act of worship, \"you will come and reach me alone -- this is the truth, I swear it,\" says Krishna.",
  "18.66": "sarvadharmān parityajya mām ekam śaraṇaṃ vraja |\nahaṃ tvām sarva pāpebhyaḥ mokṣayiṣyāmi mā śucaḥ -- Giving up all lesser dharmas, take refuge in me alone. I shall release you from all sins. Do not grieve.\n\n\"Sarva-dharmān parityajya\" means performing every meritorious act without expecting any fruit from it. The dharma we perform should never become a transaction. An excellent illustration of this is the conversation between Dharmarāja and Draupadī. Draupadī says: \"I have heard that 'dharma protects those who protect it' (dharmo rakṣati rakṣitaḥ) -- why then did it not protect you? Why did we lose our kingdom and have to go to the forest?\" Dharmarāja's reply is striking. He says: \"I am not a trader in dharma; I practice dharma purely for my own inner contentment, not out of any longing for fruit.\" Thus, whatever dharma one performs, it is nothing but worship of the Lord; there is no longing for fruit in it. Living in this way, the Lord burns away the sins of all our births. When we surrender fully to the Lord, we may remain safe and fearless in the Lord's hands. This is the final assurance Krishna gives us in the teaching of the Gītā. Here the portion of the Lord's own teaching in the Gītā comes to its close. The remaining verses of this chapter give the phalaśruti (the fruit/benefit) of the Gītā.",
  "18.67": "idaṃ te na atapaskāya na abhaktāya kadācana |\nnacha aśuśrūṣave vācyaṃ nacha māṃ yaḥ abhyasūyati -- This should never be told to one without tapas, nor to one without devotion, nor to one uninterested in hearing it, nor to one who bears ill-will toward me.\n\nHere Krishna explains how the tradition of knowledge is to be protected. Do not tell this to one who lacks deep reflectiveness; do not tell it to one who, though reflective, has no interest in hearing it; do not tell it to one who has interest but lacks bhakti/love; and above all, never, under any circumstance, tell it to one who bears impatience, envy or hatred toward the Lord. Never make spiritual knowledge common property of the street.\n\nAt one time the view grew widespread that Vedānta must never be laid open publicly, that it must be kept secret. The result over time was that there came to be no one at all to open the knowledge that had been kept hidden. Today most of us are people who perform ārati to spiritual texts, smear them with kumkuma, and shut them away. For this reason, we must take up the work of opening this precious, hidden knowledge and giving it to those who are interested.\n\nWhat fruit comes from studying and reflecting upon this precious message of the Bhagavad Gītā, Krishna explains in the next four verses. The true fruit of the Bhagavad Gītā is mokṣa, the final and highest of human goals. The Gītā is the essence of all the scriptures that teach spiritual knowledge. This one text alone suffices to know the Lord's true nature and reach the Lord's realm. The entire essence, the entire secret, of all the scriptures can be found in this dialogue between Krishna and Arjuna. There is no other text that so fully gathers together the complete essence of all scripture. By learning this Gītā from a guru; by reflecting on it; by following in life the means of sādhanā stated here; and by teaching what we have understood to those who are interested -- we may reach the Lord's realm, mokṣa.",
  "18.68": "yaḥ imam paramaṃ guhyaṃ mat bhakteṣu abhidhāsyati |\nbhaktiṃ mayi parāṃ kṛtvā mām eva eṣyati asaṃśayaḥ -- One who declares this supreme secret among my devotees, having developed supreme devotion to me, comes to me alone -- of this there is no doubt.\n\nWhoever comes to know this knowledge, and gives it to the sādhaka who has śraddhā and bhakti toward me, will certainly come and reach me. There is no doubt in this.",
  "18.69": "nacha tasmāt manuṣyeṣu kaścit me priyakṛt tamaḥ |\nbhavitā nacha me tasmāt anyaḥ priyataraḥ bhuvi -- No one among men does me a dearer service than he, and no one dearer than him shall ever be born on earth hereafter.\n\n\"Those who perform the excellent task of learning the Gītā and carrying it to the community of devotees are the most beloved to me among human beings. There is no human upon this earth who does anything more pleasing to me than they,\" says Krishna. Knowers are dear to the Lord. Dearer still than they are those who, having become knowers themselves, give the sweetness of that knowledge to society. \"They are, on this earth, the very foremost among those who do what pleases me,\" says Krishna.",
  "18.70": "adhyeṣyate ca yaḥ imam dharmyaṃ saṃvādam āvayoḥ |\njñāna yajñena tena aham iṣṭaḥ syām iti me matiḥ -- And whoever studies this dharma-declaring dialogue of ours -- through that jñāna-yajña, I shall have been worshipped, this is my conviction.\n\nEven if we lack the capacity to digest the Gītā's knowledge and teach it to others, simply studying this dialogue between Krishna and Arjuna on dharma under a guru's guidance becomes, like a jñāna-yajña, worship of the Lord. \"This is my own view,\" says Krishna. One who learns through the Gītā the \"dharma of life\" -- what a person ought to know about the Lord, and how one ought to walk the path of dharma -- and lives by it, reaches mokṣa.",
  "18.71": "śraddhāvān anasūyaḥ ca śṛṇuyāt api yaḥ naraḥ |\nsaḥ api muktaḥ śubhān lokān prāpnuyāt puṇyakarmaṇām -- Even one who merely listens, with faith and without envy, is freed [from sin] and attains the auspicious realms of those of meritorious deeds.\n\nIf we lack the capacity to study the Gītā ourselves, it is enough simply to listen to it, with faith and devotion and without any envy, from those who know it -- we may wash away our sins and reach the Lord's realm. This is Krishna's assurance.",
  "18.72": "kaccit etat śrutaṃ pārtha tvayā ekāgreṇa cetasā |\nkaccit ajñāna sammohaḥ praṇaṣṭaḥ te dhanañjaya -- O Pārtha, have you heard this with a one-pointed mind? O Dhanañjaya, has your delusion born of ignorance been destroyed?\n\nThis is Krishna's final word in the Gītā. Having explained everything, Krishna asks his disciple Arjuna: \"Did what I said enter your ears and truly reach your heart? Has this dispelled the wrong understanding and confusion that was in your mind?\" Here Krishna questions Arjuna the way a guru questions his disciple. Here concludes Krishna's speech in the Gītā, comprising 574 verses. What remains of this chapter is Arjuna's final verse out of his total of 84 verses, and Sañjaya's last five verses out of his total of 41. [Krishna -- 574; Arjuna -- 84; Sañjaya -- 41; Dhṛtarāṣṭra -- 1; total 700 verses.]",
  "18.73": "arjunaḥ uvāca -- Arjuna said:\n\nnaṣṭaḥ mohaḥ smṛtiḥ labdhā tvat prasādāt mayā acyuta |\nsthitaḥ asmi gatasandehaḥ kariṣye vacanaṃ tava -- O Acyuta, my delusion is destroyed. Through your grace I have regained my memory. I stand firm, my doubts gone. I shall act on your word.\n\nThe wrong understanding that was in me is gone. The state I was in, unable to tell right from wrong, has departed. My memory, which had gone astray in attachment to kinsmen, has returned. Through the grace of you, the Acyuta (the unfailing one), my forgetfulness has vanished. All my questions have found their answer. Your words have convinced me. I shall act exactly as you say.\n\nWhat we should note here is that Arjuna, describing what Krishna's teaching brought him, does not say \"I have gained knowledge,\" but says \"memory has returned.\" What this tells us is that Arjuna was already a great knower of that age; but on the battlefield he lost all his memory and sank down. This was itself the Lord's play. Krishna used Arjuna in this way as an instrument in order to give the world this priceless work. The practical presentation of whatever message the Bhagavad Gītā carries is the Mahābhārata. Each and every character appearing there represents one or another message of the Gītā. Seen psychologically, the Mahābhārata is not simply history that took place five thousand years ago between the Kauravas and Pāṇḍavas -- it is the struggle within us, the struggle of our own life. The Lord has given us, through the Mahābhārata, both the theoretical message (Theory) and its practical demonstration (Practical presentation).",
  "18.74": "sañjaya uvāca -- Sañjaya said:\n\niti ahaṃ vāsudevasya pārthasya ca mahātmanaḥ |\nsaṃvādam imam aśrauṣam adbhutaṃ romaharṣaṇam -- Thus I have heard this wondrous, hair-raising dialogue between the great-souled Vāsudeva and Pārtha.\n\nSañjaya, having directly witnessed this extraordinary dialogue between Krishna and Arjuna, is filled with wonder. He says: I have heard this dialogue between Vāsudeva and Arjuna that took place before my very eyes. This is my great fortune. The experience I have had cannot be described in words. It is a marvel. Having heard this dialogue, my body has thrilled with joy. A wave of bliss has overtaken me.",
  "18.75": "vyāsaprasādāt śrutavān etat guhyam ahaṃ param |\nyogaṃ yogeśvarāt kṛṣṇāt sākṣāt kathayataḥ svayam -- Through Vyāsa's grace I have heard this supreme secret, this yoga, directly from Krishna, the Lord of yoga, speaking it himself.\n\nThrough the grace of my most venerable guru Vyāsa, I have received this wondrous fortune. Even the deepest of the world's secrets, which cannot be had however much one longs for it, has become known even to me. From Krishna, the Lord of Yoga, the unified embodiment of all the powers of the entire universe, I have heard directly, with my own ears, the very essence and totality of spiritual truth.",
  "18.76": "rājan saṃsmṛtya saṃsmṛtya saṃvādam imam adbhutam |\nkeśava arjunayoḥ puṇyaṃ hṛṣyāmi ca muhuḥ muhuḥ -- O King, remembering again and again this wondrous, sacred dialogue of Keśava and Arjuna, I thrill with joy again and again.\n\nO King (addressing Dhṛtarāṣṭra), remembering and remembering this wondrous dialogue, I am filled with wonder, joy, and astonishment, my body thrilling again and again. Nothing like this has happened before, and nothing like it will happen again. The power that governs the entire universe, that governs all the gods and their overlords, stood here in the form of Arjuna's charioteer and spoke this wondrous message -- no one has ever heard or experienced the like of it before. Remembering this, I am thrilled again and again.",
  "18.77": "tat ca saṃsmṛtya saṃsmṛtya rūpam atyadbhutaṃ hareḥ |\nvismayaḥ me mahān rājan hṛṣyāmi ca punaḥ punaḥ -- And remembering again and again that most wondrous form of Hari, my astonishment, O King, is great, and I thrill with joy again and again.\n\nSañjaya, who had himself received the vision of Krishna's cosmic form, continues: O King, that Paramātman -- Hariḥ, who resolves all our difficulties -- has today given us this wondrous message that resolves every difficulty. I have seen Krishna; I have seen his wondrous cosmic form; I have heard his words. This is a joy beyond what words can express. I am filled to overflowing with joy. Having thus shared his own bliss, Sañjaya goes on to declare what he takes to be the phalaśruti (the fruit) of all this.",
  "18.78": "yatra yogeśvaraḥ kṛṣṇaḥ yatra pārthaḥ dhanurdharaḥ |\ntatra śrīḥ vijayaḥ bhūtiḥ dhruvā nītiḥ matiḥ mama -- Wherever Krishna, the Lord of Yoga, is, wherever Pārtha, the wielder of the bow, is -- there is fortune, victory, prosperity and unshakeable righteousness. This is my conviction.\n\nWherever Nara and Nārāyaṇa are present, everything is present there. Every kind of wealth, victory, inner attainment, and outer prosperity abide there. This is my firm conviction, says Sañjaya.\n\nThe Bhagavad Gītā is a matchless text that gives us the spiritual knowledge and Vedic wisdom needed to rise above the pull of worldly pleasure, to a higher plane. If the Gītā stands firm in the mind, falsehood and deceit become impossible. Wherever the Gītā is studied, there sincerity is present. If we read the Gītā, touch the Gītā, and yet speak falsehood and live dishonestly, that is a betrayal we commit against the Gītācārya himself.\n\nWherever there is the knowledge of the Gītā, there is victory, there is prosperity, there is righteousness, there is fortune -- and with this, the most wondrous message of the Bhagavad Gītā comes to its close.\n\niti aṣṭādaśaḥ adhyāyaḥ -- Thus ends the eighteenth chapter.\n\nsarve janāḥ sukhinaḥ bhavantu -- May all people be happy.\n\nśrī kṛṣṇārpaṇam astu -- May this be offered unto Śrī Krishna."
};

